    <node id="YELLOW MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), the larval stage of the darkling beetle, are widely recognized for their remarkable ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), polypropylene (PP), polyethylene (PE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polylactic acid (PLA), expanded polystyrene (EPS), and ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA). This biodegradation process is facilitated by their gut microbiota, which plays a crucial role in depolymerizing and breaking down these plastics through oxidation and depolymerization mechanisms. Research has demonstrated that yellow mealworms can consume and degrade polyether-PU foam, leading to significant mass loss, and can also metabolize polystyrene, completing their life cycle even when fed a polystyrene diet. However, their survival rates and pupation success are negatively impacted when their diet consists solely of plastics, and they exhibit lower consumption efficiency compared to superworms (*Zophobas morio*).

Studies, such as those by Yang et al. (2018), have highlighted the potential of yellow mealworms in environmental cleanup efforts, as they can utilize plastics for metabolic activities and contribute to environmental remediation. Their gut microbiome undergoes significant changes during plastic consumption, with symbiotic bacteria and fungi playing a key role in the degradation process. Specifically, yellow mealworms have been shown to biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and polypropylene (PP) through gut-microbe-dependent processes. They are also capable of ingesting and degrading polypropylene (PP) materials, such as disposable surgical masks, and are used to study biodegradation processes. Research has shown that they can biodegrade surgical masks, demonstrating their role in plastic degradation. Significant changes in their gut microbial community have been observed when they are fed different layers of surgical masks, highlighting the importance of their gut bacteria and fungi in the degradation process. However, their survival rates and degradation capabilities vary depending on the type of plastic consumed, with EPS not providing necessary nutrients for their growth, which may explain their lower survival rates and reduced efficiency when consuming plastics exclusively.

Beyond their environmental applications, yellow mealworms are also valued for their high nutritional content, particularly their protein levels, making them a viable option for animal feed and a subject of research for potential human consumption. Overall, yellow mealworms are a promising focus of research for sustainable waste management due to their ability to degrade a wide range of plastics through gut-microbe-dependent mechanisms. Their gut microbial communities play a key role</data>
