efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHER-PU FOAM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyether-PU foam is a type of polyurethane foam that can be degraded by mealworms and their gut microbiota, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU foam is a type of polyurethane foam that contains ether bonds and is studied for its biodegradation properties, particularly in the context of insect larvae and gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU foam is a type of polyurethane foam that is chemically cross-linked and resistant to degradation, but can be broken down by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU foam is a type of polyurethane foam that mealworms can chew and degrade, reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU foam is a type of polyurethane plastic used in various applications, known for its high environmental resistance but low biodegradability.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU foam is a type of polyurethane waste that yellow mealworms are tested to degrade, contributing to environmental pollution reduction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut microbiome of **Tenebrio molitor** (yellow mealworms), **Zophobas atratus** (superworms), and **Galleria mellonella** (greater wax moth) is a diverse and dynamic community of microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi, that reside in their digestive tracts. This microbiome plays a critical role in the biodegradation of various plastics, such as polyethylene (PE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polystyrene (PS), polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), and even polypropylene-based materials like surgical masks. Specific bacterial families and strains, including PM S1, Citrobacter sp., Kosakonia sp., Klebsiella sp. WJ2020, and Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025, have been identified as key contributors to the breakdown process. These bacteria enhance the degradation of plastics into smaller molecules through the production of enzymes that facilitate depolymerization and degradation.

The gut microbiome of **Tenebrio molitor** larvae, in particular, is essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) and polystyrene microplastics (PS MPs). It alters intestinal metabolic pathways in response to PS polymers and contains bacteria and enzymes that facilitate depolymerization, influencing both the depolymerization and mineralization processes. The efficiency of PS MP degradation is significantly influenced by the gut microbiome, with distinct microbial communities associated with low-, medium-, and ultrahigh-molecular-weight PS. The microbial composition varies based on the molecular weight of the polystyrene fed, and changes in molecular weight and polydispersity index further evidence the microbiome's role in degradation.

The composition of the gut microbiome in mealworms and related larvae is highly influenced by their diet. Significant changes are observed when they are fed plastic-based diets, such as polyether-PU, polystyrene, low-density polyethylene, or PVC. These dietary shifts highlight the microbiome's adaptability and its active role in plastic biodegradation. The gut microbiome of mealworms and superworms exhibits distinct changes during the biodegradation process, indicating a dynamic interaction between the microorganisms and the plastic substrates. The microbial community structure shifts depending on dietary changes, further emphasizing the microbiome's responsiveness to environmental conditions.

The gut microbiome of these insect larvae consists of various bacteria, including Proteobacteria, Bacteroides, Firmicutes, and Actinobacteria, which play a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics. Specific bacterial classes and genera</data>
