    <node id="DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Depolymerization is a critical chemical process in which long-chain polymer molecules are cleaved into smaller units, playing an essential role in the biodegradation of various plastics. This process has been extensively observed in the degradation of polymers such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyether-PU foam, low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP), PET, PVC, cellulose, PU, EVA, and even surgical masks. The breakdown of these polymers into smaller molecules is facilitated by organisms like superworms (Zophobas atratus), yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae), and their gut microbiota. Analytical techniques such as FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) have provided evidence of this process, particularly in the digestive systems of these larvae.

Depolymerization is particularly significant in the context of microplastics, where long-chain structures are fragmented into smaller molecular weight components, a phenomenon observed in mealworms, especially at older larval stages. This process not only highlights the potential of these organisms in plastic waste management but also underscores the role of their gut bacteria and extracellular enzymes in enabling the breakdown of complex polymer chains. While some studies suggest that Tenebrio molitor larvae can perform depolymerization independently of gut microbes, others emphasize the critical role of gut microbiota in facilitating this process.

Evidence of depolymerization has been observed in the frass (excrement) of mealworms fed polystyrene and polyethylene, further confirming the breakdown of these polymers into smaller units. The process has been specifically noted in the degradation of PS, LDPE, PVC, PET, polypropylene, and even surgical masks, with mealworms and superworms playing a pivotal role in facilitating this breakdown through their gut microbiota and enzymatic activity.

Overall, depolymerization is the first and essential step in the biodegradation of plastics, involving the breaking down of polymer chains through oxidation or hydrolysis, followed by further oxidation and mineralization. This comprehensive understanding of depolymerization underscores its importance in addressing the global challenge of plastic waste. The process involves breaking down long-chain polymers into smaller molecules, which is a critical step in the biodegradation of various types of polyethylene (LDPE, LLDPE, HDPE) and other polymers by mealworms and their gut microbiota. Depolymerization is a key mechanism in the biodegradation of plastics, offering insights into sustainable solutions for plastic pollution. Specifically, mealworms break down polymer chains of microplastics like PS and PVC, resulting in reduced molecular weights and</data>
