    <node id="FTIR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is a technique used to confirm chemical modifications during LDPE biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is a versatile analytical technique widely used to study chemical changes and biodegradation processes in various polymers. FTIR has been employed to analyze the chemical bonds and structural modifications in materials such as polypropylene (PP), polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polystyrene (PS). 

In the context of biodegradation, FTIR has been particularly useful in detecting the formation of new functional groups, such as carbonyl and hydroxyl groups, which indicate polymer degradation. For instance, FTIR was used to study the biodegradation of polypropylene by insect larvae, including *Tenebrio molitor* and *Zophobas atratus*, revealing chemical changes such as the appearance of carbonyl and hydroxyl groups. Similarly, FTIR was applied to analyze the degradation of PET by *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, providing qualitative evidence of oxidation and structural changes in the polymer. 

FTIR has also been utilized to examine the biodegradation of PVC by yellow mealworms and polystyrene by mealworms, identifying chemical modifications and characterizing the functional groups of degradation products in frass. Additionally, FTIR has been used to study the chemical changes in EPS and LDPE, as well as to analyze the degradation of polyether-PU foam, highlighting its broad applicability in polymer research. 

Overall, FTIR serves as a critical tool for understanding the chemical composition and degradation mechanisms of polymers, providing insights into the biodegradation processes facilitated by various organisms, particularly insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XPS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of polystyrene (PS) films after biodegradation, particularly the presence of oxygen-containing functional groups.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) is employed to study the surface chemistry of polyether-PU foam, showing changes after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is used to analyze changes in surface chemical composition and functional groups of HDPE during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TGA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TGA (Thermogravimetric Analysis) is a method used to study the thermal degradation of surgical mask layers and frass after biodegradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is used to assess the thermal stability of polyether-PU foam, indicating degradation after being processed by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a technique used to analyze frass samples to confirm the degradation of plastics in the guts of superworms and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is used to measure the thermal stability and decomposition of residual PS in frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a method used to study the thermal degradation of plastic polymers and their biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is used to study the modification of polymers during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE) by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is used to support findings from NMR, confirming the amount of polymer excreted in the frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) is used to study the thermal changes of polystyrene and its degradation products under different atmospheres.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is a technique used to detect thermal modifications in polystyrene (PS) after degradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a technique used to study the thermal degradation of materials, such as polystyrene, by measuring mass loss over temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;TGA (Thermogravimetric Analysis) was used to study the thermal degradation and biodegradation of polypropylene by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a technique used to study the physical and chemical modifications of LDPE during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a technique used to study the thermal degradation of plastics, such as LDPE, during biodegradation by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPC">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is a technique used in the study to analyze the molecular weight changes in polystyrene (PS) after degradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is a widely used analytical technique for determining the molecular weight distribution of polymers, particularly in the context of biodegradation studies involving insects such as yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and superworms. GPC is employed to analyze residual polymers in frass samples, providing insights into the degradation process of various plastics, including polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). 

The technique confirms the decreased molecular weight of these polymers after biodegradation, indicating depolymerization and fragmentation. For example, GPC has been used to measure changes in the molecular weight of polyether-PU foam, EPS, LDPE, HDPE, and PVC fragments found in mealworm frass, as well as to study the biodegradation of PET by *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. By analyzing molecular weight distribution (Mw and Mn), GPC assesses the degree of depolymerization and provides critical data on the efficiency of plastic degradation in the guts of these insects. Overall, GPC serves as a key method for understanding the molecular weight changes in polymers during biodegradation, offering valuable insights into the breakdown mechanisms of plastics by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polystyrene residues during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) is a technique used to analyze changes in the molecular weight of polymers, such as polystyrene, during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) is used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of residual polymers after biodegradation by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane (PU or PUR) is a versatile synthetic polymer composed of organic units joined by carbamate (urethane) links. It is widely used in various applications, including foams, coatings, adhesives, kitchen sponges, thermal insulation, automotive components, and textiles. Known for its high environmental resistance and durability, polyurethane is a petroleum-based plastic that contributes to environmental pollution due to its persistence and low biodegradability. However, recent studies have explored its biodegradation potential through various organisms and processes.

Polyurethane can be broken down by specific organisms such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms, and yellow mealworms, which consume and degrade the material, leading to changes in its chemical functional groups. Additionally, certain bacterial consortia and enzymes, such as Candida rugosa lipase, have been shown to degrade polyurethane. Environmental fungi and bacteria are also subjects of biodegradation studies, particularly in vitro and in composite forms with materials like clay. These studies aim to address the environmental challenges posed by polyurethane's resistance to degradation and its persistence in ecosystems.

In summary, polyurethane is a widely used synthetic polymer with significant environmental implications due to its durability and resistance to biodegradation. However, ongoing research into its biodegradation by organisms like mealworms, superworms, and specific microbial consortia offers potential solutions to mitigate its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane is a type of plastic that can be degraded by specific microbial communities, contributing to biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a versatile polymer used in foams, coatings, and adhesives, known for its flexibility and durability.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a polymer used in foams, coatings, and adhesives, known for its flexibility and durability but challenging to biodegrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane is a recalcitrant plastic material that can be degraded by selected landfill microbial communities, as revealed by metagenomic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHER-PU">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyether-PU is a type of polyurethane foam that can be consumed and biodegraded by yellow mealworms, leading to mass loss and structural changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU is a type of polyurethane primarily used for thermal insulation, with limited biodegradation reported.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU is a type of polyurethane that can be degraded by certain fungi, such as Cladosporium tenuissimum and Cladosporium pseudocladosporioides.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYESTER-PU">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyester-PU is a type of polyurethane with ester bonds that can be hydrolyzed, making it more biodegradable than polyether-PU.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyester-PU is another form of polyurethane, though its biodegradation by mealworms is not explicitly detailed in the text.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran is a nutrient-rich byproduct of grain processing, commonly obtained from milling grains, and is widely recognized as a valuable dietary supplement. It is extensively used in scientific studies involving mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*) and superworms, serving as both a natural food source and a control diet in experiments. Bran provides essential nutrients such as carbohydrates, fats, proteins, vitamins, minerals, and trace elements, which are crucial for the growth, development, and survival of these larvae. It is particularly noted for its high content of monoenoic fatty acids, which contribute to the fatty acid composition of mealworm larvae.

In scientific research, Bran plays a pivotal role in studies investigating the effects of various plastics, such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS), on the survival, growth, and biodegradation capabilities of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. When co-fed with plastics, Bran exhibits a complex interaction with the biodegradation process. While it slightly reduces the efficiency of PE removal by the larvae, it significantly enhances the consumption and degradation rate of PS. Bran also influences the gut microbiome of the larvae, which is critical for the biodegradation process, as evidenced by changes in frass composition.

Bran is often used in combination with plastic waste to feed mealworms, supporting their digestion and energy needs. It alleviates the negative impacts of microplastics on plastic-degrading larvae by maintaining their nutrient levels and physiological performance. Additionally, Bran mitigates oxidative stress and enhances metabolic activities, thereby improving the survival rates and biomass levels of larvae during plastic biodegradation studies.

In some studies, Bran is specifically used as a control group without antibiotics to assess its impact on the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms. Its role in enhancing PS degradation, while slightly reducing PE removal efficiency, highlights its importance in the study of plastic biodegradation processes. Bran is also used as a control in studies to compare the survival rates and consumption rates of *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus* larvae, as well as to evaluate the effects of microplastic ingestion by mealworms.

Overall, Bran serves as a critical dietary component in scientific research, providing a baseline for comparison in experiments and contributing to the understanding of plastic biodegradation by *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. It is also used in feeding experiments with *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, often mixed with polystyrene to study biodegradation, and as a control in various studies. Its complex composition</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FRASS is the excrement produced by the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as yellow mealworms), *Tenebrio obscurus*, and other similar insects such as *Zophobas atratus* (superworms). It is a critical byproduct of their digestion process, particularly when they consume various types of plastics, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyethylene (PE), polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, polystyrene (PS), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polypropylene (PP). FRASS serves as a valuable indicator of the biodegradation process of these plastics, as it contains residual microplastics, oxidized functional groups, and other byproducts of digestion. These remnants provide insights into the efficiency and extent of plastic degradation, as well as the larvae's ability to break down plastic waste.

The analysis of FRASS is essential for assessing feedstock digestibility, biodegradation efficiency, and the degradation of microplastics (MPS). It often contains undigested or partially digested plastic residues, such as polystyrene (PS), PVC, and polypropylene (PP) fragments, which are used to measure biodegradation rates and the extent of plastic degradation. Researchers also identify degradation compounds within FRASS, such as monomers and oligomers from polystyrene biodegradation, including styrene and other byproducts. These compounds help in understanding the molecular changes and functional groups resulting from the biodegradation process, such as the incorporation of oxygen and the formation of new functional groups. Changes in molecular weight and chemical structure of polyethylene (PE) and other plastics are also studied through FRASS analysis.

In addition to its role in environmental research, FRASS has practical applications in agriculture and sustainable practices. It is nutrient-rich and can be used as a natural fertilizer and plant growth medium, contributing to a zero-waste circular economy. Its composition supports plant growth, making it a sustainable alternative to traditional fertilizers. Furthermore, FRASS has potential uses in construction materials and further recycling processes, highlighting its versatility.

However, FRASS may also contain remnants of undegraded plastics, which can contribute to environmental pollution. While the larvae partially degrade plastics, the presence of these remnants underscores the limitations of the biodegradation process and the need for further research to improve plastic waste management. Additionally, FRASS may contain microplastics or toxic substances that could impact aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems, raising</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff5632