hunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHER BONDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ether bonds are chemical linkages in polyether-PU foam that are cleaved during biodegradation by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="URETHANE BONDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Urethane bonds are chemical linkages in polyether-PU foam that are broken down during biodegradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Urethane bonds are chemical linkages in polyether-PU foam that are hydrolyzed during biodegradation by mealworms, as evidenced by FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-MOLECULAR-WEIGHT OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-molecular-weight oligomers are smaller molecules released during the depolymerization of polyether-PU foam by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL STABILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal stability refers to the ability of a material, such as polyether-PU foam, to resist decomposition at high temperatures, which is studied using TGA.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal stability refers to the resistance of polyether-PU foam to decomposition at high temperatures, which decreases after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal stability refers to the ability of materials, such as polyurethane elastomers, to withstand high temperatures without degrading.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-THROUGHPUT SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">High-throughput sequencing is a method used to analyze bacterial community dynamics in organisms like Pacific white shrimp during nursery rearing.&lt;SEP&gt;High-throughput sequencing is used to analyze changes in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms when fed polyether-PU foam.&lt;SEP&gt;High-throughput sequencing is a method used to analyze shifts in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High-throughput sequencing is used to analyze the gut microbiome of larvae and understand its role in PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High-throughput sequencing is used to analyze the gut microbiome of larvae and understand its role in PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High-throughput sequencing is a technique used to analyze changes in the gut microbial community of mealworms during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High-throughput sequencing is used to analyze shifts in the gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor larvae in response to different microplastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;High-throughput sequencing is used to analyze the diversity and composition of gut microbial communities in Tenebrio molitor larvae during microplastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial communities are groups of microorganisms that interact within an environment, often studied for their role in biodegradation and soil health.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial community refers to the diverse populations of bacteria in the mealworm gut that play a role in digesting synthetic and natural polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbial community in the gut of yellow mealworms changes significantly when exposed to a polyether-PU diet, influencing biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbial community refers to the diverse microorganisms present in the gut of Tenebrio molitor, which play a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial communities, such as those in landfills, are studied for their ability to degrade recalcitrant materials like polyurethane and xenobiotic additives.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbial community refers to the diverse microorganisms present in the guts of organisms like mealworms and grasshoppers, which play a role in biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbial community in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae is crucial for the biodegradation of microplastics, with specific families like Enterobacteriaceae and Streptococcaceae playing dominant roles.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbial community in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of aged PE and PS microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbial community in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae plays a key role in the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial community refers to the diverse populations of bacteria in the mealworm gut that play a role in digesting synthetic and natural polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIRCULAR PLASTIC ECONOMY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The circular plastic economy refers to sustainable practices aimed at reducing plastic waste, including biodegradation methods for polyether-PU foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STAPHYLOCOCCUS EPIDERMIDIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Staphylococcus epidermidis is a bacterial species studied for its potential to degrade synthetic polystyrenes, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Staphylococcus epidermidis is a bacterial strain that can modify the surface properties of polyether-PU polymers, contributing to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALTERNARIA SP. PURDK2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Alternaria sp. PURDK2 is a fungal strain capable of degrading polyether-PU polymers by cleaving urethane bonds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLADOSPORIUM TENUISSIMUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cladosporium tenuissimum is a fungal strain with high polyether-PU degradation abilities, resulting in significant weight loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Cladosporium tenuissimum is a fungus known for its ability to degrade polyether-PU, resulting in significant weight loss of the material.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLADOSPORIUM PSEUDOCLADOSPORIOIDES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cladosporium pseudocladosporioides is a fungal strain capable of degrading polyether-PU polymers, contributing to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cladosporium pseudocladosporioides is a fungus capable of degrading polyether-PU, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cladosporium pseudocladosporioides is a microorganism studied for its ability to degrade polyurethane by attacking its chemical bonds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Tenebrio molitor**, commonly known as the yellow mealworm or mealworm larvae, is a highly versatile insect species with significant applications in environmental sustainability, nutrition, and plastic waste management. As the immature stage of darkling beetles, Tenebrio molitor has gained attention for its remarkable ability to biodegrade a wide range of synthetic polymers, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyurethane (PU), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polylactic acid (PLA), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). This biodegradation process is facilitated by the insect's gut microbiome, which contains symbiotic bacteria such as *Mixta tenebrionis*, *Klebsiella sp. WJ2020*, and *Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025*. These microbes play a crucial role in enzymatic degradation and nitrogen fixation, breaking down complex polymers into simpler compounds. This makes Tenebrio molitor a key model organism in plastic waste management research.

Both larvae and adults of Tenebrio molitor can consume and degrade plastics such as Styrofoam, low-density polyethylene, and polypropylene. While slight weight increases have been observed when fed polystyrene and polyethylene, indicating their adaptation to plastic diets, they do not thrive on a plastic-only diet. The larvae exhibit higher degradation rates for PET compared to other plastics, with significant mass reduction efficiency and involvement of gut microbiota in the process. The synergistic action of their gut microbiota and host metabolic pathways enables the breakdown of long-chain polymers during degradation. Additionally, Tenebrio molitor is capable of biodegrading microplastics, particularly micro-polystyrene (MPS) and PET microplastics, and is used in studies to understand the effects of microplastic feeding on their development, survival, weight, and metabolism. Notably, Tenebrio molitor larvae can complete their life cycle even when fed polystyrene, further highlighting their adaptability.

Environmental parameters such as temperature, humidity, and light intensity influence the biodegradation capabilities of Tenebrio molitor. These factors play a role in the efficiency of plastic degradation, particularly for materials like thermocol and Styrofoam. The larvae are also studied for their light reactions, which may have implications for their role in biodegradation processes. Furthermore, Tenebrio molitor larvae exhibit oxidative stress responses when exposed to microplastics, indicating sensitivity to external abiotic disturbances.

Tenebrio molitor</data>
