    <node id="GALLERIA MELLONELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Galleria mellonella**, commonly known as the greater wax moth or waxworm, is an insect species that has gained significant scientific attention due to its remarkable ability to biodegrade various types of plastics. The larvae of Galleria mellonella are particularly notable for their capacity to break down and modify the backbone of plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), and polyurethane (PU). This biodegradation process is facilitated by the larvae's gut microbiome, which includes specific bacteria such as *Enterobacter sp. D1* and *Massilia sp. PS1903* (also referred to as *Masilia sp. FS1903*). These bacteria play a crucial role in degrading polyethylene and polystyrene, respectively. Additionally, the larvae's lipid metabolism is upregulated when they feed on plastics, further enhancing their ability to degrade these materials.

Galleria mellonella larvae are not only capable of chewing and consuming plastics but can also completely digest and biodegrade polystyrene microplastics, offering a potential solution for reducing plastic waste. They have been observed to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) films, a common type of plastic pollutant. The larvae's sharp mandibles and gut microorganisms are essential for the digestion and degradation of plastics, making them a valuable focus of research in the fight against plastic pollution. The intestinal microbiome of the larvae plays a crucial role in this process, contributing significantly to the biodegradation of polyethylene and other plastics.

In addition to their gut microbiome, enzymes in the larvae's saliva have also been identified as contributing factors to their plastic-degrading capabilities. Changes in the microbial composition of the larvae during the biodegradation process have been noted, particularly when degrading polyolefins. This unique combination of physical and biological mechanisms has positioned Galleria mellonella larvae as a promising biological tool for plastic biodegradation.

Galleria mellonella larvae are also studied for their ability to develop biodegradation mechanisms to break down polymer branches, further highlighting their potential in addressing plastic pollution. While they are known for their ability to consume bee wax, their capacity to metabolize plastics has become a significant area of research. Notably, the gut fungus *Aspergillus flavus PEDX3* has been identified as contributing to their polyethylene (PE) degradation capabilities.

Furthermore, Galleria mellonella larvae are plastic-degrading invertebrates that could be examined for oxidative stress responses to microplastics. Their gut microbiome's</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759f