    <node id="POLYSTYRENE (PS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene (PS) is a high molecular weight synthetic aromatic hydrocarbon polymer derived from the monomer styrene, widely recognized as a petroleum-based plastic. It is extensively used across various industries, particularly in packaging, insulation, and disposable products, due to its affordability, durability, and lightweight properties. PS accounts for approximately 7.1% of global plastic production and is commonly found in products such as Styrofoam, EPS insulation, XPS foam, electronic packaging, food containers, and coffee cups. Traditionally, PS has been considered non-biodegradable in natural environments because of its high molecular weight, stable structure, and non-hydrolysable carbon-carbon backbone, which make it resistant to microbial degradation and enzymatic attack. This resistance contributes to its persistence in the environment, making it a significant source of plastic waste, environmental pollution, and microplastics.

However, recent studies have revealed that PS can be biodegraded under specific conditions, particularly with the assistance of certain organisms and microorganisms. Insects such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms), *Tenebrio obscurus*, superworms, yellow mealworms, *Zophobas atratus*, and greater wax moths (*Galleria mellonella*) have demonstrated the ability to degrade PS. This biodegradation process is facilitated by the gut microbiota of these insects, including specific bacterial strains like *Priestia megaterium S1* (also referred to as PM S1), PELW2042, strain YT2, and *Achromobacter xylosidans M9*, as well as fungal genera such as *Rhodotorula* and *Cryptococcus*. These microorganisms assist in breaking down PS through mechanisms such as oxidation, depolymerization, and cleavage of its benzene ring structure. During biodegradation, PS undergoes surface alterations and oxidative changes, and trace amounts of elements such as Br, Ca, and Hf may be present. Furthermore, PS undergoes oxidative depolymerization during biodegradation, releasing reactive oxygen species (ROS) in organisms.

In experiments, *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus* larvae, as well as superworms and greater wax moths, have been shown to consume and degrade PS, resulting in a significant reduction in mass over time. A significant portion of the degraded PS is converted to CO2, while a small fraction is assimilated into biomass. Despite this, PS does not significantly contribute to the growth or survival of these insects. The</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626