    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM SP. YT2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2** is a bacterial strain isolated from the digestive tract of mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), also known as darkling beetles. This bacterium is particularly notable for its ability to degrade polystyrene (PS), a common type of plastic, converting it into carbon dioxide (CO2) and biomass. Studies have shown that Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 can achieve a weight loss of 28.6% in polystyrene over a period of 30 days, with some reports indicating a degradation rate of up to 7.4% over 60 days. These findings highlight its significant biodegradation potential and its role in addressing plastic pollution.

A key feature of Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 is its ability to form biofilms on polystyrene film surfaces, which enhances its capacity to degrade the plastic. The strain's biofilm-forming ability is a recurring theme, underscoring its effectiveness in breaking down plastic. While some descriptions mention its potential to degrade extracellular polymeric substances (EPS), the primary focus across all descriptions is its polystyrene-degrading capability.

Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 is a promising bacterial strain with potential applications in environmental remediation and plastic waste management. Its role in the gut microbiome of mealworms further emphasizes its ecological and environmental significance. Overall, Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a plastic-degrading bacterium isolated from the gut of mealworms, known for its ability to degrade polystyrene, form biofilms on plastic surfaces, and contribute to the biodegradation of plastic waste. Its potential applications make it a significant subject of study in the fields of microbiology and environmental science.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworm guts, capable of degrading polystyrene by forming biofilms and creating pits in the plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a bacterial strain isolated from the guts of mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene by forming biofilms and creating pits in the plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLYUYERA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klyuyera sp. is a dominant gut microbe in Tenebrio molitor larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).&lt;SEP&gt;Klyuyera sp. is a dominant species in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter sp. are bacteria, such as Enterobacter asburiae, capable of degrading polyethylene and potentially contributing to polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Indian mealmoth larvae, capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter sp. is a bacterium isolated from the gut of greater wax moth larvae, potentially involved in the degradation of polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of greater wax moths, involved in polyethylene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter sp. is a bacterial genus strongly associated with the gut microbiome of Zophobas atratus larvae fed polypropylene, potentially contributing to PP biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter sp. is a bacterial species associated with the gut microbiome of Zophobas atratus larvae during polypropylene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS FLAVUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus flavus is a fungus found in the guts of wax moth larvae that can biodegrade polyethylene microplastic particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus flavus is a fungus isolated from the gut of greater wax moth larvae, potentially involved in the degradation of polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus flavus is a fungus that contributes to the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) in insects like Galleria mellonella.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus flavus is a lignocellulose-degrading fungus isolated from wax moths, with upregulated laccase-like multicopper oxidases under plastic stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus flavus is a fungus isolated from the gut of greater wax moths, involved in polyethylene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Pseudomonas sp.** is a genus of bacteria known for its remarkable ability to degrade synthetic plastics and associated products, making it a significant contributor to plastic waste management. This bacterial genus includes species such as *Pseudomonas aeruginosa*, which are recognized for their capacity to degrade hydrocarbons and produce biosurfactants that enhance plastic degradation. 

A notable strain of *Pseudomonas sp.* has been isolated from the gut of superworms (*Zophobas atratus* larvae), where it plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS). This strain has been extensively studied for its enzymes involved in transforming styrene to phenylacetate, a process that underscores its role in plastic degradation. Additionally, *Pseudomonas sp.* has been identified in the gut of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms), further highlighting its contribution to the biodegradation of polystyrene. 

Beyond polystyrene, *Pseudomonas sp.* has also demonstrated the ability to degrade other synthetic materials, such as flexible polyurethane foams derived from castor oil and low molecular weight polyethylene wax under compost conditions. These capabilities emphasize the potential of *Pseudomonas sp.* in addressing plastic pollution through biodegradation. 

The discovery of *Pseudomonas sp.* in the gut microbiota of superworms and mealworms has shed light on the role of gut-associated bacteria in plastic biodegradation, offering promising avenues for further research and applications in environmental biotechnology. Overall, *Pseudomonas sp.* represents a versatile and environmentally significant bacterial genus with the potential to contribute to sustainable plastic waste management solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter is a bacterial strain isolated from red flour beetles, involved in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter is a bacterium isolated from the gut of red flour beetles, potentially involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter is a genus of microorganisms identified in the gut of greater wax moth larvae that may play a role in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, significantly associated with the degradation of polyethylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum, known for its ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum, capable of degrading polystyrene, indicating its role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria that increases in PP-fed yellow mealworms, suggesting its involvement in PP degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria that was found to be dominant in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with polypropylene (PP) foam plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPROPYLENE (PP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) is a widely used synthetic thermoplastic polymer derived from petroleum, characterized by its carbon-carbon backbone structure. It constitutes approximately 18.9% of global plastic production and is commonly utilized in packaging, textiles, disposable surgical masks, foam plastics, and various other applications. Despite its utility, PP is a significant contributor to plastic waste and environmental pollution, including microplastic pollution, due to its resistance to biodegradation. PP is a non-hydrolyzable plastic resistant to enzymatic depolymerization, and its chemical structure resembles natural polymers, making it potentially degradable by lignin-modifying enzymes.

Recent studies have explored the potential biodegradation of PP by certain organisms, particularly Tenebrio molitor (yellow mealworm) larvae and Zophobas atratus (superworm) larvae. While PP is generally resistant to biodegradation, research indicates that Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade PP, with the process being influenced by the polymer's molecular weight (MW) and physical properties. The degradation is less dependent on gut microbes compared to other plastics like polystyrene (PS) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). However, the extent of depolymerization varies, and PP does not significantly contribute to the growth of mealworms when consumed. Zophobas atratus larvae also show a preference for PP, ranking it lower than phenol-formaldehyde resin and polyethylene (PE) but higher than PS and PVC. Additionally, PP is being studied for biodegradation by bacterial strains like PELW2042, and its degradation protocols are being compared to those of polystyrene.

In the context of mealworms, polypropylene (PP) is a type of plastic that mealworms can consume, though at a lower rate compared to polystyrene. Mealworms can chew PP, as indicated by the chewed edges of PP bags in experiments. Specific gut bacteria in yellow mealworms, such as Spiroplasma, have been identified as playing a role in the degradation process. Changes in molecular weight and thermal stability have been observed in the frass of mealworms that have ingested PP, indicating partial degradation.

Despite these advancements, PP remains a challenging material to biodegrade due to its high molecular weight and resistance to microbial action. Its widespread use and persistence in the environment highlight the need for continued research into effective waste management and biodegradation solutions. Studies have also assessed the metabolic impact of PP on insect larvae, and while some organisms like yellow mealworms and superworms have shown the ability to biode</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a synthetic plastic polymer derived from petroleum, accounting for approximately 10.4% of global plastic production. Known for its durability, resistance to environmental degradation, and robust carbon-carbon backbone structure, PVC is widely used in construction, packaging, industrial products, and various other applications due to its strength and versatility. Its non-hydrolysable nature and resistance to microbial and enzymatic attack contribute to its persistence in the environment, making it a significant contributor to environmental pollution, particularly as microplastics.

Recent studies have explored the potential for PVC degradation by biological means, particularly through the action of certain insects such as *Tenebrio molitor* larvae (mealworms) and *Zophobas atratus*. These insects have demonstrated the ability to chew and partially biodegrade PVC, facilitated by gut microbes, resulting in limited mineralization to chloride and producing residual plastics with some toxicity. However, PVC is less preferred by these insects compared to other plastics like polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), likely due to its complex molecular structure. The degradation efficiency varies, with changes in PVC's crystalline structure observed in some regions, indicating partial degradation. The maximum degradation rate of PVC by mealworms has been reported at 11.8%, which is lower than that of PE and PS.

Despite the promising potential of insect-mediated degradation, PVC remains a challenging material to fully biodegrade. Its resistance to degradation and the limited extent of biodegradation by insects highlight ongoing environmental concerns. PVC ingestion by mealworms has been shown to negatively impact their survival, growth, and nutritional value, raising questions about the safety and feasibility of using these insects for large-scale PVC degradation. Additionally, PVC induces the highest levels of oxidative stress and physiological damage among the tested plastics, leading to incomplete biodegradation and malnutrition in plastic-degrading larvae.

During biodegradation, PVC releases toxic substances such as chlorinated intermediates and hydrogen chloride, which contribute to oxidative stress and damage in organisms. Compared to other polymers like polystyrene and polylactic acid, PVC exhibits higher toxicity and is more resistant to degradation. Furthermore, when ingested by mealworms, PVC remains in the intestinal tract for a longer time compared to PE and PS, likely due to its physicochemical properties.

In summary, PVC's widespread use and persistence in the environment continue to pose significant environmental and health risks. While biological methods, such as insect-mediated degradation, show potential for mitigating plastic waste, further research is needed to enhance the efficiency</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
