    <node id="POLYLACTIC ACID (PLA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polylactic acid (PLA) is a bio-based, biodegradable, and bioactive thermoplastic polymer derived from renewable resources such as corn starch or sugarcane. It is widely recognized as an environmentally friendly alternative to petroleum-based plastics and is commonly used in packaging and other applications due to its sustainable properties. PLA is characterized by its high polymer degradability, making it a promising material for sustainable waste management solutions. 

Studies have demonstrated that PLA can be degraded and assimilated by various organisms, including Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae, yellow mealworms, and superworms. These insects play a significant role in breaking down PLA through enzymatic hydrolysis, which causes less oxidative stress compared to non-degradable plastics like polyethylene (PE), polystyrene, and polyvinyl chloride. However, while PLA accelerates pupation in mealworms, it has been observed to lead to eclosion failure, indicating potential physiological impacts on the insects. Additionally, the degradation rate of PLA varies depending on environmental conditions, which may influence its overall biodegradability.

PLA can also be fragmented by organisms such as Eisenia fetida (earthworms) and Lumbricus terrestris into sub-micron microplastics, though significant biodegradation is not always observed in these cases. Despite its biodegradability, PLA exhibits the lowest mass loss when processed by mealworms compared to other plastics, suggesting variability in its degradation efficiency. 

Overall, PLA is a promising biodegradable polymer with significant potential for advancing sustainable waste management practices. Its ability to be degraded by insects and other organisms highlights its role in resource recovery and energy supplementation. However, further research is needed to fully understand its effects on insect physiology, degradation mechanisms, and performance under varying environmental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINERALIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">**MINERALIZATION** is a critical process in the biodegradation of plastics, where organic materials, particularly plastics such as polystyrene (PS), Styrofoam, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), expanded polystyrene (EPS), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), are transformed into simpler inorganic compounds, primarily carbon dioxide (CO2) and, in some cases, water (H2O). This transformation occurs during biodegradation and is facilitated by the metabolic activities of organisms like *Tenebrio molitor* larvae (mealworms), *Zophobas atratus* (superworms), and their gut microbiota, as well as other microbial activity, including microorganisms like strain YT2.

In the case of mealworms and superworms, ingested plastics such as polystyrene and LDPE are broken down into CO2 and other inorganic compounds. These byproducts can also be incorporated into lipids, contributing to the energy needs of the larvae. Mineralization is a key mechanism in the biodegradation of plastics, as it transforms complex organic materials into simpler, inorganic substances. This process highlights the potential of certain organisms to play a role in mitigating plastic waste through natural biodegradation pathways.

The descriptions consistently emphasize that mineralization involves the conversion of organic substances, particularly plastics, into inorganic compounds like CO2 and H2O. Mealworms and superworms, along with their gut bacteria, are prominent facilitators of this process. While some descriptions focus specifically on polystyrene, Styrofoam, or LDPE, the overarching theme is the breakdown of plastics into inorganic substances, primarily CO2, during biodegradation. This process not only demonstrates the ability of these organisms to degrade plastics but also underscores their potential role in addressing plastic pollution through natural biological mechanisms.

In summary, **MINERALIZATION** is a critical process in the biodegradation of plastics, where organic materials such as polystyrene, LDPE, and other plastic intermediates are transformed into inorganic compounds like CO2 and H2O, facilitated by organisms such as mealworms, superworms, and their associated microbiota, as well as other microorganisms. This process showcases the potential of these organisms to contribute to the reduction of plastic waste through natural biological pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineralization is the process by which organic substances, such as polystyrene, are converted into inorganic compounds like CO2 during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIRCULAR APPROACH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A circular approach for PLA waste management involves using PLA as feedstock for insect biomass production, as demonstrated in studies involving Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;A circular approach involves using mealworm larvae for resource recovery from PLA waste, managing feces as fertilizer, and using agricultural products for PLA production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA METHYLATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DNA methylation refers to changes in the mitochondrial genome of Tenebrio molitor when fed polyurethane (PU) foam as the sole diet, studied by Guo et al. (2019).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEAVY METALS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Heavy metals are components found in plastics that can affect the decomposition performance of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are components in plastics that can affect the biodegradation performance of mealworms, as studied by Bieganski (2021).&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals, such as lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), and arsenic (As), are environmental pollutants that were analyzed in TMlp to assess its safety for livestock feed.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are hazardous elements found in marine plastic litter, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are toxic substances released during the incineration of plastic wastes, posing risks to human health and the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are pollutants that can be metabolized by Enterobacteriales, which are present in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are environmental contaminants that can affect the physiology and genetics of insects, such as beetles of the Carabidae family.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals such as Cd, Hg, and Pb are toxic substances that were checked for in the polyurethane composition to ensure they do not harm T. molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE ELECTRICAL AND ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT (WEEE) PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">WEEE plastics, including waste rigid polyurethane (WRPU) and waste polystyrene (WPS), are plastics that Galleria mellonella larvae show a preference for degrading.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOLUENE 2,4-DIISOCYANATE (TDI)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Toluene 2,4-diisocyanate (TDI) is a chemical used in the synthesis of polyether-PU foam, contributing to its structure and properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM (SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPY)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM is a microscopy technique used to observe the surface morphology of materials, such as PS, after enzymatic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is a technique used to visualize surface alterations of plastics during biodegradation, providing indirect evidence of enzymatic activity by microorganisms and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to characterize the surface morphology of polyether-PU foam after degradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was used to characterize the surface morphological changes of polyether-PU foam after exposure to mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is used to analyze surface alterations on polystyrene films caused by microbial biofilms, supporting evidence of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR spectrometer is an analytical instrument used to obtain the infrared spectra of materials, such as polypropylene (PP) foam and frass samples, to identify chemical bonds and functional groups.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectrometer is used to analyze changes in functional groups of polyether-PU foam and frass samples to assess biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The FTIR spectrometer is used to analyze chemical changes in polystyrene (PS) films after microbial degradation, providing insights into biodegradation mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectrometer is an instrument used to analyze the chemical alterations on the surface of residual PVC fragments after mealworm digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The FTIR spectrometer is used to analyze changes in the functional groups of plastics after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The FTIR spectrometer (Nicolet iS50) is used to analyze the functional groups in frass samples, providing data on the biodegradation of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TGA ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TGA analyzer is used for thermal characterization of polyether-PU foam and frass samples to evaluate biodegradation effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G. MELLONELLA LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">G. mellonella larvae are insects that show a preference for pristine plastics over waste plastics, such as waste rigid polyurethane (WRPU) and waste polystyrene (WPS).&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae are known for their plastic-degrading abilities and are studied alongside mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE RIGID POLYURETHANE (WRPU)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste rigid polyurethane (WRPU) is a type of plastic waste that G. mellonella larvae show less preference for compared to pristine plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE POLYSTYRENE (WPS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste polystyrene (WPS) is a type of plastic waste that G. mellonella larvae show less preference for compared to pristine plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENOL-FORMALDEHYDE RESIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenol-formaldehyde resin is a type of plastic that G. mellonella larvae show a preference for, ranking higher than other plastics like polyethylene (PE) and polypropylene (PP).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor larvae plays a significant role in the biodegradation of polypropylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of insect larvae plays a role in the degradation of plastics, though it was not analyzed in the study involving G. mellonella larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polyether-PU foam, as analyzed through microbial community analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of mealworms shifts in response to polystyrene (PS) consumption, enhancing their ability to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community refers to the microorganisms residing in the digestive tracts of organisms like Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor, which play a role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of Tenebrio species undergoes significant changes when fed different polyethylene polymers, affecting biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of larvae is influenced by diet and plays a crucial role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of yellow mealworms undergoes significant changes after feeding on disposable surgical masks, with decreased bacterial diversity and increased relative abundance of Lactococcus and Morganella.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of insect larvae plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, such as polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of insect larvae plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community refers to the collection of bacteria and fungi in the digestive tract of A. diaperinus larvae, which is modulated by polystyrene feeding.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of yellow mealworms, including both bacteria and fungi, shifts in response to different plastic diets and plays a key role in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbial community of mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene by facilitating metabolic processes and redox reactions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRAHYDROFURAN (THF)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">THF is an anhydrous chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a chemical used in analytical methods for studying the biodegradation of plastics in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used for the extraction of polystyrene (PS) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is an organic solvent used to extract polyether-PU fragments from the frass of yellow mealworms for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used in the extraction process to analyze the molecular weight of Styrofoam degradation products in superworm frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used in the extraction and analysis of degraded Styrofoam products from mealworm fecula.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used to extract residual polymers from frass samples for molecular weight analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used to extract residual polystyrene from frass for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used in THF extraction to determine the mass of PS residue in frass during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran is a solvent used to dissolve polystyrene for analysis in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used to dissolve polystyrene materials for analysis in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used in the study for extracting and analyzing polystyrene polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used for extracting PS and PVC polymers in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOXHLET EXTRACTOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A Soxhlet extractor is used to extract crude fat from mealworm larvae for fatty acid analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Soxhlet extractor is a laboratory apparatus used to extract polyether-PU fragments from frass using tetrahydrofuran (THF) as the solvent.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS (TGA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TGA is a method used to characterize the thermal properties of polyurethane foam before and after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a method used to measure changes in the mass of plastic polymers during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a method used to study the thermal stability and composition of materials, including polyether-PU foam, by measuring mass changes as a function of temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a technique used to characterize the thermal properties of polyether-PU foam and frass samples to assess biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was used to characterize the thermal properties of materials, including polyether-PU foam, under nitrogen atmosphere.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a method used to study the thermal stability and decomposition of materials, such as the biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis is used to study the thermal changes in polystyrene and polyethylene during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a technique used to measure changes in the weight of a material as a function of temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was used to characterize thermal variations in frass samples from mealworms fed with polystyrene and other feedstocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is a method used to study the thermal degradation of polypropylene (PP) foam and frass samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms, specifically the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus*, are highly versatile insects with significant roles in environmental science, sustainability, and nutrition. They are widely recognized for their remarkable ability to biodegrade a variety of synthetic polymers, including polystyrene (PS or Styrofoam), polyethylene (PE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), expanded polystyrene (EPS), high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), polyether-PU foam, PU1, PU2, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyurethane (PU), and high-crystallinity polyethylene terephthalate (PET). This biodegradation process is primarily facilitated by the mealworms' gut microbiome, which plays a critical role in breaking down these synthetic materials through enzymatic and microbial activities. Their strong mandibles and symbiotic gut microbiota enable them to chew, digest, and depolymerize plastics, contributing to their oxidation and degradation.

Mealworms are particularly noted for their ability to consume and degrade polystyrene (PS) waste materials, including expanded polystyrene (EPS) and extruded polystyrene (XPS) foams. Their gut bacteria play a key role in this biodegradation process, which has been extensively studied in environmental research. Studies have shown that mealworms can chew and degrade PS, contributing to its biodegradation, especially when supplemented with soy protein or bran, which increases their survival rates and PS consumption rates. Additionally, mealworms are capable of ingesting and metabolizing microplastics, such as PS, PVC, and LDPE, altering their chemical composition during digestion. This process is evidenced by changes in the crystalline structure and chemical composition of the plastics. However, while mealworms can survive on a diet of microplastics, these materials do not provide sufficient energy for their growth and development. Their survival, growth, and metamorphosis under different feeding conditions, including microplastic consumption, are subjects of ongoing research.

In addition to their role in plastic waste management, mealworms are also studied as a sustainable protein source, underscoring their dual significance in environmental and nutritional research. They have been used in studies to investigate the effects of different diets, such as polystyrene (PS), cellulose (CS), and corn bran, and are known for their ability to thrive on a variety of organic materials. Despite their ability to biodegrade plastics, mealworms do not thrive when fed exclusively on these materials, as they require additional nutrients</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7a49f1c1e76d6e987e0aa499923d3f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab