;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial diversity analysis was conducted to study the gut microbiota of mealworms and their role in the biodegradation of polyether-PU foam.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial diversity is studied in the context of the biodegradation of polyethylene polymers by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, as it may influence the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial diversity refers to the variety of microorganisms in an environment, which can be affected by pollutants like HCH.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial diversity refers to the variety of microorganisms present in a given environment, often studied using sequencing and bioinformatics tools.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-RAY PHOTOELECTRON SPECTROSCOPY (XPS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a technique used to analyze chemical changes in degraded polystyrene films.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a technique used to analyze surface chemical components and functional groups, such as those on polystyrene films after degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a technique used to analyze surface chemical components of PS film sheets during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a technique used to analyze the elemental composition and chemical structure of materials, such as polyether-PU foam, before and after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was employed to analyze the surface chemical components of materials, including polyether-PU foam.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a technique used to verify changes in surface chemical components and functional groups during the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;XPS is used to verify changes in surface chemical components and functional groups during the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a technique used to analyze the generation of new oxygen-containing functional groups on HDPE film after degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;XPS is used to analyze the elemental distribution on the PS film's surface to verify degradation behavior.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (GPC)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is a versatile and sensitive analytical technique primarily used to determine the molecular weights (Mw) and molecular weight distribution (MWD) of polymers. As a type of size-exclusion chromatography, GPC separates molecules based on their size and is widely applied in biodegradation studies to analyze changes in the molecular weight of various polymers, including polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), polypropylene (PP), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET).

GPC is particularly valuable in studying the depolymerization and biodegradation of plastics, as it provides insights into the molecular weight distribution of both the original polymers and their degradation products. For instance, GPC has been used to analyze the molecular weight of depolymerized polymers in frass samples from mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) and superworms, as well as to characterize residual polymers in their frass (excrement). The technique has also been employed to assess the molecular weight changes in polystyrene during biodegradation by these organisms and to analyze the molecular weight distribution of microplastics and their degradation products, including Styrofoam, in the fecula of mealworms and superworms.

Additionally, GPC has been used to measure molecular weight changes in degraded polystyrene films and to characterize the molecular weight distribution of residual PET polymers after biodegradation. In summary, Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is a critical tool in polymer science, enabling researchers to study the molecular weight and distribution of polymers during biodegradation processes, particularly in the context of plastic degradation by organisms such as mealworms and superworms. Its applications extend to a wide range of polymers, making it an essential method for understanding the environmental impact and degradation pathways of synthetic materials.

While GPC is a powerful method for assessing molecular weight distribution, it was noted that in some studies, it was not the primary method due to the presence of metabolites in frass. However, GPC is suggested for future studies to understand the impact of molecular weight on polyethylene biodegradation by lesser waxworms, highlighting its potential for broader applications in biodegradation research. Overall, GPC remains a cornerstone technique for analyzing the molecular weight distribution of polymers and their degradation products, providing critical insights into the biodegradation of plastics. Specifically, GPC has been used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of residual polystyrene in frass, further underscoring its utility in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA GENE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA gene analysis was performed to study the microbial community in the gut of mealworms, focusing on the V3–V4 hypervariable region.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILLUMINA MISEQ PLATFORM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Illumina MiSeq platform is a sequencing instrument used for high-throughput sequencing of the 16S rRNA gene to analyze microbial communities in the gut of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The Illumina MiSeq platform is a sequencing technology recommended for 16S rRNA gene amplicon analysis in gut microbiome studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Illumina MiSeq platform was used for paired-end sequencing of the 16S rRNA gene to analyze the gut microbial community of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The Illumina MiSeq platform is used for high-throughput sequencing of the 16S rRNA gene to study the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The Illumina MiSeq platform is used for high-throughput sequencing of the 16S rRNA gene to analyze microbial communities in the guts of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The Illumina MiSeq platform is used for sequencing the V3-V4 region of the 16S rRNA gene in the study to analyze microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PICRUST2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PICRUSt2 was used to predict the metagenomic functions of the gut microbial community based on OTU representative sequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL RATE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival rate analysis was conducted to evaluate the survival rates of mealworms fed with polyether-PU foam compared to controls.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANNIBALISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cannibalism is a behavior observed in various insect species, particularly among lesser waxworms, mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), and related species within the *Tenebrio* genus, such as *Tenebrio obscurus*. This behavior is primarily triggered by nutritional limitations, including food shortages, protein-deficient diets, or environments lacking essential nutrients. For instance, cannibalism is prevalent when mealworms are unfed, fed only polystyrene (PS), or provided with inadequate diets, leading to reduced survival rates and highlighting the nutritional inadequacy of plastic-only diets.

Cannibalism among mealworm larvae is observed regardless of diet, potentially impacting biodegradation studies. In nutrient-deficient environments, cannibalism serves as a survival mechanism, providing access to essential substances that facilitate nitrogen assimilation by symbiotic bacteria. This behavior is not limited to specific diets, as it has been observed regardless of the mealworms' dietary conditions. Cannibalism among mealworms can also occur when larvae and adults are kept together, leading to competition and stress, which may reduce plastic consumption rates and the efficiency of plastic biodegradation by decreasing the number of active plastic-consuming insects.

Cannibalism involves larvae consuming dead larvae, molting exoskeletons, or even pupae, particularly under conditions of hunger or weakened aggression. This behavior has been observed in experiments where mealworms were fed certain types of feed, such as wheat bran and dried whey. In studies, cannibalism has been noted to affect the biodegradation process by altering digestive products and residues in frass. It is particularly prevalent in *Tenebrio molitor* larvae under specific conditions, such as the PE-reg diet, where high mortality rates and predation on pupae have been observed despite otherwise favorable development conditions.

Overall, cannibalism in mealworms and related species is a significant factor influencing survival rates, plastic consumption, and biodegradation efficiency, particularly in nutritionally limited or stressful environments. It can also affect the accuracy of biodegradation studies by reducing the mass of larvae available for analysis and altering the composition of frass. This behavior underscores the importance of adequate nutrition in maintaining insect populations and optimizing biodegradation processes in experimental settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) analysis is a widely utilized technique for studying the chemical composition and structural changes in various materials, particularly in the context of biodegradation processes. This method is extensively applied in research involving mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and lesser waxworms, focusing on their ability to degrade different types of plastics and polymers, such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polystyrene, polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, PET polymers, and polypropylene.

FTIR analysis is employed to confirm the biodegradation and oxidation of these materials by detecting changes in their chemical structure before and after biodegradation. For example, it has been used to identify the formation of new functional groups, such as carbonyl groups (C=O), and to observe ring cleavage in polystyrene after ingestion by mealworms. These changes provide evidence of biodegradation and oxidation processes. Additionally, FTIR analysis is used to study surface morphology and functional group alterations in materials like polystyrene and corn straw after treatment by *Tenebrio molitor* larvae.

In studies involving mealworms, FTIR analysis is also applied to characterize the degradation of polyurethane (PU) foam, both in terms of chemical bonds and structural modifications. The technique is used to analyze residual materials, such as frass (excrement) and mealworm fecula, to detect changes in functional groups and molecular structure, further confirming the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene and PU foam. For PET polymers, FTIR analysis indicates oxidation and increased hydrophilicity after biodegradation.

FTIR analysis is also used to study chemical modifications in residual polymers after plastic degradation by mealworms, identifying changes such as the formation of C-O bonds and the appearance of hydroxyl groups, which indicate oxidation during biodegradation. The technique reveals degradation and partial oxidation of plastics, providing insights into the mechanisms by which mealworms and their gut bacteria break down these materials.

In the context of lesser waxworms, FTIR analysis is similarly employed to study the chemical modifications in polyethylene during biodegradation. The technique has been used to confirm the biodegradation of polyethylene by detecting new carbonyl and alcoholic groups in the egested frass of lesser waxworms, further demonstrating the utility of FTIR in analyzing biodegradation processes.

Additionally, FTIR analysis is a technique used to characterize the oxidation and chemical modification of polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) in the digestive systems of mealworms, confirming biodegradation. This application highlights the versatility of</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TG/DTG CURVES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TG/DTG curves are thermogravimetric analysis results that show weight loss and decomposition patterns of materials like Styrofoam and frass.&lt;SEP&gt;TG/DTG curves are used to analyze the thermal degradation and composition of materials, such as polyether-PU foam, after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XPS SCANNING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">XPS scanning is a method used to examine the surface composition and chemical changes in materials, such as polyether-PU foam, after being consumed by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethers are chemical compounds found in polyether-PU foam, which undergo scission during biodegradation by mealworms, as indicated by FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZENE RINGS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzene rings are a structural component of polystyrene that are broken down during the biodegradation process in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzene rings are a structural component of polystyrene that are broken down during the biodegradation process in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzene rings are chemical structures in polyether-PU foam that are cracked during biodegradation by mealworms, as shown by FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL RATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival rates are a measure of the percentage of mealworms that survive under different feeding conditions, such as polyether-PU foam or bran diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rates are measured to evaluate the toxicological effects of plastic diets on superworms and yellow mealworms over a 45-day period.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rates of T. molitor larvae are studied to understand the impact of polystyrene ingestion on their physiology and longevity.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae were measured under different dietary conditions, including consumption of PS, PE-1, and bran, to assess their ability to survive on plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rates are used to assess the viability of superworms and yellow mealworms under different feeding conditions, with lower rates observed in plastic-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rates were calculated to assess the effects of plastic diets on the growth and development of larvae over a 35-day period.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rates refer to the percentage of lesser waxworms that survive under different feeding conditions, such as wax comb, polyethylene, or a combination of both.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rates of yellow mealworms are monitored to assess the impact of consuming different types of plastics as their sole diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSUMPTION RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Consumption rate measures the amount of polystyrene and corn straw consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae during the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Consumption rate refers to the amount of polyether-PU foam consumed by mealworms over time, which increases after an initial adaptation period.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS LOSS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass loss is a metric used to evaluate the effectiveness of polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass loss refers to the reduction in the weight of polyether-PU foam due to consumption and biodegradation by mealworms over a 35-day period.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass loss refers to the reduction in weight of LDPE or PE substrates after being in contact with greater wax moth larvae or mealworms, indicating potential degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass loss is a metric used to measure the efficiency of plastic degradation by mealworms, indicating how much material is consumed or decomposed.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass loss measurements were conducted to assess the impact of plastic consumption on mealworms and the degradation of plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass loss is a metric used to measure the degradation of polystyrene (PS) by Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating the effectiveness of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="URETHANE BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The urethane bond is a chemical bond in polyurethane that is susceptible to hydrolysis and degradation, as observed in the biodegradation of polyether-PU foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHER BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ether bonds are chemical linkages in polyether-PU foam that are resistant to microbial attack but may be degraded through oxidative reactions or enzymatic activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC HYDROLYSIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic hydrolysis is a process where enzymes break down chemical bonds, such as urethane and urea bonds in polyether-PU foam, during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic hydrolysis is the process by which enzymes break down polymers, such as polylactic acid (PLA), into smaller molecules during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic hydrolysis is a process where enzymes break down polymers like PET into smaller molecules, aiding in biodegradation and recycling efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic hydrolysis is a process where enzymes break down polymers like PET into smaller molecules, aiding in biodegradation and recycling efforts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE REACTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative reactions involve the cleavage of ether bonds in polyether-PU foam, contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VOLATILE COMPONENTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Volatile components are substances released during the thermal decomposition of polyether-PU foam, contributing to weight loss in the early stages of TGA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTER LINKAGES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ester linkages are chemical bonds in polyether-PU foam that decompose at higher temperatures during thermal degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID CHAINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid chains are components of polyether-PU foam that contribute to weight loss during the later stages of thermal decomposition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XPS SCANNING SPECTRA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">XPS scanning spectra are used to analyze the elemental composition and chemical structure of polyether-PU foam before and after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon content refers to the proportion of carbon in polyether-PU foam, which increases after biodegradation due to the loss of oxygen and nitrogen.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGEN CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygen content refers to the proportion of oxygen in polyether-PU foam, which decreases after biodegradation due to the formation of volatile oxidation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen content is measured in the frass of insect larvae to evaluate the effects of different diets on protein metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen content refers to the proportion of nitrogen in polyether-PU foam, which decreases after biodegradation, indicating structural changes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STREPTOCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family that becomes highly abundant in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed low molecular weight polystyrene, particularly due to the genus Lactococcus.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family whose relative abundance increases in the gut microbiome of mealworms when they consume polyether-PU foam.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria within the Firmicutes phylum, found in the gut microbiota of mealworms and involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria, including Lactococcus lactis and Lactococcus garviae, found to be more abundant in the gut of mealworms fed with PS, suggesting a role in PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Streptococcaceae family includes bacteria like Lactococcus garvieae, which showed a significant increase in population density in the EPS-fed group, suggesting a role in EPS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that shows increased abundance in the gut bacterial community of yellow mealworms when fed the middle and inner layers of surgical masks.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus and T. molitor, which may play a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that shows increased relative abundance in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae when fed aged microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a family of gram-positive bacteria that, along with Enterobacteriaceae, plays a key role in the biodegradation of microplastics in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a family of gut bacteria in mealworms that contributes to the biodegradation of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTERIACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Enterobacteriaceae** is a family of Gram-negative bacteria within the Proteobacteria phylum, specifically classified under the Gammaproteobacteria class. This bacterial family is widely recognized for its significant role in the biodegradation of various plastics, including polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyethylene (PE), polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA), expanded polystyrene (EPS), and ethylene vinyl acetate (EVA). Enterobacteriaceae is commonly found in the gut microbiomes of insect larvae, particularly mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms (*Tenebrio obscurus*), and other Tenebrionidae beetles, such as *Alphitobius diaperinus*.

The relative abundance of Enterobacteriaceae increases significantly in the gut microbiome of these insects when they consume plastic materials, such as PS, PE, PU foam, EVA, and even surgical masks, particularly the outer layer. This suggests a strong association between Enterobacteriaceae and plastic degradation. Specific genera within this family, such as **Citrobacter sp.**, **Hafnia-Obesumbacterium sp.**, **Klebsiella pneumoniae**, **Erwinia oleae**, **Cronobacter sakazakii**, and **Kosakonia**, have been identified as key contributors to the biodegradation process. For instance, Citrobacter sp. and Hafnia-Obesumbacterium sp. are linked to PET degradation, while Klebsiella pneumoniae and Erwinia oleae are associated with PS degradation. Cronobacter sakazakii has been observed to increase in population density in mealworms fed with EPS, further supporting its role in EPS degradation. Unclassified Enterobacteriaceae has also been noted as a dominant genus in the gut bacterial community of PS-fed superworms and yellow mealworms, indicating its potential involvement in plastic biodegradation.

In summary, Enterobacteriaceae is a versatile bacterial family that plays a crucial role in the depolymerization and biodegradation of various plastics, including LDPE, PS, PU, PET, HIPS, PHA, EPS, and EVA, in the gut microbiomes of mealworms, superworms, and other Tenebrionidae beetles. Its diverse genera and species, such as Citrobacter spp. and Kosakonia spp., contribute to the degradation of these synthetic materials, making</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPIROPLASMATACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Spiroplasmataceae is a bacterial family commonly found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, whose relative abundance decreases when mealworms consume polyether-PU foam.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that significantly increased in relative abundance in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasmataceae is a family of gut bacteria in mealworms that undergoes significant shifts during polystyrene consumption and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasmataceae is a family of gut-associated bacteria in T. obscurus and T. molitor that shows noticeable changes in relative abundance when the larvae are fed polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasmataceae is a family of gut-associated bacteria in the Tenebrio gut microbiome that exhibited noticeable changes in abundance when exposed to a polystyrene diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that significantly increases in PP-fed yellow mealworms, indicating its potential role in PP degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family in the gut microbiome of mealworms, whose relative abundance decreases when mealworms consume polyether-PU foam.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria within the Firmicutes phylum, present in the gut microbiota of mealworms and contributing to polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcaceae is a family of gut bacteria in mealworms that undergoes significant shifts during polystyrene consumption and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcaceae is a family of gut-associated bacteria in T. obscurus and T. molitor that changes in relative abundance when the larvae are fed polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcaceae is a family of gut bacteria in mealworms that plays a role in the biodegradation of LDPE.</data>
