    <node id="POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) is a widely used synthetic thermoplastic polymer, available in both high-density (HDPE) and low-density (LDPE) forms. Known for its durability, lightweight properties, high molecular weight, and resistance to degradation, polyethylene is extensively utilized in packaging, containers, insulation, agricultural mulches, and various other applications. As a petroleum-based plastic, it is chemically inert and highly stable, which has contributed to its widespread use but also to significant environmental pollution due to its persistence in the environment. Its stable structure and high crystallinity have traditionally made it difficult to biodegrade under natural conditions.

However, recent research has revealed that certain organisms, particularly insects and their gut microbiota, possess the ability to biodegrade polyethylene. Key organisms capable of degrading polyethylene include yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus), superworms (Zophobas atratus), greater wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella), lesser waxworms (Achroia grisella), and Indian meal moths (Plodia interpunctella). These insects, especially their larvae, have demonstrated the ability to metabolize polyethylene, leading to its depolymerization and oxidation within their digestive systems. The biodegradation process is facilitated by specific bacterial strains and gut microbiota, such as Bacillus sp. YP1, Enterobacter asburiae VT1, Enterobacter sp. D1, Ralstonia sp. strain SKM2, Bacillus sp. strain SM1, Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450, and Bacillus sp. NyZ451, which colonize the digestive tracts of these insects. Additionally, microorganisms like Rhodococcus ruber, Phanerochaete, and Streptomyces species have also been studied for their potential to biodegrade polyethylene. Marine microbes and other organisms have further been found to contribute to the degradation of polyethylene, impacting environmental pollution.

Polyethylene has been studied in various forms, including oxo-degradable (PE-oxo) and regranulated (PE-reg) forms, which affect the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae. Specifically, PE-oxo contains starch, while PE-reg contains CaO and CaCO3-based pigments. Regranulated polyethylene has been found to be particularly beneficial for the development of mealworms. However, the inclusion of polyethylene in the diet of mealworms can also affect their survival and growth, and its degradation may produce toxic byproducts. Despite</data>
