    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL FUNGI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental fungi are studied for their ability to degrade polyurethane foams and other synthetic polymers, contributing to biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental fungi, such as Aspergillus tubingensis, have been studied for their ability to degrade synthetic polymers like polyester polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">**Toxicological Assessment** is a comprehensive evaluation process designed to determine the safety and potential health effects of various substances on organisms, including humans, animals, and ecosystems. This assessment is critical for understanding the health and environmental impacts of substances, ensuring their safe use in various applications, and supporting the development of sustainable alternatives such as insect-based proteins and biodegradable materials.

In the context of **Tenebrio molitor (mealworms)**, toxicological assessments have been conducted to evaluate the effects of **microplastics**, **polyethylene**, **polystyrene**, **polystyrene foam**, **styrene**, **formaldehyde**, and other substances on their survival, growth, metamorphosis, reproduction, and overall development. These assessments involve monitoring morphological parameters, such as body weight, feed intake, and organ health, as well as conducting biochemical and genomic analyses to determine the impact of plastic consumption on mealworms. Studies have examined oxidative stress, physiological impacts, and gene expression changes caused by the degradation of polyethylene and polystyrene in mealworms, providing insights into the broader effects of plastic pollution on these organisms. Additionally, toxicological assessments have evaluated the impact of plastic exposure on insect metabolism and potential risks to their development and protein production.

A specific toxicological assessment was conducted to evaluate the effects of feeding yellow mealworms with surgical mask layers on their survival, growth, and gut microbiome. This study highlights the adaptability of mealworms to consume and degrade synthetic materials, further emphasizing their potential role in addressing plastic pollution. The study also includes a toxicological assessment of the biodegradation process, evaluating the effects of polyethylene consumption by mealworms. The viability and mass changes of mealworms are monitored as key indicators of the toxicological effects of plastic consumption, particularly since plastics lack essential nutrients like vitamins, minerals, and proteins.

Toxicological assessments have also been performed to evaluate the safety of **TMlp (Tenebrio molitor larvae protein)** as an alternative protein source, particularly in **Sprague-Dawley rats**. These studies examined clinical signs, organ weights, hematological and serum biochemical parameters, histopathological analysis of organs (such as the liver and kidneys), and cell viability. The results consistently revealed no significant toxicological or pathological abnormalities, supporting the safety of TMlp for use in livestock feed and potentially for human consumption.

Beyond mealworms, toxicological assessments evaluate the effects of substances such as **microplastics, nanoplastics, plastics, flame retardants (e.g., HBCD), polystyrene, and sty</data>
