    <node id="MICROPLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics are small plastic particles (&lt;5 mm) that result from the degradation of larger plastic items, such as polystyrene, and are a growing environmental concern.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic items, posing significant environmental and health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic items, such as polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic items, such as polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic items, such as polystyrene, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the degradation of larger plastic items, such as polystyrene and low-density polyethylene, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles that result from the breakdown of larger plastic items and can have significant environmental and biological impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles that result from the breakdown of larger plastic items and can have toxic effects on organisms like earthworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles that result from the breakdown of larger plastic items and can have various environmental impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic items, which can be further degraded by certain organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles that result from the breakdown of larger plastic items and can disrupt ecosystems by releasing toxic constituents.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the degradation of larger plastic items like polystyrene, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles that accumulate in the environment, particularly in marine ecosystems, posing risks to wildlife and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles that Mealworms are studied for their ability to biodegrade and their potential toxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic items, which are the focus of biodegradation studies by various bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the degradation of larger plastic items, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles, such as polystyrene (PS) microspheres, that can be degraded by certain bacteria and organisms under specific conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORIZON 2020">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Horizon 2020 is a European Union research and innovation program funding projects related to environmental sustainability, including plastic biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is a technique used to analyze the functional groups and chemical changes in polypropylene materials after degradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is an analytical technique used to study the chemical composition and degradation of polymers like polystyrene and polyurethane.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of materials, including plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is an analytical technique used to identify chemical bonds and functional groups in materials, including plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is a method used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polymers, including those undergoing biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polymers, such as PS microplastics and their degradation products.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography is a method used to study the degradation of PVC by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography is a technique used to determine molecular weight changes in polymers, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is a method used to measure the molecular weight changes in polypropylene materials after degradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight changes in polypropylene after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) is a method used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polymers, including plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) is a technique used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polymers, including plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OPERATIONAL TAXONOMIC UNIT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are used to classify microorganisms based on genetic similarity, often studied in the context of biodegradation and gut microbiomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPROPYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) is a synthetic thermoplastic polymer widely used in various industries, including packaging, textiles, storage containers, and medical products such as disposable surgical masks. It is characterized by its durability, high molecular weight, and iso-paraffin structure, which make it highly resistant to chemical and thermal degradation. However, this resistance also contributes significantly to environmental plastic pollution, particularly in marine ecosystems, as polypropylene is a major source of persistent plastic waste, posing challenges for waste management and environmental sustainability.

Recent studies have explored the potential for polypropylene to be biodegraded by certain organisms, including yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms (Zophobas atratus), and Zophobas atrinus larvae, as well as mesophilic bacteria. These organisms facilitate the biodegradation process through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization, where microbial activity in their gut microbiome plays a crucial role in breaking down the plastic. This discovery has significant implications for plastic waste management, offering a potential biological solution to mitigate the environmental impact of polypropylene accumulation.

However, there are contradictions in the findings, as some studies suggest that polypropylene is impervious to degradation by these insects, highlighting the need for further research to fully understand the biodegradation potential of this material. Despite these uncertainties, the ability of certain insect larvae and bacteria to degrade polypropylene remains a promising avenue for addressing plastic pollution.

In addition to its industrial applications, polypropylene has been identified as a type of plastic found in animal-based traditional medicinal materials as microplastics (MPs), further emphasizing its pervasive presence in the environment.

In summary, polypropylene is a highly persistent and widely used plastic material that contributes to environmental pollution. While its resistance to degradation poses significant challenges, the potential for biodegradation by insect larvae and bacteria offers hope for innovative waste management solutions, though further research is needed to resolve existing contradictions and optimize this process.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a versatile thermoplastic polymer used in packaging, textiles, and automotive components, known for its resistance to chemicals and heat.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by organisms such as Zophobas atratus (superworms) through gut-microbe-dependent processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a widely used synthetic plastic polymer known for its durability, rigidity, and resistance to environmental degradation. It is a non-hydrolysable plastic that has been extensively studied due to its significant contribution to environmental pollution, primarily because of its resistance to biodegradation. However, recent research has identified certain organisms and mechanisms capable of degrading PVC, offering potential solutions for waste management. Notably, yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) larvae have been reported to degrade PVC, although the conversion rate is lower compared to other plastics, and the exact mechanism remains not fully understood. Additionally, bacteria isolated from the gut of insect larvae, including Tenebrio molitor, have shown the ability to degrade PVC, further contributing to efforts in reducing plastic waste. Despite these advancements, PVC remains a major environmental pollutant, and its presence has even been detected as microplastics (MPs) in animal-based traditional medicinal materials. The polymer has a number average molecular weight of 68.4 ± 5.1 kDa, as measured in studies assessing its degradation. While PVC's resistance to biodegradation poses significant environmental challenges, ongoing research into its degradation by organisms like Tenebrio molitor and bacterial consortia offers hope for mitigating its impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a widely used plastic that is studied for its biodegradation by yellow mealworm larvae and its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a widely used thermoplastic polymer in construction, pipes, and packaging, known for its durability but difficult to recycle.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMERASE CHAIN REACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is a molecular biology technique used to amplify DNA, often applied in studies of microbial communities involved in biodegradation.</data>
