    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, rigid foam plastic used in packaging and insulation, contributing to environmental pollution due to its slow degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, shock-absorbing, and insulating material widely used in food packaging, aquaculture buoys, and other applications, contributing to microplastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS), commonly known as Styrofoam, is a lightweight foam plastic material widely used in construction, insulation, and packaging due to its durability and insulating properties. However, EPS is also a significant environmental pollutant because of its resistance to degradation and slow decomposition, leading to long-term environmental hazards. Despite its persistence in the environment, EPS can be biodegraded by certain organisms, notably the larvae of Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) and Achatina fulica (giant African land snails). These organisms, particularly Tenebrio molitor, have been shown to consume and degrade EPS, with their intestinal microbes playing a key role in the biodegradation process. This ability makes EPS a potential target for environmental remediation efforts aimed at reducing plastic waste. While EPS remains a major contributor to environmental pollution, the discovery of its biodegradability by specific organisms offers a promising solution for mitigating its impact on ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a foam form of polystyrene that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, as demonstrated in environmental studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a common plastic waste material that can be biodegraded by mealworm larvae, contributing to environmental pollution reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a common plastic waste material that can be biodegraded by mealworm larvae, contributing to environmental pollution reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a plastic material used in insulation, which can contribute to environmental pollution due to its persistence and potential for degradation into microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a foam plastic that can be biodegraded by mealworms and superworms, breaking it down into smaller molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic foam that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by mealworm larvae under different feeding strategies, as evaluated by metabolic profiling.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a plastic material that can be biodegraded and disintegrated by organisms such as Achatina fulica.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Tenebrio obscurus**, commonly known as the dark mealworm or mini mealworm, is a species of darkling beetle larvae with a worldwide distribution. This species has garnered significant attention for its remarkable ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), polyethylene (PE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polypropylene (PP). The degradation process involves depolymerization, which breaks down plastic polymers into smaller, more manageable components.

Research indicates that *Tenebrio obscurus* can degrade plastics both independently of gut microbes and in conjunction with them, showcasing its versatility in plastic biodegradation. Studies have compared its effectiveness to bran-fed diets, demonstrating its ability to degrade polystyrene under various conditions. Additionally, *Tenebrio obscurus* exhibits distinct physiological responses, such as oxidative stress and changes in gut microbiome diversity, when exposed to polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs). These adaptations highlight its potential to adapt to plastic-based diets.

The species is not only a subject of scientific research but is also commercially reared for animal feed, further emphasizing its ecological and economic importance. *Tenebrio obscurus* has a higher cysteine content compared to its relative, *Tenebrio molitor*, which may contribute to its superior plastic degradation capabilities. Its ability to biodegrade expanded polystyrene (EPS) with the help of gut bacteria has been particularly noted, reinforcing its role as a valuable organism in addressing plastic pollution. Additionally, *Tenebrio obscurus* is considered a potential alternative protein source for livestock feed, similar to *Tenebrio molitor*.

In summary, *Tenebrio obscurus* is a versatile and ecologically significant species with superior polystyrene biodegradation capabilities compared to *Tenebrio molitor*. Its ability to degrade a wide range of plastics, coupled with its potential as an alternative protein source, makes it a valuable organism in both environmental and commercial contexts. The species' capability to biodegrade plastics, particularly polyethylene (PE), further underscores its importance in the global effort to combat plastic pollution.

*Tenebrio obscurus* is particularly noted for its ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and polystyrene (PS), with some studies indicating that it can perform this degradation independently of gut microbes, though the process is enhanced by them. While some research suggests that *Tenebrio</data>
