    <node id="RHODOCOCCUS RUBER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Rhodococcus ruber** is a bacterial species belonging to the actinomycete group, widely recognized for its ability to degrade various types of plastics, particularly polyethylene (PE). This bacterium has garnered significant attention in environmental microbiology and waste management studies due to its potential role in addressing plastic pollution. Extensive research has demonstrated that Rhodococcus ruber can colonize and form biofilms on polyethylene surfaces, contributing to their breakdown. This capability has positioned the bacterium as a promising candidate for microbial plastic degradation, especially in the context of polyethylene waste management.

In addition to polyethylene, Rhodococcus ruber has been investigated for its potential to degrade polystyrene (PS). Some studies suggest that the bacterium can colonize polystyrene film surfaces, form biofilms, and partially biodegrade the material, as evidenced by biofilm production and weight reduction of PS flakes. However, the evidence regarding its effectiveness in degrading polystyrene remains inconclusive. While some reports claim that Rhodococcus ruber can contribute to polystyrene degradation, others emphasize that conclusive evidence is still lacking, and its role in this process remains controversial. Earlier studies have claimed its ability to degrade polystyrene, but no definitive proof has been provided.

Rhodococcus ruber is also known for its ability to colonize and degrade plastic surfaces, including polystyrene, and has been isolated from soil, further emphasizing its potential in plastic waste management. Overall, Rhodococcus ruber is recognized as a significant bacterium in the field of plastic biodegradation, particularly for its well-documented ability to degrade polyethylene. Its potential to degrade polystyrene, while still under investigation, continues to be a subject of research. The bacterium's ability to degrade hydrocarbons and synthetic plastics underscores its importance in environmental microbiology and highlights its potential applications in tackling plastic pollution.

In summary, Rhodococcus ruber is a bacterial strain capable of colonizing, forming biofilms, and biodegrading polyethylene, with its role in polystyrene degradation still under investigation. While some evidence supports its ability to degrade polystyrene, further research is needed to confirm its effectiveness in this regard. The bacterium's isolation from soil and its ability to degrade plastic surfaces further highlight its potential in addressing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber is a bacterial species known for its potential to degrade plastics, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE FOAMS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane foams are materials derived from chemical reactions involving polyols and isocyanates, often used in insulation and cushioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foams are materials studied for their ignition, combustion, toxicity, and fire retardancy properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foams are plastic materials whose biodegradability and ecotoxicity have been studied, with some microorganisms capable of degrading them.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foams are synthetic materials that can be biodegraded by microorganisms like Pseudomonas sp., contributing to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foams are widely used materials that contribute to environmental pollution due to their persistence and challenges in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foams are synthetic polymers used in various applications, including insulation and cushioning, and are studied for their biodegradability in different environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter bacterium is a microorganism isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum, capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter bacterium is a newly isolated strain from Tribolium castaneum larvae, capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter bacterium is a strain isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum, capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles, typically ranging in size from 100 nanometers to 5 millimeters, that result from the fragmentation or breakdown of larger plastic items. These particles are derived from various polymers, including polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polystyrene (PS), polylactic acid (PLA), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and expanded polystyrene (EPS). Microplastics are considered emerging environmental contaminants due to their resistance to biodegradation and their widespread presence in marine, terrestrial, and freshwater ecosystems. They pose significant ecological and health risks, as they persist in the environment and can accumulate in organisms, leading to pollution in soil, water, and air.

Microplastics can be generated through various processes, including the degradation of larger plastic items by macro-organisms such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), wax moth larvae, and the greater wax moth. Certain organisms, including yellow mealworms and superworms (Zophobas atratus), have demonstrated the ability to degrade microplastics, including PE, PS, PVC, and LDPE. However, after biodegradation, undigested microplastics may remain in the frass (feces) of mealworm larvae, indicating partial depolymerization of plastics like polystyrene (PS). Despite this biodegradation capability, microplastics can cause physiological stress, intestinal damage, and impaired nutrient absorption in organisms like Tenebrio molitor, affecting their growth and metabolism.

The ingestion of microplastics by various organisms, including koi carp and mealworm larvae, highlights the widespread nature of this pollution and its potential risks to the food chain. Microplastics can activate oxidative and inflammatory adverse outcome pathways, posing environmental and health risks. Their presence in sewage sludge, insect feces, and other environments underscores their persistence and the challenges they pose to ecosystems and human health. Additionally, microplastics can interact with environmental pollutants and biological entities, such as SARS-CoV-2 RNA fragments, further complicating their impact on public health.

Microplastics are emerging contaminants in terrestrial environments, posing major challenges due to their persistence and environmental impact. They can contaminate land, groundwater, and waterways, contributing to environmental pollution, particularly in marine ecosystems. They are also found in seafood, posing direct risks to human health through the food chain. Furthermore, microplastics can be incorporated into the burrows of earthworms, impacting terrestrial ecosystems. Overall, microplastics are a</data>
