    <node id="TRIBOLIUM CASTANEUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Tribolium castaneum**, commonly known as the red flour beetle, is a species of darkling beetle that has garnered significant scientific interest due to its potential role in plastic biodegradation. This insect, often considered a pest in stored grain products, has been studied extensively for its ability to interact with and degrade various types of plastics, particularly polystyrene (PS) and expanded polystyrene (Styrofoam). Research has shown that the larvae of *Tribolium castaneum*, as well as its gut microbiota, play a crucial role in this process. Specifically, a polystyrene-degrading bacterium, identified as *Acinetobacter* (including the strain *Acinetobacter* sp. AnTc-1), has been isolated from the organism, highlighting the symbiotic relationship between the beetle and its gut bacteria in breaking down plastic materials.

In addition to polystyrene, *Tribolium castaneum* has been hypothesized to possess mechanisms for biodegrading polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and polyethylene (PE), similar to those observed in *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm). This suggests that the red flour beetle may have broader applications in addressing plastic waste. Furthermore, its interaction with plastic packaging has been a focus in pest management studies, as the beetle's ability to chew and digest plastic poses challenges for food storage and safety.

Overall, *Tribolium castaneum* represents a promising model organism for understanding plastic biodegradation mechanisms and developing sustainable solutions for plastic waste management. Its unique capabilities, combined with the role of its gut microbiota, make it a valuable subject for ongoing and future research in environmental biotechnology. The beetle's larvae, in particular, host *Acinetobacter* bacteria, which are key to its plastic-degrading abilities, further emphasizing its potential in tackling global plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOTECHNOLOGICAL PLASTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biotechnological plastic degradation refers to the use of biological processes, such as microbial or enzymatic activity, to break down plastic materials into simpler compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Biotechnological plastic degradation refers to the use of biological processes, such as microbial or enzymatic activity, to break down plastics into less harmful substances.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE MONOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene monomers are components of polystyrene (PS) that are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike the polymerized form of PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are components of polystyrene that are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike the polymer itself, which is more resistant.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are the building blocks of polystyrene and are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike the polymerized form of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are the fundamental building blocks of polystyrene (PS), a widely used synthetic polymer. Unlike their polymerized form, polystyrene, styrene monomers are susceptible to biodegradation under certain conditions. This biodegradation process can be facilitated by specific microorganisms, as well as by organisms such as mealworms and their gut microbiota. The susceptibility of styrene monomers to biodegradation contrasts with the resistance of polystyrene, which does not break down as easily in the environment. In some cases, styrene monomers may also form as small molecules during the degradation of styrene and can accumulate if biodegradation is incomplete. Overall, styrene monomers play a critical role in the structure of polystyrene but exhibit distinct environmental behavior due to their biodegradability, unlike the more persistent polymerized form.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are the basic units of polystyrene (PS) and are produced during the enzymatic degradation of PS by insects like mealworms and waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are the building blocks of polystyrene and are susceptible to biodegradation by certain microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are the building blocks of polystyrene and are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike the polymer itself.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are the building blocks of polystyrene and are susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are the building blocks of polystyrene and are susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are the building blocks of polystyrene, susceptible to biodegradation but often released as pollutants during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a widely used synthetic thermoplastic polymer derived from petroleum, characterized by its significant branching structure. This structure contributes to its flexibility, chemical resistance, and widespread use in various applications, including packaging, household films, plastic bags, agricultural films, and multiple industries. Despite its versatility, LDPE is known for its resistance to natural degradation, which has led to significant environmental pollution and the accumulation of plastic waste. This persistence in the environment has made LDPE a major focus of biodegradation studies aimed at mitigating its environmental impact.

Research has demonstrated that LDPE can be partially biodegraded by certain organisms, including insect larvae, microbial strains, and bacteria. Among the most studied organisms are mealworms (Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus), superworms (Zophobas atratus), rice moth larvae (Corcyra cephalonica), and the greater waxmoth (Galleria mellonella). These organisms have shown the ability to ingest and break down LDPE, although the efficiency of degradation varies. For instance, mealworms have been found to biodegrade LDPE more efficiently compared to high-density polyethylene (HDPE). Additionally, bacteria extracted from earthworm guts, certain fungi, and marine microbes have also been investigated for their potential to degrade LDPE, offering potential for soil restoration and marine ecosystem recovery.

The biodegradation process by these organisms often involves gut-microbe-dependent mechanisms, which can lead to the fragmentation of LDPE into microplastics. While this fragmentation poses its own environmental challenges, the overall process represents a promising avenue for reducing plastic waste. The ongoing research into LDPE depolymerization and biodegradation underscores its environmental significance and highlights the need for sustainable solutions to address its widespread use and persistence in ecosystems.

In summary, LDPE is a versatile and widely used plastic with significant environmental challenges due to its resistance to natural degradation. However, studies have shown that certain organisms, particularly insect larvae like mealworms and superworms, can biodegrade LDPE, albeit with varying degrees of efficiency. This biodegradation process, while potentially leading to the formation of microplastics, offers a promising approach to reducing plastic waste and addressing the environmental impact of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROWAVE IRRADIATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microwave irradiation is a method used in the chemical recycling of plastics, such as poly(bisphenol-A carbonate), to facilitate their breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTICS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04</data>
      <data key="d1">Achatina fulica can ingest and degrade various types of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastics are widely produced and used, often ending up in landfills and oceans, causing significant environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastics, including polyethylene, are major environmental pollutants that are being studied for biodegradation by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastics are synthetic materials that pose significant environmental challenges due to their persistence and accumulation in ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastics are synthetic materials that are widely used but pose significant environmental challenges due to their persistence and accumulation in ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastics, including LDPE, PS, and PU, are environmental pollutants that can be degraded by gut bacteria and fungi in yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a widely used synthetic polymer characterized by its linear-chain structure with significant long and short chain branching, resulting in low crystallinity. This structural feature makes LDPE more susceptible to biodegradation compared to other polyethylene types such as Linear Low-Density Polyethylene (LLDPE) and High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE). LDPE is commonly utilized in a variety of applications, including packaging, toys, fuel storage tanks, plastic bottles, carry bags, cling films, and shopping bags. Despite its versatility, LDPE is a major contributor to plastic pollution, prompting extensive research into its biodegradation.

LDPE is a non-hydrolysable polymer with a longer half-life compared to other plastics like PET, making it more resistant to biodegradation. However, studies have shown that LDPE can be degraded by certain bacterial strains, including *Enterobacter* sp., *Acinetobacter* sp., *Priestia megaterium* S1, and *Achromobacter xylosoxidans*, as well as by gut bacteria found in insects such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and *Galleria mellonella* larvae. These bacterial strains and insect gut microbiota play a crucial role in breaking down LDPE, leading to weight loss, structural changes, and alterations in molecular weight.

Mealworms, particularly *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, have been extensively studied for their ability to ingest and metabolize LDPE, contributing to its biodegradation and mineralization. These larvae can consume and depolymerize LDPE, although their consumption rates are lower compared to other plastics like high-impact polystyrene (HIPS) and expanded polystyrene (EPS). The degradation process involves shifts in the intestinal flora of the mealworms, which aid in breaking down the plastic. However, LDPE contributes minimally to the growth of mealworms, and the biodegradation process is often incomplete, resulting in the generation of microplastics. Research has demonstrated that the degradation of LDPE by mealworms and their gut microbiota leads to observable changes in the plastic's surface structure and molecular composition. Notably, the depolymerization pattern of LDPE by *Tenebrio molitor* larvae changes under antibiotic suppression, indicating the critical role of gut microbiota in the degradation process.

In addition to mealworms, other insects like *Galleria mellonella* and *Zophobas atratus* have also been studied for their potential to biode</data>
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT">
      <data key="d2">chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental pollutants are substances that cause harm to the environment, primarily through contamination and persistence in ecosystems. These pollutants encompass a wide range of synthetic materials, with plastics being a significant contributor. Key plastic pollutants include polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyurethane (PU1, PU2), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polypropylene (PP). These materials are major contributors to plastic pollution due to their non-biodegradable nature, slow degradation rates, and widespread use in packaging, textiles, and other applications.

Polystyrene is particularly highlighted as a major environmental pollutant due to its persistence and extensive use in packaging, leading to its accumulation in soils, rivers, lakes, and oceans, and contributing to microplastic pollution. Polyethylene and its variants (LDPE, HDPE) are also noted for their significant environmental impact, as they persist in the environment and resist natural degradation processes. PVC is identified as a persistent pollutant because of its resistance to biodegradation, while PET is recognized for its slow degradation rate and widespread use in packaging and textiles. Additionally, substances like hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD) and microplastics are mentioned as environmental pollutants that pose risks to ecosystems and human health due to their persistence and potential toxicity.

Efforts to address these pollutants include biodegradation studies targeting materials like PS, PE, and PVC, with microorganisms such as Achromobacter and Pseudomonas species being explored for their potential to degrade these materials. Innovative solutions, such as the use of mealworms, are also being investigated for their ability to degrade plastics like polystyrene, polypropylene, and polyethylene. Overall, environmental pollutants, particularly synthetic plastics, are a major concern due to their persistence, accumulation in natural environments, and detrimental effects on ecosystems and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental pollutants are substances that contaminate the environment, including plastics, chemicals, and other waste materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental pollutants include substances like microplastics and nanoplastics that negatively impact ecosystems and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene and other polymeric materials are significant environmental pollutants, particularly in marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*) are highly versatile organisms that have garnered significant attention in scientific research due to their dual potential in addressing environmental and nutritional challenges. These larvae are particularly renowned for their ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including expanded polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyurethane (PU), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). This biodegradation process is facilitated by their gut microbiome, which plays a crucial role in breaking down these microplastics. During this process, mealworm larvae exhibit physiological responses such as oxidative stress and lipid peroxidation, which are indicators of the stress induced by plastic consumption.

In addition to their role in plastic waste management, mealworm larvae are extensively researched for their potential as an alternative protein source, making them valuable in both environmental and nutritional studies. They are used in experiments to assess their growth on different substrates, including plastics, and to evaluate their performance in waste degradation. Furthermore, mealworm larvae serve as bioindicators to monitor environmental contamination and the impact of various pollutants.

Mealworm larvae are also studied for their ability to adapt to different diets, including bran, potatoes, whey, and polystyrene foam, with their fatty acid content varying depending on the feed. This adaptability makes them a key subject in studies aimed at understanding the effects of dietary changes on their metabolism and mineral content. Additionally, research focuses on the changes in their gut microbiome under different feeding strategies, particularly when consuming polystyrene.

Overall, mealworm larvae are significant in scientific research due to their ability to degrade plastics, their physiological responses to plastic consumption, and their potential as a sustainable food source. Their versatility and adaptability make them a valuable organism in studies aimed at understanding biodegradation mechanisms, environmental health, and sustainable nutrition.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae are capable of biodegrading expanded polystyrene, with their cellular lipids and proteins undergoing alterations during the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae are used in studies to degrade polystyrene and other plastics, demonstrating their potential in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE STRESS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative stress is a physiological and toxicological condition characterized by an imbalance between the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and the body's ability to detoxify these reactive molecules through antioxidant defenses. This condition is frequently induced by exposure to microplastics and nanoplastics, as well as by the biodegradation of plastics such as polyethylene (PE), oxo-degradable polyethylene, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polystyrene. The ingestion and biodegradation of these materials can lead to increased ROS levels, lipid peroxidation, and alterations in antioxidant enzyme activities, which are key indicators of oxidative stress.

In various organisms, including mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor), Crassostrea gigas, Eisenia fetida, Daphnia magna Straus, and Black Soldier Fly larvae, oxidative stress has been observed as a response to microplastic exposure. For example, in Tenebrio molitor larvae, oxidative stress is induced during the biodegradation of PE, PET, and polystyrene microplastics, leading to physiological changes such as increased ROS production, lipid peroxidation, and altered metabolic pathways. The upregulation of lipid metabolism-related genes in Tenebrio molitor larvae further highlights the metabolic disruptions caused by PET microplastic ingestion. Similarly, in Crassostrea gigas, oxidative stress results in proteomic and physiological changes, while in Eisenia fetida, it is studied in conjunction with exposure to chemicals like atrazine. In Daphnia magna Straus, oxidative stress is linked to cellular damage caused by toxic plastics such as PVC. In Black Soldier Fly larvae, oxidative stress leads to upregulated stress response pathways and perturbed nucleotide metabolism.

Oxidative stress is also evaluated through measurements of biomarkers such as ROS, malondialdehyde (MDA), and antioxidant enzyme activities, which help assess the health impact of plastic consumption on gut cells and overall cellular integrity. For instance, in insects fed with polystyrene (Styrofoam), oxidative stress parameters are measured to determine potential cellular damage or stress in gut cells. Furthermore, oxidative stress can disrupt lipid homeostasis and metabolic pathways, as observed in Tenebrio molitor larvae exposed to microplastics. Specifically, oxidative stress is a biochemical response investigated after continuous feeding with PE and ultrahigh-MW polystyrene polymers, which damage antioxidant systems and membrane integrity in mealworms.

Overall, oxidative stress serves as a critical biological response to microplastic exposure, reflecting the potential for cellular damage, physiological alterations, and toxicological effects across a range of</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Residual microplastics are undigested plastic particles found in the frass (excrement) of mealworm larvae after biodegradation of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental pollutants, such as polyethylene microplastics, pose significant threats to ecosystems and human health due to their persistence and bioaccumulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, produced during oxidative stress in mealworms fed with microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, which increase during oxidative stress in mealworm larvae during PE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, which increase in mealworm larvae exposed to polyethylene microplastics, contributing to oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, which can trigger the oxidative degradation of polystyrene in the gut of superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species are chemical compounds that play a role in the toxic effects of imidazolium-based ionic liquids on Caenorhabditis elegans.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIOXIDANT ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Antioxidant enzymes are compounds in insects that help mitigate oxidative stress and are studied for their health benefits.&lt;SEP&gt;Antioxidant enzymes are proteins that help mitigate oxidative stress by neutralizing reactive oxygen species (ROS) in organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Antioxidant enzymes are proteins that mitigate oxidative stress by neutralizing reactive oxygen species, and their activity increases in mealworm larvae exposed to polyethylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Antioxidant enzymes, including SOD, GST, and CAD, are produced by mealworm larvae to counteract oxidative stress during PE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antioxidant enzymes are proteins that counteract oxidative stress by neutralizing reactive oxygen species (ROS) in organisms exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Antioxidant enzymes, such as SOD, GST, and CAT, protect organisms from oxidative damage by neutralizing reactive oxygen species (ROS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID PEROXIDATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid peroxidation is a biochemical indicator used to evaluate oxidative stress in macroinvertebrates during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a process in which free radicals attack lipids in cell membranes, leading to cell damage, and is increased in response to microplastic exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a process induced by oxidative stress, leading to cellular damage in mealworm larvae during PE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a process where free radicals attack lipids in cell membranes, leading to cell damage, often induced by oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a process where free radicals attack lipids in cell membranes, leading to oxidative damage, as observed in mealworm larvae during PE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a process where reactive oxygen species degrade lipids, leading to cell damage, and is observed in mealworm larvae exposed to polyethylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a toxicological effect observed in mealworm larvae during the biodegradation of PE, indicating cellular damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a process where free radicals attack lipids in cell membranes, leading to oxidative damage, often measured by malondialdehyde (MDA) levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a process in which free radicals attack lipids, leading to cell damage, observed in mealworms fed with microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation is a process where free radicals attack biomacromolecules like membrane lipids, leading to oxidative damage, as observed in mealworms fed with microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste is a major global environmental pollutant resulting from the accumulation of non-biodegradable or slowly degrading synthetic plastic materials in various ecosystems, particularly in marine and terrestrial environments. It encompasses a wide range of synthetic polymers, including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU1, PU2), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP), and other types of plastics, as well as foam plastics. These materials contribute significantly to global pollution, posing substantial ecological challenges that threaten biodiversity, wildlife, and human health due to their persistence, toxicity, and resistance to natural degradation. Plastic waste is often classified as hazardous because of its long-term environmental impact and potential to release harmful substances.

The issue of plastic waste has been exacerbated by factors such as the COVID-19 pandemic, which increased the production and disposal of single-use plastics. Accumulation in oceans and landfills has led to the proliferation of microplastics and nanoplastics, further intensifying environmental pollution. Plastic waste enters ecosystems, particularly oceans, as highlighted by Jambeck et al. (2015), causing significant ecological harm and pollution. It persists in the environment for hundreds of years, contributing to the degradation of ecosystems and posing ongoing challenges for mitigation efforts.

Research has identified potential solutions to mitigate the impact of plastic waste, including biodegradation by organisms like mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms (Zophobas atratus), and waxworms. These organisms have demonstrated the ability to degrade certain types of plastic waste, such as polystyrene, polyurethane, polyethylene, and polypropylene, offering a natural approach to reducing plastic pollution. Additionally, studies are exploring the use of bacteria and other biological agents for the biodegradation and valorization of plastic waste.

Plastic waste is a significant concern in developing countries, where it affects marine and terrestrial ecosystems, and is a major focus of biodegradation studies aimed at mitigating its environmental impact. The accumulation of non-biodegradable plastics in the environment contributes to pollution and poses significant challenges for waste management and ecosystem health. Efforts to address plastic waste include improving waste management practices and leveraging biological solutions for its degradation.

Overall, plastic waste remains a pressing environmental issue, with ongoing studies focusing on both understanding its ecological hazards and developing sustainable methods for its degradation and management. Despite the challenges, advancements in biodegradation research provide hope for reducing the environmental impact of plastic waste in the future.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7a49f1c1e76d6e987e0aa499923d3f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Physiological responses are the biological reactions of mealworm larvae to polyethylene microplastics, including weight loss, biomass reduction, and oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Physiological responses are the changes in growth, hydration, and nutrient levels in plastic-degrading larvae due to exposure to different types of plastics and their degradability.&lt;SEP&gt;Physiological responses in Tenebrio molitor larvae include weight loss, decreased survival rates, biomass depletion, and oxidative damage caused by the ingestion and biodegradation of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT REDUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight reduction is a key indicator of plastic biodegradation, observed in the breakdown of polyethylene by bacterial strains like PELW2042.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight reduction refers to the decrease in the size of polymer molecules during biodegradation, as observed in polyethylene degraded by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight reduction is observed in the egested polymer residues of mealworms, indicating degradation of plastics such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight reduction is a key indicator of polystyrene degradation, observed after incubation with bacterial strains like Klebsiella sp. WJ2020 and Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight reduction is a key indicator of the biodegradation process, measured by changes in molecular weights (Mm, Mw, Ms) of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight reduction refers to the decrease in the molecular weight of polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) after ingestion by mealworms, indicating significant depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight reduction refers to the decrease in the molecular weight of polymers, such as polyethylene (PE), during the depolymerization process by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL DECOMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal decomposition is a method proposed for breaking down plastics, though it generates harmful byproducts like dioxins and aromatic compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal decomposition is a process observed during the breakdown of polystyrene by mealworms, where the material is heated and decomposed into smaller components.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal decomposition is the breakdown of materials at elevated temperatures, used as a signal to demonstrate the biodegradation of polyethylene by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal decomposition refers to the breakdown of polystyrene and frass components at high temperatures, as analyzed by TGA, showing multiple decomposition stages and reduced PS content in frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal decomposition is a process used to analyze the mass loss ratio of frass from larvae, indicating the extent of polystyrene (PS) biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanoplastics (NPs) are even smaller plastic particles than microplastics, posing potential risks to environmental and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles, typically less than 1 μm in size, with some sources specifying sizes less than 100 nm. These particles result from the fragmentation or breakdown of larger plastic materials, such as microplastics or plastic debris, and are considered emerging environmental pollutants. Nanoplastics are a growing concern in both aquatic and terrestrial environments due to their high mobility, persistence, and potential to induce toxic effects in organisms. They can cause physiological stress, oxidative stress, genotoxicity, intestinal damage, and mitochondrial damage in various organisms, including freshwater benthic clams, earthworms, and human liver and lung cells. Additionally, nanoplastics are associated with behavioral disorders in terrestrial organisms and can undergo trophic transfer via vascular plants, further amplifying their ecological impact.

Interestingly, studies have shown that nanoplastics do not accumulate in the frass of mealworm larvae after the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE). They are rapidly eliminated in the gut of these larvae, and their absorption by intestinal cells may be potentially inhibited by corona proteins. Despite their small size, nanoplastics pose significant biological and environmental risks, making them a critical area of research for understanding their long-term effects on ecosystems and human health. Their ability to disrupt microbial signaling and accumulate in ecosystems further underscores the need for continued investigation into their environmental and toxicological impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are extremely small plastic particles that pose environmental risks due to their ability to penetrate biological systems and accumulate in organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD SECURITY">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Food Security refers to the availability and access to safe and nutritious food, including studies on the prevention of food losses caused by insects using low-permeability plastic bags.&lt;SEP&gt;Food security refers to the availability and access to safe and nutritious food, including alternative protein sources like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Food security refers to the availability and accessibility of safe and nutritious food, with edible insects being explored as a sustainable solution to global food challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Food security refers to the availability and accessibility of safe food, which is threatened by the accumulation of plastic waste in ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMAN HEALTH">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Human health can be impacted by microplastics and their additives, which are studied for their potential toxicological effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Human health is a key focus in studies examining the potential effects of microplastics and their additives, which may have toxicological implications.&lt;SEP&gt;Human health is endangered by the bioaccumulation of microplastics and their associated pollutants, which can disrupt food webs and release toxic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTICS (MNPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics (MNPs) are small plastic particles that can be generated during the biofragmentation of ingested plastics by organisms like mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTARCTIC KRILL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Antarctic krill (Euphausia superba) are marine organisms that can digestively triturate polyethylene microplastics into nanoplastics, increasing their capacity to cross biological barriers.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill are macroinvertebrates studied for their physiological stress responses to micro(nano)plastics, though they lack plastic-degrading abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill are marine organisms that can fragment ingested plastics into microplastics, similar to mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill are marine organisms that can fragment microplastics into nanoplastics through digestive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill are marine organisms with poor plastic-degrading abilities, often studied for their role in biofragmentation of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill (Euphausia superba) are organisms that fragment polyethylene (PE) microplastics (MPs) into smaller particles, including nanoplastics (NPs), in their digestive gland.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill are macroinvertebrates that have been studied for their role in the generation and accumulation of nanoplastics (NPs) through biofragmentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill are marine organisms that can biofragment ingested microplastics into nanoplastics, although they have poor plastic-degrading abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill can turn microplastics into nanoplastics through digestive fragmentation, impacting marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EARTHWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Earthworms (Eisenia andrei) are organisms that have been exposed to polyethylene (PE) microplastics (MPs) and are studied for their role in plastic fragmentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms (Eisenia fetida) are used in studies to assess the effects of low-density polyethylene microplastics in soils.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are invertebrates that can fragment ingested plastics into microplastics, contributing to plastic degradation in soil ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are soil-dwelling organisms that experience higher mortality and weight reduction when exposed to high doses of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are macroinvertebrates capable of biodegrading degradable polymers, but they are not known for degrading petroleum-based plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are macroinvertebrates with limited plastic-degrading abilities, often used in studies comparing their biodegradation capabilities to other organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms have gut bacteria capable of degrading low-density polyethylene, offering potential for soil restoration.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are invertebrates that have been observed to ingest and retain microplastics in terrestrial environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are soil-dwelling organisms that can biofragment ingested microplastics into nanoplastics, but they have limited plastic-degrading abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms, such as Eisenia fetida, play a role in the biodegradation of bioplastics like polylactic acid, contributing to their breakdown in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms (Eisenia fetida) are organisms studied for their ability to degrade polylactic acid bioplastics, showing slow excretion rates after ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms (Eisenia fetida) are used in studies to assess the toxicity of microplastics and their interactions with soil particles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC BIOFRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic biofragmentation is the process by which ingested plastics are broken down into smaller particles by organisms like mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DARKLING BEETLE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Darkling beetles (Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae) are a family of insects that include species like Tenebrio molitor, which are capable of biodegrading plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The darkling beetle (Tenebrionidae) family includes superworms (Zophobas morio) and mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), which are voracious agricultural insect pests and protein-rich food sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles are a family of beetles that include species like Tenebrio molitor, which are capable of biodegrading plastics such as expanded polystyrene (EPS).&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles are a family of beetles that include species like Tenebrio molitor, which are capable of biodegrading plastics such as expanded polystyrene (EPS).&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles are a family of beetles (Tenebrionidae) that include species like Tenebrio obscurus, known for their adaptability and ecological significance.&lt;SEP&gt;The darkling beetle is an insect that can accelerate its development under improved rearing conditions, but it may resort to cannibalism when food is scarce.&lt;SEP&gt;The darkling beetle is the adult form of Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, and is studied for its life cycle and nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles, belonging to the family Tenebrionidae, include species like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, which are known for their ability to biodegrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7b499dc783c21f764eb35989a449f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEST MOTH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pest moths (Lepidoptera: Pyralidae) are insects that can masticate and fragment plastics, contributing to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE foam is a type of low-density polyethylene foam tested for biodegradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE foam is a type of low-density polyethylene plastic that can be biodegraded by insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, through depolymerization and mineralization.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam is a type of plastic that mealworm larvae can digest and biodegrade efficiently.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam is another type of plastic waste tested for biodegradation, showing less dependency on gut microbes compared to EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES (ROS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are biochemical indicators used to measure oxidative stress in macroinvertebrates during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen that increase in response to oxidative stress induced by microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, such as superoxide radicals, which are measured in cells undergoing oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are generated during the biodegradation of PE microplastics, leading to oxidative stress and damage in mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are molecules produced during oxidative stress, measured in mealworm larvae to assess their physiological response to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are molecules produced during oxidative stress, which can damage cells and tissues in organisms like mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are measured to assess oxidative stress levels in the gut cells of insects fed with Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are molecules involved in oxidative stress, measured using the Muse® Oxidative Stress Kit in cell homogenates.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules generated during the biodegradation of plastics, causing oxidative stress in organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, produced at high nanoplastic concentrations, which can damage bacterial cell membranes.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemical compounds produced during the degradation of polystyrene (PS) by Zophobas atratus larvae, contributing to the breakdown of the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen that can cause oxidative damage in organisms exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemicals that indicate oxidative stress in mealworms, which can be induced by the ingestion of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are free radicals produced during oxidative stress, which increase in mealworms due to microplastic ingestion and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are molecules that indicate oxidative stress levels in mealworms exposed to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC APPROACHES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic approaches involve using biological enzymes to depolymerize and biodegrade plastics, offering an environmentally friendly solution to plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT BIOMASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect biomass refers to the total mass of mealworms, which was measured in the study to evaluate growth and survival rates under different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect biomass, such as that from mealworm larvae, is a sustainable source of protein and fats for livestock and human consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2,4-TRICHLOROBENZENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (1,2,4-TCB) is an organic compound used as a solvent and in the production of dyes and pesticides.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a chemical reagent used in extraction experiments to measure the biodegradation of polyethylene by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a solvent used to extract low-density polyethylene (LDPE) samples for gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a chemical solvent used to extract residual polyethylene (PE) from frass samples for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a chemical solvent used to extract residual polyethylene from frass for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD-GRADE JELLY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Food-grade jelly is used as an adhesive to create a mixture of polyethylene (PE) and bran for feeding mealworm larvae in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-TEMPERATURE GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (HT-GPC)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">HT-GPC is a method used to analyze the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by mealworm larvae, verifying depolymerization and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High-temperature gel permeation chromatography is used to analyze the molecular weights of low-density polyethylene polymers extracted from the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is a technique used to analyze changes in the molecular weight of PE mulching films after bacterial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;High-temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight changes in polyethylene (PE) during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is a method used to determine the molecular weight of polypropylene (PP) in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is a technique used to analyze the degradation of polyethylene by yellow mealworms, measuring changes in molecular weight.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is used to determine the molecular weight values of virgin and residual polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs).&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is a technique used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polymers, such as polyethylene, in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography (HT-GPC) is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of residual polypropylene (PP) in frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is used to determine the molecular weight of plastic samples and frass from mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;High-temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of microplastics (MPs) after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;High-temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) is a specialized technique used to analyze the molecular weight and distribution of polymers at elevated temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is used to identify the depolymerization of ingested PE polymers by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSIOLOGICAL PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Physiological performance refers to the assessment of mealworm larvae's survival, growth, and biomass changes after exposure to polyethylene (PE) during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Physiological performance refers to the functional capabilities of mealworm larvae under environmental exposure to microplastics and nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which can be ingested and biodegraded by mealworms, leading to changes in their hydrophobicity and molecular structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which can induce oxidative stress and physiological impairments in invertebrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, a common environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which are studied for their biodegradation by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from the fragmentation of polyethylene, which can be biodegraded by organisms like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles that can have adverse effects on organisms, such as zebrafish, at molecular and individual levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which can be biodegraded by mealworms and their gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STAINLESS STEEL MESH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Stainless steel mesh is used to sieve polyethylene microplastics to ensure they are small enough for mealworm larvae to ingest.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUARDO FOOD CO.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gurado Food Co. is a supplier of food-grade jelly used in the preparation of polyethylene (PE) and bran mixtures for mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BINZHOU MEALWORM BREEDING CO.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Binzhou Mealworm Breeding Co. is a supplier of mealworm larvae and their regular food (bran) used in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIGMA-ALDRICH CO.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sigma-Aldrich Co. is a supplier of chemical reagents, including 1,2,4-trichlorobenzene, used in biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLASS CONTAINER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glass containers are used to house mealworm larvae during biodegradation experiments to prevent contamination and monitor plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER-EXTRACTED FRACTION (CW)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The water-extracted fraction (Cw) is a component of frass residue analyzed to measure the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The water-extracted fraction is a component of frass analyzed to determine the residual PS content after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHANOL-EXTRACTED FRACTION (CE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The ethanol-extracted fraction (Ce) is a component of frass residue analyzed to measure the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The ethanol-extracted fraction is a component of frass analyzed to determine the residual PS content after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2,4-TCB-EXTRACTED FRACTION (CT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The 1,2,4-TCB-extracted fraction (Ct) is a component of frass residue analyzed to measure the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL RATE (SR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival rate (SR) is a metric used in the study to evaluate the percentage of mealworms that survive under different feeding conditions, including plastic-only and bran-co-diet groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a metric used in the study to measure the percentage of mealworms surviving under different feeding conditions, such as PS alone or PS with bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a physiological index measured to assess the health and survival of mealworm larvae during biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a physiological parameter calculated to assess the effects of PS MPs and other diets on larval health.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a physiological parameter calculated to determine the percentage of live larvae during the biodegradation study.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate is a metric used to assess the impact of Styrofoam consumption on the health and survival of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) refers to the percentage of Tenebrio molitor larvae that survive during experiments involving the consumption and biodegradation of polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a metric used to evaluate the effects of polystyrene consumption and environmental conditions on the survival of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a metric used to evaluate the survival of mealworm larvae under different dietary conditions, including polystyrene (PS) consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a metric used in the study to measure the percentage of larvae that survived when fed LDPE foams or bran, indicating the larvae's ability to ingest and survive on plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate is a metric used to evaluate the health and viability of mealworms under different feeding conditions, including PS MPs and starvation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AVERAGE WEIGHT (AW)">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Average weight (AW) is a physiological index measured to assess the growth of mealworm larvae during biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOTAL CARBOHYDRATE CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Total carbohydrate content is a physiological index measured to assess the biomass changes in mealworm larvae during biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAT CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fat content is a key indicator of the nutritional quality of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which decreases during starvation and changes the taste of the insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Fat content is a measure of the lipid levels in mealworm larvae, which can be affected by their diet, including exposure to PE microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Fat content is a physiological index measured to assess the biomass changes in mealworm larvae during biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fat content refers to the amount of fat present in larvae and excrement, which is influenced by the type of feed, such as polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein content in feed positively affects the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae, making it a critical component of their diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content in mealworms was analyzed to determine the impact of feeding on different types of polystyrene, showing no significant increase compared to the control group.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content is a nutritional metric used to evaluate the dietary impact of PE microplastics and bran on mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content is a physiological index measured to assess the biomass changes in mealworm larvae during biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content is measured in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed different diets, with the highest content observed in the PS group and the lowest in the OAT group.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content is analyzed in the biomass of insect larvae to determine the impact of PS consumption on their growth and nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content in mealworms varies depending on the type of food consumed, with PLA-fed larvae showing the highest protein levels among non-control groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MALONDIALDEHYDE (MDA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a biomarker of lipid peroxidation, indicating oxidative damage in organisms exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a biomarker of oxidative stress, used to assess the toxic effects of microplastics on organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a marker of lipid peroxidation measured to assess oxidative damage in Tenebrio molitor larvae exposed to polyethylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a marker of lipid peroxidation, indicating oxidative damage in Tenebrio molitor larvae due to microplastic ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a marker of lipid peroxidation, indicating oxidative damage in organisms exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a marker of lipid peroxidation, used to evaluate oxidative stress in mealworm larvae during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a biomarker used to measure oxidative damage in mealworms exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a chemical that indicates lipid peroxidation levels in mealworms, reflecting oxidative damage caused by microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a marker of oxidative damage in organisms, with increased levels observed in mealworms fed with microplastics, indicating lipid peroxidation and oxidative damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPEROXIDE DISMUTASE (SOD)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme measured in mealworm larvae to assess their antioxidant response during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme that helps eliminate superoxide anions, protecting organisms from oxidative damage caused by microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme that helps mitigate oxidative stress in Tenebrio molitor larvae exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase is an enzyme involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics, including the degradation of plastics and lignocellulose by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an enzyme involved in the oxidative stress response, studied in the context of microplastic toxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an enzyme measured to evaluate oxidative stress levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae exposed to polyethylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme that increases in mealworms as a defense mechanism against oxidative stress caused by microplastic ingestion and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an enzyme that helps mitigate oxidative stress in mealworms exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an enzyme that helps organisms combat oxidative stress by converting superoxide radicals into less harmful molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an enzyme that reflects the antioxidant performance and physiological stress responses in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUTATHIONE-S-TRANSFERASE (GST)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glutathione-S-transferase (GST) is an enzyme involved in detoxification processes, measured in Tenebrio molitor larvae to assess oxidative stress responses to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Glutathione-S-transferase (GST) is an enzyme involved in detoxification, measured in mealworm larvae to evaluate their response to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Glutathione-S-transferase (GST) is an enzyme that scavenges lipid peroxidation metabolites, alleviating oxidative damage in organisms exposed to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATALASE (CAT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Catalase (CAT) is an antioxidant enzyme measured in mealworm larvae to assess their oxidative stress response during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Catalase (CAT) is an antioxidant enzyme that converts hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen, protecting cells from oxidative stress induced by microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Catalase (CAT) is an enzyme that breaks down hydrogen peroxide, playing a role in reducing oxidative stress in Tenebrio molitor larvae exposed to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD SAFETY TESTING">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Food safety testing standards were referred to in the preparation and testing of mealworm larvae to ensure safe and standardized procedures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHEAT BRAN">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Wheat bran (WB) is a byproduct of wheat milling, renowned for its rich content of dietary fiber, carbohydrates, fats, and proteins. It is widely utilized as a versatile dietary component in scientific studies, particularly those involving insect larvae such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm), *Tenebrio obscurus*, and *Zophobas atratus*. Wheat bran serves as a common feed for commercially reared insect larvae, including mealworms and Black Soldier Fly larvae, supporting their growth, survival, and development. It is frequently employed as a control diet in experiments to compare the effects of various diets, such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyurethane foam, and other plastics, on the larvae's growth, plastic degradation efficiency, and gut microbial diversity. Additionally, it is used as a control feedstock to evaluate the effects of natural diets versus alternative diets, such as mask-based diets, on yellow mealworms.

In biodegradation studies, wheat bran plays a critical role in supporting the growth and plastic degradation capabilities of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. It is often used as a standard feed before larvae are subjected to experimental diets involving plastics, serving as a baseline for comparison with Styrofoam-fed or plastic-fed insects. Wheat bran is also used as a co-diet alongside plastics, such as polyethylene (PE), to enhance the larvae's affinity for plastic consumption and improve their biodegradation efficiency. Its nutrient-rich composition helps mitigate the negative effects of microplastic ingestion, making it a valuable component in studies assessing the impact of plastic diets on insects.

Furthermore, wheat bran is utilized as a natural feed additive to alleviate the toxicity caused by micro(nano)plastics in macroinvertebrates like *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. When supplemented, it improves the survival rate, biomass nutrient content, and biodegradation efficiency of larvae exposed to microplastics. In some studies, wheat bran is part of a standard culture substrate, composed of 70% wheat bran, 25% corn, and 5% soybean, to create a controlled environment for plastic degradation experiments.

Wheat bran is also recognized as a dietary supplement that enhances the polystyrene consumption rate of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, aiding in the biodegradation process. It serves as a normal diet for these larvae, providing sufficient nutrients and carbon for their growth. However, its presence can interfere with thermogravimetrical analysis (TGA) results if not</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a706f2bd66291a3ef925114ffd9300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H2O2 SOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A 30% H2O2 solution is used to digest organic matter in frass samples from mealworms to analyze residual plastic particles.&lt;SEP&gt;A 30% w/w H2O2 solution is used to digest organic matter in frass samples to recover residual plastic particles for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LASER PARTICLE ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A laser particle analyzer (Mastersizer 3000) is used to measure the size distribution of residual plastic particles after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STATISTICAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Statistical analysis is performed to compare the effects of food additives on polystyrene consumption and the effects of different media on cacti growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical analysis was conducted using Statistica 13.1 to determine the significance of the results, including t-Student tests and ANOVA with post hoc Tukey's test.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical analysis, including ANOVA and student's t-test, is performed to assess differences among experimental groups in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical analysis, including ANOVA and regression models, was used to evaluate the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical analysis is performed to evaluate differences in PE consumption, survival rates, molecular weight changes, and microbial diversity in mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical analysis is used to assess significant differences in extraction rates, plastic removal, and other parameters related to the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIOXIDANT PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The overall antioxidant performance of mealworm larvae is calculated and evaluated in response to plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE (PE) MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) microplastics are small plastic particles that mealworm larvae can ingest and biodegrade, though they cause oxidative stress and physiological damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which are ingested and biodegraded by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which are ingested and biodegraded by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) are small plastic fragments that Tenebrio larvae are exposed to during the biodegradation of plastic foams or particles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SR (SURVIVAL RATE)">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival rate (SR) is a metric used to assess the physiological response and health of mealworm larvae under different dietary conditions, including exposure to PE microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AW (AVERAGE WEIGHT)">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Average weight (AW) is a measure used to evaluate the growth and nutritional status of mealworm larvae when fed with PE microplastics and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LINEAR STRUCTURE OF PE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The linear structure of polyethylene (PE) polymers is a factor that may affect the digestibility of PE microplastics by mealworm larvae and their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYSTALLINITY OF PE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The crystallinity of polyethylene (PE) polymers is another factor that may influence the biodegradation efficiency of PE microplastics by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymes are biological catalysts that play a crucial role in the degradation of various materials, including synthetic plastics, plant biomass, and other complex polymers. They are produced by a diverse range of sources, such as microorganisms, plastic-degrading larvae (e.g., Tenebrio molitor and Galleria mellonella), and their gut microbiota. Enzymes facilitate the breakdown of complex plastic polymers into simpler molecules through processes such as hydrolysis, oxidation, and depolymerization, making them essential for biodegradation.

Specific enzymes, including hydrolases, oxidases, decarboxylase, depolymerase, oxidoreductase, and dehydrogenase, are involved in the degradation of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) by Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae. These enzymes are also implicated in the breakdown of other plastics, such as polyethylene, polystyrene, and polypropylene. Enzymes produced by mealworms and their gut microbiota, such as lipases, esterases, proteases, and laccases, are particularly effective in hydrolyzing polyurethane (PU) and other plastic polymers. Research has shown that enzymes secreted by Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade PET independently of gut microbes, highlighting their direct role in plastic degradation.

Additionally, enzymes isolated from bacteria present in plastic-eating worms, as well as those produced by microorganisms like Pseudomonas sp., are being studied for their potential to degrade plastics. For example, enzymes from Pseudomonas sp. can transform styrene into phenylacetate during plastic degradation. Enzymes such as CYP6/4, Lac640, and SOD are upregulated in mealworms fed on polystyrene (PS) and lignin, playing a role in the oxidation and degradation of these materials. Enzymes produced by Galleria mellonella (waxworm) saliva have also been shown to degrade polyethylene, further demonstrating the diverse sources and mechanisms by which enzymes contribute to plastic degradation.

Enzymes secreted by Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut microorganisms are essential for the depolymerization and biodegradation of microplastics (MPs). Specifically, the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae plays a critical role in the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene microplastics. These enzymes facilitate the oxidation and depolymerization of aged polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) microplastics, further emphasizing their importance in addressing plastic pollution.

Enzymes such as esterases, lipases, and phosphatases are also produced by the digestive tract and symbiotic bacteria of</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOHYDRATE CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbohydrate content in mealworms was measured to assess the metabolic impact of polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbohydrate content is a nutritional metric used to assess the dietary impact of PE microplastics and bran on mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROS (REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are free radicals produced during PE biodegradation, leading to oxidative damage in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MDA (MALONDIALDEHYDE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a marker of lipid peroxidation and oxidative damage in mealworm larvae due to PE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIOXIDANT ENZYMES (SOD, GST, CAT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Antioxidant enzymes such as SOD, GST, and CAT are produced by mealworm larvae to counteract oxidative stress caused by PE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EISENIA FETIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Eisenia fetida, commonly known as the red wiggler worm or earthworm, is a soil-dwelling macroinvertebrate widely used in scientific studies to assess the effects of environmental pollutants, particularly microplastics and chemicals, on its health and ecosystem functions. This species is frequently exposed to various types of microplastics, including polystyrene (PS MPs) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), as well as chemicals like atrazine and phenanthrene. Studies have shown that exposure to microplastics can lead to oxidative stress, DNA damage, lipid peroxidation, and gut toxicity in Eisenia fetida. Additionally, these earthworms can ingest and biofragment microplastics, producing nanoplastics in the process, and have been observed to disintegrate bio-based polylactic acid (PLA) microplastics into sub-micron particles within their digestive tracts. Despite their limited ability to degrade plastics, Eisenia fetida is used to evaluate the biodegradation of materials like PLA and the bioaccumulation of hazardous organic compounds from microplastics in soil. Furthermore, research focuses on their interactions with soil particles and their responses to oxidative stress and gene expression changes induced by pollutants. Overall, Eisenia fetida serves as a critical model organism for understanding the ecological and toxicological impacts of microplastics and other contaminants in terrestrial environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS (PS-MP)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene microplastics (PS-MP) are small plastic particles that can induce oxidative stress and toxicity in soil invertebrates like Eisenia fetida.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MULCH FILM-DERIVED PE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Mulch film-derived polyethylene (PE) microplastics are environmental pollutants that can cause oxidative damage and DNA damage in organisms like earthworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE DAMAGE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative damage refers to the cellular and molecular harm caused by excessive reactive oxygen species (ROS) during the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLIC DYSBIOSIS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolic dysbiosis occurs in mealworms when fed with ultrahigh-MW polystyrene polymers, altering their intestinal metabolic pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic dysbiosis refers to the disruption of metabolic processes in macroinvertebrates due to exposure to micro(nano)plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic dysbiosis refers to the disruption of metabolic processes in mealworm larvae due to oxidative stress caused by PE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOD (SUPEROXIDE DISMUTASE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme that helps eliminate superoxide anions and protect cells from oxidative damage in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GST (GLUTATHIONE S-TRANSFERASE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glutathione S-transferase (GST) is an enzyme that scavenges lipid peroxidation metabolites, protecting mealworm larvae from oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAT (CATALASE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Catalase (CAT) is an antioxidant enzyme that breaks down hydrogen peroxide, reducing oxidative stress in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA DAMAGE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">DNA damage refers to the genetic harm caused by oxidative stress in organisms like Eisenia fetida exposed to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL PE PARTICLES">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Residual PE particles are undigested plastic fragments generated during the biodegradation of PE by mealworm larvae, with sizes ranging from micro- to submicron levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOD">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SOD (superoxide dismutase) is an enzyme upregulated in PS-/CS-fed mealworms, involved in the oxidative degradation of polystyrene and lignin.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme that scavenges free radicals and protects larval tissues from oxidative damage during PE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme that scavenges excess peroxidative free radicals, playing a role in mitigating oxidative stress in Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GST">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glutathione S-transferase (GST) is an enzyme that scavenges metabolites of lipid peroxidation and protects organisms from oxidative stress during PE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Catalase (CAT) is an antioxidant enzyme that helps mitigate oxidative stress by breaking down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen during PE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MDA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a marker of lipid peroxidation, indicating oxidative damage in mealworm larvae during PE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a marker of oxidative damage to membrane lipids, indicating the level of oxidative stress in Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIOXIDANT SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Antioxidant systems in mealworm larvae, including enzymes like SOD, GST, and CAT, are triggered to scavenge free radicals during PE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antioxidant systems in mealworms are damaged due to oxidative stress caused by the ingestion of ultrahigh-MW polystyrene polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Antioxidant systems in mealworms are impacted by oxidative stress during the biodegradation of polystyrene, affecting their ability to defend against oxidative damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE STRESS LEVELS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative stress levels in mealworm larvae are influenced by the biodegradation of PE and can be alleviated by supplementing bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOMASS LEVELS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biomass levels of mealworm larvae are enhanced by supplementing bran, which improves their survival during PE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE REMOVAL EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PE removal efficiency refers to the ability of mealworm larvae to degrade polyethylene, which is slightly reduced when bran is supplemented.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXABROMOCYCLODODECANE (HBCD)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD) is a widely used flame retardant commonly found in polystyrene and other plastics. It has been extensively studied for its environmental fate, particularly during biodegradation processes involving organisms such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae). Research indicates that HBCD can be egested by mealworms via frass, demonstrating their ability to process and eliminate this compound without bioaccumulation. This suggests that mealworms may play a role in mitigating the environmental impact of HBCD by breaking it down during biodegradation. However, HBCD poses environmental concerns due to its persistence and potential toxicity, making its fate in ecosystems an important area of study. While some descriptions mention polyethylene degradation, the primary focus of research has been on polystyrene, where HBCD is most commonly found. Overall, HBCD's interactions with mealworms and its biodegradation pathways are key areas of investigation, highlighting both its environmental risks and potential mitigation strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">**Alternative Protein Sources: A Comprehensive Overview**

Alternative protein sources, particularly edible insects such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms), *Zophobas morio* (superworms), *Zophobas atratus*, Black Soldier Fly, and Wax Moth larvae, are being extensively explored as sustainable and eco-friendly options for both human consumption and animal feed. These organisms are highly valued for their high protein content, nutritional benefits, and potential to address global challenges related to food security and environmental sustainability.

Mealworms, specifically *Tenebrio molitor*, are highlighted for their unique ability to biodegrade plastics, including polystyrene foam, which not only positions them as a sustainable food option but also as a potential solution to plastic waste management. Additionally, edible insects like mealworms are being studied for their capacity to degrade pollutants, further enhancing their environmental appeal. This dual capability makes them a promising candidate for addressing both food production and waste management challenges. Similarly, other insects like Black Soldier Fly and Wax Moth larvae are also being explored for their ability to convert plastic carbon into viable protein, further expanding their potential as alternative protein sources.

These alternative protein sources are being considered for a wide range of applications, including livestock feed, poultry and fish feed, and direct human consumption. Their high nutritional value, combined with their low environmental footprint, makes them an attractive option for reducing the ecological impact of traditional protein sources. Studies have particularly focused on the nutritional value and safety of mealworms in poultry diets, as well as their potential use in livestock feed. Despite some challenges, such as limitations in fatty acid synthesis when bred on synthetic polymer diets, mealworms remain a viable and innovative protein source.

Overall, *Tenebrio molitor*, *Zophobas morio*, *Zophobas atratus*, Black Soldier Fly, and Wax Moth larvae represent multifaceted solutions to the dual challenges of food security and environmental sustainability. Their ability to provide high-quality protein, coupled with their ecological benefits, aligns with global efforts to promote sustainable food systems and reduce reliance on resource-intensive agricultural practices. As research and development continue, these alternative protein sources are poised to play a significant role in shaping a more sustainable and resilient food future.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOYBEAN MEAL">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Soybean meal is a common protein source in broiler diets, which is being compared to Tenebrio molitor larvae as an alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Soybean meal is a protein-rich feed source for mealworms, affecting their growth and nutritional content.&lt;SEP&gt;Soybean meal is a plant-based protein source used in livestock feed, comparable in protein quality to Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROILER DIETS">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Broiler diets are formulated to provide optimal nutrition for chickens, and alternative protein sources like Tenebrio molitor larvae are being explored to replace traditional ingredients like soybean meal.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CROP RESIDUES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crop residues are organic materials left after harvest, which can be used as co-diets in biodegradation studies involving mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Crop residues are used as co-diets in studies to enhance the biodegradation of polyethylene by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON AND NITROGEN METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon and nitrogen metabolism are crucial metabolic activities in Tenebrio larvae that facilitate the degradation of nitrogen-free and carbon-rich PE polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon and nitrogen metabolism are key processes during the biodegradation of polyethylene by Tenebrio molitor larvae, influenced by their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon and nitrogen metabolism refers to the biochemical processes involved in the breakdown and utilization of carbon and nitrogen, often studied in biodegradation contexts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENE EXPRESSION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gene expression changes are studied in organisms like Eisenia fetida to assess the toxicological effects of microplastics and chemicals.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene expression changes are studied in organisms like Eisenia fetida to understand the effects of microplastics and chemicals on cellular and molecular levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATRAZINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Atrazine is a chemical studied for its combined effects with microplastics on oxidative stress and gene expression in earthworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Atrazine is a herbicide that can contaminate soil and is remediated using microwave-activated persulfate systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Atrazine is a herbicide that, when combined with microplastics, can induce oxidative stress and gene expression changes in organisms like Eisenia fetida.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MITOCHONDRIAL DAMAGE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mitochondrial damage is a toxicological effect observed in human liver and lung cells exposed to nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane microplastics are studied for their toxicity to earthworms, with soil particles forming a protective layer that reduces their harmful effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL PARTICLES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil particles can form a protective layer on microplastics, reducing their toxicity to organisms like earthworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil particles can form protective layers on plastics, reducing the toxicity of polystyrene microplastics to earthworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORE GUT MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The core gut microbiome of organisms like Galleria mellonella plays a role in the biodegradation of plastics like polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The core gut microbiome refers to the essential microbial community in the digestive tracts of organisms like Galleria mellonella, which aids in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The core gut microbiome of organisms like waxworms plays a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics such as polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The core gut microbiome refers to the dominant microbial communities in the guts of superworms and yellow mealworms that are associated with plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The core gut microbiome refers to the community of microorganisms present in the digestive systems of organisms, which can play a key role in processes like plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYLACTIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polylactic Acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic substrate that enhances the metabolic activity of Mealworm and Wax Moth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic Acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic, but its presence in the environment can still pose challenges if not properly managed.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic polymer with a number average molecular weight of 30.5 ± 2.3 kDa, preferentially consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that can be broken down by Tenebrio molitor larvae, offering a sustainable approach to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that can be broken down by yellow mealworms, offering a sustainable approach to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid is a biodegradable plastic that can be broken down by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to sustainable waste management practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid is a biodegradable plastic that can be broken down by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to sustainable waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic tested in the study to assess its impact on insect metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that can be degraded by yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) through resource recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic derived from renewable resources, used in packaging and biomedical applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid is a biodegradable plastic that can be broken down by mealworms, offering a sustainable approach to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid is a biodegradable plastic that can be broken down by organisms like earthworms, contributing to its environmental degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable thermoplastic derived from renewable resources, used in packaging and 3D printing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Superworms, scientifically known as *Zophobas atratus* (also referred to as *Zophobas morio* or *Zophobas atinus* in some contexts), are insect larvae that have garnered significant attention for their remarkable ability to biodegrade various types of plastics. These include polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyurethane (PU), and polypropylene (PP). Superworms are particularly effective at degrading polystyrene, including Styrofoam, and can mineralize it into CO2, contributing to its complete biodegradation. They exhibit higher polystyrene consumption efficiency compared to yellow mealworms, making them a promising candidate for addressing PS waste.

The biodegradation process in superworms is facilitated by their gut microbiome, which supports oxidative degradation and depolymerization of plastics. Specific bacteria, such as *Pseudomonas aeruginosa*, have been identified as key contributors to this process. Superworms are capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam with mass losses of 0.30 and 0.59 mg/larva/day, as well as polyurethane foam plastics. They also biodegrade polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent processes, similar to those observed in *Tenebrio molitor* larvae.

Studies have shown that superworms can survive and grow on plastic diets, with 100% survival rates observed in both plastic-fed and control bran-fed groups. Their resilience and higher degradation rates, combined with less significant changes in nutrient composition compared to mealworms, highlight their potential for plastic waste management and environmental remediation. Beyond their environmental applications, superworms are also used as feed for fish and poultry, underscoring their versatility and utility in various industries.

In summary, superworms (*Zophobas atratus*) are highly effective in degrading a wide range of plastics, supported by their gut microbiome. Their ability to survive on plastic diets and their higher degradation rates compared to other insects position them as a valuable resource in efforts to mitigate global plastic pollution and promote environmental sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DARK MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dark mealworms (T. obscurus) are larvae that exhibit a higher polystyrene (PS) foam consumption rate of 0.32 mg/larva/d compared to yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are another species of mealworms that, like Tenebrio molitor, can biodegrade polystyrene and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are another species of mealworms that, like Tenebrio molitor, can biodegrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, demonstrating their potential in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are larvae capable of biodegrading high-purity polyethylene microplastics, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are insects that have been shown to digest polystyrene, similar to other plastic-eating insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are larvae studied for their ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene independently of gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus larvae) have been studied for their ability to degrade plastics, including polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (larvae of Tenebrio obscurus) are capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene via gut microbe-independent depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBE-INDEPENDENT DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbe-independent depolymerization refers to the breakdown of plastics by organisms like mealworms without relying solely on their gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbe-independent depolymerization refers to the breakdown of plastics like low-density polyethylene by mealworms without relying solely on gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbe-independent depolymerization refers to the process by which mealworms and superworms can biodegrade plastics like low-density polyethylene without relying on gut microbes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE CARBON ISOTOPE LABELLING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable carbon isotope labelling is a method used to assess the biodegradation of plastics like poly(butylene succinate) in soil laboratory incubations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(BUTYLENE SUCCINATE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(butylene succinate) is a biodegradable plastic that can be broken down in soil, as assessed by stable carbon isotope labelling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KOI CARP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Koi carp are fish studied for their ecological and isotopic responses to microplastics under different food conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Koi carp are fish whose ecological stoichiometric and stable isotopic responses to microplastics are influenced by food conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESOURCE RECOVERY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Resource recovery refers to the process of converting waste materials, such as plastics, into useful resources through biodegradation by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource recovery refers to the process of reclaiming useful materials from waste, such as the biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource recovery refers to the sustainable approach of using organisms like yellow mealworms to biodegrade plastics such as polylactic acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource recovery refers to the process of extracting valuable materials or energy from waste, such as the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms to recover resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource recovery refers to the process of extracting valuable materials or energy from waste, such as the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms to recover resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource recovery refers to the process of converting waste materials, such as plastics, into useful resources through biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource recovery refers to the process of reclaiming useful materials from waste, such as the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbes are microorganisms residing in the digestive tracts of various insect larvae, including mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), and waxworms. These microbes play a critical role in the biodegradation of a wide range of plastics, such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). By facilitating depolymerization and mineralization processes, gut microbes break down plastic polymers into smaller, more manageable components, contributing to environmental sustainability and advancing sustainable waste management solutions.

In mealworms, gut microbes are particularly effective in degrading polystyrene (PS) and PVC. Their activity is essential, as demonstrated by the inhibition of depolymerization when these microbes are suppressed by antibiotics like gentamicin. Similarly, gut microbes in mealworms and superworms are involved in the degradation of PET, showing chemotaxis towards PET metabolic intermediates and producing enzymes that depolymerize the plastic. However, there is some contradiction regarding the essentiality of gut microbes in PET depolymerization, with some studies suggesting they are not strictly necessary, while others emphasize their significant role, supported by transcriptome and metabolome analyses.

Under anaerobic conditions, gut microbes in mealworms continue to play a critical role in the biodegradation of polystyrene. Additionally, changes in microbial communities, particularly the dominance of specific families like Enterobacteriaceae and Streptococcaceae, are observed during the degradation process of microplastics in *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. Emerging evidence also suggests that gut microbes may contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene in lesser waxworms, similar to their role in yellow mealworms, though further research is needed to confirm this.

Overall, gut microbes in these insect larvae are essential for the biodegradation of various types of plastics, including polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene. Their ability to degrade plastics such as PP, PS, LDPE, and PET highlights their potential in addressing plastic pollution and advancing sustainable waste management solutions. These findings make gut microbes a subject of significant interest for research into environmental remediation and sustainable practices.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYLACTIC ACID MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polylactic acid microplastics are biodegradable plastics that can degrade photolytically, potentially releasing toxic byproducts.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid microplastics are biodegradable plastics that can undergo microbial adhesion and degradation, affecting the adsorption behavior of chemicals like oxytetracycline.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZEBRAFISH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Juvenile zebrafish (Danio rerio) are used in studies to assess the combined toxic effects of polystyrene microplastics and di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebrafish are used as a model organism to study the developmental toxicity of microplastics, such as polylactic acid and polyamide.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebrafish (Danio rerio) are used as model organisms to study the effects of microplastic particles on intestinal health and overall well-being.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebrafish are model organisms used to study the effects of microplastics, including mechanical damage and inflammatory responses caused by long-term exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebrafish are aquatic organisms used in studies to observe the effects of oxidative stress and metabolic alterations caused by plastics on microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebrafish are used in studies to assess the adverse effects of polyethylene microplastics on heart function at molecular and individual levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYAMIDE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyamide microplastics are synthetic polymers that can undergo photo-oxidative degradation, affecting their toxicity to aquatic organisms like zebrafish.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTOLYTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Photolytic degradation is a process where plastics like polylactic acid microplastics break down under light exposure, potentially increasing their toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTO-OXIDATIVE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Photo-oxidative degradation refers to the breakdown of plastics like polyamide microplastics under light and oxygen, altering their environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL PROTISTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil protists are microorganisms found in soil ecosystems that interact with microplastics and nanoplastics, influencing their environmental fate.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil protists are microorganisms found in soil ecosystems that interact with microplastics and nanoplastics, influencing their environmental fate.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil protists are microorganisms that interact with polystyrene micro- and nanoplastics, influencing their fate in soil ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil protists are microorganisms that play a crucial role in soil ecosystems and can be affected by microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
