    <node id="CELLULOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulose is a natural polymer found in plant cell walls and is one of the materials biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer found in plant waste that mealworms can break down, aided by their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer found in plant-based waste that can be degraded by mealworms, contributing to waste management solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a polymer waste that is metabolized by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to the study of their digestive tract microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio monitor larvae, along with synthetic polymers like polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a polysaccharide found in plant cell walls, which Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade, potentially contributing to their ability to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer that is sometimes used as a co-diet supplement in studies of plastic biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is an organic compound and a major component of plant cell walls, which can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer that can be biodegraded by organisms like Tenebrio molitor, often studied in comparison to synthetic polymers like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer that yellow mealworms can biodegrade, with changes observed in their gut microbiome and enzymatic profile during the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is an organic compound that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, alongside plastics like polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer that can be biodegraded by organisms such as Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer that can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms and insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer found in plant waste that mealworms can break down, aided by their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer found in plant-based waste that can be degraded by mealworms, contributing to waste management solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer that is also metabolized by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to the study of waste degradation in their digestive tract.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC PROFILE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The enzymatic profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae changes during the biodegradation of cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene waste, indicating their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae changes during the biodegradation of cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene, indicating the role of enzymes in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of Tenebrio monitor larvae changes during the biodegradation of cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of mealworms includes enzymes like phosphatases, esterases, and β-glucosidase, which are involved in the degradation of plastics and cellulose.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile refers to the specific enzymes produced by organisms like Tenebrio molitor that facilitate the biodegradation of plastics such as polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile refers to the specific enzymes produced by organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae, that facilitate the biodegradation of materials like cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile refers to the specific enzymes produced by organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor, that facilitate the biodegradation of materials like polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of yellow mealworms changes during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating the role of specific enzymes in breaking down plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of organisms like Tenebrio molitor refers to the specific enzymes they produce, which are involved in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of organisms like Tenebrio molitor changes during the biodegradation of cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile refers to the set of enzymes produced by organisms, such as insect larvae, that play a role in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of organisms like Tenebrio molitor changes during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating the role of enzymes in the breakdown process.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of mealworms includes enzymes like phosphatases, esterases, and β-glucosidase, which are involved in the degradation of plastics and cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste management challenges, including plastic accumulation in landfills and marine environments, are addressed by research on plastic biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Waste management involves strategies to reduce, reuse, and recycle waste materials, including the biodegradation of plastics by organisms like Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Waste management is a critical area of research focused on developing sustainable methods to handle and degrade various types of waste, including plastics and cellulose.&lt;SEP&gt;Waste management refers to strategies and research aimed at reducing and managing waste, including plastic and organic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Waste management involves the collection, transport, processing, and disposal of waste materials, with a focus on reducing environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Waste management involves strategies like biodegradation by mealworms to reduce the environmental impact of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Waste management is a critical area of research focused on developing sustainable methods to handle and degrade various types of waste, including plastics and cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OAT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">OAT is a control variant used in the study to compare enzyme activity levels in the digestive tract and bacterial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;OAT is a control variant used in the study to compare enzyme activity levels in the digestive tract and bacterial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Oat is a natural diet used as a control in studies to compare the gut microbiome and enzymatic activity of mealworms when fed different types of waste.&lt;SEP&gt;OAT is a diet variant used in the study, characterized by a broad range of simple sugar metabolism in the gut microbiota of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;OAT refers to oatmeal, a diet used in the study to compare the effects of different diets on larvae metabolism and protein content.&lt;SEP&gt;Oat is a natural diet for mealworms, influencing their protein and fatty acid content and serving as a control diet in studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Oat is a natural diet used as a control in studies to compare the gut microbiome and enzymatic activity of mealworms when fed different types of waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Oat is a food source used in experiments to supplement the diet of mealworms, often combined with polystyrene to study its effects on growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1</data>
    </node>
