    <node id="PROTEOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Proteobacteria is a highly diverse and significant phylum of bacteria that plays crucial roles in various environmental and biological processes. This phylum includes subgroups such as Alphaproteobacteria, Betaproteobacteria, and Gammaproteobacteria, which are involved in processes like nitrogen fixation in marine environments and the biodegradation of various materials. Proteobacteria are particularly dominant in the gut microbiome of insects such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms, and Alphitobius diaperinus, where they contribute significantly to digestive processes and the biodegradation of plastics and other xenobiotic pollutants.

In the gut microbiota of mealworms, Proteobacteria are a dominant phylum, with their abundance varying depending on the substrate consumed. They are especially prevalent when mealworms feed on materials like polyethylene (PU), polyvinyl acetate (EVA), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polystyrene (PS), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC), suggesting their active role in the degradation of these plastics. Proteobacteria also exhibit an inverse relationship with Firmicutes abundance during polystyrene degradation, indicating a dynamic interplay between bacterial groups in the gut microbiome. Specific species within this phylum, such as Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 and Serratia proteamaculans, are known for their ability to degrade PET, a common plastic. Additionally, Pseudomonas and Achromobacter species have been identified as major contributors to polystyrene biodegradation in the gut of Tenebrio molitor.

Proteobacteria supply insects with essential amino acids, further highlighting their importance in the digestive tract of insects. The phylum is also identified as the major bacterial community in the gut of Tenebrio molitor, contributing to the degradation of nanopolystyrene. Their increased relative frequency in polystyrene (PS) samples compared to control (CT) samples further underscores their involvement in plastic degradation. Overall, Proteobacteria are a versatile and essential group of bacteria, playing a pivotal role in both environmental biodegradation processes and the digestive health of insects like mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTEROIDES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteroides are a dominant phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a bacterial class in the gut microbiome of mealworms, showing higher proportions in oatmeal waste-fed variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a genus of Bacteroidetes that plays a significant role in breaking down complex organic compounds, including potential plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a phylum of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to the digestion of various diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, especially in Diet 3.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a dominant phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, involved in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a minor phylum found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with varying abundance depending on the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a bacterial class in the gut microbiome of mealworms, showing higher proportions in oatmeal waste-fed variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a genus of Bacteroidetes that plays a significant role in breaking down complex organic compounds, including potential plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a phylum of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to the digestion of various diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIRMICUTES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Firmicutes** is a dominant bacterial phylum found in the gut microbiome of mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and other insect larvae, such as *Alphitobius diaperinus*. This phylum plays a significant role in the biodegradation of various materials, including plastics like polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyurethane (PU), and cellulose. Firmicutes includes well-known classes such as Clostridia and Bacilli, with specific species like *Bacillus gothetiili*, *B. cereus*, and *B. muralis* being capable of degrading polyethylene terephthalate (PET). 

The abundance and activity of Firmicutes in the gut microbiome vary depending on the diet of the mealworms. For instance, Firmicutes is enriched in mealworms fed polystyrene, polyethylene, or polyurethane, indicating its critical role in plastic degradation. Conversely, its abundance decreases in bran-fed mealworms, suggesting a reduced role in the degradation of materials like PVC. The phylum is also associated with digestive and metabolic functions, contributing to the overall health and biodegradation capabilities of the mealworms. 

In summary, Firmicutes is a versatile and dominant bacterial phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with its abundance and functional roles heavily influenced by dietary substrates. Its ability to degrade plastics and organic materials highlights its importance in both environmental biodegradation processes and the digestive physiology of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a phylum in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, with decreased relative abundances observed in plastic-fed groups compared to bran-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a phylum of bacteria that is part of the gut microbial community in mealworms and includes nitrogen-fixing species.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a dominant phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly when fed low molecular weight polystyrene or bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a bacterial phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, showing variations in proportion based on the substrate fed.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a phylum of bacteria that includes Clostridia and Bacilli, which are known for their role in various biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a phylum of bacteria that is abundant in the gut microbiome of mealworms and plays a role in the digestion of plastics and cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACTINOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Actinobacteria is a prominent bacterial phylum (or class, as some descriptions refer to it) that plays a significant role in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly in species such as *Tenebrio molitor* and *Alphitobius diaperinus*. This group of bacteria is highly versatile and contributes to various biodegradation processes, including the breakdown of organic materials, plastics, and microplastics. Specifically, Actinobacteria are involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) and high-density polyethylene (HDPE), making them crucial in the biodegradation of these synthetic polymers. 

The presence and proportions of Actinobacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms vary depending on the substrate or diet fed to the insects. For instance, genera such as *Arthrobacter*, *Rhodococcus*, *Mycobacterium*, and *Kocuria* are notable members of this phylum, with *Kocuria* being overrepresented in polystyrene-fed samples, suggesting its active role in polystyrene degradation. Additionally, species like *Rhodococcus* sp. are known to supply insects with essential nutrients, including amino acids, further highlighting their importance in the digestive tracts of mealworms.

Actinobacteria are also associated with nitrogen metabolism and contribute to the microbial community's overall functionality in the gut of mealworms. Their ability to degrade plastics and organic materials, coupled with their role in nutrient provision, underscores their ecological and functional significance in the gut microbiome of these insects. Overall, Actinobacteria are a dominant and versatile group of bacteria that play a critical role in both biodegradation processes and the nutritional dynamics of mealworms.</data>
