    <node id="LACTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactococcus is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed plastic diets, indicating its role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus is a genus of bacteria unique to the polyethylene diet in the gut microbiome of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus is a genus of bacteria within the Streptococcaceae family, associated with the degradation of low molecular weight polystyrene and other biodegradable plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus is a genus of lactic acid bacteria commonly found in the gut microbiomes of various insect larvae, particularly Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) and superworms. This bacterial genus plays a significant role in the digestive processes and overall gut health of these insects. Lactococcus species, such as Lactococcus lactis and Lactococcus garviae, are known to increase in relative abundance in the gut microbial community of mealworms when the insects are fed diets containing synthetic materials like polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), and polypropylene (PP). This suggests a potential role for Lactococcus in the biodegradation of these plastics. 

Studies have shown that Lactococcus is particularly prevalent in the gut of mealworms fed on polystyrene and polyethylene, indicating its involvement in plastic degradation. Additionally, Lactococcus has been observed to increase in abundance in the gut of yellow mealworms after feeding on disposable surgical masks, further supporting its association with plastic breakdown. Beyond plastic degradation, Lactococcus contributes to the regulation and maintenance of the gut microbiome, aiding in digestion and promoting gut health in mealworms. 

While some descriptions highlight its unique presence in polyethylene-fed mealworms, others emphasize its broader role in the degradation of various plastics, including polystyrene and polyurethane. Overall, Lactococcus is a key bacterial genus in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with dual roles in maintaining gut health and potentially facilitating the biodegradation of synthetic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus is a genus within the Streptococcaceae family, associated with the degradation of low molecular weight polystyrene and other biodegradable plastics.</data>
