    <node id="ESTERASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Esterases are enzymes identified in the gut microbiome of mealworms, playing a role in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Esterases are enzymes in the gut microbiome of mealworms that act during the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Esterases are enzymes with high activity in the digestive tract of mealworms, particularly involved in the degradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Esterases are enzymes in the gut of mealworms that help break down ester bonds in polyurethane molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Esterases are enzymes involved in the hydrolysis of ester bonds in polyurethane (PU) foam, aiding in its degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Esterases are enzymes produced by Achromobacter xylosidans M9 that catalyze the hydrolysis of ester bonds, playing a key role in the biodegradation of synthetic organic compounds like DEHP and NPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Esterases are enzymes that catalyze the hydrolysis of ester bonds, tested using Tween 20 as a substrate in bacterial activity assays.&lt;SEP&gt;Esterases are enzymes with high activity in the digestive tract of mealworms, particularly involved in the degradation of polyethylene.</data>
