    <node id="16S RRNA GENE SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA gene sequencing is a method used to analyze the microbial community in the gut tissues of Tenebrio molitor larvae, providing insights into the role of gut microbiota in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is a method used to examine the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, revealing changes in microbial diversity when fed different polyethylene polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is a molecular technique used to identify and classify bacterial isolates from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, revealing their phylogenetic relationships.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is a technique used to analyze the microbial communities in mealworm digestive tracts.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is used to identify and classify mealworm gut bacteria based on their genetic sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is used to analyze the microbial community composition in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, identifying bacteria involved in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is used to analyze the microbial community in the gut tissues of Tenebrio molitor larvae, providing insights into the role of gut microbiota in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is used to analyze the gut microbiome of mealworms, identifying the microbial communities involved in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is a technique used to analyze the microbial community composition in the gut of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA gene sequencing is a technique used to analyze the microbial communities in mealworm digestive tracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic activity in mealworms is modified during polystyrene biodegradation, contributing to the breakdown of the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity in mealworms is responsible for the breakdown of polystyrene and the production of metabolites such as fatty acids and cholesterol.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity in the gut intestine of Tenebrio molitor larvae plays a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity in the gut of mealworm larvae facilitates the biodegradation of polystyrene, leading to the formation of bioactive molecules and hydrocarbons.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity refers to the biochemical processes catalyzed by enzymes, particularly in the context of biodegradation of materials like polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity refers to the role of enzymes secreted by gut bacteria in breaking down synthetic and natural polymers during mealworm digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity refers to the potential of gut microbiota in superworms to produce enzymes capable of degrading polystyrene, which could be used for plastic waste recycling.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity refers to the biochemical processes by which microorganisms break down polystyrene (PS) through biofilm formation and metabolic pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity refers to the ability of bacterial enzymes to break down complex substances, such as nano-polystyrene, into simpler compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity refers to the production of enzymes such as laccases, esterases, and lipases by bacterial strains, which aid in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity in the gut of insects is responsible for breaking down polystyrene into smaller molecules like styrene and oligomers.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity refers to the role of enzymes secreted by gut bacteria in breaking down synthetic and natural polymers during mealworm digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OATMEAL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crushed oatmeal was used as a supplementary food source for larvae in the study, providing additional nutrients.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is a control feed used in the study, providing a baseline for comparing the effects of synthetic polymers and lignocellulose on mealworm development.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is a feed type used in the study to compare the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is a natural diet for mealworms, providing essential nutrients and influencing their fatty acid and protein content.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is used as a control diet in the study, providing a baseline for comparing the effects of synthetic polymer diets on Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is used as a control feed in studies to compare the degradation efficiency of mealworm larvae on different substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is a control food used in the study to compare the biochemical characteristics of mealworms fed polystyrene materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was used as a control diet for mealworms in the experiment to compare the effects of polystyrene consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is a nutritious food source used as a control diet for mealworms in experiments to compare the effects of other materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is used as a control food for mealworms in studies, providing a baseline for lipid content and energy usage comparisons.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was used as a control food source for mealworms in the study to compare the effects of plastic consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is used as a control feed in studies to compare the degradation efficiency of mealworm larvae on different substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is a dietary supplement used in experiments to improve the survival and functioning of Tenebrio molitor larvae, enhancing their ability to degrade PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is used as a dietary supplement in the study to support the survival and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal is used as a standard food source for Tenebrio molitor larvae in control groups to study their growth and development under normal feeding conditions.</data>
