    <node id="PRINCIPAL COMPONENT ANALYSIS (PCA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PCA is a statistical method used to analyze relationships between administered substrates, microorganisms, and enzymatic activity in mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PCA is a statistical method used to analyze relationships between administered substrates, microorganisms, and enzymatic activity in mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PCA is a statistical method used to differentiate metabolites between PET-fed and agar-fed larvae, indicating distinct metabolic responses to plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used in the study to analyze the relationship between gut microbiome data and chemical properties of the diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal component analysis (PCA) is used to determine relationships between observations in the study, based on the Pearson correlation-similarity matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal component analysis (PCA) is a statistical technique used to reduce the dimensionality of data while preserving as much variance as possible.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used in this study to analyze and distinguish the metabolic profiles of larvae fed with different feedstocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was conducted to analyze the gut microbiome data and identify patterns in microbial diversity.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used to evaluate data on PS biodegradation, insect growth, survival rates, and frass composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used to analyze the differences in gut bacterial communities of yellow mealworms based on their diet of surgical mask layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used to analyze and visualize the differences in microbial community composition based on genus-level profiles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGGLOMERATIVE HIERARCHICAL CLUSTERING (AHC)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">AHC is a clustering method used to analyze microbial community structures in mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;AHC is a clustering method used to analyze microbial community structures in mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;AHC is a clustering method used to group variables based on their similarities, applied in the study to analyze frass and larvae population data.&lt;SEP&gt;Agglomerative hierarchical clustering (AHC) is used to analyze data based on the Bray and Curtis dissimilarity matrix, with dendrogram construction performed using Ward's method.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </node>
