    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE PLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradable plastic is a substrate used in studies to assess the degradation capabilities of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable plastic is a substrate used in studies to assess the degradation capabilities of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable plastics are materials that can be broken down by microorganisms, but research is ongoing to find organisms capable of degrading conventional plastics as well.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable plastic is a type of plastic designed to break down more easily in the environment, offering a potential solution to plastic waste pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-OXO">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxo-degradable polyethylene (PE-oxo) is a type of plastic that degrades through oxidation, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxo-degradable polyethylene (PE-oxo) is a type of plastic that degrades through oxidation, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-oxo is a type of polyethylene substrate used in biodegradation studies with mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-oxo is a variant used in the study to measure enzyme activity levels, showing significant differences in bacterial communities compared to the control variant.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-oxo is a type of oxidized polyethylene that was used as a diet for mealworms in the study, characterized by urease activity and specific bacterial orders in the gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-oxo is a variant of polyethylene containing ~1% starch as an additive, used as a substrate for mealworm larvae in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-oxo refers to oxo-degradable polyethylene, a type of plastic used in the study to assess its impact on larvae metabolism and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-oxo is a type of polyethylene substrate used in biodegradation studies with mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-oxo is a variant used in the study to measure enzyme activity levels, showing significant differences in bacterial communities compared to the control variant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-REG">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PE-reg is a type of polyethylene substrate used in biodegradation studies with mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-reg is a type of polyethylene substrate used in biodegradation studies with mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-reg is a variant used in the study to measure enzyme activity levels, showing significant differences in bacterial communities compared to the control variant.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene regranulate (PE-reg) is a recycled form of polyethylene, which can still contribute to environmental pollution if not properly managed.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-reg is a type of polyethylene used as a diet for mealworms, associated with a unique gut microbiome profile and the degradation of synthetic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-reg is a variant of polyethylene containing ~2% CaO and CaCO3-based pigments, used as a substrate for mealworm larvae in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-reg refers to polyethylene regranulate, a type of plastic used in the study to assess its impact on larvae metabolism and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-reg is a variant used in the study to measure enzyme activity levels, showing significant differences in bacterial communities compared to the control variant.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene regranulate (PE-reg) is a recycled form of polyethylene, which can still contribute to environmental pollution if not properly managed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CEL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CEL is a diet variant used in the study, where the gut microbiota of mealworms switched from glucose metabolism to adipic acid consumption and acetoin production.&lt;SEP&gt;CEL is a substrate used in biodegradation studies with mealworm larvae, representing cellulose-based materials.&lt;SEP&gt;CEL is a variant used in the study to measure enzyme activity levels, showing significant differences in bacterial communities compared to the control variant.&lt;SEP&gt;CEL refers to cardboard, a diet used in the study to compare the effects of different diets on larvae metabolism and protein content.&lt;SEP&gt;CEL is a substrate used in biodegradation studies with mealworm larvae, representing cellulose-based materials.&lt;SEP&gt;CEL is a variant used in the study to measure enzyme activity levels, showing significant differences in bacterial communities compared to the control variant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64</data>
    </node>
