    <node id="BACTEROIDETES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteroidetes are bacteria found in the digestive tract of termites, responsible for cellulose degradation and nutrient supply.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with proportions influenced by the type of substrate fed.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a phylum of bacteria that includes Bacteroides, which play a role in the breakdown of complex organic compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a phylum of bacteria in the mealworm gut microbiome, with lower abundance in the PU diet compared to the bran diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum present in the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus, contributing to the microbial diversity and biodegradation activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum well-represented in control (CT) samples but nearly absent in polystyrene (PS) samples, indicating a potential shift in microbial communities due to polystyrene consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a phylum in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, with increased abundances observed in PP- and EVA-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a phylum present in the gut bacterial communities of yellow mealworms, particularly in the EVA-fed group.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a phylum of bacteria that is part of the gut microbial community in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes are bacteria responsible for nutrient cycling and fermentation in the digestive tracts of insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with proportions influenced by the type of substrate fed.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a phylum of bacteria that includes Bacteroides, which play a role in the breakdown of complex organic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VERRUCOMICROBIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Verrucomicrobia is a bacterial phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, showing increased proportions in the polystyrene-fed variant.&lt;SEP&gt;Verrucomicrobia is a bacterial phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, showing increased proportions in the polystyrene-fed variant.&lt;SEP&gt;Verrucomicrobia is a less abundant phylum identified in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed polystyrene diets, indicating its role in nitrogen assimilation.&lt;SEP&gt;Verrucomicrobia is a less abundant bacterial phylum in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed polystyrene diets, contributing to nitrogen assimilation.</data>
