    </node>
    <node id="BACILLI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacilli is a bacterial class identified in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus, playing a role in the degradation of organic materials like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacilli is a bacterial class in the gut microbiome of mealworms, showing higher proportions in oxo-degradable polyethylene and polystyrene-fed variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacilli is a class of aerobic bacteria within the Firmicutes phylum, found in the gut microbiota of mealworms and involved in polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacilli is a class of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacilli is a bacterial class in the gut microbiome of mealworms, showing higher proportions in oxo-degradable polyethylene and polystyrene-fed variants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAMMAPROTEOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gammaproteobacteria are microorganisms that can degrade lignocellulose and petroleum hydrocarbons, and are present in the gut of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a bacterial class in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with higher proportions in polyethylene-fed variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a class of bacteria found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, playing a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a class of bacteria enriched in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, indicating its potential role in PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a class of bacteria that includes the family Erwiniaeae and the genus Mixta, to which strain BIT-26 belongs.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a class of bacteria that includes Pseudomonas and Cronobacter, which were overrepresented in PS samples, suggesting their potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a predominant bacterial class in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus, significantly involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a class of bacteria that includes several families such as Enterobacteriaceae, Yersiniaceae, Aeromonadaceae, Pseudomonadaceae, and Moraxellaceae, identified in the study for their potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a class of bacteria that includes several strains isolated from mealworms, which are capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a bacterial class in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with higher proportions in polyethylene-fed variants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARTHROBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Arthrobacter is a bacterial genus in the gut microbiome of mealworms, present in all feeding variants but with varying proportions.&lt;SEP&gt;Arthrobacter is a bacterial genus in the gut microbiome of mealworms, present in all feeding variants but with varying proportions.&lt;SEP&gt;Arthrobacter is a genus of Actinobacteria known for its role in the degradation of organic materials, including plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Arthrobacter is a genus of Actinobacteria known for its role in the degradation of organic materials, including plastics.</data>
