    <node id="BACTERIAL COMMUNITIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacterial communities are groups of bacteria, such as Pseudomonadaceae and Enterobacteriaceae, that play a significant role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers within insect guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacterial communities isolated from larvae were studied to measure enzyme activity levels, showing variations across different diets and experimental conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacterial communities isolated from larvae were studied to measure enzyme activity levels, showing variations across different diets and experimental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae are organisms used in the study to assess the degradation of polystyrene and their survival under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae are the immature stage of Tenebrio molitor, which are studied for their growth patterns and responses to PS diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae are the primary organisms studied, fed with polystyrene, oatmeal, and vitamins to observe their growth and development under different conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae refer to the juvenile stage of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), which are studied for their ability to survive and grow under different feeding conditions, including polystyrene consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae were used in the study to measure enzyme activity levels in their digestive tracts and bacterial communities across different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae, specifically the immature forms of insects such as Tenebrio molitor (mealworms), Zophobas atratus (superworms), and yellow mealworms, are widely used in biodegradation studies due to their ability to consume and degrade various types of plastics, including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polylactic acid (PLA), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). These larvae are studied for their physiological responses, biodegradation rates, and the role of their gut microbiomes in breaking down plastics. Key physiological parameters assessed include survival rates, pupation rates, cannibal rates, enzyme activity levels in their digestive tracts, and the composition of their gut bacterial communities. Additionally, the effects of different diets—such as polystyrene foam, wheat bran, dried whey, corn straw, and potatoes—on their metabolism, protein content, fatty acid composition, and reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels are analyzed. The larvae's ability to degrade plastics is closely linked to their gut metabolites and the presence of plastic-degrading bacteria. Studies also investigate how different feedstocks influence their growth, mortality, and development, providing insights into their nutritional and biodegradation capabilities. Overall, larvae serve as valuable model organisms for understanding the biodegradation of plastics and the physiological impacts of various diets on their development and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a706f2bd66291a3ef925114ffd9300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </node>
