    </node>
    <node id="ESTERASE-PRODUCING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Esterase-producing bacteria, such as Pseudomonas, Enterococcus, Bacillus, and Pantoea agglomerans, are known to degrade materials like polystyrene and are found in the digestive tracts of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDIVIDUAL ENZYME ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Individual enzyme activity refers to the specific actions of enzymes like esterases and lipases in the degradation of materials like polystyrene in the digestive tracts of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEPOLYMERIZING ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Depolymerizing enzymes are enzymes that break down polymers like polystyrene, and their activity is often accompanied by high levels of phosphatase activity in the digestive tracts of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIERARCHICAL CLUSTER ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Hierarchical cluster analysis is a method used to study the similarities in enzymatic activity and bacterial community structure in the digestive tracts of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Hierarchical cluster analysis is used to group mealworm gut microbiome samples based on similarities in microbial composition, revealing patterns related to diet and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHIZOBIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhizobiales are bacteria found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polyethylene-based diets, and may play a role in synthetic waste degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhizobiales is an order of bacteria found in the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor, involved in nitrogen fixation and symbiotic relationships with the larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhizobiales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PE-reg diet group, indicating complex aerobic and anaerobic processes required for polymer degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhizobiales is an order of bacteria that is most abundant in the PE-reg group of the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhizobiales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 8.6% in the gut microbiome, showing the highest levels in the PE-reg group.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhizobiales is an order of bacteria found in the digestive tract of mealworms, involved in nitrogen fixation and symbiotic interactions with the larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROSPIRA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrosopira sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor, involved in nitrification and nitrogen cycling.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrosopira sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the digestive tract of mealworms, contributing to nitrification and nitrogen cycling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROSOMONAS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrosomonas sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor, involved in nitrification and nitrogen cycling.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrosomonas sp. is a genus of bacteria involved in nitrification processes within the digestive tract of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN FIXATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen fixation is a biological process in which atmospheric nitrogen (N₂) is converted into ammonia (NH₃) or other nitrogen-containing compounds that are biologically usable. This process is primarily facilitated by nitrogen-fixing bacteria, which often carry the nifH gene. In the context of Tenebrio molitor (commonly known as mealworms), nitrogen fixation is carried out by gut microbes, particularly when the larvae are fed nitrogen-deficient diets, such as polypropylene (PP), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), or polyethylene (PE). These gut bacteria, including species like Pantoee sp., convert atmospheric nitrogen into amino acids and other nitrogenous compounds, which support the larvae's growth, enzyme synthesis, and metabolic processes, especially during the biodegradation of plastics.

The nitrogen fixation process in Tenebrio molitor is particularly significant when the larvae are fed plastic-only diets, as it provides a supplementary source of nitrogen that would otherwise be lacking. However, this nitrogen supply is limited compared to what the larvae would obtain from normal, nitrogen-rich diets. The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor plays a crucial role in this process, enabling the larvae to survive and grow even under suboptimal nutritional conditions. This capability has been studied extensively in the context of plastic-eating mealworms, highlighting their unique metabolic adaptations and the potential applications of their gut microbiome in biodegradation research.

In summary, nitrogen fixation in Tenebrio molitor is a vital process mediated by gut bacteria, allowing the larvae to thrive on plastic diets by converting atmospheric nitrogen into usable forms, albeit in limited quantities. This process underscores the intricate relationship between the larvae and their gut microbiome, as well as their remarkable ability to adapt to challenging environmental conditions. The process is particularly stimulated during the biodegradation of polyethylene polymers, where the gut microbiota helps maintain nitrogen nutrition in nitrogen-deficient environments. This adaptation is potentially linked to the mealworms' plastic-degrading capabilities, making nitrogen fixation a key area of study in understanding their metabolic functions and potential applications in biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen fixation is a process studied in plastic-eating mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and their diazotrophic communities, which may play a role in their ability to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen fixation is a process facilitated by bacteria in the digestive tract of mealworms, allowing them to utilize atmospheric nitrogen for survival and growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
