    </node>
    <node id="PLUTELLA XYLOSTELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plutella xylostella, commonly known as the diamondback moth, is an insect whose gut bacterial flora has been studied for its role in carboxylesterase and esterase activity, potentially aiding in indoxylcarb degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLESTERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylesterase is an enzyme detected in the gut bacterial flora of Plutella xylostella, which may play a role in the degradation of indoxylcarb.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme involved in drug metabolism, showing differential expression in plastic-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of carboxylic esters, playing a role in the metabolism of plastics and other xenobiotics in organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme with a higher abundance in PP-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, suggesting its involvement in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of ester bonds, found in PS-/CS-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme that cleaves ester bonds and facilitates the surface modification of PET, and is overexpressed in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme that is highly active in degrading PET oligomers, playing a key role in the breakdown of PET polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme that is highly active toward PET oligomers, contributing to the breakdown of PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme that cleaves ester bonds and facilitates the surface modification of PET, and is overexpressed in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme that is highly active in degrading PET oligomers, playing a key role in the breakdown of PET polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylesterase is an enzyme that is highly active toward PET oligomers, contributing to the breakdown of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDOLYLCARB">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Indoxylcarb is a chemical compound that may be degraded by enzymes such as carboxylesterase found in the gut bacterial flora of Plutella xylostella.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM BEETLE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The mealworm beetle (Tenebrio molitor) is a beneficial pest studied for its immune defenses and its role in biodegrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The mealworm beetle (Tenebrio molitor) is an insect known for its immune defenses and role in biodegradation studies, particularly in breaking down polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The mealworm beetle (Tenebrio molitor) is an insect studied for its immune defenses and potential applications in food and environmental science.&lt;SEP&gt;The mealworm beetle, Tenebrio molitor, is studied for its immune defenses and its role in biodegradation processes.</data>
