    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of consuming polystyrene (PS) as food, aided by bacteria in their digestive tract, but PS alone cannot meet their nutritional needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are the immature form of the mealworm, known for their ability to host gut bacteria that degrade polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, or mealworms, are studied for their potential as a novel food source and their gut microbiota's role in digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, also known as yellow mealworms, are used in studies to investigate their ability to degrade polyethylene through their gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are plastic-degrading insects that can utilize polypropylene (PP) as a carbon source, with their gut functioning as a bioreactor for plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms, are plastic-degrading macroinvertebrates that can biodegrade PVC, PS, and PLA microplastics, though ingestion of these plastics can cause physiological stress and oxidative damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms, are plastic-degrading macroinvertebrates that can digest and biodegrade certain types of plastics, such as PLA, PS, and PVC.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, also known as mealworms, are studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene and their potential in sustainable farming.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms, are used in the study to assess the impact of different feeds, including polystyrene, on their growth and nutritional content.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, also known as yellow mealworms, are capable of degrading aged polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) microplastics, making them a potential solution for microplastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms, are capable of consuming and degrading microplastics such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
