    <node id="MICROBIAL COMMUNITIES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial communities are groups of microorganisms that interact in environments such as soil or the gut, playing a role in biodegradation and hydrogen production.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbiome quantification provides insights into the abundance and diversity of microbial communities in environmental samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial communities from various environmental sources, such as soil and seawater, are used to study the biodegradation of plastics like PE and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial communities in the gut of yellow mealworms are responsible for plastic degradation, with their structure and function influencing the process.</data>
