    </node>
    <node id="FOOD SAFETY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
      <data key="d1">**Food Safety** encompasses a wide range of practices, research, and regulations aimed at ensuring the safety and suitability of food products for human and animal consumption. A significant focus within this field is the evaluation of alternative protein sources, particularly edible insects such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), and their potential use in human diets and livestock feed. Studies assess the safety and nutritional value of these insects, especially when they are fed unconventional substrates like Styrofoam or polystyrene, which raises concerns about potential health risks. Researchers investigate whether insects that degrade plastics, such as Styrofoam, are safe for consumption, ensuring they are non-toxic and free from harmful byproducts like microplastics or chemical contaminants.

Food safety also involves evaluating the safety of novel food products, such as TMlp, for use in livestock feed, ensuring they meet safety standards. The immune defenses of mealworm beetles are studied for their potential applications in food safety and pest control, further highlighting the interconnectedness of biodegradation and food safety. Concerns about the migration of harmful chemicals from plastics into food sources, as well as the potential contamination of food by pollutants derived from plastic waste, are also addressed within the scope of food safety.

A specific concern within food safety is the assimilation of plastic carbon into insect biomass, which is used as a protein source for food and animal feed. This raises questions about the long-term safety and potential health risks associated with consuming insects that have been exposed to or have metabolized plastics. Additionally, food safety considerations are relevant when studying the use of insects like *Tenebrio molitor* as potential protein sources or in plastic biodegradation research. When mealworms are bred on synthetic polymer diets, the resulting products must be tested for potentially harmful substances to ensure they are safe for consumption.

The research indirectly relates to food safety by exploring the potential of mealworms to degrade plastic waste, which could reduce environmental contamination in food systems. This highlights the dual role of mealworms in both addressing plastic pollution and serving as a potential food source, provided that safety concerns are adequately addressed. Food safety concerns also arise from the indirect consumption of plastics by humans through contaminated food sources, emphasizing the need for rigorous testing and regulation.

Overall, food safety is a critical consideration in the development and regulation of alternative protein sources, ensuring that practices and products, including edible insects and their byproducts, are safe for both human and animal consumption. This includes addressing challenges related to biodegradation, contamination, and the nutritional</data>
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Priestia megaterium S1 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, studied for its role in PS bead biodegradation over 180 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Priestia megaterium S1 is a gut microbe isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae that shows significant potential in degrading polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) beads.&lt;SEP&gt;Priestia megaterium S1 is a microbial strain that exhibits biodegradation capabilities for LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads, confirmed by physical and structural changes over 180 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Priestia megaterium S1 is a polymer-degrading gut microbe isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BARLEY BRAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Barley bran is a component of the diet provided to Tenebrio molitor larvae, containing protein, starch, fat, fibers, and ash.&lt;SEP&gt;Barley bran is a feed material used in the study to compare the survival and biodegradation capabilities of Tenebrio molitor larvae when fed with or without Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Barley bran is a protein and lipid-rich feed used to nourish mealworms in controlled experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Barley bran was used as a dietary supplement for mealworms in the study, providing essential nutrients for their growth and development.&lt;SEP&gt;Barley bran is a feeding supplement used in studies to enhance the growth rate and polystyrene (PS) consumption yields of mealworms, contributing to their survival and growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHOBICITY PERCENTAGE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrophobicity percentage is a measure used to assess the ability of gut microbes like Priestia megaterium S1 to adhere to and degrade hydrophobic materials such as plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLEAR ZONE ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The clear zone assay is a method used to detect the ability of mealworm gut bacteria to degrade plastics by observing zones of clearance around bacterial colonies.&lt;SEP&gt;The clear zone assay is a method used to evaluate the biodegradation potential of gut microbes by measuring the zone of clearance around colonies on plastic-containing media.&lt;SEP&gt;The clear zone assay is a method used to screen bacterial strains for their ability to degrade polymers like LDPE, HDPE, and PS by forming zones of clearance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBIDITY MEASUREMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Turbidity measurement is a technique used to assess microbial growth and activity, particularly in the context of plastic degradation by Priestia megaterium S1.&lt;SEP&gt;Turbidity measurement is used to assess the utilization of plastics as a carbon source by mealworm gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA CHARACTERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA characterization is a molecular technique used to identify and classify bacterial isolates, such as Priestia megaterium S1, based on their genetic sequence.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HDPE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE) is a widely used plastic material known for its durability, resistance to degradation, and high crystallinity due to its long linear chains and minimal branching. As a high molecular weight polyethylene polymer, HDPE is more stable and less biodegradable compared to other polyethylene types such as Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) and Linear Low-Density Polyethylene (LLDPE). Its high crystallinity contributes to its resistance to degradation in natural environments, making it a significant contributor to global plastic pollution.

Despite its resistance to biodegradation, HDPE has been the subject of extensive research into its biodegradation potential. Studies have shown that certain insect larvae, including mealworms (Tenebrio species, such as T. molitor and T. obscurus), lesser waxworms (Achroia grisella), and Uloma larvae, can biodegrade HDPE. This biodegradation process involves observable changes such as weight loss, surface erosion, and biofilm formation. The gut microbiota of these larvae, including bacteria like Priestia megaterium S1 (PM S1) and Achromobacter xylosoxidans, adapts to enhance carbon and nitrogen metabolism during the degradation of HDPE. However, the degradation of HDPE by mealworms is less efficient compared to LDPE and can cause higher degrees of physiological and metabolic impairments in the larvae. Additionally, the process alters the gut microbial diversity of Tenebrio species, impacting their gut microbial communities.

While the digestion and effects of HDPE on mealworm metabolism are less understood compared to other plastics like polystyrene, the ability of mealworms, lesser waxworms, and Uloma larvae to partially degrade HDPE offers promising insights into microbial biodegradation pathways. These findings highlight the potential for using insect larvae and their gut microbiota as a solution to mitigate plastic waste. Overall, HDPE remains a significant environmental challenge, but ongoing research into its biodegradation provides hope for innovative approaches to reducing its impact on the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOGICAL OXYGEN DEMAND (BOD)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biological Oxygen Demand (BOD) is a measure of the amount of oxygen consumed by microorganisms during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating the extent of degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO2 EVOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CO2 evolution is a metric used to quantify the mineralization of plastics into carbon dioxide during biodegradation by Priestia megaterium S1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR is a technique used to characterize the functional groups of polystyrene and frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is a versatile analytical technique widely used to study chemical changes in materials, particularly in the context of biodegradation. It is employed to identify and characterize changes in functional groups and molecular structures within polymers, such as polystyrene, polyethylene (PE), polyurethane, and Styrofoam, as well as in their degradation products. FTIR analysis is particularly valuable in assessing the biodegradation of plastics by organisms like mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), lesser waxworms, and bacteria such as Priestia megaterium S1. 

The technique is used to detect chemical modifications on the surface of plastic beads, analyze the chemical composition of frass (excreted material) from organisms fed with plastics, and confirm the biodegradation process by identifying changes in functional groups. For example, FTIR has been applied to study the depolymerization and oxidation of polystyrene, the degradation of polyethylene films, and the chemical structure of residual polymers in frass. It also provides evidence of plastic degradation by analyzing the formation of new functional groups and changes in chemical bonds during biodegradation. 

In summary, FTIR is a critical tool for understanding the chemical and structural changes in plastics during biodegradation, offering insights into the mechanisms and extent of degradation by various organisms and environmental processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to analyze the chemical structure of polypropylene (PP) foam and frass samples to assess biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to analyze chemical changes and the formation of new functional groups in polymers after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is a technique used to analyze changes in the functional groups of microplastics (MPs) after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to analyze the chemical properties and changes in microplastics during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPE (SEM)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A scanning electron microscope (SEM) is used to analyze changes in the surface of polystyrene films caused by bacterial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A scanning electron microscope (SEM) is used to analyze the residual plastics in frass samples, providing detailed images of the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to visualize physical changes on the surface of plastic beads, providing evidence of biodegradation by Priestia megaterium S1.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscope (SEM) is a tool used to observe the physical changes on the surface of polyethylene (PE) films during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A scanning electron microscope (SEM) is used to observe and analyze changes in material surfaces, such as PS sheets, due to microbial growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was used to observe the surface modifications of polystyrene sheets caused by bacterial isolates from Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to observe the morphology and composition of residual polymers in the frass of yellow mealworms fed with surgical masks.&lt;SEP&gt;The SEM is used to observe the surface of freeze-dried mealworm intestines coated with palladium.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRICARBOXYLIC ACID (TCA) CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The TCA cycle is a metabolic pathway involved in the biodegradation process, producing energy and enhancing the metabolic activities of Priestia megaterium S1.&lt;SEP&gt;The TCA cycle is a metabolic pathway that processes intermediate compounds from styrene degradation, leading to the production of CO2 and H2O.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low density polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by specific bacterial consortia isolated from plastic garbage processing areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Low density polyethylene (LDPE) is another plastic polymer that can be degraded using protocols similar to those for polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of polyethylene with a lower density, commonly used in packaging and other applications. It is known for its slow degradation in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a widely used plastic that is resistant to degradation and contributes to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a synthetic polymer widely used in plastic products and is a target for biodegradation studies due to its environmental persistence.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High density polyethylene (HDPE) is a plastic polymer that can be degraded using similar protocols as those for polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of polyethylene with a higher density, used in various applications. It is also resistant to environmental degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR (TM)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as yellow mealworms, are edible insects capable of biodegrading plastics like polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE) due to their diverse gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRISTIA MEGATERIUM (PM) S1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pristia megaterium (PM) S1 is a gut bacterial strain from Tenebrio molitor larvae that plays a role in the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM (SF)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam (SF) is a type of expanded polystyrene (PS) foam widely used in packaging and insulation. It is a significant environmental pollutant due to its slow biodegradation rate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HONEYBEES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Honeybees are insects that have been studied for their potential role in biodegrading certain types of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOCELLULOSE MATRIX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The lignocellulose matrix is a complex structure found in plant cell walls. It is also present in some plastic waste, making it a target for biodegradation by certain organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHITIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chitin is a component found in the frass of mealworms and superworms, contributing to its potential as a plant growth medium.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitin is a compound found in superworm frass that has been reported to support plant rooting.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitin is a natural polymer found in the exoskeletons of insects and crustaceans. It is a byproduct of insect biomass and has commercial applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitin is a nitrogen-rich component found in the frass of mealworms and superworms, contributing to its use as a natural fertilizer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitin is a polysaccharide found in the exoskeletons of insects, such as mealworms, and is used in various applications, including as a raw material for chitosan production.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitin is a component of the insect cuticle, detected in the frass of superworms during chemical analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitin is a component of the insect cuticle, detected in the fecula of mealworms, indicating its presence as a byproduct of digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitin is a component of mealworm skeletons, ingested by starvation mealworms, and may influence the gut microbial community during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHITOSAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chitosan is a biopolymer derived from chitin, which can be produced from Tenebrio molitor and used in UV-protective packaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitosan is a biopolymer derived from insects like Tenebrio molitor and Brachystola magna, used in various applications including film production.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitosan is a derivative of chitin, used in various industries such as biomaterials, food, and agriculture. It is produced from insect biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;Chitosan is a derivative of chitin, commonly extracted from insect exoskeletons, and is used in biomedical and agricultural applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIRCULAR ECONOMY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The circular economy concept focuses on sustainable practices, such as using insects like mealworms to valorize food waste and degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The circular economy is an economic model aimed at minimizing waste and making the most of resources. It is relevant to the biodegradation of plastics and the utilization of insect biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;The circular economy is a sustainable approach aimed at minimizing waste and maximizing resource efficiency, relevant to construction and demolition waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;The circular economy is an economic model focused on minimizing waste and maximizing resource efficiency, often applied to construction and demolition waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm (MW) is a larval model from the Coleoptera order, extensively studied for its ability to biodegrade plastics and its potential in micro- and nanoplastic toxicity studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm (MW) is an insect species studied for its response to plastic exposure, including changes in larval weight and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm (MW) larvae are metabolically active on certain plastics like PLA and expanded foam, indicating potential for plastic biodegradation and protein production.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm (Tenebrio molitor larvae) is an alternative protein source for monogastric animals and has been studied for its potential in biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are larvae that can consume synthetic polymers like polystyrene and polyethylene, and their survival and growth are influenced by their diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are larvae that can ingest and degrade microplastics, and their growth, survival, and metamorphosis are affected by microplastic exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are traditional high-protein feed for poultry and fish, capable of ingesting and depolymerizing microplastics, but their growth and survival can be affected by microplastic consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms, identified as *TM Linnaeus*, are larvae that feed on dead insects, vegetables, fruits, and grains, and are used in studies to assess their ability to consume and degrade plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms, larvae of Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus, are used in studies to investigate their ability to degrade polystyrene and the role of their gut microbiome in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (MW) are insect larvae that exhibit metabolic changes when exposed to plastic substrates, showing upregulated pathways such as starch and sucrose metabolism, pentose phosphate pathway, and purine metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are insects that are often studied for their role in biodegradation and their association with bacterial strains like BIT-26.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are larvae of Tenebrio molitor, known for their ability to consume and biodegrade plastics, and their gut microbiome plays a significant role in this process.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms, including Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscura, are insects known for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene and other plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MATH TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The MATH test is a method used to assess the hydrophobicity of bacterial cell surfaces, which is relevant to their ability to degrade hydrophobic plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SF">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SF refers to Styrofoam, a type of expanded polystyrene used as feed for mealworms in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETOH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethanol (EtOH) is used to sterilize the outer layer of mealworm larvae before isolating their gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT BROTH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient broth is a growth medium used to culture and isolate gut bacteria from mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrient broth is a growth medium used to culture mealworm gut bacteria and assess their ability to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrient broth was used to culture bacterial isolates for the degradation analysis of plastics in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient Agar is a general-purpose medium used for the cultivation of microorganisms, prepared similarly to Blood Agar but without the addition of blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrient agar is a solid growth medium used to culture and isolate bacterial strains from mealworm guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COOMASSIE BLUE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Coomassie blue is a dye used in the clear zone assay to detect plastic degradation by mealworm gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETONE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetone is used in the clear zone assay for decolorization after applying Coomassie blue to detect plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOLUENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Toluene is a hydrocarbon used in the MATH test to assess the hydrophobicity of bacterial cell surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Toluene is a toxic compound detected in polystyrene samples, potentially derived from plastic additives.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM PUPAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms collected during experiments to monitor survival rates and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEAD LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dead larvae are collected during experiments to prevent surviving mealworms from consuming them, ensuring accurate survival rate measurements.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL ISOLATES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacterial isolates (S1, S2, S3, S4, S5) are strains of gut bacteria from mealworms tested for their ability to degrade plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECTROPHOTOMETER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A spectrophotometer is an instrument used to measure the absorbance of light by a sample, often used to quantify degradation products at 260 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;A spectrophotometer is used to measure turbidity and absorbance in experiments assessing bacterial growth and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival rate (SR) is a metric used in biodegradation studies to determine the percentage of larvae that survive under specific experimental conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a metric used in research studies to evaluate the viability of mealworms and superworms under different feeding conditions, including plastic-only diets and co-diets with wheat bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate (SR) is a metric used to track the survival of mealworms over time when fed with plastics like SF and barley bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate refers to the percentage of mealworms or greater wax moth larvae that survive during experiments involving the degradation of plastic substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;The survival rate of superworms is measured to assess the impact of Styrofoam consumption on their health compared to a normal diet.&lt;SEP&gt;The survival rate of Tenebrio molitor larvae was assessed to determine the effects of feeding them polystyrene, with or without supplementary food like carrots.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate is a metric used to evaluate the health and viability of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different feeding conditions and antibiotic treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate is a metric used in the study to assess the impact of plastic consumption on the health and viability of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate is a metric used to evaluate the health and viability of insect larvae during polystyrene (PS) biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate is a measure used to assess the impact of different diets, such as polystyrene, on the longevity and health of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rate is a metric used to evaluate the effects of different feeding conditions on the longevity and health of Tenebrio molitor larvae during PS degradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELL SURFACE HYDROPHOBICITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cell surface hydrophobicity (CSH) is a measure of bacterial affinity for hydrocarbons, assessed using the MATH test.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteria extracted from the mealworm gut are studied for their ability to degrade nanoplastics and produce enzymes like proteases.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm gut bacteria are microorganisms found in the digestive tract of mealworms, capable of biodegrading plastics such as LDPE, HDPE, and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm gut bacteria are microorganisms that play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics such as LDPE, HDPE, and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm gut bacteria, such as Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2, play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm gut bacteria play a crucial role in the biodegradation of EPS, converting it into carbon dioxide and biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm gut bacteria play a crucial role in the biodegradation of EPS, converting it into carbon dioxide and biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLONIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Colonization refers to the process by which mealworm gut bacteria form clusters on plastic beads, leading to their degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT LOSS STUDIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight loss studies measure the percentage of plastic weight lost due to biodegradation by mealworm gut bacteria over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHOBICITY MEASUREMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrophobicity measurement assesses changes in the surface properties of plastics after biodegradation by mealworm gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) analysis is used to observe the surface breakdown of plastic films, such as low-density polyethylene (LDPE), during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) analysis is used to study the surface morphology of HDPE films and the biofilm formation by bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) analysis is used to visualize surface alterations of plastics caused by biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM analysis examines surface structural changes in plastics after microbial degradation by mealworm gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM analysis is used to observe structural changes on the surface of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads after treatment with PM S1, confirming biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM analysis was conducted to examine changes in the surface structure of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads after microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is used to examine the physical changes in polyurethane surfaces during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM analysis is performed to study the surface morphology of polystyrene and corn straw after being processed by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM analysis is used to observe physical changes on the surface of polystyrene films after incubation with bacterial strains, such as the formation of pits and cavities.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) analysis was used to observe and compare the morphological differences in frass from yellow mealworms fed with different mask layers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COOMASSIE BLUE SOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Coomassie blue solution is used to detect zones of clearing on plates, indicating the biodegradation potential of mealworm gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SODIUM DODECYL SULPHITE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium dodecyl sulphite is a chemical used to clean plastic beads after biodegradation experiments to remove bacterial cells and debris.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKALINE POTASSIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkaline potassium is used in the iodimetric method for measuring biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELEMENTAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Elemental analysis is used to determine the carbon, hydrogen, and sulfur content in plastics after biodegradation by mealworm gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Elemental analysis is used to determine the percentage of carbon and hydrogen in plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS after degradation by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOFILM FORMATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biofilm formation by bacteria on polystyrene surfaces is a key step in initiating biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation by bacterial strains on polyethylene (PE) surfaces is a critical step in the biodegradation process, leading to surface cracks and oxidation.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation is a process where PM S1 bacteria colonize the surface of plastic beads, aiding in their degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation refers to the ability of mealworm gut bacteria to form clusters on plastic surfaces, aiding in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation was observed on the surfaces of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads, indicating microbial colonization and degradation activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation refers to the development of microbial communities on surfaces, such as polystyrene films, which play a role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation refers to the growth of bacterial communities on the surface of PE mulching films, which aids in the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation is a process where bacteria like Bacillus sp. PELW2042 adhere to and colonize surfaces, facilitating the degradation of materials like HDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation by gut bacteria on polystyrene surfaces is a key step in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation is a process where microorganisms adhere to surfaces and form protective communities, playing a role in biodegradation and environmental persistence.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation is the process by which bacteria, such as Rhodococcus ruber, create structured communities on surfaces, aiding in the degradation of materials like polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation refers to the ability of bacteria to form a structured community on surfaces, which can play a role in the degradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation is a crucial step in the biodegradation of plastics, where microorganisms like LG3 form stable communities to degrade materials such as polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation is a process by which bacteria, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, attach to surfaces like polystyrene (PS) and degrade it.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL OXYGEN DEMAND (BOD)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) measures the amount of oxygen consumed by microorganisms during the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL CONDUCTIVITY DETECTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal conductivity detection is used to monitor nitrogen levels during elemental analysis of degraded plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IR DETECTOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">IR detectors are used to measure carbon dioxide and other gases released during the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGILENT TECHNOLOGIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Agilent technologies are used for FTIR spectroscopy to analyze chemical changes in plastics before and after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CENTRALISED INSTRUMENTATION AND SERVICE LABORATORY (CISL)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">CISL is a facility where SEM analyses are conducted to study surface changes in plastics after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STURM TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Sturm test measures carbon dioxide production to confirm the biodegradation of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads by PM S1.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sturm test was used to measure CO2 evolution as an indicator of plastic biodegradation, with modifications made to the test flask setup.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PM S1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PM S1 is a bacterial isolate from mealworm guts, identified as Bacillus-related, capable of degrading PE, HDPE, and PS by utilizing them as a carbon source.&lt;SEP&gt;PM S1 is a microbial strain, specifically Priestia megaterium S1, that exhibits biodegradation capabilities for LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads, confirmed by physical and structural changes over 180 days.&lt;SEP&gt;PM S1 refers to a microbial isolate that colonized LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads, contributing to their degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON SOURCES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon sources were used in the study to analyze the survival of PM (microbial isolates) over different concentrations, indicating their role in microbial activity and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PM refers to microbial isolates used in the study to analyze their ability to degrade plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSM BROTH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MSM broth (Minimal Salt Medium) was used to culture bacterial isolates for the degradation analysis of plastics in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KOH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">KOH (potassium hydroxide) is used in microbial digestion to isolate nanoplastics without altering their chemical structure.&lt;SEP&gt;KOH (potassium hydroxide) is used to digest bacterial growth during the extraction process, aiding in the isolation of degradation products.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium hydroxide (KOH) is used to adjust the pH of polymer emulsions during their preparation.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium hydroxide (KOH) was used in the Sturm test to absorb CO2 generated from the breakdown of polymeric chains during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium hydroxide (KOH) is used in the digestion process to analyze residual plastics in mealworm intestines.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BARIUM CHLORIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Barium chloride (BaCl2) was used in the Sturm test to precipitate CO2, allowing for gravimetric determination of CO2 evolution during plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EROSION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Erosion of plastic beads (LDPE, HDPE, and PS) was observed after microbial degradation, indicating physical changes due to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S1 ISOLATE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">S1 isolate is a bacterial strain from mealworm gut bacteria that shows effective growth and biodegradation capabilities for LDPE, HDPE, and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHOBICITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrophobicity is a property of polymers like PET that affects their degradation rate by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher hydrophobicity leading to slower degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrophobicity is a property that influences bacterial adherence to surfaces and is critical in the biodegradation of non-polar polymers like LDPE, HDPE, and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrophobicity refers to the property of repelling water, which is measured in the supernatant fractions of the gut microbiome to understand their role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrophobicity refers to the property of repelling water, which is relevant in the context of polystyrene biodegradation, as emulsifying agents reduce hydrophobicity to enhance degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrophobicity refers to the water-repellent nature of microplastics, which decreases with aging due to UV radiation, facilitating microbial adhesion and degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT AGAR MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient Agar Medium is a general-purpose medium used for the cultivation of microorganisms, prepared similarly to Blood Agar.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrient agar medium is a growth medium used to culture bacteria, including those involved in the biodegradation of plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXO-ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Exo-enzymes are extracellular enzymes produced by bacteria that break down polymers like LDPE, HDPE, and PS, leading to their biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXADECANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexadecane is a chemical compound with the formula C16H34.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexadecane is a hydrocarbon excreted by mealworms during the metabolism of PS-containing diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexadecane is a hydrocarbon used in experiments to measure the hydrophobicity of bacterial cells, which is critical for their adherence to plastic surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOFILM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biofilm formation by PM S1 bacteria on plastic surfaces aids in the degradation of plastics like PE, HDPE, and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm is a layer of microorganisms that adheres to surfaces, such as plastic beads, and plays a role in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm refers to the layer of microorganisms that attach to the surface of plastics, which can influence biodegradation processes and must be removed for accurate testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation by bacterial strains on PE mulching films is a key step in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm is a structured community of microorganisms that adhere to surfaces, such as polyethylene (PE) films, and contribute to their degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation by bacteria like Bacillus sp. PELW2042 on HDPE surfaces facilitates the degradation of the plastic by increasing microbial colonization and interaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm is a layer of microorganisms that forms on the surface of polystyrene (PS), leading to its deterioration through enzymatic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm is a layer of microorganisms that forms on surfaces, such as polystyrene films, and plays a role in the degradation process by breaking down the material.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm refers to the structured community of bacterial cells adhered to surfaces, which can play a role in the degradation of plastics like nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm refers to the microbial community formed by Enterobacter sp. LG3 on the surface of polystyrene (PS) films, facilitating the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA sequencing is a method used to analyze the microbial diversity in the gut of mealworms, which may play a role in PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is a method used to identify bacterial species involved in plastic biodegradation, such as those isolated from mealworm gut microbiomes.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is a method used to identify bacterial strains associated with plastic degradation by analyzing their genetic material.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is a molecular technique used to identify and classify bacterial isolates, such as those from mealworm guts involved in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is used to identify and confirm the bacterial isolates, such as PM S1, from mealworm guts.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is used to analyze changes in the bacterial community diversity in the guts of superworms and mealworms fed with plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is used to identify bacterial species involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly in microbial enrichments.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is a molecular technique used to identify and classify bacterial strains, such as Klebsiella aerogenes WJ2020 and Cellulosimicrobium funkei WJ2025, based on their genetic similarity.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is a molecular technique used to identify and classify bacterial strains isolated from mealworms based on their genetic sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is a molecular technique used to identify and classify bacterial strains isolated from the gut of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used for the structural characterization of plastic debris in the marine environment, supported by statistical methods for monitoring studies.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze the chemical changes in LDPE during degradation experiments with organisms like the greater wax moth.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of materials, such as LDPE beads, during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze changes in the chemical structure of PS beads after treatment with PM S1, indicating biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is used to identify degradation products, chemical moieties, and the presence of additives in polymers like LDPE, HDPE, and PS after treatment with PM S1.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy is used to characterize chemical changes in polystyrene (PS) films after microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to examine chemical alterations on the surface of residual PVC fragments after mealworm digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical composition of gaseous compounds evolved during the thermal decomposition of materials like Styrofoam and frass.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze the gaseous compounds produced during the thermal decomposition of polystyrene and its degradation products, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to identify chemical changes in synthetic polymers during digestion by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to identify chemical changes in synthetic polymers during digestion by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON-STARVED CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon-starved conditions refer to environments where bacteria adapt by increasing their surface hydrophobicity, enhancing their ability to degrade non-polar polymers like plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZONE OF CLEARANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The zone of clearance is an indicator of bacterial biodegradation activity, where exo-enzymes break down polymers like LDPE, HDPE, and PS, creating a visible clear area.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of polyethylene characterized by its high crystallinity, molecular weight, and strength-to-density ratio, making it a durable and rigid plastic commonly used in various applications such as containers, pipes, and commercial bags. HDPE is known for its chemical inertness and resistance to degradation, which contributes to its widespread use but also to environmental pollution due to its persistence in the environment. 

Despite its resistance to biodegradation, HDPE can be degraded by certain bacterial strains, including *Micrococcus luteus* strain CGR112, *Bacillus cereus*, and *Achromobacter denitrificans*. Additionally, mealworms have been observed to consume HDPE, though at a slower rate compared to other plastics like polystyrene and polypropylene. The consumption of HDPE by mealworms results in visible marks, holes, and significant mass reduction, particularly in the initial stages. However, HDPE is considered one of the least favorable types of plastic for mealworm consumption due to its minimal branching and higher resistance to biodegradation compared to low-density polyethylene (LDPE). 

In summary, HDPE is a highly durable and widely used plastic that poses environmental challenges due to its resistance to degradation. While it can be broken down by specific bacteria and consumed by mealworms, the process is slower and less efficient compared to other plastics, highlighting the need for further research into effective biodegradation methods for HDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus is a bacterial genus strongly associated with the polystyrene diet of greater wax moth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a bacterial genus that decreased in abundance in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, suggesting its reduced role in PVC degradation compared to other genera.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria known for its potential role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria, including PM S1, identified in mealworm guts that can degrade plastics like PE, HDPE, and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria that increased in abundance in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed polystyrene (PS), suggesting a role in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria commonly found in the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae and adults, involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria isolated from the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae and adults, potentially involved in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria that plays a significant role in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) through biofilm formation and enzymatic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria known for its role in plastic and lignocellulose degradation, found in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria that maintains high abundance in the gut microbiome of CS-fed larvae, indicating its potential role in cellulose degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, associated with the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria identified in the gut of mealworms, playing a role in their digestive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria used in studies to investigate the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP) films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a genus of bacteria found in landfill sediments, capable of efficiently decomposing micro-sized polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabacillus is a genus of bacteria related to PM S1, identified in mealworm guts, capable of degrading plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOBACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytobacillus is a genus of bacteria related to PM S1, identified in mealworm guts, capable of degrading plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillaceae is a family of Gram-positive bacteria that was significantly more abundant in polystyrene (PS) samples than in control (CT) samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillaceae is a family of bacteria enriched in the bran-fed mealworm group, suggesting its reduced role in PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillaceae is a family of bacteria that includes PM S1, identified in mealworm guts, capable of degrading plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillaceae is a family of bacteria within the Firmicutes phylum, found in the gut microbiota of mealworms and involved in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillaceae is a family of bacteria that includes Virgibacillus and Pseudogracilibacillus, which were overrepresented in PS samples, suggesting their role in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOD TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) test measures the dissolved oxygen consumed by PM S1 bacteria during the biodegradation of plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT LOSS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight loss in mealworms is measured during the experiment, with significant losses observed in groups fed polystyrene foam, indicating poor nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight loss is a direct measure of the degradation efficiency of polystyrene, indicating the extent to which the material is broken down by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight loss is a key indicator of HDPE biodegradation, reflecting the consumption of polyethylene by microorganisms like strain PELW2042.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight loss is a measure of the degradation of plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS when inoculated with PM S1 bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight loss is a measure of degradation efficiency, indicating the extent to which a material like polystyrene is broken down by microorganisms such as strain YT2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURFACE EROSION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Surface erosion is a process observed during the degradation of LDPE beads when inoculated with PM S1 bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON DIOXIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a byproduct of the biodegradation process of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads by PM S1, indicating mineralization.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide is a byproduct of plastic degradation by yellow mealworms, indicating mineralization of the plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide is a byproduct of the biodegradation of EPS by mealworms, indicating the mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide is a byproduct of the biodegradation of EPS by mealworms, indicating the mineralization of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON AND HYDROGEN ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Elemental analysis of carbon and hydrogen is conducted to determine the percentage loss in LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads after degradation by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOGICAL OXYGEN DEMAND">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biological Oxygen Demand (BOD) studies support the significant weight loss of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads after biodegradation by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAMIL NADU STATE COUNCIL FOR HIGHER EDUCATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Tamil Nadu State Council for Higher Education provided partial funding for the research on the biodegradation of plastics by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a widely used thermoplastic polymer made from the monomer ethylene. It is characterized by its flexibility, durability, and lower crystallinity due to its branched molecular structure, with a molecular weight of less than 10,000. LDPE is partially composed of starch and is commonly employed in applications such as shopping bags, cling films, packaging, and mulching films. Despite its utility, LDPE is a significant contributor to plastic waste and microplastics, posing substantial environmental challenges due to its resistance to biodegradation in natural conditions. This resistance is attributed to its stable C-C and C-H covalent bonds, high molecular weight, and hydrophobic properties.

However, recent studies have shown that LDPE can be biodegraded under certain conditions. Certain microorganisms and insects have demonstrated the ability to degrade LDPE. Microbial species such as *Aspergillus clavatus*, *Pseudomonas* (as studied by Kyaw et al. in 2012), and bacterial strains like PELW2042 have been identified as capable of breaking down LDPE. Additionally, insects such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* and *Zophobas atratus*), the greater wax moth (*Galleria mellonella*), and waxworms (*Corcyra cephalonica*) have shown the ability to ingest and metabolize LDPE, altering its crystalline structure from a crystalline to an amorphous state. Regional variations in digestion efficiency have been observed, with mealworms from Tai'an and Shenzhen showing notable degradation capabilities. Bacteria such as *Pantoea*, present in the gut of *Tenebrio molitor* adults, have also been implicated in LDPE degradation.

The biodegradation process involves complex mechanisms, with some depolymerization occurring independently of gut microbes. However, the degradation efficiency varies depending on the form and density of the plastic, and the process does not provide sufficient nutrition for the growth of these organisms. Despite these advancements, LDPE remains a persistent environmental pollutant due to its slow degradation rate in natural conditions.

Mealworms, in particular, have been studied for their ability to consume LDPE, as evidenced by holes and scratches in plastic bags. However, LDPE is less favorable compared to other plastics like polystyrene and polypropylene, and its consumption by mealworms results in significant mass reduction and lower consumption rates. The lower density of LDPE can influence its biodegradation rate by organisms like *Tenebrio molitor*, further highlighting the importance of density</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS CEREUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus cereus is a bacterial species known for its role in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus cereus is a bacterium studied for its ability to degrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE), demonstrating potential in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus cereus is a bacterium that has been studied for its ability to degrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAMIL NADU STATE COUNCIL FOR HIGHER EDUCATION (TANSCHE)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Tamil Nadu State Council for Higher Education (TANSCHE) provided partial funding for the research work, supporting studies on biodegradation and environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANNAMALAI UNIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Annamalai University, specifically the Department of Microbiology, Faculty of Agriculture, supported the research work on biodegradation and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOWASTES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Biowastes are organic waste materials that can be used for the production of biodegradable bioplastics, contributing to a circular bioeconomy.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE BIOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradable bioplastics are plastics derived from biowastes that can be broken down by microorganisms, offering an eco-friendly alternative to traditional plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROCOCCUS LUTEUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Micrococcus luteus is a bacterium isolated from cow dung that has shown efficient biofilm-forming ability and degradation potential toward high-density polyethylene (HDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Micrococcus luteus strain CGR112 is a bacterium isolated from cow dung that demonstrates efficient biofilm-forming ability and degradation potential toward high-density polyethylene (HDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas species are bacteria capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE), as reported by Kyaw et al. (2012).&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas species are bacteria capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE), contributing to environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas species are bacteria studied for their ability to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas species are bacteria known for their ability to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) in environmental settings.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas species are bacteria known for their ability to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), contributing to biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas species are bacteria known for their ability to degrade low-density polythene, contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas species are bacteria known for their ability to degrade low-density polythene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIRCULAR BIOECONOMY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Circular bioeconomy refers to the sustainable use of biowastes and biodegradable materials to create a closed-loop system, reducing environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAME RETARDANTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flame retardants are chemical additives, such as hexabromocyclododecane, used in plastics to reduce flammability and may affect biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Flame retardants, such as hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD), are chemicals added to plastics that are studied for their environmental impact during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Flame retardants, such as hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD), are chemicals added to plastics to reduce flammability, studied for their environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH IMPACT POLYSTYRENE (HIPS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High Impact Polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by microbial assistance, as studied in biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;High impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic containing additives like decabromodiphenyl oxide and antimony trioxide, which complicate its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic that can be degraded with microbial assistance.&lt;SEP&gt;High impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic containing additives like decabromodiphenyl oxide and antimony trioxide, which can be degraded by specific microbial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;High Impact Polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of polystyrene that has been associated with degradation by esterases produced by Pseudomonas and Bacillus.&lt;SEP&gt;High impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by microbial assistance, as studied by Mohan et al. (2016).&lt;SEP&gt;High impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic containing additives like decabromodiphenyl oxide and antimony trioxide, which can be degraded by certain microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;High impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic containing decabromodiphenyl oxide and antimony trioxide, often used in electronic products and considered an environmental pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORN STRAW">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Corn straw (CS) is a lignocellulosic material consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with partial digestion of its cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin components.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn straw (CS) is a natural lignocellulose material that serves as a diet for mealworms and shares similar degradation pathways with polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn straw is a plant material used in studies to compare the gut microbiome responses of mealworms when fed alongside polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn straw is a plant-based material used as a diet for mealworms in studies comparing its effects on gut microbiome to polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROALGAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microalgae are organisms cultivated in wastewater and used to produce biopolymers, contributing to sustainable material production.&lt;SEP&gt;Microalgae are photosynthetic organisms used in bioenergy production and carbon capture, often enhanced by advanced technologies like graphene oxide quantum dots.&lt;SEP&gt;Microalgae are photosynthetic microorganisms used in bioenergy production and carbon capture, enhanced by ultraviolet spectrum conversion via graphene oxide quantum dots.&lt;SEP&gt;Microalgae are studied for their potential in bioenergy production and carbon capture, with enhancements using graphene oxide quantum dots.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYANOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cyanobacteria are microorganisms cultivated in wastewater and studied for their ability to produce biopolymers, offering eco-friendly alternatives to traditional plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPOLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biopolymers are sustainable materials produced from microalgae and cyanobacteria, offering alternatives to petroleum-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTEWATER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Wastewater is used as a medium for cultivating microalgae and cyanobacteria to produce biopolymers, contributing to environmental sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENTAMICIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gentamicin is a bactericidal antibiotic widely used in research to study the role of gut microbiota in mealworms, particularly in the biodegradation of plastics and nitrogen fixation. It is primarily employed to suppress gut bacteria in mealworm species such as *Tenebrio molitor*, *Tenebrio obscurus*, and *Zophobas atratus*, significantly impacting their ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), Styrofoam, polypropylene (PP), and polyethylene (PE). Gentamicin has been shown to inhibit the depolymerization and mineralization of PS and PP, effectively reducing the mealworms' capacity to degrade these plastics. However, its effect on low-density polyethylene (LDPE) degradation is less pronounced, as it reduces but does not completely stop LDPE depolymerization.

The antibiotic's suppression of gut bacteria, including EPS-degrading bacteria such as *Exiguobacterium* sp. YT2, highlights the critical role these microbes play in the biodegradation process. By inhibiting these bacteria, Gentamicin negatively affects the mealworms' ability to break down plastics, underscoring the importance of gut microbiota in plastic degradation. Gentamicin has been identified as the most effective inhibitor of gut bacteria in mealworms, making it a valuable tool in research aimed at understanding the mechanisms of plastic biodegradation by mealworms and the role of their gut bacteria in this process.

In addition to its role in plastic degradation, Gentamicin is also used to study the impact of gut microbiota on nitrogen fixation in mealworms. The antibiotic's suppression of gut bacteria affects the mealworms' nitrogen-fixing activity, further emphasizing the interconnected roles of gut microbiota in both plastic degradation and nutrient cycling.

Overall, Gentamicin serves as a crucial agent in studies designed to isolate and examine the mealworms' intrinsic capabilities in plastic degradation and nitrogen fixation, independent of their gut microbiome. It effectively reduces the effectiveness of gut bacteria in mealworms, confirming the role of bacteria in EPS, PP, and PE biodegradation. The antibiotic's role in these studies underscores the importance of gut microbiota in the biodegradation of plastics and provides valuable insights into the mechanisms of plastic degradation by mealworms. In summary, Gentamicin is an antibiotic that strongly inhibits the growth of gut flora in *Tenebrio molitor*, *Tenebrio obscurus*, and *Zophobas atratus* larvae, significantly affecting their ability to degrade</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Food waste is a focus of studies exploring its valorization and circular economy concepts in insect production and processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Food waste is a potential feed source for larvae, though its integration with plastic degradation is complicated by the excretion of non-digested microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Food waste is a significant environmental issue, with insect-based bioconversion proposed as a sustainable solution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO(NANO)PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro(nano)plastics are small plastic particles that can affect ecosystem services provided by insects, raising concerns about environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-EATING WAXWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-eating waxworms are insects studied for their ability to degrade polyethylene (PE) plastics, with bacterial strains like Bacillus sp. YP1 and Enterobacter asburiae VT1 isolated from their guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae capable of degrading polyethylene, with their gut bacteria playing a key role in the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STURM-TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Sturm-test is a method used to investigate the biodegradation of plastics in laboratory settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT BALANCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient balancing studies analyze the dietary requirements and growth performance of mealworms under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS REARING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass rearing studies explore optimal conditions for breeding mealworms on a large scale, particularly for use as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGAL DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungal degradation refers to the breakdown of materials like polystyrene foam by fungi, as studied using FTIR spectroscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungal degradation refers to the breakdown of petroleum-based polymers, including macro- and microplastics, by fungi.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM-BASED POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum-based polymers, such as polystyrene and polyethylene, are major contributors to plastic waste and are studied for their biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC VALORIZATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic valorization studies explore innovative methods, such as using mealworms, to degrade and repurpose plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste pollution is a significant environmental issue caused by the accumulation of non-biodegradable plastics, such as polystyrene, in ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste pollution refers to the accumulation of plastic materials in the environment, which poses significant threats to ecosystems and human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOSPHERE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">**Chemosphere** is a scientific journal dedicated to publishing research on environmental chemistry and pollution, with a particular focus on studies related to the biodegradation of plastics and other environmental pollutants. The journal has featured numerous studies on the biodegradation of polystyrene and low-density polyethylene by organisms such as yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and other microbial strains. Research published in Chemosphere highlights the environmental implications of these biodegradation processes, including factors affecting the efficiency of plastic degradation and the life cycle of organisms involved. Additionally, the journal covers broader topics in environmental chemistry, such as pollution and the role of various organisms, including *Zophobas atratus*, in plastic waste management. Overall, Chemosphere serves as a key platform for advancing knowledge in environmental science, particularly in the context of plastic waste degradation and its ecological impacts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IOP CONF SER EARTH ENVIRON SCI">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">IOP Conference Series: Earth and Environmental Science is a publication that discusses the role of polystyrene biodegradation and solutions to plastic waste pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">**Environmental Pollution** refers to the contamination of the environment by harmful substances, particularly synthetic plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyurethane (PUR), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polystyrene (PS), as well as microplastics, hydrocarbons, and other pollutants. These substances are resistant to natural degradation, leading to their persistence and accumulation in ecosystems. This contamination disrupts ecosystems, threatens marine life, and negatively impacts both environmental and human health. 

A significant aspect of environmental pollution is the growing concern over plastic waste, including microplastics and polystyrene, which pose substantial ecological risks due to their persistence and widespread distribution. Efforts to address this issue focus on understanding and enhancing biodegradation processes. Research has highlighted the potential of certain organisms, such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), to break down synthetic plastics like polystyrene. For example, a 2020 study published in the journal *Environmental Pollution* examined the microbiome diversity in *Tenebrio molitor* fed with polystyrene waste, demonstrating the potential of these organisms to mitigate plastic pollution. 

The contamination of ecosystems by these substances underscores the urgent need for innovative solutions to protect biodiversity and human health. Biodegradation by organisms like mealworms and superworms offers a promising avenue for addressing the global challenge of plastic pollution. Overall, environmental pollution caused by synthetic plastics and other pollutants remains a pressing issue, necessitating continued research, mitigation efforts, and sustainable practices to reduce its impact on the planet.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental pollution refers to the contamination of the environment by substances like polystyrene, which are resistant to degradation and harmful to ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
      <data key="d1">Honeybees have been studied for their potential role in biodegrading certain types of plastics, though their capacity is still under investigation.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process by which plastics are broken down by microorganisms or other biological means.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation is the process by which insects like Black Soldier Fly, Mealworm, and Wax Moth larvae break down plastic substrates into organic biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process by which plastics, such as polystyrene and polyurethane, are broken down by the gut microbiome of insect larvae like superworms and yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process by which plastics like PS and PE are broken down by organisms such as mealworms and their gut microbes into simpler compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process by which yellow mealworms and their gut microbiome break down various types of plastics into simpler compounds.</data>
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBIDITY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
      <data key="d1">A spectrophotometer is used to measure turbidity, indicating bacterial growth in nutrient broth during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC BEADS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium dodecyl sulphite is used to clean plastic beads after biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOSYSTEM SERVICES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro(nano)plastics can negatively affect ecosystem services provided by insects, raising environmental concerns.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT BACTERIAL-MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut bacterial-microbiome refers to the community of bacteria in the digestive tract of insect larvae, which plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT FUNGAL-MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut fungal-microbiome refers to the community of fungi in the digestive tract of insect larvae, which is also involved in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Superworm (Zophobas atratus) is an insect capable of biodegrading plastics such as polystyrene and polyethylene through its gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm (Zophobas atratus) is an insect larvae species capable of ingesting and degrading polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) plastics, often studied for its role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus) are larvae that consume plastic, particularly polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), but experience significant weight loss when fed solely on plastic due to insufficient nutrition.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus) are larvae used in studies to assess their ability to degrade plastics such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm (Zophobas morio L.) larvae are edible insects being investigated for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene in an environmentally friendly manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm (Zophobas atratus) is another insect species confirmed to degrade Styrofoam, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus Fab.) are insect larvae capable of surviving on a sole diet of plastics, such as polystyrene (PS) and polyurethane (PU), and exhibit distinct gut microbiome changes associated with plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm is an insect species whose intestinal microorganisms have the highest ability to degrade polystyrene into low-molecular-weight substances.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm refers to the larvae of Zophobas morio, which are studied for their ability to digest polystyrene and their gut microbiome changes upon PS feeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm (Zophobas atratus) is an organism that shows distinct plastic ingestion preferences and efficiencies, associated with changes in gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YELLOW MEALWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The yellow mealworm (*Tenebrio molitor*), commonly referred to as the yellow mealworm, is a species of beetle larvae with significant ecological, nutritional, and environmental value. It is widely recognized for its remarkable ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including expanded polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (PE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polyurethane (PU). This biodegradation process is facilitated by the insect's gut microbiome, which adapts to diet shifts when consuming plastic materials. Studies have shown that yellow mealworms can ingest and degrade these plastics, converting them into carbon dioxide and feces, thereby contributing to plastic waste management. The efficiency of this process is evidenced by lower PS content in their frass and unique FTIR and NMR peaks, indicating higher degradation efficiency for polystyrene compared to other plastics.

In addition to their environmental benefits, yellow mealworms are studied as a nutrient-rich, sustainable alternative protein source. Recognized as safe for human consumption, they have potential applications in feed, food, and medicine. Their larvae are particularly valued for their high nutritional content, making them a promising candidate for addressing global food security challenges. Yellow mealworms exhibit slight weight increases when fed plastic diets, demonstrating their adaptability to unconventional food sources.

Overall, the yellow mealworm (*Tenebrio molitor*) is a multifaceted organism with significant potential in both environmental sustainability and human nutrition. Its ability to biodegrade plastics and serve as a sustainable protein source highlights its importance in addressing global challenges related to plastic pollution and food security.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworm (Tenebrio molitor) is an organism that can biodegrade expanded polystyrene and low-density polyethylene, with varying degrees of depolymerization and microbe-dependence.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworm (Tenebrio molitor) larvae are studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene and other plastics, contributing to environmental remediation efforts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene foam (PE) is a type of plastic consumed by superworms and yellow mealworms, with evidence of oxidation and depolymerization in their guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene foam is a type of plastic material used in various applications, contributing to environmental pollution due to its resistance to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene foam is a type of plastic waste that can be biodegraded by superworms and yellow mealworms, contributing to environmental pollution reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene foam is a type of plastic waste used in packaging materials, which can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene foam, commonly known as Styrofoam or expanded polystyrene (EPS and XPS), is a lightweight plastic material widely used in packaging and insulation. It is a significant environmental pollutant, particularly in marine environments, due to its slow degradation rate and contribution to plastic waste issues. Polystyrene foam is difficult to degrade naturally, making it a persistent environmental hazard. However, recent studies have explored its biodegradation potential through the action of certain organisms, such as superworms (Zophobas morio) and yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor). These insects, particularly their larvae, have demonstrated the ability to consume polystyrene foam, leading to oxidation and depolymerization in their guts. This process suggests that polystyrene foam can be biodegraded under specific conditions, potentially reducing its environmental impact.

Despite this biodegradation potential, polystyrene foam poses challenges when used as feed for mealworms. Experiments have shown high mortality rates in mealworms fed with polystyrene foam due to its lack of nutritional value. Additionally, it affects the fatty acid composition of the larvae, indicating that while biodegradation is possible, it may not be a sustainable or healthy solution for the organisms involved. Overall, polystyrene foam remains a problematic plastic waste material, but ongoing research into its biodegradation by insects offers a potential pathway for mitigating its environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOTORULA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodotorula is a fungal genus in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, strongly associated with the degradation of plastics such as PE and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodotorula is a fungal genus strongly associated with the biodegradation of plastics like PP, PU, and EVA in the guts of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodotorula is a fungal genus that showed higher relative abundances in the guts of PS- and PE-fed yellow mealworms, indicating its potential role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodotorula is a genus of fungi found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, associated with the degradation of polyethylene (PE) plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodotorula is a genus of fungi that was found to be dominant in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with foam plastics, regardless of the polymer type.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYPTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cryptococcus is a fungal genus in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, strongly associated with the degradation of plastics such as PE and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryptococcus is a fungal genus that showed higher relative abundances in the guts of PS- and PE-fed yellow mealworms, indicating its potential role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryptococcus is a genus of fungi found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, associated with the degradation of polystyrene (PS) plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryptococcus is a fungal genus uniquely associated with the biodegradation of plastics, including PP, PU, and EVA, in yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryptococcus is a genus of fungi that was found to be dominant in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with foam plastics, regardless of the polymer type.</data>
