    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Pseudomonas** is a genus of Gram-negative bacteria that plays a significant role in the biodegradation of various types of plastics, particularly polystyrene (PS) and polypropylene (PP). This bacterial genus has been extensively studied in the context of insect larvae, specifically in the gut microbiomes of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms, and yellow mealworms. Research indicates that Pseudomonas is commonly found in the gut microbiota of these insects, where it is associated with the degradation of polystyrene (PS) plastics, including nanopolystyrene and polyethylene microplastics. 

Studies have shown that Pseudomonas species are significantly more abundant in the gut microbiomes of larvae fed with polystyrene, suggesting their active involvement in the biodegradation process. Additionally, Pseudomonas has been identified in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus, further supporting its role in plastic degradation. The genus contains species capable of degrading styrene and its derivatives, as well as polyurethane, highlighting its versatility in breaking down various synthetic polymers. 

In some cases, Pseudomonas has been found to be overrepresented in samples where larvae were fed with polypropylene (PP) foam plastics, indicating its potential role in PP degradation as well. Overall, Pseudomonas is a key bacterial genus in the gut microbiomes of insect larvae, contributing to the biodegradation of multiple types of plastics, including polystyrene, polypropylene, and polyethylene. Its presence and activity in these environments underscore its importance in plastic waste management and environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, potentially involved in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas is a genus of bacteria used in studies to investigate the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP) films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITROBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrobacter is a genus of bacteria associated with the degradation of polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) in the guts of superworms and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, associated with the degradation of polyethylene (PE) and polypropylene (PP) plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, associated with nitrogen metabolism and the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter is a genus of bacteria that may be involved in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers like polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter is a genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria isolated from the gut of mealworms, involved in nitrogen fixation.</data>
