    <node id="KLEBSIELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella is a bacterial genus identified in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly in the control diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria associated with the degradation of polystyrene (PS) in the guts of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, associated with the degradation of polystyrene (PS) plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that can negatively impact gut health and is associated with lower polystyrene biodegradation yields in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria isolated from mealworms that can degrade bioplastics, suggesting a potential role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, playing a role in their digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria to which the PS-degrading strain WJ2020 belongs, as identified through phylogenetic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria, including Klebsiella sp. WJ2020, which has been isolated from Tenebrio molitor intestines and is capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria found in the gut of mealworms, contributing to nitrogen fixation and overcoming nitrogen limitation in plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria found in the gut of mealworms, contributing to their ability to fix atmospheric nitrogen.</data>
