    <node id="SOLE PLASTIC DIET">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Sole plastic diet refers to the feeding of insect larvae exclusively with plastic materials, such as polyethylene (PE) or polystyrene (PS), to study their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GPC (Gel Permeation Chromatography) analysis is a widely used analytical method for studying molecular weight changes in various plastics during biodegradation processes. This technique is particularly valuable for investigating the degradation of plastics by insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor (mealworms), superworms, and yellow mealworms. GPC analysis measures the molecular weight distribution and reduction of residual polymers, providing critical insights into the extent of depolymerization and biodegradation.

The method has been applied to study the degradation of several types of plastics, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polystyrene (PS), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs). For example, GPC analysis has been used to quantify molecular weight changes in LDPE and EPS after ingestion by mealworms, as well as to measure the molecular weight distribution of residual PS and PET polymers extracted from frass (insect excrement). Additionally, GPC analysis has been employed to examine molecular weight changes in surgical mask layers and polyethylene microplastics after biodegradation by yellow mealworms.

In summary, GPC analysis serves as a critical tool for characterizing the molecular weight distribution of polymers during biodegradation, particularly in the context of plastic degradation by insect larvae. It provides detailed data on the molecular weight changes of various plastics, helping researchers understand the mechanisms and effectiveness of biodegradation processes. The technique is essential for studying depolymerization patterns and biodegradation efficiency, offering valuable evidence of plastic degradation in environmental and experimental settings.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC analysis measures changes in molecular weight (Mn, Mw, Mz) of plastics before and after being consumed by yellow mealworms, indicating biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) analysis is used to characterize changes in molecular weight distribution during the biodegradation of PP by Z. atratus and T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analysis is used to confirm the depolymerization and degradation of microplastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC analysis is used to characterize the molecular weight of residual plastics after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analysis is a method used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polymers like PS and LDPE during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE DISPOSAL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste disposal is a significant environmental challenge, with microbial routes being explored for effective degradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste disposal refers to the management and treatment of plastic waste, including strategies such as biodegradation by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste disposal refers to the management and handling of discarded plastic materials, particularly in developing countries, where it poses significant environmental challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC INGESTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic ingestion refers to the consumption of plastic materials by insect larvae, which is a key step in the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic depolymerization is the process of breaking down long-chain polymer molecules into smaller units, a key step in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic depolymerization refers to the breakdown of long-chain plastic molecules into smaller units during the biodegradation process by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC MINERALIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic mineralization refers to the conversion of plastic materials into inorganic substances, such as CO2, during biodegradation by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC RECALCITRANCE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic recalcitrance refers to the resistance of plastic materials to degradation, making them persistent environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE ACCUMULATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste accumulation refers to the buildup of plastic materials in the environment due to their slow degradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste recycling is a study area focused on transforming plastic waste into reusable materials, addressing environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste recycling refers to the process of reusing plastic materials to reduce environmental pollution, though only a small fraction of plastic waste is currently recycled.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste management involves research on the biodegradation of plastics by organisms like mealworms, superworms, and wax moth caterpillars.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste management refers to research and strategies aimed at reducing, recycling, and biodegrading plastic waste to mitigate environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste management refers to strategies and methods for handling and reducing plastic waste, including biodegradation by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste management refers to the study and implementation of strategies to reduce and manage plastic pollution, including the use of plastic-eating insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste management refers to the strategies and methods, such as using mealworms for biodegradation, to address the environmental challenges posed by plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste management refers to strategies and research aimed at reducing plastic pollution, including biodegradation by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste management refers to strategies and research aimed at reducing plastic pollution, including the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste management refers to strategies and studies aimed at reducing the environmental impact of plastic waste, including biodegradation by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE THREATS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste threats refer to the environmental and health risks posed by the accumulation of plastic materials in ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE IMPACTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste impacts refer to the negative effects of plastic pollution on wildlife, ecosystems, and human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE SOLUTIONS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste solutions refer to innovative approaches, such as biodegradation by insect larvae, to address the global plastic pollution crisis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGROVIBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mangrovibacter is a dominant gut microbe in superworms associated with the polystyrene diet, indicating its role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mangrovibacter is a genus of bacteria associated with the degradation of polyurethane (PU) in the guts of superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus is a fungal genus associated with the degradation of several plastic materials, including polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a fungal genus found in the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus, potentially involved in the breakdown of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a fungal genus known for its plastic biodegradation capacity, appearing in all mask diets with high relative abundance.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a fungal genus reported to degrade polyethylene (PE) plastics, particularly in the guts of wax moth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a genus of fungi involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) in the guts of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that exhibits high abundance in the gut of mealworms fed with polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS).&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a genus of fungi found in the gut of yellow mealworms, known for its plastic biodegradation capacity, particularly for PE degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that was found to be overrepresented in PS samples, indicating its potential role in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that was overrepresented in polystyrene (PS) samples, indicating its involvement in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a fungal genus observed in higher abundances in PU-fed groups, contributing to the biodegradation of plastics through extracellular enzyme production.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that was found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae and has been reported to have plastic degradation abilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPODERMALELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypodermaella is a genus of fungi involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) in the guts of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRICHODERMA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Trichoderma is a fungal genus found in the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus, associated with the degradation of plastic compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Trichoderma is a fungal genus that has been reported to increase in abundance in the guts of plastic-fed lesser mealworms, suggesting a potential role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Trichoderma is a genus of fungi involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) in the guts of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Trichoderma is a fungal genus, with species like Trichoderma viride, known to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Trichoderma is a genus of fungi that was overrepresented in PS samples, but its significance is uncertain due to low recovery frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;Trichoderma is a genus of fungi that was overrepresented in polystyrene (PS) samples, suggesting its potential role in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGHLY EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Highly expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic used in experiments to study its degradation by superworms and mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The fungal community in the gut of A. diaperinus is being studied for its potential role in plastic degradation, particularly polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The fungal community in the guts of superworms and mealworms is studied to understand its role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The fungal community in the gut of yellow mealworms, including genera like Rhodotorula, Cryptococcus, and Aspergillus, plays a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The bacterial community in the gut of yellow mealworms contributes to the biodegradation of plastics, working alongside fungi in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;The bacterial community in the guts of superworms and mealworms is studied to understand its role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS SAMPLES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass samples are collected and analyzed to assess the biodegradation of plastics by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass samples are fecal matter collected from larvae, used to isolate PS-degrading bacteria in enrichment cultures.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass samples are the excrement of larvae, which were analyzed to study the degradation of polypropylene (PP) foam by Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass samples are the excrement of superworms and mealworms, analyzed to confirm the degradation of plastics in their guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATR-FTIR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance-Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a versatile spectroscopic technique widely used to analyze chemical changes and structural transformations in various materials, particularly during biodegradation processes. It is employed to study the degradation of plastics, such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and polyurethane foams, as well as nanoplastics and surgical mask layers, by measuring changes in their infrared spectra. ATR-FTIR is particularly effective in analyzing the chemical composition and functional groups of materials, including surface oxidation, the formation of hydroxyl groups, and other structural modifications. 

The technique is extensively used to examine frass samples (excreted material) from organisms like superworms, yellow mealworms, and larvae, comparing the chemical composition of these samples to the original plastic feedstocks to confirm degradation. For instance, it has been applied to assess the depolymerization and degradation of residual polymers in the frass of yellow mealworms, as well as to analyze changes in functional groups of polystyrene and polyurethane foams after consumption by these organisms. Additionally, ATR-FTIR is used to study the degradation of PS films, PE mulching films, and other plastic materials, providing insights into the chemical changes that occur during biodegradation. Overall, ATR-FTIR serves as a critical tool for understanding the chemical transformations and degradation mechanisms of various materials in environmental and biological contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR is a technique used to analyze the biodegradation and depolymerization of foam plastics after passing through the larval guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Attenuated total reflection Fourier transformed infrared spectroscopy (ATR-FTIR) is used to evaluate the chemical modifications of ingested polymers like PS and LDPE after passing through mealworm intestines.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ITS GENE SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ITS gene sequencing is used to analyze changes in the fungal community diversity in the guts of superworms and mealworms fed with plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL GRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal gravimetric analysis is a method used to characterize the thermal changes of plastic polymers in frass pellets, providing insights into the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal gravimetric analysis was conducted to characterize the thermal stability changes in frass compared to plastic feedstock.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Pupation is a developmental stage in mealworms that is influenced by their diet, with some diets promoting rapid pupation to ensure species continuity.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation is the process by which larvae transform into pupae, which is negatively impacted when superworms and yellow mealworms are fed solely on plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation rates are studied to assess the developmental effects of plastic diets on superworms and yellow mealworms, with significant differences observed in yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation is the process by which mealworm larvae transform into pupae, which was observed to occur faster in larvae fed with plastics compared to those fed with bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation is the process by which mealworm larvae transform into pupae, which temporarily reduces their ability to consume plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation refers to the process by which mealworm larvae transform into pupae, which can affect the efficiency of plastic biodegradation due to cannibalistic behavior and reduced feeding activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation is the process by which Tenebrio molitor larvae transition to the pupal stage, which is influenced by their diet and energy reserves.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation is the process by which larvae transform into pupae, which is influenced by the density of the reproduction buffer and energy reserves.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN-FED CONTROL GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The bran-fed control group consists of superworms and yellow mealworms fed a standard diet of bran, used as a baseline for comparison in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The PS-fed group consists of superworms and yellow mealworms fed polystyrene (PS) as their sole diet, used to study the effects of plastic consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS-fed group refers to superworms and yellow mealworms fed with polystyrene foam, showing lower survival and pupation rates compared to bran-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The PE-fed group consists of superworms and yellow mealworms fed polyethylene (PE) as their sole diet, used to study the effects of plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAL FRASS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Larval frass refers to the excreted material from larvae after consuming plastic diets, analyzed to study the degradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OPTICAL MICROSCOPE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An optical microscope is used to observe the residual plastics in frass samples, aiding in the analysis of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL CHANGES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal changes refer to the alterations in plastic polymers observed during thermal gravimetric analysis, indicating the extent of degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACCUMULATIVE NET WEIGHT CHANGES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Accumulative net weight changes are measured to assess the growth and development of larvae fed plastic diets over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CUMULATIVE FOAM CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cumulative foam consumption refers to the total amount of plastic consumed by larvae during the experiment, used to evaluate the effects of plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidation is a chemical process observed in the biodegradation of LDPE, leading to changes in its molecular structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation is a chemical process that occurs during the biodegradation of polystyrene, leading to changes in its physical and chemical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation is a fundamental chemical process observed during the biodegradation of various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), and EPS (expanded polystyrene), as well as in the degradation of microplastics and mask layers. This process is facilitated by organisms such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms (Zophobas atratus), and certain bacterial strains. Oxidation involves the incorporation of oxygen functional groups, such as carbonyl (C=O) and hydroxyl (OH) groups, into the polymer chains of plastics. This modification makes the plastics more hydrophilic and susceptible to further breakdown.

The evidence for oxidation is consistently observed through analytical techniques like Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and attenuated total reflectance-FTIR (ATR-FTIR), which detect the formation of new oxygen-containing functional groups on the plastic surfaces. For example, during the biodegradation of PS and PE by mealworms and superworms, oxidation leads to the appearance of hydroxyl groups and carbon-oxygen bonds. Similarly, in the degradation of PP by T. molitor and Z. atratus, oxidation results in the formation of carbonyl and hydroxyl groups. The process is also observed in the frass excreted by mealworms, indicating that oxidation modifies the functional groups of microplastics within their digestive systems.

Oxidation is a critical step in the overall biodegradation process, as it weakens the polymer structure, making it more accessible to further enzymatic or microbial breakdown. This process has been documented in various contexts, including the degradation of plastics by bacterial strains and the biodegradation of mask layers by yellow mealworms. Overall, oxidation plays a central role in transforming synthetic polymers into more environmentally benign forms, contributing to the mitigation of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation is a chemical process that occurs on the surface of aged PE microplastics, reducing their hydrophobicity and facilitating their breakdown by microbial enzymes in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation refers to the chemical process that occurs during the biodegradation of plastics, leading to changes in molecular structure and properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATR-FTIR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance-Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) analysis was used to characterize the oxidation and degradation of mask layers after passing through larval guts.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR analysis is a method used to study the chemical properties of nanopolystyrene (NPS) in the research.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR analysis is a technique used to confirm the oxidation and depolymerization of polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE) in the guts of superworms and yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR analysis is used to detect chemical modifications on the surface of PE mulching films after bacterial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR analysis is a technique used to examine the deterioration and identify changes in functional groups on the surface of NPS during degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR analysis is used to study changes in functional groups of nanoplastics during biodegradation, providing insights into the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR analysis is used to detect changes in functional groups in plastics after being consumed and degraded by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOAM PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Foam plastics are lightweight plastic materials that can persist in the environment and are studied for their biodegradation by organisms like Zophobas aratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Foam plastics are lightweight plastic materials used in packaging and insulation, contributing to environmental pollution due to their persistence in ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Foam plastics are lightweight plastic materials used in packaging and insulation, contributing to environmental pollution due to their persistence in ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Foam plastics, including polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), are consumed by superworms and yellow mealworms, with evidence of degradation in their guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Foam plastics, including PP, PU, and EVA, are used in the experiment to assess their consumption and potential biodegradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Foam plastics, such as expanded polystyrene, are a significant environmental pollutant due to their persistence and resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAL GROWTH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Larval growth refers to the development of superworms and yellow mealworms, which is adversely affected when they are fed solely on plastic due to insufficient nutrition.&lt;SEP&gt;Larval growth refers to the study of the development and size changes of Tenebrio molitor larvae under various feeding and environmental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIFE CYCLE COMPLETION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Life cycle completion refers to the ability of mealworms to develop from larvae to adults when fed polystyrene, indicating their adaptability to plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Life cycle completion refers to the ability of organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor, to complete their life cycle while feeding on plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Life cycle completion refers to the ability of superworms and yellow mealworms to complete their life cycle, which is hindered when they are fed solely on plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Life cycle completion studies evaluate whether polystyrene-fed mealworm larvae can complete their life cycle, providing insights into the sustainability of plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Life cycle completion studies evaluate whether polystyrene-fed mealworm larvae can complete their life cycle, providing insights into the sustainability of plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT AMENDMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient amendment refers to the addition of nutrients, such as bran, to improve the growth and survival of superworms and yellow mealworms when fed plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PE foam is a laminated polyethylene structure that is degraded by larvae, contributing to environmental pollution reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;PE foam is a type of polyethylene foam used in the experiment, obtained from a packaging factory in China, and studied for its degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PS foam, or polystyrene foam, is a plastic material that undergoes biodegradation in the guts of larvae, reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene foam (PS foam) is a type of plastic waste used as feedstock in experiments to study its biodegradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PS foam, or polystyrene foam, is a type of plastic waste that can be degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with their gut microbiota playing a role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;PS foam, or polystyrene foam, is a type of plastic waste that can be degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with their gut microbiota playing a role in the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM) is used to observe the biofilm formation of PS-degrading bacteria on polystyrene films.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is a technique used to obtain high-resolution images of the surface of materials by scanning them with a focused beam of electrons.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is used to observe the morphological changes in plastics after being degraded by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is used to observe the morphological characteristics of bacterial strains and biofilm formation on polyethylene (PE) surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is a technique used to analyze the surface morphology of polystyrene (PS) films after biodegradation by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is used to observe and analyze the size distribution of micro- and submicroplastic particles in mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DSC-TGA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DSC-TGA (Differential Scanning Calorimetry and Thermogravimetric Analysis) is employed to analyze the thermal properties of residual polymers in the frass of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;DSC-TGA (Differential Scanning Calorimetry-Thermogravimetric Analysis) is a method used to assess the thermal stability and weight loss of plastics and frass samples during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential Scanning Calorimetry-Thermogravimetric Analysis (DSC-TGA) is a technique used to study the thermal stability and degradation of plastics in frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;DSC-TGA is a method employed to study the thermal properties and degradation of residual polymers from the larval frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHOBIC TO HYDROPHILIC CHANGE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The transformation of plastic surfaces from hydrophobic to hydrophilic is a key indicator of biodegradation, as observed in the frass of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT BACTERIAL MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut bacterial microbiome of superworms and yellow mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacterial microbiome of yellow mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, with shifts in bacterial communities observed depending on the type of plastic consumed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DSC-TGA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DSC-TGA (Differential Scanning Calorimetry-Thermogravimetric Analysis) was used to analyze the thermal properties and weight loss of mask-derived frass, indicating the formation of new organic substances.&lt;SEP&gt;DSC-TGA analysis is a method used to study the thermal stability and degradation of plastics, such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS), in the guts of superworms and yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;DSC-TGA analysis is employed to assess the thermal stability and composition changes of plastics after passing through the gut of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The 16S rRNA gene is a genetic marker used for phylogenetic analysis and taxonomic classification of bacteria, including strain BIT-26.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is a genetic marker used in microbial community analysis to identify and classify bacteria based on hypervariable regions such as V3/V4.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is a molecular marker used to identify and classify bacterial isolates from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, confirming their role in polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is used for molecular identification and phylogenetic analysis of bacteria isolated from Tenebrio molitor's gut.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is used for sequencing and identifying bacterial strains isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is used in microbiome studies to identify and analyze bacterial communities in the guts of superworms and yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is used to analyze microbial communities in the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae, providing insights into their role in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene was targeted for PCR amplicon sequencing to analyze the gut microbial community of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is used in polymerase chain reaction (PCR) to amplify bacterial DNA for analyzing gut microbial communities in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is used in phylogenetic studies to identify and classify bacterial strains, such as BIT-26, based on genetic sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is amplified and sequenced to analyze the gut microbiome of mealworms and identify microbial communities involved in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is used in sequencing to study the composition and changes in the gut bacterial community of yellow mealworms when fed surgical masks.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is sequenced to analyze the microbial community in the gut of insect larvae involved in polystyrene (PS) biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is amplified to analyze the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms and its role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The 16S rRNA gene is a genetic marker used to identify and classify bacterial isolates based on sequencing and phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT BACTERIAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut bacterial community of insect larvae plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, with changes in diversity and composition observed in response to plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacterial community of superworms and yellow mealworms undergoes significant changes when fed polyethylene (PE) or polystyrene (PS) diets, affecting plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacterial community of mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, with changes in biodiversity linked to the diet of the larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacterial community of yellow mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of surgical masks, with changes in bacterial composition observed based on the type of mask layer ingested.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacterial community of yellow mealworms undergoes significant changes when fed different diets, including surgical masks, and plays a role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A bran diet is used as a control in studies to compare the effects of plastic diets on the gut bacterial microbiome of superworms and yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Bran diet refers to a feeding regimen for mealworms that includes bran, which influences their gut microbiome and plastic degradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;A diet used for feeding mealworms, often supplemented with bacterial suspensions for experimental purposes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASCOMYCOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ascomycota is a fungal phylum that showed a significant increase in the bran-fed group and is associated with the degradation of plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Ascomycota is a predominant fungal phylum in the gut of bran-fed and PS-fed superworms, associated with plastic ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Ascomycota is a phylum of fungi that includes Aspergillus, Penicillium, and Trichoderma, which were overrepresented in PS samples, indicating their potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ascomycota is a phylum of fungi that was predominant in both polystyrene (PS) and control (CT) samples, with higher abundance in CT samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Ascomycota is one of the predominant fungal phyla in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, involved in the degradation of various polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BASIDIOMYCOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Basidiomycota is a fungal phylum that increases in relative abundance in the gut of PS- and PE-fed yellow mealworms, suggesting its role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Basidiomycota is a phylum of fungi that is frequently encountered in polystyrene (PS) samples and may play a role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Basidiomycota is a phylum of fungi that was almost absent in CT samples but present in PS samples, suggesting its potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Basidiomycota is a phylum of fungi that was well-represented in polystyrene (PS) samples but rare in control (CT) samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Basidiomycota is one of the predominant fungal phyla in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, showing a strong association with plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESSER MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lesser mealworms (Alphitobius diaperinus) are larvae that have been studied for their gut bacterial community in relation to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Greater waxworms (Galleria mellonella larvae) are capable of degrading polyethylene and other plastics, with gut microbes playing a significant role.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater waxworms (Galleria mellonella) are larvae known for their ability to degrade polyethylene (PE) and have been studied for their gut bacterial community.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPHOPICHIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyphopichia is a fungal genus that was predominant in the guts of bran-fed superworms and yellow mealworms but decreased or disappeared in plastic-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEBARYOMYCES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Debaryomyces is a fungal genus dominant in bran-fed yellow mealworms, but its abundance decreases significantly in plastic-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Debaryomyces is a fungal genus that was predominant in the guts of bran-fed superworms and yellow mealworms but decreased or disappeared in plastic-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Debaryomyces is a genus of fungi predominant in the gut of bran-fed yellow mealworms, with reduced abundance in mask-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISSATCHENKA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Issatchenka is a fungal genus that showed high relative abundance in the guts of PE-fed superworms and PS-fed superworms, suggesting its potential role in plastic degradation.</data>
