    <node id="BRAN-FED SAMPLES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran-fed samples refer to the control groups in which superworms and yellow mealworms were fed bran, showing a different gut microbiome composition compared to plastic-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT FUNGI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut fungi are microorganisms present in the digestive tracts of superworms and yellow mealworms, with certain genera like Issatchenka, Rhodotorula, and Cryptococcus showing potential roles in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut fungi in Tenebrio molitor larvae are also involved in the biodegradation of plastic polymers, with changes in fungal communities observed depending on the type of plastic ingested.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGAL OTUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungal OTUs (Operational Taxonomic Units) are groups of fungi identified in the guts of superworms and yellow mealworms, with some showing significant changes in abundance in response to plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE (Low-Density Polyethylene) degradation refers to the breakdown of LDPE plastics, with certain fungi like Aspergillus being reported to play a role in this process.</data>
