    </node>
    <node id="PENICILLIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Penicillium is a fungal genus that has been associated with plastic degradation in some studies, though its role in superworms and yellow mealworms is less clear.&lt;SEP&gt;Penicillium is a fungal genus, with species like Penicillium simplicissimum and Penicillium variabile, capable of degrading polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Penicillium is a genus of fungi that appeared to be overrepresented in PS samples, though its significance is questioned due to low recovery frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;Penicillium is a genus of fungi that was overrepresented in polystyrene (PS) samples, indicating its involvement in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPHODERMA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyphoderma is a fungal genus that has been reported to increase in abundance in the guts of plastic-fed lesser mealworms, suggesting a potential role in plastic degradation.</data>
