    <node id="ZOPHOBAS ARATUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zophobas aratus larvae are capable of biodegrading foam plastics, with changes in gut digestive enzyme activities and microbiome observed during the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus, a species of darkling beetle larvae, is known for its ability to biodegrade foam plastics, with associated changes in gut digestive enzymes and microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus, a species of darkling beetle larvae, is known for its ability to biodegrade foam plastics, with associated changes in gut digestive enzymes and microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus, also known as superworm, is another insect larvae studied for its plastic biodegradation capabilities, particularly for different types of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus, also known as superworms, are studied for their ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas aratus, also known as superworms, are capable of biodegrading polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas aratus, also known as superworms, are larvae capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene and polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas aratus, also known as superworm, is an insect larvae studied for its ability to biodegrade plastics such as polystyrene and low-density polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas aratus, also known as superworms, are larvae that can degrade polystyrene, similar to Tenebrio molitor.</data>
