    <node id="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial degradation involves the breakdown of plastic wastes by microorganisms, contributing to the reduction of environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of plastics, such as PET, by microorganisms, offering a potential solution for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as plastics, by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as plastics, by microorganisms, which is a key process in environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the process by which microorganisms break down plastics, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as plastics, by microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation involves the use of microorganisms to break down environmental pollutants, such as plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of plastics, such as polystyrene and polyethylene, by microorganisms like gut bacteria in mealworms and waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of plastics by microorganisms, such as bacteria, which is a key area of research for addressing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of synthetic polymers, such as polystyrene, by microorganisms like Rhodococcus ruber.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of materials like plastics by microorganisms, such as bacteria and fungi.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as plastics, by microorganisms, which is a key process in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances like plastics by microorganisms, including those in the guts of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation involves the breakdown of synthetic plastics by microorganisms, offering potential solutions to environmental pollution with plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as plastics, by microorganisms, as studied in various contexts in the provided text.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as starch-based polypropylene, by microorganisms, as studied in the referenced research.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of plastics and other materials by microorganisms, such as bacteria and fungi, often isolated from the guts of insects like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as plastics, by microorganisms, which can help reduce environmental pollution.</data>
