    </node>
    <node id="WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Waxworms (Plodia interpunctella) are larvae capable of chewing and eating polystyrene films, aided by gut bacteria such as Enterobacter asburiae YT1 and Bacillus sp. YP1.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms are insect larvae that have been studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, with bacterial strains from their guts playing a key role.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms are insect larvae known for their ability to degrade polyethylene, a plastic similar to polystyrene, through their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms (Plodia interpunctella) are larvae known for their ability to degrade polyethylene, with Enterobacter asburiae isolated from their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms are larvae that have been studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, demonstrating the role of gut microorganisms in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms are another type of plastic-eating insect studied for their gut microbiota's role in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms are larvae known for their ability to chew and eat plastic, including polyethylene, and host bacteria that aid in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms, the larvae of the Indian mealmoth (Plodia interpunctella), are capable of chewing and eating polyethylene (PE) films, and their gut bacteria can degrade PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene, with their gut bacteria playing a key role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms are larvae capable of degrading polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental Science &amp; Technology published evidence of polyethylene biodegradation by bacterial strains from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene biodegradation refers to the process by which polyethylene is broken down by organisms, such as mealworms and their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene biodegradation is the process by which polyethylene, a common plastic, is broken down by organisms such as mealworms and wax moth caterpillars.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene biodegradation is the process by which polyethylene is broken down by organisms like wax moth larvae and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene biodegradation refers to the breakdown of polyethylene plastic by organisms like caterpillars, which has implications for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID DEGRADATION PATHWAY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The fatty acid degradation pathway is a metabolic process in mealworms that plays a key role in digesting intermediates produced during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The fatty acid degradation pathway is a metabolic process that was significantly enriched in mealworms fed on EPS and LDPE, indicating its role in plastic breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEPOLYMERASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Depolymerases are enzymes that act on the distal backbone of plastic polymers, producing shorter linear chains during the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transcriptomics is a research approach used to study the gene expression patterns in mealworms during plastic degradation, revealing key pathways involved in the process.</data>
