    <node id="LINEAR LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a plastic that mealworms cannot ingest or degrade, likely due to its higher crystallinity.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a polyethylene variant with linear chains and short branching, showing limited biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XENOBIOTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Xenobiotics are compounds, including synthetic plastics like polystyrene, that are degraded by mealworms through specific metabolic pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Xenobiotics are foreign chemical substances, such as plastic degradation intermediates, that mealworms further decompose during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Xenobiotics are foreign chemical substances, such as plastic degradation intermediates, that organisms like mealworms must detoxify.&lt;SEP&gt;Xenobiotics are foreign chemical substances, such as plastics and hydrocarbons, that Achromobacter xylosoxidans can degrade.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC CRYSTALLINITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic crystallinity refers to the structural arrangement of polymers, affecting the ability of mealworms to degrade certain plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic crystallinity refers to the structural property of plastics that affects their consumption and degradation by mealworms, with higher crystallinity reducing degradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOME ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transcriptome analysis is a method used to study the gene expression patterns in mealworms during plastic degradation, revealing key metabolic pathways involved in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptome analysis is conducted to understand the mechanisms of plastic degradation by mealworms at the molecular level.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptome analysis is conducted to study changes in gene expression in the gut microbiome of mealworms during PP biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptome analysis is used to evaluate the role of Tenebrio molitor larvae in PET biodegradation by examining gene expression related to hydrolysis and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptome analysis is used to evaluate the role of Tenebrio molitor larvae in PET degradation, particularly under conditions where gut microorganisms are inhibited.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptome analysis is used to evaluate the role of Tenebrio molitor larvae in PET biodegradation by examining gene expression related to hydrolysis and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptome analysis is used to evaluate the role of Tenebrio molitor larvae in PET degradation, particularly under conditions where gut microorganisms are inhibited.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptome analysis of gut bacteria in mealworms has been conducted to understand their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptome analysis is used to study the expression of degradative enzymes like peroxidase and hydroxylase during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG MAPPING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KEGG mapping is a bioinformatics tool used to analyze metabolic pathways, such as the fatty acid degradation pathway, in mealworms during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded Polystyrene (EPS), also known as Styrofoam, is a lightweight, rigid foam plastic primarily composed of polystyrene (93-96%), pentane (4-7%), and small amounts of additives such as talc, calcium stearate, butane, and hexabromocyclododecane (less than 1%). It is widely used in food and beverage containers, packaging, insulation, and construction due to its low cost, low density, compressive strength, and excellent thermal insulation properties. However, EPS is a significant environmental pollutant, contributing to plastic waste accumulation, microplastics, and toxic byproducts. It is highly resistant to natural degradation, making it difficult to biodegrade and recycle, and it often contaminates construction and demolition debris.

Recent studies have explored the biodegradation potential of EPS by certain organisms, particularly Tenebrio molitor larvae (mealworms) and Zophobas atratus beetles, as well as bacterial strains. These organisms can chew and degrade EPS, showing a broad depolymerization pattern, with an estimated biodegradation rate of 48.2% based on carbon mass balance. However, EPS does not provide sufficient nutrition for the growth of mealworms, limiting its utility as a food source. The biodegradation process is being investigated for its environmental and food safety implications, with particular attention to the role of gut microbiota in these organisms. Additionally, EPS has been studied for its effects on mealworm development and weight gain, further emphasizing its environmental impact and the need for sustainable solutions.

Despite its biodegradation potential, EPS remains a major environmental concern due to its widespread use and persistence in the environment. Efforts to address EPS pollution include studying its biodegradation mechanisms and exploring sustainable alternatives to reduce its environmental impact. EPS is a durable single-use plastic that poses significant environmental challenges, and its role as a common environmental waste highlights the need for continued research and innovation in waste management and material alternatives.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNA SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RNA sequencing was used to study the mechanism of plastic degradation by mealworms, identifying differentially expressed genes (DEGs) and metabolic pathways involved in the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROIA GRISELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Achroia grisella**, commonly known as the lesser waxworm or lesser wax moth, is a species of pyralid moth whose larvae have garnered significant attention for their remarkable ability to biodegrade various types of plastics. This insect, often considered a pest in beekeeping due to its tendency to infest and consume beeswax, has demonstrated the capacity to break down and modify the backbone of plastics such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polypropylene (PP). 

The larvae of Achroia grisella are particularly efficient at biodegrading polyethylene waste, a common and persistent environmental pollutant. Research suggests that this ability may be attributed to unique digestive enzymes or symbiotic gut bacteria that enable the larvae to digest both beeswax and polyethylene. Studies have shown that the larvae can not only degrade these plastics but also complete their life cycle when fed with HDPE, highlighting their potential as a biological solution to plastic pollution. 

While primarily recognized for its role in plastic biodegradation, Achroia grisella is also studied for its biology, growth, and survivability on different diets, further underscoring its ecological and scientific significance. Overall, Achroia grisella represents a promising organism in the field of environmental biotechnology, offering a potential avenue for addressing the global challenge of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Achroia grisella, also known as the lesser waxmoth, is an organism capable of efficiently biodegrading polyethylene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Achroia grisella, or the lesser waxmoth, is an organism capable of efficiently biodegrading polyethylene waste, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Achroia grisella, the lesser waxworm, is an insect capable of efficiently biodegrading polyethylene (HDPE) waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Achroia grisella, known as the lesser waxworm, is an insect capable of biodegrading polyethylene, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Achroia grisella, also known as the lesser wax moth, is a plastic-degrading insect that can break down plastics like polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLESIOPHTHALIMUS DAVIDIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plesiophthalimus davidis is an insect reported to break down and modify the backbone of plastics like PS, PE, PVC, and PP.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLODIA INTERPUNCTELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Indian mealmoths (Plodia interpunctella) are known to degrade plastics, including polyethylene, through their gut microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella is a moth species whose larvae are known to penetrate and damage packaging films.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella is an insect with the ability to degrade plastics, including PS, PE, PVC, and PP, through depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella, also known as the Indian mealmoth, is a pest moth whose larvae have been studied for plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella, commonly known as the Indian meal moth, is an insect from which plastic-degrading bacterial strains such as Enterobacter asburiae strain YT1 and Bacillus sp. YP1 were isolated.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella, also known as the Indian meal moth, is an insect whose larvae can chew and likely degrade polyethylene films.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella, also known as Indian meal moths, are insects reported to have the ability to digest plastics, with their gut microbes playing a crucial role.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella, commonly known as the Indian meal moth, is a species of pyralid moth whose larvae have been studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella, also known as the Indian meal moth, is an insect that has shown the ability to biodegrade polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella is a stored-product insect pest known for its ability to penetrate packaging films.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella, also known as the Indian meal moth, is reported for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-IMPACT POLYSTYRENE (HIPS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">High-impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a rigid plastic that mealworms can degrade, with rubber as an additive influencing its properties.&lt;SEP&gt;High-impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic that mealworms were fed in the study, but it did not significantly contribute to their growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LINEAR LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LLDPE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a plastic material that mealworms can degrade, with its properties affecting the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a type of plastic that mealworms were fed in the study, but it did not significantly contribute to their growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a type of polyethylene with a linear structure and lower crystallinity, making it less recalcitrant to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a type of polyethylene that is studied for its biodegradability under various conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a type of polyethylene that contributes to environmental pollution, with its biodegradation rate influenced by its molecular structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a type of polyethylene with linear molecules and short-chain branching, which affects its biodegradation rate by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a type of plastic that Tenebrio molitor larvae cannot degrade, unlike other forms of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A group of antibiotics, including ampicillin, chloramphenicol, erythromycin, gentamicin, tetracycline, and vancomycin, were tested for their ability to inhibit mealworm gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;A mixture of five antibiotics was used to suppress gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing that PET depolymerization continued even without gut microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A mixture of five antibiotics was used to suppress gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing that PET depolymerization continued even without gut microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics are substances used to inhibit the growth of bacteria, including those in the gut microbiome of mealworms, which can affect their ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics are used to suppress gut microbes in mealworms, which severely inhibits their ability to degrade polystyrene, highlighting the role of gut bacteria in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics were used to suppress the activities of gut bacteria in mealworms, demonstrating the essential role of gut bacteria in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics are used to suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics were used to treat specimens, potentially affecting their microbiome and behavior in LDPE degradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics were used in the study to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade polystyrene and other feedstocks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIFFERENTIALLY EXPRESSED GENES (DEGS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) are genes that show changes in expression levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed on different molecular weight (MW) polypropylene (PP) diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) are genes that show different levels of expression in response to specific conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) were identified through RNA sequencing to understand the metabolic pathways and functional enzymes involved in plastic degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLIC PATHWAYS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolic pathways are the series of chemical reactions involved in the degradation of lignocellulose (CS) and polystyrene (PS) by mealworms, including the formation of aromatic monomers, ring opening, and β-oxidation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic pathways describe the biochemical processes by which Tenebrio molitor larvae degrade polystyrene and lignocellulose, producing energy and intermediates.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic pathways describe the enzymatic processes involved in the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose, including oxidation, aromatic ring cleavage, and fatty acid metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic pathways for styrene biodegradation involve both aerobic and anaerobic processes, mediated by specific enzymes and microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic pathways were studied to understand the mechanism of plastic degradation by mealworms, particularly for PS and PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic pathways in mealworms, such as xenobiotics, aromatic compounds, and fatty acid degradation, are involved in the breakdown of both polystyrene and corn straw.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic pathways in mealworms are reprogrammed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, with changes dependent on the molecular weight of the PS polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic pathways in mealworms, such as riboflavin metabolism, histidine metabolism, and glyoxylate and dicarboxylate metabolism, are perturbed during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONAL ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Functional enzymes are proteins that catalyze biochemical reactions, including the breakdown of polymers like polystyrene during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Functional enzymes in mealworms are involved in the depolymerization of microplastics, particularly attacking short-chain and side-chain polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Functional enzymes were identified as part of the metabolic pathways involved in the degradation of plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LLDPE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is a type of polyethylene polymer characterized by its linear-chain structure and medium crystallinity, with more short-chain branching compared to high-density polyethylene (HDPE). This plastic is widely used in applications such as packaging, agriculture (e.g., mulching films), and other industries due to its durability, flexibility, and resistance to biodegradation. LLDPE is also classified as a type of microplastic, and its biodegradation properties have been the subject of extensive research, particularly involving mealworms (Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae).

Research indicates that LLDPE can be biodegraded by mealworms, albeit at slower rates compared to low-density polyethylene (LDPE). The biodegradation process involves depolymerization, which results in changes to the molecular weight of the plastic. Mealworms consume LLDPE, and their gut microbiota adapts to enhance carbon and nitrogen metabolism, playing a crucial role in the degradation process. However, the biodegradation of LLDPE by mealworms is not without consequences; it can lead to oxidative stress, ecotoxicity, and significant physiological and metabolic impairments in the invertebrates.

The biodegradation of LLDPE is influenced by its physical properties, such as branching and crystallinity, which make it less degradable than LDPE. Some studies suggest that LLDPE is poorly degradable and resistant to biodegradation, while others highlight its potential for biodegradation by mealworms, albeit at reduced efficiency. These contradictory findings may stem from variations in experimental conditions, the specific strains of mealworms used, or the physical properties of the LLDPE samples tested. Additionally, LLDPE has been studied for its degradation properties when formulated with pro-oxidant additives, which may enhance its biodegradability.

Overall, LLDPE represents a significant environmental pollutant, and its biodegradation by mealworms offers a potential pathway for mitigating plastic waste. However, further research is needed to optimize and fully understand the biodegradation process, as well as to address the ecological and physiological impacts on the organisms involved.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, rigid foam plastic commonly used in packaging and insulation. It is a type of polystyrene that contributes to environmental pollution due to its persistence and slow degradation. Recent studies have focused on the biodegradation of EPS by Tenebrio molitor larvae (mealworms), revealing that these organisms can consume and degrade EPS. The experiments demonstrated significant mass loss of EPS after ingestion by mealworms, indicating biodegradation. However, while EPS can be depolymerized and broken down by the larvae, it negatively affects their growth and survival, contributing little to their nutritional needs. Additionally, the ingestion of EPS alters the gut microbial flora of mealworms and leads to changes in the molecular weight and chemical structure of the plastic. Despite its biodegradation potential, EPS remains a significant environmental pollutant, and its consumption by mealworms is being studied as a potential method for reducing plastic waste. The findings also suggest that EPS causes less pronounced changes in lipid levels in mealworms compared to other forms of polystyrene, such as PS and PSr. Overall, EPS is a foamy, amorphous polymer that, while biodegradable by certain organisms, poses challenges due to its environmental impact and limited benefits to the organisms consuming it.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of plastic waste that is commonly found in the environment and is a target for biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of plastic waste that is commonly found in the environment and is known for its resistance to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of polystyrene foam that mealworms can consume and degrade, reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of polystyrene foam used in the study as a diet for T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae to assess biodegradation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by certain bacteria and fungi.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of polystyrene used as a sole energy source for mealworms in the study, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High-impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a plastic material tested for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;High-impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a plastic pollutant tested for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;High-impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic that mealworms can ingest and degrade, though it contributes little to their growth.&lt;SEP&gt;High-impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of polystyrene with improved impact resistance, used in packaging and consumer products.&lt;SEP&gt;High-impact polystyrene (HIPS) is an amorphous polymer that mealworms can consume and degrade, with higher consumption rates compared to other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;High-impact polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by certain bacteria, including those in the Enterobacteriaceae family.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PP (Polypropylene) is a thermoplastic polymer used in a wide range of applications, including packaging, textiles, and automotive components.&lt;SEP&gt;PP (Polypropylene) is a type of plastic that yellow mealworms can consume, leading to changes in their gut bacterial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a highly crystalline polymer that mealworms find difficult to consume and degrade, with limited depolymerization observed.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic material tested for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic pollutant tested for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a thermoplastic polymer used in packaging, textiles, and automotive parts, which can be biodegraded under specific conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic material that Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume, though it provides minimal nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a thermoplastic polymer that T. molitor larvae can ingest and degrade, highlighting their role in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic that can be depolymerized by Tenebrio molitor larvae, though the process is nearly stopped under antibiotic suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic that can be depolymerized by Tenebrio molitor larvae, though the process is nearly stopped under antibiotic suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic that has been targeted for biodegradation by Tenebrionidae larvae, including Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic that can be degraded by gut bacteria in yellow mealworms, particularly Lactococcus.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic polymer that has not been tested for biodegradation by Tenebrio obscurus but is a potential target for future research.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a thermoplastic polymer used in various applications, mentioned in the context of biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOME SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transcriptome sequencing is used to analyze the RNA of mealworms to understand the mechanisms of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIFFERENTIAL EXPRESSED GENES (DEGS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Differential expressed genes (DEGs) are identified to compare the gene expression of mealworms fed on plastics versus traditional food like wheat bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG ANNOTATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KEGG annotation is used to map and analyze the pathways of genes involved in plastic degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) annotation is used for pathway mapping and functional analysis of DEGs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNA EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RNA extraction is performed on mealworms to study the molecular mechanisms of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HT-GPC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">HT-GPC analysis is a method used to analyze the molecular weight changes of residual polymers after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC analysis is a method used to measure the molecular weight changes in polymers, such as PE and PS, during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography (HT-GPC) is used to analyze the molecular weight of plastics like LDPE after degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;High-temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) was used to analyze the molecular weight changes in polyethylene (PE) during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC analysis is a technique used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polypropylene foam, providing insights into its biodegradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;High-temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) analysis is a technique used to study the molecular weight distribution of polymers, such as PP and PS, in the frass of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC analysis is a method used to characterize the depolymerization and biodegradation of LDPE by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, showing changes in molecular weight distribution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTROLLED-ENVIRONMENT CHAMBER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A controlled-environment chamber is used to maintain stable conditions (25 °C, 70% RH, in dark) for rearing mealworms during the study.&lt;SEP&gt;A controlled-environment chamber is used to maintain stable conditions (temperature, humidity, and darkness) for rearing mealworms during experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION CONTROL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Starvation control refers to mealworms that are not fed any diet, used as a control in the study to compare the effects of plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation control refers to mealworms that are not fed any plastic or bran, used as a baseline to compare mortality and weight changes in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation control refers to mealworms that are not fed, used as a baseline to compare the effects of microplastics (MPS) and bran on survival and growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEEDING CONTROL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Feeding control refers to mealworms fed on wheat bran, used as a control in the study to compare the effects of plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Feeding control refers to mealworms fed with bran only, used as a baseline to compare the effects of microplastics (MPS) and bran co-feeding on survival and growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Feeding control refers to the experimental group in the study where mealworms were fed without microplastics, used as a baseline for comparison.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MORTALITY RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mortality rate is measured to evaluate the survival of mealworms fed on different plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIVE WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Relative weight of larvae is measured to assess the growth and development of mealworms fed on different plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CUMULATIVE CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cumulative consumption measures the total amount of microplastics consumed by mealworms over time in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Cumulative consumption refers to the total amount of plastic consumed by mealworms, used to assess biodegradability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR SPECTRA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR spectra are used in research studies to analyze the chemical composition and structural changes of polymers, such as PE, during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze changes in the surface chemistry of polystyrene films after microbial degradation, identifying functional groups associated with degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze the chemical composition of plastics like PU, identifying additives and structural differences that influence their biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze the chemical modifications in microplastics after ingestion by mealworms, such as oxidation and cleavage of polymer chains.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy is used to analyze chemical changes in EPS and LDPE, such as the appearance of new functional groups, indicating biodegradation and oxidation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectra are used to analyze the chemical bonds in residual polymers after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing changes in aromatic, ether, and carbonyl bonds.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze the molecular structure of polymers, such as polystyrene and its derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze the chemical composition of fecula and identify decomposition products like carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide during the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze the chemical changes in polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS) samples treated with or without the Lac640 enzyme.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze the functional groups formed during the degradation of polystyrene and cellulose by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to analyze the functional groups in residual polymers from mealworm frass, providing insights into the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze the chemical changes in polyethylene during biodegradation by lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra are used to analyze the chemical changes in polystyrene residues from mealworm frass, indicating degradation through bond changes and oxidation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCBI NR DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The NCBI NR database is used for taxonomic and functional annotation of unigenes by aligning sequences against it.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCBI NR database is used for taxonomic and functional annotation of unigenes by aligning sequences against it.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCBI Nr database is used for comparing assembled sequences of DEGs to proteins, aiding in gene annotation and functional analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLASTX ALGORITHM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The BLASTX algorithm is used to compare assembled sequences of DEGs to proteins in the NCBI Nr database, with a threshold E-value of &lt;10^-5.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENE ONTOLOGY (GO)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gene Ontology (GO) enrichment analysis is performed using eggNOG to assign functional terms to genes, restricted to Metazoa.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene Ontology (GO) is a framework for the unified representation of gene and gene product attributes across species.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene Ontology (GO) is a functional annotation system used to classify genes based on biological processes, molecular functions, and cellular components.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene Ontology (GO) is a functional annotation system used to classify genes based on biological processes, molecular functions, and cellular components.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLUSTERPROFILER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The R package 'clusterProfiler' is used for functional enrichment analysis of DEGs, with adjusted p-value cutoff of 0.20.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REAL-TIME PCR (RT-PCR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RT-PCR is used for validating target genes, with cDNA synthesis and amplification performed using specific primers and SYBR Green Supermix.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPSS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SPSS (IBM SPSS Statistics 17) is used for statistical analysis, including ANOVA, Tukey's post hoc test, and correlation analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;SPSS is a statistical software used for data analysis, including ANOVA and t-tests, in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEGS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) are analyzed for functional annotation and enrichment using tools like KEGG, GO, and clusterProfiler.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TML27A">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">TmL27a is used as a housekeeping gene in RT-PCR validation to normalize gene expression data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRIMER3PLUS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Primer3Plus is used to design primers for RT-PCR analysis of target genes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEARSON CORRELATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pearson correlation is used to evaluate relationships between cumulative plastic consumption and molecular weight or crystallinity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPEARMAN CORRELATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Spearman correlation is used to assess non-parametric relationships between variables, such as crystallinity and plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYSTALLINITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crystallinity is a key factor influencing the biodegradability of plastics by mealworms, with amorphous polymers like HIPS and EPS being more easily consumed than crystalline polymers like PP.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity is a structural property of plastics that affects their biodegradability, with higher crystallinity making plastics like PP less consumable by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity is a property of polymers like PET that affects their degradation rate by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher crystallinity leading to slower degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in polymers, such as PET, which influences their biodegradation rate.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a polymer, which affects its biodegradation rate, with higher crystallinity slowing down degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a polymer, which affects its biodegradation rate, with higher crystallinity slowing down degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity refers to the degree of long-range order in a polymer, affecting its hardness, density, and resistance to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity refers to the degree of ordered molecular structure in polymers like polyethylene, which affects their physical properties and biodegradation rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in polyethylene (PE), which negatively impacts its biodegradability by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MORTALITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mortality rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae are influenced by the type of feed provided, with starvation leading to higher mortality.&lt;SEP&gt;Mortality rates of mealworms are assessed under different feeding conditions, with polystyrene foam showing the highest mortality due to its lack of nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Mortality refers to the death rate of Tenebrio molitor larvae during the biodegradation process, which is influenced by the type of EPS consumed.&lt;SEP&gt;Mortality refers to the death rates of mealworms fed different diets, including plastics and bran, used to assess the toxicological effects of plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIVE LARVAL WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Relative larval weight measures the growth and weight changes of mealworms over time when fed different diets, including plastics and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMORPHOUS POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Amorphous polymers, such as HIPS and EPS, lack a crystalline structure and are more easily consumed and degraded by mealworms compared to crystalline polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYSTALLINE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crystalline polymers, such as PP, have a highly ordered structure that makes them less consumable and degradable by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEEDING DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Feeding density refers to the accessibility and distribution of plastic pieces in the mealworms' environment, influencing their consumption rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRACELLULAR DEPOLYMERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Extracellular depolymerase is an enzyme that facilitates the degradation of polymers, preferentially targeting amorphous regions over crystalline lamellae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDEONELLA SAKAIENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ideonella sakaiensis is a bacterium capable of degrading and assimilating poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET), a common plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Ideonella sakaiensis is a bacterium capable of degrading and assimilating poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET).&lt;SEP&gt;Ideonella sakaiensis is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade poly(ethylene terephthalate), referenced in the study to compare plastic degradation mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Ideonella sakaiensis is a bacterium capable of degrading plastic, specifically PET, through the action of enzymes like MHETase.&lt;SEP&gt;Ideonella sakaiensis is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade PET plastic using PETases.&lt;SEP&gt;Ideonella sakaiensis is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade and assimilate polyethylene terephthalate (PET), a common plastic pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid degradation is a metabolic pathway enriched in PET-fed larvae, contributing to the breakdown of plastic-derived compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid degradation is a metabolic pathway in mealworms that is influenced by their diet, including plastics like EPS and LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid degradation is a metabolic process in which fatty acids are broken down, and it was found to be involved in the breakdown of PS and PE by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid degradation is a metabolic pathway in mealworms that is involved in the breakdown of both polystyrene and corn straw.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROLASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrolases are enzymes that catalyze the breakdown of chemical bonds through hydrolysis, and they showed significant changes in mealworms fed on plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION CONTROL GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The starvation control group in the study consisted of mealworms that were not provided with any food, used to observe the effects of starvation on larval weight and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC FEEDING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic feeding refers to the experimental practice of feeding polystyrene to yellow mealworms to study its effects on their growth, development, and biodegradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic feeding refers to the experimental setup where mealworms were fed various types of plastics to study their degradation capabilities and effects on larval growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic feeding refers to the process of feeding mealworms with plastic, such as polystyrene, to study their biodegradation capabilities and life cycle completion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEGS (DIFFERENTIALLY EXPRESSED GENES)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) are genes that show changes in expression levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae when fed with polypropylene, indicating adaptive responses to plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) are genes that show significant changes in expression levels in mealworms fed on EPS and LDPE compared to those fed on bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) are genes that show significant changes in expression levels between different experimental conditions, such as PS-fed and control groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG PATHWAY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KEGG pathway analysis is a method used to identify biological pathways enriched with differentially expressed genes in mealworms fed on plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;KEGG pathway analysis is used to identify enriched metabolic pathways in PS-/CS-fed mealworms, revealing their role in the degradation of synthetic and natural polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACYL-COA OXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acyl-CoA oxidase is an enzyme involved in the fatty acid degradation pathway, which was upregulated in mealworms fed on plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Acyl-CoA oxidase is an enzyme that oxidizes fatty acids, with different isoforms showing varying expression levels in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENOYL-COA HYDRATASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Enoyl-CoA hydratase is an enzyme involved in the fatty acid degradation pathway, which was upregulated in mealworms fed on plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3-HYDROXYACYL-COA DEHYDROGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">3-Hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase is an enzyme involved in the fatty acid degradation pathway, which was upregulated in mealworms fed on plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETYL-COA ACYLTRANSFERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetyl-CoA acyltransferase is an enzyme involved in the fatty acid degradation pathway, which was upregulated in mealworms fed on plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME P450 6A1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytochrome P450 6A1 is an enzyme involved in the fatty acid degradation pathway, which was upregulated in mealworms fed on plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytochrome P450 6A1 is an enzyme involved in the oxidation of fatty acids, showing increased expression in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KETOACYL-COA THIOLASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ketoacyl-CoA thiolase is an enzyme involved in fatty acid degradation, showing downregulation in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN-FED MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran-fed mealworms are mealworms fed with bran, used as a control group to compare metabolic changes with plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-FED MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-fed mealworms are mealworms fed with plastics like EPS and LDPE, showing metabolic adaptations to degrade these materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEDIUM CHAIN ACYL-COA DEHYDROGENASE (MCAD)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Medium chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) is an enzyme that oxidizes medium-chain fatty acids, showing suppression in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEROXISOMAL ACYL-COA OXIDASE 3 (BRACCOX)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peroxisomal acyl-CoA oxidase 3 (BRACCoX) is an enzyme that oxidizes branched-chain fatty acids, showing greater suppression in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3-KETOACYL-COA THIOLASE (KAT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">3-Ketoacyl-CoA thiolase (KAT) is an enzyme involved in fatty acid degradation, showing downregulation in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME P450 6A2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytochrome P450 6A2 is an enzyme involved in fatty acid oxidation, showing increased expression in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME P450 9F2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytochrome P450 9F2 is an enzyme involved in fatty acid oxidation, showing increased expression in bran-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUTATHIONE S-TRANSFERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glutathione S-transferase is an enzyme involved in detoxification, showing differential expression in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISONIAZID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Isoniazid metabolism is a pathway involving enzymes like carboxylesterase and glutathione S-transferase, showing changes in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRUG METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Drug metabolism pathways are enriched in PS-/CS-fed mealworms, indicating their role in the degradation of synthetic and natural polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Drug metabolism refers to the enzymatic processes involved in breaking down drugs, showing altered pathways in plastic-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G. MELLONELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">G. mellonella, also known as the greater wax moth, is an insect species studied for its ability to consume and degrade polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;G. mellonella, also known as the waxworm, is another insect model used in plastic biodegradation studies, capable of metabolizing polyethylene even when its gut microbiome is suppressed.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella, commonly known as waxworms, are larvae capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) and other plastics, with gut microbiota playing a crucial role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella, commonly known as waxworms, are larvae capable of degrading polyethylene and polystyrene through enzymatic processes involving their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella, commonly known as waxworms, are larvae that can consume and potentially degrade plastics such as Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella, commonly known as the waxworm, is another insect larvae capable of biodegrading plastics, including LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEESWAX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Beeswax is a feed material used as a control in the study to compare the effects of polystyrene (PS) consumption on larvae growth and survival rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Beeswax is a long-chain hydrocarbon that Galleria mellonella larvae can metabolize, supporting their microbiota-independent metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Beeswax is a natural substance with hydrocarbon chains similar to polyethylene, used in studies to understand the metabolic pathways involved in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Beeswax is a natural substance produced by honey bees, consisting of esters of long-chain alcohols and fatty acids, and serves as a food source for lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Beeswax is a natural wax produced by honeybees, composed of long-chain hydrocarbons and esters, and is metabolized by certain insects like Galleria mellonella.&lt;SEP&gt;Beeswax is a natural wax produced by honeybees, used as a diet for Galleria mellonella larvae in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUTATHIONE S-TRANSFERASES (GSTS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">GSTs are enzymes involved in detoxification processes, upregulated in mealworms fed plastics, indicating the presence of xenobiotic intermediates from plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISONIAZID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Isoniazid is a compound whose metabolism involves acetylation and hydrolysis, similar to the metabolic pathways observed in plastic degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipase is an enzyme encoded by the PueA gene in Pseudomonas chlororaphis, playing a role in the degradation of polyurethane.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipase is an enzyme involved in the breakdown of lipids and waxes, with its expression levels affected in G. mellonella larvae fed beeswax.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipase is an enzyme that can degrade polyester materials, such as those found in biodegradable plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipase is an enzyme that breaks down lipids and long-chain fatty acids, playing a role in the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipase is an enzyme that acts on ester bonds and is activated in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation, contributing to PET hydrolysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipase is an enzyme that acts on ester bonds and is activated in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation, contributing to PET hydrolysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipase is an enzyme produced by bacterial strains such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8, playing a role in the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHORT-CHAIN FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Short-chain fatty acids are metabolic products of wax degradation, with their degradation pathways upregulated in G. mellonella larvae fed beeswax.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon metabolism is a pathway enriched in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio larvae during the biodegradation of polyethylene polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon metabolism refers to the biochemical pathways involved in the breakdown and utilization of carbon-containing compounds, upregulated in G. mellonella larvae fed beeswax.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHANE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methane metabolism involves biochemical pathways related to the breakdown of methane, upregulated in G. mellonella larvae fed beeswax.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROPANOATE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Propanoate metabolism refers to the biochemical pathways involved in the breakdown of propanoate, upregulated in G. mellonella larvae fed beeswax.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-ACETYLTRANSFERASE (NAT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">N-acetyltransferase is an enzyme that catalyzes the acetylation of aryl amines, playing a role in the metabolism of isoniazid and potentially plastic degradation intermediates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLESTERASE 2 (CES2)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylesterase 2 is an enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of carboxylic esters, with its expression affected by plastic degradation intermediates in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXATOMIC RINGS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexatomic rings, such as pyridine and benzene, are structurally similar to styrene monomers and may play a role in the depolymerization of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRACELLULAR DEPOLYMERASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Extracellular depolymerases are enzymes that catalyze the breakdown of polymer backbones, such as those in polystyrene, into shorter chains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RIGID PP">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Rigid polypropylene (PP) is a type of plastic that mealworms cannot degrade effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Rigid polypropylene (PP) is a type of plastic that was found to be non-degradable by mealworms in this study, highlighting the challenges in managing rigid plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DETOXIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Detoxification refers to the process by which organisms like mealworms neutralize and eliminate toxic substances, such as plastic degradation intermediates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC MIXTURES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic mixtures are combinations of different types of plastics that can be biodegraded by organisms like mealworms, with effects on their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic mixtures refer to combinations of different plastics, such as polystyrene and polyethylene, studied for biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic mixtures refer to combinations of different types of plastics that can be biodegraded by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic mixtures refer to combinations of different types of plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, which can be biodegraded by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic mixtures are combinations of different types of plastics that can be biodegraded by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic mixtures, such as PE + PS, are also biodegradable by mealworms, demonstrating the adaptability of their gut microbiome to chemically dissimilar plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic mixtures refer to combinations of different types of plastics, which can be biodegraded by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic mixtures refer to combinations of different types of plastics that can be biodegraded by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVC">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a type of plastic that is difficult for mealworms to chew and digest, resulting in minimal mass loss during consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a widely used synthetic plastic polymer known for its durability, resistance to environmental degradation, and versatility in applications such as construction and packaging. Despite its robust properties, PVC is a significant contributor to environmental pollution due to its persistence and low biodegradation rates under normal environmental conditions. However, recent research has revealed that PVC can be biodegraded by certain insect larvae, particularly Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as yellow mealworms. These larvae are capable of chewing, digesting, and depolymerizing PVC, leading to measurable consumption rates and changes in the plastic's chemical structure. The degradation process is facilitated by shifts in the gut microbial communities of the larvae, which play a crucial role in breaking down the plastic. 

Interestingly, while mealworms can consume PVC, the process is severely inhibited when the gut microbes of the larvae are suppressed by antibiotics, highlighting the importance of microbial activity in the biodegradation process. Additionally, the conversion rate of PVC by mealworms is lower compared to other plastics, such as PET. Despite this, the ability of mealworms to degrade PVC offers a potential avenue for reducing its environmental impact. However, the ingestion of PVC by Tenebrio molitor larvae has been shown to cause significant oxidative stress and physiological damage, underscoring the complex interplay between the biodegradation potential of PVC and its adverse effects on the organisms involved.

It is also noted that PVC has not been tested for biodegradation by Tenebrio obscurus in the study, but it remains a potential target for future research. Overall, while PVC remains a persistent environmental pollutant, ongoing studies into its biodegradation by mealworms provide valuable insights into potential solutions for mitigating its environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLA">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PLA (polylactic acid) is a biodegradable plastic that could be studied for its degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable microplastic that causes the least oxidative stress and physiological damage in Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to PVC and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic derived from renewable resources, such as corn starch or sugarcane, and is used in various applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic derived from renewable resources, such as corn starch, used in packaging and 3D printing.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic material that Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume, with consumption rates measured in previous studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that has been studied for degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable polymer with higher degradation rates compared to persistent plastics like PET, PS, and PE when consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that T. molitor larvae can ingest and break down, demonstrating their ability to degrade various plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable polymer used in blends and films, often modified to enhance compatibility with other materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic derived from renewable resources, used in the study to evaluate its impact on ROS levels in larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic polymer that is preferentially consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to other plastics like PS and PVC.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that certain insects, such as WM model insects, can degrade effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that mealworms can consume, showing the highest mass loss among the materials tested.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
