    <node id="SALINE SOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A saline solution is prepared by mixing 0.85% (w/v) sodium chloride with distilled water, commonly used in biological and chemical experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Saline solution (0.85%, w/v) is prepared by mixing NaCl with distilled water and is used for washing mealworms during the extraction of their digestive tract.&lt;SEP&gt;Saline solution is used to extract and pool the guts of larvae for microbial enrichment studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Saline solution is used to suspend the gut cells of mealworms during the homogenization process in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DOUNCE HOMOGENIZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A Dounce homogenizer is used to homogenize the gut samples of mealworms for bacterial analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE EMULSION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene emulsion is a suspension of polystyrene particles used as a carbon source for culturing microorganisms capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene emulsion is used in the study as a substrate to assess the biodegradation capabilities of bacteria isolated from mealworm guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MODIFIED POLYSTYRENE AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Modified polystyrene agar is a culture medium used to isolate and study polystyrene-degrading bacteria from the gut of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Modified polystyrene agar is used to culture and isolate bacteria capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA ATCC 13182">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a bacterial strain identified through PCR amplification of the 16S ribosomal RNA gene, with an expected product size of 337 base pairs.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a specific bacterial strain confirmed using PCR in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a specific strain of Klebsiella oxytoca used in the study for molecular identification and phylogenetic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a specific strain of bacteria identified in the study, containing the alkB gene involved in hydrocarbon degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a specific strain of bacteria isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor, linked to the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a specific strain of bacteria studied for its ability to degrade polystyrene using enzymes like alkane-1-monooxygenase.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms and is associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a gram-negative bacterium isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor, potentially responsible for polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 is a strain of Gram-negative bacteria isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, with 87.70% similarity to the reference strain.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca strain ATCC 13182 is a bacterial isolate from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, identified as identical to isolates 2 and 3, and involved in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA JCM 1665">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665 is a bacterial strain identified through PCR amplification of the 16S ribosomal RNA gene, with an expected product size of 262 base pairs.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665 is a bacterial strain isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor, potentially involved in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665 is a specific bacterial strain confirmed using PCR in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665 is a specific strain of Klebsiella oxytoca used in the study for molecular identification and phylogenetic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665 is a gram-negative bacterium isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor, potentially contributing to polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665 is a strain of Gram-negative bacteria isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, with 99.77% similarity to the reference strain.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca strain JCM 1665 is a bacterial isolate from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, identified as isolate number 4, and involved in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA NBRC 102593">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593 is a bacterial strain identified through PCR amplification of the 16S ribosomal RNA gene, with an expected product size of 247 base pairs.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593 is a bacterial strain isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor, potentially involved in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593 is a specific bacterial strain confirmed using PCR in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593 is a specific strain of Klebsiella oxytoca used in the study for molecular identification and phylogenetic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms and is linked to the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593 is a gram-negative bacterium isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor, potentially involved in polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593 is a strain of Gram-negative bacteria isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, with 99.77% similarity to the reference strain.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca strain NBRC 102593 is a bacterial isolate from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, identified as isolate number 5, and involved in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR PRIMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PCR primers are designed and used to amplify the 16S ribosomal RNA gene for identifying bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ONE TAQ® QUICK-LOAD® 2X MASTER MIX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">One Taq® Quick-Load® 2X Master Mix is used in PCR reactions to amplify the 16S ribosomal RNA gene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGAROSE GEL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Agarose gel is used in gel electrophoresis to visualize PCR products and confirm the identity of bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GEL ELECTROPHORESIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gel electrophoresis is a technique used to analyze PCR products and confirm the identity of bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GORDONIA ALKANIVORANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gordonia alkanivorans strain DSM 44369 is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade alkanes and other hydrocarbons.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER CALCOACETICUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter calcoaceticus strain M10 is a bacterium known for its role in biodegradation and environmental remediation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRADYRHIZOBIACEAE BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bradyrhizobiaceae bacterium MS228e is a soil bacterium involved in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkanes are bioactive compounds identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating their role in the metabolic pathways involved.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkanes are hydrocarbons that are often targeted for biodegradation by various bacterial strains, including Gordonia alkanivorans and Rhodococcus sp.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkanes are hydrocarbons that can be degraded by enzymes like alkane hydroxylases, which are also involved in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkanes, mostly with small carbon chains, were identified in the gut intestine tissue of mealworm larvae during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NOCARDIA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nocardia sp. SoB clone B78 is a bacterial strain involved in the biodegradation of environmental pollutants, particularly hydrocarbons.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIUM ALKW43">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacterium alkW43 is a bacterial strain known for its role in the biodegradation of hydrocarbons.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOCOCCUS FASCIANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodococcus fascians is a bacterium involved in the biodegradation of environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GORDONIA RHIZOSPHERA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gordonia rhizosphera strain DSM 44383 is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade hydrocarbons and other environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNCULTURED SOIL BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Uncultured soil bacterium clone GO0VNXF07H1URE is a bacterial strain found in soil, potentially involved in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrocarbons are organic compounds often targeted for biodegradation by various bacterial strains, including Gordonia, Rhodococcus, and Nocardia species.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbons identified in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae, likely related to the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbons, mostly with longer carbon chains, were identified in the polar fraction of frass, indicating biochemical activity during PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbons, particularly those with longer carbon chains, are identified during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) in insects, indicating increased enzymatic and biochemical activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENBANK">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GenBank is a database used for storing and retrieving nucleotide sequences, which are essential for phylogenetic analysis and biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;GenBank is a genetic sequence database used to store and retrieve nucleotide sequences, such as the alkB gene sequences used in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEGAX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">MegaX is a software tool used for phylogenetic analysis, particularly for inferring evolutionary relationships among bacterial strains based on gene sequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAMURA-NEI MODEL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Tamura-Nei model is a statistical method used in phylogenetic analysis to construct evolutionary trees based on genetic sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tamura-Nei model is a statistical method used in phylogenetic analysis to infer evolutionary relationships among organisms based on genetic data.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tamura-Nei model is a statistical method used in phylogenetic analysis to infer evolutionary relationships among sequences, such as the alkB gene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALK B GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The alkB gene is associated with the degradation of hydrocarbons and is used in phylogenetic analysis to study the evolutionary relationship of polystyrene-degrading bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUADRANT STREAK METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The quadrant streak method is a microbiological technique used to obtain pure colonies of bacteria, such as Klebsiella oxytoca, from mixed cultures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEGA X">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mega X is a software tool used for phylogenetic analysis, particularly for inferring evolutionary relationships among bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKB-F PRIMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">AlkB-f is a degenerate forward primer designed to amplify the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKB-R PRIMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">AlkB-r is a degenerate reverse primer designed to amplify the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKB-BFB PRIMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">AlkB-BFB is a primer selected from literature to amplify the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKB-BRB PRIMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">AlkB-BRB is a primer selected from literature to amplify the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEG-F PRIMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Deg-f is a forward primer designed to amplify the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca strains, with a Tm of 42.81°C and 50% GC content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEG-R PRIMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Deg-r is a reverse primer designed to amplify the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca strains, with a Tm of 47.69°C and 58.82% GC content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JCM-SPECIFIC PRIMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">JCM-specific primers are used to amplify the 16S ribosomal RNA gene in Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665, with an expected product size of 262 base pairs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NBRC-SPECIFIC PRIMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NBRC-specific primers are used to amplify the 16S ribosomal RNA gene in Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593, with an expected product size of 247 base pairs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATCC-SPECIFIC PRIMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ATCC-specific primers are used to amplify the 16S ribosomal RNA gene in Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182, with an expected product size of 337 base pairs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR AMPLIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PCR amplification is a molecular biology technique used in the study to identify the presence of the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca strains.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR amplification was used to study the bacterial 16S rRNA gene in mealworm guts, providing insights into the microbial role in polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR amplification was used to analyze the V3-V4 regions of 16S rRNA genes in the gut microbiome of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplification is used to amplify DNA sequences from gut microorganisms for microbial community analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR amplification is a molecular biology technique used to amplify specific DNA sequences, such as the nifH gene, to study the gut microbiota of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEQUENCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Sequence analysis was conducted to confirm the identity of the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182 and compare it with other sequences in the GenBank database.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYLOGENETIC TREE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A phylogenetic tree is a diagram constructed from molecular data, such as M13 RAPD-PCR results, to show the evolutionary relationships among bacterial isolates from the gut of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree is a diagram that represents evolutionary relationships among organisms, constructed using molecular data such as 16S rRNA sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree is a diagram used to represent evolutionary relationships among organisms based on genetic data, such as 16SrRNA sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree was constructed to analyze the evolutionary relationships of the alkB gene sequence with other bacterial species.&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree is a diagram used to represent the evolutionary relationships among bacterial isolates based on genetic data, such as 16S rRNA sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree is a graphical representation of the evolutionary relationships among bacterial isolates from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, constructed using 16S rRNA gene sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree is constructed to analyze the evolutionary relationships of the Lac640 protein, which is involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS).&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree is constructed to determine the taxonomic relationships of bacterial strains based on 16S rRNA sequencing results.&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree was constructed to analyze the genetic relationships of Achroia grisella, confirming its identity through molecular taxonomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A phylogenetic tree is a diagram used to represent the evolutionary relationships among bacterial strains isolated from mealworms based on genetic data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALK-F AND ALK-R PRIMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alk-f and Alk-r are degenerate primers used in PCR amplification to detect the alkB gene in Klebsiella oxytoca strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALK-BFB AND ALK-BRB PRIMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alk-BFB and Alk-BRB are degenerate primers used in PCR amplification, though they did not successfully amplify the alkB gene in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEG-F AND DEG-R PRIMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Deg-f and Deg-r are degenerate primers used in PCR amplification, which also failed to amplify the alkB gene in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1.5% AGAROSE GEL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1.5% agarose gel is a medium used in gel electrophoresis to separate and visualize DNA fragments, such as those amplified by PCR.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCBI DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The NCBI database is a repository of genetic sequences used for sequence analysis and comparison, including the alkB gene sequence from Klebsiella oxytoca.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OP959069">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">OP959069 is the accession number assigned to the novel nucleotide sequence of the alkB gene from Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182, published in the NCBI database.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE HYDROXYLASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane hydroxylase is an enzyme encoded by the alkB gene, which plays a role in the degradation of hydrocarbons, including polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkane hydroxylase is an enzyme from Pseudomonas sp. E4 that shows activity for low molecular weight polyethylene in compost.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYP153">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CYP153 is a gene encoding cytochrome P450 enzymes, which are involved in the degradation of hydrocarbons, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIETZIA SP. DQ12-45-1B">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dietzia sp. DQ12-45-1b is a bacterial strain known to have multiple alkane hydroxylases, including AlkB and CYP153, which aid in hydrocarbon degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMYCOLICOCCUS SUBFLAVUS DQS3-9A1T13">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Amycolicoccus subflavus DQS3-9A1T13 is a bacterial strain with multiple alkane hydroxylases, contributing to the degradation of hydrocarbons.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOCOCCUS STRAINS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodococcus strains are bacteria known to have multiple alkB homologous genes, which play a role in the degradation of hydrocarbons, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMERASE CHAIN REACTION (PCR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PCR was used to amplify and analyze the genetic material of gut bacteria in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a molecular biology technique used to amplify and detect specific DNA sequences, such as the alkB gene in bacterial isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RIBOSOMAL RNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">16S ribosomal RNA is a component of the bacterial ribosome, used as a genetic marker for identifying and classifying bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOUCHDOWN PCR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Touchdown PCR is a variant of PCR used to amplify genes with degenerate primers, such as the alkB gene in bacterial isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE HYDROXYLASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane hydroxylases are enzymes involved in the degradation of alkanes and are studied for their role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkane hydroxylases are enzymes, including AlkB and CYP153, that play a role in the degradation of hydrocarbons, such as polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TCOFFE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TCOFFE is a tool used for sequence alignment, employed in this study to align alkB gene sequences from bacterial isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JALVIEW">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Jalview is an offline tool used to visualize sequence alignments, such as those of the alkB gene in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSURF">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ConSurf is an online tool used to analyze and visualize sequence conservation, such as the conserved regions of the alkB gene in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane degradation refers to the breakdown of alkanes by microorganisms, often involving genes like alkane hydroxylase and alkane 1-monooxygenase.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkane degradation refers to the breakdown of hydrocarbons, such as polystyrene, by enzymes like alkane hydroxylases and CYP153.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microorganisms in mealworms play a crucial role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms, particularly those residing in the digestive tracts of insects such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), yellow mealworms, superworms (Zophobas atratus), and other larvae like Tenebrio obscurus and Alphitobius diaperinus, play a crucial role in the biodegradation and mineralization of synthetic polymers, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polylactide. These microorganisms, which include bacteria such as Klebsiella oxytoca and specific strains like LG3 and SCGB1, contribute to the breakdown of complex plastic polymers through enzymatic activity. Their role is essential in facilitating the degradation of plastics into simpler compounds, including CO2 and other byproducts, as demonstrated in studies such as those by Yang et al. (2015b). This process not only aids in the digestion of plastics within the gut of these organisms but also highlights the potential of gut microorganisms in addressing plastic pollution. The consistent emphasis across descriptions underscores the significant contribution of these microorganisms to the biodegradation of various plastics, making them a key focus in environmental and biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental microorganisms are studied for their potential to degrade synthetic plastics like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane-degrading bacteria, such as Klebsiella oxytoca, are studied for their role in breaking down hydrocarbons and plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic biodegradation refers to the breakdown of materials, such as plastics, by enzymes produced by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic biodegradation refers to the breakdown of plastics by enzymes produced by microorganisms, such as alkane hydroxylases.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic biodegradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as plastics, by enzymes produced by microorganisms or other organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNTHETIC PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Synthetic plastics are human-made polymers derived from petrochemicals, widely used in various industries but challenging to degrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic plastics are man-made polymers widely used in various industries but pose significant environmental challenges due to their non-biodegradable nature.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic plastics, including polystyrene and polyethylene, are materials that are being studied for biodegradation by organisms and enzymes.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic plastics are petroleum-based materials that contribute to environmental pollution due to their slow degradation rates and persistence in ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE HYDROXYLASE SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane hydroxylase systems are enzymatic pathways involved in the degradation of alkanes and synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA DN1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas aeruginosa DN1 is a bacterial strain studied for its alkane hydroxylase systems and role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIETZIA STRAIN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dietzia strain is a novel bacterial strain capable of degrading petroleum hydrocarbons and crude oil.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE HYDROXYLASE GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane hydroxylase genes are genetic components that encode enzymes involved in the degradation of alkanes and plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkane hydroxylase genes are involved in the degradation of alkanes and are found in diverse microorganisms and environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE OIL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude oil is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons that can be degraded by microorganisms with alkane hydroxylase systems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum hydrocarbons are compounds found in crude oil that are targets for biodegradation by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Petroleum hydrocarbons are pollutants that can be degraded by Gammaproteobacteria, which are present in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GORDONIA SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gordonia spp. are microorganisms that possess alkane 1-monooxygenase genes, which are involved in the degradation of alkanes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE 1-MONOOXYGENASE GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The alkane 1-monooxygenase gene is a key gene involved in the degradation of alkanes, found in microorganisms like Gordonia spp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA POLYMORPHISM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">DNA polymorphism refers to variations in DNA sequences among individuals, which can be studied using primer design techniques.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRNA PROFILING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">mRNA profiling is a technique used to analyze gene expression levels in higher plants, often involving primer design for specific mRNA sequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCIENTIFIC REPORTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scientific Reports is a peer-reviewed journal that publishes research articles, including studies on diverse alkane hydroxylase genes in microorganisms and environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYSTEMATIC &amp; APPLIED MICROBIOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Systematic &amp; Applied Microbiology is a journal that publishes research on the molecular detection and phylogenetic analysis of genes, such as the alkane 1-monooxygenase gene in Gordonia spp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANT METHODS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plant Methods is a journal that focuses on techniques and methodologies in plant research, including primer design for DNA polymorphism and mRNA profiling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHODS IN ECOLOGY AND EVOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Methods in Ecology and Evolution is a journal that publishes research on methods for studying evolutionary history and phenotypic evolution, including viral and host traits.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRIMER DESIGN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Primer design is a technique used in molecular biology to create short DNA sequences for amplifying specific regions of DNA or mRNA.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TMLP">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">TMlp refers to Tenebrio molitor larvae powder, which has been proven safe for consumption and is proposed as an alternative protein source for livestock feed.&lt;SEP&gt;TMlp refers to Tenebrio molitor larvae powder, which is evaluated for its safety and potential as an alternative protein source for livestock feed.&lt;SEP&gt;TMlp refers to Tenebrio molitor larvae powder, which is prepared as an alternative protein source and tested for its effects on cell viability.&lt;SEP&gt;TMlp refers to Tenebrio molitor larvae powder, which is proposed as an alternative protein source for livestock feed and has been tested for its safety and nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;TMlp refers to Tenebrio molitor larvae powder, which is proposed as an alternative protein source for livestock feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPRAQUE-DAWLEY RATS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sprague-Dawley rats are a breed of rats used in the study to assess the toxicity of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MCF-7 CELLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">MCF-7 cells are a breast cancer cell line used in experiments to assess cell viability and proliferation in response to various treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;MCF-7 cells are a breast cancer cell line used in in vitro experiments to evaluate the effects of TMlp on cell viability.&lt;SEP&gt;MCF-7 cells are an estrogen-dependent human breast cancer cell line used in vitro to assess the estrogenic activity and toxicity of TMlp extracts.&lt;SEP&gt;MCF-7 cells are estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer cells used in in vitro experiments to assess the estrogenic activity and toxicity of TMlp extracts.&lt;SEP&gt;MCF-7 cells are estrogen-dependent human breast cancer cells used in cytotoxicity tests to evaluate the effects of TMlp-extract.&lt;SEP&gt;MCF-7 cells are estrogen-dependent human breast cancer cells used in vitro to assess the effects of TMlp extracts on cell viability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTROGEN RESPONSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Estrogen response refers to the biological reaction of cells or organisms to estrogen, which was investigated in the study using TMlp-extract.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIVESTOCK FEED">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Insects like Black Soldier Fly, Mealworm, and Greater Wax Moth are recognized as sustainable protein sources for livestock feed.&lt;SEP&gt;Livestock feed refers to the use of TMlp as an alternative protein source for feeding broilers and pigs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROILER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Broilers are chickens raised for meat production, which can be fed with TMlp as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIGS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pigs are livestock animals that can be fed with TMlp as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EUROPEAN FOOD SAFETY AUTHORITY (EFSA)">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) is a regulatory body that provides scientific opinions on food safety, including the safety of alternative protein sources and biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) validated mealworms as edible insects in 2021, confirming their safety for food production.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) is an organization associated with one of the authors, though the study does not represent an official EFSA opinion.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) validated mealworms as edible insects in 2021, confirming their safety as a food source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE PROTEIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude protein is a key nutritional component in yellow mealworm larvae, enhanced by the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Crude protein is a measure of the protein content in TMlp, which is a key component for its use as an alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Crude protein refers to the protein content in Tenebrio molitor larvae, which is over 50% and rich in essential amino acids.&lt;SEP&gt;Crude protein is a nutritional component found in Tenebrio larvae, making them a potential alternative protein source for livestock and humans.&lt;SEP&gt;Crude protein is a measure of the protein content in larvae and excrement, which varies depending on the feed provided, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESSENTIAL AMINO ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Essential amino acids are amino acids that must be obtained through diet, and Tenebrio molitor larvae contain abundant amounts of them.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNSATURATED FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Unsaturated fatty acids are healthy fats found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to their nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Unsaturated fatty acids are studied for their effects on quorum sensing, motility, and biofilm formation in bacteria like Acinetobacter baumannii.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINERALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Minerals such as calcium and magnesium are abundant in Tenebrio molitor larvae, enhancing their nutritional profile.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPRAGUE DAWLEY RATS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sprague Dawley rats were used in toxicological assessments to evaluate the safety of TMlp as a protein source in livestock feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THREONINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Threonine is an essential amino acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to their nutritional value as a protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Valine is an essential amino acid present in Tenebrio molitor larvae, enhancing their suitability as a protein source for livestock feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISTIDINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Histidine is an essential amino acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to their nutritional profile as a protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYSINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lysine is an essential amino acid present in Tenebrio molitor larvae, making them a valuable protein source for livestock feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALCIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Calcium is a mineral found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to their nutritional value as a protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is an element present in plastics, often added as calcium carbonate (CaCO3) to enhance strength and reduce production costs.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is a mineral measured in the frass of mealworms, indicating biochemical changes during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is one of the cations determined and quantified in the study using a set of commercial standards.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAGNESIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Magnesium is a mineral measured in the frass of mealworms, showing changes in concentration due to polystyrene consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium is a mineral present in Tenebrio molitor larvae, enhancing their nutritional profile as a protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium is one of the cations analyzed in the study, quantified using a set of commercial standards.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIOXINS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Dioxins are highly toxic chemicals released during the incineration of plastic wastes, particularly those containing PVC, PET, PS, and PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Dioxins are toxic substances generated during the incineration of expanded polystyrene, posing environmental and health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Dioxins are toxic compounds generated during the thermal decomposition of plastics, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Dioxins are toxic chemicals released during the degradation of EPS, posing significant environmental and health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Dioxins are toxic chemicals released during the degradation of EPS, posing significant environmental and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDOCRINE-DISRUPTING CHEMICALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Endocrine-disrupting chemicals are substances that interfere with hormonal systems, studied for their effects on wildlife and humans.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine-disrupting chemicals are synthetic substances released from polystyrene, exhibiting hormonal activity and adversely affecting human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine-disrupting chemicals, such as O-phenylenediamine, are harmful substances that can be biodegraded by bacteria like Citrobacter freundii.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RED FLOUR BEETLES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Red flour beetles (Tribolium castaneum) are edible insects being studied for their potential in environmentally friendly solutions to polystyrene waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DARK MEALWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dark mealworm (Tenebrio obscurus L.) larvae are edible insects studied for their potential in biodegrading polystyrene and reducing environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworm (Tenebrio obscurus) is a species of mealworm that can biodegrade polystyrene and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are insect larvae that exhibit higher polystyrene foam consumption rates compared to yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS FEED BLOCKS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS feed blocks are made of expanded polystyrene, a type of environmental pollutant, and are used as feed for Tenebrio molitor larvae in experimental studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MTT ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The MTT assay is a cell viability test used to assess the effects of TMlp extracts on MCF-7 cell proliferation and estrogenic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;The MTT assay is a cell viability test used to assess the effects of substances on cell proliferation and toxicity in vitro.&lt;SEP&gt;The MTT assay is a colorimetric test used to assess cell viability and proliferation in response to experimental treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The MTT assay is a method used to measure cell viability and assess the toxicological effects of substances, such as TMlp extract, on MCF-7 cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The MTT assay is a cytotoxicity test used to evaluate the impact of insect homogenates on endothelial cell viability.&lt;SEP&gt;The MTT assay is a method used to measure cell viability and cytotoxicity, applied in studies to assess the effects of mealworm and superworm extracts on cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="17-Β ESTRADIOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">17-β estradiol is a chemical used in the experiment as a positive control to assess cell proliferation in MCF-7 cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DMSO">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">DMSO (Dimethyl sulfoxide) is a solvent used to dissolve formazan crystals in the MTT assay for absorbance measurement.&lt;SEP&gt;Dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO) is a solvent used to dissolve and prepare the TMlp extract for cell viability analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) is a solvent used to dissolve formazan crystals in the MTT assay for measuring cell viability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RPMI 1640">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">RPMI 1640 is a cell culture medium used to grow MCF-7 cells in the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FETAL BOVINE SERUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fetal bovine serum is a supplement added to the RPMI 1640 medium to support the growth of MCF-7 cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STREPTOMYCIN/PENICILLIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Streptomycin/penicillin is an antibiotic mixture added to the cell culture medium to prevent bacterial contamination.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOSPHATE-BUFFERED SALINE (PBS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) is used to wash MCF-7 cells during cell culture maintenance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRYPSIN-EDTA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trypsin-EDTA is used to detach MCF-7 cells from the culture flask for passaging.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3-(4,5-DIMETHYLTHIAZOL-2-YL)-2,5-DIPHENYLTETRAZOLIUM BROMIDE (MTT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MTT is a chemical used in the MTT assay to measure cell viability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYL ALCOHOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethyl alcohol is used to leach TMlp for the preparation of the extract.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIMETHYLSULFOXIDE (DMSO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">DMSO is used as a solvent to dissolve the concentrated TMlp extract for cell viability analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHINESE CABBAGE AND LETTUCE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chinese cabbage and lettuce are used as supplementary feed to provide moisture to Tenebrio molitor larvae in the experimental group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-PRESSURE STEAM STERILIZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A high-pressure steam sterilizer is used to sterilize TMlp at 120°C for 10 minutes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOT-AIR OVEN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A hot-air oven is used to heat-dry TMlp at 80°C–85°C for 8 hours.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-TEMPERATURE COMPRESSOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A low-temperature compressor is used to extract oil from TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROTARY VACUUM CONCENTRATOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A rotary vacuum concentrator is used to concentrate the TMlp extract under reduced pressure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPRAGUE-DAWLEY RATS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sprague-Dawley rats are a breed of rats used in in vivo experiments to evaluate the effects of TMlp on feed intake, body weight, and organ weights.&lt;SEP&gt;Sprague-Dawley rats are a breed of rats used in toxicological assessments to evaluate the safety of substances like TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;Sprague-Dawley rats are used in the study to assess the toxicity and safety of TMlp as an alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Sprague-Dawley rats are used in toxicological assessments to evaluate the effects of TMlp, including organ weights, hematology, and serum biochemistry.&lt;SEP&gt;Sprague-Dawley rats are used in toxicological assessments to evaluate the safety of Tenebrio molitor larvae powder (TMlp) as a potential alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Sprague-Dawley rats are used in toxicological studies to assess the safety of mealworm-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Sprague-Dawley rats were used in the study to assess the toxicity and safety of TMlp as an alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Sprague-Dawley rats were used in the study to assess the toxicological effects of TMlp fed with expanded polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MTT SOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MTT solution is a chemical reagent used in cytotoxicity assays to measure cell viability by converting to formazan in living cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FORMALIN SOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Formalin solution is used to fix tissue samples for histopathological examination, preserving cellular structures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARAFFIN EMBEDDING MACHINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A paraffin embedding machine is used to embed tissue samples in paraffin wax for sectioning and histopathological analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMATOXYLIN AND EOSIN (H&amp;E)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) are dyes used for staining tissue sections to visualize cellular structures under a microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) are dyes used to stain tissue sections for microscopic examination, highlighting cellular structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) staining is a histological technique used to study tissue damage caused by microplastic exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDTA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">EDTA (Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid) is an anticoagulant used in blood collection tubes to prevent clotting for hematological analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;EDTA is a chemical used for calibration in protein and nitrogen content analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;EDTA is a chemical used for calibration in the analysis of nitrogen/protein content in biomass using a Dumatherm Nitrogen/Protein analyzer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alanine Aminotransferase (ALT) is an enzyme found primarily in the liver, measured in U/L in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Alanine transaminase (ALT) is an enzyme measured in serum biochemical analysis to assess liver function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AST">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspartate Aminotransferase (AST) is an enzyme found in the liver and other tissues, measured in U/L in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspartate aminotransferase (AST) is an enzyme measured in serum biochemical analysis to assess liver and heart function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) is an enzyme found in various tissues, measured in U/L in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is an enzyme measured in serum biochemical analysis to assess liver and bone health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) is a measure of the amount of nitrogen in the blood from urea, measured in mg/dL in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) is a measure of kidney function, assessed in serum biochemical analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CREATININE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Creatinine is a waste product measured in serum biochemical analysis to evaluate kidney function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Γ-GTP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gamma glutamyl transpeptidase (γ-GTP) is an enzyme measured in serum biochemical analysis to assess liver health.&lt;SEP&gt;Gamma-Glutamyl Transferase (γ-GTP) is an enzyme found in the liver, measured in U/L in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RBC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Red blood cell (RBC) count is a hematological parameter measured to assess blood health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WBC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">White blood cell (WBC) count is a hematological parameter measured to assess immune system function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMOGLOBIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hemoglobin is a protein in red blood cells measured in hematological analysis to assess oxygen-carrying capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin is a protein in red blood cells responsible for carrying oxygen throughout the body, measured in g/dL in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin is a protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. Hemoglobin levels were measured in the study to assess potential effects of TMlp on oxygen transport.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMATOCRIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hematocrit is a measure of the volume of red blood cells in blood, assessed in hematological analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematocrit is the proportion of blood volume occupied by red blood cells. Hematocrit levels were measured in the study to evaluate potential effects of TMlp on blood composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematocrit is the volume percentage of red blood cells in blood, measured as a percentage in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLATELET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Platelet count is a hematological parameter measured to assess blood clotting ability.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets are small blood cells involved in clotting, measured in K/μL in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYMPHOCYTE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lymphocyte count is a hematological parameter measured to assess immune system function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUTROPHIL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Neutrophil count is a hematological parameter measured to assess immune system function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EOSINOPHIL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Eosinophil count is a hematological parameter measured to assess immune system function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BASOPHIL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Basophil count is a hematological parameter measured to assess immune system function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MONOCYTE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Monocyte count is a hematological parameter measured to assess immune system function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RDW">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Red Cell Distribution Width (RDW) is a measure of the variation in red blood cell size, measured as a percentage in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Red cell distribution width (RDW) is a hematological parameter measured to assess red blood cell size variability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MPV">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mean Platelet Volume (MPV) is a measure of the average size of platelets, measured in fL in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Mean platelet volume (MPV) is a hematological parameter measured to assess platelet size and activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR LARVAE POWDER (TMLP)">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor larvae powder (TMlp) is a protein-rich feed material derived from mealworms, containing 65.41% crude protein, and is proposed as an alternative protein source for livestock feed.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae powder (TMlp) is derived from yellow mealworms and is studied as a potential alternative protein source for livestock feed, with a focus on its safety and effects when fed expanded polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae powder (TMlp) is derived from yellow mealworms and is studied as a potential alternative protein source for livestock feed, with research focusing on its safety and nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FORMALIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Formalin is a 10% neutral solution of formaldehyde used for tissue fixation in laboratory experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARAFFIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Paraffin is a wax-like substance used for embedding tissue samples in histology to prepare them for sectioning.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTROGEN (E2)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Estrogen (E2) is a hormone used in experiments to stimulate the proliferation of estrogen-dependent MCF-7 cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MYCOTOXINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mycotoxins are toxic compounds produced by fungi, such as aflatoxins, which were tested for in TMlp to ensure food safety.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE FAT">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude fat is a measure of the fat content in TMlp, contributing to its nutritional value as a feed material.&lt;SEP&gt;Crude fat is a nutritional component found in Tenebrio larvae, contributing to their value as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE FIBER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude fiber is a measure of the indigestible portion of plant material in TMlp, which is important for assessing its suitability as feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE ASH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude ash is a measure of the mineral content in TMlp, which is essential for evaluating its nutritional composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALCIUM (CA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Calcium (Ca) is an element found in significant concentrations in polyurethane (PU1) and trace amounts in polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), contributing to their chemical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is a mineral found in TMlp, contributing to its nutritional profile as a feed material.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOSPHORUS (P)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phosphorus (P) is an element present in polyurethane (PU2), which may influence its chemical behavior and environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorus is a mineral present in TMlp, important for its role in livestock nutrition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POTASSIUM (K)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Potassium (K) is an element detected in trace amounts in polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU2), and polyethylene (PE), which may contribute to their chemical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium is a mineral detected in TMlp, which is vital for maintaining electrolyte balance in animals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAGNESIUM (MG)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Magnesium is a mineral found in TMlp, essential for metabolic processes in livestock.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IRON (FE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Iron (Fe) is an element found in trace amounts in polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU1), and polyethylene (PE), which may contribute to their chemical and environmental behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron is a trace mineral present in TMlp, important for oxygen transport in animals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COPPER (CU)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Copper (Cu) is an element detected in trace amounts in polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU1), and polyethylene (PE), which may influence their properties and environmental interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Copper is a trace mineral detected in TMlp, necessary for enzyme function and metabolism in livestock.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGANESE (MN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Manganese (Mn) is an element detected in trace amounts in polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU1), and polyethylene (PE), which may influence their chemical and environmental properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Manganese is a trace mineral found in TMlp, important for bone development and metabolic processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZINC (ZN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Zinc is a trace mineral present in TMlp, essential for immune function and growth in animals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SELENIUM (SE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Selenium is a trace mineral detected in TMlp, important for antioxidant defense in livestock.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIVER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The liver is a vital organ involved in metabolism, detoxification, and nutrient storage. Histopathological examination of the liver was conducted to evaluate long-term toxicity of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is an organ extracted from Sprague-Dawley rats during autopsy to measure its absolute and relative weights as part of the toxicological assessment of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is an organ in rats that was examined for histopathological abnormalities in the study, showing no significant changes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KIDNEY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The kidney is an organ extracted from Sprague-Dawley rats during autopsy to measure its absolute and relative weights as part of the toxicological assessment of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADRENAL GLAND">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The adrenal gland is an organ extracted from Sprague-Dawley rats during autopsy to measure its absolute and relative weights as part of the toxicological assessment of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEART">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The heart is a vital organ in rats and other organisms, responsible for pumping blood throughout the body. In this study, heart weight was measured to assess potential toxicological effects of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;The heart is an organ extracted from Sprague-Dawley rats during autopsy to measure its absolute and relative weights as part of the toxicological assessment of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THYMUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The thymus is an organ extracted from Sprague-Dawley rats during autopsy to measure its absolute and relative weights as part of the toxicological assessment of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is an organ involved in the immune system, particularly in the development of T-cells. Its weight was measured in the study to evaluate the impact of TMlp on immune function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TESTES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The testes are male reproductive organs responsible for producing sperm and hormones. Testes weight was measured in the study to assess potential reproductive toxicity of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;The testes are organs extracted from Sprague-Dawley rats during autopsy to measure their absolute and relative weights as part of the toxicological assessment of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LUNG">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The lung is an organ extracted from Sprague-Dawley rats during autopsy to measure its absolute and relative weights as part of the toxicological assessment of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;The lungs are respiratory organs responsible for gas exchange. Lung weight was measured in the study to evaluate the effects of TMlp on respiratory health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPLEEN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The spleen is an organ extracted from Sprague-Dawley rats during autopsy to measure its absolute and relative weights as part of the toxicological assessment of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;The spleen is an organ in rats that showed significant differences in weight between groups fed with and without expanded polystyrene in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;The spleen is an organ involved in immune response and blood filtration. Spleen weight was measured in the study to assess potential immune-related effects of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUTROPHILS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Neutrophils are a type of white blood cell measured in the hematological examination of Sprague-Dawley rats to assess the effects of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;Neutrophils are a type of white blood cell that play a key role in fighting bacterial infections. Neutrophil levels were measured in the study to assess immune response to TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;Neutrophils are a type of white blood cell that showed an increasing trend in rats fed with expanded polystyrene in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYMPHOCYTES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell measured in the hematological examination of Sprague-Dawley rats to assess the effects of TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that showed a decreasing trend in rats fed with expanded polystyrene in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are white blood cells involved in adaptive immunity. Lymphocyte levels were measured in the study to evaluate immune system effects of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AST (ASPARTATE AMINOTRANSFERASE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">AST is a liver enzyme measured in serum biochemistry tests to assess liver function in Sprague-Dawley rats exposed to TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspartate aminotransferase (AST) is an enzyme measured in blood tests to assess liver function and detect liver damage in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALT (ALANINE AMINOTRANSFERASE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ALT is a liver enzyme measured in serum biochemistry tests to assess liver function in Sprague-Dawley rats exposed to TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;Alanine aminotransferase (ALT) is an enzyme measured in blood tests to assess liver function and detect liver damage in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALP (ALKALINE PHOSPHATASE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ALP is a liver enzyme measured in serum biochemistry tests to assess liver function in Sprague-Dawley rats exposed to TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is an enzyme measured in blood tests to assess liver and bone health in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-BILI (TOTAL BILIRUBIN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">T-Bili is a biochemical parameter measured in serum biochemistry tests to assess liver function in Sprague-Dawley rats exposed to TMlp.&lt;SEP&gt;Total bilirubin (T-Bili) is a measure of bilirubin in the blood, used to assess liver function and detect jaundice in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KIDNEYS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The kidneys are organs in rats that were examined for histopathological abnormalities in the study, showing no significant changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are organs responsible for filtering blood and maintaining fluid and electrolyte balance. Histopathological examination of the kidneys was conducted to assess long-term toxicity of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WBC (WHITE BLOOD CELLS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">White blood cells (WBCs) are immune cells involved in defending the body against infections. WBC counts were measured in the study to evaluate immune system effects of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MONOCYTES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Monocytes are white blood cells that differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells. Monocyte levels were measured in the study to assess immune response to TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EOSINOPHILS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Eosinophils are white blood cells involved in combating parasitic infections and allergic reactions. Eosinophil levels were measured in the study to evaluate immune system effects of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BASOPHILS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Basophils are white blood cells involved in inflammatory and allergic responses. Basophil levels were measured in the study to assess immune response to TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RBC (RED BLOOD CELLS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Red blood cells (RBCs) are responsible for transporting oxygen throughout the body. RBC counts were measured in the study to evaluate potential hematological effects of TMlp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR LARVAE POWDER">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor larvae powder (TMlp) is a protein-rich product derived from mealworms, proposed as an alternative protein source for livestock feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MCV">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mean Corpuscular Volume (MCV) is a measure of the average volume of red blood cells, measured in fL in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MCH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mean Corpuscular Hemoglobin (MCH) is the average amount of hemoglobin per red blood cell, measured in pg in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MCHC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mean Corpuscular Hemoglobin Concentration (MCHC) is the average concentration of hemoglobin in red blood cells, measured in g/dL in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total Protein (TP) is a measure of the total amount of protein in the blood, measured in g/dL in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALBUMIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Albumin is a protein produced by the liver, measured in g/dL in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-BILI">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total Bilirubin (T-Bili) is a measure of the amount of bilirubin in the blood, measured in mg/dL in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucose is a simple sugar found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with plastic-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a simple sugar used as a control in experiments to assess the biodegradation of polystyrene by the gut microbiome of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a type of sugar used as an energy source by the body, measured in mg/dL in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is used as a positive control in microbial respiration assays to compare the metabolic activity of bacteria on polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is present in the hemolymph of insects and plays a vital role in maintaining organ function, especially during food deprivation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CREA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Creatinine (Crea) is a waste product from muscle metabolism, measured in mg/dL in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLOBULIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Globulin is a group of proteins in the blood that are measured to assess immune function and overall health in toxicological studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Globulin is a group of proteins in the blood, measured in g/dL in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E2 (ESTRADIOL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">E2 (estradiol) is an estrogen receptor agonist used as a positive control in experiments to evaluate the estrogenic activity of TMlp extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Γ-GTP (GAMMA GLUTAMYL TRANSPEPTIDASE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">γ-GTP is an enzyme measured in blood tests to assess liver function and is used as a biomarker in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NA+ (SODIUM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium (Na+) is an electrolyte measured in blood tests to evaluate electrolyte balance and kidney function in toxicological assessments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K+ (POTASSIUM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Potassium (K+) is an electrolyte measured in blood tests to assess kidney function and electrolyte balance in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CL- (CHLORIDE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloride (Cl-) is an electrolyte measured in blood tests to evaluate electrolyte balance and kidney function in toxicological assessments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TP (TOTAL PROTEIN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total protein (TP) is a measure of the total amount of protein in the blood, used to assess liver and kidney function in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUN (BLOOD UREA NITROGEN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) is a measure of nitrogen in the blood, used to assess kidney function in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CREA (CREATININE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Creatinine (Crea) is a waste product measured in blood tests to evaluate kidney function in toxicological assessments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H&amp;E STAIN (HEMATOXYLIN AND EOSIN STAIN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">H&amp;E stain is a histological staining method used to examine tissue samples for histopathological analysis in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDOCRINE-DISRUPTING SUBSTANCES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Endocrine-disrupting substances are chemicals that interfere with hormonal systems, potentially causing adverse effects on health and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTROGEN RECEPTORS (ER)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Estrogen receptors (ER) are proteins that bind to estrogen and regulate gene expression, playing a role in cell growth and differentiation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISTOPATHOLOGICAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Histopathological analysis involves examining tissue samples under a microscope to detect abnormalities or damage caused by toxic substances.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM POWDER">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm powder, derived from Tenebrio molitor larvae, is evaluated for its safety and potential as an alternative protein source for livestock feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYOPHILIZED MEALWORM POWDER">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Lyophilized mealworm powder is a form of mealworm powder used in toxicological studies to assess its safety and effects on Sprague-Dawley rats.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H&amp;E STAINING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">H&amp;E staining is a histological technique used in the study to examine liver and kidney tissues for abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXICOLOGICAL SAFETY EVALUATION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxicological safety evaluation refers to the assessment of the safety of TMlp fed with expanded polystyrene through in vitro and in vivo experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTESTINAL MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Intestinal microbes in Tenebrio molitor play a crucial role in the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YELLOW MEALWORM LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Yellow mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor) are capable of biodegrading plastics, including polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), through the action of their gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor) are used as a protein-rich feed ingredient and studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROILER CHICKENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Broiler chickens are poultry birds studied for their growth performance and nutrient digestibility when fed with mealworm-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Broiler chickens are studied in the context of their gut microbiota and how it is affected by different management practices, including the use of alternative protein sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GROWING PIGS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Growing pigs are studied for their nutrient digestibility and growth performance when fed with mealworm hydrolysate or mealworm-based diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HERMETIA ILLUCENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hermetia illucens, also known as the black soldier fly, is an edible insect studied for its nutritional value and potential as a sustainable protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Hermetia illucens, or black soldier fly larvae, is another insect species studied for its nutritional value as a protein source in animal feed.&lt;SEP&gt;Hermetia illucens, or black soldier fly larvae, are being studied for their potential in entomoremediation and as a protein source for livestock.&lt;SEP&gt;Hermetia illucens, commonly known as Black Soldier Fly (BSF), is an insect species used in organic waste upcycling and plastic biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Hermetia illucens, commonly known as the black soldier fly, is an edible insect used for its high protein content and ability to convert organic waste into biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNTHETIC PLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Synthetic plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, are major environmental pollutants due to their persistence and slow degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic plastics, such as polystyrene, are persistent environmental pollutants that pose challenges for waste management and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal degradation analysis reveals the weight loss stages and decomposition rates of microplastics and frass, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal degradation refers to the breakdown of materials, such as polystyrene, through heat, which is studied as an alternative to biological degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal degradation refers to the breakdown of polymers, such as polystyrene, under high temperatures, producing various byproducts.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal degradation refers to the process of breaking down materials using heat, studied using a thermogravimetric (TG) analyzer.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal degradation is the process of breaking down polymers like polystyrene at high temperatures, studied to understand changes in molecular weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal degradation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene at high temperatures, as studied using TGA in the context of insect larvae frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NOVEL FOOD SOURCE">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Novel food sources, such as mealworms, are evaluated for their safety and nutritional value before being approved for human or animal consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL CHEMICALS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental chemicals refer to synthetic or natural substances released into the environment that can adversely affect human and wildlife reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMAN CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A human cell line derived from a pleural effusion of a breast carcinoma, used in biomedical research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYCHLORINATED BIPHENYLS (PCBS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs) are hazardous byproducts generated from the disposal of EPS, contributing to environmental contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs) are hazardous byproducts generated from the disposal of EPS, contributing to environmental contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Polychlorinated biphenyls are synthetic organic chemicals that were widely used in industrial applications and are now recognized as persistent environmental pollutants with adverse health effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZEBRAFISH (DANIO RERIO)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zebrafish are a model organism used in ecotoxicological studies to assess the effects of environmental pollutants, such as microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebrafish are used in studies to assess the effects of bio-based polylactic acid microplastics on intestinal health and microbiota dysbiosis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARCH-POLYSTYRENE LOOSE-FILL FOAMS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Starch-polystyrene loose-fill foams are biodegradable materials used in packaging, studied for their degradation in composting environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DARK MEALWORMS (TENEBRIO OBSCURUS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are another species of mealworms capable of biodegrading polystyrene and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms are a species of mealworm studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene, similar to Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YELLOW MEALWORMS (TENEBRIO MOLITOR)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Yellow mealworms are a species of mealworm known for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene and serve as an alternative protein source.</data>
