    <node id="ZOPHOBAS MORIO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">**Zophobas morio**, commonly known as superworms or supermeal worms, is a species of darkling beetle belonging to the Tenebrionidae family. These larvae are closely related to *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms) and have garnered significant scientific interest due to their unique abilities and potential applications. One of the most notable characteristics of *Zophobas morio* is its capability to degrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyethylene (PE), and polyurethane (PU). This plastic-degrading ability is facilitated by gut bacteria, such as *Pseudomonas aeruginosa*, which play a crucial role in breaking down these synthetic materials. Studies have shown that *Zophobas morio* can consume and degrade plastics like Styrofoam without exhibiting negative health impacts, making them a promising candidate for addressing plastic waste. However, their efficiency in degrading polyurethane is reported to be lower compared to *Tenebrio molitor*.

In addition to their environmental applications, *Zophobas morio* is also studied for its nutritional value and potential health benefits. These insects are edible and have been investigated as a source of anticancer peptides, chitin, and chitosan, which have various industrial and medical uses. Research has explored their potential as an alternative protein source, highlighting their high nutritional content. Furthermore, studies have examined the impact of polystyrene consumption on the nutritional value and oxidative stress parameters of *Zophobas morio*, demonstrating their resilience and adaptability. 

Overall, *Zophobas morio* represents a multifaceted insect species with significant potential in both environmental sustainability and human nutrition. Its ability to degrade plastics, combined with its nutritional benefits, positions it as a valuable subject for ongoing research in biodegradation and food science.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas morio, commonly known as superworms, are larvae that can biodegrade expanded polystyrene (EPS) but at a slower rate compared to mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM SP. STRAIN YT2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a PS-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the guts of mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene in liquid medium.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a bacterial strain isolated from the guts of mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene with a 7.4% weight reduction over 60 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a gut bacterium found in mealworms that plays a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a plastic-degrading bacterium isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of mealworms, known for its role in polystyrene (PS) biodegradation and mineralization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITIONAL VALUE">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutritional value refers to the assessment of the nutrient content and safety of food sources, such as mealworms, especially when they consume plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritional value refers to the assessment of the nutrient content of polystyrene-fed insects and their potential use as a food source.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritional value refers to the content of proteins, fats, carbohydrates, and other nutrients in edible insects like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas morio.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritional value refers to the crude protein and phosphorus content in yellow mealworm larvae, which is influenced by the degradation of plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritional value refers to the content of nutrients in food sources, which is lower in synthetic polymers compared to natural fibers like cardboard and molded pulp.&lt;SEP&gt;The nutritional value of mealworms, such as Tenebrio molitor, is being studied for their potential as a sustainable food source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytotoxicity is a measure of the toxic effects of substances, such as plastics, on cells, which is relevant when assessing the safety of mealworms that consume plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytotoxicity refers to the potential of insect extracts to cause cell damage, which was studied in relation to insects fed with polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytotoxicity refers to the toxic effects of substances on cells, studied in the context of extracts from mealworms and superworms fed polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytotoxicity refers to the toxic effects of substances on cells, often induced by exposure to microplastics and nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM UTILIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam utilization refers to the process by which edible insects like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas morio consume and break down expanded polystyrene foam, contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE UTILIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene utilization involves the consumption and degradation of polystyrene by insects, facilitated by their gut bacteria, leading to its conversion into CO2 and biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTOMOPHAGY">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Entomophagy refers to the practice of consuming insects as food, which is promoted for its high nutritional value and environmental benefits.&lt;SEP&gt;Entomophagy refers to the practice of eating insects, which is being studied for its potential as a sustainable food source, including insects fed on Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT FARMING">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect farming is the cultivation of insects for food, which is considered more sustainable than traditional livestock farming due to lower greenhouse gas emissions and resource requirements.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOACTIVE PEPTIDES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioactive peptides are compounds found in insects that have potential health benefits, including antioxidant properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT CELLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut cells are the cells lining the digestive tract of insects, which were studied to assess the health impact of polystyrene consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSUMER SURVEY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A consumer survey is conducted to evaluate public willingness to consume insects fed with polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;A consumer survey was conducted to assess public willingness to consume insects, particularly those fed with Styrofoam, and their perceptions of such practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A consumer survey was conducted to assess the willingness of people to eat insects fed with Styrofoam, providing insights into public acceptance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS 80">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS 80 refers to a specific type of expanded polystyrene foam used in the study to feed insects and assess its impact on their nutritional value and health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VEGETABLE AND FRUIT WASTES">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Vegetable and fruit wastes are alternative feed sources for mealworms, highlighting their adaptability and potential for sustainable farming.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RED FLOUR BEETLE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The red flour beetle (Tribolium castaneum) is another insect species capable of degrading polystyrene, expanding the range of plastic-eating insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX MOTH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The wax moth (Galleria mellonella) is a caterpillar species that can chew and eat polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The wax moth (Galleria mellonella) is an insect whose larvae can biodegrade polyethylene, a type of plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax Moth (WM) is an insect species included in the study to assess the effects of plastic exposure on larval weight and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax Moth (WM) larvae exhibit elevated Vitamin B6 metabolism when exposed to certain plastics, suggesting a positive interaction with specific plastic types.&lt;SEP&gt;The wax moth (Galleria mellonella) is an insect whose caterpillars are known for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;The wax moth (Galleria mellonella) is an insect whose caterpillars have been shown to biodegrade polyethylene, offering a potential solution for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESSER WAXWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achroia grisella, known as the lesser waxworm, is an insect capable of efficiently biodegrading polyethylene (HDPE) waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The lesser waxworm (Achroia grisella) is an insect species capable of degrading polystyrene, further highlighting the potential of insects in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;The lesser waxworm (Achroia grisella) is another insect species whose larvae have demonstrated the ability to degrade high-density polyethylene under various conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The lesser waxworm (Achroia grisella) is a species of moth larvae capable of chewing and degrading polyethylene (PE) films, potentially due to the presence of PE-degrading gut bacteria or unique enzymes.&lt;SEP&gt;The lesser waxworm (Achroia grisella) is an organism studied for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene and complete its life cycle when fed with plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;The lesser waxworm (Achroia grisella) is an insect capable of efficiently biodegrading polyethylene (HDPE) waste, as studied in environmental research.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesser waxworms (Achroia grisella) are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RUBBER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Rubber is a material that has been explored for biodegradation by insects like mealworms, extending the potential applications of plastic-degrading organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Rubber is a type of plastic waste that certain insects can degrade, contributing to the broader field of plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Rubber is a natural or synthetic material that has been studied for biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor, particularly in the context of environmental waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Rubber is a natural or synthetic polymer known for its elasticity and resistance to degradation, often used in tires and industrial products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYLACTIDE (PLA)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polylactide (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that certain insects can degrade, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactide is a biodegradable thermoplastic derived from renewable resources like corn starch, used in food packaging and other applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE YT1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) with higher molecular weights.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a bacterial strain isolated from Plodia interpunctella larvae, capable of degrading polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a bacterial strain isolated from darkling beetles, capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a bacterial strain isolated from waxworms that contributes to the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a gut bacterium from mealworms that can degrade LDPE films, demonstrating its role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a gut bacterium from mealworms that can degrade LDPE films.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of waxworms, capable of degrading polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a polyethylene-degrading bacterium isolated from the gut of Indian mealmoth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a bacterial strain that can degrade higher molecular weight polyethylene, though at slower rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IN VITRO CYTOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">In vitro cytotoxicity tests are used to evaluate the potential toxicity of polystyrene-fed insects on human cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE STRESS PARAMETERS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative stress parameters are measured to assess the health impacts of polystyrene consumption on insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexane is a chemical solvent used in the Soxhlet extraction method to determine the fat content of the insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexane is used to redissolve residual polymers for GC-MS analysis of degradation products from polystyrene (PS) biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HECA10 CELLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">HECa10 cells are endothelial cells used in in vitro cytotoxicity assays to assess the effects of insect homogenates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STREPTOMYCIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Streptomycin is an antibiotic used in cell culture media to maintain aseptic conditions during in vitro cytotoxicity assays.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptomycin is an antibiotic used in combination with other antibiotics to suppress gut bacteria in superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUTRAL RED ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Neutral Red assay is a cytotoxicity test used to evaluate the viability of cells exposed to insect homogenates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIMETHYL SULFOXIDE (DMSO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) is a solvent used to dissolve formazan crystals in cell viability assays, allowing for the measurement of absorbance at 570 nm.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUTRAL RED">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Neutral Red is a dye used in cell viability assays to assess lysosomal activity, with absorbance measured at 570 nm after dissolution in a rinsing solution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SULFORHODAMINE B (SRB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sulforhodamine B is a fluorescent dye used in cell proliferation assays, with fluorescence measured at excitation 570 nm and emission 590 nm.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTATE DEHYDROGENASE (LDH)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme released into the culture medium upon cell membrane damage, used as an indicator of cytotoxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLUOSTAR OMEGA READER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The FLUOstar Omega reader is a device used to measure absorbance and fluorescence in various cell-based assays, such as LDH, SRB, and Neutral Red assays.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUSE® CELL ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Muse® Cell Analyzer is a flow cytometer used to determine cell viability, apoptosis, and oxidative stress in cell homogenates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gastrointestinal tract of insects was prepared and homogenized for analysis of oxidative stress parameters and cell viability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FORMAZAN CRYSTALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Formazan crystals are produced in cell viability assays and dissolved using DMSO for absorbance measurement at 570 nm.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRICHLOROACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trichloroacetic acid is used to fix cells in the Sulforhodamine B (SRB) assay, allowing for the measurement of cell proliferation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANNEXIN V REAGENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Annexin V reagent is used in apoptosis assays to detect cells in various stages of apoptosis using flow cytometry.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUSE® COUNT AND VIABILITY KIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Muse® Count and Viability Kit is used to determine cell viability and total cell count in cell homogenates using flow cytometry.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUSE® OXIDATIVE STRESS KIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Muse® Oxidative Stress Kit is a tool used to measure reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as superoxide radicals, in cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The Muse® Oxidative Stress Kit is used to measure reactive oxygen species (ROS) in cells undergoing oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BALL HOMOGENIZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A ball homogenizer is used to gently homogenize gastrointestinal tract tissue to obtain a cell suspension for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PBS BUFFER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PBS buffer (pH 7.4) is used to prepare and homogenize gastrointestinal tract tissue for oxidative stress and cell viability analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NAD+">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NAD+ is a coenzyme involved in the LDH assay, where it is reduced to NADH during the transformation of lactate to pyruvate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NADH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NADH is produced in the LDH assay and used to reduce tetrazolium salt (INT) to a red formazan product, indicating cell damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRAZOLIUM SALT (INT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tetrazolium salt (INT) is reduced to a red formazan product in the LDH assay, indicating the release of LDH due to cell damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RED FORMAZAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Red formazan is the product of the LDH assay, measured at 490 nm to indicate the amount of LDH released and cell damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPEROXIDE RADICALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Superoxide radicals are a type of reactive oxygen species (ROS) measured in cells undergoing oxidative stress using the Muse® Oxidative Stress Kit.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide radicals are a type of reactive oxygen species (ROS) measured using the Muse® Oxidative Stress Kit in cells undergoing oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are edible insects that can consume and biodegrade Styrofoam, leading to changes in their nutritional composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSUMER STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A consumer study evaluates public attitudes toward eating insects fed with Styrofoam, assessing acceptance and safety perceptions.&lt;SEP&gt;A consumer study was conducted to assess public willingness to consume insects, particularly those fed with Styrofoam, and their perceptions of such practices.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">The nutrient composition of mealworms fed with Styrofoam was analyzed, showing higher protein and ash content but reduced fat and carbohydrate levels compared to conventionally fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLOW CYTOMETER MUSE® CELL ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Muse® Cell Analyzer is a flow cytometer used to measure reactive oxygen species (ROS) in cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM-FED MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms fed with Styrofoam exhibit changes in their nutritional composition, including higher protein and ash content and reduced fat and carbohydrate levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipids are a class of organic compounds that serve as energy storage in mealworms, with reduced levels observed in Styrofoam-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are organic compounds extracted from mealworms, which can be converted into biodiesel as part of a sustainable energy solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are organic compounds found in sewage sludge, scum, and mealworm remains that can be converted into biodiesel, serving as a renewable energy source.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are organic compounds extracted from mealworm bodies to analyze the metabolic changes during polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are organic compounds that are incorporated into the biomass of mealworms during the mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are metabolites identified in the gut of mealworms, showing the metabolic changes during the degradation of polystyrene and cellulose.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are a major energy source for mealworms, and their levels decrease significantly when mealworms are fed less accessible forms of polystyrene, indicating starvation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are organic compounds in mealworms that are influenced by their diet, including polystyrene, and are part of the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are stored energy sources in mealworms, with levels varying depending on the type of food or starvation conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOHYDRATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbohydrates are one of the key nutrients measured in the larval biomass, following food safety testing standards (GB/T 15,672-2009).&lt;SEP&gt;Carbohydrates are organic compounds that provide energy, with reduced levels observed in mealworms fed with Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein content in mealworm biomass decreases when their diet is supplemented with polystyrene, indicating altered metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content in mealworms is influenced by their diet, with synthetic polymers like polystyrene affecting protein synthesis and fatty acid profiles.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content in the biomass and frass of mealworms varies depending on the diet, with implications for the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein is a macronutrient found in higher levels in mealworms fed with Styrofoam, contributing to their improved nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein content was measured in the study to evaluate the nutritional value of larvae fed different diets and exposed to plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein levels in mealworms are not significantly affected by the type of polystyrene consumed, although some studies suggest protein synthesis decreases during food deprivation.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein is a critical nutrient that supports the growth, development, and survival of Tenebrio molitor larvae, especially when supplemented in their diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteins are essential for body functioning in insects and are hydrolyzed during long periods of starvation to serve as an energy source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASH CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ash content refers to the inorganic residue left after burning organic material, which is higher in mealworms fed with Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy content refers to the caloric value of mealworms, which is lower in those fed with Styrofoam due to reduced fat and carbohydrate levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL ASSAYS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical assays are conducted to study the metabolic processes and energy usage of mealworms during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochemical assays were conducted to analyze the nutrient composition and energy storage in mealworms, particularly those fed with Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE-EATING INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene-eating insects, such as mealworms and superworms, are studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene and their resulting nutrient composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FECULA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fecula is the waste material produced by mealworms after consuming EPS, containing smaller polymer chains and other byproducts of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula is the waste material produced by mealworms after consuming EPS, containing smaller polymer chains and other byproducts of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the excrement of mealworms, which contains degraded products of Styrofoam and is analyzed to study biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the residual Styrofoam fragments and intermediates egested by mealworms, where further degradation can continue.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the residues of polystyrene egested through the gut of mealworms, which show evidence of depolymerization and the formation of lower molecular weight fragments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the waste material egested by mealworms after consuming and biodegrading Styrofoam, containing residual polystyrene byproducts.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the waste material egested by mealworms after consuming and degrading polystyrene, containing depolymerized metabolites.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the waste material egested by mealworms, which contains degraded polystyrene fragments and is analyzed to study the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the waste material produced by mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene, containing decomposed components of the ingested Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CO2 (carbon dioxide) is a byproduct of the biodegradation process, particularly observed in the headspace of experiments involving polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 (carbon dioxide) is a byproduct of the mineralization of polyethylene during biodegradation by lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 (carbon dioxide) is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms and their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of Styrofoam by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, indicating the breakdown of the plastic into simpler compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is a byproduct of microbial respiration and is measured to assess the mineralization of polystyrene (PS) by the mealworm gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms and their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is a byproduct of the mineralization process, where polystyrene is converted into carbon dioxide by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is a byproduct of the biodegradation of plastics such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) by mealworms, indicating mineralization of the plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of polystyrene by insects.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is the final metabolite produced during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by Enterobacter sp. LG3 under anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a gas produced during bacterial degradation processes, detected using gas chromatography.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID BIOMASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid biomass is a byproduct of polystyrene mineralization, indicating the incorporation of degraded polystyrene into cellular components.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid biomass is a byproduct of the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, indicating the incorporation of plastic-derived carbon into biological material.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid biomass refers to the fraction of ingested polystyrene (PS) that is incorporated into the biomass of mealworms during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid biomass refers to the incorporation of degraded plastic components, such as polystyrene (PS), into the lipid content of organisms during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid biomass refers to the portion of ingested Styrofoam that is incorporated into lipids during the biodegradation process by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid biomass is a small fraction of the ingested polystyrene that is incorporated into the biomass of organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid biomass refers to the portion of ingested polystyrene that is incorporated into the body tissues of mealworms during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Z. MORIO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Z. morio is an insect species whose gut microbiota, including Aeromonas sp. TM1, have been studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Z. morio, also known as superworms, are insect larvae that can consume polystyrene and are studied for their reactions to polystyrene consumption and cytotoxicity effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas morio, also known as the superworm, is an insect studied for its ability to consume polystyrene foam and its potential as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM EXTRACTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm extracts are derived from Tenebrio molitor larvae and are studied for their cytotoxic effects on various cell lines, including normal and cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERWORM EXTRACTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Superworm extracts are derived from Z. morio larvae and are studied for their cytotoxic effects on various cell lines, including normal and cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NR ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The NR assay is a method used to measure cell viability and cytotoxicity, applied in studies to assess the effects of mealworm and superworm extracts on cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SRB ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The SRB assay is a method used to measure cell viability and cytotoxicity, applied in studies to assess the effects of mealworm and superworm extracts on cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDH ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The LDH assay is a method used to measure cell cytotoxicity by detecting lactate dehydrogenase release, applied in studies to assess the effects of mealworm and superworm extracts on cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEP3B CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hep3B is a human liver cancer cell line used in cytotoxicity studies to evaluate the effects of mealworm extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SK-HEP-1 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">SK-HEP-1 is a human liver cancer cell line used in cytotoxicity studies to evaluate the effects of mealworm extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CACO-2 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Caco-2 is a human colon cancer cell line used in cytotoxicity studies to evaluate the effects of mealworm extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPG2 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">HepG2 is a human liver cancer cell line used in cytotoxicity studies to evaluate the effects of mealworm extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HELA CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">HeLa is a human cervical cancer cell line used in cytotoxicity studies to evaluate the effects of mealworm extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L929 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">L929 is a normal murine fibroblast cell line used in cytotoxicity studies to evaluate the effects of mealworm extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VERO CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Vero is a normal kidney epithelial cell line extracted from an African green monkey, used in cytotoxicity studies to evaluate the effects of superworm extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RED BLOOD CELL GLUTATHIONE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Red blood cell glutathione is a biochemical parameter studied in the context of mealworm extracts, showing potential health benefits in hypertensive rats.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPONTANEOUSLY HYPERTENSIVE RATS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Spontaneously hypertensive rats are used in studies to evaluate the effects of mealworm extracts on blood pressure and other health parameters.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T. MOLITOR">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">T. molitor is a species of Tenebrio larvae that exhibits enhanced gut microbiome functions associated with carbon and nitrogen metabolism, particularly during the degradation of PE polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor (T. molitor), commonly known as mealworms, are insects whose gut microbiota play a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as the mealworm, is an insect studied for its ability to consume polystyrene foam and its potential as an alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor, commonly known as yellow mealworms, are larvae known for their ability to degrade plastics and have been studied for their gut microbiome composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, are larvae that can biodegrade polyethylene (PE) microplastics, including LLDPE, LDPE, and HDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, are larvae that can biodegrade polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) through their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor, commonly known as mealworms, is a species of larvae that can biodegrade polystyrene (PS) but is less efficient compared to T. obscurus.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor, commonly known as yellow mealworm, is a species of larvae that can consume polystyrene foam, though at lower rates compared to T. obscurus.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor, commonly known as the yellow mealworm, is an insect species capable of consuming and degrading polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, are larvae that can consume and degrade polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are smaller in size compared to Z. atratus and are also involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, are larvae capable of biodegrading polypropylene (PP) and other plastics, with gut microbiota playing a key role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, are larvae that can biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) through oxidation and chemical modifications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHANOLIC EXTRACT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethanolic extract of Z. morio is used in cytotoxicity studies to assess its effects on various cell lines, including MCF-7 and Vero cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOTOXICITY TEST">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytotoxicity tests are performed to evaluate the potential toxic effects of insect extracts on different cell lines, such as endothelial cells, fibroblasts, and epithelial cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VERO CELLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Vero cells are normal kidney epithelial cells extracted from an African green monkey, used in cytotoxicity studies to assess the effects of Z. morio extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MCF-7 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">MCF-7 is a human breast cancer cell line used in cytotoxicity studies to evaluate the effects of Z. morio extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOPROPANOLIC EXTRACT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Isopropanolic extract of Z. morio is used in cytotoxicity studies to assess its effects on cancer cell lines, such as MCF-7.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDOTHELIAL CELLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Endothelial cells are a type of cell used in cytotoxicity tests to evaluate the effects of insect extracts, such as those from Z. morio and T. molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIBROBLASTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fibroblasts are a type of cell suggested for future cytotoxicity studies to further evaluate the effects of insect extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPITHELIAL CELLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Epithelial cells are a type of cell suggested for future cytotoxicity studies to further evaluate the effects of insect extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLOW CYTOMETRY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Flow cytometry is a technique used to measure cell viability, apoptosis, and reactive oxygen species in the gut cells of insects fed with Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scientific research is conducted to assess the safety and effects of insects consuming Styrofoam, particularly for potential use in human or animal feeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Scientific research is conducted to evaluate the safety and feasibility of using insects that consume Styrofoam as a food source for humans or animals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Insects, specifically those studied in the research, are capable of degrading polystyrene through their gut microbiota and enzymatic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Insects, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas morio, are studied for their ability to consume and degrade Styrofoam, with potential applications in food safety and environmental remediation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxicity refers to the harmful effects of substances like PS metabolites and impurities on Tenebrio molitor larvae, impacting their growth and development.&lt;SEP&gt;Toxicity refers to the harmful effects of substances, such as microplastics and nanoplastics, on organisms and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Toxicity refers to the potential harmful effects of residual plastics and byproducts produced during the biodegradation process by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Toxicity studies are performed to determine whether insects that consume Styrofoam are safe for human consumption, addressing potential health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL REMEDIATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A research study exploring the use of larvae as an environmental remediation strategy for plastic waste, though it is deemed problematic due to the generation of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental remediation involves the use of organisms, such as insects, to degrade pollutants like Styrofoam, contributing to waste management solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental remediation refers to strategies and research aimed at cleaning up polluted environments, including plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental remediation refers to the use of organisms like mealworms and superworms to address plastic pollution through biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROIA GRISEOLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achroia griseola, commonly known as the lesser wax-worm, is an organism capable of biodegrading polyethylene (PE) waste, contributing to environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Achroia griseola, known as the lesser wax moth, is an insect reported to consume polyethylene, contributing to plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Achroia griseola, the lesser waxmoth, is capable of efficiently biodegrading high-density polyethylene (HDPE) waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM DEBRIS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam debris refers to fragmented pieces of expanded polystyrene that pose environmental hazards, particularly in marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a common plastic material that can be biodegraded by certain organisms, such as the lesser waxworm.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (HDPE) is a common plastic waste that can be biodegraded by certain insects like Achroia grisella.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of plastic waste that can be efficiently biodegraded by certain organisms, such as the lesser waxmoth.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a major persistent plastic pollutant that is resistant to biodegradation and contributes to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXICOLOGICAL RESEARCH">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxicological research involves evaluating the safety and potential toxicity of substances, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae powder, through in vivo and in vitro studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAZARDOUS ADDITIVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hazardous additives are chemicals released from Styrofoam debris that can negatively impact marine organisms and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-PROTEIN CONVERSION FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-protein conversion factors are used to determine the protein content of edible insects like Tenebrio molitor, Alphitobius diaperinus, and Hermetia illucens.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALPHITOBIUS DIAPERINUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Alphitobius diaperinus, a beetle in the Tenebrionidae family, exhibits metabolic responses to food deprivation similar to Tenebrio molitor, particularly in triglyceride levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, also known as the lesser mealworm, is a beetle species studied for its metabolic responses during food shortage and recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, also known as the lesser mealworm, is an edible insect studied for its nutritional composition and potential as an alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, also known as the lesser mealworm, is an insect studied for its gut bacterial and fungal diversity when fed polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, also known as the lesser mealworm, is an edible insect studied for its nutritional value and potential as an alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, commonly known as the lesser mealworm, is a coleopteran species capable of feeding on and degrading polystyrene, aided by its gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, commonly known as the lesser mealworm, is an insect studied for its ability to degrade polystyrene and its gut microbial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, the lesser mealworm, is a pest of stored food grain products and has been shown to feed on and degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Genotoxicity refers to the potential of a substance to damage genetic material, as evaluated in studies on Tenebrio molitor larvae powder.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FREEZE-DRIED POWDER">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Freeze-dried powder of Tenebrio molitor larvae is a form of edible insect product evaluated for safety and toxicity in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Sustainable development involves strategies like using mealworms for polystyrene biodegradation to reduce environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Sustainable development refers to practices and research aimed at meeting current needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Sustainable development refers to research efforts aimed at finding eco-friendly solutions, such as using insects for plastic biodegradation and alternative protein sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-DEGRADING PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The plastic-degrading process involves the breakdown of plastics like expanded polystyrene by insects such as Tribolium castaneum.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE ORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine organisms are affected by hazardous additives released from Styrofoam debris, highlighting the environmental impact of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine organisms are affected by microplastics, which can cause physical impacts and trophic transference of marine litter.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOAMED POLYSTYRENE RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Foamed polystyrene recycling involves the use of insects like Tenebrio molitor to biodegrade and recycle expanded polystyrene waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAT CONTENT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexane is used in the Soxhlet extraction method to determine the fat content of insects fed with Styrofoam or wheat bran.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELL CULTURE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
      <data key="d1">Streptomycin is added to cell culture media to prevent bacterial contamination during in vitro experiments.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTICANCER PEPTIDES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
      <data key="d1">Zophobas morio larvae are studied for their potential to produce anticancer peptides, highlighting their medicinal value.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO-POLYSTYRENE (MPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro-polystyrene (MPS) is a type of microplastic derived from polystyrene, used in this study to investigate its biodegradation by mealworms and its effects on their development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RIGID PLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Rigid plastic waste, including micro-polystyrene (MPS), is a significant environmental pollutant that poses challenges for waste management and ecosystem health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic degradation refers to the process by which mealworms break down microplastics, such as MPS, into smaller, less harmful components.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastic degradation refers to the process by which microplastics, such as polystyrene, are broken down by organisms like Galleria mellonella larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RIGID MICRO-POLYSTYRENE (MPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Rigid micro-polystyrene (MPS) is a specific type of microplastic used in this study to investigate its biodegradation by mealworms and its effects on their development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOAMY PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Foamy plastics, such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), are lightweight plastic materials that contribute to environmental pollution and are targeted for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAL AGE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Larval age is a factor studied in mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) to understand its impact on the biodegradation of micro-polystyrene and the development of the larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Larval age is a key factor in the study, influencing the ability of mealworms to consume and degrade microplastics, as well as their survival and development.&lt;SEP&gt;Larval age refers to the developmental stage of mealworms, which affects their ability to consume and degrade microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARTICLE SIZE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Particle size is a critical variable in the study, affecting the consumption rate of microplastics by mealworms and the efficiency of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Particle size is a factor studied in mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) to understand its role in regulating the biodegradation of micro-polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pupae are a life stage of larvae, and their formation was monitored as part of the study to assess the effects of plastic diets on development.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which is affected by the availability of nutrients from bran or plastic foam diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms following the larval stage, and their formation is influenced by microplastic feeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms that are removed during the experiment to prevent cannibalism by other mealworm stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms, used to assess the health and development of larvae under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms observed during the experiment to study their survival and transformation under different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of lesser waxworms that follow the larval stage and precede the adult moth stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms that follow the larval stage and precede the adult beetle stage, studied in the context of PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms that follow the larval stage, observed in the study as part of the mealworm life cycle during polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms between larvae and adults, and their formation and hatching rates are influenced by the type of feed provided.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms, collected and reared separately to the adult stage in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of Tenebrio molitor larvae that occur after the reproduction buffer energy threshold is reached.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHEAT BRAN DIET">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Wheat bran diet is used as a control in the study to compare the effects of microplastic feeding on mealworms' growth, survival, and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO-PS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro-PS (MPS) refers to rigid microplastic polystyrene particles used in experiments to study their consumption and degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC PARTICLE SIZE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic particle size refers to the dimensions of microplastic particles, which influence their consumption and degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVORSHIP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survivorship refers to the survival rate of mealworms under different experimental conditions, such as varying microplastic particle sizes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that can induce gut epithelium damage and metabolic dysbiosis in mealworms during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles, often derived from the fragmentation of larger plastic items, that can be biodegraded by mealworms, especially those with sizes below 250 μm.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles, typically less than 5 mm in size, that result from the breakdown of larger plastic items such as polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP), and polyethylene. These particles are pervasive environmental pollutants found in various ecosystems, including marine, terrestrial, and landfill leachate environments. MPs are known to contribute significantly to environmental pollution, particularly in aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems, and are considered emerging pollutants due to their widespread presence and potential ecological impacts.

MPs can be ingested by a variety of organisms, including macroinvertebrates like mealworms (Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus). The ingestion of MPs by mealworms can lead to a range of harmful effects, including weight loss, higher mortality rates, dehydration, malnutrition, growth inhibition, and disruptions in metamorphosis. Additionally, MPs can induce biological stress, oxidative stress, and antioxidant enzyme responses in these organisms during the biodegradation process. Despite these negative physiological responses, mealworms have been studied for their ability to degrade certain types of MPs, with degradation efficiency influenced by the physicochemical properties of the plastics, such as molecular weight and crystallinity.

The environmental impacts of MPs are multifaceted, as they not only pose risks to individual organisms but also contribute to broader ecological challenges. MPs are generated during the biodegradation process by macroinvertebrates and can persist in the environment, leading to long-term pollution. Their presence in ecosystems underscores the need for further research into their effects on organisms and the development of strategies to mitigate their environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YOUNG LARVAL STAGE (YLS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Young larval stage (YLS) refers to the early developmental stage of mealworms, which showed lower microplastic consumption and no measurable degradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIDDLE LARVAL STAGE (MLS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Middle larval stage (MLS) refers to the intermediate developmental stage of mealworms, which showed moderate microplastic consumption and oxidation but no depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OLD LARVAL STAGE (OLS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Old larval stage (OLS) refers to the late developmental stage of mealworms, which showed the highest microplastic consumption and both depolymerization and oxidation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MPS-FED TREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">MPS-fed treatment refers to the experimental group in the study where mealworms were fed microplastics, used to assess consumption and degradation effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (M_N, M_W, M_P)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (M_n, M_w, M_p) refers to the measurements used to evaluate the depolymerization of microplastics by mealworms, with reductions indicating degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROXYL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroxyl groups (-OH) are chemical functional groups detected in frass samples, suggesting a shift from hydrophobic to more hydrophilic surface properties during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups (OH) are chemical groups detected in frass samples, suggesting the formation of hydrogen bonds and carboxylic acids during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups are chemical functional groups observed in FTIR spectra, indicating oxidation of microplastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups (OH) are functional groups detected in frass samples, indicating a shift from hydrophobic to hydrophilic properties during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups (C-OH) are chemical groups that appear on the surface of polystyrene films after biodegradation, indicating oxidation and chemical changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups are chemical groups formed on the surface of polystyrene during microbial degradation, leading to reduced hydrophobicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups (-OH) are detected in polystyrene residues from mealworm frass, suggesting a shift from hydrophobic to hydrophilic properties during degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups are chemical functional groups detected in polystyrene residues, indicating chemical modification during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups are chemical functional groups that appear in frass, indicating a shift from hydrophobic to hydrophilic properties during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl groups (O-H) are observed in the frass samples of T. molitor and Z. atratus, indicating a shift from hydrophobic to hydrophilic properties during PP biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C—N STRETCH VIBRATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">C—N stretch vibration is a chemical modification observed in FTIR spectra, indicating the insertion or replacement of nitrogen in the microplastic structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL AND DEVELOPMENT">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival and development refer to the toxicological effects of microplastic consumption on mealworms, including mortality rates and growth patterns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAMORPHOSIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Metamorphosis in Tenebrio molitor larvae is disrupted by diets like CEL and PE-oxo, while the PE-reg diet supports optimal development.&lt;SEP&gt;Metamorphosis refers to the developmental process of Tenebrio molitor larvae into pupae and adults, with rates varying based on feed type.&lt;SEP&gt;Metamorphosis refers to the developmental transformation of Tenebrio molitor larvae into pupae and adults, which varies depending on the diet provided.&lt;SEP&gt;Metamorphosis refers to the transformation of mealworms from larvae to pupae, which is influenced by their diet, including microplastics and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">YLS refers to young larval stage mealworms, which are studied for their survival and growth when fed with microplastics (MPS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">MLS refers to middle larval stage mealworms, which are studied for their survival, growth, and metamorphosis when fed with microplastics (MPS) and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">OLS refers to old larval stage mealworms, which are studied for their survival, growth, and metamorphosis when fed with microplastics (MPS) and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPATION RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pupation rate measures the percentage of Tenebrio molitor larvae that successfully develop into pupae under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation rate refers to the percentage of mealworms that successfully transform from larvae to pupae, which is influenced by their diet, including microplastics (MPS) and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAL WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Larval weight is a measure of mealworm growth, which is influenced by their diet, including microplastics (MPS) and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FITR SPECTRA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR spectra are used to analyze the chemical composition of microplastics (MPS) feedstock and frass from mealworms, providing insights into biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXICITIES OF MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The toxicities of microplastics refer to their harmful effects on organisms, including weight loss, higher mortality, and altered gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROPHIC TRANSFER OF MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The trophic transfer of microplastics refers to the movement of microplastics through the food chain, raising concerns about their impact on higher trophic levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AQUATIC LUGWORMS (ARENICOLA MARINA)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aquatic lugworms (Arenicola marina) are marine organisms that show a positive correlation between microplastic concentration in sediment and weight loss.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOLSOMIA CANDIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Folsomia candida is a soil-dwelling microarthropod that shows weight reduction and altered reproduction under microplastic exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROPHIC TRANSFER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Trophic transfer refers to the movement of microplastics and associated pollutants through the food chain, posing risks to organisms and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Trophic transfer refers to the movement of microplastics through the food chain, raising concerns about their impact on higher trophic levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECALCITRANT MICROPLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Recalcitrant microplastic waste refers to persistent plastic waste that is difficult to degrade and poses environmental challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORMONAL SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The hormonal system regulates processes such as molting and pupation in mealworms, which can be disrupted by nutrient deficiencies caused by microplastic ingestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYNAMIC ENERGY BUDGET MODEL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The dynamic energy budget model describes how mealworms assimilate energy from food and allocate it for growth, maintenance, and reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD CHAIN">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The food chain is a pathway through which microplastics and associated pollutants can be transferred, raising concerns about environmental and ecological safety.&lt;SEP&gt;The food chain is affected by microplastics, which are ingested by animals and humans, leading to health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;The food chain is impacted by microplastics, which can accumulate and pose risks to organisms and humans.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POPS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Persistent Organic Pollutants (POPs) are hazardous chemicals that can accumulate along with microplastics in the environment, posing risks to organisms and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MPS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">MPS refers to microplastic particles that mealworms can ingest and depolymerize, with effects varying based on particle size and developmental stage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POULTRY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Poultry are animals that can be fed mealworms, which may contain microplastics, raising concerns about trophic transfer and food safety.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FISH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fish are organisms that can be fed mealworms, potentially leading to the ingestion of microplastics and associated pollutants.&lt;SEP&gt;Fish are studied for endocrine disruption caused by the ingestion of polyethylene with sorbed chemical pollutants from the marine environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHICKEN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Chickens are animals that can ingest microplastics through feeding on microplastic-contaminated earthworms, as confirmed by studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EARTHWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Earthworms (Eisenia fetida) are affected by microplastics, which can damage their spermatogenesis and coelomocyte viability.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are organisms that can ingest microplastics, which can then be transferred to predators like chickens through the food chain.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are studied for their gut bacteria, which have the potential to degrade low-density polyethylene, contributing to soil restoration.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms (Lumbricus terrestris) are capable of degrading polyethylene particles, contributing to plastic biodegradation in soil environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PREDATOR-PREY RELATIONSHIP">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The predator-prey relationship is a model that explains the transport of microplastics through the food chain, enhancing their accumulation in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MPS DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">MPS degradation refers to the process by which mealworms break down microplastic particles, particularly at older larval stages with bran supplementation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
      <data key="d1">Particle size affects the consumption rate of microplastics by mealworms, with smaller particles being consumed more readily.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC FEEDING">
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic feeding affects the pupation process of mealworms, influencing their development and survival.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a non-hydrolyzable plastic that members of the Tenebrio genus are hypothesized to degrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a plastic that could potentially be degraded by Tenebrio larvae, though further research is required to validate this capability.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a widely used synthetic polymer and a common thermoplastic material, primarily derived from fossil fuels. It accounts for approximately 6.9% of global plastic production and is extensively utilized in packaging, textiles, beverage containers, and food packaging due to its durability and versatility. However, PET is highly resistant to environmental degradation, contributing significantly to global plastic pollution and microplastic contamination. Despite its persistence in the environment, PET has been the subject of extensive research into biodegradation and recycling.

PET is a common plastic waste that contributes to environmental pollution and is studied for its recycling potential. It can be degraded by specific microbial communities and enzymes, such as *Candida antarctica* lipase (CALB) and cutinase. Laboratory research has demonstrated that certain organisms, such as *Tenebrio molitor* larvae (mealworms), can consume and biodegrade PET, with the process influenced by the polymer's molecular weight and the gut microbiota of the larvae. However, in some cases, mealworms were unable to consume PET due to its thickness and density, as observed in experiments where PET was used in storage jars.

PET has also been studied for its metabolic effects on Black Soldier Fly larvae, showing varying degrees of plastic loss when exposed to these insects. These findings highlight PET's dual role as a major environmental pollutant and a material with potential for biodegradation and recycling, offering hope for mitigating its environmental impact. Overall, PET's slow degradation rate and environmental persistence make it a significant contributor to plastic waste, but ongoing research into microbial, enzymatic, and insect-based degradation processes provides promising avenues for addressing its environmental challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a petro-based polymer that may be studied in future research to understand its biodegradation and oxidative stress effects on invertebrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate is a common plastic that could be studied for biodegradation by mealworms in future research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAFNIA-OBESUMBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hafnia-Obesumbacterium is a bacterial genus that could be involved in the degradation of environmental pollutants like polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Hafnia-Obesumbacterium is a group of bacteria associated with PET degradation in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL IMPLICATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental implications refer to the negative effects of improper plastic disposal, including the release of toxic chemicals, formation of microplastics, and disruption of ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;The environmental implication of microplastics includes their accumulation in organisms and potential entry into the food chain, highlighting the need for biodegradation solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXIC CHEMICALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxic chemicals are harmful substances released into the environment due to improper plastic disposal, contributing to pollution and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REPRODUCTIVE TOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Reproductive toxicity refers to the adverse effects of pollutants, such as microplastics, on the reproductive systems of organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROTOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Neurotoxicity refers to damage to the nervous system caused by exposure to toxic substances, such as microplastics and nanoplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Neurotoxicity refers to the harmful effects of pollutants, such as microplastics, on the nervous systems of organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMMUNE TOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Immune toxicity refers to the adverse effects of pollutants, such as microplastics, on the immune systems of organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OMICS ANALYSES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Omics analyses involve the study of biological systems using techniques such as genomics, metabolomics, and transcriptomics to understand processes like PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-CARBON BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-carbon biodegradation refers to environmentally friendly methods of breaking down plastics, such as PET, with minimal carbon emissions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET RESINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET resins are commercial forms of polyethylene terephthalate used in manufacturing, which can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MW)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight-average molecular weight (Mw) is a measure of the average molecular weight of a polymer, calculated based on the weight of molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight-average molecular weight (Mw) is a measure of the molecular size of polymers, such as PET, which affects their biodegradability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA analysis is a molecular technique used to study microbial communities, such as those in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor, involved in PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA analysis is used to quantify the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae and assess the effects of antibiotic treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLOME ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolome analysis involves studying the metabolites produced during biological processes, such as PET degradation by Tenebrio molitor and its gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolome analysis involves untargeted profiling of metabolites in the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed PET microplastics, using advanced mass spectrometry techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolome analysis is a technique used to study the metabolic pathways involved in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolome analysis is performed to identify key metabolites and understand the metabolic pathways involved in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolome analysis was conducted to identify key metabolites and pathways involved in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolome analysis is performed to identify key metabolites and understand the metabolic pathways involved in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolome analysis was conducted to identify key metabolites and pathways involved in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PET depolymerization refers to the breakdown of PET into smaller molecules, a process facilitated by Tenebrio molitor and its gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYME REPERTOIRE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzyme repertoire refers to the collection of enzymes produced by Tenebrio molitor and its gut microbiota that contribute to PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITIONAL STRESS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutritional stress is a condition that Tenebrio molitor larvae adapt to when utilizing polypropylene (PP) as a carbon source.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritional stress refers to the condition where organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor, adapt to survive and utilize unconventional carbon sources like PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritional stress refers to the metabolic and physiological challenges faced by mealworm larvae due to dietary changes, such as the inclusion of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET CONSUMPTION RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PET consumption rate measures the efficiency of Tenebrio molitor in biodegrading PET, providing insights into its potential for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a common plastic used in packaging, mentioned in the context of biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a plastic polymer that has not been tested for biodegradation by Tenebrio obscurus but is a potential target for future studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a type of plastic that BSF larvae can metabolically interact with, potentially degrading it into smaller particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a widely used synthetic polymer commonly found in packaging, textiles, and various other applications. Known for its resistance to biodegradation due to its stable molecular structure, PET is typically persistent in the environment. However, recent studies have shown that PET can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as yellow mealworms. The biodegradation process involves the larvae chewing and digesting the plastic, with significant molecular weight reduction observed after ingestion. This process is influenced by factors such as the polymer's molecular weight and crystallinity, which affect its susceptibility to degradation.

The role of the gut microbiota in Tenebrio molitor larvae is a key aspect of PET biodegradation. While some descriptions suggest that the degradation process is independent of gut microbes, others emphasize the involvement of gut bacteria, such as Hafnia-Obsumbacter sp., in breaking down PET into intermediate metabolites, which are further metabolized into simpler compounds. These findings highlight the adaptability of the larvae's gut microbiome in facilitating the degradation of PET, contributing to the larvae's survival and energy needs.

Overall, PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae represents a promising area of research, offering potential solutions for managing plastic waste. The interplay between the larvae's physical digestion and their gut microbiota underscores the complexity of this biodegradation process, which is still being actively studied to better understand its mechanisms and optimize its efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET-1 is a commercial PET resin with a number-average molecular weight (Mn) of 24.83 kDa and a weight-average molecular weight (Mw) of 39.33 kDa, used in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 is a specific type of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymer studied for its biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing significant molecular weight reduction after ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 is a specific type of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymer used in the study to assess the consumption and biodegradation rates by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 is a type of polyethylene terephthalate with higher crystallinity and molecular weight, which affects its degradation rate by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 refers to a specific type of polyethylene terephthalate microplastic used in the study to assess biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET-2 is a commercial PET resin with a number-average molecular weight (Mn) of 18.59 kDa and a weight-average molecular weight (Mw) of 29.43 kDa, used in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 is a type of polyethylene terephthalate with lower crystallinity and molecular weight compared to PET-1, leading to higher degradation rates by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 is another specific type of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymer tested alongside PET-1 to evaluate the biodegradation efficiency of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 is another type of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymer studied for its biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher depolymerization extent compared to PET-1.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 is another type of polyethylene terephthalate microplastic used in the study, showing slightly higher consumption rates by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to PET-1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE (PUR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane (PUR) is a petro-based polymer that may be studied in future research to understand its biodegradation and oxidative stress effects on invertebrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a petroleum-based plastic that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae and other macroinvertebrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a petroleum-based plastic used in various applications and is resistant to degradation in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a plastic material that constitutes 7.4% of plastic production and is considered hazardous to the environment and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a plastic polymer that can be biodegraded by certain insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane is a plastic polymer that has been tested for biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, though its degradation process is less studied compared to other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a plastic that mealworms can ingest and degrade, as indicated by experimental data.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a versatile thermoplastic polymer used in various industries and contributes to plastic waste accumulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a polymer used in various applications, including foam and insulation, and is studied for its biodegradation by microbial cultures.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a versatile plastic polymer used in foam, insulation, and coatings, contributing to plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a polymer composed of organic units joined by carbamate links, used in foams, elastomers, and coatings.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a versatile polymer used in various applications, including foams and coatings, and is known for its resistance to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane is a versatile plastic used in various applications, including foams and coatings, and is resistant to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a versatile plastic material that is challenging to biodegrade and contributes to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a versatile plastic material that is challenging to biodegrade and contributes to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by certain microbial cultures, as reported in landfill studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane is another type of plastic that may be investigated for biodegradation by mealworms and other invertebrates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic recycling refers to the process of using enzymes to break down polymers, such as PET, into smaller molecules for recycling or degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DARKLING BEETLE FAMILY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The darkling beetle family (Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae) includes insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading petroleum-based plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The darkling beetle family (Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae) includes insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, that are capable of biodegrading major plastic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;The darkling beetle family (Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae) includes species like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, which are known for their ability to biodegrade petroleum-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYRALID MOTHS FAMILY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The pyralid moths family (Lepidoptera: Pyralidae) includes insect larvae that have been identified to biodegrade major plastic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MACROINVERTEBRATES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Macroinvertebrates, including certain insect larvae, have been identified to biodegrade major plastic polymers, contributing to environmental remediation.</data>
