    <node id="MASS REDUCTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass reduction refers to the decrease in plastic mass as a result of digestion and biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with significant reductions observed for PET and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass reduction refers to the decrease in weight of polystyrene (PS) materials during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPCRS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SPCRs (Specific Plastic Consumption Rates) are metrics used in the study to measure the rate at which Tenebrio molitor larvae consume plastic materials like PET, PS, and LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane (PUR) is a plastic material that Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume, with consumption rates observed in prior research.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a plastic that can be biodegraded by insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanoplastics (NPS) are small plastic particles that are the focus of microbial degradation studies, with significant reductions in size and count observed.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles resulting from the degradation of larger plastic items, posing environmental and health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are small plastic particles that were not detected in the frass of PE-fed and PS-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating their absence after digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are tiny plastic particles that were not detected in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with PE and PS, suggesting efficient degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles resulting from the fragmentation of microplastics, capable of crossing biological barriers and causing harm.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles that were not detected in this study, reducing the risk of toxicity compared to previous observations in non-plastic-degrading macroinvertebrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are even smaller plastic particles than microplastics, resulting from the breakdown of larger plastics, and are considered environmental pollutants.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles generated from the fragmentation of larger microplastics by organisms such as Gammarus duebeni and earthworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles that are rapidly biodegraded by mealworms, with no significant accumulation observed in their frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are plastic particles smaller than 100 nm that can be generated and accumulated in the frass of mealworms during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are tiny plastic particles that pose significant environmental and biological risks, but were not detected in the frass of mealworms after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A condition studied in the experiment where larvae experience a lack of food, leading to a decrease in volumetric length due to reduced reserves.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation in mealworms is studied in relation to their consumption of polystyrene, leading to significant metabolic changes and shifts in energy sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation is used as a control condition in the study to assess the survival and weight changes of mealworms without food.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation refers to the initial condition of Tenebrio molitor larvae before being fed PET-agar, which influenced their forced eating behavior and initial consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation is a treatment condition used in studies to compare the survival rates and PS consumption of mealworm larvae under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation refers to the experimental condition where mealworm larvae are deprived of a nutrition-rich codiet, affecting their survival and PS consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation is a condition studied in mealworms, affecting their lipid content and energy usage during plastic degradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation is a condition studied in insects, particularly its effects on energy reserves such as triglycerides, glycogen, and proteins.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation refers to the condition in which Tenebrio molitor larvae are deprived of food, affecting their growth, development, and survival rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation refers to the condition of insufficient food supply, which can affect the growth and energy allocation of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation refers to the physiological response of Tenebrio molitor larvae when deprived of sufficient food, leading to a decrease in reserve density and volumetric length.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation refers to the physiological response of mealworms to insufficient food supply, which affects their growth, survival, and energy distribution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOTA SHIFT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbiota shift refers to changes in the microbial community within the gut of organisms, such as Zophobas atratus, during the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The shift in gut microbiota refers to the adaptation of Tenebrio molitor larvae's digestive system to new feedstocks like PET, aiding in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) is a common plastic that is resistant to biodegradation, with degradation rates influenced by its molecular weight and physical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a persistent plastic that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, though with lower efficiency compared to PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a plastic material that was studied for its degradation by mealworms, showing changes in surface structure with the appearance of bubble-like protrusions after digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a plastic that has been researched for biodegradation, with yellow mealworms showing potential for breaking it down.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a type of plastic that is toxic to yellow mealworms when ingested in large quantities, affecting their health and growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a widely used plastic with varying structures and properties, affecting its biodegradability and resistance to microbial attack.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDEONELLA SAKAIENSIS 201-F6">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade PET, used as a comparison in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade PET, used as a comparison in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a bacterium known for its high PET-degrading capacity, though its degradation rate is slower compared to Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MW)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (MW) is a critical property of polymers that influences their biodegradation, with lower MW polymers generally being more susceptible to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) is a key parameter used to assess the changes in polymer structure during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) is the mass of a molecule, typically measured in atomic mass units (amu).&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) of polypropylene (PP) affects its depolymerization patterns and biodegradation efficiency by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) refers to the mass of a molecule, which is a critical factor in determining the biodegradability of polypropylene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) refers to the mass of PET polymers, which influences their biodegradability by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight is a critical factor influencing the biodegradation rate of plastics, with higher MW polymers degrading more slowly than lower MW polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) refers to the mass of PET polymers, which influences their biodegradability by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight is a critical factor influencing the biodegradation rate of plastics, with higher MW polymers degrading more slowly than lower MW polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (Mw) is a measure of the mass of a polymer, often used to assess degradation and changes in plastic properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (Mw) is an aging index that represents the average molecular weight of polymers, which decreases during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;The molecular weight of polystyrene microplastics affects their biodegradation rate, with lower MW PS MPs being more easily digested by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDI">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polydispersity index (PDI) is a measure of the broadness of polymer distribution, which increases after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating decomposition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1H NMR SPECTRA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">1H NMR spectra are used in research studies to analyze the molecular structure and degradation products of polymers, such as PE, during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;1H NMR spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical structure of residual polymers from mealworm frass, revealing changes in molecular composition due to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) spectra are used to analyze the breakdown of long-chain polymers during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing the formation of new peaks.&lt;SEP&gt;1H NMR spectra are used to detect chemical modifications in polystyrene residues from mealworm frass, showing evidence of transformations such as the formation of carbonyl and hydroxyl groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENTAMICIN SULFATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used in studies to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used in the suppression test to inhibit gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae, affecting PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae to study its effects on plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae, affecting their ability to biodegrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used to suppress the gut microbiome of mealworms, enabling researchers to study the mealworm's independent role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in T. obscurus larvae, effectively inhibiting their ability to biodegrade polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used in suppression tests to inhibit gut bacteria in mealworms, affecting their ability to biodegrade LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used in the study to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin sulfate is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in larvae, affecting their ability to biodegrade plastics like LDPE and HDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPIROPLASMA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Spiroplasma sp. is a common gut-associated bacterium found in mealworms, contributing to the microbial community structure during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma sp. is a dominant bacterial genus in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with PET biodegradation and metabolic pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma sp. is a dominant genus in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with plastic degradation, including PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma sp. is a genus of bacteria found abundantly in the gut microbiome of mealworms, despite being generally considered a pathogen in insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma sp. is a genus of bacteria generally regarded as a pathogen or male-killing bacterium in insects, though not detected in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma sp. is a genus of bacteria involved in the degradation of polypropylene (PP) and lignocellulosic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma sp. is a genus of bacteria that was dominant in the control group of Tenebrio molitor larvae, but its role in PET degradation is not well understood.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma sp. is a genus of bacteria that was dominant in the control group of Tenebrio molitor larvae, but its role in PET degradation is not well understood.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma sp. is a genus of gut bacteria in mealworms strongly associated with LDPE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC SUPPRESSION TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic suppression tests are used to study the role of gut bacteria in the biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The antibiotic suppression test evaluates the effect of antibiotics, such as gentamicin, on the ability of larvae to degrade polystyrene by suppressing gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;The antibiotic suppression test was conducted to examine the role of gut bacteria in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The antibiotic suppression test evaluates the effect of antibiotics, such as gentamicin, on the ability of mealworms to depolymerize and biodegrade LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;The antibiotic suppression test is a method used to determine the role of gut microbes in plastic biodegradation by inhibiting microbial activity with antibiotics like gentamicin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1H NMR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">1H NMR (Proton Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) was used to study the structural changes in polypropylene during biodegradation by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;1H NMR spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical shifts and integration of signals in the frass, providing insights into the depolymerization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) is a method used to study the chemical composition of residual PET polymers after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) is a method used to study the chemical composition of residual PET polymers after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) is used to analyze the chemical structure of residual PS in frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) was used to analyze the breakdown of long-chain polymers during PET degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical structure of polystyrene residues during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) is a method used to analyze the chemical changes in LDPE during biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYSGONOMONADACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dysgonomonadaceae is a family of bacteria that increased in relative abundance in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum, capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. is a bacterial strain strongly associated with polystyrene degradation, isolated from the guts of insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. is a gut bacterium associated with the degradation of medium molecular weight polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, potentially involved in the degradation of medium molecular weight polypropylene (PP-M).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAFNIA-OBESUMBACTERIUM SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hafnia-Obesumbacterium sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hafnia-Obesumbacterium sp. is another dominant bacterial genus in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, involved in PET degradation and lipid metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITROBACTER SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrobacter sp. are bacteria associated with the gut microbiome of PS- and PE-fed mealworms, known for degrading long-chain polymers and hydrocarbons.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a bacterial genus strongly associated with the degradation of both polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a bacterial genus associated with the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a bacterium that is more abundant in plastic-only diets and is associated with the degradation of polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a gut bacterium associated with plastic diets, potentially involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a gut bacterium strongly associated with the biodegradation of both polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a bacterial strain significantly related to both polystyrene and polyethylene diets in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a genus of bacteria within the Enterobacteriaceae family, associated with the degradation of polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) by T. molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a bacterial genus strongly associated with the gut microbiome of Zophobas atratus larvae fed polypropylene, potentially playing a role in PP biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter sp. is a bacterial species associated with the gut microbiome of Zophobas atratus larvae during polypropylene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOCOCCUS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactococcus sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus sp. is a genus of bacteria that was dominant in the control group of Tenebrio molitor larvae, but its role in PET degradation is not well understood.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus sp. is a genus of bacteria that was dominant in the control group of Tenebrio molitor larvae, but its role in PET degradation is not well understood.</data>
