    </node>
    <node id="DYSGONOMONAS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dysgonomonas sp. is a dominant bacterial genus in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, correlated with lipid metabolism and PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Dysgonomonas sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WB-REARED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">WB-reared larvae are Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with a normal diet, used as a control group in the study of PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PET-fed larvae are Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with PET, used to study the biodegradation of PET and changes in gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA GENE AMPLICONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA gene amplicons were sequenced to analyze the gut microbial communities of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with PET, WB, or unfed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHANNON INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Shannon index is a biodiversity measure used to assess the diversity of microbial flora in the intestinal microbiota of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index is an ecological metric used to quantify the diversity and evenness of gut microbial communities in Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index was used to measure the diversity of gut microbial communities in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index is a measure of microbial diversity used to analyze the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index is a measure of microbial diversity used in the study to assess the richness and evenness of bacterial communities in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index is a measure of diversity in the gut microbiome of mealworms, used to assess the impact of different feeding conditions on microbial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index is a measure used to investigate the richness and diversity of the gut microbiome in mealworms under different diet conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANOSIM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Analysis of similarities (ANOSIM) was used to statistically analyze the differences in gut microbial communities among Tenebrio molitor larvae groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OTU">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Operational taxonomic units (OTUs) are used to classify gut microbial communities based on DNA sequencing data, showing changes in diversity over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational taxonomic units (OTUs) were used to classify and analyze the gut microbial communities of Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms capable of breaking down plastic materials, such as polystyrene and polyethylene, contributing to environmental cleanup efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms capable of breaking down plastic materials, such as polystyrene, contributing to environmental cleanup efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms capable of breaking down plastics, such as Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 and Aspergillus tubingensis.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading bacteria, such as Enterococcus sp., are associated with the degradation of PET and other plastics in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms isolated from mealworms' guts that can break down plastics such as EPS, PE, and NPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms isolated from the gut microbiome of mealworms, studied for their role in breaking down plastics like EPS and PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms capable of breaking down plastics, such as Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 and potentially Mixta tenebrionis.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms in the gut of mealworms that contribute to the biodegradation of plastics like PS and LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading bacteria are specific strains of bacteria in the gut of mealworms that contribute to the breakdown of plastics like PS and PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC MIXTURE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An antibiotic mixture (gentamicin sulfate, erythrocin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline, ampicillin, and kanamycin) was used to suppress gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae to study PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (MW) is a critical factor affecting the biodegradability of PET, with lower MW polymers being more easily degraded by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) is a critical factor affecting the biodegradability of PET, with lower MW polymers being more easily degraded by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) is a key factor influencing the biodegradation of polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor, with different degradation patterns observed for low, medium, and high MW PP.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight is a critical factor in the degradation and stability of polymers like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight is a property of polymers like PET that affects their degradation rate by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher molecular weight leading to slower degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight is a critical factor affecting the biodegradability of polyethylene (PE) by insect larvae, with lower molecular weight polymers being more easily degraded.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight is a key factor influencing the biodegradability of polymers, with lower molecular weight polymers being more easily degraded.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight is a key property of polymers like polyethylene that influences their biodegradation, with lower molecular weight polymers being more easily degraded.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight refers to the mass of a polymer, which changes during the depolymerization and biodegradation processes carried out by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight refers to the size of polymer chains, such as those in low-density polyethylene (LDPE), which decreases during biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio obscurus and Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight refers to the size of polystyrene polymers, which influences the biodegradation efficiency and pattern in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidases are enzymes that are over-expressed in the gut microbiome of PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to the oxidation and degradation of PET intermediates.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidases are enzymes that play a role in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP) by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNFED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The unfed group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae that were not fed any specific diet, used as a control to study the effects of PET and WB diets on gut microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The PET-fed group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae that were fed PET, showing significant shifts in gut microbial communities compared to WB-fed and unfed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEREPHTHALIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Terephthalic acid (TPA) is a key intermediate produced during the biodegradation of PET, which is further metabolized into protocatechuic acid and catechol.&lt;SEP&gt;Terephthalic acid (TPA) is a key intermediate produced during the biodegradation of PET, which is further metabolized into protocatechuic acid and catechol.&lt;SEP&gt;Terephthalic acid is a breakdown product of PET hydrolysis, resulting from the action of enzymes like Candida antarctica lipase (CALB) and cutinase.&lt;SEP&gt;Terephthalic acid is an intermediate metabolite produced during the biodegradation of PET by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Terephthalic acid is a breakdown product of PET hydrolysis, resulting from the action of enzymes like Candida antarctica lipase (CALB) and cutinase.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYLENE GLYCOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethylene glycol is a byproduct of PET degradation, metabolized by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae into simpler compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene glycol is a chemical proposed as a potential product of polyethylene biodegradation, though its presence in degradation studies remains inconclusive.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene glycol is a chemical that was not detected in significant amounts during the degradation experiments, suggesting minimal biochemical oxidation of LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene glycol is a potential byproduct of LDPE biodegradation, which can be detected using FTIR analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene glycol is a valuable chemical that could potentially be produced through the biodegradation of polyethylene, offering a pathway for biochemical recycling.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene glycol is a valuable chemical that has been proposed as a potential product of polyethylene biodegradation, though its presence in degradation studies remains inconclusive.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene glycol is a hydrolyzed product of PET degradation, formed during the breakdown of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene glycol is a hydrolyzed product of PET degradation, formed during the breakdown of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTOCATECHUIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Protocatechuic acid is a major intermediate compound formed during the aerobic decomposition of PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Protocatechuic acid is a metabolite produced during the biodegradation of terephthalic acid by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Protocatechuic acid is a metabolite formed during the degradation of terephthalic acid, a product of PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Protocatechuic acid is a metabolite identified during PET biodegradation in Tenebrio molitor larvae, suggesting its involvement in the metabolic pathways of PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Protocatechuic acid is a metabolite formed during the degradation of terephthalic acid, a product of PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Protocatechuic acid is a metabolite identified during PET biodegradation in Tenebrio molitor larvae, suggesting its involvement in the metabolic pathways of PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOLIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycolic acid is an intermediate metabolite produced during the oxidation of ethylene glycol by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOLIC ALDEHYDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycolic aldehyde is an intermediate metabolite produced during the oxidation of ethylene glycol by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOLIC ALDEHYDE DEHYDROGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycolic aldehyde dehydrogenase is an enzyme that is up-regulated in the PET-fed group, contributing to the metabolism of ethylene glycol.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYOXYLATE REDUCTASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glyoxylate reductase is an enzyme that is up-regulated in the PET-fed group, contributing to the metabolism of ethylene glycol.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DECARBOXYLASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Decarboxylase is an enzyme that is over-represented in the PET-fed group, potentially contributing to PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEPOLYMERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Depolymerase is an enzyme that is over-represented in the PET-fed group, potentially contributing to PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDOREDUCTASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidoreductase is an enzyme that is over-represented in the PET-fed group, potentially contributing to PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEHYDROGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dehydrogenase is an enzyme that is over-represented in the PET-fed group, potentially contributing to PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Dehydrogenase is an enzyme with a higher abundance in PP-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, suggesting its involvement in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Dehydrogenase is an enzyme that plays a role in the degradation of lignin and polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHYL-ACCEPTING CHEMOTAXIS PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methyl-accepting chemotaxis proteins (MCPs) are proteins that are significantly enriched in the PET-fed group, aiding in bacterial chemotaxis towards aromatic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAGELLIN-RELATED PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flagellin-related proteins are proteins that are significantly enriched in the PET-fed group, aiding in bacterial adhesion and metabolism of organic matter.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TCA CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The TCA cycle (tricarboxylic acid cycle) is a metabolic pathway that generates energy through the oxidation of acetyl-CoA derived from long-chain fatty acids during the degradation of polystyrene and lignin.&lt;SEP&gt;The TCA cycle (tricarboxylic acid cycle) is a metabolic pathway where intermediate metabolites from PET degradation are further transformed and mineralized into CO2.&lt;SEP&gt;The TCA cycle is a metabolic pathway utilized by gut microbes and host larvae to further metabolize intermediate products of PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The TCA cycle is a metabolic pathway through which styrene monomers are broken down after being converted into phenylacetyl-CoA.&lt;SEP&gt;The TCA cycle is a metabolic pathway involved in energy production, regulated by magnesium and affected by dietary polystyrene in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteria are microorganisms that play a crucial role in biodegradation processes, including the degradation of plastics like PET, through mechanisms such as flagellar adhesion and metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET is a common plastic polymer that can be degraded by certain bacteria and insects, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae, into smaller molecules and metabolites.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a widely used plastic polymer that has been a focus of biodegradation research due to its prevalence in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid metabolism involves the biochemical processes related to the breakdown and synthesis of lipids, which are altered in LG3 during the degradation of polystyrene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid metabolism is a biological process that is upregulated in plastic-degrading insects like G. mellonella and T. molitor in response to plastic feeding, aiding in energy storage and assimilation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid metabolism refers to the biochemical pathways involved in the breakdown and synthesis of lipids, which are reprogrammed in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid metabolism refers to the metabolic pathways involving lipids, which are altered in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid metabolism is upregulated in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation, likely due to energy reservation, stress response, and assimilation of biodegraded intermediates.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid metabolism pathways are enriched in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating their role in energy reserves and cellular processes during PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid metabolism is upregulated in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation, likely due to energy reservation, stress response, and assimilation of biodegraded intermediates.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid metabolism pathways are enriched in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating their role in energy reserves and cellular processes during PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAGELLA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flagella are appendages on bacteria that enhance adhesion to surfaces and facilitate electron transport, aiding in the metabolism of organic matter like plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATP-BINDING CASSETTE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ATP-binding cassette (K06147) is a transport system identified in bacteria that aids in the uptake of small molecules, such as PET degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEPTIDE/NICKEL TRANSPORT SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The peptide/nickel transport system substrate-binding protein (K02035) is involved in the transport of small molecules, including those derived from plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MULTIPLE SUGAR TRANSPORT SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The multiple sugar transport system permease protein (K02025) is a transport mechanism that facilitates the uptake of sugars and other small molecules, potentially including plastic degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LC-MS (LIQUID CHROMATOGRAPHY-MASS SPECTROMETRY)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">LC-MS is a technique used to analyze non-targeted metabolites, providing insights into the pathways of PET biodegradation by gut microbiota and larval hosts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIERARCHICAL CLUSTERING ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Hierarchical clustering analysis is used to distinguish metabolites between PET-fed and agar-fed larvae, revealing significant differences in metabolic pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Hierarchical clustering analysis was performed to group and analyze the gut microbiome data based on similarities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HMDB CLASSIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">HMDB classification categorizes metabolites into superclasses and subclasses, identifying up-regulated metabolites like lipids and lipid-like molecules in PET-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG PATHWAYS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KEGG pathways are used to analyze differentially expressed metabolites, revealing enriched pathways such as glycerophospholipid metabolism and arginine and proline metabolism in PET-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCEROPHOSPHOLIPID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycerophospholipid metabolism is a pathway enriched in PET-fed larvae, associated with maintaining cell membrane properties and mitochondrial functions during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycerophospholipid metabolism is involved in membrane composition and repair, observed in mealworms during the biodegradation of ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARGININE AND PROLINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Arginine and proline metabolism is a critical pathway for osmotic equilibrium and protein structure protection, upregulated in response to microplastic-induced stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Arginine and proline metabolism is a metabolic pathway involved in osmotic equilibrium and protein structure protection, significantly perturbed in mealworms fed with medium- and ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUTATHIONE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glutathione metabolism is a stress response pathway that is upregulated in organisms exposed to oxidative stress caused by plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Glutathione metabolism is a stress response pathway that may be induced by microplastics, aiding in oxidative stress defense during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Glutathione metabolism is a stress response pathway upregulated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics, indicating oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALPHA-LINOLENIC ACID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Alpha-linolenic acid metabolism is a pathway enriched in PET-fed larvae, potentially linked to plastic degradation and oxidative stress responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHER LIPID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Ether lipid metabolism is a pathway involved in plastic degradation, enriched in PET-fed larvae as part of their metabolic response to plastic feeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMINO SUGAR AND NUCLEOTIDE SUGAR METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Amino sugar and nucleotide sugar metabolism is a key carbohydrate pathway significantly altered in PET-fed larvae, linked to plastic degradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Amino sugar and nucleotide sugar metabolism refers to the biochemical pathways involved in the synthesis and breakdown of these sugars, which is relevant in the metabolic processes of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Amino sugar and nucleotide sugar metabolism refers to the biochemical pathways involved in the synthesis and breakdown of these sugars, which is relevant in the metabolic processes of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEARSON ALGORITHM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Pearson algorithm is used to calculate correlations between dominant bacteria and differential metabolites, revealing relationships between gut microbes and metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOHYDRATE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbohydrate metabolism involves the breakdown and utilization of carbohydrates, which is correlated with PET degradation in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROBIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aerobic bacteria are microorganisms that contribute to the biodegradation of PET in the presence of oxygen, working alongside other gut microbiota in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Aerobic bacteria in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae contribute to PET oxidation and depolymerization during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FACULTATIVE ANAEROBIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Facultative anaerobic bacteria in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae contribute to hydrolysis, fermentation, and mineralization of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAEROBIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Anaerobic bacteria are microorganisms that contribute to PET biodegradation in the absence of oxygen, working synergistically with other gut microbiota in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic bacteria in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae play a role in the hydrolysis and fermentation of PET during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET HYDROLASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET hydrolases are enzymes secreted by gut microbes and host larvae to initiate PET degradation by breaking down the polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET OXIDASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET oxidases are enzymes involved in the oxidation of PET, contributing to its depolymerization during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOTAXIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemotaxis refers to the movement of gut microbes toward PET as a carbon source, aiding in its degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotaxis refers to the movement of gut microbes towards PET metabolic intermediates, aiding in the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotaxis refers to the movement of gut microbes towards PET metabolic intermediates, aiding in the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN FIXING MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-fixing microbes in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae convert dissolved nitrogen into ammonium, mitigating nitrogen deficiency during PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERMEDIATE PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Intermediate products are compounds formed during PET degradation, which are further metabolized by gut microbes and host larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate products are formed during the biodegradation of PET by mealworms, which are further broken down by gut microbiomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate products are formed during the biodegradation of PET by mealworms, which are further broken down by gut microbiomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-HYDROXYPHENYLACETATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">4-Hydroxyphenylacetate is a compound degraded by Xanthobacter 124X, contributing to the understanding of aromatic compound biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;4-Hydroxyphenylacetate is a major intermediate compound formed during the aerobic decomposition of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3,4-DIHYDROXYPHENYLACETATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">3,4-Dihydroxyphenylacetate is a major intermediate compound formed during the aerobic decomposition of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3-PHENYLPROPANOATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">3-Phenylpropanoate is a major intermediate compound formed during the aerobic decomposition of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3-HYDROXYANTHRANILATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">3-Hydroxyanthranilate is a major intermediate compound formed during the aerobic decomposition of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FORMIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Formic acid is a compound formed during the anaerobic decomposition of PET, utilized by gut microbes as an electron donor.&lt;SEP&gt;Formic acid is used as a solvent in the mobile phase for liquid chromatography in metabolomic profiling.&lt;SEP&gt;Formic acid is used as a solvent in the mobile phase for liquid chromatography in metabolomic profiling.&lt;SEP&gt;Formic acid is an LC-MS grade chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Formic acid is used in the mobile phase for lipid analysis in the UHPLC system.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FACULTATIVE BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Facultative bacteria are microorganisms that can switch between aerobic and anaerobic metabolism, playing a role in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOTAXIS PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemotaxis proteins are involved in the movement of microorganisms toward or away from chemical stimuli, playing a role in the gut microbiota's interaction with PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSPORT PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Transport proteins facilitate the movement of molecules across cell membranes, aiding in the biodegradation process of PET by gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Transport proteins facilitate the penetration of depolymerized PET products into bacterial cells for further metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Transport proteins facilitate the penetration of depolymerized PET products into bacterial cells for further metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAGELLA FORMATION-RELATED PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flagella formation-related proteins are involved in the development of flagella, which help microorganisms move and interact with PET during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNERGISTIC METABOLIC PATHWAYS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Synergistic metabolic pathways refer to the combined efforts of gut microbiota and host larvae in the biodegradation, mineralization, and assimilation of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET DEGRADATION KEYS SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PET degradation key species are specific microorganisms isolated from the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae that play a critical role in PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGAL ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungal enzymes are biological catalysts produced by fungi that play a role in the biodegradation of plastics, breaking down complex polymers into simpler molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungal enzymes are biological catalysts produced by fungi that play a role in the biodegradation of plastics, breaking down complex polymers into simpler molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungal enzymes are catalytic tools used for the degradation of polyethylene terephthalate (PET), contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOSTABLE IS PETASE VARIANTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermostable Is PETase variants are engineered enzymes designed for the efficient degradation of PET, enhancing plastic waste management efforts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLORIDE IONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloride ions are chemical compounds found in high salinity wastewater that accelerate the removal of micropollutants over light-driven catalysts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROXYL RADICALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroxyl radicals are reactive oxygen species generated for water purification, playing a key role in the degradation of pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOMONOSPORA FUSCA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermomonospora fusca is a thermophilic bacterium capable of biodegrading aliphatic-aromatic copolymers, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALIPHATIC-AROMATIC COPOLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aliphatic-aromatic copolymers are synthetic polymers that can be biodegraded by thermophilic bacteria like Thermomonospora fusca.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC PET DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic PET degradation refers to the breakdown of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) using enzymes, offering a sustainable approach to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANT BIOMASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plant biomass is a renewable resource that can be enzymatically degraded, offering potential applications in waste management and bioenergy production.&lt;SEP&gt;Plant biomass is organic material derived from plants, often studied for its potential in biodegradation and as a renewable resource.&lt;SEP&gt;Plant biomass refers to organic materials derived from plants, which can be degraded enzymatically for sustainable waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Synthetic polymers are man-made materials, such as plastics, that are often targeted in biodegradation research due to their environmental persistence.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers are man-made materials, such as plastics, that can be enzymatically degraded to address environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers, such as plastics, are environmental pollutants that are being studied for biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers, such as plastics, are materials that can be enzymatically degraded to reduce environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers, such as plastics, are low in bio-reactivity and are consumed by mealworms in experiments to study biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers are man-made materials, such as polystyrene, that are often resistant to biodegradation but can be broken down by certain microorganisms and insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers, such as polystyrene, are targeted for biodegradation by A. diaperinus larvae and their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers are man-made materials, such as plastics, that are resistant to natural degradation and contribute to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOSTABLE POLYESTER HYDROLASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermostable polyester hydrolases are enzymes engineered for high-temperature degradation of polyesters, enhancing plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER SIZE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer size refers to the molecular weight and structure of polymers, which can influence their biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
