    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Residual plastics are the remaining microplastic particles found in mealworm intestines and frass after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Residual plastics are the remaining plastic fragments produced during the biodegradation process by Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(ETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET) is a common plastic that can be degraded and assimilated by specific bacteria, such as Ideonella sakaiensis.&lt;SEP&gt;Poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET) is a common plastic used in bottles and packaging, which can be degraded and assimilated by certain bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Poly(ethylene terephthalate) is a type of plastic that can be degraded and assimilated by certain bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET) is a common plastic used in bottles and packaging, known for its resistance to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET) is a widely used plastic that contributes to environmental pollution due to its resistance to natural degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d7f6907ee7ce47b00537b1458fcc029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </node>
