      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect larvae, specifically those used in the study, are organisms capable of degrading polystyrene and are analyzed for their growth and biomass composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, are organisms capable of biodegrading plastics through their digestive systems and gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, are organisms capable of biodegrading plastics through their digestive systems and gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, are studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics and their toxicological responses to plastic exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect larvae, such as mealworms, are known to host gut bacteria that can degrade plastics, contributing to the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect larvae, such as those from which PVC-degrading bacteria are isolated, play a role in the biodegradation of plastic pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
