    <node id="GUT MICROFLORA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microflora are microorganisms in the digestive system of mealworms that play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microflora refers to the microorganisms present in the digestive tract of insect larvae, which play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microflora refers to the microorganisms present in the digestive tracts of organisms, which play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microflora refers to the microorganisms present in the digestive tracts of organisms, which play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIONIDAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrionidae is a family of darkling beetles, including species like Tenebrio molitor, known for their ability to biodegrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrionidae is the family of beetles to which both Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor belong, sharing similar characteristics and capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;The larvae of the Tenebrionidae family, commonly known as mealworms, have been identified as potential consumers of polystyrene and other plastics, contributing to plastic waste reduction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a common plastic used in bottles and packaging, known for its strength and recyclability.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a common plastic used in packaging and contributes to environmental pollution due to its resistance to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a widely used plastic polymer, known for its durability and resistance to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a type of plastic that can be ingested by organisms like Achatina fulica, leading to adverse effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a thermoplastic polymer used in packaging and textiles, and its biodegradation has been explored in studies involving Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a common plastic used in various applications, including packaging, and is a target for biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a common plastic used in various applications, including packaging, and is a target for biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a common plastic used in the study to evaluate its impact on insect metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a plastic substrate that impacts the central carbon metabolism of Black Soldier Fly larvae, elevating pyrimidine metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a plastic pollutant that can be degraded and assimilated by specific bacteria, such as Ideonella sakaiensis.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a type of plastic found in animal-based traditional medicinal materials as microplastics (MPs).&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a common thermoplastic polymer used in fibers, beverage containers, and packaging, known for its durability and recyclability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a durable plastic that can be degraded by marine microbes under specific conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a plastic polymer that has been shown to be degraded by lesser waxworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of polyethylene known for its strength and resistance to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is another type of plastic that can be biodegraded by marine microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene is a type of plastic waste that mealworms can biodegrade, though with lower efficiency compared to low-density polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of polyethylene plastic that has been studied for biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor and other organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a polyethylene variant with closely packed linear chains, making it more resistant to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of plastic that has been studied for its internalization in the digestive system of blue mussels (Mytilus edulis L.).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLESIOPHTHALMUS DAVIDIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plesiophthalmus davidis is a macroinvertebrate species with plastic-degrading abilities, aiding in the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Plesiophthalmus davidis is a species of darkling beetle larvae capable of biodegrading plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plesiophthalmus davidis is a species of darkling beetle larvae that has been studied for its ability to biodegrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plesiophthalmus davidis larvae have gut microbial flora capable of fast and facile biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Plesiophthalmus davidis is a species of darkling beetle larvae that can biodegrade plastics like polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Plesiophthalmus davidis larvae are insects whose gut microbial flora can rapidly biodegrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC (MP)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics are small plastic particles (&lt;0.1 mm) that can be consumed and biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, often mixed with co-diets like wheat bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO-DIET">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A co-diet, such as bran, is used to reduce oxidative stress and enhance metabolic activities in organisms exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-diets, such as wheat bran, fruits, or vegetables, are used alongside plastics to enhance the growth and plastic degradation capabilities of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-diet refers to the combination of bran and plastics fed to mealworms to study their impact on plastic biodegradation efficiency and gut microbiome composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-diet refers to the combination of polystyrene (PS) with wheat bran or corn flour, used to evaluate the effect of additional nutrients on PS biodegradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEMPERATURE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Temperature affects the survival and biodegradation rates of mealworms, with an optimal range of 25-28°C.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is a critical factor affecting the survival and plastic consumption rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae, with optimal viability between 25–28°C.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is one of the influencing factors analyzed in the study to determine its effect on the biodegradation capabilities of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is one of the influencing factors monitored in the study to optimize the biodegradation process by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature variations, caused by COVID-19 restrictions, affect mealworm eating habits and mobility.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature variations, caused by COVID-19 restrictions, affect mealworm eating habits and mobility.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is an environmental factor that influences the rate of biodegradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is a factor affecting the biodegradation rates of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with optimal degradation occurring at 25°C.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is an environmental factor studied for its impact on the survival rate and polystyrene degradation efficiency of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is an environmental factor that affects the rate of polystyrene degradation by mealworms, with optimal degradation occurring at 25°C and 30°C.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMIDITY">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Humidity is a key environmental parameter measured in the study to ensure optimal conditions for mealworms during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Humidity is an environmental factor controlled at 60–80% to ensure optimal conditions for Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Humidity variations, caused by COVID-19 restrictions, affect mealworm eating habits and mobility.&lt;SEP&gt;Humidity variations, caused by COVID-19 restrictions, affect mealworm eating habits and mobility.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REARING DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Rearing density refers to the number of Tenebrio molitor larvae per unit area, with a recommended density of 2–3 larvae/cm² for plastic degradation tests.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIMPSON INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Simpson index is a metric used to evaluate species richness and diversity in the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Simpson index is an ecological metric used to measure the dominance of species within the gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAY-CURTIS DISSIMILARITY INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Bray-Curtis dissimilarity index is used to quantify differences in gut microbial community structure in Tenebrio molitor larvae based on dietary changes, such as the inclusion of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DNA extraction is a method used to analyze the gut microorganisms of insects, such as Alphitobius diaperinus, to understand their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA extraction is a method used to analyze the microbial communities in the guts of organisms, such as Alphitobius diaperinus, to understand their role in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA extraction is a method used to collect and analyze gut microbial samples from Tenebrio molitor larvae to study their role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA extraction is performed on larvae samples to analyze the gut microbiome and its role in PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA extraction is a methodological step in the study to analyze the microflora present in the intestines of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA extraction is a process used to isolate DNA from samples, in this case, from larvae intestines, for further genetic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA extraction is a method used to isolate bacterial genomic DNA from the gut of mealworm larvae for microbial community analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DARKLING BEETLES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Darkling beetles (Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae) are a family of insects that include Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus, both of which have demonstrated the ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles (family Tenebrionidae) are insects known to chew and eat plastic packaging materials, similar to mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles are insects from which plastic-degrading bacterial strains, such as Bacillus sp. YP1 and Enterobacter asburiae YT1, have been isolated.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles, including species like Tribolium castaneum and Zophobas morio, are insects that can chew and degrade various plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles, including Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, are studied for their potential in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles, including Tenebrio molitor, are insects known for their ability to biodegrade various plastics, such as polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles, including Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, are studied for their potential in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles, including Tenebrio molitor, are insects known for their ability to biodegrade various plastics, such as polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles, specifically the yellow mealworm beetle, produce larvae (mealworms) that can consume and degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles, belonging to the family Tenebrionidae, are insects known for their ability to chew and ingest plastic materials, contributing to biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOTH LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Moth larvae are insects that have been studied for their ability to host plastic-degrading microbial species, contributing to plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER SP. STRAIN NYZ450">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain involved in the co-culture degradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a plastic-degrading bacterium isolated from Plodia interpunctella, capable of degrading low-density polyethylene in co-culture.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) when co-cultured with Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of yellow mealworm larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from yellow mealworm larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP. STRAIN NYZ451">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain involved in the co-culture degradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a plastic-degrading bacterium isolated from Plodia interpunctella, capable of degrading low-density polyethylene in co-culture.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) when co-cultured with Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of yellow mealworm larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain isolated from yellow mealworm larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PACBIO">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PacBio is a sequencing platform used for full-length 16S rRNA gene analysis in gut microbiome research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPORE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">NanoPore is a sequencing platform used for full-length 16S rRNA gene analysis in gut microbiome research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISEQ">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">HiSeq is a sequencing platform used for shotgun metagenome sequencing in gut microbiome studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEXTSEQ">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">NextSeq is a sequencing platform used for shotgun metagenome sequencing in gut microbiome studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NOVASEQ">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">NovaSeq is a sequencing platform used for shotgun metagenome sequencing in gut microbiome studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOGRAVIMETRICAL ANALYSIS (TGA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermogravimetrical analysis (TGA) is a method used to determine the thermal stability of polymers, which decreases during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetrical analysis (TGA) is a method used to determine the thermal stability of polymers, with decreased stability indicating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOSPHATE BUFFERED WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phosphate buffered water (pH 7.0) is used to rinse extracted gut tissue for DNA extraction in gut microbiome studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SODIUM DODECYL SULFATE (SDS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) is a chemical used in pretreatment processes to remove biofilm or attached biomass from residual plastics during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) is a chemical used in pretreatment to remove biofilm or attached biomass from residual plastics, ensuring accurate biodegradation results.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) is a detergent used to clean plastic samples before water contact angle analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) is a detergent used to remove biofilm from the surface of polystyrene films during degradation analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) is a chemical used to wash PE films for accurate weight measurement during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) is a detergent used to wash polyethylene (PE) films for accurate weight measurement during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) is a detergent used to treat polystyrene films during biodegradation assays to remove attached bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (TG-FTIR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TG-FTIR is a technique used to identify decomposition products from polymer samples, providing insights into biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (1H NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">1H NMR is used to study chemical modifications of polymers after biodegradation by analyzing new peaks in the spectra of biotreated polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIFFERENTIAL SCANNING CALORIMETRIC (DSC) ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DSC analysis assesses thermal properties of materials, with shifts in glass transition temperature (Tg) indicating polymer biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPY (SEM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM is a technique used to observe biofilm formation on PE mulching films by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to characterize biofilm formation on PE mulching films by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to observe microbial colonization, biofilm formation, and physical changes like cracks and holes on polymer surfaces during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is used to study the physical changes in plastic polymers and gut microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is a technique used to observe the surface changes in HDPE film after degradation by Bacillus sp. PELW2042.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is a technique used to observe physical changes in polystyrene caused by mealworm larvae and their gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to analyze the physical structure of polyurethane foam before and after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM was used to observe micro-morphological images of feedstocks and frass from mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to observe microscopic morphological changes on the surface of PS films during degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is a technique used to investigate physical changes in degraded polystyrene films.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to characterize the surface morphology and size-frequency distribution of residual plastic particles in frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER CONTACT ANGLE (WCA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">WCA is used to assess changes in the hydrophilicity of the PS film's surface during degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;WCA measures changes in hydrophobicity on polymer surfaces, serving as an indicator of biodegradation activity by gut microbes and enzymes.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) is a technique used to study the hydrophobicity of plastic surfaces during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) is a measure of surface hydrophobicity, used to analyze changes in polystyrene films after degradation by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) is a measurement used to determine the hydrophobicity of PS film surfaces during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle measurement is used to determine changes in the hydrophobicity of polystyrene film surfaces after bacterial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) is a method used to study changes in hydrophobicity of degraded polystyrene films.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) is a measurement used to assess the hydrophobicity or hydrophilicity of materials, such as polyethylene, during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Water Contact Angle (WCA) analysis is performed to study the surface properties of polypropylene (PP) foam and frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) is a measure of surface hydrophobicity, which decreases as polymers like polypropylene undergo biodegradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) is a technique used to assess changes in the surface properties of polystyrene (PS) during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOTOPIC LABELING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Isotopic labeling, using 14C or 13C, assesses biodegradation and mineralization of plastics by measuring the release of labeled CO2 and incorporation into biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO2 AND METHANE PRODUCTION-BASED BIODEGRADATION METHODS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">These methods, developed by ASTM and ISO, evaluate biodegradation of plastics under aerobic or anaerobic conditions by measuring CO2 and CH4 production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARCH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Starch is a biodegradable polymer that has been blended with polypropylene to enhance its biodegradability.&lt;SEP&gt;Starch is a co-diet used in biodegradation studies that can cause misinterpretation of results in thermogravimetrical analysis (TGA).&lt;SEP&gt;Starch is an additive in PE-oxo, used as a substrate for mealworm larvae in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Starch is a dietary component that, along with protein and vitamins, plays a crucial role in the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIVE/DEAD BACLIGHT BACTERIAL VIABILITY KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">This kit is used to characterize the viability of bacterial cells on polystyrene films using fluorescence microscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;This kit is used to stain and observe live and dead cells during microscopic observations of microbial activity on plastic surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTICIZERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plasticizers are additives in plastic foam that may contribute to the formation of toxic compounds during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Plasticizers are additives used in plastics that can affect human health by causing endocrine disorders, asthma, and other diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Plasticizers are biodegradable additives in polymers that microorganisms can consume, potentially misleading biodegradation assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;Plasticizers are chemical additives found in PS and PE foams, analyzed using GC-MS to understand their role in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Plasticizers are chemicals added to plastics to increase their flexibility, durability, and workability, but they can also contribute to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="14C-LABELED POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">14C-labeled polymers are used in isotopic labeling to track biodegradation and mineralization by measuring the release of 14CO2 and 14CH4.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C-LABELED POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">13C-labeled polymers are used to study biodegradation and mineralization by measuring δ13C in released CO2 and biomass components.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMERICAN SOCIETY FOR TESTING AND MATERIALS (ASTM)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ASTM develops standardized methods for evaluating the biodegradation of plastics under various conditions, such as aerobic or anaerobic environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERNATIONAL STANDARDS ORGANIZATION (ISO)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ISO provides standards for evaluating biodegradation, focusing on chemical changes in materials, such as oxidation, during degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-STATE 13C CROSS-POLARIZATION/MAGIC ANGLE SPINNING NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (CP/MAS NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This technique is used to analyze changes in surface chemical components and functional groups during biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-STATE &lt;SUP&gt;13&lt;/SUP&gt;C CROSS-POLARIZATION/MAGIC ANGLE SPINNING NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (CP/MAS NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Solid-state &lt;sup&gt;13&lt;/sup&gt;C CP/MAS NMR is a method used to identify newly appearing functional groups in solid samples during microbial biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATTENUATED TOTAL REFLECTION FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (ATR-FTIR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ATR-FTIR is a technique used to identify new functional groups in liquid or solid samples during microbial biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR is used to analyze the chemical changes in PS films during degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (&lt;SUP&gt;1&lt;/SUP&gt;H NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;H NMR) is a technique used to study the chemical structure of plastic polymers during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOY PROTEIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Soy protein is a co-diet used alongside polystyrene to study its impact on the consumption and degradation rates of T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is used as a nutrition-rich co-diet for Tenebrio molitor larvae in plastic biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is a dietary supplement used in experiments to enhance the plastic consumption rate of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is a nutritious feed additive that can increase the specific consumption rate of polystyrene (PS) by mealworms, enhancing their plastic degradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is a nutrient supplement that, when fed to mealworms along with polystyrene, significantly increases the rate of PS degradation and mealworm survival rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is a nutritional supplement that can enhance the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is a nutritional supplement used in mealworm feeding experiments to study its effects on survival rates and PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is a nutritional supplement used in the study to enhance the survival and PS degradation rates of mealworms when added to their diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2 DICHLOROBENZENE (DCB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2 Dichlorobenzene (DCB) is a hazardous chemical used for GPC analysis of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and polypropylene (PP).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DICHLOROMETHANE (DCM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dichloromethane (DCM) is a hazardous chemical used for the extraction of LDPE, PP, and polylactic acid (PLA).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JELLY POWDERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Jelly powders are used as an adhesive to create mixed diets of polystyrene (PS) and wheat bran for Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL MEDIA CHEMICALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial media chemicals, such as KH2PO4, K2HPO4, MgSO4, NH4NO3, NaCl, FeSO4·7H2O, ZnSO4, MnSO4·H2O, agar, bacteriological tryptone, and yeast extract, are used to culture gut microbiota for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS MPS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene microplastics (PS MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, used as feedstock in experiments to study their biodegradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics (PS MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, used in the study to assess their impact on Eisenia fetida.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JELLY POWDER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Jelly powder is used as an adhesive to create mixed diets of PS MPs and wheat bran for feeding larvae in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANNIBAL RATE (CR)">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cannibal rate (CR) is a physiological parameter calculated to determine the percentage of larvae that disappear due to cannibalism during the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Cannibal rate (CR) is a physiological parameter measured to evaluate larval behavior under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPATION RATE (PR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pupation rate (PR) is a physiological parameter calculated to determine the percentage of larvae that successfully pupate during the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation rate (PR) is a physiological parameter used to assess the developmental progress of larvae under different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation rate (PR) measures the percentage of mealworms that successfully transition from larvae to pupae, indicating the impact of different diets on their development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethanol (70%) is used to disinfect polystyrene films before they are used in biodegradation assays.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol (75%) was used to disinfect mealworms before extracting gut samples for microbial analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used to extract biodegraded intermediates and residues from frass during the biodegradation analysis process.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used to preserve larvae samples for gut microbiome analysis and DNA extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used to sterilize Tenebrio molitor larvae and PE mulching films to ensure aseptic conditions in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used as a solvent in the extraction of central carbon metabolism metabolites from insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used as a sterilizing agent for plastic materials like PSF and NPS in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used for surface sterilization of polystyrene films and as a solvent in biofilm staining procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used to clean residual plastics and frass from containers during the plastic consumption rate experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used to sterilize mealworms before gut microbiome analysis to ensure the removal of external contaminants.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used for surface sterilization of mealworms during the isolation of gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used in the dehydration process of mealworm intestines during sample preparation for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP CONTAINER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) containers are used to store larvae and frass samples during experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALUMINUM PAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aluminum pans are used to air-dry mixed diets of PS MPs and wheat bran before feeding larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF (TETRAHYDROFURAN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">THF is a solvent used to extract degraded polystyrene fragments from mealworm fecula for molecular weight analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;THF is a solvent used to extract residual polymers like polystyrene from frass for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used in the extraction of polystyrene from frass samples for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;THF is a solvent used to extract residual plastic from frass (insect excrement) for analysis during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DCM (DICHLOROMETHANE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dichloromethane is a solvent used to extract residual polymers such as PLA, LDPE, and PP from frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER-SOLUBLE FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The water-soluble fraction in frass contains hydrophilic, digested organic substances and salts, indicating biodegradation progress.&lt;SEP&gt;The water-soluble fraction in frass contains hydrophilic, digested organic substances and salts, indicating biodegradation progress.&lt;SEP&gt;The water-soluble fraction of frass includes soluble salts and dissolved organic products, which are analyzed to assess biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHANOL-EXTRACTABLE FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The ethanol-extractable fraction of frass includes biodegraded intermediates and residues of the co-diet, which are analyzed to assess biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF-EXTRACTABLE FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The THF-extractable fraction of frass includes residual polymers like polystyrene, which are analyzed to assess biodegradation rates.&lt;SEP&gt;The THF-extractable fraction refers to the portion of residual polystyrene that can be extracted from frass using tetrahydrofuran (THF) for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DCM-EXTRACTABLE FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The DCM-extractable fraction of frass includes residual polymers like PLA, LDPE, and PP, which are analyzed to assess biodegradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL POLYMER CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Residual polymer content in frass is determined to characterize the biodegradation rate, extent, and mass balance of synthetic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL GRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS (TGA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal Gravimetric Analysis (TGA) is a method used in this study to assess the weight loss and thermal stability of polyurethane during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) is employed to study the thermal changes in polystyrene feedstock and frass samples under different atmospheres.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) is a method used to study the thermal stability and decomposition of fecula, indicating the extent of polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) is a method used to study the thermal stability and decomposition of fecula, indicating the extent of polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal gravimetric analysis is used to study the thermal changes during the conversion of polystyrene to frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) is a technique used to study the thermal decomposition of polymers, such as polypropylene, after biodegradation by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">NMR is a technique used to analyze the biodegradation of plastics by studying changes in the molecular structure of polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;NMR is a technique used to analyze the biodegradation of plastics by studying changes in the molecular structure of polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) analysis is used to characterize changes in the end groups of the egested polymer, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;NMR is used to analyze the chemical structure of polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) is used to characterize the chemical changes in polystyrene after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nuclear magnetic resonance is used to analyze changes in the end groups of the egested polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS FEEDSTOCK">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS feedstock refers to the polystyrene material provided to the larvae for consumption and biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECIFIC POLYMER CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Specific polymer consumption rate (SPCR) is a metric used to quantify the amount of polystyrene consumed by larvae over a test period.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS REMOVAL EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PS removal efficiency is a measure of the percentage of polystyrene removed by larvae during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TGA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TGA (Thermogravimetric Analysis) is used to study the thermal decomposition of polystyrene and frass, revealing changes in polymer structure due to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;TGA analysis is a technique used to confirm the biodegradation and oxidation of EPS and LDPE in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) is used to characterize thermal changes in polystyrene feedstock and frass samples under different atmospheres.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is used to study the thermal decomposition of polystyrene and frass from Tenebrio molitor larvae, revealing multiple decomposition stages and reduced PS content in frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a method used to study the thermal degradation of polyurethane and its breakdown products during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted to measure weight loss and structural changes in polyurethane (PU) foam after degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;TGA analysis measures the weight loss of materials like polystyrene and cellulose during thermal decomposition, providing insights into their degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is used to measure the mass loss of mealworm fecula compared to polystyrene feedstock, indicating the extent of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is used to measure the mass loss of mealworm fecula compared to polystyrene feedstock, indicating the extent of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">NMR analysis is used to study the chemical modifications of polymers during biodegradation, revealing degradation and partial oxidation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) analysis is conducted to study changes in the end groups of the egested polymer, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) analysis is used to confirm changes in the molecular structure of polystyrene after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL ENRICHMENT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbial enrichment refers to the process of isolating and characterizing bacteria from the guts of organisms, such as larvae, that are capable of degrading plastics like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LCFBM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LCFBM (Low Carbon Film Basal Medium) is a culture medium used to grow bacterial strains for studying their ability to degrade PE mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;LCFBM (Low Carbon Footprint Biodegradation Medium) is a medium used in biodegradation experiments to culture bacteria and study their ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Liquid carbon-free basal medium (LCFBM) is a nutrient medium used to enrich and characterize plastic-degrading gut bacteria, containing essential salts and nutrients for bacterial growth.&lt;SEP&gt;LCFBM is a culture medium used in experiments to study the growth of bacterial strains on polyethylene (PE) as the sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LB MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LB medium is a growth medium used to culture bacteria like Bacillus sp. PELW2042, which are involved in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Luria-Bertani (LB) medium is a nutrient-rich medium used for the growth of bacteria, prepared using tryptone, yeast extract, and NaCl.&lt;SEP&gt;Luria-Bertani (LB) medium is a nutrient-rich medium used for the isolation and cultivation of bacterial colonies, containing tryptone, yeast extract, and NaCl.&lt;SEP&gt;LB medium is a nutrient-rich agar used to culture and grow bacteria, including those isolated from mealworm guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Luria-Bertani (LB) medium is a nutrient-rich growth medium used to culture bacterial strains, including those involved in polystyrene (PS) degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;LB medium is a nutrient-rich growth medium used for cultivating bacteria, prepared with tryptone, yeast extract, NaCl, and agar.&lt;SEP&gt;LB medium is a nutrient-rich growth medium used for culturing bacteria, including those isolated from mealworms for polystyrene degradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;LB medium is a nutrient-rich growth medium used to cultivate bacteria, including those isolated from mealworms, to study their growth characteristics and potential for polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;LB medium is a nutrient-rich growth medium used to culture bacteria, including E. coli and other strains mentioned in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used for the extraction of polystyrene (PS) in the analysis of biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used in the extraction of polymers, including polystyrene, from samples for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used in the extraction of polystyrene from mealworm frass for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is a solvent used to extract residual polystyrene from frass samples in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROFORM-D">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloroform-D is a deuterated solvent used in NMR analysis to dissolve and analyze residual polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform-D is a solvent used for extracting samples for \(^{1}H\) NMR analysis to study the chemical transformations of polystyrene in frass from Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform-d is a chemical used in NMR experiments to lock signals.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform-D is a solvent used in the extraction process to isolate residual polyethylene (PE) from the egested frass of lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform-D is a solvent used to extract residual polyethylene from the egested frass of lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform-D is a deuterated solvent used in nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) analysis to study the chemical changes in polystyrene after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform-D is a deuterated solvent used in nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy to dissolve and analyze residual polymers extracted from frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform-D is a deuterated solvent used in the extraction and analysis of residual polymers, such as LDPE, in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVDF FILTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) filters are used to filter extracts during the preparation of samples for NMR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEIONIZED WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Deionized water is used in various steps of the experimental process, including cleaning plastic samples and preparing solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NMR SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A 500-MHz NMR spectrometer is used to measure the \(^{1}H\) NMR spectra of polystyrene extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER CONTACT ANGLE (WCA) ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Water contact angle analysis is a method used to measure the hydrophobicity of plastic samples after microbial treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T. MOLITOR LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms, are capable of chewing, ingesting, and digesting polystyrene (PS) foam and other plastics, impacting their physiology and survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS MICRO POWDERS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PS micro powders are small polystyrene particles (0.1-0.2 mm) used as a carbon source in biodegradation experiments with T. molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PS consumption refers to the amount of polystyrene (PS) consumed by insect larvae, which is measured to assess biodegradation efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;PS consumption refers to the amount of polystyrene ingested by T. molitor larvae, which is measured to assess their biodegradation capacity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF EXTRACTABLE FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">THF (tetrahydrofuran) extractable fraction is used to measure the residual polystyrene in frass, indicating the extent of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;THF (tetrahydrofuran) extractable fraction refers to the portion of polystyrene residue that can be extracted from frass using THF, indicating the extent of digestion by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The THF extractable fraction refers to residual polystyrene (PS) in mealworm frass, which constitutes about 35% of the weight of frass.&lt;SEP&gt;The THF extractable fraction of frass indicates the presence of degraded PVC components, showing the extent of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION (MWD)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a factor that influences the biodegradation rate of plastics, with lower molecular weight polymers degrading more easily.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a factor that influences the biodegradation rate of plastics, with lower molecular weight polymers degrading more easily.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a measure of the range of molecular weights in a polymer, used to assess the depolymerization of polystyrene during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a measure of the range of molecular weights in polystyrene residues, which shifts to lower values after degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a measure used to analyze the changes in the molecular weight of polystyrene after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a measure used to assess changes in the molecular weight of polystyrene during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a key metric used to analyze changes in LDPE before and after digestion by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a measure used to assess the depolymerization of LDPE, showing shifts toward lower molecular weights after digestion by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) refers to the range of molecular weights in a polymer sample, which changes during biodegradation processes like depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution is analyzed to understand the depolymerization pattern of polystyrene microplastics during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RING CLEAVAGE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ring cleavage refers to the breaking of the benzene ring structure in polystyrene, a key step in its biodegradation, as observed in FTIR analysis of frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbonyl groups (-C=O) are chemical functional groups that form during the biodegradation of HDPE, indicating the breakdown of the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonyl groups (C=O) are chemical groups formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating the breakdown of the polymer structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonyl groups (C═O stretch, 1700 cm⁻¹) are chemical functional groups that appear in the FTIR spectra of frass samples, indicating the biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonyl groups (C=O) are functional groups formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, indicating chemical changes in the polymer structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonyl groups are chemical structures that may form during the biochemical oxidation of polyethylene, as indicated by FTIR spectroscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonyl groups (C=O stretch) are formed during the degradation of polystyrene by mealworms, indicating oxidation and chemical transformation of PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonyl groups are chemical functional groups detected in polystyrene residues, indicating oxidative degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonyl groups are chemical functional groups that appear in frass as a result of polystyrene oxidation and depolymerization during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonyl groups (C=O) are formed during the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP) by T. molitor and Z. atratus, indicating oxidation of the polymer chains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOSPHERE STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Chemosphere study by Yang et al. (2018) investigates the biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae, including factors affecting biodegradation rates and life cycle completion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="\(^{1}H\) NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">\(^{1}H\) NMR analysis is used to detect new peaks in frass extracts, indicating the presence of oxidized functional groups resulting from the biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VOLATILE ORGANIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Volatile organic compounds are detected in the decomposition stages below 100°C in TGA analysis, likely originating from intestinal secretions and carboxylic acid compounds from polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN YT1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain YT1 is a bacterial strain involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene, as indicated by its ability to reduce the mass of PS pieces during incubation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE STRAIN YT1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter asburiae strain YT1 is a plastic-degrading bacterium isolated from Plodia interpunctella, capable of degrading low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT PLASTIC-DEGRADERS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut plastic-degraders are bacterial strains isolated from the guts of insects like Tenebrio molitor and Plodia interpunctella, capable of degrading plastics such as polystyrene and low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDIZED FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidized functional groups are chemical groups formed during the biodegradation of LDPE, identified using 1H NMR analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidized functional groups are chemical groups formed during the degradation of polypropylene (PP) foam, identified using NMR spectroscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidized functional groups, such as C-O, are formed during the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating chemical modification of the plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO OPACUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio opacus is a species of darkling beetle studied for its ecological and biological characteristics, particularly in relation to its distribution and taxonomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio opacus is a species of darkling beetle, related to Tenebrio molitor, and is studied for its ecological and biological characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacterium is a bacterial strain isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum, capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC PACKAGING MATERIALS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic packaging materials are common environmental pollutants that can be penetrated and degraded by stored-product insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic packaging materials are commonly used in food packaging and are a significant source of environmental pollution due to their resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STORED-PRODUCT INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Stored-product insects are a group of insects capable of penetrating and degrading plastic packaging materials, contributing to plastic waste issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Stored-product insects are pests that infest stored grains and other products, often studied for their ability to penetrate packaging materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION RATES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradation rates refer to the speed at which organic substances, such as plastics, are broken down by living organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBE-DEPENDENT DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbe-dependent depolymerization refers to the breakdown of polymers, such as plastics, by microorganisms present in the gut of insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER SP. NYZ450">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworms ingesting polyethylene mulching film, showing the ability to form biofilm and decompose polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450 is a bacterial strain that, in co-culture with Bacillus sp. NyZ451, can degrade polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) mulching films, showing a decrease in molecular weight of PE when treated.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of biodegrading polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading PE mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of biodegrading polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP. NYZ451">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. NyZ451 is a bacterial strain that, in co-culture with Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450, can degrade polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NYZ451 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading PE mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain that, when combined with Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450, enhances the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterium isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae that contributes to the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain that, in co-culture with Acinetobacter sp., can biodegrade polyethylene mulching films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-EATING WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-eating waxworms are insect larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene, with bacterial strains like Bacillus sp. YP1 and Enterobacter asburiae VT1 playing a role.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are insects that have been studied for their ability to degrade polyethylene, with their gut bacteria playing a key role.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene, with bacterial strains isolated from their guts contributing to the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae capable of degrading polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae capable of degrading polyethylene, with gut bacteria playing a key role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms, such as Galleria mellonella, are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene and polystyrene, with their gut microbiome playing a crucial role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are organisms capable of biodegrading polystyrene, with their gut bacteria playing a key role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae that have been studied for their ability to degrade polyethylene and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene, demonstrating the potential of insects in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae that can degrade polyethylene, a type of synthetic polymer, with the help of gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae that have been studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, facilitated by bacterial strains in their guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae of Galleria mellonella, known for their ability to metabolize polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE MULCHING FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene mulching films are a major source of plastic pollution in soil, contributing to environmental degradation and requiring biodegradation solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene mulching films are a type of plastic waste that can be biodegraded by bacterial strains isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene mulching films are widely used in agriculture but contribute to environmental pollution due to their resistance to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene mulching films are plastic materials used in agriculture that can be biodegraded by a co-culture of Acinetobacter sp. strain NyX450 and Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERNATIONAL BIODETERIORATION AND BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">International Biodeterioration and Biodegradation is a journal that published research on the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films by bacterial strains from Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROSCOPIC OBSERVATIONS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
      <data key="d1">This kit is used to stain and observe live and dead cells during microscopic observations of microbial activity on plastic surfaces</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-STATE &lt;SUP&gt;13&lt;/SUP&gt;C CP/MAS NMR">
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
      <data key="d1">Solid-state &lt;sup&gt;13&lt;/sup&gt;C CP/MAS NMR is used to identify functional groups formed during plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrition refers to additional dietary components that enhance the survival rate and polystyrene consumption rate of mealworms, supporting their ability to degrade PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic wastes are a major environmental pollutant, causing soil, water, and air contamination, and posing risks to human health and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic wastes are discarded plastic materials that accumulate in the environment, posing significant ecological and environmental challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic wastes refer to discarded plastic materials, including polystyrene, that contribute to environmental pollution and are a focus of biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic wastes are synthetic polymers derived from fossil fuels that persist in the environment, causing significant pollution and ecological harm.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PS degradation refers to the process by which polystyrene is broken down, primarily by mealworms and their gut microbes, into simpler compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITIONAL ENHANCEMENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutritional enhancement involves adding dietary components to improve the survival and polystyrene consumption rates of mealworms, aiding in their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITIZEN SCIENTISTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Citizen scientists are individuals who contribute to scientific research, in this case, confirming the ability of mealworms to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Citizen scientists are individuals who contribute to scientific research, such as confirming the ability of mealworms to digest polystyrene across multiple countries.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOME SHIFT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbiome shift refers to changes in the microbial composition of mealworms' guts due to polystyrene feeding, which affects their biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOMASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biomass is a byproduct of polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms, representing the portion of consumed carbon incorporated into the mealworm's body.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the limited carbon assimilation from polystyrene degradation products into the mealworm's body during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the organic material produced by mealworms during the biodegradation process, which includes the incorporation of carbon from ingested Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the organic material produced during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, representing a portion of the plastic converted into biological matter.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the residual material from mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), which contains bioactive compounds but no detectable PS monomers or oligomers.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the total mass of live Tenebrio molitor larvae at the end of the experiment, showing variations based on feed type.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the organic material produced by mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene, representing a portion of the carbon from consumed EPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the total mass of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which is used as an indicator of their growth and development in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENTAMYCIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gentamycin is an antibiotic that suppresses gut bacteria in mealworms, significantly reducing their ability to depolymerize and mineralize polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Exiguobacterium sp. is a bacterial strain identified as capable of creating biofilms and degrading polystyrene (PS) in the gut of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. is a bacterial strain identified in previous studies as capable of degrading polystyrene, particularly in the gut of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworm guts that plays a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. is a plastic-degrading bacterium identified in the guts of insects like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED (FTIR) SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR spectroscopy is used to characterize the chemical modifications and oxidation of polyethylene during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is used to identify the primary functional groups of virgin and residual polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs).&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy is used to analyze chemical modifications in frass residues resulting from polystyrene digestion by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSTAR 5-7 MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Instar 5-7 mealworms are developmental stages of Tenebrio molitor larvae used in the study to test their ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH DENSITY POLYPROPYLENE (PP) CONTAINERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High-density polypropylene (PP) containers are used to cultivate mealworms during the study, providing a controlled environment for PS degradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON-13 (13C) LABELED PS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon-13 (13C) labeled polystyrene is used in isotopic studies to track the mineralization of PS into CO2 and its incorporation into lipids by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS RESIDUES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass residues are the excrement of mealworms, analyzed in the study to identify chemical modifications resulting from polystyrene digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STANFORD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stanford conducted experiments with 120 mealworms incubated in 475 mL PP containers to study their plastic-eating behavior and biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NORTHERN IRELAND">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Northern Ireland conducted experiments with 200 mealworms incubated in 600 mL containers to study their plastic consumption and biodegradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHINA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">China conducted experiments with 500 mealworms incubated in 1200 mL containers to study their plastic-eating behavior and biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNIVERSITY OF QUEENSLAND">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The University of Queensland, St. Lucia, Australia, reported that local mealworms consumed polystyrene at a rate of 13 mg PS per 100 worms per day.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BANDAR LAMPUNG, INDONESIA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A team in Bandar Lampung, Indonesia, reported that polystyrene foam feeds supported mealworm growth without significant effects on larvae mortality.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARSHALL SCHOOL STUDENTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Marshall School students in Duluth, MN, USA, reported that mealworms consumed polystyrene at a rate of 33 mg PS per 100 worms per day.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEIJING STRAIN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The Beijing strain of mealworms was tested with 13C-labeled polystyrene, showing that 13C from the labeled PS was assimilated into mealworm biomass, indicating energy and carbon recovery from plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EGESTED FRASS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Egested frass from larvae contains residual PP polymer and partially degraded intermediates, providing evidence of PP depolymerization and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Egested frass is the waste material excreted by lesser waxworms, containing residual or modified polyethylene after digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Egested frass is the waste material excreted by lesser waxworms, which contains evidence of polyethylene biodegradation, such as new functional organic groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Egested frass is the waste material excreted by lesser waxworms, which shows evidence of polyethylene biodegradation through the formation of new functional organic groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Egested frass is the waste material excreted by mealworms after consuming polystyrene (PS), showing evidence of PS depolymerization and chemical changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Egested frass is the waste material produced by lesser waxworms after consuming PE and WC, which is analyzed to study the extent of PE degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Egested frass refers to the waste material excreted by lesser waxworms after digesting polyethylene (PE), which may contain residual or modified PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEEDSTOCK">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Feedstock refers to the initial polystyrene (PS) material provided to mealworms, which undergoes depolymerization and degradation in their guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (M&lt;SUB&gt;N&lt;/SUB&gt; AND M&lt;SUB&gt;W&lt;/SUB&gt;)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (M&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt; and M&lt;sub&gt;w&lt;/sub&gt;) are measures used to track the depolymerization of polystyrene (PS) in mealworm frass, showing significant decreases compared to the feedstock.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR ANALYSES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) analyses are used to detect the formation of oxidative functional groups in residual polyethylene polymers after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analyses are used to verify chemical changes and the formation of new functional groups in polystyrene (PS) after passage through the mealworm gut.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy is used to analyze the formation of oxygen-containing functional groups during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE) by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT DEFICIENCY">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient deficiency refers to the lack of essential nutrients (e.g., nitrogen, phosphorus, trace elements) in polystyrene (PS), which limits its ability to support long-term survival and growth of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS CONSUMPTION RATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PS consumption rates measure the amount of polystyrene (PS) consumed by mealworms under different feeding conditions, such as PS alone or PS with bran.&lt;SEP&gt;PS consumption rates refer to the amount of polystyrene consumed by mealworms over a specific period, with higher rates observed when mealworms are fed PS supplemented with soy protein or bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM BIOMASS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm biomass refers to the total mass of surviving mealworms, which remains similar between those fed PS alone and those fed PS with bran, despite differences in survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillaceae is a family of bacteria that dominates the gut bacterial community of yellow mealworms fed on normal diets, but its abundance changes when fed surgical masks.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillaceae is a family of bacteria within the Firmicutes phylum, found in the gut microbiota of mealworms and involved in the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillaceae is a family of beneficial bacteria that can grow in the intestines of mice fed with Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillaceae is a family of lactic acid bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, associated with inositol metabolism and nitrogen redistribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillaceae is a family of bacteria whose relative abundance decreases in response to the additives in polymers, which are harmful to their growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillaceae is a family of bacteria whose relative abundance decreases in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae when fed microplastics.</data>
