    <node id="STYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene is a versatile chemical compound with significant roles in both industrial production and environmental degradation processes. It serves as the monomer for polystyrene, a widely used plastic in various industrial applications, making it essential for the synthesis of this material. Simultaneously, styrene plays a critical role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly through microbial activity and the gut microbiota of organisms such as mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*).

In the context of biodegradation, styrene is identified as a by-product of polystyrene degradation. It is detected in low amounts in the frass and gut tissue of mealworms, indicating its role as a degradation product during the biodegradation process. Studies have shown that styrene can be microbially decomposed through both aerobic and anaerobic pathways. During these processes, it is broken down into intermediate compounds, such as phenylacetyl-CoA, which subsequently enter the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle for further degradation into carbon dioxide (CO2) and water (H2O). Additionally, styrene can be oxidized to form acetophenone as a side reaction product, and it is also oxidized to phenylacetate by microorganisms, contributing to the degradation of polystyrene.

The degradation pathways of styrene have been extensively studied, particularly in the context of polystyrene-fed mealworms. Techniques such as gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) analysis of worm frass and thermal decomposition of polystyrene (Styrofoam) have confirmed styrene as the main thermal decomposition product of polystyrene. Furthermore, KEGG pathway analysis of polystyrene-fed mealworms has highlighted the involvement of styrene in oxidation and aromatic ring cleavage during biodegradation processes.

Despite its role in biodegradation, styrene and its metabolites, such as styrene oxide, can have toxicological effects on organisms. This underscores the importance of understanding its degradation pathways and potential environmental impacts. Styrene's dual role as a building block for plastics and a key player in their microbial degradation highlights its significance in both industrial and environmental contexts. Its potential biotechnological applications in biodegradation processes have also been discussed in scientific literature, such as by Mooney et al. (2006).

In summary, styrene is a critical compound in the lifecycle of polystyrene, serving as both a monomer for its production and a key intermediate in its microbial degradation. Its presence in the frass and gut tissue of mealworms, along with its involvement in various degradation pathways, underscores its importance in the biodegradation of polystyrene and its potential environmental and biotechn</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene oligomers are degradation products of polystyrene, quantified in the frass of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of styrene, which can be metabolized by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate products formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are also susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are more biodegradable than the full polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are also susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene, which are also susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, which can be further broken down by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are more biodegradable than the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate components of polystyrene (PS) that are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike the polymerized form of PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and can be further broken down by microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are more biodegradable than the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are biodegradable.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </node>
