    </node>
    <node id="FUNGI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungi are microorganisms studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene in environments such as dumpsites.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungi have been studied for their potential to biodegrade polystyrene, though their effectiveness in axenic cultures has been limited.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungi, including species like Alternaria alternata and Aspergillus versicolor, are found in the gut of mealworms and may play a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Fungi are predominant microorganisms responsible for the degradation of soil-buried polyester polyurethane under various soil conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungi are microorganisms that have been identified as efficient in degrading synthetic polymers, potentially playing a role in polystyrene breakdown within insect guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungi have been studied for their synergistic effects in the biodegradation of polypropylene, particularly when combined with pretreatment methods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
