      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrospira defluvii is a bacterial species identified in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly in studies involving PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrospira defluvii is a bacterium associated with the polystyrene-fed gut microbiome and may play a role in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LISTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Listeria is a bacterial genus identified in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly in studies involving PS biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIET 2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Diet 2 consists of a bran:PS ratio of 20:1 w/w with hydration, used to study the effect of hydration on PS metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 is a feeding condition in the study where additional water was provided to the insect larvae, showing distinct effects on PS degradation and biomass composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 is a feeding strategy in the study that includes high PS:bran with water, which improves PS consumption by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 refers to a specific experimental diet (20:1 bran:PS + H2O) used in the study to evaluate the gut microbiome and PS biodegradation in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 refers to a specific experimental diet with a high bran:polystyrene ratio and water (20:1 + H2O) used to study the effects on Tenebrio molitor's gut microbiota and polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 refers to a specific feeding strategy used in experiments with Tenebrio molitor larvae, involving bran and polystyrene to study biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 refers to a 20:1 bran-to-polystyrene (PS) diet with added water, used in experiments to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 refers to a feeding regime with a bran-to-PS ratio of 20:1 and the presence of water, showing the highest extent of polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 refers to a specific experimental diet with a bran:PS ratio of 20:1 and the inclusion of water, which showed a different distribution of PS oligomers compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 refers to a specific experimental diet with hydration that showed the highest polystyrene consumption yields and improved mealworm growth and survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIET 3">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Diet 3 consists of a bran:PS ratio of 20:1 w/w without hydration, used as a control to assess PS metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3 is a feeding condition with a specific bran-to-polystyrene ratio, showing higher larvae weight compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3 is a feeding strategy in the study that includes PS/barley 20:1, requiring less handling but showing lower PS degradation rates compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3 refers to a specific experimental diet (20:1 bran:PS, no water added) used in the study to evaluate the gut microbiome and PS biodegradation in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3 refers to a specific experimental diet with a high bran:polystyrene ratio (20:1) used to study the effects on Tenebrio molitor's gut microbiota and polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3 refers to a specific feeding strategy used in experiments with Tenebrio molitor larvae, involving minimal handling and polystyrene to study biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3 refers to a 20:1 bran-to-polystyrene (PS) diet without added water, used in experiments to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3 refers to a feeding regime with a bran-to-PS ratio of 20:1 without water, used to compare the effects on polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3 refers to an experimental diet with a bran:PS ratio of 20:1 and no water, which also resulted in higher excretion of PS oligomers in the frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTROL DIET">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Control diet refers to a bran-only diet used as a baseline in experiments to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The control diet is a baseline feeding condition used in the study to compare the effects of different diets on insect larvae growth and PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The control diet refers to the baseline diet used in the study to compare the effects of different diets on the gut microbiome and PS biodegradation in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHORT-CHAIN FATTY ACIDS (SCFAS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) are metabolic byproducts produced by gut bacteria, such as Clostridium, and play a role in intestinal homeostasis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIET 1">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Diet 1 consists of a bran:PS ratio of 4:1 w/w, used to assess the effect of polystyrene supplementation in the mealworm diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1 is a feeding condition with a 4:1 bran-to-polystyrene ratio, used to study the effects of PS consumption on insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1 is a feeding strategy in the study that includes low PS:bran, showing comparable PS consumption rates to Diet 2 by the end of the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1 refers to a specific experimental diet with a low bran:polystyrene ratio (4:1) used to study the effects on Tenebrio molitor's gut microbiota and polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1 refers to a specific feeding strategy used in experiments with Tenebrio molitor larvae, involving barley and polystyrene to study biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1 refers to a 4:1 bran-to-polystyrene (PS) diet used in experiments to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1 refers to a feeding regime with a bran-to-PS ratio of 4:1, used to study the effects on polystyrene degradation by insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1 refers to an experimental diet with a bran:PS ratio of 4:1 and no water, which resulted in higher excretion of PS oligomers in the frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIAS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Non-intentionally added substances (NIAS) are degradation compounds observed during the biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE (SMO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene monooxygenase (SMO) is an enzyme involved in the aerobic biodegradation of styrene, a monomer of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE-2,3-DIOXYGENASE (SDO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene-2,3-dioxygenase (SDO) is an enzyme that introduces two oxygen atoms into styrene, forming styrene cis-glycol during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VINYLCATECHOL 1,2-DIOXYGENASE (VC12O)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vinylcatechol 1,2-dioxygenase (VC12O) is an enzyme involved in the meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol during styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VINYLCATECHOL 2,3-DIOXYGENASE (VC23O)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vinylcatechol 2,3-dioxygenase (VC23O) is an enzyme involved in the meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol during styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE OXIDE ISOMERASE (SOI)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) is an enzyme involved in the conversion of styrene oxide during the biodegradation of styrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) is an enzyme that catalyzes the isomerization of styrene oxide to phenylacetaldehyde during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLACETALDEHYDE DEHYDROGENASE (PAD)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenylacetaldehyde dehydrogenase (PAD) is an enzyme involved in the metabolic pathways of styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE OXIDE REDUCTASE (SOR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene oxide reductase (SOR) is an enzyme involved in the conversion of styrene oxide to phenylethanol during styrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oxide reductase (SOR) is an enzyme involved in the reduction of styrene oxide during the biodegradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAEROBIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Anaerobic degradation refers to the breakdown of styrene in the absence of oxygen, leading to the formation of intermediate compounds that can enter the TCA cycle.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic degradation refers to the breakdown of styrene in the absence of oxygen, mediated by specific enzymes and microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROBIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Aerobic degradation refers to the breakdown of styrene in the presence of oxygen, leading to the formation of CO2 and H2O.&lt;SEP&gt;Aerobic degradation refers to the breakdown of styrene in the presence of oxygen, mediated by specific enzymes and microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLACETALDEHYDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenylacetaldehyde is an intermediate compound formed during the degradation of styrene, which is converted to phenylacetic acid and introduced into the TCA cycle.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenylacetic acid is a compound formed from phenylacetaldehyde during styrene degradation, which can be introduced into the TCA cycle for further degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenylacetic acid is a metabolic byproduct found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polyethylene-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenylacetic acid levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are negatively correlated with the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE OXIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene oxide is a compound formed during the oxidation of styrene by styrene monooxygenase, indicating its role in styrene biodegradation pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oxide is an intermediate compound in the biodegradation of styrene, often metabolized by microorganisms such as Xanthobacter strain 124X.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oxide is an intermediate compound in the degradation of styrene, which can be isomerized to phenylacetaldehyde or hydrolyzed to phenylethanol-1,2-diol.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oxide is a reactive metabolite of styrene, known to show carcinogenic effects in oral exposure bioassays.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oxide is a metabolite in the degradation pathway of styrene, produced by certain bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLETHANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenylethanol is a compound formed from styrene oxide during the degradation process, which can be converted back to phenylacetaldehyde.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The microbiome of mealworms is influenced by their diet, with synthetic polymers like polystyrene and polyethylene affecting bacterial populations.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome of mealworms plays a crucial role in their ability to degrade polyethylene and adapt to different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the community of microorganisms in the digestive tract of mealworms, which plays a crucial role in their ability to metabolize synthetic polymers and lignocellulose.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the community of microorganisms in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics such as polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the distinct microbial communities present in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which play a role in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the microbial community in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which supports the biodegradation of styrene and its derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the community of microorganisms in the gut of greater wax moth larvae, which may play a role in the degradation of LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the community of microorganisms in the larvae's gut, which plays a role in digestion and biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the community of microorganisms, including bacteria, fungi, and viruses, that inhabit a particular environment and play a role in processes like biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the collective community of microorganisms in a specific environment, such as the gut of insects, playing a role in biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the collective community of microorganisms in a specific environment, such as the gut of insects, playing a role in biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the community of microorganisms, including bacteria, fungi, and viruses, that inhabit a particular environment, such as the gut of insects, and play a role in processes like biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the community of microorganisms, including bacteria, fungi, and viruses, that inhabit a particular environment, such as the gut of insects, and play a role in processes like biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the microbial community in the gut of insects, which varies with diet and influences polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3-VINYLCATECHOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">3-Vinylcatechol is an intermediate compound formed during the degradation of styrene, which can undergo meta-cleavage breakdown by vinylcatechol 1,2-dioxygenase (VC12O) or vinylcatechol 2,3-dioxygenase (VC23O).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-VINYL-CIS,CIS-MUCONIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Vinyl-cis,cis-muconic acid is a product of the meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-HYDROXY-6-OXOOCTA-2,4,7-TRIENOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Hydroxy-6-oxoocta-2,4,7-trienoic acid is a compound formed during the degradation of 3-vinylcatechol, which can be introduced into the TCA cycle.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLACETALDEHYDE REDUCTASE (PAR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenylacetaldehyde reductase (PAR) is an enzyme that catalyzes the equilibrium reaction between phenylethanol and phenylacetaldehyde during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPOXIDE HYDROLASE (EH)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Epoxide hydrolase (EH) is an enzyme that catalyzes the hydrolysis of styrene oxide to phenylethanol-1,2-diol during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANDELIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mandelic acid is a compound formed from the oxidation of phenylethanol-1,2-diol during the aerobic degradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-ETHYLPHENOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Ethylphenol is a compound formed during the anaerobic degradation of styrene, which can undergo ring cleavage or sidechain oxidation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-ETHYLHEXANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Ethylhexanol is a product of the ring cleavage of 2-ethylphenol during the anaerobic degradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-HYDROXYPHENYL-ACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Hydroxyphenyl-acetic acid is a compound formed during the sidechain oxidation of 2-ethylphenol during the anaerobic degradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORYNEBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Corynebacterium is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, contributing to nitrogen metabolism and the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;Corynebacterium is a genus of bacteria that contains species capable of degrading styrene and its derivatives.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodococcus is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus is a genus of bacteria that contains species capable of degrading styrene and its derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus is a genus of bacteria that shows higher abundance in the gut microbiome of PS- and CS-fed larvae, indicating its potential role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Water availability is a limiting factor in nutrient-deficient habitats, influencing the survival and nitrogen assimilation capabilities of insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Water is added to some experimental diets to study its influence on the growth of Tenebrio molitor larvae and their ability to degrade polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROFLORA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microflora refers to the microorganisms present in the intestines of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which may play a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED PS (EPS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded PS (EPS) foam packaging is a type of polystyrene material used in the study to assess its biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded PS (EPS), commonly known as Styrofoam, is widely used in building insulation and packaging, contributing to environmental pollution due to its durability and resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROLLED BARLEY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Rolled barley is a component of the diet tested in the study, used alongside polystyrene to evaluate the biodegradation capabilities of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Rolled barley is a dietary component used as a control diet for Tenebrio molitor larvae in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;Rolled barley is a feed component used in the study as part of the larvae's diet to investigate the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Rolled barley is a primary component of the control diet provided to Tenebrio molitor larvae, containing nutrients essential for their growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHANOL (ETOH)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethanol (70% EtOH) is used to sterilize the external surface of Tenebrio molitor larvae to eliminate external microflora before collecting internal samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRD LIQUID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MRD liquid is a solution containing peptone and sodium chloride, used to dilute larvae intestines and internal liquid for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;MRD liquid is a solution used to dilute the internal liquid and intestines of Tenebrio molitor larvae for microflora collection and analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLIMATE-CONTROLLED ROOM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A climate-controlled room is used to maintain optimal conditions (25°C ± 1°C and 55 ± 5% relative humidity) for the biodegradation experiments involving Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPROPYLENE (PP) CONTAINERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) containers are used to house Tenebrio molitor larvae during the biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATR-FTIR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ATR-FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used to assess the chemical composition of expanded PS foam in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used to confirm the polystyrene nature of the test materials in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze surface chemical modifications of PE mulching films treated with bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIFFERENTIAL SCANNING CALORIMETRY (DSC)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DSC is a method used to confirm the nature of polystyrene material by analyzing its thermal properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) is a technique used to analyze thermal properties and structural changes in HDPE film after degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) is used to analyze the thermal properties of polystyrene materials in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAE SURVIVAL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae survival is monitored during the biodegradation experiments to assess the impact of different diets on Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAE INTESTINES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae intestines are part of the digestive system of larvae, used in the study for DNA extraction and analysis of microbial content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUCLISENS LYSIS BUFFER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NucliSENS Lysis Buffer is a chemical solution used for DNA extraction, facilitating the breakdown of cell walls to release DNA.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S-18S ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The 16S-18S assay is a next-generation sequencing-based method used to detect and differentiate nuclear ribosomal genes in microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN/PROTEIN CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen/protein content analysis is used to determine the protein levels in insect biomass, relevant for assessing food safety and nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZIRCONIUM BEADS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Zirconium beads are used in DNA extraction processes to mechanically break down cell walls and release DNA.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TISSUELYSER II">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">TissueLyser II is a device used for homogenizing samples, such as larvae intestines, to facilitate DNA extraction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMAC NUCLEIC ACID EXTRACTION SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The EMAC Nucleic Acid Extraction System is an automated device used for extracting DNA from samples according to a standardized protocol.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="18S RRNA GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">18S rRNA genes are ribosomal RNA genes used as markers for identifying and differentiating eukaryotic organisms in microbial studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RDNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rDNA is a gene used as a marker for identifying and differentiating prokaryotic organisms in microbial studies.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rDNA is a genetic marker used for the identification and classification of bacterial strains based on their ribosomal RNA sequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRACT-N-AMP PCR READYMIX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Extract-N-Amp PCR ReadyMix is a chemical solution used for PCR amplification of DNA samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUANT-IT DSDNA HIGH SENSITIVE ASSAY KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Quant-iT dsDNA High Sensitive Assay Kit is used to quantify DNA concentrations in samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGENCOURT AMPURE XP BEADS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Agencourt AMPure XP beads are used to purify DNA amplicons by removing undesirable DNA fragments during sequencing preparation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DUMATHERM NITROGEN/PROTEIN ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A DumaTherm Nitrogen/Protein analyzer is used to measure the protein content in mealworms and frass.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dumatherm Nitrogen/Protein Analyzer is a device used to determine the nitrogen and protein content in insect biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NAOH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NaOH, also known as sodium hydroxide, is a chemical used in various analytical processes, including the determination of nitrogen/protein content in biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHCL3">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CHCl3, or chloroform, is a solvent used in the extraction and analysis of styrene and polystyrene oligomers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PTFE FILTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PTFE filters are used to filter organic extracts, such as CHCl3, in the analysis of styrene and polystyrene oligomers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GC-TOF-MS SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The GC-TOF-MS system is an analytical tool used for the separation and quantification of styrene and polystyrene oligomers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HP-5MS CAPILLARY COLUMN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">HP-5MS capillary column is a chromatographic column used for the separation of metabolites in GC-TOF-MS analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;HP-5MS capillary column is used for the separation of metabolites in the gas chromatography system.&lt;SEP&gt;The HP-5MS capillary column is used in gas chromatography for the separation of metabolites, including styrene and polystyrene oligomers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RSTUDIO">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RStudio is a software used for statistical analysis, including one-way ANOVA and principal component analysis (PCA), in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;RStudio is a software used for statistical analysis, including one-way ANOVA, in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIMCA 17.0.1">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SIMCA 17.0.1 is a software used for principal component analysis (PCA) to assess discrimination between different feeding strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,1-DIPHENYL CYCLOBUTENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,1-diphenyl cyclobutene is a polystyrene oligomer detected in the frass of Tenebrio molitor as a degradation product.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANS-1,2-DIPHENYL CYCLOBUTENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trans-1,2-diphenyl cyclobutene is a polystyrene oligomer found in the frass of Tenebrio molitor as a degradation product.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,3,5-TRIPHENYLCYCLOHEXENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,3,5-triphenylcyclohexene is a polystyrene oligomer detected in the frass of Tenebrio molitor as a degradation product.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1A-PHENYL-4-(1-PHENYLETHYL)-1,2,3,4-TETRAHYDRONAPHTHALENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1a-phenyl-4-(1-phenylethyl)-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene is a polystyrene oligomer identified in the frass of Tenebrio molitor as a degradation product.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AARHUS UNIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aarhus University is an institution where research on animal nutrition and biodegradation of plastics is conducted, particularly within the Department of Animal Science.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene oligomers are low-molecular-weight compounds derived from polystyrene, which can be detected in food simulants and are relevant in food safety studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY MASS SPECTROMETRY (GC-MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gas chromatography mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of plastics and their degradation products.&lt;SEP&gt;Gas chromatography mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is an analytical method used for the quantification of non-intentionally added substances and polystyrene/polyurethane oligomers in food simulants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLUS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus sp. are probiotic bacteria that play a role in modulating the gut microbiota of swine, contributing to improved gut health and overall well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLOSTRIDIUM SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Clostridium species are being explored for their potential as probiotics, although they present challenges due to their pathogenic potential in some contexts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein degradation is a biological process regulated by coding and non-coding genes, playing a critical role in maintaining protein homeostasis in cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IRON AND SULFUR HOMEOSTASIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Iron and sulfur homeostasis in plants is regulated through selective protein degradation, which is essential for maintaining cellular balance.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron and sulfur homeostasis refers to the regulation of iron and sulfur levels in plants, which is critical for cellular processes and is maintained through selective protein degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CROSSTALK IN PROTEIN DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Crosstalk in protein degradation pathways refers to the interaction between different pathways that regulate protein degradation, leading to ultrasensitive responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EUROPEAN COMMISSION REGULATION (EU) NO 10/2011">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">European Commission Regulation (EU) No 10/2011 is a regulatory document that outlines safety standards for materials and articles intended to come into contact with food.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN HOMEOSTASIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein homeostasis refers to the balance of protein synthesis and degradation, which is crucial for cellular function and is regulated by various genes and pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEURODEGENERATIVE DISEASES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Neurodegenerative diseases are conditions characterized by the progressive degeneration of nerve cells, often linked to disruptions in protein homeostasis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRASENSITIVITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Ultrasensitivity in protein degradation pathways refers to the highly sensitive responses that occur due to interactions between different regulatory pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PERIDROMA SAUCIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Peridroma saucia, commonly known as the variegated cutworm, is an insect species studied for its genetic elements, such as retrotransposons.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YAKPSL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">YAKPsl is a 5' truncated non-LTR retrotransposon identified in the variegated cutworm, Peridroma saucia, and is studied for its genetic implications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLETHANOL-1,2-DIOL">
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene oxide is hydrolyzed to phenylethanol-1,2-diol by epoxide hydrolase (EH) during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROBIOTICS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
      <data key="d1">Clostridium species are being investigated for their potential as probiotics, despite challenges related to their pathogenic nature.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOISTURE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2</data>
      <data key="d1">Moisture is an environmental factor that affects the biodegradation rate of polystyrene by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The growth and development of Tenebrio molitor are influenced by moisture levels, as studied in controlled environments.</data>
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANTS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
      <data key="d1">Iron and sulfur homeostasis is critical for maintaining cellular balance in plants.&lt;SEP&gt;Plants are organisms that can remove contaminants from the environment and are studied for their phytoremediation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Plants are organisms that can remove contaminants from the environment, as studied in environmental remediation research.</data>
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTOMOREMEDIATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Entomoremediation is a novel bioremediation approach that uses insects like Tenebrio molitor and Hermetia illucens to degrade plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Entomoremediation is a subtype of bioremediation that involves the use of insects to degrade and manage plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Entomoremediation is the process of using insects, such as mealworms, to degrade plastic waste, with potential applications in reducing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Entomoremediation refers to the use of insects, such as Tenebrio molitor, in the biodegradation of plastics and other waste materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL BUILDING INSULATION POLYSTYRENE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal building insulation polystyrene foam is a type of plastic waste used in construction, which can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KITCHEN SPONGE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Kitchen sponge is a type of polyurethane (PU1) plastic waste that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMERCIAL THERMAL INSULATION FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Commercial thermal insulation foam is a type of polyurethane (PU2) plastic waste that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MECHANICAL RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mechanical recycling is a method of re-introducing plastic waste into the production cycle, suitable for thermoplastics like polyethylene, polypropylene, and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical recycling is a process for recycling polystyrene, though it is not currently of great market interest for its recycled form.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemical recycling involves converting plastic waste into valuable chemical substances or precursors of plastic, including methods like pyrolysis and gasification.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical recycling is a method of plastic waste disposal that involves breaking down plastics into their chemical components, but it is not a complete solution to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYROLYSIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrolysis is a chemical process used to break down plastic wastes into smaller molecules, often for energy recovery or recycling purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolysis is a chemical recycling method where plastics decompose under high temperatures and anaerobic conditions, producing biofuel.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolysis is a thermal decomposition process studied using thermogravimetric analysis to understand the biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolysis is a thermal decomposition process used to break down materials like polystyrene into smaller molecules, often aided by catalysts.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolysis is a thermal decomposition process that occurs at high temperatures, producing combustible gases and biochar from organic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolysis is a thermal decomposition process that occurs at high temperatures, producing combustible gases and biochar from organic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolysis is a process used to mitigate microplastics in sewage sludge by breaking down plastic materials at high temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolysis is a process used to mitigate microplastics in sewage sludge by breaking them down at high temperatures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gasification is a chemical recycling method that converts plastic waste into gaseous products for use in the energy industry.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioplastics are plastics capable of degradation under natural conditions, such as exposure to sunlight or moisture, and include materials like polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA) and polylactide (PLA).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYHYDROXYALKANOATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA) are bioplastics that can degrade under natural conditions, offering a sustainable alternative to petroleum-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYLACTIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polylactide (PLA) is a bioplastic that can degrade under natural conditions, providing an eco-friendly alternative to traditional plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BREVIBACILLUS BORSTELENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Brevibacillus borstelensis is a bacterial species that contributes to the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Brevibacillus borstelensis is a bacterium associated with soil that has shown promise in the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Brevibacillus borstelensis is a bacterium associated with soil that shows potential for the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Brevibacillus borstelensis is a thermophilic bacterium capable of biodegrading polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Brevibacillus borstelensis is a thermophilic bacterium known for its ability to degrade polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Brevibacillus borstelensis is a thermophilic bacterium known for its ability to degrade polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Brevibacillus borstelensis is a thermophilic bacterium capable of biodegrading polyethylene, making it significant in environmental studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Brevibacillus borstelensis is a thermophilic bacterium known for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene, making it significant in environmental research.&lt;SEP&gt;Brevibacillus borstelensis is a thermophilic bacterium capable of biodegrading polyethylene, a common synthetic plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS BREVIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus brevis is a bacterium that has been studied for its ability to biodegrade plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS STUTZERI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas stutzeri is a bacterial strain reported to contribute to the biodegradation of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) in selective enrichment cultures.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas stutzeri is a bacterium known for its ability to efficiently biodegrade high-molecular-weight polyethylene glycols.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas stutzeri is a soil bacterium that is promising for the biodegradation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHIZOPUS DELEMAR">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhizopus delemar is a fungus that has been studied for its potential in the biodegradation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUCOR SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mucor sp. is a fungus that has shown potential for the biodegradation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PAECILOMYCES SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Paecilomyces sp. is a fungus that has been studied for its ability to biodegrade plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOMYCES SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermomyces sp. is a fungus that has shown potential for the biodegradation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIBOLIUM CONFUSUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tribolium confusum is a beetle species that can penetrate packaging films, contributing to material degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tribolium confusum is a beetle species that has been studied for its ability to penetrate packaging films.&lt;SEP&gt;Tribolium confusum is a beetle species whose larvae have been shown to biodegrade polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Tribolium confusum is a species of beetle studied for its genetic analysis of benzoquinone production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORCYRA CEPHALONICA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Corcyra cephalonica, the rice moth, is an insect capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE) even after antibiotic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE-BUTADIENE RUBBER (SBR)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR) is a synthetic rubber that can be biodegraded by insects like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIRES CRUMBS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Tire crumbs are small particles of rubber from tires that can be biodegraded by insects like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIFIDOBACTERIACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bifidobacteriaceae is a family of beneficial bacteria that can grow in the intestines of mice fed with Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NPK FERTILIZERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NPK (Nitrogen, Phosphorus, and Potassium) fertilizers are traditional fertilizers that can be substituted by worm frass in a circular economy.&lt;SEP&gt;NPK fertilizers are chemical fertilizers containing nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium, which can be substituted or supplemented with Tenebrio molitor frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOGAS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biogas is a renewable energy source that can be produced through the anaerobic digestion of Tenebrio molitor frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UV-PROTECTIVE PACKAGING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">UV-protective packaging is a type of packaging material that can be produced using chitosan derived from Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE FOAM (PU1)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane foam (PU1) is a type of plastic used in the experiment, specifically in the form of kitchen sponges, to study its biodegradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE THERMAL INSULATION FOAM (PU2)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane thermal insulation foam (PU2) is a type of plastic used in the experiment to study its biodegradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE FOAM (PE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene foam (PE) is a type of plastic used in the experiment to study its biodegradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The experimental procedure involves studying the biodegradation of various plastics by mealworm larvae under controlled laboratory conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHEAT BRANS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Wheat brans are used as a substrate for acclimatizing mealworm larvae before the experiment begins.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KITCHEN SPONGE (PU1)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Kitchen sponge (PU1) is a form of polyurethane foam used in the experiment to study its biodegradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPHENYLPOLYISOCYANATE POLYMETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyphenylpolyisocyanate polymethylene is a component of polyurethane thermal insulation foam (PU2) used in the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE PACKAGING FOAM (PE)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene packaging foam (PE) is used as a filling material in packages and is tested for biodegradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LABORATORY BALANCE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A laboratory balance (OHAUS EX224M) is used to weigh the plastic substrates daily during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADULT BEETLES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Adult beetles are the final developmental stage of mealworms and are capable of eating plastics, similar to the larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FT-IR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FT-IR is a technique used to characterize the functional groups of polypropylene (PP) in biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;FT-IR is used to analyze the chemical composition of plastic remnants after biodegradation by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) is a technique used to obtain the infrared spectrum of absorption or emission of a solid, liquid, or gas.&lt;SEP&gt;FT-IR is a tool used to characterize the biodegradation of plastics by identifying the formation of oxidized groups during the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;FT-IR is a tool used to characterize the biodegradation of plastics by identifying the formation of oxidized groups during the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATTENUATED TOTAL REFLECTION (ATR/FT-IR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ATR/FT-IR is a technique used in FT-IR spectroscopy to analyze the chemical properties of plastic remnants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FT-IR TENSOR 27 SPECTROPHOTOMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The FT-IR Tensor 27 spectrophotometer is an analytical instrument used to collect FT-IR spectra, equipped with a PIKE measuring cell featuring crystalline diamond embedded in zinc selenide.&lt;SEP&gt;The FT-IR Tensor 27 spectrophotometer is used to develop FT-IR spectra of plastic remnants after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FT-IR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FT-IR) is an analytical technique used to identify chemical bonds and functional groups in materials by measuring their absorption of infrared radiation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDXRF">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy Dispersive X-ray Fluorescence (EDXRF) is a non-destructive analytical technique used to determine the elemental composition of materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLASS FILTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A glass filter with a pore size of 1 μm was used to filter digestates in the experimental process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIKE MEASURING CELL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The PIKE measuring cell is a component of the FT-IR spectrophotometer, featuring crystalline diamond embedded in zinc selenide for accurate spectral measurements.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDX-7000">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The EDX-7000 is an Energy Dispersive X-ray Fluorescence (EDXRF) instrument used to measure the elemental concentrations in plastic samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EM ACE COATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The EM ACE coater is a device used to sputter coat plastic samples with a 30 nm Au layer for SEM imaging.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIBRA SEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Libra SEM is a scanning electron microscope used to visualize the surface morphology of plastic samples at high resolution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIKON D7100">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Nikon D7100 is a digital camera used for photography and time-lapse imaging in the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AF-D DX NIKKOR 18-105MM LENS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The AF-D DX NIKKOR 18-105mm lens is a camera lens used with the Nikon D7100 for capturing detailed images.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADOBE AFTER EFFECTS 2020">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Adobe After Effects 2020 is software used to create time-lapse movies from photographs taken during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADOBE MEDIA ENCODER 2020">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Adobe Media Encoder 2020 is software used to encode and process video files, including time-lapse movies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STATISTICA 13.1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Statistica 13.1 is statistical software used to analyze experimental results, including t-Student tests and ANOVA with post hoc Tukey's test.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE 1 (PU1)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane 1 (PU1) is a type of plastic that was consumed and degraded by Tenebrio molitor, with a significant reduction in mass observed during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE 2 (PU2)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane 2 (PU2) is a type of plastic that was consumed and degraded by Tenebrio molitor, showing a higher percentage of utilization compared to other plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MORPHOLOGICAL PARAMETERS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Morphological parameters refer to the physical changes in mealworm larvae, such as weight loss or gain, when fed with different types of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Morphological parameters, such as mass and length of mealworm larvae, were measured to assess the impact of plastic consumption on their growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC UTILIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic utilization refers to the percentage of plastic mass consumed and degraded by mealworms, with PE showing the highest utilization rate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STUDENT T-TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Student t-test was used to determine significant differences in the mass reduction of plastics consumed by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANOVA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ANOVA (Analysis of Variance) is a statistical method used in the study to compare the effects of different diets on PS degradation and larvae growth.&lt;SEP&gt;ANOVA (Analysis of Variance) is a statistical method used to evaluate differences in plastic consumption, turbidity, biofilm formation, and particle count analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;ANOVA was used to assess differences in plastic consumption rates, survival rates, and gut microbial diversity between larvae species.&lt;SEP&gt;ANOVA with post hoc Tukey's test was used to analyze significant differences between the degradation rates of different plastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Analysis of variance (ANOVA) is a statistical method used to evaluate differences in data, such as contact angle changes and weight loss in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;ANOVA is a statistical method used to evaluate significant differences in mealworm survival indices and plastic degradation metrics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE (PU1)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane (PU1) is a type of plastic characterized by high concentrations of Ca, Al, Si, and Cl, often used in kitchen sponges and other products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE (PU2)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane (PU2) is another type of plastic with a distinct elemental composition, containing high levels of Cl and P, used in various industrial applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELEMENTS CONTENT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A research study focused on analyzing the elemental composition of various plastics, including PS, PU1, PU2, and PE, using EDXRF techniques to understand their environmental implications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROMINE (BR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bromine (Br) is a chemical element found in trace amounts in polystyrene (PS), which may have implications for its environmental impact and toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALUMINUM (AL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aluminum (Al) is an element present in polyurethane (PU1) and polyethylene (PE), which may influence their structural and environmental characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SILICON (SI)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Silicon (Si) is an element detected in polyurethane (PU1), contributing to its material properties and potential environmental interactions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLORINE (CL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chlorine (Cl) is an element found in high concentrations in polyurethane (PU2) and trace amounts in polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), which may affect their degradation and toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAFNIUM (HF)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hafnium (Hf) is a trace element detected in polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), which may have implications for their material properties and environmental interactions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHROMIUM (CR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chromium (Cr) is an element found in trace amounts in polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU1), and polyethylene (PE), which may affect their environmental and toxicological profiles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NICKEL (NI)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nickel (Ni) is an element found in trace amounts in polyurethane (PU1), which may have implications for its environmental and toxicological behavior.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SULFUR (S)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sulfur (S) is an element found in trace amounts in polystyrene (PS) and polyurethane (PU1), which may influence their environmental and chemical behavior.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PU1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PU1 is a specific type of polyurethane plastic that mealworms can consume and degrade.&lt;SEP&gt;PU1 is a type of polyurethane foam that was studied for its degradation by mealworms, showing significant surface wrinkling after digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;PU1 refers to a type of soft polyurethane (PU) used in kitchen sponges, which is less efficiently degraded by mealworms compared to rigid PU due to its flexible and elastic properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PU2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PU2 is another specific type of polyurethane plastic that mealworms can consume and degrade.&lt;SEP&gt;PU2 is another type of polyurethane foam that was studied for its degradation by mealworms, showing smoothed edges and wrinkled surfaces after digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;PU2 refers to a type of rigid polyurethane (PU) used in commercial insulation foam, which is more efficiently degraded by mealworms due to its brittle and stiff properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORN FLOUR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Corn flour is a dietary supplement used in experiments to enhance the plastic consumption rate of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is a food additive used in studies to enhance the degradation efficiency of plastics like PS by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is used as a control diet in the study to compare the effects of polystyrene feeding on Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is a dietary supplement used in the study to enhance the polystyrene consumption rate of Tenebrio obscurus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is a normal diet for Tenebrio obscurus larvae, providing sufficient nutrients and carbon for their growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is a nutrition-rich codiet that enhances the consumption and biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is used as a codiet in feeding experiments with Tenebrio obscurus to study its effect on polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is used as a codiet in the study to assess its impact on the survival and PS consumption rates of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MITOCHONDRIAL DNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mitochondrial DNA in Tenebrio molitor can undergo epigenetic modifications when the larvae are fed polyurethane (PU) as a sole source of feed, indicating potential biological impacts of plastic consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Mitochondrial DNA is studied in the context of epigenetic modifications caused by polyurethane (PU) as a sole source of feed for mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mitochondrial DNA is the genetic material found in mitochondria, which can undergo epigenetic modifications due to environmental factors like polyurethane foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROPHOTOGRAPHY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scanning electron microphotography is a technique used to analyze the surface structure of plastics before and after degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPIGENETIC MODIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Epigenetic modification refers to changes in gene expression caused by environmental factors, such as exposure to polyurethane foam in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Epigenetic modification refers to changes in gene expression caused by environmental factors, such as polyurethane (PU), studied in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD ADDITIVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Food additives like wheat bran and corn flour are used to enhance the degradation efficiency of plastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Food additives such as cinnamon powder, bran, and sucrose are used in experiments to study their effects on the consumption of polystyrene by superworms and mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELEMENT CONTENT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Element content analysis is a method used to deduce the chemical composition of plastics, such as PU, and identify additives that affect their biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPERIMENT DESIGN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The experiment design refers to the methodology used to study the plastic consumption rates of mealworms, including the use of larvae and adult insects in one container.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENETIC DIFFERENCES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Genetic differences between mealworm populations can significantly affect their plastic consumption rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXABROMOCYCLODODECANE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD) is a common flame retardant found in polystyrene, studied for its fate in mealworms during biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexabromocyclododecane is a chemical additive found in polystyrene foam, though in minimal amounts (&lt;1%) in the materials used in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexabromocyclododecane is a flame retardant added to polystyrene, which may affect its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALCIUM CARBONATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Calcium carbonate (CaCO3) is a common filler added to plastics like polyethylene to enhance strength and reduce production costs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRICLOSAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Triclosan is an antimicrobial agent that may be added to polyurethane (PU) plastics, potentially affecting their biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterial species capable of breaking down polystyrene (PS) and has been previously isolated from the gut of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterial species involved in the biodegradation of various forms of polystyrene, including raw, processed, and expanded polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium capable of degrading crude oil and producing biosurfactants, found in the gut microbiome of PS-degrading mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium isolated from the gut extract of superworms, capable of biodegrading various types of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium isolated from the gut of Zophobas morio, capable of degrading plastics like polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium isolated from the gut of Zophobas morio, known for its role in the biodegradation of plastics through enzymatic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium known for producing rhamnolipid biosurfactants, which are used in bioremediation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a soil bacterium proven to degrade various types of plastic waste, including PS foam.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterial species involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIGMENTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pigments are colorants added to plastics, such as green and red pigments, which may influence the biodegradation process by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SILICON">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Silicon is an element found in plastics, often added as wollastonite (CaSiO3) or other compounds to improve flame retardant properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLORINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chlorine is an element present in plastics, often added as chlorinated compounds like chlorinated alkyl phosphates, which act as flame retardants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOSPHORUS">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Phosphorus is a nutrient in yellow mealworm larvae, influenced by the degradation of plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorus is an element found in plastics, often added in the form of chlorinated alkyl phosphates, which serve as flame retardants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALUMINUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aluminum is an element added to plastics as stabilizers or fillers, such as mica or kaolin, to enhance material properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAFNIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hafnium is a trace element found in plastics, likely due to contamination from catalysts used in polyolefin production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXIC HEAVY METALS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxic heavy metals, such as cadmium (Cd), mercury (Hg), and lead (Pb), are pollutants that were not detected in the plastics studied.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDXRF ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">EDXRF analysis is a technique used to determine the concentration of elements in mealworm larvae after plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FILLERS AND FLAME RETARDANTS (FRS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fillers and flame retardants (FRs) are additives in plastic waste that may influence the consumption rates and biodegradation efficiency of plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTOMOREMEDIATION OPTIMIZATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Entomoremediation optimization involves strategies to improve the efficiency of plastic biodegradation by mealworms, such as separating larvae and adults to prevent cannibalism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE UTILIZATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste utilization refers to the process of using mealworms to degrade plastic waste, with the goal of increasing efficiency and reducing environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC RISK ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic risk assessment involves evaluating the potential environmental risks of microplastics spread through insect feces after the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REFUSE-DERIVED FUEL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Refuse-derived fuel is a type of fuel produced from plastic waste and other materials, used in small gasification systems for energy recovery.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL POLYESTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial polyesters are biodegradable polymers produced by microorganisms, which can be degraded by specific enzymes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOCCOCCUS RUBER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodococcus ruber is a bacterium known for its ability to colonize and degrade polystyrene surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOPHILES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermophiles are heat-loving microorganisms capable of degrading poly(L-lactic acid) and other plastics under high-temperature conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(L-LACTIC ACID)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(L-lactic acid) is a biodegradable plastic that can be degraded by thermophilic microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE GLYCOLS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene glycols are high-molecular-weight polymers that can be biodegraded by specific bacteria like Pseudomonas stutzeri.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX MOTH LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella) are capable of biodegrading polyethylene and polystyrene, with their gut microbiome playing a key role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax moth larvae are organisms studied for their potential to biodegrade polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella) are organisms that can chew on polyethylene films, potentially contributing to biodegradation, but they do not grow on a plastic-only diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax moth larvae are macro-organisms investigated for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene and polystyrene, offering potential solutions to plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL SAFETY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental safety involves assessing the impact of plastic waste and its degradation products on ecosystems and human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GIANT AFRICAN LAND SNAILS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Giant African land snails (Achatina fulica) are capable of biodegrading expanded polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLACK SOLDIER FLY LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Black soldier fly larvae (Hermetia illucens) are being studied for their potential in entomoremediation and as a protein source for livestock.&lt;SEP&gt;Black soldier fly larvae are used to deal with food waste and can be bred alongside mealworms and superworms for waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Black soldier fly larvae (Hermetia illucens) are insects reared on organic waste streams, studied for their microbiota and potential in waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Black soldier fly larvae (Hermetia illucens) are studied for their head morphology and mouthparts, with potential applications in biodegradation and waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VULCANIZED SBR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vulcanized SBR (Styrene-Butadiene Rubber) is a synthetic rubber used in tires and other industrial applications, which can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Vulcanized SBR is a type of rubber waste that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Vulcanized styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR) is a synthetic rubber that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIRE CRUMB">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Tire crumb is a form of recycled rubber from tires, often used in playgrounds and sports fields, and can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Tire crumb is a type of rubber waste that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Tire crumb, a byproduct of recycled tires, can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, reducing environmental waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RICE MOTH LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rice moth larvae (Corcyra cephalonica) are capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONFUSED FLOUR BEETLE LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Confused flour beetle larvae (Tribolium confusum) are capable of biodegrading plastic wastes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm frass is the excrement of mealworms, studied for its potential use as a fertilizer and its impact on crop growth and soil properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is the excrement of mealworms, used to study its effects on plant growth and as a by-product of polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is the excrement of mealworms, which has been found to be less effective in supporting plant growth compared to superworm frass, possibly due to its poorer moisture retention and pungent odor.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is the excrement of mealworms, which has potential as a biofertilizer and can improve soil properties and plant growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is the waste produced by mealworms and has potential use as a fertilizer, impacting crop growth and soil properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is the excrement of mealworms, which can be used as a fertilizer after the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is the excrement produced by mealworms after consuming plastics, which contains residual polymers that are analyzed to study plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is the excrement of mealworms, which is analyzed to study the degradation of polystyrene and other materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is the excrement produced by mealworms, containing degraded polystyrene and other byproducts of the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR PROTEIN">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor protein is a source of peptides that can modulate physiological processes and is considered an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane foam (PU) is a type of plastic that can be consumed by superworms and yellow mealworms, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foam is a synthetic material that can be a source of environmental pollution and is studied for its biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foam is a type of plastic polymer widely used in industries such as automotive and textiles, known for its resistance to degradation and contribution to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foam is a type of plastic that can induce epigenetic modifications in organisms like Tenebrio molitor during metamorphosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane foam is another type of plastic that contributes to environmental pollution and has been studied for its biodegradation by organisms like mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEWAGE SLUDGE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Sewage sludge contains microbial communities capable of degrading complex substances, including polystyrene, in wastewater treatment systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Sewage sludge is a byproduct of wastewater treatment that often contains microplastics and other pollutants.&lt;SEP&gt;Sewage sludge is a byproduct of wastewater treatment that can be fractionated and converted into biodiesel, offering a potential solution for waste management and energy production.&lt;SEP&gt;Sewage sludge is a byproduct of wastewater treatment that can contain microbial communities capable of degrading plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Sewage sludge is a byproduct of wastewater treatment that can contain microplastics and other pollutants, requiring purification processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sewage sludge is a waste material that can contain microplastics and is treated using hyperthermophilic composting technology for biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sewage sludge is a byproduct of wastewater treatment that can contain microplastics and other pollutants, requiring microbial degradation for safe disposal.&lt;SEP&gt;Sewage sludge is a byproduct of wastewater treatment that can contain microplastics and other pollutants, requiring mitigation strategies like pyrolysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLACK SOLDIER FLY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Black Soldier Fly (BSF) is an insect species from the Diptera order, known for its ability to upcycle organic substrates and its potential in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Black Soldier Fly (BSF) is an insect used in studies to evaluate the impact of plastic exposure on insect metabolism and larval weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Black Soldier Fly (BSF) larvae are used in organic waste treatment and can assimilate plastic carbon into organic biomass, showing unique metabolic responses to different plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Black soldier fly frass has been shown to support the growth of crops like maize and ryegrass, demonstrating the potential of insect frass as an alternative fertilizer.&lt;SEP&gt;Black soldier fly is an insect whose fats have antimicrobial effects and can impact gut microbiota in animals.&lt;SEP&gt;Black soldier fly larvae are used to produce frass fertilizer, which has nitrogen fertilizer equivalence and is beneficial for maize production.&lt;SEP&gt;The black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens) is an insect studied for its head morphology and mouthparts, with potential applications in biodegradation and waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
