    <node id="PLASTIC-DEGRADING MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-degrading microorganisms are bacteria and fungi capable of breaking down synthetic polymers, contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading microorganisms are bacteria and fungi capable of breaking down synthetic polymers, contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading microorganisms are bacteria or fungi capable of breaking down plastics, such as Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 and Aspergillus tubingensis.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading microorganisms are bacteria or fungi capable of breaking down synthetic polymers, such as polystyrene, PCL, PBSA, and PBS.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-degrading microorganisms, such as those found in the gut of mealworms, are being studied for their ability to break down environmental pollutants like polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE REDUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste reduction refers to strategies and research aimed at minimizing plastic pollution, including biodegradation by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste reduction refers to the process of decreasing plastic waste in the environment, which can be achieved through the biodegradation capabilities of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
