    <node id="DRAGON FRUIT CACTI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dragon fruit cacti (Hylocereus undatus) are used as indicator plants to study the effectiveness of superworm frass as a growth medium.&lt;SEP&gt;Dragon fruit cacti (Hylocereus undatus) were used in the study to test the effectiveness of worm frass as a natural fertilizer for plant growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Dragon fruit cacti are plants used in experiments to study the effects of worm frass on growth and rooting.&lt;SEP&gt;Dragon fruit cacti are plants used in the study to evaluate the effectiveness of worm frass in supporting plant growth, with superworm frass showing superior results compared to mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUCROSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sucrose is a chemical compound used in the preparation of working standards for nitrogen isotope calibration.&lt;SEP&gt;Sucrose is a food additive found to enhance the consumption of polystyrene (PS) by mealworms and superworms, optimizing biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sucrose is a food additive that significantly increases the consumption of polystyrene by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Sucrose is a food additive that was found to increase the consumption of polystyrene (PS) by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Sucrose is a food additive used in experiments to study its effect on the consumption of polystyrene by superworms and mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZERO-WASTE CIRCULAR ECONOMY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A zero-waste circular economy is a concept explored in the study, focusing on using superworms and mealworms to manage plastic waste and support plant growth.&lt;SEP&gt;A zero-waste circular economy is a sustainable approach that utilizes mealworms and superworms to address plastic waste and food production challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;The study explores the use of mealworms and superworms to create a zero-waste circular economy by consuming polystyrene (PS) and producing frass as a natural fertilizer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZERO WASTE INTERNATIONAL ALLIANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Zero Waste International Alliance defines zero waste as a goal, which aligns with the study's aim of creating a zero-waste circular economy using worms for plastic agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECO CYCLE SOLUTIONS HUB">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Eco Cycle Solutions Hub supports the concept of zero waste, which is relevant to the study's methodology of using worms to convert PS waste into useful resources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARDUINO DEVICES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Arduino devices were used to monitor the environmental conditions (humidity and temperature) for the worms during the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CINNAMON POWDER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cinnamon powder is a food additive used in experiments to study its effect on the consumption of polystyrene by superworms and mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYLOCEREUS UNDATUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hylocereus undatus, commonly known as dragon fruit cactus, is used in experiments to study the effects of worm frass on plant growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WORM FRASS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Worm frass is the excrement of worms, used in experiments to study its effects on plant growth and as a by-product of polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY-MASS SPECTROMETRY (GC-MS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">GC-MS is a technique used to analyze the presence of polystyrene and its by-products in worm frass and worm carcasses.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-MS was used to detect intermediates of polystyrene and other feedstock metabolism in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-MS is used to identify depolymerization products of plastics after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEA LEAVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tea leaves are used as a control medium in experiments comparing the effects of worm frass on cacti growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Used tea leaves are used as a positive control in experiments to study their effects on plant growth compared to worm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DICHLOROMETHANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dichloromethane is a solvent used for extracting polyethylene from mealworm frass samples during the characterization of plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Dichloromethane is a solvent used in the preparation of polymer emulsions, including polystyrene and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Dichloromethane is a solvent used to dissolve polystyrene and its by-products for GC-MS analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Dichloromethane is a solvent used to dissolve polystyrene for the preparation of polystyrene emulsion in microbial degradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Dichloromethane is a solvent used to prepare polystyrene emulsions for microbial degradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Dichloromethane is a solvent used to dissolve and prepare polystyrene films for microbial degradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Dichloromethane is a solvent used to dissolve polystyrene foams in the preparation of PS emulsions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GC-MS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GC-MS analysis is a method used to detect and identify chemical compounds in worm frass, providing insights into the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-MS analysis is a method used to detect and quantify polystyrene and its degradation by-products in worm frass and worm carcasses.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-MS analysis is used to detect chemicals in the carcasses and frass of PS-fed superworms and mealworms, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-MS analysis was used to detect individual chemicals in mealworm and superworm frass, identifying compounds like Heptacosane, Nonadecane, and Octadecane.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-MS analysis is used to identify intermediates produced during the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-MS analysis is used to detect the degradation products of NPS, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is used to analyze the intermediates and products generated from the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;GC-MS analysis is a method used to identify and quantify the biodegradation products of PE and PS microplastics in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Superworm frass is the excrement of superworms, used to study its effects on plant growth and as a by-product of polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm frass is the excrement of superworms, which has been found to support superior rooting and growth in plants compared to mealworm frass, possibly due to its moisture retention and presence of growth-promoting compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS BALLS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS balls are small polystyrene particles used in experiments to study their consumption by superworms and mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WORM WEIGHT CHANGES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Worm weight changes are measured to assess the effects of polystyrene consumption on superworms and mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CACTI ROOTING FREQUENCY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cacti rooting frequency is analyzed to study the effects of different media, such as worm frass and tea leaves, on plant growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CACTI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cacti are plants used in experiments to study the effects of worm frass on growth and rooting.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="9-OLEAMIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">9-Oleamide is a fatty acid primary amide detected in the frass of worms fed with polystyrene, indicating chemical changes during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID PRIMARY AMIDES (FAPAS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid primary amides are compounds detected in the frass of worms fed with polystyrene, suggesting chemical transformations during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CINNAMON">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cinnamon is a food additive tested for its effect on polystyrene consumption by superworms and mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DI WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Deionized (DI) water is used as a control in contact angle analysis to compare the hydrophobicity of mealworm supernatant fractions.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized (DI) water is used as a control in contact angle measurements to assess hydrophobicity changes in polystyrene (PS) films.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized (DI) water is used to ensure adherence of food additives to polystyrene balls in experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZYL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzyl groups are chemical structures detected in GC-MS analysis of polystyrene, indicating its presence in worm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHORT-CHAIN ALKANES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Short-chain alkanes are compounds detected in the frass of worms fed with polystyrene, suggesting chemical breakdown during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALCOHOLS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alcohols are compounds detected in the frass of worms fed with polystyrene, indicating chemical transformations during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYCLOALKANES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cycloalkanes are compounds detected in the frass of worms fed with polystyrene, suggesting chemical changes during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TABLE SUCROSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Table sucrose is a food additive that significantly increases the consumption of polystyrene (PS) by mealworms and superworms, enhancing biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD WASTE CONTAMINATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Food waste contamination is considered an advantage for polystyrene (PS) degradation by mealworms and superworms, as it enhances their consumption of PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DELAYED PUPATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Delayed pupation in mealworms and superworms is beneficial for plastic degradation, as larvae consume more plastic than adult beetles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPTACOSANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Heptacosane is a short-chain alkene semiochemical detected in superworm frass, which may contribute to its growth-promoting properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Heptacosane is a short-chain-growth-promoting alkene semiochemical detected in the GC-MS analysis of superworm frass, aiding plant growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NONADECANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nonadecane is a hydrocarbon identified in the study, with no specific industrial applications mentioned.&lt;SEP&gt;Nonadecane is a short-chain alkene semiochemical detected in superworm frass, which may contribute to its growth-promoting properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Nonadecane is a short-chain-growth-promoting alkene semiochemical detected in the GC-MS analysis of superworm frass, contributing to plant growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OCTADECANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Octadecane is a short-chain alkene semiochemical detected in superworm frass, which may contribute to its growth-promoting properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Octadecane is a short-chain-growth-promoting alkene semiochemical detected in the GC-MS analysis of superworm frass, supporting plant growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTICIZER ACCUMULATION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plasticizer accumulation in the food chain is a concern that requires further research to ensure the safety of using plastic-eating worms in food production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER-TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical composition of residual plastics in frass, comparing it to original PS and LDPE foams.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is used to identify chemical changes in synthetic polymers during digestion by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to analyze the chemical properties of polystyrene (PS) residues in the frass of mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to analyze chemical changes in polystyrene (PS) during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is a method used to analyze the chemical structure and functional groups of polymers during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIQUID-STATE \(^{1}H\) NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Liquid-state \(^{1}H\) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) is a sensitive method used to study the depolymerization and oxidation of polystyrene (PS) in the frass of mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Worms, including superworms and mealworms, are organisms that can degrade polystyrene and produce frass that can be used to support plant growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="URBAN FARMING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Urban farming is a concept explored in the study, where worms are used to degrade plastic waste and their frass is utilized to support food crop growth, contributing to sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD CROPS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Food crops are plants that can be supported by worm frass, making worms a dual solution for plastic waste degradation and food production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COVID-19 PANDEMIC">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The COVID-19 pandemic has led to increased use of disposable face masks, resulting in environmental pollution from microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The COVID-19 pandemic has worsened the plastic waste problem, increasing the urgency for solutions like worm-based plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN ENGINEERING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein engineering of enzymes from plastic-eating worms is a promising area of research, though it requires further optimization for large-scale implementation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDOOR IMPLEMENTATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The study demonstrates the feasibility of implementing worm-based plastic degradation and frass-supported plant growth indoors, making it a scalable urban solution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD SUSTAINABILITY">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Food sustainability is addressed by using worms as an alternative protein source and their frass to support food crop growth, contributing to both plastic waste reduction and food production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMMONIA-SMELLING MEALWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm frass has a pungent ammonia smell, which makes it less suitable for indoor use compared to superworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHORT-CHAIN-GROWTH-PROMOTING ALKENE SEMIOCHEMICALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Short-chain-growth-promoting alkene semiochemicals, such as Heptacosane, Nonadecane, and Octadecane, are detected in superworm frass and may contribute to its plant growth-promoting properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLACK SOLDIER FLY FRASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Black soldier fly frass is the excrement of black soldier fly larvae, which is used as a biofertilizer to improve crop growth and nitrogen use efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXIC POLLUTANTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxic pollutants are harmful substances released from plastic waste, posing risks to the environment and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Toxic pollutants from plastic waste are a significant environmental concern, and their mitigation is studied in the context of biodegradation by insects like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Toxic pollutants from plastic waste are a significant environmental concern, and their mitigation is studied in the context of biodegradation by insects like mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECTS AS FEED">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Insects, such as mealworms and black soldier flies, are being explored as sustainable feed alternatives for livestock and aquaculture.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EU FOOD SAFETY AGENCY">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">The EU Food Safety Agency is a regulatory body that evaluates the safety of food products, including edible insects like yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN FERTILIZER EQUIVALENCE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen fertilizer equivalence refers to the ability of organic fertilizers, such as insect frass, to provide nitrogen to crops in a manner comparable to synthetic fertilizers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL PROPERTIES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil properties are influenced by organic amendments, such as insect frass, which can improve soil health and fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil properties can be altered by the presence of microplastics, which can affect plant performance and ecosystem health.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil properties can be altered by the presence of microplastics, which may affect plant growth and ecosystem health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANT GROWTH">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plant growth can be enhanced by the use of biofertilizers, such as mealworm frass, which provide essential nutrients and improve soil conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLACK SOLDIER FLY FRASS FERTILIZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Black soldier fly frass fertilizer is an organic fertilizer derived from the larvae of black soldier flies, used to improve soil fertility and crop growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AERATED COMPOST TEA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aerated compost tea is a liquid fertilizer used to promote the growth of plants like lettuce, soybean, and sweet corn in organic cultivation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MILK TEA WASTE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Milk tea waste is co-applied with NPK fertilizers to improve sandy soil biochemical properties and enhance wheat growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas spp. are bacteria belonging to the Proteobacteria phylum and are known potential nitrogen-fixing bacteria found in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas spp. are bacteria that produce plant growth-promoting volatile organic compounds, benefiting the growth of Vigna radiata seedlings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAIZE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Maize is a crop that benefits from the application of black soldier fly frass fertilizer, which provides nitrogen fertilizer equivalence.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RYEGRASS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ryegrass is a type of grass that benefits from the agricultural value of black soldier fly larvae frass as an organic fertilizer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTUCA SATIVA (LETTUCE)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactuca sativa, commonly known as lettuce, is a plant whose growth is promoted by the application of aerated compost tea in organic cultivation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCINE MAX (SOYBEAN)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycine max, or soybean, is a crop that benefits from the growth-promoting effects of aerated compost tea in organic farming.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZEA MAYS (SWEET CORN)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zea mays, or sweet corn, is a crop that shows improved growth when treated with aerated compost tea in organic cultivation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIGNA RADIATA (MUNG BEAN)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Vigna radiata, or mung bean, is a plant whose seedlings respond positively to the volatile organic compounds produced by Pseudomonas spp.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHEAT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Wheat is a crop that benefits from the co-application of milk tea waste and NPK fertilizers, which improve sandy soil properties and enhance growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FARMED FISH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Farmed fish are aquatic organisms that have been studied for their potential to be fed with insects as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH FIBER FOOD WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">High fiber food waste is a type of organic waste that can be managed and utilized for the cultivation of black soldier fly larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHORT-CHAIN CHITIN OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Short-chain chitin oligomers are compounds that promote plant growth and are derived from chitin, a natural polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A polystyrene diet refers to the feeding of polystyrene to organisms like Tenebrio molitor to study its effects on growth, development, and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYNAMIC ENERGY BUDGET (DEB) MODEL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is a mathematical framework used to analyze the effects of polystyrene diet on the growth, development, and survival of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is a mathematical framework used to describe energy and mass fluxes in organisms, including the effects of environmental stressors like polystyrene on mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is a tool used to analyze the effects of polystyrene diet on Tenebrio molitor larval growth, development, and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MHETASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MHETase is an enzyme produced by Ideonella sakaiensis that binds to and degrades PET, a common plastic pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-DEGRADING AROMATIC POLYESTERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A plastic-degrading aromatic polyesterase is an enzyme engineered to break down aromatic polyesters, contributing to plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(ETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE) (PET)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET) is a widely used plastic that is a significant environmental pollutant and can be degraded by certain bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAB. MED.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Lab. Med. is a research study that investigates the role of plants in removing contaminants from the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NAT. COMMUN.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nat. Commun. is a research study that explores the structure and function of plastic-degrading enzymes like MHETase in Ideonella sakaiensis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROC. NATL. ACAD. SCI. USA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA is a research study that characterizes and engineers plastic-degrading enzymes, such as aromatic polyesterase, for environmental applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="APPL. ENVIRON. MICROBIOL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Appl. Environ. Microbiol. is a research study that provides biotechnological perspectives on microbial degradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCIENCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Science is a research study that identifies and characterizes bacteria, such as Ideonella sakaiensis, capable of degrading and assimilating PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENGINEERING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Engineering is a research study that focuses on enhancing the activity of PET hydrolase through protein engineering for improved plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET HYDROLASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET hydrolase is an enzyme engineered to enhance the degradation of poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET), a common plastic pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL IMPROVEMENT">
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
      <data key="d1">Milk tea waste, when co-applied with NPK fertilizers, improves sandy soil properties and enhances wheat growth.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTAMINANT REMOVAL">
      <data key="d2">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
      <data key="d1">Plants are studied for their ability to remove contaminants from the environment through phytoremediation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUANGZHOU MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Guangzhou are capable of metabolizing PS and LDPE microplastics, but not PVC, indicating regional differences in gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms from Guangzhou, China, which showed the ability to metabolize polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE) but not polyvinyl chloride (PVC).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAI'AN MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Tai'an are capable of metabolizing PS, PVC, and LDPE microplastics, though with varying efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms from Tai'an, China, which demonstrated the ability to metabolize all three tested microplastics: polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHENZHEN MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Shenzhen are capable of metabolizing PS, PVC, and LDPE microplastics, with regional differences in digestion efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms from Shenzhen, China, which effectively metabolized all three tested microplastics: polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-RAY DIFFRACTION (XRD)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">X-ray diffraction (XRD) is a technique used to analyze the crystal structure of frass and microplastics to understand the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray diffraction (XRD) is a technique used to analyze the crystalline structure of microplastics and frass produced by mealworms after metabolizing PS, PVC, and LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;XRD analysis is used to study changes in the crystalline structure of microplastics after being metabolized by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray diffraction (XRD) is used to measure changes in the crystallinity of HDPE film after degradation by Bacillus sp. PELW2042.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYZER (TGA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A thermogravimetric analyzer is used to perform thermal characterization of fecula and Styrofoam, providing insights into their degradation properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA) is a tool used to analyze the thermal properties of frass and microplastics to evaluate degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA) is used to study the thermal properties of Styrofoam and its degradation products in superworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIMETHYLFORMAMIDE (DMF)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dimethylformamide (DMF) is a solvent used to extract polystyrene (PS) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) samples for gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL GROUP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The carbonyl group is a functional group that appears in the frass of mealworms after they metabolize microplastics, indicating oxidation of hydrocarbon chains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS FROM GUANGZHOU">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Guangzhou are a specific population of mealworms studied for their ability to metabolize PS, PVC, and LDPE microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms from Guangzhou are capable of metabolizing PS and PVC, leading to changes in molecular structure and the formation of new functional groups in frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS FROM TAI'AN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Tai'an are a specific population of mealworms studied for their ability to metabolize PS, PVC, and LDPE microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms from Tai'an can metabolize PS, LDPE, and PVC, resulting in the formation of carbonyl groups and changes in crystalline structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS FROM SHENZHEN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Shenzhen are a specific population of mealworms studied for their ability to metabolize PS, PVC, and LDPE microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms from Shenzhen can degrade PS, LDPE, and PVC, with evidence of oxidation and changes in crystalline structure in frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZENE RING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The benzene ring is a chemical structure in polystyrene that is damaged during the metabolic process of mealworms, as indicated by the disappearance of its characteristic vibrations in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="O–H STRETCHING VIBRATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">O–H stretching vibration is a functional group observed in frass from mealworms fed with PVC, indicating the formation of water during the metabolic process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CH₂ SHEAR VIBRATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CH₂ shear vibration is a molecular vibration that disappears in frass from mealworms fed with PVC, indicating structural changes in the microplastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CH₂ PLANE SWING VIBRATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CH₂ plane swing vibration is a molecular vibration that disappears in frass from mealworms fed with PVC, indicating structural changes in the microplastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL GROUP FORMATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The formation of carbonyl groups in frass indicates the oxidation of hydrocarbon chains during the biodegradation of microplastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The formation of carbonyl groups is a chemical change observed during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating oxidative degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVC MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PVC microplastics are small particles of polyvinyl chloride that are less efficiently digested by mealworms compared to other microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC microplastics are small plastic particles made of polyvinyl chloride, which undergo decomposition in two stages: dechlorination and chain scission.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE microplastics are small particles of low-density polyethylene that mealworms can ingest and metabolize, with regional variations in digestion efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE microplastics are small plastic particles made of low-density polyethylene, which decompose in a single stage through chain scission, producing alkenes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DECHLORINATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Dechlorination is the first stage of PVC microplastic decomposition, involving the removal of chlorine atoms from the polymer structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHAIN SCISSION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Chain scission is the process of breaking polymer chains into smaller fragments, observed in the decomposition of both PVC and LDPE microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Chain scission refers to the breaking of long-chain polystyrene (PS) molecules into smaller fragments, indicating enzymatic depolymerization or microbial attack.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PS microplastics are small particles of polystyrene that mealworms can ingest and metabolize, with varying effects depending on the region of the mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;PS microplastics are small plastic particles made of polystyrene, which undergo depolymerization and degradation when metabolized by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;PS microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, used in microbial cultures to study degradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CROSS-LINKING REACTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cross-linking reaction is a chemical process that occurs during the biodegradation of microplastics, leading to changes in molecular weight distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-linking reaction refers to the formation of bonds between polymer chains, which can occur during the degradation of PS microplastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TG-DTG CURVE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TG-DTG curves are used to analyze the thermal decomposition of materials, such as microplastics and frass, to study their degradation behavior.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight distribution (MWD) refers to the range of molecular weights in a polymer sample, which changes during biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution analysis is used to study the changes in polystyrene structure after degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution refers to the range of molecular weights in the degraded microplastics found in mealworm frass, indicating the extent of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) refers to the range of molecular weights in a polymer, which changes during the biodegradation of LDPE by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOTA COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbiota composition refers to the specific microorganisms present in the digestive tracts of mealworms, which influence their ability to metabolize microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DAPHNIA MAGNA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Daphnia magna is a freshwater crustacean used in studies to assess the toxicity of environmental pollutants, including microplastics and nickel mixtures.&lt;SEP&gt;Daphnia magna is a small freshwater crustacean used in ecotoxicological studies to assess the toxicity of substances, including polyethylene degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(VINYL CHLORIDE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(vinyl chloride) (PVC) is a synthetic plastic polymer that is widely used in construction and packaging but poses environmental challenges due to its resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOPHILIC BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A thermophilic bacterium is a heat-loving microorganism capable of degrading low-molecular-weight polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </node>
