      <data key="d1">Radicals are highly reactive chemical species formed during the thermodegradation of polystyrene, contributing to its breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-TEMPERATURE GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">High-temperature gel permeation chromatography is a technique used to characterize polymers like low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TERRESTRIAL FOOD CHAIN">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The terrestrial food chain can be affected by the transfer of plastic debris, impacting organisms at various trophic levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRESHWATER MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Freshwater microplastics are emerging environmental contaminants that pose risks to aquatic ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Freshwater microplastics are small plastic particles found in freshwater systems, posing risks to aquatic life and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REEF BUILDING CORALS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Reef-building corals are marine organisms affected by microplastic exposure, which can impact their health and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OYSTER REPRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Oyster reproduction is negatively affected by exposure to polystyrene microplastics, as shown in environmental studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACARTIA TONSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acartia tonsa is a marine copepod species whose toxicity is exacerbated by microplastics and triclosan exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a thermoplastic material that is a common environmental pollutant and is studied for its biodegradation potential by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of plastic studied for its thermal degradation and potential for producing petrochemical feedstock.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of plastic that can be pyrolyzed to produce petrochemical feedstock.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL DEGRADATION OF POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal degradation of polystyrene involves the breakdown of the polymer under high temperatures, producing various byproducts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE2O4 NANOPARTICLES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fe2O4 nanoparticles are studied for their properties in superlattices within a polystyrene matrix.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAL DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Larval density refers to the study of how population density affects the food utilization efficiency of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMIDITY BEHAVIOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Humidity behavior refers to the study of how environmental factors like humidity affect organisms such as Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEMPERATURE AND RELATIVE HUMIDITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Temperature and relative humidity are environmental factors studied for their effects on the survival capacity of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEEP-SEA SEDIMENTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Deep-sea sediments are environments where microplastic pollution has been detected, indicating widespread contamination.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTARCTIC MARINE SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Antarctic marine system is an emerging area of research for studying the impact of plastics and microplastics on marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE-NANO TIO2 COMPOSITE FILM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE-nano TiO2 composite film is a material studied for its functionality and stability in packaging applications, as explored by Wooseok (2017).&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE-nano TiO2 composite film is a material studied for its functionality and stability in packaging applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLUIDISED BED PYROLYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fluidised bed pyrolysis is a process studied for converting low-density polyethylene into petrochemical feedstock, as described in the research by Williams and Williams (1999).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL AND PHYSICAL CHARACTERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemical and physical characterization is a part of the research process used to study the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, as described by Yang et al. (2015a).&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical and physical characterization is a research method used to study the properties and degradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical and physical characterization is a research method used to study the properties of materials, such as polystyrene, during biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOTOPIC TESTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Isotopic tests are used to study the biodegradation and mineralization of plastics, such as polystyrene, by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Isotopic tests are used to study the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, as described by Yang et al. (2015a).&lt;SEP&gt;Isotopic tests are used to study the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, providing insights into the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Isotopic tests are used to study the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, providing evidence of the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Isotopic tests are used to trace the degradation of plastics, such as polystyrene, by labeling them with isotopes like 13C to monitor their breakdown.&lt;SEP&gt;Isotopic tests are used to trace the degradation and mineralization of materials, such as polystyrene, by organisms like mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
      <data key="d1">Oyster reproduction is negatively affected by exposure to polystyrene microplastics, highlighting ecological risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that affect the gut microbiome of mice, leading to changes in fat and lipid metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles that can be completely biodegraded by Galleria mellonella larvae, offering a potential solution to microplastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles that can induce microbial dysbiosis and dysfunction in surrounding seawater.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics (PS-MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, posing environmental and health risks due to their persistence and accumulation in organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles that can cause gut toxicity in organisms like Eisenia fetida.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles that can be toxic to organisms like earthworms, but their toxicity can be reduced by protective soil layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics are small particles derived from polystyrene, which can accumulate in the environment and affect organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics (PS MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, which are degraded by mealworms in their intestinal tract.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, which mealworms can biodegrade, with degradation efficiency influenced by molecular weight.</data>
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PACKAGING">
      <data key="d2">chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE-nano TiO2 composite film is studied for its functionality and stability in packaging applications, as explored by Wooseok (2017).&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE-nano TiO2 composite film is studied for its functionality and stability in packaging applications.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRONOBACTER SAKAZAKII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cronobacter sakazakii is a bacterium from the Enterobacteriaceae family that increased by 24.70% in the EPS-fed group, potentially involved in EPS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter sakazakii is a bacterium identified as a potential EPS-degrading microorganism, showing increased population in mealworms fed with EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOCOCCUS GARVIEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactococcus garvieae is a bacterial species from the Streptococcaceae family that showed a significant increase in population density in the EPS-fed group, suggesting a role in EPS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus garvieae is another bacterium identified as a potential EPS-degrading microorganism, showing increased population in mealworms fed with EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTESTINAL MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Intestinal microbiota refers to the microbial community in the gut of organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor, which can shift due to plastic consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;The intestinal microbiota of mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, as it contains microorganisms capable of degrading the plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;The intestinal microbiota of mealworms plays a significant role in the degradation of EPS, with changes in microbial populations observed based on diet.&lt;SEP&gt;The intestinal microbiota of mealworms refers to the microbial community in their digestive tract, which changes based on their diet (bran or EPS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITOPCIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Citopcin is an antibiotic that has been shown to inhibit EPS degradation in mealworms more effectively than gentamicin, suggesting the presence of other EPS-degrading bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL HORMONES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental hormones are toxic substances released during the incineration of EPS, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIOXIN">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Dioxin is a toxic substance produced during the incineration of EPS, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLACETYL-COA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenylacetyl-CoA is an intermediate compound formed during the microbial degradation of styrene, which enters the TCA cycle for further breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRACELLULAR OXIDATIVE ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Extracellular oxidative enzymes are released by gut microorganisms and assist in the degradation of EPS by breaking down its chemical structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOMICS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenomics analysis is a research method used to study changes in the intestinal microbiota of mealworms based on their diet, particularly EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODIVERSITY INDICES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodiversity indices are used to measure the diversity of microbial populations in the gut of mealworms, particularly in response to EPS feeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS (T. MOLITOR LARVAE)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) are used in the study to investigate the effects of different diets (bran and EPS) on their intestinal microbiota and biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS (EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS is a type of polystyrene foam used in construction and packaging, which is a significant environmental pollutant due to its persistence in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a plastic material used as a diet for mealworms in the study, consisting of polystyrene, pentane, and hexabromocyclododecane.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of plastic used in construction for insulation, which is resistant to biodegradation and can be consumed by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, foam-like form of polystyrene that is more accessible for mealworms to consume and biodegrade compared to other forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight plastic foam used in packaging and insulation, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL FLORA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial flora analysis involves metagenomics to study the changes in the intestinal microbiota of mealworms based on their diet (bran or EPS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PENTANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pentane is a chemical component of EPS, making up 4%-7% of its composition, and is used as an effervescent gas in the production of EPS beads.&lt;SEP&gt;Pentane is a volatile organic compound with the CAS number 109-66-0, used as a blowing agent in the production of expanded polystyrene (EPS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2,5,6,9,10-HEXABROMOCYCLODODECANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2,5,6,9,10-Hexabromocyclododecane is a brominated flame retardant with the CAS number 3194-55-6, present in small quantities in expanded polystyrene (EPS).&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,5,6,9,10-Hexabromocyclododecane is a chemical component of EPS, present in less than 1% of its composition, and is used as a flame retardant.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,5,6,9,10-hexabromocyclododecane is a flame retardant found in polystyrene materials, present in less than 1% in the PS foam used in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE MONOMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene monomer is the base chemical used in the production of EPS, provided by Lotte Chemical Co.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomer is the basic building block of polystyrene, formed through the polymerization process, and is susceptible to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA GENE LIBRARY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The 16S rRNA gene library is used for metagenomic analysis to study the composition of the intestinal microbiota in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SØRENSEN–DICE COEFFICIENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Sørensen–Dice coefficient is a biodiversity index used to compare the similarity of microbial flora between the bran-fed and EPS-fed mealworm groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DOMINATION INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The domination index is used to evaluate the dominance of specific microbial species in the intestinal microbiota of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS ALKALINITRILICUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus alkalinitrilicus is a bacterial species found in the bran-fed group with a population density of 9.13%, but absent in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS VELEZENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus velezensis is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 0.96%, but absent in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLUS CRISPATUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus crispatus is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 1.09%, but absent in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLUS GRAMINIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus graminis is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 6.44%, but reduced to 3.04% in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLUS JOHNSONII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus johnsonii is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 0.11%, but absent in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLUS SALIVARIUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus salivarius is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 1.38%, but absent in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEDIOCOCCUS PENTOSACEUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pediococcus pentosaceus is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 10.16%, but reduced to 0.48% in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOCOCCUS TAIWANENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactococcus taiwanensis is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 2.40%, but reduced to 0.41% in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLOSTRIDIUM ORYZAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Clostridium oryzae is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 5.28%, but absent in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLOSTRIDIODES DIFFICILE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Clostridiodes difficile is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 0.25%, but absent in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAECALIBACTERIUM PRAUSNITZII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Faecalibacterium prausnitzii is a bacterial species present in the bran-fed group with a population density of 0.20%, but absent in the EPS-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPIROPLASMA VELICICRESCENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Spiroplasma velicicrescens is a bacterial species present in both the bran-fed and EPS-fed groups, with population densities of 50.53% and 29.66%, respectively.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L. TAIWANENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">L. taiwanensis is a microorganism identified in the study, showing different responses to EPS feeding, suggesting varying EPS degradation abilities at the species level.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L. GARVIEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">L. garvieae is another microorganism identified in the study, exhibiting opposite effects compared to L. taiwanensis when exposed to EPS feeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOMIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenomic analysis involves the study of genetic material recovered directly from environmental samples, such as the gut microbiota of mealworms, to understand their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic analysis is a research method used to study the intestinal microbiota of mealworms that ingested expanded polystyrene, providing insights into polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic analysis was used to study the changes in gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, identifying key microbial genera and functional pathways involved in PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic analysis was used to study the impact of PVC consumption on the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, identifying key microorganisms involved in PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic analysis is used to reveal the biodegradative potential of a landfill microbial community in degrading polyurethane and xenobiotic additives.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG PATHWAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The KEGG pathway associated with styrene degradation is referenced in the study, providing a framework for understanding the metabolic processes involved in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYMBIOTIC MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Symbiotic microorganisms in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae play a crucial role in the biodegradation of EPS and LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic microorganisms in the gut of mealworms are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene, as they perform host-specific metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenomics is a research approach used to characterize the gut microbiota of polystyrene-fed Alphitobius diaperinus, identifying microbial taxa associated with plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomics is a research method used to analyze the intestinal microbiota of mealworms, identifying microorganisms involved in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL STRESS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental stress refers to the challenges faced by microorganisms in nature, which must adapt to various conditions, including the presence of pollutants like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental stress, such as exposure to polystyrene, is studied using the DEB model to understand its impact on mealworms' energy budgets and physiological processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOMEOSTASIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Homeostasis refers to the balance of biochemical processes in mealworms, which is altered during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Homeostasis refers to the stable internal environment provided by the host (mealworms) that supports the metabolism of symbiotic microorganisms involved in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOGICAL MECHANISM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A biological mechanism refers to the process by which microorganisms degrade polystyrene, which can be studied to develop solutions for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;The biological mechanism refers to the processes by which symbiotic microorganisms in mealworms degrade polystyrene, which is a focus of future research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM CRUDE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum crude is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons that can contaminate agricultural soil, requiring bioremediation approaches for cleanup.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGRICULTURAL SOIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Agricultural soil can be contaminated by petroleum crude, requiring bioremediation approaches to restore its quality.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOREMEDIATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioremediation is the use of living organisms, such as bacteria or insects, to degrade or remove pollutants, including plastics, from the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioremediation is the use of living organisms, such as bacteria or insects, to degrade or remove pollutants, including plastics, from the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioremediation is the use of living organisms, such as mealworms, to degrade and remove environmental pollutants like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioremediation refers to the use of biological processes, such as biodegradation, to clean up plastic pollution and reduce environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioremediation refers to the use of living organisms, such as microorganisms or plants, to clean up contaminated environments like agricultural soil affected by petroleum crude.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioremediation refers to the use of living organisms, such as bacteria and fungi, to degrade environmental pollutants, including plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioremediation refers to the use of microorganisms to degrade environmental pollutants, such as polystyrene nanoplastics, offering a sustainable solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioremediation refers to the use of microorganisms to degrade environmental pollutants, such as polystyrene, offering potential applications in wastewater treatment and plastic pollution mitigation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDIAN JOURNAL OF MICROBIOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Indian Journal of Microbiology publishes research on microbial applications, including studies on bioremediation of petroleum-contaminated soil.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADVANCES IN TECHNOLOGY AND INNOVATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Advances in Technology and Innovation is a journal that publishes research on technological advancements, including studies on microbes capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOLOGICAL SOCIETY OF KOREA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Microbiological Society of Korea is an organization that conducts research on microbiology, including studies on plastic-degrading bacteria in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QIIME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">QIIME is a bioinformatics pipeline for analyzing high-throughput microbial community sequencing data, enabling taxonomic and functional profiling.&lt;SEP&gt;QIIME is a bioinformatics tool used for analyzing high-throughput community sequencing data, including studies on the gut microbiome of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;QIIME is a bioinformatics tool used for analyzing high-throughput microbial community sequencing data, relevant to studies on biodegradation and gut microbiomes.&lt;SEP&gt;QIIME is a bioinformatics pipeline used for analyzing microbial community data, including alpha diversity, beta diversity, and differential abundance analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;QIIME is a bioinformatics tool used for analyzing microbial community data, including alpha diversity and taxonomic composition, in the gut of mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLASH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FLASH is a bioinformatics tool used for fast length adjustment of short reads to improve genome assemblies, relevant in studies on microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRAFAST CLUSTERING ALGORITHMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Ultrafast clustering algorithms are used in metagenomic sequence analysis, including studies on the gut microbiota of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PACIFIC WHITE SHRIMP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Litopenaeus vannamei, commonly known as Pacific white shrimp, is studied for bacterial community dynamics during nursery rearing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL COMMUNITY DYNAMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacterial community dynamics refer to the changes in bacterial populations, often studied in organisms like Pacific white shrimp during rearing.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacterial community dynamics refer to the study of changes in bacterial populations, particularly in organisms like Pacific white shrimp during rearing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">IME (Integrated Microbial Ecology) is a method used for analyzing high-throughput community sequencing data in microbial ecology studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NON-SYMBIOTIC ENDOPHYTIC FUNGI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Non-symbiotic endophytic fungi are fungi that colonize plants like timothy grass and perennial ryegrass without forming symbiotic relationships, studied for their ecological roles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIMOTHY GRASS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Timothy grass is a forage grass species studied for its colonization by non-symbiotic endophytic fungi.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PERENNIAL RYEGRASS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Perennial ryegrass is another forage grass species studied alongside timothy grass for its colonization by non-symbiotic endophytic fungi.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LITOPENAEUS VANNAMEI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Litopenaeus vannamei, commonly known as Pacific white shrimp, is a species studied for bacterial community dynamics during its nursery rearing phase.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial biodegradation involves the use of microorganisms to break down pollutants, such as polystyrene, into simpler compounds like CO2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM-BASED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum-based plastics are synthetic materials derived from fossil fuels that contribute to environmental pollution due to their slow degradation rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Petroleum-based plastics are synthetic polymers derived from petroleum, widely used in various industries and contributing significantly to global plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Petroleum-based plastics, including polystyrene, are major environmental pollutants that contribute to global plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Petroleum-based plastics, including polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene, are widely used but contribute significantly to environmental pollution due to their non-biodegradable nature.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL CULTURES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial cultures are communities of microorganisms used in research to study the biodegradation of plastics, including polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial cultures are microorganisms that could potentially be used to degrade plastics in composting or digestion installations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACTIVATED SLUDGE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Activated sludge is a microbial community used in wastewater treatment and has been studied for its potential to biodegrade plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Activated sludge is a mixed microbial community used in wastewater treatment and has been studied for its potential role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil microbes are microorganisms found in soil that have been investigated for their ability to degrade plastics, including polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil microbes, including bacteria and fungi, play a significant role in the biodegradation of polystyrene foam and other plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANURE MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Manure microbes are microorganisms present in animal manure that have been studied for their potential role in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermoplastics are a category of plastics, including polystyrene, that can be melted and reshaped, contributing significantly to plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermoplastics are a type of plastic that can be molded and remolded when heated, widely used in various industries and contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL CONTAMINATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil contamination by plastic wastes causes severe environmental pollution, leading to the decline of crop production and the release of hazardous chemicals into the soil.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER CONTAMINATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Water contamination by plastic wastes harms marine life, deteriorates the fishing industry, and damages aquatic ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AIR POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Air pollution from the incineration of plastic wastes releases harmful pollutants such as VOCs, heavy metals, PAHs, and dioxins, posing risks to human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VOLATILE ORGANIC COMPOUNDS (VOCS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Volatile organic compounds (VOCs) are harmful air pollutants released during the incineration of plastic wastes, contributing to air pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYCYCLIC AROMATIC HYDROCARBONS (PAHS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) are a group of environmental pollutants that can be degraded by certain bacterial strains like Achromobacter sp. HZ01 and Achromobacter sp. LH-1.&lt;SEP&gt;Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) are carcinogenic pollutants released during the incineration of plastic wastes, contributing to air pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYCHLORINATED DIBENZO-FURANS (PCDFS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polychlorinated dibenzo-furans (PCDFs) are toxic pollutants released during the incineration of plastic wastes, posing serious health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITRO-PAHS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitro-PAHs are carcinogenic substances released as airborne pollutants during the incineration of plastic wastes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTES MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic wastes management involves strategies for reducing, reusing, recycling, and developing clean-up technologies to mitigate environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRE-CONSUMER PLASTIC WASTES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Pre-consumer plastic wastes are discarded plastics generated during production and fabrication processes, which can be recycled and reused.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POST-CONSUMER PLASTIC WASTES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Post-consumer plastic wastes are discarded plastics from consumer use, contributing significantly to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRUDED POLYSTYRENE (XPS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is a denser and stronger form of polystyrene foam compared to EPS, used in food trays and insulation, and is investigated for its biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is a denser form of Styrofoam with higher compressive strength, used in this study to compare its effects on mealworms with EPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Extruded polystyrene (XPS) is a type of polystyrene used in various applications, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Extruded polystyrene (XPS) is a type of polystyrene used in containers like coffee cups and food trays, contributing to plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Extruded polystyrene foam is another form of polystyrene used in construction and packaging, also contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is another type of plastic used in the study to evaluate its effects on larval growth and nutritional content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS FLUORESCENTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas fluorescents is a bacterial strain capable of growing on styrene as a sole carbon source, indicating its role in styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XANTHOBACTER SP. STRAIN 124X">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Xanthobacter sp. strain 124X is a styrene-degrading bacterium isolated from sewage cultures, contributing to the understanding of styrene biodegradation pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROHACTER CLOACAE DG-6">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterohacter cloacae DG-6 is a bacterial strain that can convert styrene to CO2 and other compounds under anaerobic conditions, demonstrating its role in styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRUDED PS (XPS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Extruded PS (XPS) is used in containers such as coffee cups and food trays, adding to plastic waste and environmental concerns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene monooxygenase is an enzyme identified as the first step in the styrene degradation pathway, regulated by styS and styR genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monooxygenase is an enzyme involved in the bacterial degradation of styrene, catalyzing the conversion of styrene to styrene oxide.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monooxygenase is an enzyme that oxidizes styrene to styrene oxide, playing a role in the metabolic pathways of styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PAENIBACILLUS URINALIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Paenibacillus urinalis is a bacterial strain isolated from buried polystyrene film in soil, capable of using polystyrene as a sole carbon source.&lt;SEP&gt;Paenibacillus urinalis is a bacterial strain reported to have potential in the biodegradation of polystyrene, as studied in environmental research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXED MICROBIAL COMMUNITIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixed microbial communities, including those from soil, sewage, and decaying plastics, have been investigated for their ability to degrade polystyrene, albeit at slow rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL INVERTEBRATES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil invertebrates have been investigated for their role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, contributing to the breakdown of plastic waste in natural environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil invertebrates, such as earthworms, isopods, millipedes, slugs, and snails, have been tested for their ability to degrade polystyrene, with no significant results.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTODEGRADED POLYSTYRENE-5% VINYL KETONE COPOLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Photodegraded polystyrene-5% vinyl ketone copolymers are laboratory-synthesized polymers studied for their biodegradation potential after UV pretreatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="14C-LABELED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">14C-labeled polystyrene is used as a tracer in biodegradation studies to track the breakdown and mineralization of polystyrene by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;14C-labeled polystyrene is used as a tracer in research to study the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene into CO2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOCOCCUS RUBER C208">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodococcus ruber C208 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene, resulting in a 0.5% to 0.8% weight reduction in polystyrene flakes.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber C208 is a biofilm-producing strain capable of degrading polystyrene, as evidenced by weight reduction in PS flakes during incubation.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber C208 is a bacterium capable of degrading UV-pretreated LDPE films.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber C208 is a bacterial strain previously reported to degrade PS films by 0.8% over 56 days.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP. NB6">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. NB6 is a bacterial strain isolated from buried polystyrene film in soil, capable of colonizing and using polystyrene as a sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA NB26">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas aeruginosa NB26 is a bacterial strain isolated from buried polystyrene film in soil, capable of colonizing and using polystyrene as a sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AZOTOBACTER BEIJERINCKII HM21">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Azotobacter beijerinckii HM21 is a lignin decolorizing bacterium that has shown the ability to degrade polystyrene using hydroquinone peroxidase.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROQUINONE PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroquinone Peroxidase is an enzyme produced by Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 that is capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroquinone peroxidase is an enzyme produced by Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 that degrades polystyrene, as studied by Nakamiya et al. (1997).&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroquinone peroxidase is an enzyme produced by Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 that degrades polystyrene, contributing to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroquinone peroxidase is an enzyme produced by Azotobacter beijerinckii HM21 that can degrade polystyrene in a dichloromethane-water system.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroquinone peroxidase is an enzyme secreted by Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121, capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNIN DECOLORIZING BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignin decolorizing bacteria, such as Azotobacter beijerinckii HM21, are microorganisms capable of degrading complex polymers like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERMEDIATE COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Intermediate compounds, such as 1-phenyl-1,2-ethandiol and 2-phenylethanol, are byproducts detected during the biodegradation of polystyrene by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPLC">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) is a method used to detect intermediate compounds formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL MICROBIAL CONSORTIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil microbial consortia are communities of microorganisms in soil that can degrade polystyrene, albeit at low rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil microbial consortia are communities of microorganisms in soil that have been studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene, albeit at limited rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM-BASED PLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum-based plastics are synthetic polymers derived from petroleum, widely used in manufacturing but resistant to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Petroleum-based plastics, such as polystyrene, are non-biodegradable materials that contribute significantly to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-STATE $^{13}$C CROSS-POLARIZATION/MAGIC ANGLE SPINNING NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE ($^{13}$C-CP/MS NMR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Solid-state $^{13}$C cross-polarization/magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance ($^{13}$C-CP/MS NMR) is a technique used to analyze the chemical structure of polymers, such as polystyrene, during degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIQUID-STATE $^{1}$H NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE ($^{1}$H-NMR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Liquid-state $^{1}$H nuclear magnetic resonance ($^{1}$H-NMR) is a method used to study the chemical changes in polymers, such as polystyrene, during depolymerization and oxidation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRACELLULAR ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Extracellular enzymes are produced by gut microbiota to break down polystyrene into smaller, digestible molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Extracellular enzymes are proteins that may play a role in the depolymerization of plastic polymers, such as polystyrene, and are a focus of future biochemical research.&lt;SEP&gt;Extracellular enzymes are secreted by gut microbiota in mealworms and play a key role in catalyzing the depolymerization of polystyrene into smaller molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Extracellular enzymes produced by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae are involved in breaking down polyurethane bonds during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Extracellular enzymes produced by gut fungi and bacteria are crucial for the biodegradation of complex plastics like polyurethane (PU).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-DEPOLYMERIZING ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-depolymerizing enzymes are proteins that could be produced for large-scale use in breaking down plastic polymers, such as polystyrene, as part of future biotechnological solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymers are large molecules composed of repeating structural units, commonly used in the production of plastics, and can have environmental and health hazards based on their chemical composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine pollution refers to the contamination of oceans by substances such as plastic waste, which negatively impacts marine life and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RENEWABLE ENERGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Renewable energy research focuses on sustainable energy sources and their potential to reduce environmental pollution and dependence on fossil fuels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL HAZARD RANKING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental hazard ranking assesses the potential risks of plastic polymers based on their chemical composition and environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL MIGRATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemical migration refers to the transfer of chemicals from plastics into the environment or food, posing risks to ecosystems and human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE DEBRIS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine debris includes plastic waste and other materials that accumulate in marine environments, harming marine life and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine debris includes various types of waste materials, particularly plastics, that accumulate in marine environments and pose threats to marine life and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine debris refers to waste materials, including plastics, found in coastal seawaters and beaches, posing threats to marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC INGESTION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic ingestion studies examine the effects of microplastics on organisms, particularly marine species, and their potential to enter the food chain.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradable polymers are designed to break down more easily in the environment, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable polymers are materials designed to break down naturally, reducing their environmental impact compared to traditional plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable polymers are synthetic or natural polymers that can be broken down by microorganisms, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable polymers are materials that can be broken down by microorganisms, contributing to environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable polymers are materials that can be broken down by microorganisms, reducing environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable polymers are plastics designed to break down more easily in the environment, studied for their potential to reduce plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable polymers are studied for their environmental impact and potential to replace conventional plastics, with a focus on their degradation rates and ecological effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable polymers are materials designed to break down more easily in the environment, potentially reducing plastic pollution and its impact on ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradable polymers are materials designed to break down more easily in the environment, reducing long-term pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic production research examines the global trends, environmental impacts, and sustainability of plastic manufacturing processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XANTHOBACTER STRAIN 124X">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Xanthobacter strain 124X is a bacterial strain that metabolizes styrene oxide and 2-phenylethanol, contributing to styrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Xanthobacter strain 124X is a bacterium capable of degrading styrene oxide and 2-phenylethanol, contributing to the biodegradation of styrene-related compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS FLUORESCENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas fluorescens is a bacterial strain capable of catabolizing styrene, a component of polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas fluorescens is a bacterial strain known for its ability to catabolize styrene, playing a role in the biodegradation of plastic-related compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas fluorescens is a bacterium capable of biodegrading polyethylene through complete solubilization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTALLY DEGRADABLE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmentally degradable polymers are materials designed to break down under environmental conditions, reducing their impact as pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-PHENYLETHANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Phenylethanol is a compound produced during the microbial degradation of styrene, often studied in biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;2-Phenylethanol is an intermediate in the anaerobic biodegradation pathways of polystyrene and styrene, produced by enzymes such as peroxidases and hydrolases.&lt;SEP&gt;2-phenylethanol is a secondary product of polystyrene degradation produced by Enterobacter LG3 under anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;2-Phenylethanol is a metabolite produced during the degradation of styrene by microorganisms like Xanthobacter strain 124X.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AZOTOBACTER BEIJERINCKII HM121">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 is a bacterial strain known to produce Hydroquinone Peroxidase, an enzyme capable of degrading polystyrene in a dichloromethane-water system.&lt;SEP&gt;Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 is a bacterial strain that produces enzymes capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 is a bacterium capable of enzymatically degrading polystyrene using hydroquinone peroxidase.&lt;SEP&gt;Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 is a lignin-degrading bacterium that secretes hydroquinone peroxidase, an enzyme capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 is a bacterium that produces hydroquinone peroxidase, an enzyme capable of degrading polystyrene, as studied by Nakamiya et al. (1997).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS 305-STR-1-4">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas 305-STR-1-4 is a bacterial strain that produces β-phenethyl alcohol from styrene, contributing to styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL CONSORTIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial consortia are communities of microorganisms that can degrade complex materials, such as polystyrene, in environments like soil or sewage sludge.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that work together to degrade complex materials, such as plastics, in various environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that work together to degrade complex substances like plastics in various environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that work together to degrade complex substances, including synthetic polymers like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that work together to degrade complex substances, such as polystyrene, in environments like soil and sewage sludge.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that work together to degrade plastics more efficiently than single bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia, such as those found in soil or sewage sludge, have been studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) at low rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that work together to degrade complex materials, such as plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that can degrade plastics, including polystyrene, in various environments such as soil and sewage sludge.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia refer to communities of microorganisms that work together to degrade complex substances, such as plastics, in various environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are communities of microorganisms that work together to degrade complex substances like plastics, including polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that work together to degrade complex pollutants, such as synthetic plastics, in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are communities of microorganisms that can degrade synthetic plastics in environments like soil and sewage sludge.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial consortia are groups of microorganisms that can degrade polystyrene, though at varying rates depending on the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGRICULTURAL PLASTIC FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Agricultural plastic films are used in farming but contribute to environmental pollution due to their slow degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Agricultural plastic films are widely used in farming but contribute to environmental pollution due to their slow biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTODEGRADED POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Photodegraded polymers are plastics that have been broken down by exposure to light, making them more susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodegraded polymers are plastics that have been broken down by light exposure, making them more susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodegraded polymers are synthetic polymers that have been broken down by exposure to light, studied for their biodegradation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodegraded polymers are synthetic materials that have been broken down by exposure to light, often studied for their biodegradability.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodegraded polymers are synthetic materials that have undergone degradation due to exposure to light, often studied for their biodegradability and environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,3-DIPHENYLBUTANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,3-Diphenylbutane is a chemical compound used in studies to understand microbial degradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;1,3-Diphenylbutane is a compound used in biodegradation studies to understand microbial degradation pathways of aromatic hydrocarbons.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAVIN ADENINE DINUCLEOTIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD) is a cofactor involved in the bacterial degradation of styrene, particularly in the activity of styrene monooxygenase.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENRICHMENT CULTURES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Enrichment cultures are microbial communities grown under specific conditions to study their ability to degrade substances like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enrichment cultures are used to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworm gut microbiome, providing insights into microbial activity and degradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil microorganisms play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, including polystyrene, in terrestrial environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DECAYING GARBAGE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Decaying garbage contains microbial communities that can contribute to the biodegradation of plastics such as polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Decaying garbage hosts microbial consortia that contribute to the biodegradation of plastics and other organic waste materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Decaying garbage refers to organic waste that can contain synthetic plastics and other pollutants, which are degraded by microbial consortia in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANURE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Manure contains microbial communities that can degrade plastics like polystyrene, contributing to environmental cleanup.&lt;SEP&gt;Manure contains microbial populations that can degrade plastics, including polystyrene, in agricultural and waste management settings.&lt;SEP&gt;Manure is an organic waste product that can contain microplastics and other pollutants, requiring microbial degradation for safe use in agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STANFORD UNIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stanford University is the institution where the research on mealworms and their role in polystyrene degradation was conducted.&lt;SEP&gt;Stanford University provided financial support for the research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms, emphasizing the importance of this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATIONAL NATURE SCIENCE FOUNDATION OF CHINA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The National Nature Science Foundation of China funded the research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms, supporting advancements in environmental science.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHINA POSTDOCTORAL SCIENCE FOUNDATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The China Postdoctoral Science Foundation provided financial grants to support the research on polystyrene biodegradation by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STATE KEY LABORATORY OF URBAN WATER RESOURCE AND ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The State Key Laboratory of Urban Water Resource and Environment contributed to the research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The State Key Laboratory of Urban Water Resource and Environment focuses on water resource management and environmental studies, including the impact of microplastics on aquatic systems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Recycling is a conventional method to tackle plastic pollution, but it is no longer sufficient due to the massive production and accumulation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Recycling refers to the process of collecting and reprocessing plastic waste to reduce environmental pollution and promote sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Recycling is a method of managing plastic waste, but only a small percentage of polystyrene is recycled, leading to environmental challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY RECOVERY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy recovery is another conventional method to address plastic pollution, but it is insufficient to handle the scale of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Energy recovery refers to the process of converting plastic waste into energy, reducing the amount of waste sent to landfills.&lt;SEP&gt;Energy recovery refers to the incineration of residues from plastic degradation by larvae, with the potential to recover energy.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LANDFILLING">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Landfilling refers to the disposal of plastic waste in landfills, contributing to environmental pollution and the accumulation of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON DIOXIDE (CO2)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a byproduct of polystyrene degradation by mealworms, indicating mineralization of the plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a byproduct of polystyrene mineralization during biodegradation by mealworms and their gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a byproduct of the mineralization of polystyrene during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of polystyrene by superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide is a byproduct of the biodegradation of Styrofoam by superworms, indicating mineralization of the plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide is a byproduct of the biodegradation of LDPE by mealworms, measured during carbon mass balance analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER (H2O)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Water (H2O) is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW MOLECULAR INTERMEDIATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low molecular intermediates are compounds formed during the biodegradation of plastics, such as polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS foam, a type of expanded polystyrene, is used as a feed for mealworms, showing non-lethal effects but impacting their development and reproduction.&lt;SEP&gt;EPS foam, or expanded polystyrene foam, is a common environmental pollutant derived from polystyrene, often used in packaging and insulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) foam is a type of plastic waste that has been tested for biodegradation, showing dependency on gut microbes for depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene foam (EPS foam) is a type of PS foam with higher permeability and recycled content, used in studies to assess its impact on mealworm productivity.&lt;SEP&gt;EPS foam, or expanded polystyrene foam, is a common construction and packaging material that contributes to environmental pollution due to its persistence and slow degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil is a natural environmental medium where biodegradation processes, such as the breakdown of polystyrene-graft-starch copolymers, occur.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil is one of the environmental sources used to test the biodegradation of plastics like PE and PS, harboring diverse microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEAWATER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Seawater is another environmental source used to study the biodegradation of plastics, containing microbial communities that attempt to degrade polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEDIMENTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Sediments are environmental sources that harbor microbial communities capable of degrading plastics like PE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SLUDGE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Sludge is a microbial-rich environmental source used in studies to assess the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMPOST">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Compost is an environmental source containing microbial communities that attempt to biodegrade plastics like PE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXED MICROBIAL CULTURES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixed microbial cultures from various sources are used to study the biodegradation of plastics, showing limited efficiency in degrading PE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOLATED BACTERIAL STRAINS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Isolated bacterial strains from environmental sources are reported to have limited capabilities in degrading plastics like PE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTESTINAL FLORA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Intestinal flora in Tenebrio molitor larvae shifts under different dietary conditions, influencing the biodegradation of EPS and LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Intestinal flora in yellow mealworms is influenced by the type of plastic consumed, affecting their digestion and nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2,4-TRICHLOROBENZENE (1,2,4-TCB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (1,2,4-TCB) is a chemical used in analytical methods for studying the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (1,2,4-TCB) is a solvent used for extracting and analyzing low-density polyethylene (LDPE) polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRYPTICASE SOY AGAR (TSA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trypticase Soy Agar (TSA) is a growth medium used in microbiological studies, potentially relevant to the cultivation of gut bacteria in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Trypticase soy agar (TSA) is a growth medium used in studies involving microbial cultures and biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC SUPPRESSION TESTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic suppression tests are conducted to study the effects of gut bacteria suppression on the biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression tests are conducted to study the role of gut microbiota in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP) by T. molitor and Z. atratus.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression tests were conducted to determine the role of gut microbiota in the depolymerization of polypropylene by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">THF extraction is a method used to determine the mass of PS residue in frass (excreted material) during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;THF extraction is a method used to prepare polystyrene samples for analysis in gel permeation chromatography.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DICHLOROMETHANE EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Dichloromethane extraction is a method used to prepare low-density polyethylene samples for analysis in high-temperature gel permeation chromatography.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATR METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The ATR method is used in Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy to analyze the functional groups of residual polymers in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANNIBALIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cannibalization refers to the behavior of Tenebrio molitor larvae consuming weaker larvae when food sources are limited, particularly when fed only EPS or LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PE-1 is a control sample of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam used in the study to compare with frass samples from LDPE-fed larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-1 is a sample of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) used in experiments to study its depolymerization and biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-1 is a type of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to plastic biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-1 is a type of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing significant depolymerization patterns during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-1 is a specific type of LDPE foam used in the study to assess the biodegradation capabilities of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-1 is a type of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam used in the study to assess the biodegradation capabilities of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-1 is a type of polyethylene feedstock used in studies to assess the biodegradation capabilities of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PE-2 is another control sample of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam used in the study for comparison with frass samples from LDPE-fed larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-2 is another sample of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) used in experiments to study its depolymerization and biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-2 is another type of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, exhibiting different depolymerization patterns compared to PE-1.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-2 is another type of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam used in biodegradation tests with Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing similar consumption patterns to PE-1.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-2 is another type of LDPE foam used in the study, showing different molecular weight reductions after digestion by larvae compared to PE-1.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-2 is another type of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam used in the study to evaluate the biodegradation efficiency of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-2 is another type of polyethylene feedstock used in biodegradation studies involving Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OPERATIONAL TAXONOMIC UNITS (OTU)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are used to classify and analyze microbial communities in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, identifying key bacteria involved in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SILVA 16S RRNA DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Silva 16S rRNA database is a reference database used to classify and identify microbial taxa based on 16S rRNA gene sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;The SILVA 16S rRNA database is a reference database used to classify and analyze microbial sequences from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The Silva 16S rRNA database is used in the study to classify and analyze the taxonomy of 16S rRNA gene sequences.&lt;SEP&gt;The SILVA 16S rRNA database is a reference database used to classify and analyze microbial sequences obtained from the gut of mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANNIBAL RATE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cannibal rate refers to the percentage of Tenebrio molitor larvae that resort to cannibalization when food sources like EPS or LDPE are insufficient for their growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M&lt;SUB&gt;N&lt;/SUB&gt;">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">M&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt; (number-average molecular weight) is a parameter used in GPC analysis to measure the lowest molecular weight portion of polymers, indicating depolymerization during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M&lt;SUB&gt;W&lt;/SUB&gt;">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">M&lt;sub&gt;w&lt;/sub&gt; (weight-average molecular weight) is a parameter used in GPC analysis to measure the average molecular weight of polymers, showing changes during depolymerization by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M&lt;SUB&gt;Z&lt;/SUB&gt;">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">M&lt;sub&gt;z&lt;/sub&gt; (z-average molecular weight) is a parameter used in GPC analysis to measure the highest molecular weight portion of polymers, indicating changes during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPATION RATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pupation rates are used as an indicator of the health and development of superworms and yellow mealworms, with lower rates observed in plastic-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupation rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae were studied under different dietary conditions, providing insights into their development when fed plastics like PS and PE-1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANNIBAL RATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cannibal rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae were observed under different dietary conditions, indicating behavioral changes when fed plastics like PS and PE-1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNIFRAC">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">UniFrac is a metric used in microbial community analysis to compare the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed different diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO GENUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The Tenebrio genus includes species like Tenebrio molitor, which are known for their ability to biodegrade plastics such as polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. is a Gram-positive bacterium isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of degrading polystyrene and forming grooves and holes on PS sheets.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from mangrove sediment, capable of degrading polypropylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a genus of bacteria found in the gut of larvae like Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor, potentially involved in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a genus of bacteria known for its ability to transform styrene to phenylacetate, contributing to the degradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a genus of bacteria identified as a key microorganism in the degradation of polypropylene (PP) and lignocellulosic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a gut bacterium associated with the degradation of low molecular weight polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, enriched in PP-L-fed larvae and potentially involved in polypropylene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a plastic-degrading bacterium identified in the guts of insects like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial genus studied for its role in the biodegradation of plastics in the gut microbiome of Galleria mellonella.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a genus of bacteria indigenous to the Niger Delta mangrove swamp, studied for its polyethylene biodegradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial strain involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene, particularly when pre-treated with UV light.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from mangrove sediment, known for its ability to degrade polypropylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from mangrove sediment, known for its ability to biodegrade polypropylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial strain that, in co-culture with Acinetobacter sp., can biodegrade polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that has been shown to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MORGANELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Morganella is a bacterial genus present in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, possibly contributing to the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is a bacterial genus that showed increased relative abundance in the gut microbial community of yellow mealworms after feeding on disposable surgical masks.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is a genus of bacteria found in the gut of yellow mealworms, showing a significant increase in abundance when fed the inner layer of surgical masks.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is a genus of bacteria found in the gut of yellow mealworms, showing increased abundance when fed the inner layer of surgical masks, suggesting a role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is a genus of gut microbes whose abundance decreases in larvae fed with PS and LDPE, suggesting a potential role in plastic digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is a genus of bacteria that may play a role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers like polypropylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COPROCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Coprococcus is a genus of anaerobic gut microbes whose abundance increases significantly in larvae fed with polystyrene (PS), potentially playing a role in plastic digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIMITED EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Limited extent depolymerization is a pattern where Mn or both Mw and Mn increase during biodegradation, observed in LDPE biodegradation by T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Limited extent depolymerization is a process where the molecular weight (Mw) of a polymer increases while the number-average molecular weight (Mn) decreases, observed during the biodegradation of certain plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROAD DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Broad depolymerization is a pattern observed during biodegradation, where both Mw and Mn decrease simultaneously, as seen in PS biodegradation by Tenebrio genus.&lt;SEP&gt;Broad depolymerization is a process where both the molecular weight (Mw) and number-average molecular weight (Mn) of a polymer decrease, observed during the biodegradation of plastics like LDPE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CROSSLINKING REACTIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crosslinking reactions are chemical processes that can occur during depolymerization or biodegradation, potentially leading to the formation of longer chain polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Crosslinking reactions are chemical processes that can occur during the depolymerization or biodegradation of polymers, affecting their molecular structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Crosslinking reactions are chemical processes that occur during depolymerization, potentially causing an increase in the molecular weight (Mw) of polymers like polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dyella is a genus of bacteria significantly associated with the microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed polystyrene (PS), potentially involved in the degradation of PS intermediates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KOSAKONIA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kosakonia sp. is a bacterial genus linked to the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosakonia sp. is a bacterial genus strongly associated with the degradation of both polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosakonia sp. is a bacterium that is more abundant in plastic-only diets and is associated with the degradation of polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosakonia sp. is a gut bacterium associated with plastic diets, potentially involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosakonia sp. is a gut bacterium strongly associated with the biodegradation of both polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosakonia sp. is a bacterial strain significantly related to both polystyrene and polyethylene diets in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosakonia sp. is a genus of bacteria within the Enterobacteriaceae family, associated with the degradation of polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) by T. molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYELLA GINSENGISOLI STRAIN IA-4">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dyella ginsengisoli strain IA-4 is a bacterial strain isolated from activated sludge, capable of degrading biphenyl and potentially involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) intermediates in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignin is a biodegradable polymer that has been used in grafting strategies to improve the biodegradability of polypropylene materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignin is a complex natural polymer found in plant cell walls, sharing structural similarities with synthetic plastics like polystyrene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignin is a complex organic polymer found in plant cell walls, and its degradation is studied in relation to plastic degradation pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignin is a complex organic polymer found in plant cell walls, composed of aromatic rings and carbon chains, and shares degradation pathways with polystyrene due to structural similarities.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignin is a complex organic polymer found in plant cell walls, which Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade, potentially contributing to their ability to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignin is a complex organic polymer found in plant cell walls, which can be degraded by enzymes like laccase for high-value chemical production.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignin is a natural polymer found in corn straw that shares degradation pathways with polystyrene in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignin is a complex organic polymer that is co-lysed with plastic polymers, as evaluated using TG-FTIR and py-GC/MS techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignin is a complex organic polymer found in plant cell walls, and T. molitor larvae have been shown to degrade lignin materials in rice straw, corn straw, and wheat straw.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMICELLULOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hemicellulose is a component of plant cell walls that can be decomposed by anaerobic bacteria in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemicellulose is a polysaccharide found in plant cell walls, which Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade, potentially contributing to their ability to degrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL FUNGI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil fungi are microorganisms that have been studied for their ability to degrade synthetic polymers like polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LUMBRICUS TERRESTRIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Earthworms (Lumbricus terrestris) are capable of degrading polyethylene particles, contributing to plastic biodegradation in soil environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbricus terrestris, commonly known as the earthworm, can incorporate microplastics from litter into its burrows, affecting soil ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbricus terrestris, or earthworms, have gut bacteria that can degrade low-density polyethylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbricus terrestris, commonly known as the earthworm, is affected by microplastics in terrestrial ecosystems, as studied in environmental research.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbricus terrestris, or earthworms, are affected by microplastics in terrestrial ecosystems, as studied in environmental research.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbricus terrestris is an earthworm species that fragments and depolymerizes biodegradable microplastics such as polylactic acid (PLA) and poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) into smaller microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZOPHOBAS ATINUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zophobas atinus, also known as superworms, are larvae capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene and polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atinus, also known as the superworm, is a species of darkling beetle larvae that can biodegrade low-density polyethylene and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTO DEGRADED POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Photo-degraded polymers are synthetic materials that break down under the influence of light, often studied for their biodegradability in environmental contexts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYELLA GINSENGISOLI LA-4">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dyella ginsengisoli LA-4 is a bacterial strain isolated for its ability to degrade biphenyl, a persistent organic pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHANEROCHAETE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Phanerochaete is a genus of fungi capable of degrading degradable plastic polyethylene, contributing to biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Phanerochaete is a genus of fungi known for its ability to degrade plastics, including polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Phanerochaete is a genus of fungi known for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene and other plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STREPTOMYCES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Streptomyces is a genus of bacteria capable of biodegrading polyethylene and other degradable plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptomyces is a genus of bacteria capable of degrading synthetic polymers, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESEARCH STUDY ON PLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This research study investigates the inputs of plastic waste from land into the ocean, highlighting its environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESEARCH STUDY ON BIODEGRADABILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This research study explores the biodegradability of photo-degraded polymers and develops experimental procedures for assessment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESEARCH STUDY ON POLYSTYRENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This research study examines the biodegradation of polystyrene by microorganisms and insects, offering potential solutions to plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MONITOR">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio monitor, commonly known as mealworms, are larvae that can biodegrade cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene waste, altering their gut microbiome and enzymatic profile.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALORIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Valorization refers to the process of converting plastic wastes into valuable products through microbial degradation and other methods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NORMAL DIET (BRAN)">
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
      <data key="d1">Spiroplasma is more abundant in larvae fed with a normal diet (bran) compared to those fed with PS and LDPE, indicating its role in non-plastic digestion.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITROBACTER FREUNDII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrobacter freundii is a bacterium known for degrading long-chain polymers, linear hydrocarbons, and cyclic hydrocarbons, and is prevalent in the gut microbiome of PS- and PE-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter freundii is a gut microorganism identified in Tenebrio molitor that is associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter freundii is a gut microorganism identified as being associated with PS biodegradation in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA AEROGENES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella aerogenes is a bacterial strain linked to hydrocarbon degradation and bioremediation, identified in the gut microbiome of PS-degrading mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella aerogenes is a gut microorganism in Tenebrio molitor that is involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella aerogenes is a bacterial strain that plays a role in the decomposition of polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella aerogenes is a gut microorganism identified as playing a role in PS biodegradation in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMULSIFYING FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Emulsifying factors (30–100 kDa) secreted by Tenebrio molitor mediate the bioavailability of plastics, enhancing their biodegradation by the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Emulsifying factors are secreted by mealworms in their gut, assisting gut microbes in facilitating plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Emulsifying factors are substances secreted by mealworms that assist gut microbes in breaking down plastics during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESPIRATION ENHANCEMENT FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Respiration enhancement factors (&lt;30 kDa) secreted by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor enhance the respiration and degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOME ENRICHMENT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbiome enrichment refers to the cultivation of gut microorganisms from Tenebrio molitor to enhance plastic biodegradation rates outside the insect gut microenvironment.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiome enrichment refers to the enhancement of microbial communities in mealworms that facilitate the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROLYZABLE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrolyzable plastics, such as polyethylene terephthalate, are susceptible to higher rates of microbial degradation compared to non-hydrolyzable plastics like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM-DERIVED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum-derived plastics, including polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene, are highly resistant to biodegradation due to their high molecular weight and hydrophobic nature.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SALIVARY GLANDS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The salivary glands of insects, such as Tenebrio molitor, secrete proteins that play a role in the degradation of plastics, highlighting the insect host's contribution to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED PS FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded PS foam is a type of polystyrene foam used as a feed for mealworms in the study to assess its biodegradation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded PS foam, also known as Styrofoam, is a common environmental pollutant used as a feedstock for mealworm larvae in biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded PS foam is a type of polystyrene foam used in the study to assess its biodegradation by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERNATANT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Supernatant is the liquid fraction derived from the gut microbiome of mealworms, containing factors that enhance the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Supernatant is the liquid phase collected from mealworm gut samples, containing secreted factors from the mealworm and its gut bacteria, used in experiments to study plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Supernatant is the liquid portion of a sample collected from PS-fed mealworms, used in experiments to study the role of gut bacteria in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Supernatant refers to the liquid portion of a sample, which in this context is derived from the mealworm gut and used to study microbial respiration and PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT SPIN COLUMNS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight spin columns are used to fractionate supernatant samples based on molecular weight, aiding in the study of secreted factors involved in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight spin columns are used to fractionate the supernatant into different molecular weight ranges, aiding in the analysis of secreted factors involved in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAINBOW MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rainbow Mealworms is the supplier of mealworms used in the study, sourced for their ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXOTIC NUTRITION">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Exotic Nutrition is the supplier of natural wheat bran used as a control diet for mealworms in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHI MANUAL COMPRESSION PRESS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PHI Manual Compression Press is a device used to heat-press PS microplastics into uniform thin films for use in microbial cultures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARLISLE CONSTRUCTION MATERIALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carlisle Construction Materials is the source of the expanded polystyrene insulation material used in the study, which was cut into cubes for experimentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carlisle Construction Materials is the supplier of expanded PS foam used as a feed for mealworms in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JINSHUOWANG PLASTIC MATERIALS CO., LTD">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Jinshuowang Plastic Materials Co., Ltd is the supplier of PS microplastics used in microbial cultures for the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FALCON">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Falcon is the brand of nylon cell strainers used to filter gut bacteria samples during the collection process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMICON ULTRA-15 CENTRIFUGAL FILTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Amicon Ultra-15 Centrifugal Filters are used to fractionate the supernatant into different molecular weight ranges for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUSHNELL HAAS (BH) MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bushnell Haas (BH) media is a carbon-free medium used to culture and enrich PS-degrading bacteria from the mealworm gut.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-degrading bacteria are microorganisms isolated from the gut and frass of larvae, capable of degrading polystyrene under anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-degrading bacteria are microorganisms isolated from the mealworm gut that are capable of breaking down polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENRICHMENT CULTURE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An enrichment culture is a method used to isolate and study PS-degrading bacteria from the mealworm gut in a controlled environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Enrichment culture is a method used to isolate and cultivate specific microorganisms, such as PS-degrading bacteria, from gut and frass samples of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Enrichment culture is a method used to increase the population of polystyrene-degrading bacteria from the intestines of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO2 PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CO2 production is a measurable outcome of microbial respiration during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating the extent of mineralization.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 production is a measure of the mineralization of polystyrene, indicating the extent of biodegradation by PS-degrading bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 production is a measure of the mineralization of polystyrene, indicating the extent of biodegradation by the gut microbiome of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON-FREE BUSHNELL HAAS (BH) MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon-free Bushnell Haas (BH) media is used to culture and enrich PS-degrading bacteria from the mealworm gut in a controlled environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS FILM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS film is a form of polystyrene used in experiments to study the biodegradation capabilities of mealworm gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;PS film refers to polystyrene film used as a carbon source in biodegradation experiments to study microbial activity and degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PS film refers to polystyrene film sheets used in biodegradation assays to study the degradation process by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;PS film is a form of polystyrene, a petroleum-based plastic, studied for its biodegradation by microorganisms in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) film is a thin layer of polystyrene used in experiments to study its biodegradation by mealworms and their gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SERUM BOTTLE ASSAYS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Serum bottle assays are used to measure CO2 production, indicating the extent of polystyrene mineralization by PS-degrading bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRYPTIC SOY MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tryptic soy media is used to grow bacteria isolated from the mealworm gut for further study of their PS-degrading capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOG MT2 MICROPLATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biolog MT2 Microplates are used to screen microbial activity, providing insights into the metabolic capabilities of PS-degrading bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Biolog MT2 microplates are used to screen the metabolic activity of bacteria and the mealworm gut microbiome in degrading polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM GUT MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut microbiome of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) has been studied for its role in biodegrading polystyrene and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) plays a crucial role in the degradation of polyurethane, involving various bacterial species such as Lactococcus, Paraclostridium, Chryseobacterium, Kosakonia, and Pseudomonas.&lt;SEP&gt;The mealworm gut microbiome consists of bacteria that play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) as a sole carbon source.&lt;SEP&gt;The mealworm gut microbiome consists of microorganisms that play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, enhancing the breakdown process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRAZOLIUM VIOLET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tetrazolium violet is a redox dye used as a colorimetric indicator of carbon oxidation (respiration) by bacteria in Biolog MT2 microplates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENEIOUS PRIME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Geneious Prime is a software used for de novo assembly and sequence analysis, including the identification of bacterial strains via BLAST searches.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCBI 16S RRNA DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The NCBI 16S rRNA database is used for identifying bacterial strains based on sequence similarity, with a threshold of &gt;97%.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNERGY HTX MULTI-MODE PLATE READER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Synergy HTX Multi-Mode Plate Reader is used for colorimetric measurements in microbial activity assays, such as those involving polystyrene (PS) degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GOW-MAC GAS CHROMATOGRAPH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The GOW-MAC gas chromatograph is used to measure CO2 production in microbial degradation experiments, indicating mineralization of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTACT ANGLE GONIOMETER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A contact angle goniometer is used to measure the hydrophobicity of supernatants and polystyrene (PS) films after microbial treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRISM SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Prism software is used for statistical analysis of experimental data, including respiration activity and contact angle measurements.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESPIRATION-ENHANCING FACTOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A respiration-enhancing factor is a substance secreted by the gut microbiome of mealworms that increases the ability of the microbiome to respire and degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON-FREE MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon-free media is a growth medium used to culture bacteria capable of degrading polystyrene, supplemented with PS gut supernatant.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon-free media is a nutrient solution used in biodegradation experiments to ensure that the sole carbon source is the plastic being tested.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon-free media is a nutrient solution used in experiments to culture the gut microbiome of mealworms without introducing external carbon sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon-free media is used in experiments to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworm gut microbiome, with PS microplastics as the sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SDS-PAGE GEL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SDS-PAGE gel analysis is a technique used to analyze proteins and other molecules in the supernatant of the gut microbiome of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTACT ANGLE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Contact angle analysis is a method used to measure the hydrophobicity of supernatant fractions, revealing their emulsifying activity on hydrophobic surfaces like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Contact angle analysis is a method used to measure the hydrophobicity of the supernatant fractions derived from the gut microbiome of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Contact angle analysis measures the hydrophobicity of polystyrene films before and after microbial degradation, indicating changes in surface properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMULSIFYING AGENTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Emulsifying agents are substances that may be present in the supernatant of the gut microbiome and could promote the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SERUM BOTTLES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Serum bottles are containers used in experiments to measure CO2 production during the biodegradation of polystyrene by the gut microbiome of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Serum bottles are used as containers in experiments to measure microbial respiration and CO2 production during the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Serum bottles are used in experiments to measure microbial respiration and CO2 production during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworm gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO2 CONCENTRATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CO2 concentration is a measure of the mineralization of polystyrene, indicating the extent of biodegradation by the gut microbiome of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMULSIFYING AGENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An emulsifying agent is a substance produced by mealworms that aids in the breakdown of solid hydrocarbons, such as plastics, during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;An emulsifying agent produced by mealworms increases the bioavailability of hydrophobic plastic particles, accelerating biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Emulsifying agents are substances secreted by mealworms that increase the bioavailability of polystyrene, facilitating its biodegradation by gut microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERNATANT FRACTIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Supernatant fractions are liquid extracts from the mealworm gut microbiome, containing factors that enhance microbial respiration and plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Supernatant fractions from mealworm diets, particularly the 30-100 kDa fraction, exhibit emulsifying properties that enhance the biodegradation of polystyrene by increasing its bioavailability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMULSIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Emulsification is the process by which hydrophobic substances, such as polystyrene, are made more bioavailable through the action of emulsifying agents, enhancing biodegradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TWEEN-80">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tween-80 is a known surfactant used as a control in emulsification studies, demonstrating the emulsifying properties of mealworm supernatant fractions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL ENRICHMENT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A microbial enrichment is a cultured community of bacteria selected for enhanced plastic biodegradation, derived from the mealworm gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL SECRETED FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial secreted factors are compounds produced by microorganisms in the mealworm gut that enhance the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC-TREATED SUPERNATANT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic-treated supernatant is a liquid extract from the mealworm gut microbiome treated with antibiotics to study the role of gut bacteria in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXED COMMUNITY ENRICHMENT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A mixed community enrichment is a cultured group of bacteria derived from the mealworm gut microbiome, selected for enhanced plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter asburiae is a bacterial species that has been studied for its role in biodegradation and environmental processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polyethylene, isolated from the gut microbiome of plastic-eating waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae is a plastic-degrading bacterium identified in the guts of insects like mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STENOTROPHOMONAS MALTOPHILIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Stenotrophomonas maltophilia is a bacterial species capable of cyclic hydrocarbon degradation, with enhanced biodegradation rates in the presence of surfactants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOSURFACTANTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biosurfactants are surface-active compounds produced by microorganisms that can enhance the biodegradation of pollutants.&lt;SEP&gt;Biosurfactants are surface-active substances produced by microorganisms, such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa, that enhance hydrocarbon degradation, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHAMNOLIPID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhamnolipid is a biosurfactant produced by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which accelerates hydrocarbon degradation, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROCARBON DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrocarbon degradation refers to the breakdown of hydrocarbon compounds, such as those found in plastics, by microorganisms like Citrobacter freundii and Pseudomonas aeruginosa.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbon degradation refers to the breakdown of hydrocarbons by microorganisms, often studied in the context of petroleum-contaminated environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXED MICROBIAL ENRICHMENT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixed microbial enrichment refers to a community of microorganisms that form biofilms on polystyrene films and contribute to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella sp. are bacteria closely related to Klebsiella aerogenes, found in the gut microbiome of PS-degrading mealworms and associated with hydrocarbon degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, known for its role in plastic degradation and nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STENOTROPHOMONAS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Stenotrophomonas sp. are bacteria, including Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, known for cyclic hydrocarbon degradation and enhanced biodegradation in the presence of surfactants.&lt;SEP&gt;Stenotrophomonas sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from waste dumpsites, capable of biodegrading unpretreated low-density polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Stenotrophomonas sp. is a Gram-negative bacterium isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of growing on polystyrene as a sole carbon source and forming cellular aggregates on PS sheets.&lt;SEP&gt;Stenotrophomonas sp. is a bacterium that forms tunnels, cracks, and cellular aggregates on polystyrene (PS) sheets, indicating its role in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS GUT SUPERNATANT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS gut supernatant is a liquid derived from the gut of mealworms, used to enhance the growth and respiration of bacteria on polystyrene films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OPTICAL DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Optical density is a measure used to monitor bacterial growth and respiration on polystyrene films during biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic degradation involves the breakdown of substances, such as plastics, by enzymes produced by living organisms, including insects and microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic degradation involves the use of enzymes to break down polymers, such as plastics, into smaller, environmentally benign molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic degradation refers to the breakdown of plant biomass and synthetic polymers by enzymes, which is a key process in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic degradation refers to the breakdown of substances, such as polystyrene, by enzymes produced by microorganisms like Citrobacter freundii and Pseudomonas aeruginosa.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic degradation refers to the breakdown of materials, such as polyurethanes, by specific enzymes like cholesterol esterase.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic degradation is the process by which enzymes break down polymers, with factors like crystallinity and molecular weight affecting the rate of degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic degradation involves the breakdown of synthetic polymers and plant biomasses by enzymes, offering a potential solution for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic degradation refers to the breakdown of plant biomasses and synthetic polymers by enzymes, which can be a potential solution for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic degradation involves the use of enzymes to break down complex substances, such as plastics, into simpler, less harmful compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOSURFACTANT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biosurfactants are emulsifying agents that enhance the bioavailability of hydrocarbons, including polystyrene, facilitating their biodegradation by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROCARBON XENOBIOTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrocarbon xenobiotics are organic compounds that are foreign to biological systems and can be degraded by certain bacterial strains, including those found in the mealworm gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL RESPIRATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial respiration refers to the process by which microorganisms break down organic substances, such as polystyrene, into simpler compounds, releasing energy in the form of CO2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EIGHT UNIQUE BACTERIAL SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Eight unique bacterial species were identified in the mealworm gut microbiome, many of which are associated with the biodegradation of hydrocarbons and other xenobiotics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STANFORD WOODS INSTITUTE FOR ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Stanford Woods Institute for Environment supported this research study, which investigates the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms and their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATIONAL SCIENCE FOUNDATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The National Science Foundation provided funding for part of this research study, which focuses on the biodegradation of plastics using mealworm gut-derived enrichments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STANFORD NANO SHARED FACILITIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Stanford Nano Shared Facilities supported part of this research study, providing resources for the investigation of plastic biodegradation mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL BIOTECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial Biotechnology Addressing the Plastic Waste Disaster is a study that explores the role of microorganisms in tackling plastic waste through biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NON-HYDROLYZABLE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Non-hydrolyzable plastics are a type of plastic that resists degradation and poses significant environmental challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-hydrolyzable plastics are resistant to degradation and pose significant environmental challenges, requiring innovative solutions like biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-hydrolyzable plastics are materials that resist biodegradation and are studied for their bio-oxidation potential in interdisciplinary research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) films are a type of plastic waste that can be degraded by bacterial consortia, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine microbes are microorganisms capable of degrading low- and high-density polyethylenes, contributing to plastic biodegradation in marine environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine microbes are microorganisms that can mediate the biodegradation of low- and high-density polyethylenes in marine environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine microbes are microorganisms that can degrade low- and high-density polyethylenes, contributing to biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET DEPOLYMERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An engineered enzyme designed to break down and recycle polyethylene terephthalate (PET) plastic bottles.&lt;SEP&gt;PET depolymerase is an engineered enzyme capable of breaking down and recycling plastic bottles made of poly(ethylene terephthalate).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOGICAL DEGRADATION OF PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biological Degradation of Plastics is a comprehensive review that examines the role of microorganisms and enzymes in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRO-OXIDANT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pro-oxidants are additives used in plastics like LDPE to facilitate their abiotic degradation, making them more susceptible to microbial biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pro-oxidants are chemicals used in thermo-oxidative degradation to accelerate the breakdown of polymers like LDPE, making them more susceptible to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL CONSORTIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A bacterial consortium enriched from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae is capable of degrading polyvinyl chloride (PVC), demonstrating the role of gut microbiota in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial consortium refers to a group of bacteria isolated from soil that are capable of degrading polyurethane.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial consortium refers to a group of bacteria that work together to degrade abiotically aged LDPE films containing pro-oxidants.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial consortium refers to a group of bacteria that work together to degrade complex materials like polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENGINEERED PET DEPOLYMERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Engineered PET depolymerase is a synthetic enzyme designed to break down and recycle PET plastics, such as plastic bottles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemicals such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and HBCD are studied for their environmental impact and biodegradation potential by organisms like Tenebrio molitor and Galleria mellonella.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANISM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Organisms like Tenebrio molitor and Galleria mellonella are studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics such as polystyrene and polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOME DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbiome diversity in Tenebrio molitor is assessed to understand the impact of polystyrene waste on gut microbial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbiome diversity in Tenebrio molitor is studied to understand its role in the biodegradation of polystyrene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbiome diversity in Tenebrio molitor is studied to understand the role of gut microorganisms in the biodegradation of polystyrene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbiome diversity in Tenebrio molitor is studied to understand the role of gut microorganisms in the biodegradation of polystyrene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbiome diversity refers to the variety of microorganisms in the gut of organisms like Tenebrio molitor, which plays a role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAME RETARDANT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flame retardants are trace chemicals present in polystyrene products that may affect the microbial community and degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Flame retardants like HBCD are chemicals added to plastics such as polystyrene to reduce flammability, but they pose environmental concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Flame retardants like hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD) are chemicals added to plastics to reduce flammability, which can be egested by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO-DIET SUPPLEMENTATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Co-diet supplementation involves adding additional nutrients or substances to the diet of organisms, such as mealworms, to enhance their ability to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-diet supplementation involves adding materials like cellulose to the diet of organisms like Galleria mellonella to study their effects on plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-diet supplementation refers to the addition of alternative food sources to the diet of organisms like waxworms, which can influence their gut microbiome and plastic biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SALIVARY GLANDS PROTEOME">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The salivary glands proteome refers to the proteins expressed in the salivary glands of organisms like Galleria mellonella, which may play a role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial community analysis involves studying the diversity and function of microorganisms in environments like the guts of plastic-degrading organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial community analysis was conducted to evaluate differences in gut microbial structure among mealworms fed with different feedstocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial community analysis involves studying the gut microbiota of larvae to understand their role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial community analysis was conducted to study the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae and their role in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP) foam.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial community analysis is a study method used to compare changes in gut communities of mealworm larvae before and after feeding on LDPE, providing insights into the role of gut bacteria in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOG MT PLATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biolog MT plates are used in research to study the metabolic capabilities of microbial communities, including those involved in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOCELLULOSIC-STRAW-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignocellulosic-straw-degrading bacteria are microorganisms capable of breaking down plant-based materials, sometimes studied alongside plastic-degrading organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROCYSTIN-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microcystin-degrading bacteria are microorganisms capable of breaking down microcystins, which are toxic compounds produced by cyanobacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROCYSTIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microcystin is a toxic chemical produced by cyanobacteria, posing environmental and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROCARBON-DEGRADING MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrocarbon-degrading microbes are microorganisms that can break down hydrocarbons, aiding in the remediation of oil-contaminated soils.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHAMNOLIPID BIOSURFACTANTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhamnolipid biosurfactants are surface-active agents produced by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, used to enhance the degradation of hydrocarbons in contaminated soils.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhamnolipid biosurfactants are surface-active agents produced by microorganisms, used to enhance the bioavailability and degradation of hydrocarbons in contaminated soils.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITROBACTER FREUNDI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrobacter freundii is a bacterium capable of biodegrading tannic acid and other pollutants, such as used engine oil and endocrine-disrupting chemicals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TANNIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tannic acid is a polyphenolic compound that can be biodegraded by certain bacteria, such as Citrobacter freundii.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OCHROBACTUM ANTHROPI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ochrobactrum anthropi is a bacterium capable of biodegrading used engine oil, contributing to the remediation of oil-contaminated environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="USED ENGINE OIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Used engine oil is a hazardous environmental pollutant that can be biodegraded by specific bacterial strains, such as Ochrobactrum anthropi and Citrobacter freundii.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="O-PHENYLENEDIAMINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">O-Phenylenediamine is an endocrine-disrupting chemical that can be biodegraded by Citrobacter freundii using intracellular enzymes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-NITROANILINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">4-Nitroaniline is a chemical found in textile dye wastewater that can be biodegraded by pure and mixed bacterial cultures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL MICROBIAL POPULATION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil microbial populations are communities of microorganisms in soil that can degrade hydrocarbons, especially when enhanced by surfactants like β-cyclodextrin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Β-CYCLODEXTRIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">β-Cyclodextrin is a surfactant used to enhance the bioavailability of hydrocarbons for degradation by soil microbial populations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TANNERY EFFLUENT">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Tannery effluent is a pollutant containing tannic acid and other harmful substances, which can be biodegraded by bacteria like Citrobacter freundii.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OIL-CONTAMINATED SOIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oil-contaminated soil is a hazardous environmental pollutant that can be remediated by hydrocarbon-degrading bacteria like Ochrobactrum anthropi and Citrobacter freundii.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEXTILE DYE WASTEWATER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Textile dye wastewater contains harmful chemicals like 4-nitroaniline, which can be biodegraded by specific bacterial cultures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a</data>
      <data key="d1">Biolog MT2 Microplates are used to screen the metabolic activity of PS-degrading bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SERRATIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Serratia is a bacterial genus strongly associated with the polyethylene diet of greater wax moth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Serratia is a genus of bacteria isolated from mealworms that can degrade bioplastics, highlighting its potential in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Serratia is a genus of bacteria that is dominant in the gut microbiome of PS-fed larvae, suggesting its involvement in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Serratia is a genus of bacteria found in the guts of greater wax moth larvae, associated with polystyrene digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioplastics, such as poly(butylene succinate) (PBS) and poly-caprolactone (PCL), are environmentally friendly alternatives to conventional plastics that can degrade naturally.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(BUTYLENE SUCCINATE) (PBS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(butylene succinate) (PBS) is a biodegradable plastic that degrades naturally into water and CO2, making it environmentally friendly.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(BUTYLENE SUCCINATE-CO-BUTYLENE ADIPATE) (PBSA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(butylene succinate-co-butylene adipate) (PBSA) is a biodegradable plastic similar to PBS but more flexible due to the addition of adipic acid.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY-CAPROLACTONE (PCL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly-caprolactone (PCL) is a biodegradable plastic that can degrade in soil, water, and compost through microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterococcus is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, potentially involved in the biodegradation of plastics like polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a common genus in the mealworm gut microbiome, involved in digestion and potentially in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a common genus in the mealworm gut microbiome, present in the hindgut and midgut, and involved in digestion and plastic degradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a dominant gut microbe in superworms associated with the polystyrene diet, suggesting its involvement in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria in the Firmicutes phylum, found in the mealworm gut microbiome, and associated with PU degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria predominant in the gut microbiome of mealworms, playing a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, playing a role in their digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a genus of gut microbes found in mealworms and superworms, associated with plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria present in the gut of mealworms, aiding in digestion and gut health.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria that can be found in the gut microbiota and may play a role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHYZOPERTHA DOMINICA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhyzopertha dominica, a type of beetle, is studied for its interaction with plastic packaging in grain management.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhyzopertha dominica, commonly known as the lesser grain borer, is an insect capable of chewing and digesting plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The bacteriome is a symbiotic organ in insects where microorganisms aggregate, playing a significant role in digestion and detoxification processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPLASTICS (PBSA, PBS, PCL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioplastics such as PBSA, PBS, and PCL are biodegradable polymers studied for their degradation by bacterial strains isolated from mealworm guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAW POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Raw polystyrene is an unprocessed form of polystyrene used in experiments to study its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROCESSED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Processed polystyrene is a form of polystyrene that has undergone extrusion, injection molding, and grinding, used in biodegradation studies by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PACKAGING POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Packaging polystyrene is a commercially available material used for parcels and studied for its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Starved larvae are mealworms deprived of food, used as a control group in experiments to compare the effects of different feeding types on biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Starved larvae of Tenebrio molitor were used as a control group to compare the effects of feeding on polystyrene, showing significant mass loss and reduced metabolite levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Starved larvae were used as a control group in the study to compare the effects of different plastic diets on mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Starved larvae are mealworms deprived of food, used to study the effects of starvation on their biochemical characteristics and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Starved larvae were used as a control group in the study to compare the effects of plastic consumption on mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OATMEAL-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Oatmeal-fed larvae are mealworms provided with oatmeal as a control group in experiments to study the effects of different feeding types on biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE BACTERIAL STRAINS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradable bacterial strains are microorganisms isolated from mealworm guts that have the ability to degrade bioplastics such as PBSA, PBS, and PCL.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE GRANULES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene granules are raw materials obtained from Krakchemia S.A., used in experiments to prepare raw polystyrene for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PBSA (BIONOLLE 3020MD)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PBSA (Bionolle 3020MD) is a bioplastic pellet obtained from Showa Denko K.K., used in experiments to study biodegradation by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PBS (BIONOLLE 1020MD)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PBS (Bionolle 1020MD) is a bioplastic pellet obtained from Showa Denko K.K., used in experiments to study biodegradation by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PCL is a bioplastic pellet purchased from TRESNO, used in experiments to study biodegradation by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Polycaprolactone (PCL) is a biodegradable polyester used in medical and environmental applications due to its ability to degrade under certain conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SARKOSYL NL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sarkosyl NL is a chemical used in the preparation of polymer emulsions for biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Sarkosyl NL is a surfactant used in the preparation of polymer emulsions, aiding in the dispersion of plastic particles in water.&lt;SEP&gt;Sarkosyl NL is a surfactant used in the preparation of PS emulsions to stabilize the mixture.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT AGAR (NA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient agar (NA) is a medium used for the isolation and estimation of the total number of microorganisms in mealworm guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POTATO COUNT AGAR (PCA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Potato count agar (PCA) is a medium used for the isolation and estimation of the total number of microorganisms in mealworm guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINIMUM MEDIUM (FMM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Minimum medium (FMM) is a medium used to select bacteria with the ability to degrade bioplastics, containing emulsified plastic as a sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PBSA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polybutylene succinate adipate (PBSA) is a biodegradable polymer used in packaging and agricultural films, known for its environmental compatibility.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PBS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) is a buffer solution used in biological research to maintain pH and osmotic balance.&lt;SEP&gt;Polybutylene succinate (PBS) is a biodegradable polymer used in various applications, including packaging and disposable products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRESS MOLDING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Press molding is a process used to form plastic plates for physico-mechanical assessment in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAAKE RHEOMEX 252">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Haake Rheomex 252 is a one-screw extruder used for processing polystyrene in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOY 35A">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">BOY 35A is an injection molding machine used for processing polystyrene in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WANNER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Wanner is a grinder used for processing polystyrene in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LABTECH LP-20B">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LabTech LP-20B is a press molding machine used under specific temperature and pressure conditions for plastic processing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MELT FLOW JUNIOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Melt Flow Junior is an apparatus used to assess the melt flow ratio of polymers under controlled conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHORE D">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Shore D is a hardness scale used to measure the hardness of polystyrene and related materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHORE A">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Shore A is a hardness scale used to measure the hardness of certain plastic samples, such as PSp and EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MULTI GLOSS 268 PLUS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Multi Gloss 268 Plus is a device used to measure the gloss of materials at a 60° angle.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADVEX INSTRUMENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Advex Instrument is used for contact angle measurements on film surfaces under static conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plate Count Agar (PCA) is a medium used for the aerobic incubation of microorganisms to assess total viable counts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NA MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient Agar (NA) medium is used for the cultivation and isolation of microorganisms in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FMM PLATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FMM plates are used in the clear zone test to isolate plastic-degrading microorganisms by providing a carbon source from emulsified plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S. MARCESCENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Serratia marcescens is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworms that has been identified as having plastic-degrading capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P. AERUGINOSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas aeruginosa is another bacterial strain isolated from mealworms that has been identified as having plastic-degrading capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K. OXYTOCA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworms that has been identified as having plastic-degrading capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium found in the intestines of mealworms that can digest polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENETIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenetic analysis involves the use of next-generation sequencing to study the genetic material of microbial communities, such as those in the gut of mealworms, to understand their role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA SEQUENCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA sequence analysis is a method used to identify bacterial strains isolated from mealworms, such as those capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCRATCH TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The scratch test is a method used to confirm the biodegradable activity of bacterial strains by observing clearance zones on agar plates containing plastic emulsions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL ANALYSES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical analyses involve the assessment of total sugar, lipid, and protein content in mealworms to study the effects of plastic degradation on their physiology.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochemical analyses were conducted to assess the protein, sugar, and lipid content in mealworms fed with different types of polystyrene, revealing significant differences in metabolite levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochemical analyses were conducted on mealworms to assess the effects of polystyrene consumption, including total sugars, lipids, and soluble protein content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STATISTICA SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Statistica software is used for statistical analysis, such as calculating significant differences in mass loss and biochemical content in mealworms fed with different PS species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PSp is a form of polystyrene that mealworms were fed, resulting in moderate changes in lipid and carbohydrate levels.&lt;SEP&gt;PSp is a modified form of polystyrene that showed the highest mass loss when consumed by mealworms, indicating its susceptibility to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene used for packing purposes (PSP) is a form of polystyrene that showed the highest mass loss in mealworms, indicating potential degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PSp is a type of polystyrene material derived from food packaging, exhibiting notable mass loss during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS CHANGES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass changes in mealworms were recorded to evaluate the impact of feeding on different types of polystyrene, showing significant mass loss in larvae fed with PS and EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAW POLYSTYRENE (PS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Raw polystyrene (PS) is a form of polystyrene that showed significant mass loss in mealworms, indicating it is not a suitable food source for larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Raw polystyrene (PS) is the unprocessed form of polystyrene used in the study to evaluate its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROCESSED POLYSTYRENE (PSR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Processed polystyrene (PSr) is a form of polystyrene that has undergone multiple processing cycles, including extrusion and injection molding, to study its biodegradability.&lt;SEP&gt;Processed polystyrene (PSr) is a modified form of polystyrene used in experiments to assess its biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Processed polystyrene (PSr) is a recycled form of polystyrene that showed moderate mass loss in mealworms, suggesting limited degradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMAGO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Imago is the adult stage of Tenebrio molitor, with significant differences in counts observed across different feed types.&lt;SEP&gt;Imago refers to the adult stage of Tenebrio molitor, which showed minimal mass changes when fed with polystyrene, indicating limited impact on adult mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTROL GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Control group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with oatmeal ad libitum to serve as a baseline for growth and development studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The control group consisted of mealworms fed with oatmeal, showing a 36% mass increase, indicating a healthy growth rate compared to polystyrene-fed larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The control group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae maintained under acclimation conditions without exposure to polystyrene, used as a baseline for comparison in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;The control group in the study consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed a standard diet, used as a baseline for comparing growth and development under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The control group is used as a baseline in the study to compare the effects of different diets on Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL COUNTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacterial counts were conducted to assess the microbial populations in mealworms fed with different types of polystyrene, revealing variations in bacterial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUGAR CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Sugar content in mealworms was analyzed, revealing a 10.69% decrease in larvae fed with polystyrene compared to the control group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid content in mealworms is highest in control groups and decreases during starvation or when fed non-nutritive materials like PVC and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid content in mealworms was analyzed, showing a 43.60% decrease in larvae fed with polystyrene, indicating significant metabolic stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid content in mealworms was measured to assess the metabolic impact of polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA (KI2Y)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Ki2y) is a bacterial strain isolated for its ability to biodegrade plastic materials, such as PBS and PCL.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SERRATIA MARCESCENS (6A)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Serratia marcescens (6A) is a bacterial strain that demonstrated biodegradable activity on plastic materials in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA (9A)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella oxytoca (9A) is a bacterial strain that showed clear zones on FMM medium, indicating its ability to biodegrade plastic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microorganisms are being studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi, are key players in the biodegradation of plastics, producing enzymes that break down complex polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi, are key players in the biodegradation of plastics, producing enzymes that break down complex polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi, play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) under specific environmental conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi, play a crucial role in the degradation of synthetic plastics and other environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PSr is a form of polystyrene that was tested for its susceptibility to biodegradation by mealworms, showing lower mass loss compared to PSp and EPS.&lt;SEP&gt;PSr refers to a type of polystyrene that mealworms were fed, leading to significant changes in lipid and carbohydrate levels.&lt;SEP&gt;PSr refers to a specific type of polystyrene material used in the study, showing significant mass loss during biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMAGINES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Imagines are adult forms of mealworms that were included in the study to analyze species-level taxonomic categories.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHORE HARDNESS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Shore hardness is a measure of material hardness, tested for polystyrene (PS and PSr) and packaging material (PSP) using Shore D and Shore A scales.&lt;SEP&gt;Shore hardness is a measure of material hardness, which was used to assess the susceptibility of different polystyrene materials to mealworm biting.&lt;SEP&gt;Shore hardness is a measure of the hardness of materials, used to evaluate the physical properties of polystyrene variants in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Shore hardness is a measure used to characterize the mechanical properties of polystyrene variants tested in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLOSS MEASUREMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gloss measurement is a method used to assess the surface reflectivity of materials, such as polystyrene, at a 60° angle.&lt;SEP&gt;Gloss measurement was used to evaluate the surface properties of polystyrene materials, with less glossy surfaces being more susceptible to mealworm biting.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MFR (MELT FLOW RATE)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">MFR is a measure of polymer chain length, which was used to assess the ease of chewing and biting by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Melt flow rate (MFR) is a measure of polymer chain length, which was used to assess the consistency between polymer properties and mealworm accessibility.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTACT ANGLE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Contact angle is a measure of surface hydrophilicity, which was used to determine the interaction between water and polymer surfaces in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;The contact angle is a measure of the hydrophilicity or hydrophobicity of a surface, which changes during the biodegradation of HDPE by bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PBS (POLYBUTYLENE SUCCINATE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PBS is a biodegradable plastic material used in the study to test the biodegradation ability of isolated bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCL (POLYCAPROLACTONE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PCL is another biodegradable plastic material used in the study to evaluate the biodegradation potential of bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FMM MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FMM medium is a chemical medium used in experiments to study the biodegradation of polystyrene under anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;FMM medium is a culture medium used to assess the biodegradation capabilities of bacteria, such as strain LG3 and SCGB1, by measuring weight loss of polystyrene films.&lt;SEP&gt;FMM medium is a growth medium used in the study to culture anaerobic PS-degrading bacteria, containing microspheres polystyrene or maltose monohydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;FMM medium is a growth medium used in the study to test the biodegradation ability of bacterial strains on plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;FMM medium is a nutrient-rich liquid medium used in biodegradation assays to support the growth of microorganisms and facilitate the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;FMM medium is a nutrient-rich medium used in the study to culture bacterial strains and assess their ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) films under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOTAL PROTEIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total protein content was measured in mealworms fed different plastic diets to assess the nutritional impact of plastic consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Total protein refers to the concentration of proteins in mealworms, which was measured to assess the impact of polystyrene feeding on their biochemical characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOTAL SUGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total sugar content was analyzed in mealworms to evaluate the effects of different plastic diets on their nutritional status.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOTAL FAT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total fat content was measured in mealworms, showing significant declines when fed certain types of plastic, indicating starvation effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN LEVEL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein level in mealworms is relatively stable but slightly affected by the type of food consumed, with significant differences observed between control and polystyrene-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TREHALOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trehalose is a sugar involved in stress response mechanisms in plants, particularly in response to abiotic stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Trehalose is the main carbohydrate in the insect body, crucial for maintaining organ function, particularly the nervous system, and is affected during starvation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PAENIBACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Paenibacillus is a genus of bacteria found in landfill sediments, capable of efficiently decomposing micro-sized polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Paenibacillus is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, involved in their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NGS STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The NGS (Next-Generation Sequencing) study analyzed the gut microbiome of mealworms, revealing differences in bacterial community composition based on the type of polystyrene consumed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR GUT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut of Tenebrio molitor contains microorganisms that play a critical role in the biodegradation of plastics, such as polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION MECHANISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The biodegradation mechanism involves the breakdown of plastics by microorganisms, such as those in the gut of mealworms, into simpler compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHIZOPHORA DOMINICA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhizophora dominica is a species of beetle studied for its interaction with plastic packaging in pest management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMAN IMPACT ON MARINE ECOSYSTEMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A global map of human impact on marine ecosystems is a study that assesses the effects of human activities on marine environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTICS RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastics recycling is a study that explores the challenges and opportunities in recycling plastic materials to reduce environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect gut bacteria are microorganisms that live in the digestive tracts of insects and play a role in processes like biodegradation and symbiosis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYLLOSPHERE YEASTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Phyllosphere yeasts are microorganisms that live on plant surfaces and have been found to rapidly break down biodegradable plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR GUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) is studied for its response to environmental changes and its role in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRASSHOPPERS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Grasshoppers (Locusta migratoria migratoroides) are insects sold for human consumption and studied for their microbial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Grasshoppers, such as Locusta migratoria, are insects being explored for their potential in biodegradation and as sustainable protein sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STAG BEETLE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aegus laevicollis, commonly known as the stag beetle, is an organism whose larval midgut contains a yeast capable of degrading biodegradable plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The stag beetle (Aegus laevicollis) is an insect studied for its larval midgut microorganisms, which can degrade biodegradable plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEGUS LAEVICOLLIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aegus laevicollis is a stag beetle whose larval midgut was found to contain a yeast capable of degrading biodegradable plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE PLASTIC-DEGRADING ENZYME">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A biodegradable plastic-degrading enzyme is produced by Aspergillus oryzae, enabling the degradation of certain plastics, as studied by Maeda et al. (2005).&lt;SEP&gt;A biodegradable plastic-degrading enzyme was purified and characterized from a yeast isolated from the larval midgut of Aegus laevicollis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARCTIC MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Arctic microorganisms are studied for their ability to decompose bioplastics, contributing to environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Arctic microorganisms have been isolated and characterized for their ability to decompose bioplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YEAST">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A yeast isolated from the larval midgut of Aegus laevicollis is capable of producing a biodegradable plastic-degrading enzyme.&lt;SEP&gt;Yeast is a standard diet for mealworms, providing essential nutrients like B vitamins, proteins, and amino acids, leading to better growth and reproduction compared to polystyrene-based diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic metabolism refers to the process by which organisms like Tenebrio molitor break down microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(TETRAMETHYLENE SUCCINATE)-CO-ADIPATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(tetramethylene succinate)-co-adipate is a biodegradable plastic that can be degraded by specific bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A bacterium capable of degrading solid poly(tetramethylene succinate)-co-adipate, a biodegradable plastic, has been identified.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER EMULSION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466</data>
      <data key="d1">Sarkosyl NL is used as a surfactant in the preparation of polymer emulsions, aiding in the dispersion of plastic particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Sarkosyl NL is used in the preparation of polymer emulsions for biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MFR">
      <data key="d2">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
      <data key="d1">MFR measurements indicated that PSp had the lowest mean polymer chain length, making it easier for mealworms to chew.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLIC RESPONSES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
      <data key="d1">Alphitobius diaperinus is studied for its metabolic responses during food shortage and recovery, which can inform biodegradation research</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHTHALIC ACID, DI(2-PROPYLPENTYL) ESTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phthalic acid, di(2-propylpentyl) ester is a chemical compound identified as a potential degradation product of PVC during the biodegradation process by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Phthalic acid, di(2-propylpentyl) ester is a potential degradation product identified during the biodegradation of PVC by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-PROPENOIC ACID, TRIDECYL ESTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Propenoic acid, tridecyl ester is another chemical compound identified as a potential degradation product of PVC during the biodegradation process by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;2-Propenoic acid, tridecyl ester is another potential degradation product identified during the biodegradation of PVC by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOMIC SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenomic sequencing is a research method used to analyze the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms and identify key gene modules involved in PVC biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic sequencing was used to identify the gut microbiome's response to PVC degradation and to identify key gene modules involved in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic sequencing is a technique used to investigate the genetic potential of gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae for PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic sequencing is used to study the functional potential of the gut microbiome in larvae, particularly in relation to PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic sequencing is a technique used to investigate the genetic potential of gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae for PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metagenomic sequencing is used to study the functional potential of the gut microbiome in larvae, particularly in relation to PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOUR MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Four specific microorganisms in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms were identified as actively involved in the biodegradation of PVC.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="111 KEY GENE MODULES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">111 key gene modules were identified through metagenomic functional analysis as significantly enriched in the gut microbiome during PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERWORMS (ZOPHOBAS ATRATUS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Superworms (Zophobas atratus) are larvae known for their ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE) materials, with their gut microbiota playing a crucial role in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms, or Zophobas atratus, are another insect species capable of biodegrading polystyrene (PS) and converting it into minerals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDIAN MEALMOTHS (PLODIA INTERPUNCTELLA)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Indian mealmoths, or Plodia interpunctella, are larvae that can consume polyethylene (PE) films, contributing to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAX MOTH (GALLERIA MELLONELLA)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The greater wax moth, Galleria mellonella, is an insect capable of consuming polyethylene (PE) over short periods, aiding in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESSER WAXWORMS (ACHROIA GRISELLA)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lesser waxworms, or Achroia grisella, are insects that can degrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE) with a significant survival rate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAND SNAILS (ACHATINA FULICA)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Land snails, or Achatina fulica, are capable of ingesting polystyrene and expelling microplastics, contributing to plastic mass loss.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYMBIOTIC INTESTINAL MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Symbiotic intestinal microbes in yellow mealworms are responsible for their remarkable ability to degrade various plastics, including PVC.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHER-PU FOAMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyether-polyurethane (PU) foams are plastics that yellow mealworms can consume, leading to a significant reduction in mass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NMR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) is a technique used to analyze the degradation of PVC by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy is employed to study the chemical shifts and formation of new peaks in polypropylene (PP) residues after biodegradation by T. molitor and Z. atratus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT GUT MICROBIOMES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect gut microbiomes, particularly in yellow mealworms, are influenced by diet and play a significant role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOME SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenome sequencing is employed to analyze the microbial communities in the intestines of mealworms, which may play a role in PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVC FILM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PVC film is a type of plastic material used in packaging and other applications, known for its durability and resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PY-GCMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrolysis-gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (PY-GCMS) is a technique used to analyze the composition of PVC film, identifying its chemical structure and degradation products.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolyzer-gas chromatography mass spectrometry (Py-GCMS) is a technique used to quantify the plastic components in solid residues, particularly nanoplastics, in mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GC-MS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GC-MS is a technique used to identify and quantify the degradation intermediates of polystyrene and cellulose in mealworm frass and gut samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of intestinal feces from mealworms, identifying degradation products of PVC.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAGPURE-SOIL-DNA-KF-KIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The MagPure-Soil-DNA-KF-Kit is a tool used to isolate genomic DNA from intestinal specimens of mealworms for metagenomic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOMIC DATA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenomic data refers to the genetic information obtained from sequencing the microbial communities in the intestines of mealworms, providing insights into PVC biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FASTP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FASTP is a tool used for preprocessing sequencing data, including adapter removal and quality filtering, to generate clean reads for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Fastp is a tool used for quality enhancement and trimming of raw metagenomic reads, ensuring accurate sequencing data for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOWTIE2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bowtie2 is a software tool used for aligning sequencing reads to reference genomes, such as the Tenebrio molitor genome, to filter out host contamination.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEGAHIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">MEGAHIT is a tool used for assembling metagenomic sequences into contigs, which are then used for gene prediction and analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRODIGAL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Prodigal is a gene prediction tool used to identify open reading frames (ORFs) within assembled contigs for functional and taxonomic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CD-HIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">CD-HIT is a tool used for clustering and generating a non-redundant gene catalogue by comparing predicted ORFs.&lt;SEP&gt;CD-HIT is a tool used for clustering coding sequences (CDS) to obtain unigenes in metagenomic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;CD-HIT is a tool used for clustering coding sequences (CDS) to obtain unigenes in metagenomic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NR DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The NR database is a comprehensive protein sequence database used for taxonomic and functional classification of metagenomic data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The KEGG database is used for functional profiling of metagenomic data, providing insights into biological pathways and functions encoded in the data.&lt;SEP&gt;The KEGG database is used for metabolite annotation and pathway analysis in metabolomic studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The KEGG database is used for metabolite annotation and pathway analysis in metabolomic studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIAMOND">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DIAMOND is a tool used for aligning gene sequences to the KEGG database to create functional profiles of metagenomic data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA gene sequencing is used to analyze the bacterial diversity in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms during the biodegradation of surgical masks.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA is a component of the 30S small subunit of prokaryotic ribosomes, used in phylogenetic studies to identify and classify microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is used to analyze bacterial communities by targeting the V3-V4 hypervariable regions of the 16S rRNA gene.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA is a component of the ribosomal RNA used in phylogenetic studies to identify and classify bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ITS RRNA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ITS rRNA sequencing is used to analyze fungal communities by targeting the ITS1 and ITS2 regions of the rRNA gene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILLUMINA NOVASEQ 6000">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Illumina NovaSeq 6000 is a high-throughput sequencing system used for generating metagenomic and amplicon sequencing data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CUTADAPT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cutadapt is a tool used for removing adapter sequences and refining sequencing reads to ensure data quality.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutadapt is a tool used to process raw sequencing reads by removing adapters and barcodes in gut microbiome analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFRARED SPECTRA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Infrared spectra are used to analyze chemical changes in polymers, such as PVC, after degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PELOMONAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pelomonas is a bacterial genus that showed a significant increase in abundance in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, suggesting its role in PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESCHERICHIA-SHIGELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Escherichia-Shigella is a bacterial genus that increased in abundance in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, indicating its potential involvement in PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Escherichia-Shigella is a group of bacteria found in the guts of yellow mealworm larvae, associated with polystyrene digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPHINGOMONAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sphingomonas is a bacterial genus that increased in abundance in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, suggesting its role in the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACIDOVORAX">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acidovorax is a bacterial genus that showed increased abundance in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, indicating its potential role in PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTERIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacteriales are bacteria that form part of the gut microbiome of mealworms, regardless of diet, and may contribute to the degradation of synthetic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriales are microorganisms involved in the degradation of pesticides, lignocellulose, and aromatic compounds, and are found in the gut of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria enriched in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, indicating its potential role in PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria that is highly abundant in the PE-based groups of the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 4.5% in the gut microbiome, showing higher levels in the PE-based groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AQUABACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aquabacterium is a bacterial genus enriched in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, suggesting its involvement in PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Aquabacterium is a genus of bacteria capable of degrading alkanes, quinoline, and indole, and is found in the gut of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANTOEA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pantoea is a bacterial genus enriched in the bran-fed mealworm group, indicating its reduced role in PVC degradation compared to other genera.&lt;SEP&gt;Pantoea is a genus of bacteria found in the digestive tract of adult Tenebrio molitor, contributing to the gut microbiota composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Pantoea is a genus of bacteria isolated from the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor adults, potentially involved in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pantoea is a genus of bacteria identified in the gut of mealworms, involved in their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ERWINIAACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Erwiniaaceae is a family of bacteria enriched in the bran-fed mealworm group, suggesting its reduced role in PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOCLOSTRIDIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermoclostridium is a bacterial genus enriched in the bran-fed mealworm group, indicating its reduced role in PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillales is an order of bacteria enriched in the bran-fed mealworm group, indicating its reduced role in PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITRATE CYCLE (TCA CYCLE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The citrate cycle (TCA cycle) is a metabolic pathway enriched in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, indicating its role in energy production during PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The citrate cycle, or TCA cycle, is a central metabolic pathway involved in energy production, which is part of the metabolic activities in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The citrate cycle, or TCA cycle, is a central metabolic pathway involved in energy production, which is part of the metabolic activities in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE PHOSPHORYLATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative phosphorylation is a metabolic pathway enriched in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, suggesting its role in energy production during PVC degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidative phosphorylation is a metabolic pathway that generates energy in the gut microbes of yellow mealworms, particularly after PVC exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PENTOSE AND GLUCURONATE INTERCONVERSIONS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Pentose and glucuronate interconversions are metabolic pathways enriched in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, indicating their role in PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Valine metabolism is a metabolic pathway enriched in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, suggesting its role in PVC degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEFSE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">LEfSe (Linear Discriminant Analysis Effect Size) is a statistical method used to identify biomarker species and functional pathways in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOMIC FUNCTION RECOGNITION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenomic function recognition involves identifying key gene modules and metabolic pathways in the gut microbiota of mealworms feeding on PVC, providing insights into PVC degradation mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID BIOSYNTHESIS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid biosynthesis is a metabolic process that is upregulated in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms following PVC consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid biosynthesis is the process of creating fatty acids, which is part of the lipid metabolism in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid biosynthesis pathways are involved in the production of essential components of cell membranes and energy reserves during PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid biosynthesis is the process of creating fatty acids, which is part of the lipid metabolism in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid biosynthesis pathways are involved in the production of essential components of cell membranes and energy reserves during PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPOPOLYSACCHARIDE BIOSYNTHESIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipopolysaccharide biosynthesis is a metabolic pathway that is enriched in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms after PVC exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LINEAR ALKYLBENZENE SULFONATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Linear alkylbenzene sulfonate is a chemical compound that can be degraded by Proteobacteria, which are present in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oil is a pollutant that can be degraded by Proteobacteria, which are found in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOCELLULOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignocellulose is a complex carbohydrate found in plant cell walls that mealworms can digest, though it is less beneficial for their development compared to synthetic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a complex polymer that can be broken down by Enterobacteriales and Gammaproteobacteria, which are present in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a feed type used in the study to compare the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing lower larval mass and metamorphosis rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a natural polymer that can be degraded by key microorganisms such as Bacillus sp., Spiroplasma sp., Pediococcus sp., and Enterococcus sp.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a complex organic compound found in plant cell walls, used as a comparative diet (CS) in this study to assess its impact on the gut microbiome of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a complex organic compound found in plant cell walls, which can be deconstructed by the gut microbiota of wood-feeding beetles.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a complex organic compound found in plant cell walls, which mealworms can also degrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a complex organic compound found in plant materials like corn straw, affecting its degradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a complex organic material found in plant cell walls, often pretreated for the production of high-value chemicals.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a component of corn straw, consisting of cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin, which are partially digested by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a natural polymer found in corn straw and other plant materials that mealworms can degrade, sharing mechanisms with polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a natural component of the diet of Tenebrio molitor larvae, and its digestion by gut microbes may contribute to PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose is a natural component of the diet of Tenebrio molitor larvae, and its digestion by gut microbes may contribute to PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AROMATIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aromatic compounds are chemicals that can be metabolized by Enterobacteriales, which are found in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Aromatic compounds are harmful byproducts produced during the thermal decomposition of plastics, exacerbating environmental damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Aromatic compounds are degraded by mealworms through metabolic pathways shared with the degradation of polystyrene and corn straw.&lt;SEP&gt;Aromatic compounds are intermediates produced during the degradation of polystyrene and lignin by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Aromatic compounds, such as benzoate and fluorobenzoate, are metabolic intermediates of PET degradation that gut microbes show chemotaxis towards.&lt;SEP&gt;Aromatic compounds, such as benzoate and fluorobenzoate, are metabolic intermediates of PET degradation that gut microbes show chemotaxis towards.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUINOLINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Quinoline is a chemical compound that can be biodegraded by symbiotic systems involving bacteria and algae.&lt;SEP&gt;Quinoline is a chemical compound that can be degraded by Aquabacterium, which is found in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDOLE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Indole is a chemical compound that can be degraded by Aquabacterium, which is present in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRICHLOROETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trichloroethylene is a chemical compound that can be dechlorinated by Aquabacterium under anaerobic conditions, which is found in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME BC1 COMPLEX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Cytochrome bc1 complex is a component of the oxidative phosphorylation pathway that is enriched in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms following PVC exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME BC1 COMPLEX RESPIRATORY UNIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Cytochrome bc1 complex respiratory unit is part of the oxidative phosphorylation pathway and is significantly enriched in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms after PVC exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAT AND LIPID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fat and lipid metabolism are key metabolic pathways that are upregulated in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms and mice exposed to polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mice are among the organisms studied for the biological toxicity of long-term exposure to microplastics, which can cause inflammatory responses and mechanical damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Mice are organisms used in studies to observe the effects of polystyrene microplastics on the gut microbiome and metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT-DEFICIENT FOOD">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient-deficient food refers to food that lacks essential nutrients, which can impact the gut microbiome and metabolic pathways of organisms like yellow mealworms and mice.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY PROJECT FUND">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Science and Technology Project Fund of Jiangxi Provincial Department of Education supported the research on the biodegradation of PVC by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JIANQUAN XU">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Jianquan Xu is a researcher who designed and performed experiments on the biodegradation of PVC by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YONGQUAN DONG">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Yongquan Dong provided the breeding place for yellow mealworm larvae and technical guidance for the research on PVC biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE-CHLORELLA BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A symbiotic system involving Polyurethane-chlorella bacteria that enhances the biodegradation of quinoline and indole.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NESIOBACTER EXALBESCENCE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">An alpha-proteobacteria capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE) in saltpans.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum degradation refers to the breakdown of petroleum-based substances, such as plastics, by microorganisms under specific environmental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW TEMPERATURE ANAEROBIC CONDITION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Low temperature anaerobic conditions are environments where petroleum degradation can occur, often involving specific microbial consortia.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(ETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)-DEGRADING BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A bacterium capable of degrading and assimilating poly(ethylene terephthalate), a common plastic, as identified in scientific research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEQUENCING DATA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">FASTP is used to preprocess sequencing data, ensuring high-quality reads for downstream analysis.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR GENOME">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">Bowtie2 aligns sequencing reads to the Tenebrio molitor genome to filter out host contamination.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTIGS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">MEGAHIT assembles metagenomic sequences into contigs for gene prediction and analysis.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORFS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">Prodigal predicts open reading frames (ORFs) within assembled contigs for functional and taxonomic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NON-REDUNDANT GENE CATALOGUE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">CD-HIT generates a non-redundant gene catalogue by clustering predicted ORFs.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONAL PROFILING">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">The KEGG database provides functional profiles of metagenomic data by mapping genes to biological pathways.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGAL COMMUNITIES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungal communities are groups of fungi that may contribute to the degradation of synthetic polymers, potentially working synergistically with bacteria in insect guts.&lt;SEP&gt;ITS rRNA sequencing is used to analyze the composition and diversity of fungal communities.</data>
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEQUENCING READS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">Cutadapt refines sequencing reads by removing adapters and low-quality sequences.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass contains degraded PVC components, indicating the extent of biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC DEGRADATION PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxicological assessments evaluate the potential hazards of plastic degradation products on organisms and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE MULCHING FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PE mulching films are a major source of plastic pollution in soil, often made of LDPE or LLDPE, and are challenging to biodegrade.&lt;SEP&gt;PE mulching films are agricultural plastics composed of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), which are difficult to recycle and contribute to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;PE mulching films are plastic materials used in agriculture that contribute to environmental pollution and are the focus of biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;PE mulching films are polyethylene-based materials used in agriculture, which can contribute to environmental pollution due to their persistence and difficulty in degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CROSS-LINKED POLYETHYLENE (XLPE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cross-linked polyethylene (XLPE) is a type of polyethylene with a cross-linked structure, increasing its stability and resistance to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW MOLECULAR WEIGHT POLYETHYLENE (LMWPE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low molecular weight polyethylene (LMWPE) is a type of polyethylene with a lower molecular weight, making it more susceptible to biodegradation compared to higher molecular weight variants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDIAN MEAL MOTHS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Indian meal moths (Plodia interpunctella) are insects reported to have the ability to digest plastics, with their gut microbes playing a crucial role.&lt;SEP&gt;Indian meal moths (Plodia interpunctella) are insects that have been studied for their potential role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Indian meal moths are omnivorous insect larvae that have been reported to degrade polyethylene (PE) in preliminary studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAX MOTHS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Greater wax moths (Galleria mellonella) are insects capable of consuming plastics, and their gut microbes are essential for plastic digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater wax moths (Galleria mellonella) are insects known for their ability to degrade polyethylene (PE) and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater wax moths are larvae that can consume polystyrene (PS) but show lower survival rates and weight changes compared to superworms and yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAND SNAILS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Land snails (Achatina fulica) are organisms that disintegrate and biofragment ingested polystyrene (PS) foam into microplastics (MPs).&lt;SEP&gt;Land snails (Achatina fulica) are soil animals reported to have the ability to eat plastics, contributing to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Land snails are soil animals that have been studied for their potential role in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Land snails have been shown to depolymerize and biodegrade polystyrene foam, contributing to plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Land snails are organisms studied for their potential to degrade plastics, similar to mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS ARYABHATTAI 5-3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus aryabhattai 5-3 is a soil bacterium that can degrade polyethylene, though its efficiency is limited.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SP. AKS2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas sp. AKS2 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading low-density polyethylene through biofilm formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas sp. AKS2 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading low-density polyethylene through biofilm formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas sp. AKS2 is a soil bacterial isolate capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE) carrier bags.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SP. E4">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas sp. E4 is a bacterium that can degrade low molecular weight polyethylene (LMWPE) films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACCASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccase is a copper-binding enzyme produced by Rhodococcus ruber, playing a key role in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase is an enzyme that can degrade lignin and has been reported to show low activity on polyethylene plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase is an enzyme that contributes to the biodegradation of polypropylene by oxidizing the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase is an enzyme produced by fungi like Trametes versicolor to degrade lignin.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase is an enzyme that mediates lignin degradation, which is crucial for the breakdown of lignocellulosic feedstocks in biofuel production.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase is an enzyme used for lignin degradation, isolated and characterized for its potential in breaking down complex organic compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase is an oxidative enzyme known to decompose polyethylene (PE) and aromatic compounds, and is activated in the gut tissue of Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase is an oxidative enzyme known to decompose polyethylene (PE) and aromatic compounds, and is activated in the gut tissue of Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase is an enzyme produced by bacterial strains such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 and Achromobacter xylosidans M9, aiding in the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAINS NYZ450 AND NYZ451">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strains NyZ450 and NyZ451 are bacterial isolates from Tenebrio molitor larvae that demonstrate the ability to degrade PE mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Strains NyZ450 and NyZ451 are bacterial isolates from Tenebrio molitor larvae, known for their synergistic ability to degrade PE mulching films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYSOGENY BROTH (LB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lysogeny broth (LB) is a nutrient-rich medium used for culturing bacterial strains, including those isolated for PE degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIQUID CARBON FREE BASAL MEDIUM (LCFBM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Liquid carbon-free basal medium (LCFBM) is a specialized medium used to culture bacteria with PE as the sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON FREE BASAL AGAR MEDIUM (CFBAM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon-free basal agar medium (CFBAM) is a solid medium used for isolating and culturing bacterial strains capable of degrading PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SDS (SODIUM DODECYL SULFATE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SDS is a detergent used to wash PE particles and mulching films to remove contaminants before biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STERILE WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sterile water is used to wash PE particles and mulching films, as well as to prepare bacterial cultures and suspensions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2-DICHLOROBENZENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2-Dichlorobenzene is a chemical solvent used to dissolve PE mulching films for molecular weight analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HT-GPC">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">HT-GPC (High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography) is a method used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polymers, though it was not used in this study for mixed polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC (High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography) is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight of PE mulching films after treatment with bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;High-temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) is a method used to analyze the molecular weight changes in residual polymers from mealworm frass, indicating the extent of plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;High-temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) is used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of residual polymers in frass samples after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT LOSS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight loss analysis is a method used to measure the degradation of PE mulching films by bacterial strains over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight analysis is conducted to assess changes in the molecular structure of PE mulching films after bacterial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight analysis is conducted using GPC and HT-GPC to study the degradation of PS and PE by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM OBSERVATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) observation is a technique used in the study to analyze surface erosion and changes in polystyrene (PS) films degraded by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM observation involves using scanning electron microscopy to study the morphological changes and biofilm formation on PE mulching films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLONY COUNTING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Colony counting is a method used to quantify the growth of bacterial strains on PE mulching films in mixed cultures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSORTIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A consortium of Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450 and Bacillus sp. NyZ451 is essential for the effective biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) mulching films, demonstrating synergistic effects.&lt;SEP&gt;A consortium refers to a group of microorganisms working together to degrade complex materials like polyethylene, often achieving better results than individual strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil pollution caused by discarded polyethylene (PE) mulching films is a significant environmental issue addressed through biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil pollution refers to the contamination of soil by substances such as microplastics, which can harm soil health and the organisms living within it.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT CHANGE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight change is a key indicator of polyethylene (PE) biodegradation, measured to assess the effectiveness of bacterial strains in degrading PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight change refers to the reduction in the molecular weight of polyethylene during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, indicating the breakdown of the polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN NYZ450">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain that plays a major role in the degradation of polyethylene mulching films, primarily responsible for breaking down PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN NYZ451">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain that assists in the degradation of polyethylene mulching films, working synergistically with strain NyZ450 to enhance PE breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS (TENEBRIO MOLITOR)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are insects capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) through their gut microbiome, contributing to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are larvae used in experiments to study their ability to consume and degrade plastics such as polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are macro-organisms that have been studied for their ability to chew and potentially digest polyethylene, contributing to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms, the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, are known for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene and plastic mixtures, with their gut microbiome playing a crucial role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are larvae capable of digesting polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), with their gut microbiota essential for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYSINIBACILLUS SPHAERICUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lysinibacillus sphaericus is a bacterial strain involved in the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) in mixed cultures with other microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS NIGER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus niger is a fungal species involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus niger is a fungal strain that collaborates with Lysinibacillus sphaericus in the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) in soil environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus niger is a soil fungus capable of degrading plastic waste, including PS foam.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus niger is a fungus that can degrade thermally treated low-density polyethylene, contributing to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus niger is a fungus capable of colonizing and degrading thermally oxidized high-density polyethylene, as reported by Mathur et al. (2011).&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus niger is a fungus that can biodegrade low-density polyethylene, often in co-culture with other microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROXYLATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroxylation is a chemical process that activates inert C-C bonds in polypropylene, facilitating its biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxylation is a chemical process that introduces hydroxyl groups into a molecule, often necessary for breaking down inert "C-C" bonds in polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylation is a chemical process that introduces carboxyl groups into a molecule, aiding in the activation and breakdown of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxylation is a chemical process that modifies polypropylene polymers, making them more susceptible to biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRETREATMENTS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Pretreatments such as peroxidation and preheating are used to make polyethylene more bioavailable and easier to degrade by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLLABORATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Collaboration between bacterial strains, such as NyZ450 and NyZ451, enhances the biodegradation of polyethylene by combining complementary catabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYVINYL ALCOHOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinyl alcohol is a synthetic polymer that can be degraded by bacterial strains, often requiring cooperative interactions between microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl alcohol is a synthetic polymer that can be utilized by mixed microbial cultures for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON SOURCE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon source refers to the material, such as polystyrene, used by microorganisms like gut bacteria of Tenebrio molitor for growth and energy.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene serves as a carbon source for certain bacterial strains, such as NyZ450, during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene serves as a carbon source for mealworm larvae and their gut bacteria, although it is insufficient for larval growth and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) serves as a carbon source for Tenebrio molitor larvae during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene serves as a carbon source for bacteria like Klebsiella sp. WJ2020 and Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025, enabling their growth and degradation activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECALCITRANT SUBSTRATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Recalcitrant substrates, like polyethylene, are difficult to degrade due to their stable chemical structure, requiring specialized microbial activity for breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS LICHENIFORMIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus licheniformis is a bacterium that produces biosurfactants, aiding in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus licheniformis is a bacterium used in the biodegradation of polyethylene waste by producing biosurfactants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYSINIBACILLUS FUSIFORMIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lysinibacillus fusiformis is a bacterium involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZOPHOBAS ATRATA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zophobas atrata, also known as superworms, are larvae capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atrata, or superworms, are larvae capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene and polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE MULCHES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene mulches are widely used in agriculture but contribute to environmental pollution due to their persistence and difficulty in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE MULCHES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradable mulches are alternatives to polyethylene mulches, designed to break down more easily in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FILAMENTOUS FUNGI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Filamentous fungi are a group of fungi capable of degrading low-density polyethylene, playing a role in plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Filamentous fungi are microorganisms capable of utilizing chemically oxidized polystyrene as a co-substrate, aiding in its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Filamentous fungi are a group of fungi that can utilize chemically oxidized polystyrene as a co-substrate, contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXO-BIODEGRADABLE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxo-biodegradable plastics are designed to degrade more quickly than traditional plastics but still pose environmental challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAZILIAN CERRADO SOIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Brazilian Cerrado soil contains microbial communities with potential for biodegrading unpretreated polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial enzymes are biological catalysts that play a role in the recycling of recalcitrant petroleum-based plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial enzymes are enzymes produced by microorganisms that can degrade recalcitrant petroleum-based plastics, offering potential solutions for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial enzymes are enzymes produced by microorganisms that can degrade recalcitrant petroleum-based plastics, offering potential solutions for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALK B CLONED RECOMBINANT CELL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">AlkB cloned recombinant cell is a genetically modified organism capable of degrading polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A soil bacterium capable of degrading polyethylene, as studied in biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JOURNAL OF BIOREMEDIATION AND BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Journal of Bioremediation and Biodegradation publishes research on the breakdown of pollutants by biological processes, including plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL MODIFICATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa</data>
      <data key="d1">ATR-FTIR analysis detects chemical modifications on PE mulching films after bacterial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical modification involves changes in the molecular structure of plastics, such as the incorporation of oxygen, during degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
      <data key="d1">Achatina fulica contributes to the biodegradation of expanded polyethylene, highlighting its role in environmental cleanup.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polyethylene is a foam plastic that contributes to environmental pollution and is often found in waste streams.</data>
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAX MOTH LARVAE (GALLERIA MELLONELLA)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae of the greater wax moth (Galleria mellonella) are macro-organisms that have shown potential in accelerating the biodegradation of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TECHNO-ECONOMIC ANALYSIS (TEA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Techno-economic analysis (TEA) is a method used to evaluate the economic feasibility and technological prospects of biodegradation processes, such as those involving mealworms and wax moth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Techno-economic analysis evaluates the costs and potential revenue streams of converting mealworm remains into biodiesel as part of a waste treatment process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIFE CYCLE ASSESSMENT (LCA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Life cycle assessment (LCA) is a method used to assess the environmental impacts of biodegradation processes, such as those involving mealworms and wax moth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Life cycle assessment (LCA) is a methodology used to evaluate the environmental impacts of products or processes, such as the production of biofuels or mealworms as a protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYHYDROXYALKANOATES (PHA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA) are biodegradable polymers derived from renewable resources, often requiring specific conditions for degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYCAPROLACTONE (PCL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polycaprolactone (PCL) is a fossil-based biodegradable polymer that degrades under specific conditions, often used in medical applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYBUTADIENE (PBD)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polybutadiene (PBD) is an unsaturated polyolefin used in synthetic rubber production, resistant to biodegradation due to its chemical structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANDIDA TROPICALIS ATCC 20962">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Candida tropicalis ATCC 20962 is a yeast strain reported to functionalize smaller, liquid alkyl chains, contributing to biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIO-REMEDIATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Bio-remediation refers to the use of living organisms, such as mealworms and wax moth larvae, to degrade environmental pollutants like plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical recycling involves the use of biological processes to break down synthetic polymers like polyethylene into valuable chemicals, such as ethylene glycol.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAX MOTH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Galleria mellonella, also known as the greater wax moth, is an insect capable of biodegrading polystyrene and styrofoam, with its gut microbiome playing a significant role in this process.&lt;SEP&gt;The greater wax moth (Galleria mellonella) is an insect whose larvae have been observed to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) films, creating holes and causing mass loss in the plastic substrate.&lt;SEP&gt;The greater wax moth (Galleria mellonella) is an insect whose larvae have been studied for their ability to degrade polyethylene, potentially aiding in bioremediation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The greater wax moth (Galleria mellonella) is an organism studied for its ability to chew and degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), contributing to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The greater wax moth is an organism used in experiments to study the digestion of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) films, contributing to plastic waste treatment research.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater wax moth (Galleria mellonella) larvae can completely digest and biodegrade polystyrene microplastics, independent of gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella, commonly known as the greater wax moth, is a species whose larvae can completely digest and biodegrade polystyrene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater Wax Moth (WM) is an insect species from the Lepidoptera order, known for consuming bee wax and potentially metabolizing plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater wax moth (Galleria mellonella) is an insect larva studied for its gut microbiome changes and potential role in polystyrene digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater wax moth is an insect species whose gut microbiome changes in response to polystyrene feeding, showing an increase in community diversity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TECHNO-ECONOMIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Techno-economic analysis (TEA) is a method used to assess the economic feasibility of scaling up plastic biodegradation processes for waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOMBELLI ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bombelli et al. conducted research on the biodegradation of polyethylene by larvae of the greater wax moth, though their results were later questioned for methodological issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEBER ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weber et al. raised methodological concerns about the results of Bombelli et al., questioning the presence of ethylene glycol and mass loss in polyethylene degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRANDON ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Brandon et al. conducted research showing that yellow mealworms could convert ingested polyethylene (PE) into CO2 with a conversion rate of 42.0 ± 1.4% within 32 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon et al. published work on the degradation of polyethylene and polystyrene mixtures by mealworms, though their mass balances were not fully closed.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon et al. investigated the biodegradation of polyethylene and plastic mixtures in mealworms and their effects on the gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CASSONE ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cassone et al. studied changes in the intestinal microbiome of greater wax moth larvae fed polyethylene, identifying Acinetobacter as a key genus in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KUNDUNGAL ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kundungal et al. demonstrated the ability of lesser waxworm larvae to degrade high-density polyethylene under various conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang et al. conducted research on the mineralization of polystyrene by superworms (Zophobas atratus), showing higher degradation rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. studied the biodegradation of polystyrene wastes in yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and factors affecting biodegradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER-TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is an analytical method used to detect chemical changes, such as the presence of ethylene glycol, in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLING FILM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cling film, made of low-density polyethylene (LDPE), is a common plastic material used in packaging and is studied for its biodegradation by insects like Galleria mellonella and Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLACK LDPE SHOPPING BAG">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">A black LDPE shopping bag is a type of plastic waste used in experiments to study its degradation by wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIQUID PARAFFIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Liquid paraffin is a chemical used in experiments as a substrate to study the biodegradation capabilities of insect homogenates.&lt;SEP&gt;Liquid paraffin is a chemical used in experiments to study the biodegradation of plastics when mixed with homogenate paste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOMOGENATE PASTE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Homogenate paste is a mixture created by grinding insects like Galleria mellonella and Tenebrio molitor, used to study their enzymatic activity in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Homogenate paste is a mixture derived from greater wax moth larvae, used in experiments to study the degradation of LDPE films.&lt;SEP&gt;Homogenate paste is a mixture derived from organisms like mealworms and greater wax moth larvae, used in experiments to study biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Homogenate paste is a mixture used in experiments to study the degradation of LDPE, though it showed negligible effects in short time frames.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR-ATR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR-ATR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy with Attenuated Total Reflectance) is a technique used to analyze the chemical changes in plastic substrates after exposure to insects or their homogenates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAX MOTH LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Greater Wax Moth larvae (Galleria mellonella) are known to develop biodegradation mechanisms to break down polymer branches, producing smaller molecules and CO2.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella L.) are capable of biodegrading polyethylene and polystyrene, with their gut microbiome playing a key role.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater wax moth larvae are organisms studied for their ability to degrade plastics, particularly LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Greater wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella) can completely digest and biodegrade polystyrene microplastics, with evidence of gut microbiota independence and potential metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETONITRILE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetonitrile is a solvent used in the mobile phase for liquid chromatography, often containing formic acid for metabolomic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Acetonitrile is a solvent used in the mobile phase for liquid chromatography, often containing formic acid for metabolomic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Acetonitrile is a solvent used to wash LDPE films after biodegradation experiments to remove homogenate paste residues.&lt;SEP&gt;Acetonitrile is an LC-MS grade chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Acetonitrile is used as part of the mobile phase in the UHPLC system for lipid analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGEN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygen is maintained at atmospheric levels in biodegradation experiments to ensure microbial activity during the degradation of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIVE HUMIDITY">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Relative humidity is controlled in biodegradation experiments to maintain optimal conditions for microbial activity and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR-MICROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR-microscopy is a technique used to analyze the surface of LDPE films after biodegradation experiments, focusing on chemical changes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLUESENS BLUEINONE FERM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bluesens BlueInOne Ferm is a solid-state fermenter used to control oxygen levels and relative humidity during biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GWP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Global Warming Potential (GWP) is a metric used to estimate the environmental impact of processes, such as converting mealworm lipids to biodiesel.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODIESEL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodiesel can be produced from mealworms grown on synthetic polymer diets, offering an environmental use for insect fats.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodiesel is a renewable fuel derived from biological sources, such as grease from yellow mealworm beetles, explored as a potential feedstock.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodiesel is a renewable fuel derived from lipids, including those sourced from mealworm remains, and is used as an alternative to traditional diesel.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodiesel is a renewable fuel that can be produced from lipids extracted from mealworms, potentially offsetting fossil fuel use.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIFE CYCLE IMPACT ASSESSMENT (LCIA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Life cycle impact assessment is a method used to evaluate the environmental impacts of treating plastic waste using mealworms, including greenhouse gas emissions and energy consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXED PLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixed plastic waste consists of various types of plastic materials that are treated by mealworms, with the potential to be converted into biodiesel.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCUM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Scum is a waste material collected from wastewater treatment facilities that can be processed into biodiesel, contributing to waste valorization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTEWATER TREATMENT FACILITIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Wastewater treatment facilities are sites where sewage sludge and scum are processed, with potential for converting these materials into biodiesel.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW S DIESEL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low S diesel is a type of diesel fuel with low sulfur content, which can be offset by biodiesel produced from mealworm remains and other waste materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREENHOUSE GAS EMISSIONS (GHG)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Greenhouse gas emissions are environmental pollutants released during the treatment of plastic waste and the production of biodiesel, contributing to global warming.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MONTE CARLO SIMULATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Monte Carlo simulation is a statistical method used to estimate the variability and uncertainty in energy, cost, and environmental metrics for treating mixed plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRADLE-TO-GATE GWP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cradle-to-gate GWP refers to the greenhouse gas emissions associated with the production and treatment of mixed plastic waste, including mealworm digestion and biodiesel production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATURAL GAS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Natural gas is a fossil fuel used in the energy inputs for rearing mealworms and treating plastic waste, contributing to greenhouse gas emissions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELECTRICITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Electricity is an energy source required for rearing mealworms and treating plastic waste, with associated greenhouse gas emissions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUGITIVE GAS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fugitive gas refers to the CO2 emissions released during the digestion of mixed plastic waste by mealworms, contributing to greenhouse gas emissions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW S DIESEL OFFSET">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Low S diesel offset refers to the environmental benefit of replacing traditional diesel with biodiesel produced from mealworm remains and other waste materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE FILM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE film is a type of plastic material used in experiments to study its degradation by greater wax moth larvae and mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE SUBSTRATE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene substrate refers to the plastic material used in experiments to assess its degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEWING PATTERNS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Chewing patterns refer to the visible marks on LDPE substrates caused by greater wax moth larvae, indicating physical degradation of the plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Infrared spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze the chemical changes in LDPE substrates after degradation by greater wax moth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Infrared spectroscopy is a tool used to analyze changes in the chemical structure of HDPE during biodegradation, identifying functional groups such as carbonyl and ether groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Infrared spectroscopy is a tool used to analyze changes in the chemical structure of HDPE during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MECHANICAL WEAR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mechanical wear refers to the physical damage caused to LDPE substrates by greater wax moth larvae, which may be mistaken for biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical wear refers to the physical degradation of LDPE caused by chewing and ingestion by organisms like the greater wax moth, rather than biochemical degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLIC GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylic groups are chemical structures that may form during the biochemical oxidation of polyethylene, as indicated by FTIR spectroscopy.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRANDON ET AL. (2018)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Brandon et al. (2018) is a study that reported degradation rates of LDPE by mealworms, providing insights into the mass balance and energy uptake during the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon et al. (2018) studied the effects of nutrient-poor, plastic-only diets on mealworms, linking low survival and fertility rates to insufficient protein production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOMBELLI ET AL. (2017)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bombelli et al. (2017) is a study that reported rapid degradation rates of LDPE by waxworms, though these findings have been questioned by later research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEBER ET AL. (2017)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weber et al. (2017) suggested that earlier rapid degradation rates of LDPE may be due to mechanical wear rather than biochemical degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL. (2019)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang et al. (2019) demonstrated the role of gut bacteria in the biodegradation of polyethylene by mealworms and waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CASSONE ET AL. (2020)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cassone et al. (2020) questioned the ubiquity of earlier observations on LDPE degradation by waxworms, suggesting variations in experimental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL. (2014)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang et al. (2014) discovered that bacteria isolated from the gut of waxworms can degrade polyethylene, though at lower rates than previously reported.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TECHNO-ECONOMIC FEASIBILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A research study evaluating the feasibility of using larvae for plastic waste management, concluding that it is not economically viable due to high energy requirements and low value creation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMPOSTING/DIGESTION INSTALLATIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Composting or digestion installations are systems that could be inoculated with microbial cultures to mitigate plastic anomalies in food waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODIESEL PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodiesel production from larvae is a potential application, though the yield of lipids is not directly caused by plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE MOIETIES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane moieties are chemical structures that could be targeted for functionalization during plastic degradation, potentially yielding high-value molecules.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX MOTH CATERPILLARS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax moth caterpillars (Galleria mellonella) are known for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, offering a potential solution for plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax moth caterpillars (Galleria mellonella) are organisms known for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, a common plastic material.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax moth caterpillars (Galleria mellonella) are known for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, offering a potential solution for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIRCULAR ORGANICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Circular Organics is an organization contributing to research on sustainable solutions for plastic waste, including biodegradation by organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AROMATIC POLYESTERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aromatic polyesterase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of aromatic polyesters, a type of plastic, as studied in environmental research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANDIDA TROPICALIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Candida tropicalis is a microorganism studied for its role in the conversion of fatty acids and alkanes, which may have implications for plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESSER WAX-WORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The lesser wax-worm (Achroia griseola) is an organism capable of efficiently biodegrading polyethylene (PE) waste, contributing to environmental remediation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOFUELS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biofuels are renewable energy sources derived from organic materials, such as vegetable oils, and are studied for their environmental and economic impacts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VEGETABLE OIL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vegetable oil is a chemical used in the production of biofuels and is studied for its energy content and environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCUM-TO-BIODIESEL TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scum-to-biodiesel technology is a process studied for converting wastewater scum into biodiesel, evaluated through life cycle assessment and economic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DANDORA DUMPSITE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Dandora dumpsite is a location in Nairobi, Kenya, where bacteria and fungi capable of biodegrading polyethylene have been identified.&lt;SEP&gt;Dandora dumpsite is a location where studies on the biodegradability of polyethylene by fungi have been conducted.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTHROPOGENIC RADIATIVE FORCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Anthropogenic radiative forcing refers to the impact of human activities on climate change, studied in the context of environmental management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISO 14040">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ISO 14040 is a standard for life cycle assessment, providing principles and frameworks for evaluating environmental impacts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JAMBECK ET AL., 2015">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Jambeck et al., 2015 is a study that quantifies plastic waste inputs from land into the ocean, highlighting the global environmental concern of plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KUNDUGAL ET AL., 2019">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kundugal et al., 2019 is a study that investigates the biodegradation of polyethylene by the lesser wax-worm, Achroia griseola.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEHTA AND ANAND, 2009">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mehta and Anand, 2009 is a study that estimates the lower heating value of vegetable oil and biodiesel fuel, contributing to biofuel research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MU ET AL., 2016">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mu et al., 2016 is a study that evaluates the life cycle assessment and economic analysis of scum-to-biodiesel technology in wastewater treatment plants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUHONJA ET AL., 2018">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Muhonja et al., 2018 is a study that examines the biodegradability of polyethylene by fungi from the Dandora dumpsite in Nairobi, Kenya.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MYHRE ET AL., 2013">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Myhre et al., 2013 is a study that assesses anthropogenic and natural radiative forcing, contributing to climate change research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OONINCX AND DE BOER, 2012">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Oonincx and de Boer, 2012 is a study that evaluates the environmental impact of mealworm production as a protein source for humans using life cycle assessment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy recovery refers to the incineration of residues from plastic degradation by larvae, with the potential to recover energy.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONALIZATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane moieties could be targeted for functionalization during plastic degradation, potentially yielding high-value molecules.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLIMATE CHANGE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
      <data key="d1">Anthropogenic radiative forcing is a key concept in climate change research, highlighting the impact of human activities.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodepolymerization is the process by which mealworms break down complex polymers like plastics and polystyrene into simpler compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AVENA SATIVA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Avena sativa, commonly known as oats, was used as an energy source in feeding experiments with mealworms to study their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE PAPERS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste papers are discarded paper materials that contribute to environmental pollution and were studied alongside plastics and polystyrene in the mealworm biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGHT INTENSITY">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d1">Light intensity is an environmental factor studied to understand its impact on the biodegradation process of plastics and polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Light intensity is monitored in the study as it affects the activity and growth of mealworms during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOISTURE CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Moisture content is a key factor analyzed in the study to assess its influence on the biodegradation of plastics and polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Moisture content is measured gravimetrically to identify differences in the diets of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Moisture content is measured in the larval biomass and frass to assess the effects of hydration and diet composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY SOURCES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy sources, such as oats (Avena sativa), were used in feeding experiments to study their effect on the biodegradation capabilities of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLONY FORMING UNITS (CFU)">
      <data key="d0">BIOLOGICAL MEASURE</data>
      <data key="d1">Colony forming units (CFU) are used to quantify the number of microorganisms present in the gut of mealworms, which are essential for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPERIMENTAL SETUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The experimental setup involves tanks and plastic containers with mealworms, designed to study the biodegradation of plastics under controlled conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOCOL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermocol is a form of polystyrene used in packaging and insulation, included in the study for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermocol is a type of expanded polystyrene plastic waste that can be biodegraded by mealworms, with 50% degradation achieved in 15 days with nutrients.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermocol is a type of expanded polystyrene used in packaging and insulation, which can be biodegraded by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrients are substances that support the growth and biodegradation activities of mealworms, enhancing their ability to degrade thermocol and Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrients such as oats, apple slices, carrots, and potatoes are provided to mealworms to enhance their growth and biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLOOD AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Blood Agar is a nutrient medium used for culturing bacteria, composed of peptone, beef extract/yeast extract, agar, NaCl, distilled water, and sheep blood, with a pH range of 7.2 to 7.6.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood agar is a culture medium used to isolate and grow bacteria from the gut of mealworms for further study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STERILIZED EQUIPMENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sterilized equipment, including surgical knives, loops, gloves, and petri dishes, is used to prepare and culture bacteria from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOSAFETY CHAMBER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A biosafety chamber is used to maintain a sterile environment during the culturing of bacteria from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIOLOGICAL INCUBATOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A bacteriological incubator is used to maintain a constant temperature for culturing bacteria from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MACCONKEY AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MacConkey Agar is a selective and differential culture medium used to isolate and differentiate bacteria, particularly those present in the excreta of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;MacConkey Agar is a selective and differential medium used to isolate and identify Gram-negative bacteria, particularly from mealworm excreta.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARRY BAGS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Carry bags are plastic waste materials that can be biodegraded by mealworms, with higher efficiency observed when nutrients are present.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC BOTTLES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic bottles are a type of plastic waste that can be biodegraded by mealworms, though with lower efficiency compared to Styrofoam and carry bags.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SUBTILIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus subtilis is a bacterial species known for its role in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus subtilis is a bacterial strain known for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus subtilis is a bacterium present in the gut of mealworms, contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus subtilis is a soil bacterium that has been shown to degrade plastic waste, including PS foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHEEP BLOOD">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sheep blood is a component of Blood Agar, used to provide nutrients for bacterial growth in culture media.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEPTONE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peptone is a protein derivative used in nutrient media like Blood Agar to support bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEEF EXTRACT/YEAST EXTRACT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Beef extract and yeast extract are nutrient sources used in culture media to promote bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NACL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium chloride (NaCl) is an inorganic salt used in the preparation of culture medium and saline solution for bacterial growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium chloride (NaCl) is used in culture media to maintain osmotic balance and support bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISTILLED WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Distilled water is used as a solvent in the preparation of culture media to ensure purity and consistency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT AGAR POWDER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient agar powder is a dehydrated form of nutrient medium used for culturing bacteria, containing peptone, beef extract, and agar.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AUTOCLAVE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Autoclaving is a sterilization process used to prepare culture media by heating at 121°C for 15 minutes to eliminate contaminants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL PARAMETERS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental parameters such as temperature, humidity, and light intensity influence the mealworms' intake capacity and growth during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental parameters such as temperature, humidity, and light intensity significantly influence the biodegradation efficiency of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVA (II STAGE)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The second stage of mealworm development, during which biodegradation of thermocol and Styrofoam occurs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPA (III STAGE)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The third stage of mealworm development, during which biodegradation efficiency decreases as the larvae transition to pupae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DR. S. PRADEEP KUMAR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Dr. S. Pradeep Kumar is the supervisor of the research study on the biodegradation of thermocol and Styrofoam by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VNRVJET">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">VNRVJET is the institution where the research on mealworm biodegradation of thermocol and Styrofoam was conducted.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS CLAVATUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus clavatus is a fungal strain isolated from landfill soil, known for its ability to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus clavatus is a fungal strain isolated from landfill soil, known for its ability to degrade low-density polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus clavatus strain JASK1 is a fungus isolated from landfill soil that has the ability to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOLYTE PS POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ecolyte PS Polystyrene is a type of polystyrene that undergoes biooxidation, a process studied in the Department of Chemistry.&lt;SEP&gt;Ecolyte PS polystyrene is a type of polystyrene that has been studied for its biodegradability, particularly through biooxidation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ecolyte PS polystyrene is a type of polystyrene that has been studied for its biodegradability under specific conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Ecolyte PS polystyrene is a specific type of polystyrene that has been modified for photodegradation and is studied for its biodegradability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEGRADABLE STARCH-POLYETHYLENE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Degradable starch-polyethylene plastics are materials designed to break down in compost environments, studied for their biodegradation properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC ACCUMULATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic accumulation refers to the buildup of plastic debris in specific areas, such as the North Atlantic Subtropical Gyre, causing environmental concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic accumulation refers to the buildup of plastic waste in environments such as the North Atlantic subtropical gyre, posing significant ecological threats.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JAMECK J R ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Jambeck et al. conducted a study on plastic waste inputs from land into the ocean, highlighting the global issue of marine plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JOHNSON K E ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Johnson et al. studied the degradation of degradable starch-polyethylene plastics in a compost environment, focusing on microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KAPLAN D V ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kaplan et al. investigated the biodegradation of polystyrene, poly(methyl methacrylate), and phenol formaldehyde, contributing to the understanding of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KATHIRESAN K.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kathiresan studied polythene and plastic-degrading microbes in Indian mangrove soil, emphasizing the role of microorganisms in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KYAW B M ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kyaw et al. researched the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) by Pseudomonas species, highlighting microbial degradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAW K L ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Law et al. studied plastic accumulation in the North Atlantic Subtropical Gyre, addressing the environmental impact of plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BALFOUR C E AND CARMICHAEL L.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Balfour and Carmichael studied the light reactions of Tenebrio molitor (mealworms), contributing to early research on insect behavior.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KAMAR H M ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kamar et al. investigated the effects of medical staff movements on airflow and particle concentration in operating rooms, focusing on environmental control.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARCH-POLYETHYLENE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Starch-polyethylene plastics are biodegradable materials designed to break down in compost environments, reducing plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDIAN MANGROVE SOIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Indian mangrove soil is studied for its microbial communities capable of degrading polythene and plastics, highlighting natural biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CULTURE MEDIA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
      <data key="d1">Autoclaving is used to sterilize culture media, ensuring they are free from contaminants.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOOXIDATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b</data>
      <data key="d1">Biooxidation is a process by which microorganisms break down synthetic polymers, such as Ecolyte PS polystyrene, into simpler compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Ecolyte PS Polystyrene undergoes biooxidation, a process studied to understand its degradation in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OCEAN">
