      <data key="d1">Plastic waste from land enters the ocean, contributing to marine pollution.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMPOST ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
      <data key="d1">Degradable starch-polyethylene plastics are designed to break down in compost environments, reducing their environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NORTH ATLANTIC SUBTROPICAL GYRE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic accumulation in the North Atlantic Subtropical Gyre highlights the issue of marine plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OPERATING ROOM ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
      <data key="d1">Kamar et al. investigated the effects of medical staff movements on airflow and particle concentration in operating rooms, focusing on environmental control.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-STATE 13C CP/MAS NMR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Solid-state 13C CP/MAS NMR spectroscopy is a method used to study the chemical structure and degradation of polystyrene in the superworms' guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TG-FTIR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TG-FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze the thermal degradation and chemical changes in polystyrene during the biodegradation process by superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESPIROMETRY TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A respirometry test was conducted to measure the conversion of ingested Styrofoam carbon into CO2, demonstrating the mineralization process by superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Respirometry tests are used to measure CO2 production by superworms during the biodegradation of Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC SUPPRESSION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic suppression is a method used to inhibit gut microbiota in mealworms to study their role in PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression is a method used to inhibit gut microbiota in mealworms to study their role in PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression is a method used to study the role of gut microbes in the biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression is used to study the role of gut microbes in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression is used to study the role of gut microbes in PET biodegradation, revealing that the host can degrade PET independently.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression refers to the use of antibiotics to inhibit gut microbiota, which affects the biodegradation of certain plastics like PS and PVC but has less impact on PE and PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression refers to the use of antibiotics to inhibit gut microbiota, which was shown to reduce the polystyrene-degrading capability of superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression is a method used to study the role of gut microbes in the biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression is used to study the role of gut microbes in PET biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression is used to study the role of gut microbes in PET biodegradation, revealing that the host can degrade PET independently.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression refers to the use of antibiotics to inhibit gut microbiota, which affects the biodegradation of certain plastics like PS and PVC but has less impact on PE and PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression refers to the use of antibiotics like gentamicin to reduce gut microbes in larvae, which helps determine the role of gut microbes in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA BARCODE OF COI GENE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The DNA barcode of the COI gene (cytochrome c oxidase subunit I) was used to analyze the genetic divergence between Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL SYMBIONTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbial symbionts are microorganisms in the gut of superworms that may play a role in the biodegradation of Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC SUPPRESSION TREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic suppression treatment is used to study the role of gut microbes in the biodegradation of Styrofoam by superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic suppression treatment is used to study the role of gut microbial symbionts in the biodegradation of Styrofoam by superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-STATE 13C NMR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Solid-state 13C nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) is used to analyze the chemical composition of Styrofoam and its degradation products in superworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESPIROMETRY TESTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Respirometry tests are conducted to determine the conversion efficiency of ingested Styrofoam into CO2 by superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RIFAMPICIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Rifampicin is an antibiotic used in combination with other antibiotics to suppress gut bacteria in superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM BLOCKS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam blocks are expanded polystyrene foam materials that superworms can consume and degrade.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An antibiotic diet containing gentamicin, rifampicin, and streptomycin is used to suppress gut bacteria in superworms, affecting their ability to degrade Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON MASS BALANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon mass balance experiments are conducted to measure the conversion of ingested Styrofoam into CO2 and frass by superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbon mass balance study investigates the conversion of ingested Styrofoam into CO2 and mealworm biomass, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon mass balance is a method used to estimate the biodegradation rate of EPS by mealworms, suggesting that 48.2% of consumed EPS is biodegraded.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon mass balance is a method used to track the distribution of carbon from consumed polystyrene into biomass, carbon dioxide, and fecula during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon mass balance is a method used to estimate the biodegradation rate of EPS by mealworms, suggesting that 48.2% of consumed EPS is biodegraded.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon mass balance is a method used to track the distribution of carbon from consumed polystyrene into biomass, carbon dioxide, and fecula during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELEMENTAL ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An elemental analyzer (Vario EL cube, Elementar, Germany) was used to determine the elemental composition of wheat bran, showing a C:H:O:N ratio of 40.1:7.7:47.3:3.4.&lt;SEP&gt;An elemental analyzer is a tool used to measure the carbon content of Styrofoam, biomass, and fecula in the study of polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;An elemental analyzer is used to determine the carbon content of Styrofoam and frass in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENT SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPE (ESEM)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An ESEM is used to observe the morphology of microorganisms dwelling on the gut walls of superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;ESEM is used to observe the morphology of microorganisms in the midgut contents of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass analysis involves examining the waste material excreted by superworms to study the degradation of Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW MOLECULAR WEIGHT PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low molecular weight products are generated during the degradation of Styrofoam in the guts of superworms, indicating the breakdown of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM-EATING SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam-eating superworms are a group of superworms that consume Styrofoam as their diet, showing no significant difference in survival rate compared to those on a normal diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN-EATING SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran-eating superworms are a control group of superworms fed a normal diet of bran, used for comparison with Styrofoam-eating superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF-EXTRACT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">THF-extract refers to the tetrahydrofuran-extracted fraction of frass or Styrofoam, used for chemical analysis to determine degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPC CHROMATOGRAMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GPC chromatograms are used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polymers, such as Styrofoam and its degradation products in frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C CP/MAS NMR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">13C CP/MAS NMR is a solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance technique used to analyze the chemical composition of materials like Styrofoam and frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYL DERIVATIVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenyl derivatives are low molecular weight degradation products generated during the degradation of Styrofoam in the guts of superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenyl derivatives are smaller molecules produced during the depolymerization and oxidation of polystyrene, detected in the fecula of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC SUPPRESSION ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An antibiotic suppression assay was conducted to determine the role of gut microbiota in the biodegradation of polystyrene by superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON MONOXIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon monoxide is a gaseous compound evolved during the thermal decomposition of lower molecular weight degradation products of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TG/DTG CURVE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TG/DTG curves are used to analyze the thermal degradation behavior of materials, such as Styrofoam and frass, to study weight loss and decomposition products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3D FTIR PROFILES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">3D FTIR profiles are used to identify evolved gaseous compounds during the thermal decomposition of materials, such as styrene, carbon monoxide, and carbon dioxide.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANDIBULATE MOUTHPART">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mandibulate mouthparts are a physical feature of superworms and mealworms that enable them to chew and consume plastic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOMICS SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenomics sequencing is a technique proposed for studying the functional diversity of gut microbiota in superworms to identify novel enzymes for plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHORIA GRISELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achoria grisella, the lesser wax moth, is an insect that can consume and degrade polyethylene, making it a potential agent for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARANTHRENE ROBINIAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Paranthrene robiniae, the western poplar clearing borer, is an insect capable of biodegrading hardwood lignocellulosics, contributing to organic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECO-FRIENDLY SOLUTIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Research studies focus on eco-friendly solutions for plastic waste management, including the use of organisms like mealworms and bacteria for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOCELLULOSICS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
      <data key="d1">Paranthrene robiniae can biodegrade hardwood lignocellulosics, aiding in organic waste management</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP. PELW2042">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. PELW2042 is a Gram-positive bacterium isolated from mealworms, capable of degrading HDPE by forming biofilms and creating pits on its surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. PELW2042 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms, capable of biodegrading high-density polyethylene (HDPE) with high efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. PELW2042 is a bacterial strain isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading high-density polyethylene (HDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HDPE FILM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">HDPE film is a type of high-density polyethylene used in various applications, which poses environmental challenges due to its resistance to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE film is a type of plastic waste that is resistant to degradation but can be biodegraded by certain bacteria and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) film is a type of plastic used in experiments to study its biodegradation by mealworms and their gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM-INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FT-IR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier Transform-Infrared Spectroscopy (FT-IR) is used to detect the formation of carbonyl and other functional groups on HDPE film after degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS BACTERIOPHAGE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas bacteriophage is a type of virus that infects bacteria, potentially involved in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROCOCCUS ASBURIAE YT1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterococcus asburiae YT1 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of mealworms, capable of degrading polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAROOF ET AL. [26]">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Maroof et al. [26] conducted a study screening six strains of bacteria with potential biodegradation activity from a waste treatment plant in Pakistan, focusing on the degradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL. [28]">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang et al. [28] isolated Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 from the intestine of mealworms, demonstrating its role in polystyrene (PS) biodegradation and mineralization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL. [30]">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang et al. [30] found that yellow mealworms could biodegrade both polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), with higher degradation rates for PS compared to LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL. [31]">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang et al. [31] isolated two polyethylene (PE)-degrading bacterial strains, Enterobacter asburiae YT1 and Bacillus sp. YP1, from the intestine of Plodia interpunctella larvae (Indian meal moth).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YIN ET AL. [32]">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yin et al. [32] isolated two polyethylene (PE)-degrading bacteria, Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 and Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451, from the intestine of yellow mealworm larvae, demonstrating their ability to biodegrade PE when co-cultured.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLODIA INTERPUNCTELLA LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plodia interpunctella larvae, also known as Indian meal moths, are insects from which Enterobacter asburiae YT1 and Bacillus sp. YP1 were isolated for their ability to degrade polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K2HPO4">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">K2HPO4 (Potassium phosphate dibasic) is a chemical used in the preparation of culture media for bacterial growth in biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium phosphate dibasic (K2HPO4) is an inorganic salt used in the preparation of culture medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PELW2042">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PELW2042 is a Gram-positive bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms, capable of degrading HDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;PELW2042 is a Gram-positive bacterium identified as Bacillus sp., capable of degrading HDPE by forming biofilms and creating pits on its surface.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KH2PO4">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Potassium phosphate monobasic (KH2PO4) is an inorganic salt used in the preparation of culture medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MGSO4-7H2O">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Magnesium sulfate heptahydrate (MgSO4-7H2O) is an inorganic salt used in the preparation of culture medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NH4NO3">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ammonium nitrate (NH4NO3) is an inorganic salt used in the preparation of culture medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FESO4-7H2O">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ferrous sulfate heptahydrate (FeSO4-7H2O) is an inorganic salt used in the preparation of culture medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZNSO4-7H2O">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Zinc sulfate heptahydrate (ZnSO4-7H2O) is an inorganic salt used in the preparation of culture medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MNSO4-H2O">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Manganese sulfate monohydrate (MnSO4-H2O) is an inorganic salt used in the preparation of culture medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRYPTONE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tryptone is a peptone derived from casein, used as a nutrient source in LB medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YEAST EXTRACT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Yeast extract is a nutrient-rich component of the FMM medium used in biodegradation assays to support bacterial growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Yeast extract is a nutrient-rich substance derived from yeast, used in LB medium for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SALINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Saline is a solution of sodium chloride (NaCl) in water, used for bacterial suspension and preparation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16SRRNA GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The 16SrRNA gene is a component of the ribosomal RNA used for phylogenetic analysis and identification of bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The T-test is a statistical method used for pairwise comparisons in the study of mealworm gut microbiome and plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The t-test is a statistical method used for pairwise comparisons of data, such as weight loss and contact angle changes in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURFACTANTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Surfactants are substances secreted by bacteria like Bacillus sp. PELW2042 that lower the surface tension of plastics, aiding in their degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAM-POSITIVE BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gram-positive bacteria, such as Bacillus sp. PELW2042, are microorganisms that play a role in the biodegradation of plastics like HDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALMODULIN GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Calmodulin (CaM) gene sequence analysis was used to identify Bacillus sp. PELW2042 as a Gram-positive bacterium.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN PELW2042">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain PELW2042 is a Bacillus sp. isolated from the gut of mealworms, known for its high efficiency in biodegrading high-density polyethylene (HDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Strain PELW2042 is a microorganism capable of degrading HDPE, leading to changes in its chemical structure and molecular weight.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHER GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ether groups (-C-O-C-) are chemical functional groups that form during the biodegradation of HDPE, indicating structural changes in the polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DECREASE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight decrease is a key indicator of polystyrene degradation, showing the breakdown of long-chain molecules into smaller fragments.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight decrease refers to the reduction in the molecular weight of polystyrene (PS) after being degraded by strain YT2, indicating the breakdown of its polymer structure.&lt;SEP&gt;The decrease in molecular weight of HDPE is a key indicator of its biodegradation by microorganisms like strain PELW2042.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XRD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">X-ray diffraction (XRD) is a method used to characterize material properties, such as crystallinity changes in HDPE during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROXYLATING ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroxylating enzymes, such as AlkB or P450, play a role in the initial hydroxylation of polyethylene during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANOIC ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkanoic acids are produced during the biodegradation of polyethylene, resulting from the conversion of carboxyl groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMULSIFIERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Emulsifiers are substances secreted by insect larvae that mediate the biodegradation of plastics by breaking down their structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNERGISTIC EFFECTS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Synergistic effects refer to the combined action of insect larvae and their gut microbiome in accelerating the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURFACE MORPHOLOGY CHANGES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Changes in surface morphology, such as the formation of pits and cavities, are observed during the biodegradation of plastics by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHOBICITY REDUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Reduction in hydrophobicity is a key change observed during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating the breakdown of their chemical structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYSTALLINITY REDUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Reduction in crystallinity is a structural change observed during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating the breakdown of their molecular order.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste plastics are discarded plastic materials that contribute to environmental pollution and are a focus of biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Waste plastics, including polypropylene (PP), are environmental pollutants that can be remediated through biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AQUATIC ENVIRONMENTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Aquatic environments are ecosystems affected by microplastics and nanoplastics, which can aggregate, deposit, and enhance the transport of contaminants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE MICROPLASTIC DEBRIS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine microplastic debris is an emerging issue affecting food security, food safety, and human health due to its presence in marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNTHETIC FIBERS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Synthetic fibers are a source of microplastics in the environment, found in atmospheric fallout and contributing to pollution in indoor and outdoor environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GORGAN BAY">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gorgan Bay in the Caspian Sea is a site where microplastics have been found in sediments, fishes, and benthic organisms, indicating widespread pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COASTAL LAGOONS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Coastal lagoons across the globe are affected by microplastic pollution in water, sediments, and organisms, as highlighted in systematic reviews.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLATEAU CULTIVATED LAND">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plateau cultivated land in Yunnan Province, China, has been studied for the abundance and distribution characteristics of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAWAL LAKE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Rawal Lake in Pakistan has been identified as a site with environmental exposure to microplastics in sediments and water.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL-GROUNDWATER ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The soil-groundwater environment is affected by microplastics, which undergo aging, migration, and co-transport of contaminants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE FOOD WEB">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The marine food web is impacted by the trophic transfer of microplastics and mixed contaminants, posing risks to human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAXMOTH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The greater waxmoth (Galleria mellonella) is an insect known for its ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene films, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;The greater waxmoth is an organism studied for its role in the enhanced biodegradation and mineralization of low-density polyethylene films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C-LABELED PS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">13C-labeled PS is a form of polystyrene used in tracer studies to track its biodegradation and mineralization by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene (PS) is a chemically modified form of polystyrene used in experiments to trace the assimilation and degradation of PS by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene (PS) is used as a tracer in experiments to study the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene is a tracer used in studies to track the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene is used in experiments to trace the carbon flow during the biodegradation process by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene is used in tracer experiments to study the mineralization and biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled PS is used in research studies to trace the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene is used in studies to trace the biodegradation and mineralization process of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled PS is a tracer used to study the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene (PS) by microorganisms like strain YT2.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene is a tracer used in experiments to study the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene is used in experiments to trace the degradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene is used in studies to trace the biodegradation and mineralization process of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter sp. is a bacterial genus identified in the gut of Tenebrio molitor, contributing to the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from waste dumpsites, known for its ability to biodegrade unpretreated low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRICHODERMA HARZIANUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Trichoderma harzianum is a fungal species capable of degrading polyethylene, as evidenced by SEM, FTIR, and NMR analyses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHROMOBACTERIUM VIOLACEUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Chromobacterium violaceum is a bacterial species capable of forming biofilms and contributing to the biocorrosion of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine bacteria are microorganisms found in pelagic waters that can degrade low-density polyethylene, aiding in the reduction of marine plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine bacteria from the Arabian Sea are capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE), aiding in the reduction of marine plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine bacteria from the Arabian Sea have been found to biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), contributing to the reduction of plastic pollution in marine environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine bacteria from the Arabian Sea have been studied for their ability to degrade low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P450-DRIVEN SYNTHETIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">P450-driven synthetic bacteria are engineered microorganisms designed to degrade plastics, including polyethylene and polystyrene, using P450 enzymes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS CLAVATUS STRAIN JASK1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus clavatus strain JASK1 is a fungal strain isolated from landfill soil, capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus clavatus strain JASK1 is a fungal strain isolated from landfill soil, known for its ability to degrade low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE PLASTIC MULCH FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradable plastic mulch films are used in agriculture but can still contribute to environmental pollution if not properly managed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYME DISCOVERY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzyme discovery focuses on identifying enzymes involved in the biodegradation of plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzyme discovery refers to the identification of enzymes that can degrade plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, aiding in biodegradation efforts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL DETERIORATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial deterioration refers to the breakdown of materials, such as plastics, by microorganisms, contributing to biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROCOCCUS LUTEUS STRAIN CGR112">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Micrococcus luteus strain CGR112 is a bacterium isolated from cow dung, known for its biofilm-forming ability and potential to degrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LANDFILL SOIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Landfill soil is a source of microorganisms, such as Aspergillus clavatus strain JASK1, that can degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COW DUNG">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cow dung is a source of bacteria, such as Micrococcus luteus strain CGR112, that can degrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARABIAN SEA">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Arabian Sea is a marine environment where bacteria capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE) have been identified.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CULTURE MEDIUM">
      <data key="d2">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
      <data key="d1">K2HPO4 is used in the preparation of culture media for bacterial growth in biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;K2HPO4 is used in the preparation of the inorganic salt medium for bacterial growth</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM-FEEDING LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam-feeding larvae refer to mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) that are fed Styrofoam as their sole diet, demonstrating their ability to degrade polystyrene efficiently.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-STATE $^{13}$C CP/MAS NMR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Solid-state $^{13}$C cross-polarization/magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (CP/MAS NMR) spectroscopy is used to characterize the chemical structure of polystyrene degradation products in mealworm fecula.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED (TG-FTIR) SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermogravimetric Fourier transform infrared (TG-FTIR) spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze the thermal degradation and chemical composition of polystyrene in mealworm fecula.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\ALPHA$ $^{13}$C-LABELED PS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">$\alpha$ $^{13}$C-labeled polystyrene is a tracer used in isotopic tests to confirm the mineralization of polystyrene into $^{13}$CO2 by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\BETA$ $^{13}$C-LABELED PS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">$\beta$ $^{13}$C-labeled polystyrene is a tracer used in isotopic tests to confirm the incorporation of polystyrene into lipids by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHITE POLLUTANTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">White pollutants refer to dispersed polystyrene debris in the environment, contributing to global environmental concerns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANDIBULATE INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mandibulate insects are capable of chewing and eating plastic packages, including polyvinyl chloride (PVC), but their ability to degrade polystyrene is limited.&lt;SEP&gt;Mandibulate insects, such as mealworms, are capable of chewing and eating plastic, contributing to the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM-FEEDING MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms that are fed Styrofoam as their sole diet, used in studies to investigate the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPC (GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">GPC is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polymers, such as polystyrene residues from larval frass.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC is a technique used to determine the molecular weights and distributions of degraded Styrofoam products in mealworm fecula.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC is an analytical technique used to assess changes in the molecular weight and distribution of polystyrene during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CP/MAS NMR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cross-polarization/magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (CP/MAS NMR) is used to analyze the chemical structure of degraded Styrofoam products in mealworm fecula.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR (FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR is an analytical tool used to identify functional groups in plastic residues extracted from frass during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is used in conjunction with TGA to analyze the chemical composition of degraded Styrofoam products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON BALANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon balance refers to the estimation of Styrofoam loss, fecula residues, CO2, and biomass in batch Styrofoam-feeding trials, used to study the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon balance studies estimate the conversion of ingested Styrofoam into CO2 and mealworm biomass, providing quantitative data on biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon balance refers to the measurement of carbon distribution during the biodegradation of plastics, such as polystyrene, by organisms like mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13CO2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">13CO2 is a carbon dioxide isotope used in tracer experiments to study the mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOTOPE RATIO MASS SPECTROMETRY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Isotope ratio mass spectrometry is used to analyze the isotopic composition of carbon in 13CO2, providing data on the mineralization process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYOPHILIZATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lyophilization is a freeze-drying process used to prepare mealworm bodies for lipid extraction and further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Lyophilization is a freeze-drying process used to preserve the larval biomass for nutrient concentration analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Lyophilization is a process used to euthanize and prepare larvae for biomass composition analysis in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROFORM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloroform (CHCl3) is a chlorinated solvent used in the extraction of organic compounds from frass and larvae gut tissue during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform (CHCl3) is a solvent used in the extraction process of gut intestine tissue for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform is a chemical solvent used in the extraction of lipids from mealworm bodies during the analysis of polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform is a solvent used to extract lipids from lyophilized mealworm bodies for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform is a solvent used in the extraction of nanoplastics, mixed with isopropanol for separation.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform is a chemical solvent used to dissolve polystyrene film in the preparation of EPS solution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C-LABELED PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">13C-labeled products are used in experiments to trace the metabolic processes and degradation pathways of polystyrene in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY (GC)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gas chromatography is a technique used to detect and quantify gases like CH4 and CO2 produced during bacterial degradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Gas chromatography is a technique used to separate and analyze individual fatty acids derived from the degradation of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMBUSTION-ISOTOPE RATIO MASS SPECTROMETRY (GC-C-IRMS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GC-C-IRMS is a method used to analyze the isotopic composition of fatty acids, providing insights into the metabolic pathways of polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEOH/NAOH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MeOH/NaOH is a chemical mixture used in the methylation process of lipids extracted from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEOH/ETHYL ETHER-BORON TRIFLUORIDE (MEBT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MEBT is a chemical mixture used in the methylation of fatty acids extracted from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-HEXANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">N-hexane is a solvent used to extract methylated derivatives of fatty acids for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACIDS (FAS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acids (FAs) are biomarkers analyzed to determine carbon assimilation in mealworms fed with 13C-labeled polystyrene or bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acids are organic compounds derived from lipids, analyzed to understand the metabolic changes during polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acids are important energy sources and components of cell membranes, with their metabolism being enriched in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acids are important energy sources and components of cell membranes, with their metabolism being enriched in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal analysis is a method used to study the chemical composition changes in polystyrene and its degradation products by analyzing weight loss and gaseous compounds during pyrolysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALIPHATIC HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aliphatic hydrocarbons are newly formed compounds detected in the fecula of mealworms, resulting from the depolymerization of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TG/DTG ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermogravimetric (TG) and differential thermogravimetric (DTG) analysis are used to study the weight loss and decomposition stages of polystyrene and its degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM GUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm gut microbiota includes bacteria like Enterobacter spp. that play a crucial role in the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiota of mealworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, facilitating its depolymerization and mineralization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer degradation refers to the breakdown of long-chain polymers, such as polystyrene, into smaller fragments, as observed in the mealworm gut.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymer degradation refers to the breakdown of synthetic polymers like polyurethane by biological processes, such as those carried out by Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON MONOXIDE (CO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon monoxide is a decomposition product observed during the thermal breakdown of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C SIGNATURES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">13C signatures are used to trace the carbon flow from ingested polystyrene to CO2 and biomass during the biodegradation process by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NMR SPECTRA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">NMR spectra are used to analyze the chemical components of gaseous compounds produced during the thermal decomposition of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra are used to identify chemical modifications in plastics, such as the incorporation of oxygen during biodegradation by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GC-C-IRMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GC-C-IRMS (Gas Chromatography-Combustion-Isotope Ratio Mass Spectrometry) is a technique used to analyze the δ13C values of fatty acids in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVING MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Starving mealworms are mealworms deprived of food, used as a control group to study the effects of polystyrene degradation on biomass weight.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOMASS DRY WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biomass dry weight is a measure used to assess the growth and metabolic activity of mealworms under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYMBIOTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Symbiotic degradation refers to the mutualistic relationship between mealworms and their gut microbiota in breaking down polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT BIOREACTOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut bioreactor is a term used to describe the mealworm gut, where physical and biochemical processes facilitate the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEWING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Chewing is the initial step in the degradation process, where mealworms reduce the size of polystyrene, increasing its surface area for microbial action.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PACKAGING FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Packaging films are materials tested for resistance to stored-product insects, highlighting their role in food safety and environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Packaging films are materials used for food packaging that can contribute to environmental pollution if not properly managed or biodegraded.&lt;SEP&gt;Packaging films are synthetic materials used for food packaging that have been tested for resistance to insect pests and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT PESTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect pests, such as Ephesia cautella and Plodia interpunctella, are known to penetrate and damage packaging films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPHESIA CAUTELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ephesia cautella is a moth species whose larvae are known to penetrate and damage packaging films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GC-TOF-MS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GC-TOF-MS (Gas Chromatography-Time of Flight-Mass Spectrometry) is a metabolomics technique used to analyze the chemical content of mealworm larvae gut tissue and frass during PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gas chromatography-time-of-flight mass spectrometry (GC-TOF-MS) is an analytical method used to identify and quantify metabolites in the biodegradation study of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-TOF-MS (Gas Chromatography-Time of Flight-Mass Spectrometry) is a technique used for metabolic profiling in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;GC-TOF-MS (Gas Chromatography-Time of Flight-Mass Spectrometry) is a technique used for metabolic profiling in studies of plastic biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS oligomers are low-molecular-weight byproducts of polystyrene degradation, detected in the frass of insects.&lt;SEP&gt;PS oligomers are smaller molecules derived from the depolymerization of polystyrene, observed in the frass of insects during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PS oligomers, such as dimers and trimers, are low-molecular-weight compounds identified in the frass of mealworm larvae during PS biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2,4 DI-TERT BUTYLPHENOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2,4 di-tert butylphenol is a non-intentionally added substance (NIAS) identified in both frass and gut tissue, originating from the PS polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLOMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolomics is a research approach used to study the impact of polybrominated diphenyl ether-47 on breast cancer mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolomics is a scientific approach used in the study to analyze the metabolic profiles of mealworm larvae during the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolomics is a scientific approach used to study the biochemical impact of plastics on insect metabolism, providing insights into plastic biodegradation and insect performance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acids are metabolic intermediates detected during the biodegradation of polystyrene in insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acids are organic compounds that play a significant role in various biological and metabolic processes, particularly in the context of Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae and bacterial species such as Mixta tenebrionis and strain BIT-26T. In mealworm larvae, fatty acids are a key component of their nutritional profile, with their composition and content being influenced by the type of feed provided, such as bran, potatoes, whey, or polystyrene foam. The analysis of fatty acids in larval biomass is conducted using gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS), following the standard GB 5009.168–2016, to assess the impact of different diets on larvae metabolism and nutritional value. 

Fatty acids are also bioactive molecules identified in the polar fraction of frass, which results from the enzymatic activity in the gut of mealworm larvae during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS). This process reflects changes in metabolic pathways and enzymatic activity, with fatty acids such as monounsaturated fatty acids (MUFAs) and saturated fatty acids (SFAs) serving as metabolic byproducts that indicate bacterial activity in the gut microbiome. 

In bacterial studies, fatty acids are used as chemotaxonomic markers to characterize and identify species, including strain BIT-26T and Mixta tenebrionis. Specific fatty acids such as C&lt;sub&gt;14:0&lt;/sub&gt; (myristic acid), C&lt;sub&gt;16:0&lt;/sub&gt; (palmitic acid), C&lt;sub&gt;18:0&lt;/sub&gt; (stearic acid), C12:0, C17:0 cyclo, and C19:0 cyclo ω8c are analyzed to determine the chemotaxonomic characteristics of these bacterial strains. These fatty acids are essential for understanding the metabolic and biochemical properties of the bacteria. 

Overall, fatty acids are critical components in both mealworm larvae and bacterial species, serving as indicators of metabolic activity, nutritional quality, and chemotaxonomic identity. Their profiles vary depending on environmental factors such as diet and biodegradation processes, making them a valuable focus of study in both entomological and microbiological research.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acids are oxidized during starvation to provide energy for mealworms, derived from stored triglycerides.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a706f2bd66291a3ef925114ffd9300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb542a26376f51c3cd9247fe3d56854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NON-INTENTIONALLY ADDED SUBSTANCES (NIAS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Non-intentionally added substances (NIAS) are chemical compounds, such as 2,4 di-tert butylphenol, originating from the PS polymer and identified in frass and gut tissue.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD CONTACT MATERIALS (FCMS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Food contact materials (FCMs) are materials, such as polystyrene, used in packaging that come into contact with food and are associated with environmental and health concerns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOPLASTIC POLYMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene is a thermoplastic polymer widely used in consumer and industrial applications, including food contact materials, due to its low cost and durability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIORECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biorecycling refers to the biodegradation of plastic-based materials, such as polystyrene, by organisms like mealworm larvae, bacteria, and fungi.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL CONCERNS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental concerns are raised due to the widespread use of polystyrene and its potential harm to the environment and human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,1-DIPHENYL-ETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,1-Diphenyl-ethylene is a chemical compound with a purity of 97%, used in the study of polystyrene oligomers.&lt;SEP&gt;1,1-Diphenyl-ethylene is an organic compound detected in frass, resulting from the biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHOXIME HYDROCHLORIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methoxime hydrochloride is a chemical reagent used in derivatization processes for analytical purposes, with a purity of 98%.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROFORM (CHCL3)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloroform is a solvent used in the extraction of frass and other materials in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYRIDINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyridine is a chemical used in derivatization processes and analytical methods in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHANOL (MEOH)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methanol is a solvent used in liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS) and extraction processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-PROPANOL (ISOH)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Propanol is a solvent used in LC-MS grade applications for analytical purposes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRAPURE WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ultrapure water, with a resistivity of 18.2 MΩ, is used for preparing stock solutions in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYTETRAFLUOROETHYLENE (PTFE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) is a material used for membrane filters in the filtration of samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VEGETABLE MIXTURE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A mixture of vegetables is used as a source of water in the diet of Tenebrio molitor larvae prior to experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass extraction is a method used to analyze the degradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT INTESTINE TISSUE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut intestine tissue from larvae is extracted and analyzed to study the biodegradation process of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut intestine tissue is extracted and analyzed to study the biodegradation process in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GC-BASED METABOLOMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GC-based metabolomics is an analytical method used to study the metabolites produced during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MYRISTIC ACID-D27">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Myristic acid-d27 is a deuterated internal standard used in the GC-MS metabolomics analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are analyzed in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHOXYAMINE HYDROCHLORIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methoxyamine hydrochloride is a reagent used in the derivatization process for GC-MS metabolomics analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSTFA/1% TMCS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MSTFA/1% TMCS is a derivatization agent used in the analysis of metabolites in the frass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;N-Methyl-N-trimethylsilyl trifluoroacetamide with 1% Trimethylchlorosilane (MSTFA/1% TMCS) is a derivatization agent used in GC-MS metabolomics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGILENT TECHNOLOGIES 7890B">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Agilent Technologies 7890B is a gas chromatography system used for the separation and analysis of metabolites in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Agilent Technologies 7890B is a gas chromatography system used in the study for the analysis of metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGILENT TECHNOLOGIES 7200 ACCURATE-MASS Q-TOF">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Agilent Technologies 7200 Accurate-Mass Q-ToF is a mass spectrometer used for mass spectral analysis in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Agilent Technologies 7200 Accurate-Mass Q-ToF is a mass spectrometer used in the study for metabolite identification and quantification.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAVIMETRIC METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gravimetric method is used to determine the humidity of samples in the study, ensuring consistent experimental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS EXTRACT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass extract refers to the excrement of larvae, which is analyzed to study the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASSHUNTER 10.0">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">MassHunter 10.0 is software used for processing and analyzing mass spectrometry data.&lt;SEP&gt;Masshunter 10.0 is software used for processing analytical data in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS PROFILER PROFESSIONAL (MPP) V.15.0">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass Profiler Professional (MPP) v.15.0 is a chemometrics platform used for alignment and exploitation of MS data in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIMCA 16.0.2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SIMCA 16.0.2 is software used for principal component analysis (PCA) to assess discrimination between different feeding strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIET 2 (HIGH PS:BRAN, WATER INCLUDED)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Diet 2 is a feeding strategy that includes high PS:bran with water, improving PS consumption by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIET 1 (LOW PS:BRAN)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Diet 1 is a feeding strategy that includes low PS:bran, showing comparable PS consumption rates to Diet 2 by the end of the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIET 3 (PS/BARLEY 20:1)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Diet 3 is a feeding strategy that includes PS/barley 20:1, requiring less handling but showing lower PS degradation rates compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE (PS) DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene (PS) degradation refers to the process by which mealworm larvae break down PS into smaller components, contributing to environmental remediation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL RATIO">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival ratio refers to the percentage of mealworm larvae that survived under different feeding conditions during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT GAIN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight gain refers to the increase in mass of mealworm larvae during the experiment, influenced by different feeding strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2,4-DTBP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2,4-DTBP (2,4-bis(1,1-dimethylethyl)-phenol) is a chemical compound used as a pesticide, antifungal agent, and antioxidant, identified in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;2,4-DTBP is a compound detected in frass, originating from the degradation of Irgafos 168, an antioxidant additive in polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BARLEY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Barley is a type of grain used as a diet component in feeding experiments with Tenebrio molitor larvae to study polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXADECANOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexadecanoic acid is a fatty acid detected in the chloroform extracts of frass, indicating enzymatic activity during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexadecanoic acid, also known as palmitic acid, is a saturated fatty acid that is a common metabolite in the metabolic processes of mealworms fed PS-containing diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexadecanoic acid is a metabolite found in the frass and gut samples of mealworms fed with both polystyrene and cellulose.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexadecanoic acid is a long-chain fatty acid detected in the gut of insect larvae fed on polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OCTADECANOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Octadecanoic acid is a fatty acid detected in the chloroform extracts of frass, indicating enzymatic activity during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Octadecanoic acid, also known as stearic acid, is a saturated fatty acid that is excreted by mealworms during the metabolism of PS-containing diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Octadecanoic acid, also known as Stearic acid, is an emulsifier with the formula C18H36O2.&lt;SEP&gt;Octadecanoic acid is a long-chain fatty acid detected during the biodegradation of polystyrene in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </node>
