    <node id="OCTANE, 2,6,6-TRIMETHYL-">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Octane, 2,6,6-trimethyl- is a hydrocarbon excreted by mealworms during the metabolism of PS-containing diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PENTACOSANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pentacosane is a hydrocarbon excreted by mealworms during the metabolism of PS-containing diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCEROL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycerol is a chemical compound used to prepare a 50% glycerol solution, which is often used as a cryoprotectant in biological studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycerol is a compound whose levels vary during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) in mealworms, suggesting metabolic stress due to nutrient-poor PS-rich diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycerol is a precursor for triacylglycerols and phospholipids, and its variation indicates metabolic stress in mealworms fed PS-containing diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANNOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mannose is a monosaccharide involved in protein glycosylation, and its decrease in mealworms fed PS-containing diets indicates metabolic changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Mannose is an important monosaccharide involved in protein glycosylation in mammals and is considered a non-efficient cellular energy source. It plays a role in N-linked glycosylation, a post-translational modification of proteins.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOSYLATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycosylation is a metabolic pathway involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by mealworms. It is a post-translational modification of proteins and is linked to increased enzymatic activity during PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid metabolism is one of the main metabolic pathways identified in the polar fraction of frass, indicating its role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid metabolism pathways are enriched in PS-/CS-fed mealworms, indicating their role in the degradation of synthetic and natural polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oleic acid is a fatty acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, accounting for about 50% of the fatty acid content in the PE and CEL groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Oleic acid is a fatty acid that increases in the frass of PS-fed mealworms, likely due to lipid metabolism during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Oleic acid levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are negatively correlated with the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Oleic acid is an unsaturated fatty acid found in the biomass of Tenebrio larvae, contributing to their nutritional profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Oleic acid is a long-chain fatty acid detected during the biodegradation of polystyrene in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYLBIS(TRIMETHYLSILYL) AMINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethylbis(trimethylsilyl) amine is a silyl derivative of ammonia identified in the frass of mealworms, possibly excreted during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIMETHYLSILYL ETHER OF GLYCEROL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trimethylsilyl ether of glycerol is a metabolite identified in the frass of mealworms, indicating its role in the biodegradation process of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLETHANOLAMINE TRITMS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenylethanolamine triTMS is a metabolite identified in the frass of mealworms, showing down-regulation in PS-fed insects compared to the control group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OCTADECANOIC ACID, TERT-BUTYLDIMETHYLSILYL ESTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Octadecanoic acid, tert-butyldimethylsilyl ester is a metabolite identified in the frass of mealworms, showing down-regulation in PS-fed insects compared to the control group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N,N-O-TRIS(TRIMETHYLSILYL)-4-AMINOMETHYLCYCLOHEXANE CARBOXYLIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A chemical compound identified in the gut intestine content of Tenebrio molitor larvae during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;N,N-O-Tris(trimethylsilyl)-4-aminomethylcyclohexane carboxylic acid is a metabolite identified in the frass of mealworms, showing down-regulation in PS-fed insects compared to the control group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANNOSE, 2,3,4,5,6-PENTAKIS-O-(TRIMETHYLSILYL)-, D.">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A chemical compound identified in the gut intestine content of Tenebrio molitor larvae during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mannose, 2,3,4,5,6-pentakis-O-(trimethylsilyl)-, D. is a metabolite identified in the frass of mealworms, showing down-regulation in PS-fed insects compared to the control group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXADECANOIC ACID, TRIMETHYLSILYL ESTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A chemical compound identified in the gut intestine content of Tenebrio molitor larvae during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexadecanoic acid, trimethylsilyl ester is a metabolite identified in the frass of mealworms, showing down-regulation in PS-fed insects compared to the control group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acids are bioactive compounds identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating their role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamins are bioactive compounds identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating their role in the metabolic pathways involved.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamins are dietary supplements used in experiments to support the survival and enzymatic activity of Tenebrio molitor larvae, aiding in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamins are essential nutrients that, when supplemented in the diet, can significantly influence the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamins are supplements added to the diet of mealworms in experiments to study their impact on growth and survival when combined with polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ester is a bioactive compound identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating its role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SQUALENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A chemical compound identified in the gut intestine content of Tenebrio molitor larvae during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Squalene is a bioactive compound identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating its role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FCM SUBSTANCES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FCM substances refer to monomers and oligomers that were not detected in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating their absence in the residual material.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VOLATILE COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Volatile compounds are substances excreted by mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), resulting in low mass fractions detected in the frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GC-TOF-MS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GC-TOF-MS analysis is a method used to analyze the metabolites and bioactive compounds in the frass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCA SCORE PLOT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PCA score plot is a statistical tool used to analyze the differences in metabolite profiles between control and PS-fed mealworms, indicating the impact of feeding strategies on frass composition.</data>
