      <data key="d2">chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLIC STRESS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolic stress observed in Tenebrio molitor larvae due to the consumption of polystyrene, compared to their typical diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic stress refers to the physiological stress experienced by organisms, such as mealworms, when consuming non-nutritive substances like polystyrene, compared to their typical diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic stress refers to the physiological changes in insects, such as mealworms, due to nutrient-poor polystyrene (PS)-rich diets, affecting their growth and biochemical processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG CHAIN FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Long chain fatty acids identified in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae, likely related to the enzymatic activity during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Esters identified in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae, likely related to the enzymatic activity during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOODOMICSGR_RI">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FoodOmicsGR_RI is a research project focused on the comprehensive characterization of foods, funded by the European Union and Greece.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EUROPEAN COMMISSION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The European Commission is involved in regulatory and research activities, including the development of standards for food safety and packaging materials.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Commission is involved in setting sustainability goals, including efforts to reduce the environmental impact of plastics like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JOINT RESEARCH CENTRE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Joint Research Centre (JRC) is a European Commission institution that supports research and innovation, including studies on food safety and environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGILENT GC/MS METABOLOMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Agilent GC/MS Metabolomics is a technology used for analyzing metabolic profiles, often applied in research studies related to food safety and toxicology.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID AMIDE SIGNALING MOLECULES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid amide signaling molecules are bioactive compounds involved in various physiological processes, including metabolism and stress responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESSER WAXMOTH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achroia grisella, also known as the lesser waxmoth, is an insect capable of biodegrading polyethylene (HDPE) waste, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARROTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carrots are a potential supplemental nutrient that could be used in future studies to enhance EPS consumption rates by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carrots are a potential supplemental nutrient that could be used in future studies to enhance EPS consumption rates by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carrots are used as part of the diet in the study to assess the effects of polystyrene feeding on Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Carrots were used as a supplementary food source for Tenebrio molitor larvae in the study to assess their survival and development rates when fed with polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAM-NEGATIVE BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gram-negative bacteria are a group of bacteria identified by their cell wall structure, some of which are involved in the degradation of NPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Gram-negative bacteria, such as Klebsiella oxytoca, are isolated from the guts of Tenebrio molitor and are studied for their role in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M13 RAPD-PCR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">M13 RAPD-PCR is a molecular technique used to investigate phylogenetic relationships among bacterial isolates using random amplified polymorphic DNA.&lt;SEP&gt;M13 RAPD-PCR is a molecular technique used to investigate the phylogenetic relationships of bacterial isolates from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, revealing their identity and role in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAM STAIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gram stain is a staining technique used to classify bacterial isolates from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, confirming their identity and role in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHIDIUM BROMIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethidium bromide is a fluorescent dye used to stain DNA for visualization during gel electrophoresis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethidium bromide is a fluorescent dye used to stain DNA in agarose gel electrophoresis, allowing visualization of genomic DNA and 16S rRNA gene amplification products from bacterial isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGAROSE GEL ELECTROPHORESIS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Agarose gel electrophoresis is a technique used to separate and visualize DNA fragments, such as genomic DNA and 16S rRNA gene amplification products, from bacterial isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BASIC LOCAL ALIGNMENT SEARCH TOOL (BLAST)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">BLAST is a bioinformatics tool used to identify bacterial isolates from the gut of Tenebrio molitor by comparing their DNA sequences to known sequences in databases.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITROBACTER GILLENNII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrobacter gillennii is a Gram-negative bacterium closely related to Klebsiella oxytoca, identified in phylogenetic analysis as part of the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter gillennii is a bacterial species found in the gut of mealworms and is associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER HORMAECHEI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter hormaechei is a Gram-negative bacterium identified in phylogenetic analysis as part of the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor, potentially involved in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterial species previously isolated from Tenebrio molitor and associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterial species studied for its role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, studied for its role in PS film biodegradation over 15 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter hormaechei is a gut bacterium from mealworms that can biodegrade polystyrene microplastics under anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterial strain capable of heterotrophic denitrification in the presence of methanol and sodium-succinate, relevant to wastewater treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter hormaechei is an opportunistic pathogen that contains lipid A, a toxic component in endotoxins, and is associated with changes in lipid metabolism during polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KOSAKONIA PSEUDOSACCHARI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kosakonia pseudosacchari is a Gram-negative bacterium identified in phylogenetic analysis as part of the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor, potentially involved in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosakonia pseudosacchari is a bacterial species identified in the gut of mealworms and is linked to the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITROBACTER AMALONICUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrobacter amalonicus is a Gram-negative bacterium identified in phylogenetic analysis as part of the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor, potentially involved in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrobacter amalonicus is a bacterial species found in the gut of mealworms and is involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAPD-PCR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Random Amplified Polymorphic DNA PCR (RAPD-PCR) is a molecular technique used to analyze genetic polymorphisms among bacterial isolates from the gut of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA JCM 16655">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 16655 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms and is involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPROPYLENE (PP) STORAGE CONTAINERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) storage containers are used to cultivate mealworms in laboratory studies on polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIQUID CARBON-FREE MEDIUM (LCFBM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Liquid carbon-free medium is a nutrient solution used in microbial culture experiments to ensure that the sole carbon source is the target substance, such as polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT CELL SUSPENSION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut cell suspension refers to the microbial community derived from the gut tissues of organisms, used as an inoculum for polystyrene-degrading bacterial enrichment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene-degrading bacteria are microorganisms capable of breaking down polystyrene, isolated from gut cell suspensions and cultured in specific media.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RIBOSOMAL DNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">16S ribosomal DNA is a genetic marker used for the identification and phylogenetic analysis of bacterial isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS FEEDSTOCKS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS feedstocks refer to expanded polystyrene materials used as a carbon source in microbial degradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGAR POWDER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Agar powder is a gelling agent used to prepare solid media, such as polystyrene agar, for microbial culture.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAM STAINING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gram staining is a microbiological technique used to classify bacteria based on their cell wall properties, aiding in the identification of bacterial isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANODROP SPECTROPHOTOMETER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A nanodrop spectrophotometer is an instrument used to measure the concentration and purity of DNA samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S UNIVERSAL PRIMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">16S universal primers are oligonucleotides used in PCR to amplify the 16S rRNA gene for bacterial identification.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLAST SEARCH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">BLAST search is a bioinformatics tool used to analyze coxl sequences of lesser waxworms to understand genetic variations related to PE degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;BLAST search is a bioinformatics tool used to compare DNA sequences against a database to identify phylogenetic relationships.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTOCATALYTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Photocatalytic degradation is a process that uses light to break down pollutants, such as polystyrene, into smaller, less harmful substances.&lt;SEP&gt;Photocatalytic degradation is a process where light-activated catalysts, such as TiO2, break down pollutants like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer biodegradation refers to the breakdown of synthetic polymers, such as plastics, by biological processes, often involving microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTICS IN SEA TURTLES">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics in sea turtles refer to the presence of small plastic particles in marine turtles, which can lead to health issues and ecological impacts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTICS AND HUMAN HEALTH">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastics and human health refers to the study of the effects of plastic pollution on human health, including exposure to microplastics and associated chemicals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE-DEGRADING MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene-degrading microbes are microorganisms capable of breaking down polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS AND POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms and polystyrene refers to the ability of mealworms to digest and biodegrade polystyrene, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUMBLE BEES (BOMBUS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bumble bees (Bombus) are wild bees studied for their gut microbial communities and their association with pathogen infection.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE-GRAFT-STARCH COPOLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene-graft-starch copolymers are synthetic materials studied for their biodegradation in different types of soil.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE AGAR">
      <data key="d2">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
      <data key="d1">Agar powder is used to prepare polystyrene agar, a medium for culturing polystyrene-degrading bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
      <data key="d1">DNA is extracted from the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae to study microbial communities and their role in biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethidium bromide is used to stain DNA for visualization during gel electrophoresis.</data>
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENYLACETATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenylacetate is a compound produced during the enzymatic transformation of styrene by microorganisms like Pseudomonas sp.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenylacetate is a metabolic product formed during the oxidation of styrene by microorganisms, indicating the degradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHEAT FLOUR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Wheat flour is a nutrient-rich food source for Tenebrio molitor larvae, supporting their biomass growth, unlike polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat flour is used as a food source for Tenebrio molitor larvae during the acclimation period in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat flour was used as an acclimation diet for Tenebrio molitor in the present study, affecting the composition of their gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINIMAL SALT MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Minimal salt medium is used to culture and evaluate the growth of gut bacteria isolates from Tenebrio molitor larvae on polystyrene as the sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITIVE AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutritive agar is used to culture and isolate gut bacteria from Tenebrio molitor larvae after serial dilutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPERIMENTAL CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Experimental conditions include the controlled environment (temperature, light, and food supply) used to acclimate and study Tenebrio molitor larvae and their interaction with polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SERIAL DILUTIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Serial dilutions are performed to isolate and identify gut bacteria from Tenebrio molitor larvae for further biochemical and molecular characterization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL CHARACTERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical characterization involves tests such as TSI, MRVP, Citrate, Urease, Catalase, Oxidase, and Gram staining to identify gut bacteria isolates from Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR BARCODING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular barcoding via 16S rRNA sequencing is used to identify gut bacteria isolates from Tenebrio molitor larvae by comparing sequences with databases like RDP, Greengenes, and NCBI RefSeq/RNA.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYOPRESERVATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cryopreservation is used to store bacterial isolates from Tenebrio molitor larvae for future research and analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADULT TENEBRIO MOLITOR">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Adult Tenebrio molitor beetles are used to evaluate their capacity to ingest polystyrene as a food source after reaching the imago stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Adult Tenebrio molitor, or beetles, are studied to determine their ability to consume EPS and their impact on mealworm EPS consumption in co-cultures.&lt;SEP&gt;Adult Tenebrio molitor, or beetles, are studied to determine their ability to consume EPS and their impact on mealworm EPS consumption in co-cultures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TREATMENT GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Treatment groups consist of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with expanded polystyrene plates to evaluate their ingestion and degradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANALYTICAL BALANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An analytical balance is used to measure the change in mass of Tenebrio molitor larvae and polystyrene plates during the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STERILE CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Sterile conditions are maintained during the excision and isolation of gut bacteria from Tenebrio molitor larvae to prevent contamination.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROBIC AND ANAEROBIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aerobic and anaerobic bacteria are isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae and cultured under different oxygen conditions to study their growth and biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS PLATES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PS plates are expanded polystyrene plates used as a food source for Tenebrio molitor larvae in the study to evaluate their biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL STRAINS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacterial strains from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, as evidenced by research studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Nine bacterial strains were isolated from the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae and adults, some of which may play a role in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZNSO4·7H2O">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Zinc sulfate heptahydrate (ZnSO4·7H2O) is a chemical compound used in the preparation of agar media for microbial growth studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MNSO4·H2O">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Manganese sulfate monohydrate (MnSO4·H2O) is a chemical compound used in the preparation of agar media for microbial growth studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS SHEETS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene (PS) sheets are plastic materials used in experiments to study microbial degradation and consumption by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR ADULTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor adults are the mature stage of mealworms, which appear to lose the ability to consume polystyrene compared to larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor adults may lose the ability to digest polystyrene (PS) due to structural changes in their jaws and shifts in intestinal microbiota composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STENOTROPHOMONAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Stenotrophomonas is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Stenotrophomonas is a genus of bacteria isolated from the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor, capable of growing on polystyrene as a sole carbon source.&lt;SEP&gt;Stenotrophomonas is a genus of bacteria isolated from the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae, potentially involved in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Exiguobacterium is a genus of bacteria identified in the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor larvae, known for its role in the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS ANTHRACIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus anthracis is a Gram-positive bacterium isolated from the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor, capable of forming tunnels and cracks on polystyrene sheets during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus anthracis is a bacterium that forms tunnels, cracks, and cellular aggregates on polystyrene (PS) sheets, indicating its role in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANTOEA AGGLOMERANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pantoea agglomerans is a Gram-negative bacterium isolated from the digestive tract of adult Tenebrio molitor, contributing to the gut microbiota composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Pantoena agglomerans is a species of the genus Pantoena, closely related to Mixta tenebrionis BIT-26, as identified through phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ERWINIA PERSICINA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Erwinia persicina is a Gram-negative bacterium isolated from the digestive tract of adult Tenebrio molitor, playing a role in the gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Erwinia persicina is a bacterial species found in various environments, with strain LMG 11254 being analyzed for its genetic and biochemical characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Erwinia persicina is a species of the genus Erwinia, closely related to Mixta tenebrionis BIT-26, as identified through phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014b72d0a2657cbc89c4dee5f2b32bba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OAT FLAKES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oat flakes were used as an acclimation diet for Tenebrio molitor in previous studies, influencing the composition of their gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINIMAL SALT AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Minimal salt agar is a medium used to culture bacterial isolates from Tenebrio molitor, allowing them to grow on polystyrene as a sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANTHEA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pantoea is a genus of bacteria present in the adult gut of Tenebrio molitor, known for its capacity to degrade plastics such as low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITRIC ACID CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The citric acid cycle is a metabolic pathway involved in the oxidation of styrene to phenylacetate, contributing to the degradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM-ASSISTED WORKS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM-assisted works refer to studies using scanning electron microscopy to document bacterial-driven transformations of polystyrene (PS) and other plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLA/PBAT BLEND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PLA/PBAT blend is a flexible film material studied for its properties and compatibility, often used in biodegradable packaging applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E-BEAM IRRADIATED PLA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">E-beam irradiated PLA is a modified form of polylactic acid used as a compatibilizing agent in polymer blends to enhance material properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROMPHENOLS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bromophenols are chemical compounds identified as dioxin precursors in combustion gases of fire-retarded extruded polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE CATABOLIC CLUSTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The styrene catabolic cluster is a genetic and functional system in Pseudomonas sp. Strain Y2 that enables the degradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLEXIBLE FILMS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flexible films are materials studied for their properties and applications, often made from blends like PLA/PBAT and modified with agents such as E-beam irradiated PLA.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PBAT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PBAT (polybutylene adipate terephthalate) is a biodegradable plastic that may be explored for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;PBAT is a biodegradable plastic that is being investigated for its environmental impact and biodegradation in soil environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Polybutylene adipate terephthalate (PBAT) is a biodegradable polymer often blended with PLA to create flexible films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIRE-RETARDED EXTRUDED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fire-retarded extruded polystyrene is a type of polystyrene treated with flame retardants, which can release harmful chemicals like bromophenols during combustion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE MORTARS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded polystyrene mortars are construction materials made by incorporating expanded polystyrene into mortar, studied for their durability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE-G-CASSAVA STARCH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene-g-cassava starch is a modified polymer used in filled polystyrene plastics, studied for its degradation properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SILICA-ALUMINA CATALYST">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Silica-alumina catalyst is used in the pyrolysis of polystyrene to break down the plastic into smaller molecules.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRICKET HINDGUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The cricket hindgut microbiota consists of microorganisms that play a role in digestion and nutrient processing in crickets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DECABROMODIPHENYL OXIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Decabromodiphenyl oxide is a flame retardant chemical used in plastics, including high impact polystyrene, and is considered an environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Decabromodiphenyl oxide is a flame retardant used in high impact polystyrene, which complicates its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIMONY TRIOXIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Antimony trioxide is a chemical compound used as a flame retardant in plastics, including high impact polystyrene, and is considered an environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Antimony trioxide is a flame retardant used in high impact polystyrene, contributing to its environmental persistence.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SP. STRAIN Y2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas sp. Strain Y2 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading styrene through its styrene catabolic cluster.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas sp. strain Y2 is a bacterium capable of catabolizing styrene, contributing to the biodegradation of styrene-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL CONSORTIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacterial consortia are groups of bacteria isolated from plastic garbage processing areas that demonstrate enhanced degradation of low density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms, including bacteria, in the gut of Tenebrio molitor, which are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiota in the mealworm gut plays a crucial role in adapting to new diets, including the degradation of polyurethane (PU) foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPOLYMER DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biopolymer degradation refers to the breakdown of synthetic polymers like polystyrene by biological agents, such as mealworm larvae and their gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAS DO TRATO GASTROINTESTINAL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteria from the gastrointestinal tract of Tenebrio molitor have been found to form biofilms on PS surfaces, indicating their potential role in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e276f74ca596eed69175a31dab0bf51b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PAULA M. PEÑA-PASCAGAZA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Paula M. Peña-Pascagaza is an environmental engineer with expertise in bioprocesses, microbiology, and environmental monitoring, contributing to research and guidelines in environmental areas.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e276f74ca596eed69175a31dab0bf51b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATHALIA A. LÓPEZ-RAMÍREZ">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nathalia A. López-Ramírez is an environmental engineer with experience in Health, Safety, and Environment (HSE), waste management, and chemical substance handling, contributing to post-consumption programs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e276f74ca596eed69175a31dab0bf51b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIGUEL A. BALLEN-SEGURA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Miguel A. Ballen-Segura is a Ph.D. and MSc researcher focusing on fundamental and applied ecology, particularly the use of microorganisms for remediation in high mountain aquatic systems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e276f74ca596eed69175a31dab0bf51b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BESOURO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Besouro refers to beetles, which are insects that may play a role in environmental processes such as biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e276f74ca596eed69175a31dab0bf51b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROORGANISMOS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microorganisms are microscopic organisms, such as bacteria, that are used in environmental remediation and biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e276f74ca596eed69175a31dab0bf51b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIGESTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
      <data key="d1">The cricket hindgut microbiota plays a role in digestion and nutrient processing in crickets</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRICKWORK DEBRIS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Brickwork debris is a mixture of materials, including bricks, mortar, and thermal insulation, generated during the demolition of brick-and-mortar buildings.&lt;SEP&gt;Brickwork debris refers to construction waste contaminated with EPS, which can be cleaned using Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUSTAINABLE DEMOLITION PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The sustainable demolition process involves using mealworms to clean brickwork debris from expanded polystyrene, making the demolition process more environmentally friendly.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRICKWORK BUILDINGS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Brickwork buildings generate significant amounts of construction and demolition waste (CWD), including mixed debris that is difficult to recycle.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSTRUCTION AND DEMOLITION WASTE (CWD)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Construction and demolition waste (CWD) is a major environmental issue, consisting of mixed debris such as bricks, mortar, thermal insulation, and other materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CERAMIC BRICKS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ceramic bricks are a component of brickwork debris that can be recycled into lightweight aggregate for concrete production.&lt;SEP&gt;Ceramic bricks are construction materials used in the study, to which EPS was glued using cement mortar, later removed by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Ceramic bricks are used in the study to model external and internal layers of brickwork walls, providing a substrate for EPS insulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CEMENT MORTAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cement mortar is a binding material in brickwork structures that contaminates debris and complicates recycling efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement mortar is a construction material used in the study to bond EPS to ceramic bricks, which was later removed by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement mortar is a construction material used to bond EPS to ceramic bricks, which was later removed by Tenebrio molitor larvae during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement mortar is used to glue EPS prisms to ceramic bricks, simulating a common multilayer external wall structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL INSULATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal insulation, often made of expanded polystyrene (EPS), is a challenging material to separate and recycle in demolition debris.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECYCLED LIGHTWEIGHT AGGREGATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Recycled lightweight aggregate is produced from crushed red ceramics and can be used in new concrete production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANUAL PROCESSING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Manual processing is a labor-intensive method for removing expanded polystyrene (EPS) from brickwork debris, often deemed economically unfeasible.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE FRAMEWORK DIRECTIVE 2008/98/EC">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Waste Framework Directive 2008/98/EC is a European Union regulation aimed at improving waste management and recycling practices.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZERO WASTE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Zero Waste is a concept focused on reducing waste generation and promoting recycling, aligned with the circular economy.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERNAL CURING PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The internal curing process is a method for utilizing crushed red ceramics as waste aggregate in concrete production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO-FILLERS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro-fillers are materials derived from recycled mortar, concrete, or plaster, used to partially substitute cement in construction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIVIL ENGINEERING PERSPECTIVE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The civil engineering perspective focuses on evaluating the potential for using mealworms to process expanded polystyrene (EPS) in demolition debris.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAPHITE-ADDED EPS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Graphite-added EPS is a type of expanded polystyrene with enhanced thermal properties, used in energy-efficient building construction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYMBIOTIC GUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Symbiotic gut microbiota in mealworms play a crucial role in the digestion of hard-to-degrade plastics, aiding in their biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARTICLE SIZE ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A particle size analyzer is used to measure the size distribution of frass particles after the digestion of EPS by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHITE EPS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">White EPS is a type of expanded polystyrene used in the study, which was consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, resulting in frass with specific chemical and physical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;White EPS is a type of expanded polystyrene with a higher material density, which affects the consumption rate by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREY EPS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Grey EPS is a type of expanded polystyrene containing graphite, which influences the consumption rate and chemical composition of frass produced by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Grey EPS is another type of expanded polystyrene used in the study, which was also consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, leading to frass with distinct characteristics compared to white EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARTICLE SIZE DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Particle size distribution refers to the analysis of frass particle sizes after Tenebrio molitor larvae consumed EPS, providing insights into the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FITR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FITR analysis is a method used to characterize the chemical composition of frass produced by Tenebrio molitor larvae after consuming EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PCA (Principal Component Analysis) is a statistical method used in the study to analyze the similarities and differences between frass from white and grey EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAE MORTALITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae mortality refers to the death rate of Tenebrio molitor larvae during the experiment, which was influenced by the type of EPS consumed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL MODIFICATIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemical modifications refer to changes in the chemical composition of frass after Tenebrio molitor larvae consumed EPS, indicating the extent of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PH VALUE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">pH value is a measure of the acidity or alkalinity of frass, which was analyzed in the study to understand the chemical properties of the waste material produced by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRANULOMETRIC TESTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Granulometric tests are used to analyze the particle size distribution of frass, providing insights into the physical properties of the waste material produced by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemical composition refers to the elements and compounds present in frass, which were analyzed to understand the biodegradation process of EPS by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS MASS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass mass refers to the amount of waste material produced by Tenebrio molitor larvae after consuming EPS, which is smaller in volume compared to the initial EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAE MASS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae mass refers to the weight of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which is inversely correlated with mortality rates during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL COMPOSITION OF FRASS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The chemical composition of frass includes elements such as C, N, P2O5, Ca, Mg, K, Na, Fe, Al, Mn, Zn, Cr, and Pb, which are derived from the biodegradation of EPS by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGRICULTURE">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Agriculture is a potential application for frass, as it can be used as a fertilizer due to its nutrient-rich composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSTRUCTION INDUSTRY">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">The construction industry could utilize frass as a thermal insulating material or lightweight aggregate, based on its chemical properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSTRUCTION AND DEMOLITION WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Construction and demolition waste refers to materials generated during the construction, renovation, or demolition of buildings and structures, often contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RED CERAMIC WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Red ceramic waste is a byproduct of ceramic production and is often used as a replacement for coarse aggregate in concrete, contributing to waste management and recycling efforts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHICS COMMITTEE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Ethics Committee is responsible for reviewing and approving animal study protocols to ensure ethical standards are met in research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGHTWEIGHT SFRC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lightweight SFRC (Steel Fiber Reinforced Concrete) is a type of concrete that benefits from pre-soaking and internal curing processes, often incorporating waste materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLENDED CEMENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Blended cement is a type of cement that combines traditional cement with supplementary materials, such as waste products, to improve sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROCARBON-BASED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrocarbon-based plastics are synthetic polymers derived from petroleum, including polyethylene and polystyrene, which are resistant to natural degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbon-based plastics are synthetic polymers derived from petroleum, including polyethylene and polystyrene, which are resistant to natural degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbon-based plastics are synthetic polymers derived from petroleum, which are persistent in the environment and pose significant pollution challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbon-based plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, are major environmental pollutants that can be biodegraded by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbon-based plastics are synthetic polymers derived from petroleum, which are persistent in the environment and pose significant pollution challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC PACKAGING">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic packaging is a common environmental pollutant that can be managed through interactions with insects like Rhyzopertha dominica and Tribolium castaneum.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMON WHEAT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Common wheat (Triticum aestivum) is a crop that can be fertilized with mealworm larvae meal, as studied in agricultural research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM LARVAE MEAL">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm larvae meal is used as a fertilizer and alternative protein source in agricultural studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONCRETE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
      <data key="d1">Red ceramic waste is used as a replacement for coarse aggregate in concrete, contributing to sustainable construction practices.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANIMAL STUDY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
      <data key="d1">The Ethics Committee reviews and approves animal study protocols to ensure ethical research practices.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE MATERIALS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
      <data key="d1">Lightweight SFRC incorporates waste materials to improve sustainability and performance.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
      <data key="d1">Blended cement combines traditional cement with waste products to enhance sustainability in construction.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms are larvae that can consume and metabolize synthetic polymers like polystyrene and polyethylene, as well as lignocellulose, impacting their development, microbiome, and biochemistry.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR (FOURIER-TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR is a method used to analyze the chemical modification of polystyrene in mealworm frass, indicating the extent of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR is a technique used to analyze changes in surface morphology and functional groups of materials, such as PS, after enzymatic treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAS-CHROMATOGRAPHY–MASS-SPECTROMETRY UNTARGETED SCREENING METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This method is used to detect and assess the degree of degradation of polystyrene in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARDBOARD (CEL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cardboard is a diet used in the study to feed Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing moderate metamorphosis and mortality rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Cardboard is a lignocellulose-based diet used in the study, consisting of shredded packaging cardboard with a density of ~0.36 g/cm³.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXO-DEGRADABLE POLYETHYLENE (PE-OXO)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxo-degradable polyethylene is a synthetic polymer diet that negatively impacts the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae, causing starvation.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxo-degradable polyethylene is a type of plastic used as a diet for Tenebrio molitor larvae, characterized by low metamorphosis and moderate mortality.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxo-degradable polyethylene is a type of polyethylene that can degrade under specific conditions, used as a diet for mealworms in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE REGRANULATE (PE-REG)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene regranulate is a form of polyethylene used as a diet for mealworms, found to be the most beneficial for their development.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene regranulate is a synthetic polymer diet that provides the most favorable development conditions for Tenebrio molitor larvae, despite high mortality rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene regranulate is a type of plastic used as a diet for Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing a development pattern similar to oatmeal but with higher mortality.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The nitrogen cycle is a biochemical process that may be influenced by the diet of mealworms, affecting their metabolism and microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANIMAL WELFARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Animal welfare refers to the ethical considerations and conditions under which mealworms are bred and studied, particularly when fed waste-based diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE BIOCONVERSION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste bioconversion refers to the process by which mealworms convert synthetic polymers and lignocellulose into simpler compounds, contributing to waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOME MODIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbiome modification refers to the changes in the gut microbial community of mealworms due to different diets, impacting their ability to metabolize waste materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical properties refer to the metabolic and enzymatic activities in the digestive system of mealworms, which are influenced by their diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN CONTENT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein content analysis is performed on mealworm larvae to determine the protein content in larvae grown on different substrates, using the Bradford method.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid analysis is a biochemical method used to characterize bacterial strains, as shown in the study with fatty acid profiles for various Erwinia strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid analysis is conducted to determine the fatty acid composition in mealworm larvae grown on different substrates, using gas chromatography.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014b72d0a2657cbc89c4dee5f2b32bba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRADFORD METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Bradford method is a biochemical assay used to determine protein concentration in mealworm homogenates.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bradford method is used to determine the protein content in mealworm larvae grown on different substrates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gas chromatography is an analytical technique used to determine the fatty acid composition of strain BIT-26T.&lt;SEP&gt;Gas chromatography is used to determine the fatty acid composition in mealworm larvae grown on different substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Gas chromatography is an analytical technique used to separate and analyze fatty acid methyl esters in samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QIAAMP POWERFECAL DNA KIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The QIAamp PowerFecal DNA Kit is used for DNA extraction from the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae to study microbial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;The QIAamp PowerFecal DNA Kit is used for DNA isolation from the digestive tracts of mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALCIUM OXIDE (CAO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Calcium oxide is a component of PE-reg, used as a pigment in the polyethylene substrate for mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALCIUM CARBONATE (CACO3)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Calcium carbonate is a component of PE-reg, used as a pigment in the polyethylene substrate for mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM ETHER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum ether is used in the Soxhlet extraction process to extract crude fat from mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID METHYL ESTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid methyl esters are compounds derived from fatty acids, often used in biodiesel production, and studied in the context of mealworm grease.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid methyl esters are prepared from mealworm larvae to determine their fatty acid composition using gas chromatography.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOVINE SERUM ALBUMIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bovine serum albumin is used as a standard in the Bradford method to determine protein content in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEMINERALIZED WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Demineralized water is used in the protein content analysis and as a blank in the Bradford method.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUANTUS FLUOROMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Quantus Fluorometer is used for fluorometric quantitation of DNA concentrations extracted from Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="API 20 E AND API 20 NE TESTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">API 20 E and API 20 NE tests are used to determine biochemical activity and carbon source uptake in the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRINCIPAL COMPONENTS ANALYSIS (PCA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Principal Components Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used to examine correlations between experimental variants and studied variables in Tenebrio molitor larvae research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGGLOMMERATIVE HIERARCHICAL CLUSTERING (AHC)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering (AHC) is used to analyze dissimilarity between variants based on a Bray-Curtis dissimilarity matrix in Tenebrio molitor larvae research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pupa is a developmental stage of Tenebrio molitor larvae, with significant differences in counts observed across different feed types.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupa is the developmental stage of mealworms following the larval stage, which is affected by feeding conditions such as polystyrene consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEWCOMBE-WILSON METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Newcombe-Wilson method is used for calculating confidence intervals in the study to assess statistical significance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OATMEAL (OAT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oatmeal is a diet used in the study to feed Tenebrio molitor larvae, resulting in the lowest mortality and highest metamorphosis rate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACIDS (FA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acids are analyzed in Tenebrio molitor larvae to determine the impact of different diets on their composition, with significant differences observed between diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LINOLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Linoleic acid is a fatty acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher content in larvae fed diets other than oatmeal (OAT).&lt;SEP&gt;Linoleic acid is a polyunsaturated fatty acid found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polystyrene diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Linoleic acid is an unsaturated fatty acid found in the biomass of Tenebrio larvae, contributing to their nutritional profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Linoleic acid is a polyunsaturated fatty acid found in mealworm larvae, with varying content depending on the type of feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALMITIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Palmitic acid is a fatty acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher content in the OAT diet compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Palmitic acid is a saturated fatty acid found in the biomass of Tenebrio larvae, contributing to their nutritional profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Palmitic acid, also known as n-hexadecanoic acid, is a saturated fatty acid commonly used in soap and detergent production, identified in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Palmitic acid is a saturated fatty acid present in mealworm larvae, with content varying based on the feed type.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STEARIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Stearic acid is a fatty acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher content in the OAT diet compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Stearic acid is a saturated fatty acid found in mealworm larvae, with its proportion varying depending on the feed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALMITOLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Palmitoleic acid is a fatty acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher content in the OAT diet compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Palmitoleic acid is a monoenoic fatty acid present in mealworm larvae, with content influenced by the feed type.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARGARIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Margaric acid is a fatty acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher content in the OAT diet compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARACHIDIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Arachidic acid is a fatty acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher content in the OAT diet compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MYRISTOLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Myristoleic acid is a fatty acid found in Tenebrio molitor larvae, with lower content in the OAT diet compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BREEDING GROWTH DYNAMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Breeding growth dynamics refers to the study of the development and growth patterns of Tenebrio molitor larvae over time under different diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolism is a critical aspect of the DEB model, used to analyze how mealworms process energy when fed polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolism refers to the biochemical processes in Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae that contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene, including changes in gut microbiome and molecular weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolism refers to the biochemical processes in larvae that are influenced by different diets and plastics, as studied in the research.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolism refers to the biochemical processes in mealworms that are affected by the lack of nutritional value in synthetic polymers, leading to mass loss.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTEROIDALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteroidales is an order of bacteria commonly found in the larvae's microbiome, contributing to digestion and biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the OAT diet group.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidales is an order of bacteria that is commonly found in the gut microbiome, with higher abundance in OAT and CEL diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 17.4% in the gut microbiome, showing higher levels in the OAT and CEL diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACTINOMYCETALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Actinomycetales is an order of bacteria found in the larvae's microbiome, playing a role in digestion and biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinomycetales is an order of bacteria that is prevalent in the gut microbiome, particularly in OAT and CEL diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinomycetales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 5.3% in the gut microbiome, showing higher levels in the OAT and CEL diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-ARABINOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">L-arabinose is a sugar found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with plastic-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;L-arabinose is a sugar intermediate in the pentose phosphate pathway, utilized by larvae during digestion and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;L-arabinose is a sugar that was taken up by the gut microbiota of mealworms in the PE and PS diet groups, indicating a unique metabolic capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;L-arabinose levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D-SORBITOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">D-sorbitol is a sugar alcohol found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polyethylene-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;D-sorbitol is a sugar alcohol whose utilization by larvae varies depending on the diet and plastic exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;D-sorbitol is a sugar taken up by the gut microbiota of mealworms in the PE-oxo diet group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UREASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Urease is an enzyme whose activity in larvae varies depending on the diet and plastic exposure, indicating metabolic changes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRISODIUM CITRATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trisodium citrate is a chemical compound found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polyethylene-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Trisodium citrate is a chemical compound whose utilization by larvae varies depending on the diet and plastic exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Trisodium citrate levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are negatively correlated with the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
