    </node>
    <node id="L-RHAMNOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">L-rhamnose is a sugar whose utilization by larvae varies depending on the diet and plastic exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;L-rhamnose levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLOSTRIDIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Clostridiales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PE-reg and OAT diet groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Clostridiales is an order of bacteria that is abundant in the gut microbiome, especially in the OAT diet, and less so in the PE-oxo diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Clostridiales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 14.5% in the gut microbiome, showing higher levels in the OAT diet and lower levels in the PE-oxo diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillales are bacteria found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with plastic-based diets, and may play a role in synthetic waste degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillales is an order of bacteria that is highly prolific in the PE-oxo, PE-reg, and PS groups of the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 10.5% in the gut microbiome, showing higher levels in the PE-oxo, PE-reg, and PS groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillales is an order of lactic acid bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PS and PE-oxo diet groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SELENOMONADALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Selenomonadales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, uniquely increased in the CEL diet group.&lt;SEP&gt;Selenomonadales is an order of bacteria that is most abundant in the CEL group of the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Selenomonadales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 3.0% in the gut microbiome, showing the highest levels in the CEL group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOBACTERALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodobacterales are bacteria negatively correlated with the second ordination axis in PCA analysis, indicating their role in the digestive system of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodobacterales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PE-oxo and OAT diet groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodobacterales is an order of bacteria that is most abundant in the OAT and PE-oxo groups of the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodobacterales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 3.9% in the gut microbiome, showing the highest levels in the OAT and PE-oxo groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROSOMONADALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrosomonadales are bacteria involved in nitrification, suggesting their role in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrosomonadales are bacteria positively correlated with the PS diet and α-linoleic acid in Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating their role in the larvae's digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrosomonadales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PS diet group, indicating a role in nitrogen metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrosomonadales is an order of bacteria that is highly abundant in the PS variant of the gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANCTOMYCETALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Planctomycetales are bacteria positively correlated with the PS diet and the second ordination axis in PCA analysis, indicating their role in the digestive system of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Planctomycetales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PS diet group, indicating a role in nitrogen metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Planctomycetales is an order of bacteria that is most abundant in the PS variant of the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Planctomycetales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 9.9% in the gut microbiome, showing the highest levels in the PS variant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAVOBACTERIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Flavobacteriales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the OAT diet group.&lt;SEP&gt;Flavobacteriales is an order of bacteria that is one of the predominant groups in the gut microbiome, with no variation by diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Flavobacteriales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 11.9% in the gut microbiome, showing no variation by diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPHINGOBACTERIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sphingobacteriales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 1.3% in the gut microbiome, showing no variation by diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Sphingobacteriales is an order of bacteria with low abundance in the gut microbiome, showing no variation by diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The PS diet refers to a diet variant that is positively correlated with protein levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae and the presence of specific bacteria in their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-OXO DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The PE-oxo diet is a diet variant that influences the abundance of specific bacterial orders, such as Lactobacillales and Enterobacteriales, in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-REG DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The PE-reg diet is a diet variant that affects the abundance of bacterial orders like Rhizobiales and Pseudomonadales in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CEL DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The CEL diet is a diet variant that influences the abundance of bacterial orders such as Selenomonadales and Bacteroidales in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OAT DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The OAT diet is a diet variant that affects the abundance of bacterial orders like Rhodobacterales and Clostridiales in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORIOBACTERIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Coriobacteriales is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 1.8% in the gut microbiome, showing lower fractions in the PS group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROSIRIPLAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrosiriplas is an order of bacteria with an average abundance of 1.5% in the gut microbiome, showing the highest levels in the PS variant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen metabolism in Tenebrio molitor larvae is positively correlated with the PS diet and the presence of the amoA gene, indicating its role in the larvae's digestive process.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen metabolism is a key process in the digestion of polystyrene by mealworms, involving the redistribution of nitrogen and amino acid synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen metabolism pathways are abundant in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating the role of gut microbes in nitrogen fixation and nutrient biosynthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen metabolism pathways are abundant in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating the role of gut microbes in nitrogen fixation and nutrient biosynthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen metabolism is a pathway stimulated in the gut of Tenebrio larvae during the biodegradation of polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UREASE GENE (UREC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The ureC gene is positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and MUFA content in Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating its role in nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROSPIRALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrosopirales are bacteria positively correlated with the PS diet and α-linoleic acid in Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating their role in the larvae's digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrospirales are bacteria with unique functions, possibly involved in the nitrification process during polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrospirales is an order of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PS diet group, indicating a role in nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMOA GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The amoA gene is responsible for nitrogen metabolism in Tenebrio molitor larvae and is positively correlated with the PS diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIFH GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The nifH gene encodes dinitrogen reductase and is used as a marker to detect nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The nifH gene encodes dinitrogenase reductase, an enzyme involved in nitrogen fixation, and its expression indicates the presence of nitrogen-fixing activity in gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;The nifH gene encodes nitrogenase, an enzyme involved in nitrogen fixation, and is used to identify nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The nifH gene is a genetic marker for nitrogen fixation, used to confirm the nitrogen-fixing ability of bacterial isolates.&lt;SEP&gt;The nifH gene is a marker gene for nitrogen fixation, used to study the diazotrophic community in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The nifH gene is associated with nitrogen fixation and was detected in the gut microbiota of mealworms, indicating the presence of nitrogen-fixing bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;The nifH gene is negatively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and positively correlated with PE-oxo and PE-reg diets in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIRS GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The nirS gene is negatively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and positively correlated with PE-oxo and PE-reg diets in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUFA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MUFA (monounsaturated fatty acids) content in Tenebrio molitor larvae is positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and the ureC gene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetic acid is an LC-MS grade chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Acetic acid levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARGAROLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Margaroleic acid levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEUCINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Leucine levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LYSINE DECARBOXYLASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lysine decarboxylase levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are negatively correlated with the first ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Α-LINOLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">α-linoleic acid levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae are positively correlated with the PS diet and the second ordination axis in PCA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CEREAL BRAN">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cereal bran is a natural food source used in combination with synthetic polymers like polystyrene to improve the survival rate of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID SYNTHESIS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid synthesis in mealworms is influenced by their diet, with synthetic polymers like polystyrene causing dysfunction in this process.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatty acid synthesis is potentially increased in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics, as indicated by elevated GABA and depleted Vitamin B6 intermediates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN SYNTHESIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein synthesis in Tenebrio molitor larvae is facilitated by the assimilation of atmospheric nitrogen by symbiotic bacteria in their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein synthesis in mealworms is influenced by their diet, with synthetic polymers like polystyrene affecting protein content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN CONVERSION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen conversion in mealworms is facilitated by microorganisms, which may redistribute nitrogen and synthesize amino acids, especially in protein-deficient diets like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTIC ACID BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactic acid bacteria are a group of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly abundant in the PS diet group, and are associated with inositol production and nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRYPTOPHAN DEAMINATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Tryptophan deamination is a metabolic activity observed in the gut microbiota of mealworms in the PE and PS diet groups, indicating a unique metabolic pathway.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INOSITOL PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Inositol production is a metabolic activity observed in the gut microbiota of mealworms in the PS diet group, linked to nitrogen metabolism and energy reduction strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRADYRHIZOBIACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bradyrhizobiaceae is a family of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, associated with inositol metabolism and nitrogen redistribution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETOBACTERACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetobacteraceae is a family of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of mealworms, associated with inositol metabolism and nitrogen redistribution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRUCTOSE AND MANNOSE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fructose and mannose metabolism is a metabolic pathway observed in the gut microbiota of mealworms, linked to inositol production and nitrogen redistribution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UREASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Urease activity is a metabolic activity observed in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PE-oxo diet group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADIPIC ACID CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Adipic acid consumption is a metabolic activity observed in the gut microbiota of mealworms in the CEL diet group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETOIN PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetoin production is a metabolic activity observed in the gut microbiota of mealworms in the CEL diet group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRISDE CITRATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trisde citrate is a compound taken up by the gut microbiota of mealworms in the PE-oxo diet group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN REDISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen redistribution is a process observed in the gut microbiota of mealworms, particularly in the PS diet group, indicating a role in amino acid synthesis and polymer degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR PROFILE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR profile analysis was used in the study to detect changes in the structure of polystyrene during digestion by mealworms, indicating partial depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UREASE SUBUNIT ALPHA (UREC) GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The ureC gene encodes a subunit of the urease enzyme, which is involved in the decomposition of urea, indicating the presence of bacteria capable of nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMMONIUM MONOOXYGENASE (AMOA) GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The amoA gene encodes an enzyme involved in nitrification, indicating an increase in the nitrifying potential of the digestive system microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROUS OXIDE REDUCTASE (NOSZ) GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The nosZ gene encodes an enzyme involved in denitrification, indicating the potential ability to convert reactive forms of nitric oxide into molecular nitrogen.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGENASE REDUCTASE (NIFH) GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The nifH gene encodes a component of the nitrogenase enzyme complex, which is involved in nitrogen fixation, indicating the presence of bacteria capable of nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLUS SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus spp. are bacteria that are part of the gut microbiome and are involved in various metabolic processes, including the degradation of synthetic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITRIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrification is a process in the nitrogen cycle where ammonia is converted into nitrates, which is observed in the gut microbiome of mealworms during polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DENITRIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Denitrification is the process of converting nitrates into nitrogen gas, facilitated by bacteria carrying the nosZ gene, which is observed in the gut microbiome of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMYGDALIN UTILIZATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Amygdalin utilization is a metabolic process observed in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polystyrene diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GONDOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gondoic acid is a monounsaturated fatty acid found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polystyrene diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEHENIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Behenic acid is a saturated fatty acid found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polyethylene-based diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-LYSINE DECARBOXYLASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">L-lysine decarboxylase is an enzyme found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with polyethylene-based diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARABIDAE FAMILY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Beetles of the Carabidae family are used as bioindicators to study the effects of environmental pollutants, such as heavy metals, on insect physiology and genetics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR SPECTROMETRY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR spectrometry is a technique used in the study to analyze the chemical composition of mealworm larvae and their digestive systems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTOZOA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Protozoa are microorganisms found in the digestive system of mealworms, contributing to the intensive digestive processes and survival on polymeric diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMERIC MONODIET">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">A polymeric monodiet refers to a diet consisting primarily of polymers, such as polyethylene, which mealworms can survive on but may have physiological impacts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT BIOINDICATORS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect bioindicators, such as mealworms and beetles of the Carabidae family, are used to study the effects of environmental pollutants on insect physiology and genetics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIGESTIVE SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The digestive system of mealworms undergoes biochemical changes when exposed to polymeric diets, such as polyethylene, indicating adaptation mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REPRODUCTIVE CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The reproductive cycle of mealworms is influenced by their diet, with some diets promoting rapid pupation to ensure species continuity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BODY MASS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Body mass is a key physiological trait in mealworms that is influenced by their diet, with some diets promoting prolonged generation times.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL CHANGES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical changes in mealworms are observed in response to different diets, particularly those involving polymeric substances like polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASSIMILATION MECHANISM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Assimilation mechanisms in mealworms facilitate their survival on polymeric monodiets, such as polyethylene, by adapting their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEDITERRANEAN FRUIT FLY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The Mediterranean fruit fly (Ceratitis capitata) is an insect whose microbiota changes during its development, as studied in microbial ecology research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOGICAL NITROGEN FIXATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biological nitrogen fixation is a process studied in insects, where nitrogen is converted into a usable form, impacting ecosystems and insect biology.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHIZOPERTHA DOMINICA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhizopertha dominica, a grain borer beetle, is studied for its interaction with plastic packaging in pest management research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOLOTRICHIA PARALLELA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Holotrichia parallela is a beetle species whose gut contains cellulolytic bacteria, which can break down cellulose.&lt;SEP&gt;Holotrichia parallela is an insect species whose gut microbiota, including P. nitroreducens and Rhizobium radiobacter, have been studied for their role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Holotrichia parallela larvae are known for their cellulolytic gut bacteria, which can degrade cellulose and other organic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULYTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulolytic bacteria are microorganisms found in the gut of Holotrichia parallela that can degrade cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOAMED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Foamed polystyrene is a type of plastic waste that can be recycled using insects like mealworms as part of sustainable development strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5</data>
    </node>
