      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="14C-LABELED PS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">14C-labeled PS is a form of polystyrene used in tracer studies to measure biodegradation rates in microbial consortia.&lt;SEP&gt;14C-labeled polystyrene is used in research studies to track the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene in various environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NYSTATIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nystatin is an antibiotic that can suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting their ability to biodegrade plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Nystatin is an antibiotic that can suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, impacting their digestive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Nystatin is an antibiotic that can suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMPICILLIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ampicillin is an antibiotic tested for its ability to suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ampicillin is an antibiotic that can suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Ampicillin is an antibiotic that can suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, impacting their ability to biodegrade plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Ampicillin is an antibiotic that suppresses the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, reducing their ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Ampicillin is an antibiotic used to suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, impacting their plastic degradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Ampicillin is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, impacting their ability to degrade PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Ampicillin is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, impacting their ability to degrade PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Ampicillin is an antibiotic that can suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, impacting their ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Ampicillin is an antibiotic that inhibits the growth of gut bacteria in mealworms, though less effectively than gentamicin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3</data>
      <data key="d1">API 20 E and API 20 NE tests are used to determine biochemical activity in the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemosphere publishes research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, highlighting its environmental significance.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLUYVERA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kluyvera sp. is a gut bacterium associated with the degradation of high molecular weight polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Kluyvera sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, strongly associated with the degradation of high molecular weight polypropylene (PP-H).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEDIOCOCCUS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pediococcus sp. is a genus of bacteria associated with the production of organic acids from water-soluble carbohydrates and shows a high correlation with PP degradation intermediates.&lt;SEP&gt;Pediococcus sp. is a gut bacterium associated with the degradation of high molecular weight polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYLACTIC ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polylactic acids (PLA) are biodegradable polymers derived from renewable resources, and their degradation has been studied in relation to Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAPHENE OXIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Graphene oxide is a chemically modified form of graphene, used in various applications due to its unique properties, including conductivity and strength.&lt;SEP&gt;Graphene oxide is a material derived from graphene, and its biodegradation has been explored in studies involving Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Graphene oxide is a material that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with the gut microbiome and enzymes playing a key role.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULOMA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Uloma sp. is a genus of darkling beetles whose larvae have been studied for their potential to biodegrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Uloma sp. larvae are a newly reported species capable of biodegrading polystyrene wastes, demonstrating the potential for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Uloma sp. is a honey bee pest that has been found to biodegrade plastics such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Uloma sp., a species of darkling beetle, has been confirmed to biodegrade polystyrene plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Uloma sp. is a genus of macroinvertebrates that can degrade plastics, contributing to environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Uloma sp. is an insect larva with plastic-degrading capabilities, studied alongside mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The transcriptome refers to the complete set of RNA transcripts produced by the genome of an organism, and in this context, it has been studied to understand the gene expression changes in Tenebrio molitor during plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIGESTIVE ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Digestive enzymes are activated in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae to facilitate the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).&lt;SEP&gt;Digestive enzymes are proteins that break down food and other substances, and their up-regulation in Tenebrio molitor has been linked to the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Digestive enzymes are proteins that facilitate the breakdown of complex substances, such as plastics, into simpler compounds during biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Digestive enzymes secreted by gut microbes and insect hosts like Tenebrio molitor are involved in the degradation of plastics, though the exact mechanisms remain unclear.&lt;SEP&gt;Digestive enzymes are proteins that facilitate the breakdown of complex substances, such as plastics, into simpler compounds during biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Digestive enzymes secreted by gut microbes and insect hosts like Tenebrio molitor are involved in the degradation of plastics, though the exact mechanisms remain unclear.&lt;SEP&gt;Digestive enzymes are essential for nutrient uptake in mealworms, and their production can be impaired by a plastic-only diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Digestive enzymes in mealworms contribute to the biodegradation of microplastics by breaking down plastic particles during digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC DEGRADATION-RELATED BACTERIAL ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic degradation-related bacterial enzymes are proteins produced by gut bacteria that assist in the breakdown of plastics, such as polypropylene, by Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYLACTIC ACIDS (PLA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polylactic acids (PLA) are biodegradable polymers derived from renewable resources, often used in packaging and biomedical applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOST GENE EXPRESSION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Host gene expression refers to the genetic activity of an organism, such as Tenebrio molitor, that influences its ability to biodegrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNERGISTIC EFFECT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Synergistic effect refers to the combined action of gut microbes and digestive enzymes in the depolymerization of polyethylene (PE) by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, which is depressed under antibiotic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Synergistic effect refers to the combined action of the host organism and its gut microbiota in enhancing the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA GENE AMPLICON SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing is a molecular technique used to analyze the composition of microbial communities, such as those in the gut of plastic-degrading insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOME PROFILING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transcriptome profiling is a method used to study gene expression patterns, providing insights into the molecular mechanisms of plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMERCIAL PP FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Commercial PP foam is a polypropylene-based material used in packaging and insulation, contributing to environmental pollution due to its resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOME DEVELOPMENT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbiome development refers to the changes in the gut microbial community of insects, which influence their ability to degrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYME SYNTHESIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzyme synthesis refers to the production of digestive enzymes by mealworms and superworms, which is essential for the biodegradation of plastics like polypropylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzyme synthesis refers to the production of enzymes by host organisms or their gut microbiota, which are essential for breaking down plastic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP MICROPLASTICS (PP MPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PP microplastics are small particles of polypropylene that are tested for consumption and degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP_{0.83}">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PP_{0.83} is a viscous liquid form of polypropylene used in biodegradation studies with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP_{6.2}">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PP_{6.2} is a gel-like soft solid form of polypropylene tested for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP_{50.4}">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PP_{50.4} is a nonuniform solid bulk material form of polypropylene used in biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP_{108}">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PP_{108} is a nonuniform solid bulk material form of polypropylene tested for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP_{575}">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PP_{575} is a pellet form of polypropylene (2-4 mm) used in biodegradation studies with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (^1H NMR) SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">^1H NMR spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical structure of polypropylene (PP) in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILLUMINA NOVASEQ PE250 PLATFORM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Illumina NovaSeq PE250 platform is used for high-throughput sequencing of bacterial 16S rRNA to analyze the gut microbiome of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STAINLESS-STEEL HIGH-THROUGHPUT TISSUE GRINDER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A stainless-steel high-throughput tissue grinder is used to reduce the size of PP samples to less than 75 µm for biodegradation testing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DELTA (Δ) NOTATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Delta (δ) notation is used to express the stable isotope signatures of residual plastics from frass relative to pristine PP plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMERCIAL PP MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Commercial PP microplastics are small plastic particles with a broad molecular weight distribution, studied for their biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE ISOTOPE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable isotope analysis is a method used to assess the biodegradation of petroleum-derived plastics, providing insights into the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Stable isotope analysis is a methodology used to assess the biodegradation of petroleum-derived plastics, such as PP, by analyzing isotopic fractionation.&lt;SEP&gt;Stable isotope analysis is a method used to monitor the biodegradation of plastics by measuring the isotopic exchange between carbon isotopes during the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Stable isotope analysis is a method used to monitor the biodegradation of plastics by measuring the isotopic exchange between carbon isotopes during the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPROPYLENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene microplastics (PP MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polypropylene, which are studied for their biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polypropylene, which can be biodegraded by certain bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE ISOTOPE FRACTIONATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable isotope fractionation is a technique used to analyze the biodegradation of plastics, such as polypropylene, by tracking isotopic changes in residual materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOTOPIC ENRICHMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Isotopic enrichment refers to the increase in the concentration of a specific isotope, such as 13C, in residual polypropylene after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECIFIC PP CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Specific PP consumption rate (SPCR) is a metric used to measure the rate at which Tenebrio molitor larvae consume polypropylene over a given period.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT CHANGE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight change refers to the change in mass of Tenebrio molitor larvae during experiments involving the consumption of polypropylene, indicating its role as a carbon and energy source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACHNATALEA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lachnatalea sp. is a dominant gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with the degradation of low molecular weight polypropylene (PP-L).&lt;SEP&gt;Lachnatalea sp. is a genus of bacteria that showed high abundance in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, suggesting its potential role in PET degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lachnatalea sp. is a genus of bacteria that showed high abundance in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, suggesting its potential role in PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRONOBACTER SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cronobacter sp. is a genus of bacteria that was dominant in the control group of Tenebrio molitor larvae, but its role in PET degradation is not well understood.&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter sp. is a genus of bacteria that was dominant in the control group of Tenebrio molitor larvae, but its role in PET degradation is not well understood.&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with the degradation of medium molecular weight polypropylene (PP-M).&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter sp. is a gut-associated bacterium in mealworms, playing a role in the microbial community during plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEDIOCCOCCUS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pediococcus sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, correlated with the degradation of high molecular weight polypropylene (PP-H).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAFNIA-OBSUMBACTER SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hafnia-Obsumbacter sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with PET and PS degradation in other organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXTA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixta sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with the degradation of medium molecular weight polypropylene (PP-M).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLOSTRIDIALES FAMILY XIV. INCERTAE SEDIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Clostridiales Family XIV. Incertae Sedis is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, enriched in PP-L-fed larvae and potentially involved in polypropylene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACHNOSPIRACEAE UNCLASSIFIED">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lachnospiraceae unclassified is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, enriched in PP-L-fed larvae and potentially involved in polypropylene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIRGIBACILLUS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Virgibacillus sp. is a gut bacterium in Tenebrio molitor larvae, enriched in PP-L-fed larvae and potentially involved in polypropylene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROCOCCUS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterococcus sp. is a bacterial genus involved in the biodegradation of plastics in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor, Galleria mellonella, and Zophobas atratus.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus sp. is a genus of bacteria identified as a key microorganism in the degradation of polypropylene (PP) and lignocellulosic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus sp. is a gut-associated bacterium in mealworms, contributing to the microbial community structure during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus sp. is a genus of gut bacteria in mealworms that plays a significant role in LDPE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CUTINASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cutinase is an enzyme produced by Humicola insolens that, along with Candida antarctica lipase (CALB), can synergistically hydrolyze PET into terephthalic acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutinase is an enzyme produced by Humicola insolens that, along with Candida antarctica lipase (CALB), can synergistically hydrolyze PET into terephthalic acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutinase is an enzyme with a higher abundance in PP-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating its potential role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER NYZ450">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter NyZ450 is a bacterial strain that cooperates with Bacillus spp. NyZ451 for the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) mulching film.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SPP. NYZ451">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus spp. NyZ451 is a bacterial strain that cooperates with Acinetobacter NyZ450 for the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) mulching film.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATP-BINDING CASSETTE (ABC) TRANSPORTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporter pathways are highly expressed in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed on polypropylene (PP), facilitating the importation of intermediate products for further metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUORUM SENSING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Quorum sensing is a bacterial communication process that coordinates microbial activities, such as plastic degradation, in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Quorum sensing is a bacterial communication system that regulates behaviors like motility and biofilm formation, influenced by chemicals like unsaturated fatty acids.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN METABOLISM PATHWAY (KO00910)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The nitrogen metabolism pathway (KO00910) is enriched in PP-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating nitrogen fixation by intestinal bacteria during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIAZOTROPHS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Diazotrophs are nitrogen-fixing bacteria that supplement the nitrogen supply of insects, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae, when fed a nitrogen-deficient diet of PP.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOST TRANSCRIPTOME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The host transcriptome analysis evaluates changes in gene expression in the intestines of Tenebrio molitor larvae during the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MONOOXYGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Monooxygenase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Monooxygenase is an enzyme involved in the oxidation and degradation of aromatic compounds, including polystyrene and lignin.&lt;SEP&gt;Monooxygenase is an enzyme involved in the oxidation and degradation of polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peroxidase is a degradative enzyme upregulated in Enterobacter LG3 during the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Peroxidase is an enzyme involved in the oxidative degradation of polystyrene and lignin, found in PS-/CS-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Peroxidase is an enzyme that aids in the biodegradation of polypropylene by breaking down the polymer chains.&lt;SEP&gt;Peroxidase is an enzyme that facilitates the degradation of lignin and polystyrene by oxidizing aromatic rings and cleaving carbon-carbon bonds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOMIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transcriptomic analysis was conducted to study the gene expression changes in Tenebrio molitor larvae when fed with polypropylene of varying molecular weights.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRPS (MULTIDRUG RESISTANCE PROTEINS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Multidrug resistance proteins (MRPs) are involved in the efflux of plastics within cells, reducing metabolic pressure exerted by plastic diets in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSMEMBRANE PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Transmembrane proteins transport fragmented polypropylene molecules into microbial cells for further metabolism during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOEMULSIFYING FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioemulsifying factors secreted by Tenebrio molitor larvae and gut microbiota help attach digestive enzymes to the surface of polypropylene polymers, aiding in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INNATE DIGESTIVE ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Innate digestive enzymes in Tenebrio molitor larvae are up-regulated when fed polypropylene, aiding in the degradation of plastic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-DEGRADATION-RELATED ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-degradation-related enzymes are produced by Tenebrio molitor larvae to break down polypropylene polymers into smaller molecules.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIGNAL TRANSDUCTION GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Signal transduction genes are involved in the adaptive response of Tenebrio molitor larvae to nutrient-deficient conditions induced by polypropylene diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ΔΔ13C">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Δδ13C is a measure used to assess the biodegradation of polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating changes in carbon isotope composition before and after degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIGNAL TRANSDUCTION FUNCTIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Signal transduction functions are activated in Tenebrio molitor larvae to support the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOHYDRATE TRANSPORT AND METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbohydrate transport and metabolism-related functions are activated in Tenebrio molitor larvae to aid in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OMICS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Omics analysis is a method used to study the biodegradation process of polypropylene (PP) by Tenebrio molitor larvae, including 16S rRNA analyses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RRNA ANALYSES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rRNA analyses are used to study the gut bacteria of Tenebrio molitor larvae and their role in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSICAL PROPERTIES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Physical properties of polymers, such as particle size, affect their biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TARGETED MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Targeted microbes are selected based on their ability to biodegrade polypropylene (PP) with different molecular weights (MWs).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRAIG S. CRIDDLE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Craig S. Criddle is a professor at Stanford University who provided support and suggestions during the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Craig S. Criddle is a professor at Stanford University who provides support and suggestions for research on plastic degradation and environmental management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CECEP DIGITAL TECHNOLOGY CO., LTD.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">CECEP Digital Technology Co., Ltd. provided algorithm models and tools for the study.&lt;SEP&gt;CECEP Digital Technology Co., Ltd. provides algorithm models and tool support for research on plastic degradation and environmental management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEILONGJIANG PROVINCE TOUYAN TEAM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Heilongjiang Province Touyan Team contributed to the study.&lt;SEP&gt;The Heilongjiang Province Touyan Team supports research initiatives, including studies on plastic degradation and environmental sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATP-BINDING CASSETTE (ABC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ATP-binding cassette (ABC) is a protein involved in the transport of molecules across cellular membranes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROAD EXTENT OF DEPOLYMERIZATION (BD)">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Broad extent of depolymerization (BD) refers to the extensive breakdown of polymer chains during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (^1H NMR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (^1H NMR) is a technique used to determine the structure of molecules by observing the magnetic properties of hydrogen atoms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-TEMPERATURE-GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (HT-GPC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High-temperature-gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) is a technique used to analyze polymers at elevated temperatures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KYOTO ENCYCLOPAEDIA OF GENES AND GENOMES (KEGG)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kyoto Encyclopaedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) is a database resource for understanding high-level functions and utilities of biological systems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIMITED EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION (LD)">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Limited extent depolymerization (LD) refers to the partial breakdown of polymer chains during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUMBER-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Number-average molecular weight (Mn) is a measure of the average molecular weight of a polymer, calculated based on the number of molecules.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIZE-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MZ)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Size-average molecular weight (Mz) is a measure of the average molecular weight of a polymer, calculated based on the size of molecules.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DISTRIBUTIONS (MWD)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight distributions (MWD) describe the range of molecular weights present in a polymer sample.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYDISPERSITY INDEX (PDI)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polydispersity index (PDI) is a measure of the distribution of molecular mass in a given polymer sample, calculated as the ratio of weight-average molecular weight (Mw) to number-average molecular weight (Mn).&lt;SEP&gt;Polydispersity index (PDI) is a measure of the distribution of molecular weights in a polymer sample.&lt;SEP&gt;The polydispersity index (PDI) is a measure of the distribution of molecular weights in a polymer, calculated as the ratio of weight-average molecular weight to number-average molecular weight.&lt;SEP&gt;The polydispersity index (PDI) is calculated to assess the molecular weight distribution of plastics before and after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The polydispersity index (PDI) measures the heterogeneity in the chain-length distributions of polymers, indicating changes in molecular weight during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The polydispersity index (PDI) is a measure of the distribution of molecular weights in a polymer, indicating the heterogeneity of the polymer chains.&lt;SEP&gt;The polydispersity index is a measure of the distribution of molecular weights in the residual polystyrene after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL RATES (SRS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival rates (SRs) are measured to assess the viability of mealworms fed different diets, including plastics and bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Survival rates (SRs) are measures used to determine the proportion of individuals or organisms that survive under specific conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR (T. MOLITOR)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor (T. molitor) is a species of darkling beetle larvae known for its ability to biodegrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2,4-TRICHLOROBENZENE (TCB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (TCB) is a chlorinated aromatic compound used as a solvent and in chemical synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (TCB) is a solvent used in high-temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) to analyze residual PE polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (TCB) is a solvent used to extract residual polypropylene (PP) from frass samples for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (TCB) is a solvent used for extracting PE polymers in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-EATING MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A research study focused on the ability of mealworms to biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene, highlighting the role of gut microorganisms in this process.&lt;SEP&gt;A research study focused on the ability of mealworms to biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene, highlighting the role of gut microorganisms in this process.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are larvae that can chew and digest polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating mealworms, specifically Tenebrio molitor, are studied for their ability to degrade plastics like polyethylene and polystyrene through their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating mealworms refer to Tenebrio molitor larvae that have been observed to consume and degrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating mealworms refer to Tenebrio molitor larvae that have been observed to consume and degrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) are studied for their ability to biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULOMA SP. LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Uloma sp. larvae are a newly reported species capable of biodegrading polystyrene wastes, demonstrating the potential for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Uloma sp. larvae are reported to biodegrade polyethylene wastes, with studies focusing on their ability to complete their life cycle while feeding on polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC FRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic fragmentation refers to the breakdown of plastic particles during biodegradation, as observed in mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID HOMEOSTASIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid homeostasis refers to the balance of lipids in the body, which is affected by the metabolism of plastics like LDPE in Galleria mellonella larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX WORM SALIVA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax worm saliva contains enzymes that are key to the degradation of polyethylene by Galleria mellonella larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHER-POLYURETHANE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyether-polyurethane foam is a material that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with effects on the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-polyurethane foam is a synthetic material that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, affecting their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-polyurethane foam is a type of plastic waste that can be biodegraded by yellow mealworms, impacting their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-polyurethane foam is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC MARINE DEBRIS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic marine debris refers to plastic waste found in marine environments, contributing to pollution and harming marine life.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic marine debris refers to plastic waste found in marine environments, which poses significant threats to marine life and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic marine debris refers to plastic waste in marine environments, which can be degraded by organisms like Galleria mellonella and Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic marine debris refers to plastic waste found in marine environments, which poses threats to marine life and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic marine debris refers to plastic waste found in marine environments, which poses threats to marine life and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental degradation refers to the deterioration of the environment due to pollutants like plastic marine debris, which is a significant concern in ecological studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental degradation refers to the deterioration of the environment through the accumulation of pollutants like plastics, which are studied for their degradation trends.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL FUNCTIONAL ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial functional enzymes are proteins produced by microorganisms that play a key role in the biodegradation of plastics, such as polystyrene and polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER SIZE CHART">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A polymer size chart is used to analyze the degradation of plastics, such as polystyrene, by organisms like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT METABOLOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut metabolome of mealworms plays a role in the biodegradation of PET intermediates, contributing to the breakdown of plastic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut metabolome of mealworms plays a role in the biodegradation of PET intermediates, contributing to the breakdown of plastic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut metabolome refers to the collection of metabolites in the gut of organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor, which play a role in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOST GENES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Host genes in mealworms are upregulated during PET depolymerization and metabolism, playing a crucial role in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Host genes in mealworms are upregulated during PET depolymerization and metabolism, playing a crucial role in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Host genes in organisms like Tenebrio molitor are studied to understand their role in the biodegradation of plastics, such as polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELEMENTAL ANALYSER/ISOTOPE RATIO MASS SPECTROMETRY (EA/IRMS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">EA/IRMS is a technique used to characterize plastic polymers and study their degradation by organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC MORPHOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic morphology refers to the physical structure of plastics, which can change during biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE ISOTOPIC COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable isotopic composition is used to study the degradation of plastics, such as polystyrene, by analyzing changes in isotopic ratios.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIAZOTROPHIC COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Diazotrophic communities are groups of microorganisms capable of nitrogen fixation, studied in the context of plastic-eating mealworms (Tenebrio molitor).&lt;SEP&gt;The diazotrophic community refers to nitrogen-fixing microorganisms in the gut of organisms like Tenebrio molitor, which may play a role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA PNEUMONIAE MK-1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella pneumoniae MK-1 is a soil bacterium isolated for its ability to degrade polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(LACTIC)-BASED BIOPLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(lactic)-based bioplastics are biodegradable plastics that can be valorized using actinobacteria like Saccharothrix sp. MY1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SACCHAROTHRIX SP. MY1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Saccharothrix sp. MY1 is an actinobacterium used for the biodegradation of poly(lactic)-based bioplastics, utilizing silk wastewater as a nutrient source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MONOCHAMUS ALTERNATUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Monochamus alternatus, a beetle species, is studied for its genes relevant to pesticide resistance and biology, providing insights into insect adaptation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYSTEINE DIGESTIVE PROTEINASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cysteine digestive proteinases are enzymes found in Coleoptera that play a role in the digestion of proteins and other organic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLA-DEGRADING ACTINOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PLA-degrading actinobacteria, such as Saccharothrix sp. MY1, are microorganisms capable of breaking down poly(lactic)-based bioplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC REMEDIATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic remediation refers to the use of enzymes to degrade plastic polymers like polyethylene terephthalate (PET) for effective plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GROUP II CHITINASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Group II chitinase (ChtII) is an enzyme involved in chitin hydrolysis in insects, playing a role in their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipases are enzymes in the gut of mealworms that assist in breaking down lipid-like components of polyurethane.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipases are enzymes that contribute to the biodegradation of polyurethane by breaking its chemical bonds.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipases are enzymes that play a role in breaking down ester bonds in polyurethane (PU) foam during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipases are enzymes used for the synthesis and degradation of polymers, offering a sustainable method for managing plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipases are enzymes involved in the hydrolysis of lipids, found in PS-/CS-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipases are enzymes that break down lipids, tested using Tween 80 as a substrate in bacterial activity assays.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL PERSISTENCE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental persistence refers to the ability of synthetic polymers to remain in the environment for extended periods, contributing to pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
      <data key="d1">CECEP Digital Technology Co., Ltd. provided algorithm models and tools for the study, leveraging artificial intelligence.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STUDY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
      <data key="d1">The Heilongjiang Province Touyan Team contributed to the study, providing support and resources.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC POLYMERS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70</data>
      <data key="d1">EA/IRMS is used to characterize plastic polymers and study their degradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PESTICIDES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
      <data key="d1">Monochamus alternatus is studied for its genes relevant to pesticide resistance, providing insights into insect adaptation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLEOPTERA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
      <data key="d1">Coleoptera is an order of insects that includes beetles, some of which, like mealworms, have been studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cysteine digestive proteinases are enzymes found in Coleoptera that aid in the digestion of proteins and organic materials.</data>
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHITIN HYDROLYSIS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
      <data key="d1">Group II chitinase (ChtII) is involved in chitin hydrolysis in insects, aiding in their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIISOCYANATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Diisocyanate is a chemical compound used in the synthesis of polyurethane, contributing to its resistant and durable properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyol is a chemical compound that reacts with diisocyanate to form polyurethane, a widely used polymer in various industries.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHOLESTEROL ESTERASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cholesterol esterases are enzymes that play a role in the biodegradation of polyurethane by breaking down its chemical bonds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Proteases are enzymes in the gut of mealworms that degrade protein-like structures in polyurethane.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteases are enzymes involved in the biodegradation of polyurethane by breaking down its chemical structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteases are enzymes that degrade proteins and may contribute to the breakdown of polyurethane (PU) foam under certain conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteases are enzymes that break down proteins, tested using casein as a substrate in bacterial activity assays.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARACOCCUS SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Paracoccus sp. is a microorganism investigated for its role in the biodegradation of polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROPHOTOGRAPHY (SEM)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM is used to analyze the physical changes in polyurethane foam after being fed to mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microphotography (SEM) is a technique used in this study to observe the physical changes in polyurethane foam during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDUCTIVELY COUPLED PLASMA (ICP) ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Inductively Coupled Plasma (ICP) analysis was used to check for the presence of heavy metals in the polyurethane composition that could be toxic to Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYME ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzyme activities in the gut of mealworms, including esterases, lipases, proteases, and laccases, are measured to understand their role in breaking down polyurethane bonds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACCASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccases are enzymes in the gut of mealworms that oxidize phenolic compounds, potentially aiding in polyurethane degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccases are enzymes that may play a role in the degradation of polyurethane (PU) foam, particularly in oxidative processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccases are enzymes produced by Achromobacter xylosidans M9, which play a role in the degradation of environmental pollutants, including plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccases are enzymes that oxidize phenolic compounds, tested using guaiacol as a substrate in bacterial activity assays.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PU-VIRGIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PU-virgin refers to the original, unaltered polyurethane foam used as a control in the biodegradation study.&lt;SEP&gt;PU-virgin refers to the original, unaltered polyurethane foam used as a control in the experiment to compare degradation effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A thermogravimetric analyzer is used to measure changes in the thermal properties of polyurethane foam during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR-ATR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR-ATR is a technique used to analyze chemical bond changes in polyurethane foam after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SODIUM HYPOCHLORITE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium hypochlorite is used to wash polyurethane foam to eliminate microorganisms before SEM analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLATINUM COATING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Platinum coating is applied to polyurethane foam samples for SEM analysis to improve imaging quality.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOSPHATE BUFFER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phosphate buffer is a solution used to prepare nano-polystyrene emulsions for experimental assays.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate buffer is used to prepare gut homogenates for enzyme activity analysis in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P-NITROANILINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">P-nitroaniline is used as a substrate to measure protease activity in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;p-Nitroaniline is a compound used in the spectrophotometric determination of protease activity through hydrolysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT DNA EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut DNA extraction is a method used to analyze the microbial composition in the gut of mealworms, particularly those fed with polyurethane foam or wheat bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMPLICON SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Amplicon sequencing is a technique used to study the microbial diversity in the gut of mealworms, providing insights into their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Protease activity is an enzymatic process measured in mealworms to assess their ability to break down proteins, which is relevant to biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Protease activity refers to the enzymatic breakdown of proteins, which can be detected using bromocresolgreen dye.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTERASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Esterase activity is an enzymatic process measured in mealworms to evaluate their capacity to degrade ester-containing compounds, important for biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipase activity is an enzymatic process measured in mealworms to determine their ability to break down lipids, which is relevant to biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipase activity refers to the enzymatic breakdown of lipids, which is relevant in the metabolic processes of Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipase activity refers to the enzymatic breakdown of lipids, which is relevant in the metabolic processes of Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACCASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccase activity is an enzymatic process measured in mealworms to assess their ability to degrade phenolic compounds, which is important for biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase activity refers to the enzymatic activity of Lac640, which is involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS) through oxidative processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENEQUANT 1300 SPECTROPHOTOMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The GeneQuant 1300 spectrophotometer is an instrument used to measure enzyme activity in samples, including protease, esterase, lipase, and laccase activities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCINE NA–OH BUFFER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycine Na–OH buffer is a chemical solution used in the reaction mixture for measuring protease activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAPNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">BAPNA (Nα-Benzoyl-DL-arginine 4-nitroanilide hydrochloride) is a substrate used in the spectrophotometric determination of protease activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P-NITROPHENYL ACETATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">p-Nitrophenyl acetate is a compound used in the spectrophotometric determination of esterase activity through hydrolysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SODIUM PHOSPHATE BUFFER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium phosphate buffer is a chemical solution used in the reaction mixture for measuring esterase and lipase activities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P-NITROPHENYL LAURATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">p-Nitrophenyl laurate is a compound used in the spectrophotometric determination of lipase activity through hydrolysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ABTS (2,2'-azino-bis(3-ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfonic acid)) is a substrate used in the spectrophotometric determination of laccase activity.&lt;SEP&gt;ABTS is a substrate used in kinetic analysis to measure the laccase activity of Lac640, an enzyme involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SODIUM ACETATE BUFFER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium acetate buffer is a chemical solution used in the reaction mixture for measuring laccase activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNEAZY POWERSOIL KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The DNeasy PowerSoil Kit is a chemical kit used for DNA extraction from the gut of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUBIT 3.0 FLUOROMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Qubit 3.0 Fluorometer is an instrument used to evaluate the quantity and quality of DNA extracts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QIIME2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">QIIME2 is a bioinformatics tool used for analyzing amplicon sequencing data, including denoising and taxonomic classification.&lt;SEP&gt;QIIME2 is a software tool used for analyzing microbial community data, including the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DADA2 ALGORITHM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The DADA2 algorithm is a bioinformatics method used for denoising amplicon sequencing data to obtain Amplicon Sequence Variants (ASVs).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SILVA 132 DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The SILVA 132 database is a reference database used for taxonomic classification of microbial sequences in bioinformatics analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PU FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane (PU) foam is a synthetic material that poses environmental challenges due to its resistance to degradation, but mealworms have shown the ability to consume and degrade it.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PU) foam is a synthetic polymer used in various applications, which can be degraded by microorganisms and enzymes in the mealworm gut.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxic compounds are byproducts of PU foam degradation that mealworms must metabolize, contributing to weight loss and energy expenditure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM WEIGHT LOSS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm weight loss is a metric used to assess the impact of consuming PU foam, which is less nutritious and more toxic than bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PU CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PU consumption refers to the amount of polyurethane foam consumed by mealworms, which is lower than bran consumption due to its lower nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm metabolism is the process by which mealworms break down and utilize nutrients from their diet, including PU foam and bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm metabolism plays a crucial role in their ability to digest plastics, with implications for their weight changes and viability over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm metabolism refers to the biological processes that occur during the consumption and degradation of plastics, affecting mass and viability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-O-C BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The C-O-C bond is a functional group in PU foam that shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-N BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The C-N bond is a functional group in PU foam that shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-H BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The N-H bond is a functional group in PU foam that shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C=O BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The C=O bond is a functional group in PU foam that shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CH2 BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The CH2 bond is a functional group in PU foam that shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OH BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The OH bond is a functional group in PU foam that shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CH BONDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The CH bonds are functional groups in PU foam that show increased intensity in PU-virgin compared to degraded PU, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORONA PROTEIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Corona protein is a substance that can inhibit the absorption of nanoparticles, such as nanoplastics, by intestinal cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMMUNE SYSTEM MOLECULES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecules related to the immune system may be synthesized in response to stress or nutrient scarcity in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEDIOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pediococcus is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, potentially involved in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Pediococcus is a genus of bacteria that shows high abundance in the gut of mealworms fed with polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS).&lt;SEP&gt;Pediococcus is a genus of lactic acid bacteria in the Firmicutes phylum, associated with maintaining gut health and contributing to PU degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pediococcus is a lactic acid bacterium that increases in abundance in the mealworm gut when fed a co-diet of plastics and bran, suggesting a role in maintaining gut health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARACLOSTRIDIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Paraclostridium is a bacterial genus that may play a role in the degradation of polyurethane in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Paraclostridium is a genus of bacteria in the Firmicutes phylum, found in the mealworm gut microbiome and associated with the PU diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KOSAKONIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kosakonia is a bacterial genus that may be involved in the degradation of polyurethane in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosakonia is a genus of bacteria in the Enterobacteriaceae family, found in the mealworm gut microbiome and associated with the degradation of plastics like PE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NON-METRICAL MULTIDIMENSIONAL SCALING (NMDS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Non-metrical multidimensional scaling (NMDS) is a statistical method used to analyze differences in the mealworm gut microbiome between PU and bran diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILLUMINA MISEQ ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Illumina MiSeq analysis is a sequencing technique used to study changes in the mealworm gut microbiome community under different diets, such as PU and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOMERGER PROJECT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Biomerger Project (2i18sae00058) is a strategic initiative funded to study the biodegradation of polyurethane by Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIO4PURT PROJECT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The BIO4Purt project (TED2021-131894B-C21) is a research initiative aimed at transitioning to ecological and digital solutions, including the study of polyurethane biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic assay is a method used to identify the presence and activity of enzymes such as laccases, esterases, and lipases in bacterial strains, contributing to polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic assays are conducted to study the activity of enzymes produced by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae in degrading polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial community assays are performed to identify and analyze the microorganisms involved in the biodegradation of polyurethane by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALTERNARIA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Alternaria sp. is a microorganism isolated for its ability to degrade ether-type polyurethane, contributing to the understanding of polyurethane biodegradation mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHOLESTEROL ESTERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cholesterol esterase is an enzyme capable of degrading poly(ether urethane) and poly(carbonate urethane), highlighting its role in enzymatic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cholesterol esterase is an enzyme that plays a role in the degradation of polyurethanes, highlighting its importance in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM PASTE">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm paste, derived from Tenebrio molitor, is explored as an alternative protein source for feed and food applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGAL BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungal biodegradation involves the breakdown of materials, such as polyurethanes, by fungi, which produce enzymes capable of degrading complex polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHER POLYURETHANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyether polyurethane is another type of polyurethane that is studied for its biodegradability by environmental fungi.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UP-CYCLING OF POLYURETHANES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Up-cycling of polyurethanes refers to the process of converting waste polyurethanes into higher-value products through biodegradation or other methods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LUPHIOSILURUS ALEXANDRI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Luphiosilurus alexandri is a fish species from which trypsin has been purified and characterized for biochemical studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXO-1,4,Β-GLUCANASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Exo-1,4,β-glucanases are enzymes that break down cellulose and are selectively determined in mixtures of cellulolytic enzymes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QIIME 2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">QIIME 2 is a tool for reproducible, interactive, scalable, and extensible microbiome data science, as described in the study by Caporaso et al. (2019).&lt;SEP&gt;QIIME 2 is a tool used for reproducible, interactive, scalable, and extensible microbiome data science, facilitating the analysis of microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DADA2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DADA2 is a method for high-resolution sample inference from Illumina amplicon data, used in microbiome research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAPESEED OIL-BASED POLYOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Rapeseed oil-based polyol is a bio-based material used in the preparation of rigid polyurethane foams, offering a sustainable alternative to petroleum-based polyols.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALM OIL-BASED POLYESTER POLYOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Palm oil-based polyester polyol is a biodegradable material used in the production of water-blown porous polyurethane foams.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METATHESIZED PALM OIL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Metathesized palm oil is a chemically modified oil used in the production of lipid-based polymers and polyurethane foams, improving their functional properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Metathesized palm oil is a modified lipid used to improve the functional properties of lipid-based polymers and derived polyurethane foams.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMMUNO-TOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Immuno-toxicity refers to the adverse effects of nano- and microplastics on the immune system, as studied in various organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL-BURIED POLYESTER POLYURETHANE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil-buried polyester polyurethane refers to plastic waste buried in soil, which can be degraded by fungi over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT MIDGUT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The insect midgut is a key organ involved in digestion and nutrient absorption, playing a role in the breakdown of complex materials like plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIGESTIVE ENZYME CORONA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Digestive enzyme corona refers to the layer of enzymes that form around nanoparticles in the gastrointestinal tract, influencing their uptake by epithelial cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANO- AND MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Nano- and microplastics are small plastic particles that pose significant environmental and health risks, particularly in marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANDIDA RUGOSA LIPASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Candida rugosa lipase is an enzyme that catalyzes the degradation of polyurethane in aqueous environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS CHLORORAPHIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas chlororaphis is a bacterial strain reported to contribute to the biodegradation of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) in selective enrichment cultures.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas chlororaphis is a bacterium that encodes the polyester polyurethanase gene (PueA), which is involved in the degradation of polyurethane.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas chlororaphis is a bacterium that produces a lipase enzyme capable of degrading polyester polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYESTER POLYURETHANASE GENE (PUEA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The polyester polyurethanase gene (PueA) encodes a lipase enzyme involved in the degradation of polyester polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUEA GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The PueA gene from Pseudomonas chlororaphis encodes a lipase that plays a role in the degradation of polyester polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethanase is an enzyme encoded by the PueA gene, which is involved in the degradation of polyester polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANIC WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Organic waste is transformed by yellow mealworms into proteins, fats, and other nutrients, contributing to waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (1H-NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">1H-NMR is a method used to study the chemical modifications in polyethylene after degradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H-NMR) is a technique used to analyze the molecular structure of fecula, providing insights into the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H-NMR) is a technique used to analyze the molecular structure of fecula, providing insights into the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC) FILM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) film is a synthetic plastic material used in the study, containing polyvinyl chloride, plasticizers, flame retardants, antiaging agents, adhesives, and chlorides.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC DEGRADATION EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic degradation efficiency refers to the ability of yellow mealworms to break down plastics, such as PE, PS, and PVC, into smaller molecules or CO2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHITE POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">White pollution refers to the environmental issue caused by plastic waste, which insect larvae are being studied to address through biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;White pollution refers to the environmental issue caused by the accumulation of non-biodegradable plastics, which yellow mealworms can help mitigate through biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITIONAL COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutritional composition refers to the analysis of the nutrient content of yellow mealworms after consuming plastics, which is important for assessing their suitability as a food source.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritional composition studies analyze the nutrient content of edible insects, including proteins, fats, and minerals, to determine their suitability as food sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUSTAINABLE PLASTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Sustainable plastic degradation refers to the environmentally friendly process of breaking down plastics using yellow mealworms, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEED MOISTURE CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Feed moisture content is maintained at 18% in the study to ensure optimal conditions for yellow mealworm growth and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KJELDAHL METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Kjeldahl method is a standardized procedure used to determine the nitrogen content in samples, which is then used to calculate crude protein content.&lt;SEP&gt;The Kjeldahl method is used to determine the protein content in yellow mealworm samples, with a protein-to-nitrogen conversion factor of 6.25.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLORIMETRIC METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The colorimetric method is used to measure total phosphorus content in yellow mealworm samples, following the GB/T 6437 standard.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAME PHOTOMETRY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Flame photometry is used to measure total potassium content in yellow mealworm samples, following the GB 9836–88 standard.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE (ANOVA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Analysis of variance (ANOVA) is performed to statistically analyze the differences in plastic degradation rates among treatments, using Duncan’s new multiple range method.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VACUUM CRYODESICCATION METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The vacuum cryodesiccation method is used to measure moisture content in yellow mealworm samples, ensuring accurate moisture calculations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE PROTEIN CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude protein content is a key nutritional indicator for yellow mealworms, which is affected by the type and amount of plastic they consume.&lt;SEP&gt;Crude protein content is determined using a DumaTherm Nitrogen/Protein analyzer in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEGRADATION EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Degradation efficiency refers to the ability of yellow mealworms to break down different types of plastics, with PS being the most suitable for continuous transformation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DAPHNIA MAGNA STRAUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Daphnia magna Straus is a species of water flea used in studies to assess the toxic effects of plastics, such as PVC, on aquatic organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXIC EFFECT">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxic effect refers to the adverse impacts of plastics, such as PVC, on organisms, including increased mortality and oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRASS CARP COMPOUND FEED STANDARD SC/T1024-2002">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">This standard specifies the minimum crude protein content required for fish feed, ensuring nutritional adequacy for edible fish and fingerlings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM MORTALITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm mortality is a key metric used to assess the toxic effects of different plastics, with PVC showing the highest mortality rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE PROTEIN ACCUMULATION">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude protein accumulation in yellow mealworms is influenced by the type and amount of plastic consumed, with PE showing the highest protein content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTESTINAL FLORA COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Intestinal flora composition in yellow mealworms changes based on the type of plastic consumed, affecting their digestion and nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COPPER PHOSPHATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Copper phosphate is a flame retardant used in studies to evaluate the thermal properties and fire behavior of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MELAMINE PHOSPHATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Melamine phosphate is a flame retardant used in combination with copper phosphate to study the fire behavior of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDOCRINE DISRUPTION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Endocrine disruption refers to the adverse effects caused by the ingestion of polyethylene with sorbed chemical pollutants, as observed in adult fish.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRASS CARP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Grass carp are studied for their dietary protein requirements, with research focusing on serum biochemistry, liver histology, and transcriptome profiling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIGHEAD CARP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bighead carp are studied for their response to different dietary protein levels, with research focusing on serum biochemistry, liver histology, and transcriptome profiling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG-TERM EFFECT">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The long-term effect of plastic feeding on the growth and transcriptomic response of mealworms is assessed to understand the impact of polystyrene on their development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FACTORS AFFECTING BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The research study investigates the factors that influence the biodegradation rates of polystyrene by yellow mealworms, such as environmental conditions and gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOMIC RESPONSE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The transcriptomic response of mealworms to long-term plastic feeding is analyzed to understand the molecular and genetic changes induced by polystyrene consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptomic response refers to changes in gene expression in mealworms when exposed to plastic feeding, studied to understand their adaptability and biodegradation mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Transcriptomic response refers to changes in gene expression, studied in mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) to understand the long-term effects of plastic feeding on their growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIETARY PROTEIN">
      <data key="d2">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
      <data key="d1">Grass carp are studied for their dietary protein requirements, with research focusing on serum biochemistry and liver histology.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYHYDROXYALKONATES (PHA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyhydroxyalkonates (PHA) are biodegradable polymers developed as alternatives to petroleum-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(BUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE) (PBAT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) is a biodegradable plastic that mealworms can degrade and use as an energy supplement.&lt;SEP&gt;Poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) is a biodegradable polymer that can be biodegraded and assimilated by Tenebrio molitor larvae for energy supplementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) is a biodegradable polymer used as an alternative to petroleum-based plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) is a biodegradable polymer that can be fragmented by earthworms (Lumbricus terrestris) into smaller microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WOODLICE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Woodlice (Porcellio scaber) are macroinvertebrates capable of ingesting and biofragmenting plastics, contributing to the formation of micro- and nanoplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Woodlice are macroinvertebrates that have been studied for their role in the generation and accumulation of nanoplastics (NPs) through biofragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENCHYTRAEIDS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enchytraeids (Enchytraeus crypticus) are small soil-dwelling organisms that can ingest and biofragment plastics, contributing to microplastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Enchytraeids are macroinvertebrates that have been studied for their role in the generation and accumulation of nanoplastics (NPs) through biofragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYRALID MOTH FAMILY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The pyralid moth family (Lepidoptera: Pyralidae) includes insect larvae capable of biodegrading petroleum-based plastics through digestive enzymes in their gut.&lt;SEP&gt;The pyralid moth family (Lepidoptera: Pyralidae) includes insect larvae capable of biodegrading petroleum-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSIOLOGICAL STRESS RESPONSES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Physiological stress responses are the biological reactions of organisms to stressors, such as microplastics, including oxidative stress and inflammatory responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD WEBS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Food webs are interconnected networks of organisms in an ecosystem, which can be disrupted by the accumulation of microplastics and nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIOXIDANT ENZYME ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Antioxidant enzyme activities are biochemical indicators used to assess the oxidative stress responses in macroinvertebrates during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antioxidant enzyme activities are biochemical processes that counteract oxidative stress, observed in mealworms exposed to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHTHALIC ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phthalic acids are chemical compounds that were not detected in the plastic materials used in the study, with concentrations below 0.5 ppm.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLORINATED INTERMEDIATES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Chlorinated intermediates are byproducts generated during the degradation of PVC, which may have a negative impact on the larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Chlorinated intermediates are byproducts released during the biodegradation of polyvinyl chloride (PVC), contributing to oxidative stress in organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Chlorinated intermediates are toxic byproducts produced during the biodegradation of PVC by mealworms, potentially harming their intestinal health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KAPLAN-MEIER SURVIVAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kaplan-Meier survival analysis was used to determine the statistical significance of changes in the survival rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae over a 6-week period.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-degrading larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, are capable of digesting and breaking down certain types of plastics, including PLA, PS, and PVC, through enzymatic and microbial processes in their gut.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHOBIC PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrophobic plastics, such as PS and PVC, are less degradable and can cause dehydration and growth inhibition in plastic-degrading larvae when used as an exclusive food source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROLYSIS REACTIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrolysis reactions are chemical processes that break down plastic polymers into smaller molecules, aided by enzymes in the gut of plastic-degrading larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLORINATED ORGANICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chlorinated organics are byproducts of incomplete PVC biodegradation in plastic-degrading larvae, contributing to their malnutrition and reduced nutrient content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient content refers to the levels of essential nutrients, such as total sugar, fat, and protein, in the biomass of plastic-degrading larvae, which are affected by their diet and plastic exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER DEGRADABILITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer degradability refers to the ease with which plastic polymers, such as PLA, PS, and PVC, can be broken down by plastic-degrading larvae, influencing their growth and physiological function.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERMEDIATE TOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Intermediate toxicity refers to the harmful effects of certain plastics, such as PVC, on plastic-degrading larvae, leading to incomplete biodegradation and malnutrition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO-DIET BRAN">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Co-diet bran is an exogenous food source that alleviates the negative impacts of microplastics on plastic-degrading larvae, maintaining their nutrient levels and physiological performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-diet bran is used to enhance PP consumption rates in biodegradation studies, indicating its role in improving the efficiency of plastic degradation by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, measured in the study to assess oxidative stress in larvae exposed to different plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, often induced by exposure to microplastics and nanoplastics, leading to oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, which increase in Tenebrio molitor larvae due to microplastic ingestion, leading to oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOFRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biofragmentation is the process by which ingested microplastics are broken down into smaller particles by Tenebrio molitor larvae, though residual particles remain at micrometer size.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofragmentation is the process by which ingested plastics are broken down into smaller particles, such as microplastics or nanoplastics, by organisms like larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofragmentation is the process by which ingested plastics are fragmented into smaller particles, such as microplastics (MPs), during digestion by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEVELOPMENTAL TOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Developmental toxicity is assessed for degradation intermediates/compounds of polyethylene using QSR prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;Developmental toxicity refers to adverse effects on the growth and development of organisms, often induced by exposure to microplastics and nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVC + BRAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A mixture of polyvinyl chloride (PVC) and bran, used in the study to assess its impact on ROS levels in larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS + BRAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A mixture of polystyrene (PS) and bran, used in the study to evaluate its effects on ROS levels in larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLA + BRAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A mixture of polylactic acid (PLA) and bran, used in the study to assess its impact on ROS levels in larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with bran only, serving as a control group in the study to compare ROS levels with larvae fed with plastic mixtures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVC + BRAN-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with a mixture of PVC and bran, used to evaluate the impact of PVC on ROS levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS + BRAN-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with a mixture of PS and bran, used to assess the effects of PS on ROS levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLA + BRAN-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with a mixture of PLA and bran, used to evaluate the impact of PLA on ROS levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLUORESCENCE INTENSITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fluorescence intensity is a measure used in the study to quantify ROS levels in larvae and earthworms exposed to different plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROGEN CHLORIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrogen chloride is a toxic byproduct released during the biodegradation of polyvinyl chloride (PVC), contributing to oxidative stress in organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrogen chloride is a toxic substance released during the depolymerization of PVC by mealworms, which can damage their intestinal epithelium.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative depolymerization is the process by which polymers, such as polystyrene (PS) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC), are broken down through oxidation, releasing reactive oxygen species (ROS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPEROXIDE ANION (O2-)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Superoxide anion (O2-) is a reactive oxygen species (ROS) that is neutralized by superoxide dismutase (SOD) to protect cells from oxidative damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROGEN PEROXIDE (H2O2)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) is a reactive oxygen species (ROS) that is converted into water and oxygen by catalase (CAT) to protect cells from oxidative damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) is used in the digestion process to break down organic matter in mealworm intestines and frass samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER BIODEGRADABILITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer biodegradability refers to the ability of microplastics like PVC, PS, and PLA to be broken down by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with PLA being the most biodegradable and PVC the least.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative degradation is a process by which plastics like polyethylene are broken down through oxidative reactions, often facilitated by organisms like superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidative degradation refers to the breakdown of plastics like LDPE under environmental conditions, as studied in long-term soil burial experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DANIO RERIO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Danio rerio, commonly known as zebrafish, are used in toxicological studies to evaluate the combined effects of microplastics and other pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DI(2-ETHYLHEXYL) PHTHALATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate is a chemical pollutant often studied in combination with microplastics for its toxic effects on organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate is a plasticizer that can be efficiently remediated by Achromobacter denitrificans strain SP1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFLAMMATORY RESPONSE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Inflammatory response is an adverse outcome pathway activated by microplastics and nanoplastics, causing harm to organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory responses are biological reactions that can occur in organisms, including Tenebrio larvae, due to exposure to microplastics or imbalances in gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISPOSABLE MEDICAL MASKS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Disposable medical masks are a source of microplastics that can affect terrestrial invertebrates and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EFSA">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) evaluates the safety of novel foods, such as dried yellow mealworm, for human consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL CHARACTERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemical characterization involves analyzing the physical and chemical properties of substances, such as plastics, to understand their biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIFE CYCLE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Life cycle analysis is a research method used to study the ability of polystyrene-fed larvae to complete their life cycle, providing insights into plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACCASE-LIKE MULTICOPPER OXIDASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccase-like multicopper oxidases are enzymes involved in the degradation of both lignin and polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Laccase-like multicopper oxidases are enzymes involved in the degradation of lignin and polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME P450">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytochrome P450 is an enzyme that plays a role in the degradation of aromatic compounds and polystyrene in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPEROXIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Superoxide is a reactive oxygen species involved in the degradation of lignin and polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SERRATIA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Serratia sp. is a gut bacterium found in mealworms that is associated with the degradation of both polystyrene and corn straw.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAC640 GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The lac640 gene is upregulated in mealworms fed both polystyrene and corn straw, and it exhibits degradation ability for both materials when overexpressed in E. coli.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOGICAL TECHNOLOGIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biological technologies are eco-friendly methods developed to manage plastic waste, including the use of mealworms for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOHYDRATE-ACTIVE ENZYMES (CAZYMES)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbohydrate-active enzymes (CAZymes) are produced by gut bacteria in insects like mealworms to degrade lignocellulose and contribute to their growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRAMETES VERSICOLOR">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Trametes versicolor is a fungus capable of degrading lignin through extracellular enzymes like lignin peroxidase and laccase.&lt;SEP&gt;Trametes versicolor is a fungus known for its ability to degrade lignin using extracellular enzymes such as lignin peroxidase, manganese peroxidase, and laccase.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHANEROCHAETE CHRYSOSPORIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Phanerochaete chrysosporium is a fungus capable of degrading lignin through extracellular enzymes like lignin peroxidase and manganese peroxidase.&lt;SEP&gt;Phanerochaete chrysosporium is a lignin-degrading fungus that produces enzymes capable of breaking down lignin and potentially synthetic plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Phanerochaete chrysosporium is a soil fungus known for its ability to degrade polystyrene and other plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNIN PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignin peroxidase is an enzyme produced by fungi like Trametes versicolor and Phanerochaete chrysosporium to degrade lignin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGANESE PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Manganese peroxidase is an enzyme produced by fungi like Phanerochaete chrysosporium to degrade lignin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP. PE3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. PE3 is a bacterium that produces ligninolytic enzymes capable of degrading polyethylene in media containing PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VANCOMYCIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vancomycin is an antibiotic tested for its ability to suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Vancomycin is an antibiotic that inhibits the growth of gut flora in Tenebrio molitor larvae, impacting their biodegradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Vancomycin is an antibiotic used in combination with other antibiotics to eliminate gut microbes in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNIN-DEGRADING ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignin-degrading enzymes, such as lignin peroxidase and laccase, are capable of breaking down synthetic plastics due to their structural similarities with lignin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNIN PEROXIDASE (LIP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignin peroxidase is an enzyme produced by fungi like Trametes versicolor and Phanerochaete chrysosporium, capable of degrading lignin and potentially synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGANESE PEROXIDASE (MN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Manganese peroxidase is an enzyme involved in lignin degradation, produced by fungi and potentially effective in breaking down synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACCASE (LAC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccase is an enzyme produced by fungi and bacteria, involved in lignin degradation and potentially capable of degrading synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAC640">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lac640 is a highly expressed gene in Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with the degradation of both polystyrene and corn stover.&lt;SEP&gt;Lac640 is a laccase enzyme encoded by LMCOs, highly upregulated in mealworms fed with polystyrene and lignocellulose, playing a key role in the degradation of these polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lac640 is an enzyme upregulated in PS-/CS-fed mealworms, involved in the oxidation and degradation of polystyrene and lignin.&lt;SEP&gt;Lac640 is an enzyme with laccase activity that is involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS), as demonstrated by its activity in the presence of Cu²⁺ and ABTS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORN STOVER (CS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Corn stover is a natural lignocellulosic polymer used as a diet for mealworms in studies comparing its degradation to that of synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CABBAGE (CB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cabbage (CB) is used as a control feedstock in studies to compare the consumption and degradation rates of other materials by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Cabbage is used as a control diet in studies involving Tenebrio molitor larvae due to its lower lignocellulose content compared to wheat bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY–MASS SPECTROMETRY (GC–MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC–MS) is a method used to identify biodegradation products of microplastics (MPs) after degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Gas chromatography–mass spectrometry is a technique used to detect and characterize degradation intermediates of polystyrene and corn stover in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METATRANSCRIPTOMIC SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metatranscriptomic sequencing is used to analyze metabolic pathways and functional enzymes associated with the degradation of polystyrene and corn stover in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REAL-TIME POLYMERASE CHAIN REACTION (RT-PCR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RT-PCR is used to verify differentially expressed genes related to the degradation of polystyrene and corn stover in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E. COLI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">E. coli is a bacterial strain used as a negative control in the study, showing less adhesion to polystyrene film.&lt;SEP&gt;E. coli is a bacterium used as a negative control in experiments to assess the degradation of polystyrene by other microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;E. coli is used as a host for overexpressing the lac640 gene to determine its ability to degrade polystyrene and corn stover.&lt;SEP&gt;Escherichia coli (E. coli) is a bacterium used as a negative control in experiments to study biofilm formation and its interaction with nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Escherichia coli (E. coli) is a bacterium used in studies to compare biofilm behavior with other bacteria, such as Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9, in the context of nanoplastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Escherichia coli (E. coli) is a bacterium commonly used in research studies, with a doubling time of 68.3 minutes in LB medium during the exponential phase.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIQUID CHROMATOGRAPHY-TANDEM MASS SPECTROMETRY (LC-MS/MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">LC-MS/MS was used for comparative metabolomic analysis to identify metabolites and metabolic pathways of polystyrene and other feedstock degradation in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRYPTIC SOY AGAR (TSA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tryptic Soy Agar (TSA) is a growth medium used to cultivate and count gut bacteria from larvae during antibiotic suppression tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Tryptic Soy Agar (TSA) is a medium used to culture gut bacteria from mealworms for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REAL-TIME QUANTITATIVE PCR (RT-QPCR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RT-qPCR was used to quantify the genetic material of gut bacteria in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VAN SOEST METHODS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Van Soest methods were used to measure the lignocellulose components of feedstocks and frass from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM-ENERGY DISPERSIVE SPECTROMETRY (SEM-EDS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM-EDS was used to analyze the elemental composition of feedstocks and frass from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG DATABASES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KEGG databases were used to annotate metabolites identified in the metabolomic analysis of frass samples from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMAN METABOLOME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The human metabolome database was used to annotate metabolites identified in the metabolomic analysis of frass samples from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPINEASY DNA KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The SPINeasy DNA Kit was used to extract total DNA from gut samples of mealworms for microbial community analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CS FEEDSTOCK">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CS feedstock refers to a type of feed given to mealworms in the study, alongside polystyrene and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CB FEEDSTOCK">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CB feedstock refers to a type of feed given to mealworms in the study, alongside polystyrene and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACCASE (LAC640)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccase (Lac640) is an enzyme identified as a potential PS-degrading enzyme, upregulated in PS-fed mealworms and involved in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E. COLI ROSETTA (DE3)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">E. coli Rosetta (DE3) is a strain of bacteria used for the heterologous expression of the Lac640 enzyme for functional assays.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METATRANSCRIPTOME ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metatranscriptome analysis is a research method used to study gene expression in microbial communities, particularly in the context of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RT-PCR VALIDATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RT-PCR validation is a technique used to confirm the expression of differentially expressed genes (DEGs) identified in metatranscriptomic studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILLUMINA HISEQ 6000">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Illumina HiSeq 6000 is a sequencing platform used for high-throughput DNA sequencing, including metatranscriptome and 16S rRNA gene analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAJORBIO CLOUD PLATFORM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Majorbio Cloud Platform is a bioinformatics tool used for analyzing microbial community structure, alpha diversity, and differential species abundance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDGER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">edgeR is a Bioconductor package used for differential expression analysis of digital gene expression data in genomics research.&lt;SEP&gt;edgeR is an R package used for statistical analysis of differentially expressed genes (DEGs) in RNA-seq data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENJAMINI-HOCHBERG METHOD">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Benjamini-Hochberg method is a statistical approach used to control the false discovery rate (FDR) in multiple hypothesis testing, such as DEG analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCBI NON-REDUNDANT PROTEIN DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The NCBI Non-redundant Protein (Nr) database is a comprehensive resource for protein sequence data, used for annotating and comparing DEGs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RT-PCR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RT-PCR (Reverse Transcription Polymerase Chain Reaction) is a molecular biology technique used to detect and quantify mRNA, in this case, to study the expression of nifH genes in mealworm gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;RT-PCR (Reverse Transcription Polymerase Chain Reaction) is a technique used to validate the expression of differentially expressed genes (DEGs) identified in metatranscriptomic studies.&lt;SEP&gt;RT-PCR is a technique used to validate the relative expression levels of PS- and CS-degrading enzymes in mealworms, with 60S ribosomal protein 27a (Tml27a) as a reference gene.&lt;SEP&gt;RT-PCR is used to validate the expression levels of plastic- and lignocellulose-degrading enzymes in PS-/CS-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) is a technique used to detect mRNA and confirm the expression of nifH genes in nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAC640 (LACCASE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lac640 is a laccase enzyme identified as a potential PS-degrading enzyme, upregulated in PS-fed mealworms and involved in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABTS (2,2'-AZINO-BIS (3-ETHYLBENZOTHIAZOLINE-6-SULFONIC ACID))">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ABTS is a substrate used to assay laccase activity by measuring its oxidation at 420 nm.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CABBAGE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cabbage (CB) is used as a control feedstock in the study to compare the effects of different diets on Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC TREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic treatment is used to suppress gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae to study their role in PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic treatment is used to suppress gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae to study their role in PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic treatment is used to suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade materials like polystyrene and corn straw.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic treatment is used to suppress the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, affecting their ability to degrade polystyrene and corn straw.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic treatment refers to the use of antibiotics to suppress or eliminate gut microbiota, which can impact the nitrogen-fixing capabilities and overall fitness of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic treatment, such as gentamicin, is used to suppress gut bacteria in larvae, affecting their ability to depolymerize and biodegrade polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM (PS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam (PS) is a type of expanded polystyrene foam, commonly used in packaging and insulation, and is a significant environmental pollutant due to its persistence in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORN STRAW (CS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Corn straw (CS) is an agricultural byproduct used as a feedstock in studies to evaluate its consumption and degradation by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL COLONIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbial colonies are the communities of microorganisms residing in the digestive tracts of larvae, essential for the degradation of materials like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSA MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">TSA medium is a growth medium used to culture and observe gut microbial colonies in laboratory studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CFUS (COLONY-FORMING UNITS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">CFUs are a measure of viable bacterial or fungal cells in a sample, used to quantify gut microbial activity in larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RT-QPCR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RT-qPCR is a molecular biology technique used to quantify the copy number of specific DNA sequences, such as gut microbes in larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SRS (SURVIVAL RATES)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival rates (SRs) are used to evaluate the health and viability of larvae under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRS (CONSUMPTION RATES)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Consumption rates (CRs) measure the amount of material (e.g., polystyrene, corn straw) consumed by larvae over a specific period.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MW (MOLECULAR WEIGHT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (MW) is a key property of polystyrene microplastics that influences their biodegradation rate, with lower MW PS MPs being more easily digested by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mw refers to the average molecular weight of polymers, such as polystyrene, and is used to assess degradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MN (NUMBER-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mn is a measure of the average molecular weight of polymers, used to evaluate the extent of depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MZ (Z-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mz is a measure of the molecular weight distribution of polymers, used to assess the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE (LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE is a type of plastic that is less susceptible to degradation by mealworms compared to polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVC FOAM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PVC foam is a type of plastic material used in studies to compare its degradation by mealworms with other materials like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS BALANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass balance is a method used to measure the weight of PE and WC added, the weight of frass, and the extractable fraction to assess the extent of PE degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass balance is a method used to track the distribution of consumed materials (e.g., polystyrene, corn straw) in larvae and their frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass balance is a method used to track the inputs, outputs, and changes in biomass during plastic degradation experiments with mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass balance is a method used to track the distribution of polyethylene during biodegradation experiments with lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOST ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Host enzymes are produced by mealworms and contribute to the depolymerization of materials like polystyrene, alongside gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNERGETIC ROLE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The synergetic role refers to the combined action of mealworms' host enzymes and gut microbiota in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULOSE (CS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulose (CS) is a natural polymer that is also degraded by mealworms, though its degradation is less dependent on gut microbiota compared to polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer used as a comparative diet in this study to assess its impact on the gut microbiome of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLOMIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolomic analysis is a research method used to study the metabolic changes in mealworms when exposed to different plastic substrates, revealing upregulated pathways and cofactor accumulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolomic analysis is used to identify and compare the metabolites produced during the degradation of polystyrene and cellulose by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolomic analysis is performed to study the metabolic changes in mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolomic analysis is used to study the metabolic changes in mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene, revealing reprogramming of metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC-TREATED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic-treated larvae are mealworms whose gut microbiota has been suppressed, affecting their ability to degrade polystyrene and cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAW PS FOAM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Raw PS foam is the initial form of polystyrene used in experiments, which is depolymerized by mealworms into smaller molecules.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM IMAGES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM images are used to observe the surface changes in polystyrene and cellulose after being digested by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LC-MS/MS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">LC-MS/MS is used for comparative metabolomic analysis to identify differential metabolites in mealworms fed with different materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG-CHAIN FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Long-chain fatty acids are intermediates produced during the degradation of polystyrene and lignin, which are further metabolized through β-oxidation for energy production.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-chain fatty acids are metabolites found in the frass and gut samples of mealworms fed with polystyrene, indicating the breakdown of the polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZENE DERIVATIVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzene derivatives are compounds whose relative abundances decrease during the degradation of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHTHALIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phthalic acid is a compound whose relative abundance decreases during the degradation of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Phthalic acid is a metabolic intermediate detected during the biodegradation of polystyrene in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EICOSANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Eicosane is a long-chain hydrocarbon whose relative abundance decreases during the degradation of cellulose by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Eicosane, also known as Icosane, is a chemical compound with the formula C20H42.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYCLOHEXANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cyclohexane is a compound whose relative abundance decreases during the degradation of cellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENOL DERIVATIVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenol derivatives are compounds whose relative abundances decrease during the degradation of cellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANIC ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Organic acids are metabolites identified in the gut of mealworms, indicating the breakdown of ingested materials like polystyrene and cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANOHETEROCYCLIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Organoheterocyclic compounds are a class of metabolites identified in the gut of larvae, making up 13.218% of the total metabolites.&lt;SEP&gt;Organoheterocyclic compounds are metabolites identified in the gut of mealworms, indicating the breakdown of ingested materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZENOIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzenoids are a class of metabolites identified in the gut of larvae, making up 11.089% of the total metabolites.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzenoids are metabolites identified in the gut of mealworms, showing the metabolic changes during the degradation of polystyrene and cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON BLACK (CB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon Black (CB) is a form of paracrystalline carbon used as a control feedstock in this study to compare its effects on larvae gut metabolites with other feedstocks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORN STARCH (CS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Corn Starch (CS) is a carbohydrate derived from corn, used as a feedstock in this study to analyze its impact on larvae gut metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLITES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolites are small molecules involved in metabolism, identified in the gut of larvae fed with different feedstocks, including PS, CB, and CS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="9,12-OCTADECADIYNOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">9,12-octadecadiynoic acid is a metabolite found in higher abundance in PS-fed larvae compared to other groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-METHYLBENZOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-methylbenzoic acid is a metabolite found in higher abundance in PS-fed larvae compared to other groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2,4-DICHLOROBENZOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2,4-dichlorobenzoic acid is a metabolite found in higher abundance in PS-fed larvae compared to other groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-HYDROXYBENZALDEHYDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">4-hydroxybenzaldehyde is a metabolite found in higher abundance in PS-fed larvae compared to other groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOMOCITRATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Homocitrate is a metabolite found in higher abundance in PS-fed larvae compared to other groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRADECANEDIOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tetradecanedioic acid is a metabolite enriched in the CS-fed group of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3-PHENOXYMETHYL ALCOHOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">3-phenoxymethyl alcohol is a metabolite enriched in the CS-fed group of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D-ARABINOSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">D-arabinose is a metabolite enriched in the CS-fed group of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYRUVATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyruvate is a metabolite found in higher abundance in the CB or unfed group of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-ACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-acetic acid is a metabolite found in higher abundance in the CB or unfed group of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANIC ACIDS AND DERIVATIVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Organic acids and derivatives are a class of metabolites identified in the gut of larvae, making up 25.39% of the total metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPIDS AND LIPID-LIKE MOLECULES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipids and lipid-like molecules are a class of metabolites identified in the gut of larvae, making up 21.54% of the total metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIERARCHICAL CLUSTERED HEAT MAPS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Hierarchical clustered heat maps are used in this study to visualize the abundance and differential expression of metabolites in larvae fed with different feedstocks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VOLCANO MAP ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Volcano map analysis is used in this study to identify differentially expressed metabolites in larvae fed with PS and CS compared to CB-fed and unfed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIFFERENTIAL METABOLITES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Differential metabolites are compounds that show significant changes in abundance between different groups of larvae fed with PS, CS, CB, or unfed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C=O STRETCHING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">C=O stretching is a chemical bond vibration detected in FTIR analysis, indicating the presence of carbonyl groups in the gut of larvae fed with different feedstocks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-O STRETCHING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">C-O stretching is a chemical bond vibration detected in FTIR analysis, indicating the presence of ether or alcohol groups in the gut of larvae fed with different feedstocks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="R-OH STRETCHING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">R-OH stretching is a chemical bond vibration detected in FTIR analysis, indicating the presence of hydroxyl groups in the gut of larvae fed with different feedstocks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with polystyrene (PS) exhibit changes in gut microbial composition, with increased abundances of Firmicutes and specific genera like Spiroplasma and Escherichia–Shigella.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CS-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with cellulose (CS) show similar gut microbial changes to PS-fed larvae, with increased abundances of Firmicutes and genera like Spiroplasma and Escherichia–Shigella.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CB-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with a control diet (CB) serve as a baseline for gut microbial composition, showing higher abundances of Proteobacteria compared to PS- and CS-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESCHERICHIA–SHIGELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Escherichia–Shigella is a genus of bacteria that exhibits higher abundance in the gut microbiome of PS- and CS-fed larvae compared to CB-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METATRANSCRIPTOMIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metatranscriptomic analysis is used to study the gene expression and metabolic pathways involved in the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Metatranscriptomic analysis is used to study the metabolically active bacteria and mechanisms involved in the degradation of plastics and lignocellulose by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Metatranscriptomic analysis was used to determine the metabolically active bacteria and the mechanisms of plastic and lignocellulose degradation in mealworms fed with PS and CS diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RUBELLIMICROBIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rubellimicrobium is a genus of bacteria that shows significant differences in the gut microbiome of PS-fed larvae, indicating its potential role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLYUVERA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kluyvera is a genus of bacteria that maintains high abundance in the gut microbiome of CS-fed larvae, indicating its potential role in cellulose degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klyuvera is a genus of bacteria that could be associated with the biodegradation of synthetic materials in certain environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEISSELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Weissella is a genus of bacteria enriched in the gut microbiome of CS-fed larvae, suggesting its involvement in the degradation of cellulose.&lt;SEP&gt;Weissella is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Weissella is a genus of bacteria identified in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PREVOTELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Prevotella is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Prevotella is a genus of bacteria that exhibits higher abundance in the gut microbiome of PS- and CS-fed larvae compared to CB-fed larvae, suggesting its involvement in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XENOBIOTIC METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Xenobiotic metabolism involves the degradation of foreign substances, such as plastics, by enzymes like cytochrome P450 and alcohol dehydrogenase in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HALOBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Halobacterium is a genus of bacteria that exhibits high abundance in the gut of mealworms fed with polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DESULFUROBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Desulfurobacterium is a genus of bacteria found in the gut of mealworms, potentially involved in plastic and lignocellulose degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALCOHOL DEHYDROGENASE (ADH)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alcohol dehydrogenase is an enzyme involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics, including the degradation of plastics and lignocellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME P450 (CYP6)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytochrome P450 is an enzyme family involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics, including the degradation of plastics and lignocellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKYLGLYCEROL MONOOXYGENASE (AGMO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkylglycerol monooxygenase is an enzyme involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics, including the degradation of plastics and lignocellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALDEHYDE DEHYDROGENASE (ALDH)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aldehyde dehydrogenase is an enzyme involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics, including the degradation of plastics and lignocellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEROXIDASE (PO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peroxidase is an enzyme involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics, including the degradation of plastics and lignocellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG-CHAIN-FATTY-ACID-COA LIGASE (ACSL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Long-chain-fatty-acid-CoA ligase is an enzyme involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics, including the degradation of plastics and lignocellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACCASE-LIKE MULTICOPPER OXIDASE (AA1)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccase-like multicopper oxidase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYL ESTERASE (CE10)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxyl esterase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPASE (CE1)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDO-BETA-1,3(4)-GLUCANASE (GH12)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Endo-beta-1,3(4)-glucanase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETYL XYLAN ESTERASE (CE1)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetyl xylan esterase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FERULOYL ESTERASE (CE1)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Feruloyl esterase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BETA-1,3(4)-GLUCANASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Beta-1,3(4)-glucanase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of polysaccharides, found in PS-/CS-fed larval groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETYL XYLAN ESTERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetyl xylan esterase is an enzyme that hydrolyzes acetyl groups from xylan, found in PS-/CS-fed larval groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FERULOYL ESTERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Feruloyl esterase is an enzyme that hydrolyzes ferulic acid esters, found in PS-/CS-fed larval groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOCELLULOSE (CS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignocellulose (CS) is a complex organic compound found in plant cell walls that can be degraded by mealworms through specific metabolic pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Lignocellulose (CS) is a natural polymer found in plant biomass, metabolized by Tenebrio molitor larvae alongside polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GH12">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">GH12 is a glycoside hydrolase family enzyme involved in the degradation of polysaccharides, found in PS-/CS-fed larval groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CE1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CE1 is a carbohydrate esterase family enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of acetyl groups from xylan and ferulic acid esters, found in PS-/CS-fed larval groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYP6/4">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CYP6/4 is a cytochrome P450 enzyme upregulated in PS-/CS-fed mealworms, involved in the oxidation and degradation of polystyrene and lignin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LMCOS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LMCOs (laccase-like multicopper oxidases) are enzymes involved in the oxidative degradation of lignin and polystyrene, found in PS-/CS-fed mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AXE1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">AXE1 (acetyl xylan esterase 1) is an enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of acetyl groups from xylan, found in PS-/CS-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;AXE1 is an enzyme involved in the degradation of cellulose and hemicellulose, converting them into glucose or xylose during lignocellulose degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XENOBIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Xenobiodegradation refers to the degradation of foreign substances, such as polystyrene, by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZOATE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzoate degradation pathways are enriched in PS-/CS-fed mealworms, indicating their role in the degradation of aromatic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROALKANE AND CHLOROALKENE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloroalkane and chloroalkene degradation pathways are enriched in PS-/CS-fed mealworms, indicating their role in the degradation of synthetic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYP4/6">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CYP4/6 is a cytochrome P450 enzyme involved in the main-chain cleavage of polystyrene during its degradation by G. mellonella larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALDEHYDE DEHYDROGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aldehyde dehydrogenase is an enzyme that plays a role in the degradation of aromatic compounds and the formation of long-chain fatty acids during polystyrene and lignin degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALCOHOL DEHYDROGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alcohol dehydrogenase is an enzyme involved in the oxidation of alcohols during the degradation of polystyrene and lignin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLESTERASE (CES1)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylesterase (CES1) is an enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of long-chain fatty acids during the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG-CHAIN FATTY ACID-COA LIGASE (ACSL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Long-chain fatty acid-CoA ligase (ACSL) is an enzyme that activates long-chain fatty acids for β-oxidation during the degradation of polystyrene and lignin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAEB">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FAEB is an enzyme involved in the degradation of cellulose and hemicellulose, converting them into glucose or xylose during lignocellulose degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EB-G">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Eb-G is an enzyme involved in the degradation of cellulose and hemicellulose, converting them into glucose or xylose during lignocellulose degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OCTADECENOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Octadecenoic acid is a long-chain fatty acid detected as an intermediate during the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXADECENOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexadecenoic acid is a long-chain fatty acid detected as an intermediate during the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACCASE (LMCO)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccase (LMCO) is an enzyme belonging to the AA1 family, involved in the cleavage of carbon-carbon bonds during the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAC-PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lac-peroxidase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and polystyrene by oxidizing aromatic rings and cleaving carbon-carbon bonds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MN-PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mn-peroxidase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and polystyrene by oxidizing aromatic rings and cleaving carbon-carbon bonds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIP-PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lip-peroxidase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and polystyrene by oxidizing aromatic rings and cleaving carbon-carbon bonds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Β-OXIDATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">β-Oxidation is a metabolic process that breaks down long-chain fatty acids into acetyl-CoA, which enters the TCA cycle for energy production during the degradation of polystyrene and lignin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="60S RIBOSOMAL PROTEIN 27A (TML27A)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">60S ribosomal protein 27a (Tml27a) of T. molitor is used as a reference gene in RT-PCR to validate the expression levels of PS- and CS-degrading enzymes in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CU²⁺">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cu²⁺ is a metal ion that influences the laccase activity of Lac640, an enzyme involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SDS-PAGE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SDS-PAGE analysis is used to determine the molecular size and purity of the Lac640 enzyme, which is involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEAN ± STANDARD DEVIATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mean ± standard deviation represents the statistical analysis of data points, such as enzyme activity or degradation rates, in the study of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS) degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELECTRON MICROSCOPY CENTER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Electron Microscopy Center of Lanzhou University provided microscopy and microscopic analysis for the study of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS) degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATIONAL NATURAL SCIENCE FOUNDATION OF CHINA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The National Natural Science Foundation of China funded the research on Mixta tenebrionis and its role in biodegradation, supporting studies on plastic-eating mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Natural Science Foundation of China provided funding for the research on the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS) by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Natural Science Foundation of China supports research on plastic degradation and environmental management, contributing to advancements in understanding microplastic toxicity and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Natural Science Foundation of China provided financial support for the research study on polystyrene (PS) degradation by bacterial strains isolated from Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERNATIONAL SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY COOPERATION PROJECT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The International Science and Technology Cooperation Project of Gansu Province supported the research on the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) and lignocellulose (CS) by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLOBAL ENVIRONMENTAL CONCERN">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Global environmental concern refers to the issues arising from the accumulation of plastic waste, including polystyrene (PS), which is addressed through biodegradation studies involving mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene (PS) and are more susceptible to biodegradation than the polymerized form of PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene (PS) by insects, indicating partial breakdown of the polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIDGUT LUMEN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The midgut lumen of mealworms harbors microorganisms that play a crucial role in the digestion and biodegradation of refractory materials like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The midgut lumen of mealworms harbors microorganisms that play a crucial role in the digestion and biodegradation of refractory materials, such as polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Chemical Engineering Journal is a publication that discusses research on environmental issues, including microplastics and their eradication.&lt;SEP&gt;The Chemical Engineering Journal publishes research on strategies for microplastic conversion and removal in wastewater treatment plants and landfills.&lt;SEP&gt;The Chemical Engineering Journal is a publication where research on enhancing bioenergy production and carbon capture using microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots was documented.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MATERIALS TODAY: PROCEEDINGS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Materials Today: Proceedings is a journal that publishes research on materials science, including studies on the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STATISTA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Statista is a platform that provides statistical data, including global production volumes of thermoplastics and other materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
