      <data key="d2">chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S. BAHL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">S. Bahl is a researcher who has contributed to studies on the biodegradation of plastics, as published in Materials Today: Proceedings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="J. DOLMA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">J. Dolma is a researcher involved in studies on the biodegradation of plastics, as published in Materials Today: Proceedings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="J.J. SINGH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">J.J. Singh is a researcher who has contributed to studies on the biodegradation of plastics, as published in Materials Today: Proceedings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S. SEHGAL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">S. Sehgal is a researcher involved in studies on the biodegradation of plastics, as published in Materials Today: Proceedings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZALERION MARITIMUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zalerion maritimum is a marine fungus capable of biodegrading polyethylene microplastics, contributing to environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Zalerion maritimum is a marine fungus that can biodegrade polyethylene microplastics, contributing to environmental remediation efforts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEPIDIOTA MANSUETA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lepidiota mansueta, commonly known as the white grub beetle, is a species whose larval gut contains cellulose-degrading bacteria, contributing to lignocellulose decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Lepidiota mansueta, or white grub beetle larvae, have gut bacteria capable of degrading cellulose, offering potential for biotechnological applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER-SOLUBLE DAUGHTER PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Water-soluble daughter products are compounds released during the biodegradation of polystyrene by bacterial strains like Exiguobacterium sp. YT2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="14C-LABELED PS TRACER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">14C-labeled PS tracer is used in research studies to track the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by microorganisms and insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PE-degrading bacteria are microorganisms found in the digestive tract of lesser waxworms that may contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-degrading bacteria are microorganisms found in the digestive tracts of insects like lesser waxworms and Indian meal moths, which aid in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-degrading bacteria, such as Bacillus sp. YP1 and Enterobacter asburiae VT1, are capable of breaking down polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste reduction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULOSE DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulose degrading bacteria, such as those found in the gut of Lepidiota mansueta, can break down cellulose, offering potential for biotechnological applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT MICROBIAL SYMBIONTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect microbial symbionts are microorganisms associated with insects that can degrade plastics and other refractory materials, offering potential for biotechnology.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHITE GRUB BEETLE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The white grub beetle (Lepidiota mansueta) is a species whose larval gut contains cellulose-degrading bacteria, contributing to lignocellulose decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;The white grub beetle (Lepidiota mansueta) is an insect whose larvae contain cellulose-degrading bacteria, offering potential for biotechnological applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULOSE-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulose-degrading bacteria are microorganisms found in the larval gut of Lepidiota mansueta, capable of breaking down cellulose, a major component of plant cell walls.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYPTODERMES BREVIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cryptodermes brevis is a wood-feeding termite species from which novel lignolytic, cellulolytic, and hemicellulolytic bacteria have been isolated, contributing to wood degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WOOD-FEEDING TERMITE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cryptodermes brevis is a wood-feeding termite species that harbors lignolytic, cellulolytic, and hemicellulolytic bacteria, aiding in wood degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOLYTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignolytic bacteria are microorganisms capable of breaking down lignin, a component of plant cell walls, found in the gut of wood-feeding termites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULOLYTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulolytic bacteria are microorganisms that degrade cellulose, a major component of plant cell walls, found in the gut of wood-feeding termites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMICELLULOLYTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hemicellulolytic bacteria are microorganisms that break down hemicellulose, a component of plant cell walls, found in the gut of wood-feeding termites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-density polyethylene foam is a plastic material that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene foam is a type of plastic material used in commercial products and is a target for biodegradation studies by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHEAT STRAW">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Wheat straw is a lignocellulosic material used as feed for yellow mealworms, contributing to insect biomass production and biochar generation.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat straw is used as a feed for yellow mealworms, contributing to the production of insect biomass and biochar for dye adsorption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochar is a byproduct of mealworm biomass production, used for heavy metal removal and environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochar is a carbon-rich material produced from the frass of yellow mealworms fed with wheat straw, used for dye adsorption and environmental applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochar is a carbon-rich solid produced during the pyrolysis of organic materials, such as mealworm fecula.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochar is a carbon-rich solid produced during the pyrolysis of organic materials, such as mealworm fecula.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochar is a high-efficiency material generated from the frass of yellow mealworms fed with wheat straw, used for dye adsorption and environmental applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHITE ROT FUNGI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">White rot fungi are a group of fungi capable of degrading lignin, often used in microbial consortia for enhanced lignin biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNIN BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignin biodegradation refers to the breakdown of lignin, a complex organic polymer, by microorganisms such as white rot fungi.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CR(VI) REMEDIATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cr(VI) remediation refers to the process of removing or reducing hexavalent chromium contamination from industrial wastewater using microbial genes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FALSE DISCOVERY RATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">False Discovery Rate (FDR) is a statistical approach for controlling the rate of false positives in multiple hypothesis testing.&lt;SEP&gt;The false discovery rate is a statistical approach used to control the rate of false positives in multiple hypothesis testing, as described by Benjamin and Hochberg.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLAST2GO">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Blast2GO is a universal tool for annotation, visualization, and analysis in functional genomics research, widely used in bioinformatics studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Blast2GO is a universal tool for functional annotation, visualization, and analysis in functional genomics research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CROP WASTE RESIDUES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Crop waste residues are agricultural byproducts that can be utilized as co-diet for mealworm biomass production, reducing environmental waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLEOPTERINC GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Coleopterinc genes are transcriptionally up-regulated in Tenebrio molitor in response to bacterial challenges, indicating their role in immune response.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACZ1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LacZ1 is a novel laccase enzyme isolated for lignin degradation, demonstrating potential in breaking down complex organic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil bacteria are microorganisms capable of degrading polystyrene, contributing to environmental remediation efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil bacteria from Sarimukti Final Landfill Cipatat Bandung have been studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGANESE SUPEROXIDE DISMUTASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Manganese superoxide dismutase is an enzyme identified for lignin oxidation, playing a role in breaking down complex organic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RETICULITERMES FLAVIPES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Reticulitermes flavipes, a species of termite, has a gut microbiome that aids in the digestion of wood and paper, contributing to biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL Β-1,4-XYLANASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial β-1,4-xylanase is an enzyme involved in the breakdown of xylan, a component of plant cell walls, and is significant in lignocellulosic biomass degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial β-1,4-xylanase is an enzyme involved in the breakdown of xylan, a component of plant cell walls, for biofuel production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL Β-1,4-ENDOGLUCANASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial β-1,4-endoglucanase is an enzyme that breaks down cellulose, playing a key role in the degradation of lignocellulosic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial β-1,4-endoglucanase is an enzyme that degrades cellulose, contributing to the breakdown of plant biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHINOTERMITIDAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhinotermitidae is a family of termites known for their role in lignocellulose degradation and potential applications in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS SYDOWII MS-19">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus sydowii MS-19 is an Antarctic fungal strain with potential as a source of lignocellulosic enzymes for biodegradation applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MULTICOPPER OXIDASE COPA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Multicopper oxidase CopA is an enzyme involved in lignin oxidation, contributing to the biodegradation of lignocellulosic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CUTINASE HOMOLOG">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A cutinase homolog is an enzyme with polyethylene terephthalate (PET)-degrading activity, isolated from compost using metagenomic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PETases are enzymes that degrade polyethylene terephthalate (PET), with implications for plastic waste management and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOCELLULOSIC ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignocellulosic enzymes are a group of enzymes that degrade lignocellulosic biomass, including cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAVIN-DEPENDENT MONOOXYGENASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flavin-dependent monooxygenases are enzymes that catalyze the oxygenation of aromatic compounds, playing a role in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNIN DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignin degradation is the process by which lignin, a complex polymer in plant cell walls, is broken down by microorganisms and enzymes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHANOL PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethanol production from lignocellulosic feedstocks involves the degradation of plant biomass into fermentable sugars, which are then converted into ethanol.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS PUTIDA KT2440">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas putida KT2440 is a bacterium known for its ability to oxidize lignin and other complex polymers, contributing to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS FLUORESCENS PF-S">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas fluorescens PF-S is a bacterial strain involved in lignin oxidation and biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEAF-BRANCH COMPOST">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Leaf-branch compost is a microbial-rich environment from which novel enzymes, such as cutinase homologs, can be isolated for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHIZOBACTER GUMMIIPHILUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhizobacter gummiiphilus is a bacterium that produces PETases, enzymes capable of degrading polyethylene terephthalate (PET).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOCELLULOSE DECONSTRUCTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut of the wood-feeding beetle promotes lignocellulose deconstruction, aiding in the breakdown of plant materials.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL GENES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial genes are explored for Cr(VI) remediation in industrial wastewater treatment.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIFFERENTIAL EXPRESSION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
      <data key="d1">edgeR is used for differential expression analysis in genomics research.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MULTIPLE HYPOTHESIS TESTING">
      <data key="d2">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
      <data key="d1">Multiple hypothesis testing is a statistical approach used to control the rate of false positives when conducting multiple statistical tests simultaneously.&lt;SEP&gt;The false discovery rate approach controls false positives in multiple hypothesis testing.</data>
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONAL GENOMICS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
      <data key="d1">Blast2GO is a tool for functional annotation and analysis in functional genomics research.&lt;SEP&gt;Blast2GO is a tool used for functional genomics research, enabling annotation and analysis of genomic data.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-VALUE CHEMICALS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignocellulose is pretreated for the production of high-value chemicals, demonstrating its potential in biorefining.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WOOD DIGESTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
      <data key="d1">Reticulitermes flavipes has a gut microbiome that aids in the digestion of wood, contributing to biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XYLAN">
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial β-1,4-xylanase degrades xylan, a component of plant cell walls, for biofuel production.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNIN OXIDATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
      <data key="d1">Multicopper oxidase CopA is involved in lignin oxidation, contributing to the biodegradation of lignocellulosic materials.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD PACKAGING POLYSTYRENE (PSP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Food packaging polystyrene (PSp) is a form of polystyrene used in food packaging and is tested for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DENATURING GRADIENT GEL ELECTROPHORESIS (DGGE)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Denaturing gradient gel electrophoresis (DGGE) is a molecular technique used to analyze the variability of bacterial communities in the guts of mealworms fed with polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MELT FLOW RATIO (MFR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Melt flow ratio (MFR) is a technique used to characterize the properties of polystyrene variants tested in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLOSS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gloss is a measure of the surface reflectivity of materials, used to assess the physical properties of polystyrene variants in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Gloss is a property measured to characterize the surface quality of polystyrene variants tested in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MACROMOLECULES (PROTEINS, LIPIDS, SUGARS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Macromolecules such as proteins, lipids, and sugars in mealworms were assessed to evaluate the efficiency of polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOTAL SUGAR CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Total sugar content in mealworms was measured to assess the metabolic impact of polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KRAKCHEMIA S.A.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Krakchemia S.A. is the manufacturer of the raw polystyrene (PS) used in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNTHOS PS GP 137">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Synthos PS GP 137 is the brand name of the raw polystyrene (PS) used in the study, provided by Krakchemia S.A.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INBREEDING FARM IN LUBLIN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The inbreeding farm in Lublin, Poland, is the source of the Tenebrio molitor larvae used in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PACKAGING MATERIAL (PSP)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Packaging material (PSP) is a type of polystyrene used for food packaging and tested for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSICO-MECHANICAL CHARACTERISATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Physico-mechanical characterisation involves testing the physical and mechanical properties of materials, such as polystyrene, before biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MELT FLOW RATIO">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Melt flow ratio is a measure of the flow properties of thermoplastic materials like polystyrene, tested under specific conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENOMIC DNA EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Genomic DNA extraction was performed on mealworm guts to study the microbial communities involved in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DGGE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DGGE (Denaturing Gradient Gel Electrophoresis) analysis was used to study the diversity of bacterial communities in mealworm guts during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOTAL SUGARS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total sugars refer to the concentration of sugars in mealworms, which decreased significantly when fed polystyrene materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOTAL LIPIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total lipids refer to the concentration of fats in mealworms, which decreased significantly when fed polystyrene materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUGARS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sugars are essential for sustaining organ function in mealworms, and their levels decrease when mealworms are fed less accessible forms of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSR (RECYCLED POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Recycled polystyrene (PSr) is a processed form of polystyrene that is less accessible for mealworms to consume and biodegrade compared to other forms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSP (PACKAGING POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Packaging polystyrene (PSp) is a form of polystyrene used in food packaging, which is moderately accessible for mealworms to consume and biodegrade.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DGGE (DENATURING GRADIENT GEL ELECTROPHORESIS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DGGE is a technique used to analyze the structure of the gut microbiome in mealworms, revealing differences based on the type of polystyrene consumed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The starvation process in mealworms is indicated by significant decreases in lipid and sugar levels when fed less accessible forms of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HARDNESS MEASUREMENTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Hardness measurements were used to assess the accessibility of different forms of polystyrene for mealworms, with EPS being the most accessible.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLOSS MEASUREMENTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gloss measurements were used to evaluate the physical condition of polystyrene surfaces, with less glossy surfaces being more accessible for mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOUR BACTERIAL SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Four unidentified bacterial species were detected in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed polystyrene, potentially involved in the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FACULTY OF ENVIRONMENTAL ENGINEERING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Faculty of Environmental Engineering at Wroclaw University of Science and Technology conducted research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by T. molitor, supported by the Ministry of Science and Higher Education.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND HIGHER EDUCATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Ministry of Science and Higher Education provided funding for the research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by T. molitor, conducted by the Faculty of Environmental Engineering.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTROL MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Control mealworms are fed oatmeal and serve as a baseline for comparison in studies assessing the effects of different materials on mealworm health and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Control mealworms are used as a baseline in experiments to compare the effects of polystyrene biodegradation by T. molitor, as they lack the specific gut bacteria responsible for the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVED MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Starved mealworms are used in studies to observe changes in gut biodiversity and their potential impact on the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT BIODIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut biodiversity refers to the variety of microorganisms in the gut of T. molitor, which plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOMES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbiomes in Tenebrio molitor larvae are studied to understand their role in the biodegradation of microplastics (MPs) and their correlation with the aging index of MPs.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiomes refer to the collective communities of bacteria and fungi in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiomes of mealworms play a synergistic role in the biodegradation of PET intermediates and provide nitrogen sources during the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiomes refer to the microbial communities in the digestive tracts of insects like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, which play a role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-CRYSTALLINITY PET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">High-crystallinity PET is a form of polyethylene terephthalate that is more resistant to degradation but can still be biodegraded by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER CHAIN SIZE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer chain size influences the degradation rate of PET by mealworms, with smaller chains being more easily biodegraded.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN SOURCES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen sources are provided by the gut microbiomes of mealworms, aiding in the biodegradation of PET intermediates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UPREGULATION OF HOST GENES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Upregulation of host genes in mealworms is observed during PET depolymerization and metabolism, indicating their critical role in the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE CARBON ISOTOPES (Δ13C)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable carbon isotopes (δ13C) are used to confirm the biodegradation of high-crystallinity PET by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PET-degrading bacteria, including those from the Actinomycetota, Pseudomonadota, and Bacteriodota phyla, have been isolated and studied for their ability to break down PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PETase is an enzyme that plays a key role in the enzymatic degradation of PET, particularly in breaking down low-crystallinity PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYRALID MOTHS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyralid moths are insects that have been found to biodegrade plastics, contributing to research on plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEAF COMPOST-CUTINASE ENZYME (LCC-ICCG)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Leaf compost-cutinase enzyme (LCC-ICCG) is an enzyme used in research to degrade PET, particularly high-crystallinity PET, though its efficiency is limited.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE CARBON ISOTOPE (Δ13C)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable carbon isotope analysis is used to monitor biodegradation by measuring the enrichment of 13C in residual plastics, indicating preferential uptake of 12C by organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC DEPRESSION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic depression refers to the suppression of gut microbiota using antibiotics, which affects the biodegradation of certain plastics like PS and PVC.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMUNITY STRUCTURE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The community structure of gut microbiota is influenced by the physical properties of ingested plastics, such as molecular weight, and plays a role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLIC ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolic activities of gut microbes are crucial for the biodegradation of plastics, as they contribute to the digestion of polymers and the release of organic nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ERYTHROMYCIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Erythromycin is an antibiotic tested for its ability to inhibit gut bacteria in mealworms during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Erythromycin is an antibiotic tested for its ability to suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Erythromycin is an antibiotic used in combination with others to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, impacting PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLORAMPHENICOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloramphenicol is an antibiotic tested for its ability to suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloramphenicol is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, influencing their ability to degrade PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloramphenicol is one of the antibiotics tested for its ability to inhibit gut bacteria in mealworms during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRACYCLINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tetracycline is an antibiotic tested for its ability to inhibit gut bacteria in mealworms during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetracycline is an antibiotic tested for its ability to suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, affecting PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetracycline is an antibiotic that inhibits the growth of gut bacteria in mealworms, though less effectively than gentamicin.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetracycline is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, affecting PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KANAMYCIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Kanamycin is an antibiotic used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, influencing PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET_{100}">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET_{100} is a low molecular weight PET polymer used in biodegradation studies with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET_{2700}">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET_{2700} is a medium molecular weight PET polymer used in biodegradation studies with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET_{63500}">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET_{63500} is a high molecular weight PET polymer used in biodegradation studies with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STAINLESS STEEL HIGH-THROUGHPUT TISSUE GRINDER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A stainless steel high-throughput tissue grinder is used to grind PET samples into smaller particles for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIQUID NITROGEN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Liquid nitrogen is used to freeze PET samples before grinding them into smaller particles for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-AGAR GELS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET-agar gels are high-purity PET materials used in experiments to study the biodegradation of PET by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FT-IR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) is used to analyze the chemical structure of residual PET polymers after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-RAY DIFFRACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">X-ray diffraction analysis is used to measure the crystallinity degree of PET polymers, which influences their biodegradability by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray diffraction analysis is used to measure the crystallinity degree of PET polymers, which influences their biodegradability by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray diffraction is used to analyze the crystallinity of PET polymers after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray diffraction is used to analyze the crystallinity of PET polymers after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray diffraction is a technique used to determine the crystallinity degrees of polyethylene samples during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RIBOSOMAL RNA GENE SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S ribosomal RNA gene sequencing is a high-throughput method used to analyze the diversity of gut microbiome in larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BETA DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Beta diversity evaluates the differences in microbiome complexity between samples, often analyzed using principal coordinate analysis (PCoA).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KRUSKAL-WALLIS TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Kruskal-Wallis test is a statistical method used to examine differences among treatments in microbiome studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E.Z.N.A.® STOOL DNA KIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The E.Z.N.A.® Stool DNA Kit is used for extracting DNA from gut microbiome samples for metagenomic sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRUSEQ NANO DNA LT LIBRARY PREPARATION KIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The TruSeq Nano DNA LT Library Preparation Kit is used for constructing sequencing libraries from fragmented DNA.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NOVASEQ6000 PLATFORM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The NovaSeq6000 platform is a high-throughput sequencing system used for generating metagenomic data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLOMIC PROFILING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolomic profiling is a research method used to analyze metabolites and build pathways, such as those involved in PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAGENOME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metagenome refers to the collection of genomes from a mixed community of microorganisms, constructed from de novo assembly of sequencing data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Coding regions (CDS) are predicted from metagenomic contigs and used for functional and taxonomic annotation in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNIGENES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Unigenes are clusters of coding sequences (CDS) used to estimate gene abundance and perform differential analysis in metagenomic studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) is a database used for functional annotation of genes and pathways in metagenomic studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIZOL REAGENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">TRIzol reagent is used for RNA extraction from bacterial colonies to analyze gene expression related to polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Trizol reagent is used for RNA extraction from larval gut tissue, including bacterial biomass, in molecular biology studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">RNA is extracted from bacterial colonies to study gene expression and identify mechanisms involved in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;RNA is extracted from larval gut tissue and used for transcriptomic analysis to study gene expression and metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY-T OLIGO-ATTACHED MAGNETIC BEADS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly-T oligo-attached magnetic beads are used to isolate poly(A) mRNA from total RNA for transcriptomic studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILLUMINA NOVASEQ™ 6000">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Illumina Novaseq™ 6000 is a sequencing platform used for high-throughput paired-end sequencing of cDNA libraries.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISAT2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">HISAT2 is a software package used for aligning sequencing reads to a reference genome, such as the Tenebrio molitor genome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRINGTIE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">StringTie is a tool used for assembling mapped reads into transcriptomes and estimating gene expression levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GFFCOMPARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gffcompare is software used to merge and compare transcriptomes from multiple samples for comprehensive transcriptomic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FPKM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FPKM (fragments per kilobase of transcript per million mapped reads) is a metric used to estimate gene expression levels in transcriptomic studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VANQUISH FLEX UHPLC SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Vanquish Flex ultrahigh-performance liquid chromatography system is used for untargeted metabolomic profiling to study metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACQUITY UPLC T3 COLUMN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The ACQUITY UPLC T3 column is used for reversed-phase separation in metabolomic profiling studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XCMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">XCMS is a software toolbox used for preprocessing raw metabolomic data in metabolomic profiling studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAMERA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">CAMERA is a software toolbox used for metabolite annotation and analysis in metabolomic studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">MetaX is a toolbox used for statistical analysis and visualization of metabolomic data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET DEGRADATION PATHWAY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The PET degradation pathway is a metabolic pathway studied in organisms like Tenebrio molitor to understand the biodegradation of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET MPS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PET MPs (microplastics) are small particles of polyethylene terephthalate that were fed to Tenebrio molitor larvae to study their biodegradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;PET MPs (microplastics) are small particles of polyethylene terephthalate that were fed to Tenebrio molitor larvae to study their biodegradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;PET MPs (microplastics) are small particles of polyethylene terephthalate used in biodegradation studies with Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET MPs (microplastics) are small particles of polyethylene terephthalate used in biodegradation studies with Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE CARBON ISOTOPE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable carbon isotope analysis is used to confirm the biodegradation of PET by measuring the enrichment of 13C in residual PET polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHANOL-EXTRACTED FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The ethanol-extracted fraction in frass reflects lipophilic substances, providing insights into the biodegradation process of PET.&lt;SEP&gt;The ethanol-extracted fraction represents ethanol-soluble intermediates in frass, which are limited during the biodegradation of PS and PE by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL SOLID CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Residual solid content in frass includes residual enzymes, microorganisms, insoluble metabolized components, and inorganic residues from PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET1100">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PET1100 is a low molecular weight polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymer that is rapidly biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with almost complete degradation observed.&lt;SEP&gt;PET1100 is a low molecular weight polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymer that is rapidly biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with almost complete degradation observed.&lt;SEP&gt;PET1100 is a low-molecular-weight PET microplastic that was fed to Tenebrio molitor larvae to study its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PET1100 is a low-molecular-weight PET microplastic that was fed to Tenebrio molitor larvae to study its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET27100">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PET27100 is a medium molecular weight PET polymer, representing the molecular weight range of most commercial PET products, and is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae with significant mass reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;PET27100 is a medium molecular weight PET polymer, representing the molecular weight range of most commercial PET products, and is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae with significant mass reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;PET27100 is a medium-molecular-weight PET microplastic that represents the average molecular weight range of commercial PET products.&lt;SEP&gt;PET27100 is a medium-molecular-weight PET microplastic that represents the average molecular weight range of commercial PET products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET63500">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET63500 is a high molecular weight PET polymer that is less efficiently biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to lower molecular weight PET polymers, but still shows notable mass reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;PET63500 is a high molecular weight PET polymer that is less efficiently biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to lower molecular weight PET polymers, but still shows notable mass reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;PET63500 is a high molecular weight PET variant that shows less efficient degradation by the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor compared to PET100.&lt;SEP&gt;PET63500 is a high-molecular-weight PET microplastic that contributes only a small portion of commercial PET products.&lt;SEP&gt;PET63500 is a high molecular weight PET variant that shows less efficient degradation by the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor compared to PET100.&lt;SEP&gt;PET63500 is a high-molecular-weight PET microplastic that contributes only a small portion of commercial PET products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYSTALLINITY DEGREE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crystallinity degree is a measure of the structural order in polyethylene microplastics, which affects their biodegradation by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity degree is a property of polyethylene (PE) that influences its biodegradation, as studied in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity degree refers to the structural arrangement of PET polymers, with amorphous regions being more susceptible to microbial degradation than crystalline regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity degree refers to the structural arrangement of polymer chains, affecting the biodegradation rate of polyethylene polymers like LDPE, LLDPE, and HDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1H NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">1H NMR analysis is a technique used to study the chemical composition of frass samples, revealing the breakdown of long-chain polyethylene polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;1H NMR analysis provides evidence of structural and chemical modifications in PET polymers after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;1H NMR analysis is used to detect chemical modifications in polymers, such as the incorporation of oxygen, during degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;1H NMR analysis was employed to study the biodegradation of polyethylene, revealing changes in the molecular structure of the plastic after passing through the gut of lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;1H NMR analysis is performed to identify oxidized functional groups in residual polymers extracted from frass samples, indicating the extent of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BD PATTERN">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">BD (Biodegradation) pattern refers to the degradation of PET polymers characterized by a decrease in all molecular weight parameters and a shift towards lower molecular weight, indicating rapid biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LD PATTERN">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">LD (Limited Degradation) pattern refers to the selective breakdown of low molecular weight PET polymers, with longer chains remaining less degraded, observed in high molecular weight PET polymers like PET63500.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDIZED GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidized groups are chemical functional groups formed during the biodegradation of PET polymers, indicating chemical changes such as increased hydrophilicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHILIC FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrophilic functional groups, such as hydroxyl groups, are formed during the biodegradation of PET polymers, increasing the hydrophilicity of the polymer surface.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTESTINAL MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The intestinal microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of PET polymers, influencing the efficiency and extent of degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The intestinal microbiome of mealworms plays a crucial role in the digestion and degradation of plastics, including polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTERASE/LIPASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Esterase/lipase is an enzyme that modifies the surface of PET, initiating the biodegradation process by attacking the ester bonds in PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE 1-MONOOXYGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane 1-monoxygenase is an enzyme that introduces oxygen into alkane substrates, breaking down hydrocarbons by acting on C-C bonds and transforming them into alcohols.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkane 1-monoxygenase is an enzyme that introduces oxygen into alkane substrates, breaking down hydrocarbons by acting on C-C bonds and transforming them into alcohols.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkane 1-monoxygenase is an enzyme that introduces oxygen to alkane substrates, breaking down hydrocarbons by acting on C-C bonds and transforming them into alcohols.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkane 1-monoxygenase is an enzyme that introduces oxygen to alkane substrates, breaking down hydrocarbons by acting on C-C bonds and transforming them into alcohols.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNCLASSIFIED ENTEROBACTERIACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Unclassified Enterobacteriaceae are gut microbes found in mealworms and superworms, playing a role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Unclassified Enterobacteriaceae is a group of bacteria that was dominant in the control group of Tenebrio molitor larvae, but its role in PET degradation is not well understood.&lt;SEP&gt;Unclassified Enterobacteriaceae is a group of bacteria found in the gut of yellow mealworms, strongly associated with the degradation of plastics like LDPE, PS, and PU.&lt;SEP&gt;Unclassified Enterobacteriaceae is a dominant genus in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, strongly associated with the biodegradation of polyurethane (PU).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG ENRICHMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KEGG enrichment refers to the analysis of metabolic pathways and gene sets involved in PET biodegradation, as identified through metagenomic sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">KEGG genes are involved in metabolic pathways such as carbohydrate, lipid, and amino acid metabolism, which are enriched in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor fed with PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTERASE/LIPASE (K14731)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Esterase/lipase (K14731) is an enzyme involved in the degradation of PET, with higher expression levels observed in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE-1 MONOOXYGENASE (K00496)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane-1 monooxygenase (K00496) is an enzyme that plays a role in hydrocarbon degradation, with higher expression levels in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAZY ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CAZy enzymes are carbohydrate-active enzymes involved in the degradation of PET, with higher expression levels in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET100">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET100 is a low molecular weight PET variant that shows more efficient degradation by the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor compared to higher molecular weight PET variants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET7100">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PET7100 is a medium molecular weight PET variant that shows less efficient degradation by the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor compared to PET100.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-fixing bacteria are microorganisms found in the gut of Styrofoam-eating mealworms that convert atmospheric nitrogen into a usable form for the mealworms' metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria are microorganisms in the gut of mealworms that may facilitate nitrogen fixation, enabling mealworms to adapt to nitrogen-deficient diets like Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria are microorganisms isolated from the guts of mealworms that play a role in nitrogen fixation and potentially in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria are microorganisms present in the guts of mealworms that play a role in nitrogen fixation, which is essential for the degradation of Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria are microorganisms that convert atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia, often found in the guts of termites and other insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae provide a limited source of nitrogen, which is essential for their survival when fed plastic-only diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria, such as Klebsiella spallanzanii BIT-96, are microorganisms that convert atmospheric nitrogen into a form usable by their host organisms, aiding in survival on nitrogen-deficient diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria, such as Klebsiella, are found in the gut of mealworms and play a crucial role in nitrogen fixation, enabling mealworms to survive on nitrogen-deficient plastic diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria, such as those found in the gut of mealworms, play a crucial role in converting atmospheric nitrogen into a usable form for organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSMEMBRANE TRANSPORTER ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Transmembrane transporter activity refers to the movement of molecules across cell membranes, which is relevant in the metabolic processes of Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEPTIDASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peptidase activity involves the breakdown of proteins into smaller peptides or amino acids, which is part of the metabolic processes in Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATION-REDUCTION PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The oxidation-reduction process is a chemical reaction involving the transfer of electrons, which is relevant in the metabolic pathways of Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHITIN METABOLIC PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chitin metabolic process refers to the biochemical pathways involved in the synthesis and breakdown of chitin, a component of the exoskeleton of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID METABOLIC PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid metabolic process involves the synthesis and degradation of lipids, which is part of the metabolic activities in Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLIC ESTER HYDROLASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylic ester hydrolase activity refers to the enzymatic breakdown of ester bonds, which is relevant in the degradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID ELONGATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid elongation is a metabolic process that involves the extension of fatty acid chains, which is part of the lipid metabolism in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABC TRANSPORTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ABC transporters are involved in nutrient acquisition and are upregulated in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation, indicating increased nutrient acquisition capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;ABC transporters are involved in nutrient acquisition and are upregulated in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation, indicating increased nutrient acquisition capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;ABC transporters are proteins involved in the transport of molecules across cell membranes, which is relevant in the metabolic pathways of Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;ABC transporters are proteins involved in the transport of molecules across cell membranes, which is relevant in the metabolic pathways of Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYRUVATE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyruvate metabolism is a biochemical pathway involved in energy production, which is part of the metabolic activities in Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOLYSIS/GLUCONEOGENESIS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycolysis/gluconeogenesis refers to the metabolic pathways involved in the breakdown and synthesis of glucose, which is relevant in the energy metabolism of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYSTEINE AND METHIONINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cysteine and methionine metabolism refers to the biochemical pathways involved in the synthesis and breakdown of these amino acids, which is relevant in the metabolic processes of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROACTIVE LIGAND-RECEPTOR INTERACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Neuroactive ligand-receptor interaction refers to the binding of signaling molecules to receptors, which is part of the cellular communication processes in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROLYSIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrolysis is a key process in PET biodegradation, involving the activation of genes related to ester bond cleavage and surface modification of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLIC ESTER HYDROLASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylic ester hydrolases, including lipase and carboxylesterase, are enzymes that act on ester bonds and are overexpressed in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME P450 (CYP450)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytochrome P450 (CYP450) is a superfamily of mono-oxygenases that catalyze diverse reactions, including C-H hydroxylation and C-C bond cleavage, and are activated in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUTATHIONE S-TRANSFERASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glutathione S-transferases are enzymes that catalyze the conjugation of glutathione to non-polar compounds, making them more water-soluble, and are upregulated in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzoic acid is a key intermediate metabolite produced during the biodegradation of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzoic acid is a key intermediate metabolite produced during the biodegradation of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzoic acid is a typical PET-degrading intermediate found in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating its role in PET biodegradation pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzoic acid is a typical PET-degrading intermediate found in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating its role in PET biodegradation pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATECHOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Catechol is a metabolite produced during the biodegradation of PET, indicating the breakdown of aromatic compounds in the plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIS (2-HYDROXY ETHYL) TEREPHTHALATE (BHET)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">BHET is a hydrolyzed product of PET degradation, formed during the enzymatic breakdown of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut microbes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MONO (2-HYDROXY ETHYL) TEREPHTHALATE (MHET)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MHET is a hydrolyzed product of PET degradation, which is further metabolized into ethylene glycol and terephthalic acid.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROXYLASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroxylases are enzymes produced by Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut microbes that play a key role in depolymerizing PET during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE STRESS RESPONSE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative stress response is triggered in Tenebrio molitor larvae during PET degradation, as indicated by changes in lipid homeostasis and metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1HMR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1HMR) is an analytical technique used to study the biodegradation of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1HMR) is an analytical technique used to study the biodegradation of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1HMR) was used to analyze the structural changes in PET during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1HMR) was used to analyze the structural changes in PET during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYMBIOTIC ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Symbiotic activities between gut microbes and the host (Tenebrio molitor) are essential for the biodegradation of PET.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLESIOTHALMUS DAVIDIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plesiothalmus davidis is a species of darkling beetle that may share similar PET biodegradation mechanisms with Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULOMA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Uloma is a genus of beetles whose larvae have been studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics like HDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Uloma is a genus of darkling beetles that may have similar PET biodegradation mechanisms as Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-CRYSTALLINITY PET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-crystallinity PET materials are widely tested in enzymatic PET degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMORPHOUS PET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Amorphous PET materials are commonly used in studies on enzymatic PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A marine bacterium is a microorganism found in marine environments that has been studied for its potential to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;A marine bacterium is a microorganism found in marine environments that has been studied for its potential to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;A marine bacterium is a microorganism studied for its ability to degrade plastics, particularly polyethylene terephthalate (PET), in marine environments.&lt;SEP&gt;A marine bacterium is a microorganism studied for its ability to degrade plastics, particularly polyethylene terephthalate (PET), in marine environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREEN CONCRETE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Green concrete is an eco-friendly construction material made by recycling waste tire rubber and polyethylene terephthalate (PET), offering resistance against sulfuric acid attack.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE TIRE RUBBER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste tire rubber is a significant environmental pollutant that can be recycled and used in the production of green concrete.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIBLIOMETRIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bibliometric analysis is a research method used to analyze publications on the biodegradation of plastics over 70 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Bibliometric analysis is a research method used to analyze trends and developments in scientific publications, such as those on the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AROMATIC POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aromatic polymers are a class of plastics containing aromatic constituents that are studied for their biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET) DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) degradation refers to the process by which PET plastics are broken down by microorganisms or enzymes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENETICALLY MODIFIED MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Genetically modified microorganisms are engineered to enhance their ability to degrade plastics, such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLESIOPHTHALUS DAVIDSI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plesiophthalmus davidsi larvae are capable of fast and facile biodegradation of polystyrene, facilitated by their gut microflora.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAX WORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The greater wax worm is an insect larva known for its ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene films, especially when pretreated.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRETREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Pretreatment refers to processes that enhance the biodegradation of plastics, such as low-density polyethylene, by organisms like the greater wax worm.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOCOCCUS OPACUS R7">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodococcus opacus R7 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polyethylene, as demonstrated by transcriptomic analysis during growth on polyethylene substrates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYOLEFINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyolefins are a group of polymers, including polyethylene and polypropylene, widely used in plastic production and known for their persistence in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET PLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PET plastic, or polyethylene terephthalate, is a common plastic used in packaging and bottles, contributing to environmental pollution due to its slow degradation rate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGROVE SEDIMENTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mangrove sediments are ecosystems that harbor diverse microbial communities capable of degrading natural organic polymers and potentially synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEBAELLIDA TERMITIDIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sebaelida termitidis is a microorganism associated with insect-dwelling environments, potentially involved in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANTOEE SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pantoee sp. is a bacterium that can increase amino acid content in plant tissues and is associated with nitrogen fixation and ammonium excretion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANDIDA ANTARCTICA LIPASE (CALB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Candida antarctica lipase (CALB) is an enzyme that, along with cutinase from Humicola insolens, can synergistically hydrolyze PET into terephthalic acid.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOBIFIDA FUSCA CARBOXYLESTERASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermobifida fusca carboxylesterase is an enzyme capable of hydrolyzing cyclic poly(ethylene terephthalate) trimers, aiding in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDIANMEAL MOTH LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Indianmeal moth larvae are another insect species studied for their gut microbial communities and their potential role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMICOLA INSOLENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Humicola insolens is a fungus that produces cutinase, an enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of PET into terephthalic acid.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOBIFIDA FUSCA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermobifida fusca is a bacterium that produces carboxylesterase, an enzyme capable of hydrolyzing cyclic PET trimers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECALCITRANT PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Recalcitrant plastics are petroleum-based plastics that are resistant to degradation, posing significant environmental challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Recalcitrant plastics are types of plastics that are resistant to degradation, posing significant environmental challenges, but mealworms have shown potential in degrading some of these materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BHET">
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd</data>
      <data key="d1">BHET is a hydrolyzed product of PET degradation, formed during the enzymatic breakdown of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MHET">
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd</data>
      <data key="d1">MHET is a hydrolyzed product of PET degradation, which is further metabolized into ethylene glycol and terephthalic acid.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET HYDROLYSIS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
      <data key="d1">Candida antarctica lipase (CALB) works synergistically with cutinase to hydrolyze PET into terephthalic acid, aiding in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOTOXICOLOGICAL SIMULATION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">An ecotoxicological simulation using T.E.S.T. confirmed that PE degradation products have low ecotoxicity to Daphnia magna and Rattus norvegicus, providing insights into the environmental safety of insect-mediated biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RATTUS NORVEGICUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rattus norvegicus, commonly known as the Norway rat, is a model organism used in toxicological assessments, including studies on the ecotoxicity of polyethylene degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZOQUINONE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzoquinone is a chemical compound found in the biomass of Tenebrio larvae, which may have implications for their nutritional and biochemical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzoquinone is a chemical compound produced by Tribolium confusum, studied for its ecological and genetic implications.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzoquinone is a chemical compound that increased in concentration in the larvae fed polyethylene, indicating oxidative stress and potential physiological damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSIOLOGY RESPONSES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Physiology responses refer to the changes in the physiological state of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae when exposed to polyethylene, including weight changes, fat content reduction, and intestinal tissue damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Ecotoxicity refers to the potential harmful effects of polyethylene degradation products on the environment and organisms, as assessed using Daphnia magna and Rattus norvegicus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL ECOLOGICAL NICHE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut microbial ecological niche refers to the specific environment within the gut of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, which adapts to polyethylene exposure, altering microbial diversity and community structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPODOPTERA FRUGIPERDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Spodoptera frugiperda larvae are plastic-degrading invertebrates that could be studied for oxidative stress responses to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Spodoptera frugiperda, commonly known as the fall armyworm, is an insect that can biodegrade polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL OXIDATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal oxidation is a process that can make polyethylene (PE) more susceptible to biodegradation in natural environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTO-OXIDATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Photo-oxidation is a pretreatment process that can enhance the biodegradability of polyethylene by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Photo-oxidation is a process that breaks down polyethylene (PE) through exposure to light, making it more susceptible to microbial biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Photo-oxidation is a process that can enhance the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by breaking down its structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Photo-oxidation is a process where polyethylene films containing pro-oxidant additives are broken down by exposure to light, facilitating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORBICULA FLUMINEA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Corbicula fluminea, a freshwater clam, has been studied for its response to long-term exposure to microplastics, which can cause mechanical damage and inflammatory responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Corbicula fluminea, or freshwater benthic clams, are used to study the genotoxic and intestinal damage effects of nanoplastics compared to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MECHANICAL DAMAGE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mechanical damage refers to physical harm caused by microplastics, such as abrasion or blockage, observed in organisms like Corbicula fluminea, mice, and zebrafish.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLIC INTERMEDIATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolic intermediates are compounds generated during the biodegradation of plastics like HDPE, LLDPE, and LDPE by Tenebrio larvae, which may affect their physiology.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE-MPS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PE-MPs refer to polyethylene microplastics, which are ingested and degraded by Tenebrio larvae, leading to oxidative stress and ecotoxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-MPs refer to polyethylene microplastics, which are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene polymers and are studied for their biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which mealworms can consume, leading to changes in their gut microbiome and nutrient utilization.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) are small plastic particles derived from the degradation of polyethylene, which are studied for their biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) are small plastic particles that Tenebrio larvae can ingest and degrade, with their gut microbiota adapting to enhance carbon and nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SINOPHARM CHEMICAL REAGENT CO., LTD.">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. is a supplier of analytical grade chemicals used in the biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEIJING AOBOXING BIO-TECH CO., LTD.">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Beijing Aoboxing Bio-Tech Co., Ltd. provided the agar used in the biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARTIFICIAL CLIMATE CHAMBER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">An artificial climate chamber is a controlled environment used to simulate specific temperature and humidity conditions for experiments, such as biodegradation studies with larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;An artificial climate chamber is used to maintain controlled environmental conditions (humidity and temperature) for the biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FENTON SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Fenton system is used to extract polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) from the frass of Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OLIVE OIL-BASED EXTRACTION METHOD">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An olive oil-based extraction method is used to extract polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) from the frass of Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL INDEX (CI)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The Carbonyl Index (CI) is used to evaluate the oxidation of polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) by measuring the intensity of the carbonyl peak.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonyl index (CI) is a measure of oxidation in plastics, used to assess the extent of biodegradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYROLYSIS-GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY/MASS SPECTROMETRY (PY-GC/MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PY-GC/MS is a technique used to study the chemical composition differences between original polyethylene and residual polyethylene from frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Py-GC/MS is used to analyze the degradation products of polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOMASS NUTRIENT CONCENTRATION">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biomass nutrient concentration levels are determined to assess the nutritional composition of mealworms after biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE MICROPLASTICS (PE-MPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which are studied for their impact on the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species and their biodegradation pathways."&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which can induce oxidative stress and damage in organisms like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE FATS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude fats are a nutrient component measured in the larval biomass, following the standard GB 5009.6–2016.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude proteins are a nutrient component measured in the larval biomass, following the standard GB/T 6433-2006.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZO-QUINONES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzo-quinones are chemical compounds measured in the larval biomass as part of the physiologic indexes for larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYSTEINE (CYS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cysteine (Cys) content is determined using a Cys assay kit, providing insights into the biochemical composition of the larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLATE READER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A microplate reader is used to measure absorbance levels in assays for ROS, MDA, and SOD in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;A microplate reader is used to measure the concentration of biochemical compounds in the larval biomass at 600 nm absorbance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OPERATIONAL TAXONOMIC UNITS (OTUS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are clusters of sequences used in the study to analyze microbial community composition and diversity.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are clusters of similar DNA sequences used to classify and study microbial communities in the guts of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are identified from 16S rRNA gene sequencing results to analyze microbial diversity in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational taxonomic units (OTUs) are used to classify microbial communities based on genetic data, providing insights into their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) were clustered to analyze the diversity of the gut microbiome in larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are clusters of similar 16S rRNA gene sequences used to classify and study microbial communities in the gut of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are clusters of similar DNA sequences used to classify and analyze microbial communities in the gut of mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SILVA 138 16S RRNA DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Silva 138 16S rRNA database is used to analyze the taxonomy of 16S rRNA gene sequences in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABUND JACCARD DISTANCE-BASED REDUNDANCY ANALYSIS (DBRDA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Abund Jaccard distance-based redundancy analysis (dbRDA) is used to analyze the relationship between larval diet, host-specificity, and PE-MP PCPs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARTIAL LEAST SQUARES STRUCTURAL EQUATION MODELING (PLS-SEM)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) is used to analyze the relationship between Tenebrio species, microbiome, and PE-MP properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHTED GENE CO-EXPRESSION NETWORK ANALYSIS (WGCNA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weighted gene co-expression network analysis (WGCNA) is a method used to study gene expression patterns and their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Weighted gene co-expression network analysis (WGCNA) is used to explore relationships between genes and identify functional gene clusters in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KYOTO ENCYCLOPEDIA OF GENES AND GENOMES (KEGG)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) is a database used to study metabolic pathways and their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) is used to screen for hub KEGG Orthology (KO) enrichment related to metabolism in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOTAL ANTIOXIDANT CAPACITY (T-AOC)">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Total antioxidant capacity (T-AOC) is a measure used to assess the oxidative stress response in organisms exposed to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Total antioxidant capacity (T-AOC) is measured to assess oxidative stress levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae exposed to polyethylene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMATOXYLIN AND EOSIN (H&amp;E) STAINING">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) staining is used to examine tissue sections of Tenebrio molitor larvae for signs of oxidative stress and damage.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACUTE ORAL TOXICITY (LD50)">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Acute oral toxicity (LD50) is estimated for degradation intermediates/compounds of polyethylene using QSR prediction in Rattus norvegicus and Daphnia magna.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANOVA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ANOVA analysis is used to assess differences in survival rates (SRs), average weight change rates (AWCRs), and microbial diversity in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STUDENT'S T-TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Student's t-test is used for pairwise comparisons in the statistical analysis of experimental data.&lt;SEP&gt;The Student's t-test is used to evaluate variations in larval diet and species, with Tukey's correction applied for statistical analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The Student's t-test was used for pairwise comparisons in the study to evaluate statistical significance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SPECR (Specific Polymer Consumption Rate) is a metric used to measure the rate at which Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae consume polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SRS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SRs (Survival Rates) are measured to assess the survival of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae when fed with polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AWCRS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">AWCRs (Average Weight Change Rates) are used to evaluate the weight changes in Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae when fed with polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative functional groups, such as C-O-C and carboxylic acids, are formed during the biodegradation of polyethylene polymers by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT CHANGES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Changes in molecular weights (Mw, Mn, and Mz) of residual PS and LDPE in mealworm frass confirm the depolymerization and biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight changes are analyzed to evaluate the degradation of polyethylene polymers by Tenebrio species, indicating the extent of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight changes in polyethylene polymers are observed as a result of biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, indicating depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight changes are studied to assess the extent of biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics by mealworms, indicating the efficiency of depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight changes refer to the alterations in the molecular weight of microplastics (Mn, Mw, Mz) after being degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight changes refer to the reduction in the molecular weight of PS and PE polymers during their biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The study examines the changes in molecular weight (MW) of polystyrene microplastics after passing through the larval intestinal tract of mealworms, indicating degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL INDEX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The carbonyl index (CI) is a measure of the degree of oxidation on the surface of microplastics, which correlates with the microbial community's ability to degrade the plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonyl index is a measure of the oxidation level of microplastics, indicating changes in their chemical structure after aging and ingestion by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonyl index is a measure of the oxidation level of plastic samples, used to assess biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonyl index is a measure of the oxidation level of residual polyethylene polymers in the frass of Tenebrio larvae, indicating the extent of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonyl index is a measure used to assess the oxidation and degradation of plastics, such as LDPE, LLDPE, and HDPE, during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonyl index is a measure used to assess the aging and degradation of microplastics, indicating the presence of carbonyl groups formed during oxidation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECIFIC PE CONSUMPTION RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Specific PE consumption rate measures the amount of polyethylene consumed by mealworm larvae over a specific period, indicating their ability to biodegrade plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL PE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Residual PE refers to the remaining polyethylene in the frass of mealworm larvae after biodegradation, indicating the extent of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MN, MW, MZ)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight metrics (Mn, Mw, Mz) are used to characterize the degradation of polyethylene by mealworm larvae, showing changes in the polymer structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGEN INCORPORATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygen incorporation refers to the addition of oxygen-containing functional groups (e.g., carbonyl, aldehyde) during the biodegradation of polyethylene by Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG-CHAIN PE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Long-chain polyethylene polymers are the primary components of LDPE, HDPE, and LLDPE, which are broken down during biodegradation by Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE CARBOHYDRATE">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude carbohydrate is a nutritional component found in Tenebrio larvae, contributing to their value as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACID COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acid composition refers to the types and proportions of fatty acids present in the dry biomass of Tenebrio larvae, which are analyzed for nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STUDENT'S T-TESTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Student's t-tests are statistical methods used to determine the significance of differences in physiological responses and biodegradation outcomes between experimental groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIMPSON DIVERSITY INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Simpson diversity index is used to measure the diversity of gut microbial communities in Tenebrio species, showing significant variation among groups fed different polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEIP EVENNESS INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Heip evenness index is used to assess the evenness of gut microbial communities in Tenebrio species, indicating variations in microbial distribution when fed different polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEVINS OTU ABUNDANCE MATRIX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Levins OTU abundance matrix is used to analyze the niche breadth of gut microbial communities, indicating interactions between OTUs and their environment in Tenebrio species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Β DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">β diversity analysis is used to compare the gut microbial communities of Tenebrio species at the OTU level, revealing differences in microbial composition when fed different polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ΒNTI/RCBRAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">βNTI/RCbray is a method used to analyze microbial community assembly and structure in Tenebrio species, providing insights into the effects of polyethylene polymers on gut microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARTIAL LEAST SQUARES PATH MODELING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Partial least squares path modeling is used to analyze the effects of major variables on gut microbial communities and molecular weight changes in Tenebrio species fed polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER CONTACT ANGLES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Water contact angles are measured to assess changes in the hydrophobicity of polyethylene polymers during biodegradation by Tenebrio species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY ASSEMBLY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The study of microbial community assembly involves understanding how deterministic and stochastic processes shape the gut microbiome of mealworms fed different diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DETERMINISTIC ECOLOGICAL PROCESSES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Deterministic ecological processes influence the assembly and structure of microbial communities in the gut of mealworms, driven by diet and species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STOCHASTIC ECOLOGICAL PROCESSES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stochastic ecological processes, such as dispersal and drift, also play a role in shaping the gut microbiome of mealworms, though to a lesser extent than deterministic processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HETEROGENEOUS SELECTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Heterogeneous selection is a deterministic process that significantly influences the gut microbial communities of mealworms, accounting for 68% of the assembly in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNDOMINATED ASSEMBLY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Undominated assembly refers to microbial community assembly processes that are not dominated by a single factor, accounting for 26% of the gut microbiome structure in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISPERSAL LIMITATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Dispersal limitation is a stochastic process that affects the gut microbiome of mealworms, accounting for 6% of the microbial community assembly in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NICHE WIDTH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Niche width refers to the diversity and adaptability of gut microbial communities in mealworms, which increases when fed polyethylene (PE) diets compared to wheat bran (WB).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONAL REDUNDANCY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Functional redundancy in the gut microbiome of mealworms decreases when fed polyethylene (PE) diets, leading to increased resource utilization efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio species, including T. molitor and T. obscurus, are studied for their gut microbiomes and their role in the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WB DIET">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">The wheat bran (WB) diet is used as a control diet in studies comparing the effects of polyethylene microplastics on the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species."&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran (WB) diet is a nitrogen-rich diet that meets the nitrogen requirements of Tenebrio larvae, contrasting with the nitrogen-deficient PE diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES (PCPS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Physicochemical properties (PCPs) of polyethylene microplastics, such as molecular weight, crystallinity, and branching, influence their biodegradation by the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASSILIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Massilia is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, contributing to the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OCHROBACTUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ochrobactrum is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, associated with the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REYRENELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Reyrenella is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, contributing to the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMAMONAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Comamonas is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, contributing to nitrogen metabolism and the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMPEDOBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Empedobacter is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, associated with the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OCEANOBACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Oceanobacillus is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, contributing to the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLIRUBROBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Solirubrobacter is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, associated with the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEUCONOSTOC">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Leuconostoc is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species, contributing to the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio larvae are plastic-degrading insects that exhibit oxidative stress responses when exposed to polyethylene microplastics, impacting their physiological functions and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio larvae, including T. obscurus and T. molitor, are mealworms that exhibit enhanced gut microbiome functions associated with carbon and nitrogen metabolism, crucial for plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T. OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">T. obscurus is a species of Tenebrio larvae that exhibits enhanced gut microbiome functions associated with carbon and nitrogen metabolism, particularly during the degradation of PE polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus, a species of darkling beetle larvae, is also capable of biodegrading polyethylene (PE) microplastics, similar to T. molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus, a species of darkling beetle larvae, is also capable of biodegrading polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) similar to T. molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus is a species of larvae that is more efficient in polystyrene (PS) biodegradation compared to T. molitor, as indicated by the mass loss ratio of frass during thermal decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus is a species of mealworm larvae that can chew and ingest polystyrene foam, showing adaptive behavior and higher PS consumption rates compared to T. molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus is another species of insect larvae that has been studied for its ability to depolymerize plastics, with the process being gut-microbe dependent.&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus is an organism mentioned in the context of biodegradation studies, though specific details about its role are not provided in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus is another species of larvae that, like Tenebrio molitor, can biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) through oxidation and chemical modifications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) polymers are nitrogen-free and carbon-rich plastics that Tenebrio larvae can degrade, with their gut microbiota adapting to enhance carbon and nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AROMATIC COMPOUND DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Aromatic compound degradation is a metabolic function enhanced in T. molitor larvae when fed with PE-MPs, indicating adaptation to nitrogen-deficient conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHITINOLYSIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Chitinolysis is a metabolic function significantly increased in T. molitor larvae fed with HDPE, indicating their preference for assimilating chitin-rich exoskeletons to obtain nitrogen.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TWO-COMPONENT SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">A two-component system in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio larvae is stimulated during PE ingestion, facilitating signal transduction and bacterial chemotaxis for PE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE STRESS INDICES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative stress indices are biochemical markers investigated in Tenebrio larvae after continuous feeding with PE-MPs, indicating potential ecotoxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAPROTAX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Functional Annotation of Prokaryotic Taxa (FAPROTAX) is a tool used to identify differences in intestinal microbial ecology, metabolic pathways, and microbial functions in Tenebrio larvae fed with different diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KO ENRICHMENT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KO enrichment analysis is a method used to identify gene functions and metabolic pathways associated with gut bacterial KO modules in Tenebrio larvae during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WGCNA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weighted Gene Co-expression Network Analysis (WGCNA) is a method used to build co-expression modules and analyze interactions among gut bacterial KO modules in Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-AOC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Total antioxidant capacity (T-AOC) reflects the organism's overall level of protection against oxidative stress damage caused by reactive oxygen species (ROS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PTS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Phosphotransferase system (PTS) is a signal transduction pathway enriched in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio larvae during the biodegradation of polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL MP PARTICLES">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Residual microplastic particles are byproducts of polyethylene biodegradation that cause oxidative stress and negative physiological responses in Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WB-FED CONTROLS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">WB-fed controls refer to mealworms fed with wheat bran, used as a baseline for comparison in studies on polyethylene biodegradation and oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSICAL AND CHEMICAL PROPERTIES (PCPS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Physical and chemical properties (PCPs) are studied to understand the degradation of polyethylene and other plastics, providing insights into their environmental impact and biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S DATA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S data is used to analyze microbial communities involved in plastic degradation, providing insights into the role of gut bacteria in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATIONAL ENGINEERING RESEARCH CENTER FOR BIOENERGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The National Engineering Research Center for Bioenergy conducts research on bioenergy and plastic degradation, contributing to sustainable solutions for environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYROLYSIS GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY MASS SPECTROMETRY (PY GC-MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrolysis gas chromatography mass spectrometry (Py GC-MS) is used to study the thermal degradation of plastics and identify their chemical components.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RIBO SOMAL DATABASE PROJECT (RDP)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Ribosomal Database Project (RDP) is a resource for analyzing microbial communities involved in plastic degradation and other environmental processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NON-METRIC MULTIDIMENSIONAL SCALING (NMDS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Non-metric multidimensional scaling (NMDS) is a statistical method used to analyze microbial community data and their relationship to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISTANCE-BASED REDUNDANCY ANALYSIS (DB-RDA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Distance-based redundancy analysis (db-RDA) is a statistical technique used to study the relationship between microbial communities and environmental factors, including plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Β-NEAREST TAXON INDEX (ΒNTI)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The β-nearest taxon index (βNTI) is used to analyze microbial community assembly processes and their role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAY–CURTIS-BASED RAUP–CRICK (RCBRAY)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bray–Curtis-based Raup–Crick (RCBray) is a statistical method used to compare microbial communities and their involvement in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARTIAL LEAST SQUARES-STRUCTURAL EQUATION MODELING (PLS-SEM)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Partial least squares-structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) is a statistical technique used to analyze complex relationships between variables, including those in plastic degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GOODNESS OF FIT (GOF)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Goodness of fit (GOF) is a statistical measure used to evaluate the accuracy of models in plastic degradation and environmental studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KEGG ORTHOLOGY (KO)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KEGG Orthology (KO) is a classification system used to analyze gene functions and their involvement in plastic degradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VARIATION PARTITIONING ANALYSIS (VPA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Variation Partitioning Analysis (VPA) is a statistical technique used to determine the contribution of factors like density and molecular weight to polystyrene consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Variation partitioning analysis (VPA) is a statistical method used to study the contributions of different factors to microbial community composition and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONAL ANNOTATION OF PROKARYOTIC TAXA (FAPROTAX)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Functional Annotation of Prokaryotic Taxa (FAPROTAX) is a tool used to predict the functional roles of microbial communities in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEDIAN LETHAL DOSE (LD50)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The median lethal dose (LD50) is a measure of toxicity, used to evaluate the lethal effects of microplastics on organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEDIAN LETHAL CONCENTRATION (LC50)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The median lethal concentration (LC50) is a measure of toxicity, used to assess the lethal effects of microplastics on organisms over a specific time period.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUANTITATIVE STRUCTURE-ACTIVITY RELATIONSHIP (QSAR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Quantitative structure-activity relationship (QSAR) is a computational method used to predict the toxicity of microplastics based on their chemical structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXICITY ESTIMATION SOFTWARE TOOL (T.E.S.T)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Toxicity Estimation Software Tool (T.E.S.T) is used to predict the toxicity of chemicals, including microplastics, based on their properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-density polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from low-density polyethylene, which can affect soil organisms like Eisenia fetida.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene microplastics are small plastic particles that can affect soil organisms like earthworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXACHLOROCYCLOHEXANE (HCH)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexachlorocyclohexane (HCH) is a persistent environmental pollutant that can affect microbial diversity in contaminated soils.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMAN INTESTINE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The human intestine hosts a mutualistic relationship with gut bacteria, which play a role in digestion and overall health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXACHLOROCYCLOHEXANE (HCH) CONTAMINATED SOIL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hexachlorocyclohexane (HCH) contaminated soil is an environmental pollutant that affects microbial diversity and vegetation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXACHLOROCYCLOHEXANE (HCH) TOLERANT VEGETATION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">HCH-tolerant vegetation refers to plants that can grow in soil contaminated with hexachlorocyclohexane, influencing microbial diversity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TERMITE HINDGUT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The termite hindgut contains a diverse microbial community that aids in the digestion of refractory materials, such as cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOSPHOTRANSFERASE SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The phosphotransferase system is a group of proteins involved in the transport of sugars, identified in the termite hindgut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucosidase is an enzyme that breaks down complex sugars, identified in the termite hindgut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental performance refers to the assessment of how bio-based and biodegradable plastics impact the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIO-BASED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bio-based plastics are derived from renewable resources and are studied for their environmental performance compared to traditional plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bio-based plastics are derived from renewable sources and are studied for their environmental performance and biodegradability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC PARTICLES">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic particles are small plastic fragments that can cause intestinal damage and adverse effects in aquatic organisms like zebrafish and nematodes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEMATODE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nematodes (Caenorhabditis elegans) are used in studies to assess the adverse effects of microplastic particles on intestinal health and other biological functions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRESHWATER BENTHIC CLAMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Freshwater benthic clams (Corbicula fluminea) are used to study the genotoxic and intestinal damage effects of nanoplastics compared to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERTHERMOPHILIC COMPOSTING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyperthermophilic composting is a high-temperature composting process that accelerates the removal of antibiotic resistance genes and mobile genetic elements in sewage sludge.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperthermophilic composting is a process that accelerates the removal of antibiotic resistance genes and mobile genetic elements in sewage sludge.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZOPHOBAS ARATUS LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zophobas aratus larvae are capable of biodegrading foam plastics, with changes in gut digestive enzyme activities and microbiome observed during the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTESTINAL EPITHELIAL BARRIER">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">The intestinal epithelial barrier is a critical structure that distinguishes between beneficial microbial flora and harmful pathogens, impacting food safety and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;The intestinal epithelial barrier is crucial for distinguishing between beneficial microbial flora and harmful pathogens, impacting food safety and health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGAVE TEQUILANA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Agave tequilana is a plant studied for its cultivable endophytic bacteria, which play a role in promoting plant growth and health.&lt;SEP&gt;Agave tequilana is a plant studied for its cultivable endophytic bacteria, which play a role in promoting plant growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODIET TREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodiet treatment is a process used in bioreactors to treat wastewater, influencing bacterial succession and pollutant removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodiet treatment is a process used in bioreactors to treat wastewater, influencing bacterial succession and the removal of pollutants like microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LINEAR POLYOLEFINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Linear polyolefins are a type of polymer that can undergo biodegradation under natural weathering conditions, as studied in the referenced research.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear polyolefins are polymers studied for their biodegradation under natural weathering conditions, contributing to understanding plastic degradation in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear polyolefins are polymers studied for their biodegradation under natural weathering conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIO-BASED AND BIODEGRADABLE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bio-based and biodegradable plastics are studied for their environmental performance and potential to reduce plastic pollution compared to traditional petroleum-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZEBRAFISH DANIO RERIO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zebrafish (Danio rerio) are model organisms used to study the effects of microplastic particles on intestinal health and overall biological functions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAENORHABDITIS ELEGANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Caenorhabditis elegans is a model organism used to study the toxic effects of imidazolium-based ionic liquids and the role of reactive oxygen species.&lt;SEP&gt;Caenorhabditis elegans is a nematode species used in research to assess the adverse effects of microplastic particles on intestinal health and other biological processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEWAGE SLUDGE PURIFICATION PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The sewage sludge purification process is developed to analyze and degrade common polymers, including microplastics, in wastewater treatment systems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPLASTIC-INDUCED GENOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanoplastic-induced genotoxicity refers to the DNA damage caused by nanoplastics in organisms like freshwater benthic clams, highlighting their environmental risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GALLERIA MELLONELLA L.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Galleria mellonella L., or greater wax moth larvae, are capable of biodegrading polyethylene and polystyrene, with their gut microbiome playing a key role in the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC RESISTANCE GENES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic resistance genes are pollutants found in sewage sludge and other environments, posing risks to public health and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOBILE GENETIC ELEMENTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mobile genetic elements are genetic materials that can transfer between organisms, contributing to the spread of antibiotic resistance in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATURAL WEATHERING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Natural weathering refers to the environmental degradation of materials like plastics over time due to exposure to elements like sunlight, rain, and microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SARS-COV-2 RNA FRAGMENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SARS-CoV-2 RNA fragment is a viral component studied for its interactions with microplastics and potential impacts on viral transport.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A bacterium capable of degrading and assimilating poly(ethylene terephthalate), contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterium isolated from the gut of insect larvae is capable of degrading polyvinyl chloride, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d7f6907ee7ce47b00537b1458fcc029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POTENTIALLY PATHOGENIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Potentially pathogenic bacteria are studied for their spatial variation in large subtropical rivers, impacting environmental health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANALYTICAL GRADE CHEMICALS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
      <data key="d1">Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. supplies the analytical grade chemicals used in the biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDOPHYTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
      <data key="d1">Agave tequilana hosts endophytic bacteria that promote plant growth, highlighting its ecological importance.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER BAUMANNII">
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter baumannii is a bacterial species known for its role in the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter baumannii is a bacterium with potential for biodegrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Unsaturated fatty acids inhibit quorum sensing, motility, and biofilm formation in Acinetobacter baumannii.</data>
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEACHATE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Leachate is a toxic byproduct generated from the breakdown of plastics like EPS in landfills, which can contaminate land and water resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Leachate is a toxic byproduct generated from the breakdown of plastics like EPS in landfills, which can contaminate land and water resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Leachate refers to the liquid that drains from waste materials, such as expanded polystyrene, and can contain toxic substances harmful to aquatic organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Leachate refers to the liquid that drains from waste materials, such as expanded polystyrene, and can contain toxic substances harmful to aquatic organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FURANS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Furans are harmful byproducts produced during the disposal of EPS, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPINACH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Spinach is a nutrient source used in experiments to study its effect on EPS consumption by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Spinach is a nutrient source used in experiments to study its effect on EPS consumption by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Spinach is used as a supplemental nutrient in habitats to study its effect on mealworm behavior and EPS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Spinach was used as a supplemental nutrient in experiments to study its effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Spinach is used as a supplemental nutrient in habitats to study its effect on mealworm behavior and EPS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Spinach was used as a supplemental nutrient in experiments to study its effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEMON-LIME SODA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lemon-lime soda (Sprite) is used as a pre-treatment for EPS cups to study its effect on mealworm consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Lemon-lime soda (Sprite) is used as a pre-treatment for EPS cups to study its effect on mealworm consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Lemon-lime soda is a pre-treatment used to enhance EPS consumption by mealworms in experimental studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Lemon-lime soda is an acidic pre-treatment that significantly increases the consumption rate of EPS by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lemon-lime soda was used as a pre-treatment for EPS to study its effect on mealworm consumption rates and biodegradation efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Lemon-lime soda is a pre-treatment used to enhance EPS consumption by mealworms in experimental studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Lemon-lime soda is an acidic pre-treatment that significantly increases the consumption rate of EPS by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lemon-lime soda was used as a pre-treatment for EPS to study its effect on mealworm consumption rates and biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOMATO PASTE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tomato paste (Hunt's) is used as a pre-treatment for EPS cups to study its effect on mealworm consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Tomato paste (Hunt's) is used as a pre-treatment for EPS cups to study its effect on mealworm consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Tomato paste is a pre-treatment that enhances the consumption rate of EPS by mealworms, possibly due to its acidity and nutrient content.&lt;SEP&gt;Tomato paste is a pre-treatment used to enhance EPS consumption by mealworms in experimental studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tomato paste was used as a pre-treatment for EPS to determine its impact on mealworm consumption rates and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tomato paste is a pre-treatment that enhances the consumption rate of EPS by mealworms, possibly due to its acidity and nutrient content.&lt;SEP&gt;Tomato paste is a pre-treatment used to enhance EPS consumption by mealworms in experimental studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tomato paste was used as a pre-treatment for EPS to determine its impact on mealworm consumption rates and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OATS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oats are used as a control diet for mealworms in the study to compare the effects of plastic diets like EPS and PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Oats are used as bedding and supplemental nutrients in habitats to study their effect on mealworm behavior and EPS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Oats are used as bedding and supplemental nutrients in habitats to study their effect on mealworm behavior and EPS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Oats were used as an edible bedding material in experiments to study their effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Oats were used as an edible bedding material in experiments to study their effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Oats were used as a control diet for mealworms in the study to compare the effects of plastic diets on their gut microbiota and plastic-degrading capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEADS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Beads were used as inedible bedding material in experiments to compare their effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN POWDER PASTE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein powder paste was used as a supplemental nutrient to study its effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CUCUMBERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cucumbers were used as a supplemental nutrient in experiments to study their effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEMON SLICES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lemon slices were used as a supplemental nutrient in experiments to study their effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER-TRANSFORMED INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier-transformed infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to analyze the chemical composition of fecula, providing evidence of polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (M_W AND M_N)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (M_w and M_n) measures are used to determine the size and length of polymer chains in fecula, indicating the extent of polystyrene breakdown.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (M_w and M_n) measures are used to determine the size and length of polymer chains in fecula, indicating the extent of polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER EXTRACTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer extracts are samples derived from fecula, analyzed to determine the molecular weight and structure of degraded polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymer extracts are the byproducts of polystyrene biodegradation, analyzed to determine the molecular weight and structure of the degraded material.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNACCOUNTED CARBON">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Unaccounted carbon refers to the portion of consumed polystyrene that is not recovered as biomass, carbon dioxide, or fecula during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM FECULA">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm fecula is the waste product of mealworms after consuming EPS, containing a mix of degraded polystyrene, biological matter, and byproducts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO-CULTURE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A co-culture of 50 mealworms and 50 beetles was studied to assess their combined EPS consumption and biodegradation rates, showing intermediate consumption rates compared to pure mealworm or beetle habitats.&lt;SEP&gt;A co-culture of 50 mealworms and 50 beetles was studied to assess their combined EPS consumption and biodegradation rates, showing intermediate consumption rates compared to pure mealworm or beetle habitats.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-culture refers to a habitat containing both mealworms and beetles, studied to understand the impact on EPS consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-culture refers to a habitat containing both mealworms and beetles, studied to understand the impact on EPS consumption rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL PEAKS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbonyl peaks in FTIR spectra indicate the presence of carbonyl groups, which are byproducts of polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKENE PEAKS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkene peaks in FTIR spectra indicate the presence of alkenes, which are byproducts of polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS-LOSS RATIO">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass-loss ratio is a measure used in TGA analysis to compare the mass loss of mealworm fecula to polystyrene feedstock, indicating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO-NUTRIENTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Co-nutrients are additional nutrients provided to mealworms in experiments to study their effect on EPS consumption rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEDDINGS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Beddings are materials used in mealworm habitats, which can influence their ability to consume and biodegrade EPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS PRE-TREATMENTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS pre-treatments are methods applied to EPS before feeding it to mealworms to study their impact on biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMBUSTIBLE GASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Combustible gases are produced during the pyrolysis of mealworm fecula, indicating the breakdown of organic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG-POLYMER CONCENTRATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Long-polymer concentration refers to the amount of undegraded polystyrene present in mealworm fecula, measured using TGA analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION RESIDUE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradation residue refers to the remaining byproducts of polystyrene degradation in mealworm fecula, including smaller polymer chains and biological matter.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEETLES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Beetles are co-cultured with mealworms in experiments to study their combined effects on EPS consumption and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are co-cultured with mealworms in experiments to study their combined effects on EPS consumption and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are ineffective for EPS consumption but may assist mealworms in the biodegradation process through potential chemical signaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are insects that primarily consume the hard outer layer of EPS cups, with minimal contribution to EPS degradation, averaging 0.00 ± 0.01 mg/day per beetle.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are the adult stage of Tenebrio molitor and are used in co-culture experiments with mealworms to study EPS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are ineffective for EPS consumption but may assist mealworms in the biodegradation process through potential chemical signaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are insects that primarily consume the hard outer layer of EPS cups, with minimal contribution to EPS degradation, averaging 0.00 ± 0.01 mg/day per beetle.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are the adult stage of Tenebrio molitor and are used in co-culture experiments with mealworms to study EPS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are the adult stage of mealworms, used in studies to evaluate the reproductive success and behavior of polystyrene-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are the adult stage of mealworms, which develop from pupae and are part of the life cycle observed in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a</data>
    </node>
