      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm cucumber refers to a nutrient source used in experiments to study its effect on EPS consumption by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM LEMON SLICE">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm lemon slice is a nutrient source used in experiments to study its effect on EPS consumption by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM PROTEIN POWDER PASTE">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm protein powder paste is a nutrient source used in experiments to study its effect on EPS consumption by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OAT HABITATS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oat habitats are a type of bedding used in experiments to study EPS consumption by mealworms, showing lower consumption rates compared to bead bedding.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPPLEMENTAL NUTRIENTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Supplemental nutrients, such as lemon slices, protein powder paste, and spinach, are used to increase EPS consumption rates by mealworms in experimental habitats.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN CO-NUTRIENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran co-nutrient is a supplemental nutrient that significantly improves the survival rate of mealworms in habitats containing polystyrene, as shown in previous studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LETTUCE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lettuce is a potential supplemental nutrient that could be used in future studies to enhance EPS consumption rates by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIRT BEDDING">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Dirt bedding is a potential alternative bedding material that could be studied for its effect on EPS consumption rates by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGGREGATE PHEROMONE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An aggregate pheromone is a potential chemical signal that could be released by beetles to increase EPS consumption rates by mealworms in co-culture studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RUTGERS UNIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Rutgers University – New Brunswick provided equipment and materials for the research study on biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Rutgers University – New Brunswick provided equipment and materials for the research study on biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Rutgers University's Department of Chemical and Biochemical Engineering supported the study with equipment and materials, contributing to the research on EPS biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Rutgers University's Department of Chemical and Biochemical Engineering supported the study with equipment and materials, contributing to the research on EPS biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL. (2018C)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang et al. (2018c) conducted a study showing the survival rates of mealworms in habitats with polystyrene and bran co-nutrient, highlighting the importance of supplemental nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHO AND LEE (2016)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Rho and Lee (2016) studied the effects of protein-to-carbohydrate ratios in the diet of mealworms, observing impacts on lifespan and reproductive output.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WOODS (1999)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Woods (1999) researched the effects of low-protein environments on insect species, including caterpillars and grasshoppers, showing increased death rates due to malnutrition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LE GALL AND BEHMER (2014)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Le Gall and Behmer (2014) studied the effects of low-protein diets on insect species, including mealworms, showing increased death rates and malnutrition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS waste refers to expanded polystyrene waste, a common environmental pollutant that poses challenges for disposal and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c</data>
