    <node id="CERIODAPHNIA DUBIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ceriodaphnia dubia is a small freshwater crustacean used in toxicological studies to assess the impact of environmental pollutants, such as leachate from expanded polystyrene cups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIERIS RAPAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pieris rapae, commonly known as the cabbage white butterfly, is an insect herbivore studied for its feeding behavior and growth in relation to environmental factors.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD PACKAGING">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Food packaging materials, including plastics like polystyrene and PVC, contribute to environmental pollution due to their persistence and resistance to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Food packaging materials, such as polystyrene, contribute to environmental pollution due to their widespread use and resistance to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXICITY ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxicity assessment involves evaluating the harmful effects of substances, such as plastics or their degradation products, on organisms and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The environmental microbiome refers to the collective microbial communities in ecosystems, which play a role in processes like plastic degradation and nutrient cycling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUSTAINABLE WASTE MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Sustainable waste management involves strategies like biodegradation to reduce the environmental impact of waste materials, such as plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBE-DEPENDENT DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbe-dependent depolymerization refers to the process by which gut microbes in mealworms and superworms break down plastics like polystyrene and polypropylene into smaller, less harmful components.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbe-dependent depolymerization refers to the process by which gut microorganisms in mealworms and superworms break down plastic polymers into simpler compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLIC PROFILING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolic profiling using GC-TOF-MS is a method used to evaluate the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by mealworm larvae under different feeding strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic profiling using GC-TOF-MS is a method used to evaluate the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by mealworm larvae under different feeding strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic profiling using GC-TOF-MS is a research method used to evaluate the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by mealworm larvae under different feeding strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YELLOW MEALWORM PROTEIN">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Yellow mealworm protein is extracted for food purposes and studied for its functional properties as a sustainable alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GEOLOGICAL CYCLE OF PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A research study examining the geological cycle of plastics and their use as indicators of the Anthropocene epoch.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTHROPOCENE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Anthropocene is a proposed geological epoch characterized by significant human impact on Earth's geology and ecosystems, including plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Xylene is a solvent used to dissolve Styrofoam for the preparation of polystyrene (PS) films in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methanol is a solvent used to rinse polystyrene (PS) films after preparation, ensuring no residual xylene remains.&lt;SEP&gt;Methanol is used as a solvent in the extraction of central carbon metabolism metabolites from insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Methanol is an LC-MS grade chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Methanol is part of the extraction solvent used for lipid extraction from tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Methanol is used to rinse experimental containers to remove potential microplastic contamination.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON-FREE BASAL AGAR MEDIUM (CFBAM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CFBAM is a growth medium used to isolate bacterial strains capable of degrading NPS, as it lacks carbon sources other than NPS.&lt;SEP&gt;CFBAM is an agar-based medium derived from LCFBM, used for cultivating PS-degrading microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LURIA-BERTANI (LB) MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LB medium is a nutrient-rich medium used for bacterial growth, prepared with tryptone, yeast extract, and NaCl.&lt;SEP&gt;Luria-Bertani (LB) medium is a nutrient-rich medium used to culture bacteria, including gut bacteria from mealworms, during antibiotic suppression tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Luria-Bertani (LB) medium is a nutrient-rich medium prepared by dissolving 25 g of LB broth in 1 L of distilled water, used for culturing bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT BROTH (NB) MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A growth medium used for culturing bacteria, including nitrogen-fixing isolates from mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;NB medium is a liquid medium used for bacterial growth, containing beef extract, tryptone, and NaCl.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SALINE WATER (SW)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Saline water is a solution of NaCl used for rinsing and preparing gut suspensions in mealworm studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CFBAM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CFBAM (Carbon-Free Basal Agar Medium) is a medium used to culture and isolate PS-degrading bacteria from mealworm guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMBIENT TEMPERATURE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Ambient temperature refers to the surrounding temperature conditions under which experiments, such as the incubation of PS-degrading bacteria, are conducted.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CFBAM PLATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CFBAM plates are used to culture PS-degrading bacteria and observe their growth on polystyrene films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) is a detergent used to remove biofilm from polystyrene films for surface property analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VORTEX MIXER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A vortex mixer is used to homogenize gut suspensions for bacterial isolation and antibiotic screening tests.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STERILE SW">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sterile seawater (SW) is used to prepare gut suspensions and cell suspensions for bacterial isolation and experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INHIBITION HALOS TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The inhibition halos test is used to screen antibiotics for their ability to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLATE COUNTING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plate counting is a method used to estimate bacterial numbers in gut suspensions after antibiotic treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE ISOTOPIC TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A stable isotopic test is used to measure the release of CO2 from the degradation of 13C-labeled polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROTARY SHAKER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A rotary shaker is used to incubate flasks containing PS-degrading bacterial enrichments under controlled conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLUORESCENCE MICROSCOPE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A fluorescence microscope is used to observe the viability of bacterial cells on polystyrene films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM (QUANTA FEG250)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A scanning electron microscope (SEM) is used to examine biofilm formation on polystyrene films by PS-degrading bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN YT2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain YT2 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) by attacking its structure and producing polar derivatives, as demonstrated by weight loss and molecular weight reduction in PS samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Strain YT2 is a bacterial strain isolated from the guts of mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) and forming biofilm on PS film surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAS CHROMATOGRAPH/MASS SPECTROMETER (GC/MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gas chromatograph/mass spectrometer (GC/MS) is used to identify water-soluble daughter products of PS degradation by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16S RDNA AMPLIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16S rDNA amplification is a molecular technique used to identify and classify bacterial strains, such as strain YT2, isolated from mealworm guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIDGUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Midgut bacteria are microorganisms inhabiting the midgut of mealworms, playing a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE FILM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene film is a form of polystyrene (PS) that can be degraded by microorganisms like strain YT2, leading to the formation of pits and cavities on its surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene film is a form of polystyrene used in experiments to study biodegradation, where it is degraded by bacterial strains like Exiguobacterium sp. YT2.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene film is a form of polystyrene that can be degraded by certain bacteria, such as Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene film is a form of polystyrene used in various applications, known for its slow biodegradation rate compared to nanopolystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene film is a form of polystyrene used in experiments to study bacterial adhesion and degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene film is a material used in experiments to study the degradation capabilities of microorganisms isolated from mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene film is a material used in the study to evaluate bacterial adhesion and biodegradation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene film is an additive-free styrene polymer with a thickness of 0.05 mm, used in research and industrial settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON (C)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon is a key element in polystyrene, and its oxidation during biodegradation is analyzed using techniques like XPS to understand the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGEN (O)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygen is introduced into polystyrene during biodegradation, forming polar groups like −C−O−, which are detected using XPS to confirm degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="R. RUBER C208">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">R. ruber C208 is a bacterial strain previously reported to degrade polystyrene, but with lower efficiency compared to strain YT2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE OLIGOMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene oligomer is a smaller chain derivative of styrene, and its microbial degradation has been studied.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTICARSIA GEMMATALIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Anticarsia gemmatalis, or velvetbean caterpillar, is an insect whose gut bacteria contribute to digestion and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPHESTIA CAUTELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ephestia cautella is a stored-product insect pest capable of penetrating packaging films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VELVETBEAN CATERPILLAR">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The velvetbean caterpillar (Anticarsia gemmatalis) is an insect whose gut bacteria contribute to its digestion and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIO2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">TiO2 (titanium dioxide) is a photocatalyst used in the solid-phase photocatalytic degradation of polystyrene plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-PHASE PHOTOCATALYTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Solid-phase photocatalytic degradation refers to the breakdown of solid pollutants, such as polystyrene, using photocatalysts like TiO2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT SUSPENSION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
      <data key="d1">A vortex mixer is used to homogenize gut suspensions for bacterial isolation and antibiotic screening.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C-LABELED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">13C-labeled polystyrene is used in research to trace the carbon flow during the biodegradation process, helping to quantify the breakdown of polystyrene into biomass and carbon dioxide.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOTRANSFORMATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biotransformation refers to the process by which insects convert plastic carbon into organic biomass, contributing to plastic degradation and protein production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic metabolism involves the biochemical processes by which insects break down and assimilate plastic carbon into their biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANIC WASTE PROCESSING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Organic waste processing involves the use of insects to treat waste streams, including plastic-contaminated materials, to produce valuable protein products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CENTRAL CARBON METABOLISM METABOLITES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Central carbon metabolism metabolites are compounds within basic cellular pathways that are influenced by plastic exposure in insects, affecting their metabolic responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMIN B6 METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamin B6 metabolism is a biochemical pathway that is elevated in Wax Moth larvae when exposed to certain plastics, indicating a specific metabolic response.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYRIMIDINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrimidine metabolism is a biochemical pathway that is elevated in Black Soldier Fly larvae when exposed to PET, indicating a unique metabolic response.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrimidine metabolism is downregulated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics, indicating altered nucleotide metabolism due to oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrimidine metabolism is an essential pathway for RNA and DNA synthesis, significantly perturbed in mealworms fed with ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PURINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Purine metabolism is a biochemical pathway that is strongly expressed in Black Soldier Fly larvae when exposed to certain plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Purine metabolism is upregulated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics, suggesting a shift in nucleotide metabolism due to oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT-SYMBIOT BREAKDOWN">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut-symbiont breakdown refers to the disruption of gut bacteria in insects, such as Black Soldier Fly larvae, when exposed to plastics, affecting their metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC CARBON ASSIMILATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic carbon assimilation is the process by which insects incorporate plastic carbon into their biomass, potentially impacting their development and protein production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste management systems involve the use of insects to process organic waste, including plastic-contaminated materials, to produce compost, feedstock, or protein products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT PHENOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect phenology refers to the study of how contaminants like plastics impact the development, behavior, and life cycle of insects used in waste processing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT AVAILABILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient availability is a critical factor in insect development and organic waste conversion efficiency, which can be negatively impacted by plastic contamination.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIT-FOR-PURPOSE INSECT BIOTRANSFORMERS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fit-for-purpose insect biotransformers are insects specifically matched to beneficial plastic carbon substrates to optimize plastic biodegradation and protein production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CENTRAL CARBON METABOLISM (CCM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Central Carbon Metabolism (CCM) comprises a suite of compounds within basic cellular pathways, providing insights into metabolic processes and responses to environmental perturbations.&lt;SEP&gt;Central carbon metabolism (CCM) refers to the biochemical pathways involved in the production of metabolites during insect interaction with plastic substrates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic is a major environmental pollutant, and its biodegradation by insects is being explored as a sustainable solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic is a persistent marine pollutant that accumulates in marine environments, causing harm to ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLASSES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molasses is a supplement used in the artificial feeding of plastic to insects in the study, mixed with water to create a coating for plastic exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Molasses is used as a supplement in the study to coat plastics for insect exposure experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEE WAX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bee wax is a substance consumed by Greater Wax Moth larvae, containing alkanes and alkenes that may aid in plastic metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanoplastic refers to extremely small plastic particles, often resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic materials, which can have significant environmental and health impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are extremely small plastic particles, and Mealworms are proposed as a model for studying their toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANIC FERTILIZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The frass of Mealworms and Greater Wax Moths has potential as an organic fertilizer, contributing to sustainable agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-INSECT EXPOSURE STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A controlled study evaluating the impact of various plastics on insect metabolism and larval weight.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRESH CARROT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fresh carrot is a food source provided to mealworms in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SWEET POTATO">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sweet potato is a food source used to rear mealworms in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUCCINIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Succinic acid (13C2) is used as an internal standard in the extraction and analysis of central carbon metabolism metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-PHENYLALANINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">L-Phenylalanine (1-13C) is used as an internal standard in the analysis of central carbon metabolism metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CENTRIFUGE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A centrifuge is used in the study to separate metabolites during the extraction process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAPTIVA EMR CARTRIDGES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Captiva EMR cartridges are used to remove the lipid fraction during the extraction of central carbon metabolism metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGILENT INFINITY FLEX II UHPLC">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An Agilent Infinity Flex II UHPLC is used to measure central carbon metabolism metabolites in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGILENT 6470 TRIPLE QUADRUPOLE MASS SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An Agilent 6470 Triple Quadrupole Mass Spectrometer is used to analyze central carbon metabolism metabolites in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOANALYST 5.0">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">MetaboAnalyst 5.0 is a software tool used for statistical analysis and pathway impact analysis in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIMCA 17.01">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SIMCA 17.01 is a software tool used for multivariate statistical analysis in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DROSOPHILA MELANOGASTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Drosophila melanogaster (fruit fly) is used as a model organism in the KEGG database for pathway impact analysis in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLACK SOLDIER FLY (BSF)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Black Soldier Fly larvae are used in studies to assess the metabolic effects of exposure to different types of plastics, including PET, PE, PS, PP, and PLA.&lt;SEP&gt;Black soldier fly (BSF) larvae are insect models used in studies to observe their metabolic interactions with plastics, such as PET and Exp PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS (MW)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms are insect larvae studied for their survival and metabolic responses when exposed to plastic treatments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX MOTH LARVAE (WM)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax moth larvae are studied for their survival and weight changes when exposed to plastic treatments, with significant effects observed due to supplementation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMIN B6">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamin B6 is a co-factor in biochemical reactions, observed to be downregulated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastic treatments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANS-TRANS-MUCONIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Trans-trans-muconic acid is a metabolite potentially originating from plastic polymers, suggesting plastic degradation activity in insect guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CENTRAL CARBON METABOLOME">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The central carbon metabolome refers to the network of metabolic pathways involving key intermediates such as amino acids, nucleotides, and vitamins, which are affected by exposure to plastics in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAMMA-AMINOBUTYRATE (GABA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gamma-aminobutyrate is a metabolite involved in the Ala-Asp-Glu metabolic pathway, observed to be elevated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-ACETYL GALACTOSAMINE (NACGAL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">N-acetyl galactosamine is a precursor of nucleotide sugars, involved in microbial adhesion and elevated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="URIDINE 5-DIPHOSPHATE (UDP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Uridine 5-diphosphate is a precursor of nucleotide sugars, involved in glycosphospholipid synthesis and elevated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-AMINOBENZOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">4-aminobenzoic acid is a metabolite suggesting plastic degradation activity in the guts of insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOMOCYSTEINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Homocysteine is a glutathione metabolism intermediate, elevated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics, indicating oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYRIDOXINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyridoxine is a form of Vitamin B6, observed to be depleted in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-PYRIDOXATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">4-pyridoxate is a Vitamin B6 intermediate, depleted in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL ADHESION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial adhesion is facilitated by glycosphospholipids synthesized from precursors like N-acetyl galactosamine and uridine 5-diphosphate, elevated in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED POLYETHYLENE (EXP PE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded polyethylene (Exp PE) is a plastic material that has been studied for its interaction with mealworms, showing varying degrees of weight loss post-exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine microbiota are microorganisms in marine environments that have been shown to experience oxidative stress and perturbed nucleotide metabolism due to plastic exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUCLEOTIDE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nucleotide metabolism is a cellular process that is perturbed in organisms exposed to plastics, leading to metabolic alterations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC TOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic toxicity refers to the harmful effects of plastics on organisms, including oxidative stress, inflammation, and metabolic alterations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Insect microbiota are microorganisms in the guts of insects that play a role in the biodegradation of plastics and are influenced by oxidative stress and plastic toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD SUPPLEMENT">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Food supplements, such as molasses, are used in studies to enhance the survival rate and metabolic performance of insect larvae exposed to plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX MOTH (WM)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax moth (WM) larvae are insect models that exhibit significant plastic loss when exposed to certain plastics, such as PE and PS, and show improved survival rates with food supplements.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BSF LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Black soldier fly (BSF) larvae are insects studied for their ability to metabolically interact with and degrade certain types of plastics, such as PET and expanded PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED PE PLASTIC">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded polyethylene (PE) plastic is a type of plastic that BSF larvae exhibit a negative or neutral metabolic interaction with.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOAM-BASED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Foam-based plastics are materials that certain insects, like WM model insects, can degrade, potentially converting them into biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CCM METABOLITES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Central carbon metabolism (CCM) metabolites are biochemical compounds produced by insects during their interaction with plastic substrates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MW MODEL INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">MW model insects are studied for their ability to degrade certain types of plastics, such as PLA and foam-based plastics, and their interaction with plastic substrates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WM MODEL INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">WM model insects are insects that perform well in degrading PLA and foam-based plastics, showing potential for plastic degradation and biomass conversion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLASSES SUPPLEMENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molasses supplement is added to plastic substrates in the study to observe its effect on insect metabolism and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPIDOMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipidomics is a research approach used to study the impact of polybrominated diphenyl ether-47 on breast cancer mice.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipidomics is a research approach used to study the lipid profiles of insects during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), providing insights into metabolic pathways and cell activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipidomics is an analytical approach used to study the lipid profiles of insects during plastic degradation, providing insights into biochemical changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipidomics is the study of lipid profiles and their changes, used in this research to evaluate the metabolic effects of polystyrene consumption on mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOME COMPOSITIONAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbiome compositional analysis is a method used to study the gut microbiome of insects and its role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MULTI-OMICS APPROACHES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Multi-omics approaches involve the integration of protein, lipid, and metabolite profiles to study the effects of plastic exposure on insects across multiple generations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GOTERRA PTY LTD">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Goterra Pty Ltd is the organization that supplied BSF larvae for the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CSIRO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CSIRO is the research organization involved in the study, providing intellectual contributions and resources for the analysis of insect plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KYNURENINES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Kynurenines are chemicals studied for their toxic and neurobehavioral effects on mealworms (Tenebrio molitor).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC IN COMPOST">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic in compost refers to the presence of plastic waste in compost materials, which can potentially enter agricultural and horticultural soils, posing environmental risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZEBRAFISH HEART">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zebrafish heart is studied to assess the adverse effects of polyethylene microplastics at molecular and individual levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EARTHWORM GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Earthworm gut bacteria are microorganisms that can degrade low-density polyethylene, contributing to soil restoration.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL RESTORATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil restoration involves the use of organisms like earthworms and their gut bacteria to degrade plastics and improve soil health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KYNURENINE PATHWAY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The kynurenine pathway is a metabolic pathway involved in tryptophan catabolism, studied for its physiological importance in insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The plastic cycle refers to the movement and transformation of plastics in the environment, considered an unknown branch of the carbon cycle.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TREHALOSE BIOSYNTHESIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Trehalose biosynthesis is a biological process that occurs in response to abiotic stresses, aiding in plant survival under harsh conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANIC WASTE STREAMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Organic waste streams are materials derived from organic sources, often used as feed for black soldier fly larvae in waste management studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT-MICROBE-DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization is a process by which microorganisms in the gut of insects like yellow mealworms and superworms break down plastics such as polypropylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL DYSBIOSIS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial dysbiosis refers to the disruption of microbial communities, often caused by environmental pollutants like polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRYPTOPHAN CATABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Tryptophan catabolism is a metabolic process involving the breakdown of tryptophan, studied for its physiological importance in insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The carbon cycle is a natural process involving the movement of carbon through the environment, now including the plastic cycle as a significant anthropogenic component.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MODULARITY OF INSECT PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Modularity of insect production refers to the study of scalable and efficient methods for rearing insects like black soldier fly larvae for waste management and protein production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-density polyethylene biodegradation refers to the breakdown of LDPE by insects like mealworms and superworms, either through gut-microbe-dependent or independent processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METAL BIOAVAILABILITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Metal bioavailability refers to the extent to which metals in the environment can be absorbed by organisms, often influenced by factors like frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADVANCED ANALYTICAL METHODS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Advanced analytical methods are techniques developed for detecting and analyzing microplastics contamination in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HENOSEPILACHNA VIGINTIOCTOPUNCTATA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Henosepilachna vigintioctopunctata is an insect studied for its physiological processes, including tryptophan catabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPPLEMENTATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
      <data key="d1">Supplementation significantly affects the survival and weight of Wax Moth larvae, with supplemented larvae showing reduced survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC TREATMENT">
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms exposed to plastic treatments show no significant effect on survival, but supplementation affects their metabolic responses.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOSPHOSPHOLIPID SYNTHESIS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
      <data key="d1">Elevated UDP levels indicate increased glycosphospholipid synthesis, facilitating microbial adhesion in Black Soldier Fly larvae exposed to plastics.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC SUBSTRATES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4</data>
      <data key="d1">Molasses supplement is added to plastic substrates to study its effect on insect metabolism and plastic degradation</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGRICULTURAL SOILS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
      <data key="d1">Agricultural soils are affected by microplastics, which can impact ecosystems and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Agricultural soils are increasingly contaminated with microplastics, which pose risks to ecosystems and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic in compost can enter agricultural soils, posing environmental risks and affecting soil health.</data>
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABIOTIC STRESSES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
      <data key="d1">Trehalose biosynthesis is a response mechanism to abiotic stresses in plants.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE ENVIRONMENTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine environments are particularly affected by plastic pollution, with mealworms offering a potential solution through biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE FRASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PE Frass refers to the degraded remnants of polyethylene after being processed by organisms, showing reduced molecular weight and density.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS FRASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS Frass refers to the degraded remnants of polystyrene after being processed by organisms, showing reduced molecular weight and density.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE + BRAN FRASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PE + Bran Frass refers to the degraded remnants of polyethylene mixed with bran, showing a reduction in molecular weight and density.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS + BRAN FRASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS + Bran Frass refers to the degraded remnants of polystyrene mixed with bran, showing a reduction in molecular weight and density.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (Mn) is a measure of the number-average mass of a polymer, used to evaluate degradation and structural changes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Density is a physical property of plastics, often measured to assess changes during degradation or processing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixed plastics, such as PE and PS, are common environmental pollutants that mealworms can degrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixed plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, are environmental pollutants that mealworms can consume and degrade.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE INSULATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded polystyrene insulation is a common plastic material used in construction and is studied for its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX MOTHS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax moths are another type of omnivorous insect larvae that have shown potential for degrading polyethylene (PE) in early research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATURAL WHEAT BRAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Natural wheat bran is used as a control diet for mealworms in biodegradation studies to compare the effects of plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD GRADE POLYPROPYLENE CONTAINER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Food grade polypropylene containers are used to house mealworms during biodegradation experiments to ensure safe and controlled conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PVDF FILTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A PVDF filter is used to filter solutions during the extraction process of residual polymers from mealworm frass.&lt;SEP&gt;PVDF (polyvinylidene fluoride) filters are used to filter extracts of frass for analysis of residual polystyrene content.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROTARY EVAPORATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Rotary evaporation is a technique used to concentrate residual polymers extracted from mealworm frass for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Rotary evaporation is a technique used to concentrate the residual polymer in the filtered solution of egested frass for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Rotary evaporation is a technique used to evaporate solvents like THF from extracts to isolate residual polystyrene for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E. COLI K12">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">E. coli K12 is a bacterial strain used as a control to assess contamination in experiments involving plastic degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUTATIVE GAS FRACTION (PGF)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The putative gas fraction (PGF) represents the portion of plastic mass lost as gas, likely CO2, during mineralization by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C-CARBON TRACING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">13C-carbon tracing is a technique used to measure the mineralization of plastics, such as PS, into CO2 by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INVERSE SIMPSON INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Inverse Simpson Index is a measure of microbial diversity used to assess the gut microbiome of mealworms under different diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAY–CURTIS DISSIMILARITY INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Bray–Curtis Dissimilarity Index is a metric used to compare microbial communities based on diet in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIFFERENTIAL ABUNDANCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Differential abundance analysis is a method used to identify specific gut bacteria associated with different diets in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEBALDELLA TERMIDITIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sebaldella termiditis is an anaerobic bacterium found in the termite gut and is associated with the polyethylene-fed microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BREVIBACTERIUM SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Brevibacterium sp. is an aerobic bacterium known for hydrocarbon degradation and is associated with the polyethylene-fed microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LISTERIA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Listeria sp. is a bacterium associated with the polystyrene-fed gut microbiome and may play a role in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEDOMICROBIUM SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pedomicrobium sp. is a bacterium associated with the polystyrene-fed gut microbiome and may play a role in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AQUIBABITAN SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aquibabitan sp. is a bacterium associated with the polystyrene-fed gut microbiome and may play a role in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNCLASSIFIED XANTHOMONADACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Unclassified Xanthomonadaceae is a bacterium associated with the polystyrene-fed gut microbiome and may play a role in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNCLASSIFIED SAPROSPIRACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Unclassified Saprospiraceae is a bacterium associated with the polystyrene-fed gut microbiome and may play a role in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNCLASSIFIED BURKHOLDERIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Unclassified Burkholderiales is a bacterium associated with the polystyrene-fed gut microbiome and may play a role in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL REPORT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The environmental report reviews waste strategies and their impacts, including the management of plastic waste and its environmental consequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE INPUTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste inputs refer to the amount of plastic waste entering the ocean from land, which is a significant environmental concern.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste inputs refer to the amount of plastic waste entering the ocean from land-based sources, contributing to marine pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOLOGICAL DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biological degradation refers to the breakdown of plastics by living organisms, such as bacteria, fungi, and insects, into simpler compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PEAR is a bioinformatics tool used for fast and accurate merging of Illumina paired-end reads, facilitating sequence analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UPARSE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">UPARSE is a bioinformatics tool for highly accurate OTU (Operational Taxonomic Unit) sequence analysis from microbial amplicon reads.&lt;SEP&gt;UPARSE is a bioinformatics tool for processing microbial amplicon reads, used in studies related to microbial communities and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Uparse is a tool used to cluster sequences into operational taxonomic units (OTUs) based on a 97% identity threshold in microbiome analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;UPARSE is a bioinformatics tool used for clustering operational taxonomic units (OTUs) from 16S rRNA gene sequences with a 97% identity threshold.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NAIVE BAYESIAN CLASSIFIER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A Naive Bayesian classifier is a bioinformatics tool used for rapid assignment of rRNA sequences into bacterial taxonomy.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VEGAN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vegan is an R package for community ecology analysis, providing tools for statistical analysis of ecological data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DESEQ2">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DESeq2 is a bioinformatics tool for moderated estimation of fold change and dispersion in RNA-seq data, enabling differential gene expression analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;DESeq2 is a statistical tool used for differential abundance analysis to identify overrepresented genera in PS samples compared to CT samples.&lt;SEP&gt;DESeq2 is a tool for moderated estimation of fold change and dispersion for RNA-seq data, as described by Love et al. (2014).&lt;SEP&gt;DESeq2 is an R package used for differential abundance analysis of microbial communities, applied to the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL MICROBIAL COMMUNITY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental microbial community analysis involves studying the diversity and function of microbial communities in various environments, often using high-throughput sequencing technologies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILLUMINA PAIRED-END READS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Illumina paired-end reads are sequencing data generated from both ends of DNA fragments, providing higher accuracy and coverage for sequence analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial community sequencing is a research approach that uses high-throughput sequencing to analyze the composition and function of microbial communities in various environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL AMPLICON READS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial amplicon reads are DNA sequences generated from specific regions of microbial genomes, often used for taxonomic and functional profiling of microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RRNA SEQUENCES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">rRNA sequences are genetic markers used for identifying and classifying microorganisms based on their ribosomal RNA genes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMUNITY ECOLOGY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Community ecology analysis involves studying the interactions and dynamics of species within ecological communities, often using statistical tools and models.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNA-SEQ DATA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">RNA-seq data is generated from sequencing RNA molecules, providing insights into gene expression and regulation in biological samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATERPILLARS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Caterpillars are larval stages of insects that have been studied for their potential role in the biodegradation of polyethylene, a common environmental pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL POLYMERS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8</data>
      <data key="d1">A PVDF filter is used to filter solutions containing residual polymers extracted from mealworm frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Residual polymers are the remaining high molecular weight fractions of polyethylene microplastics after partial biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Residual polymers are the remaining fragments of LDPE after biodegradation by larvae, analyzed to understand the extent of degradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAFNIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hafnia is a gut microbe strongly associated with the polystyrene diet in yellow mealworms, indicating its potential role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic biodegradation efficiency refers to the rate at which plastics, such as polystyrene and polyurethane, are broken down by insect larvae like superworms and yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC CONSUMPTION RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic consumption rate refers to the amount of plastic (e.g., PS, PU) consumed by yellow mealworms and superworms per unit time, measured in mg/larva/d.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic consumption rate refers to the amount of plastic, such as polystyrene or polyurethane, consumed by insect larvae like superworms and yellow mealworms over a specific period.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic consumption rate refers to the rate at which mealworms consume plastics like PS and PE, measured in the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC FEEDSTOCKS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic feedstocks refer to the types of plastics, such as polystyrene and polyurethane, used as diets for superworms and yellow mealworms in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Functional groups are chemical groups derived from oxidation and biodegradation processes in the guts of yellow mealworms and superworms during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLTING EXOSKELETON">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Molting exoskeletons are shed by larvae during growth and were removed daily to prevent cannibalism in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The bran-fed group refers to superworms and yellow mealworms fed with bran, showing higher survival and pupation rates compared to plastic-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;The bran-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with bran, showing a higher survival rate compared to mask-fed groups, indicating normal growth conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The bran-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with bran, serving as a control group to compare gut bacterial communities with plastic-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PU-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The PU-fed group refers to superworms and yellow mealworms fed with polyurethane foam, showing lower survival and pupation rates compared to bran-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;The PU-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with polyurethane (PU), showing significant changes in gut bacterial communities compared to the bran-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic consumption by mealworms refers to the process by which mealworms ingest and break down plastic materials, contributing to their degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic consumption refers to the ability of superworms and yellow mealworms to ingest and break down polystyrene and polyurethane foams, contributing to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic consumption refers to the amount of plastic consumed by mealworms during the experiments, measured by the mass difference of plastics before and after the experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic consumption refers to the amount of plastic ingested by mealworms, which varies depending on the type of plastic and time interval.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOLLOWS AND PITS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Hollows and pits are physical changes observed in polystyrene and polyurethane foams after being consumed by superworms and yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Z. ATRATUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Z. atratus, also known as the superworm, is an insect species studied for its ability to consume and degrade polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Z. atratus, commonly known as dark mealworms, are larvae studied for their gut microbiome composition and their ability to degrade plastics like polystyrene and polyurethane.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus, commonly known as superworms, are larvae that can consume and degrade polystyrene, similar to other plastic-eating insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus is a species of darkling beetle larvae known for its ability to biodegrade polypropylene (PP) and other plastics, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus larvae are large-sized mature larvae known for their strong cannibalism behavior under nutritional limitation and their ability to biodegrade polypropylene (PP).&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus, also known as the superworm, is a species of darkling beetle larvae known for its ability to biodegrade plastics like LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN-FED GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran-fed groups are control groups in the study, used to compare the gut microbiome composition and plastic degradation capabilities of mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE GLYCEROL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude glycerol is a byproduct of biodiesel production and is used as a base material for biodegradable polyurethane foams.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYVINYL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinyl refers to a group of polymers derived from vinyl compounds, commonly used in plastics and studied for their biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL ECOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial ecology involves the study of microorganisms and their interactions with the environment, including their role in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORIZON SCAN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A horizon scan is a research approach used to identify emerging issues in global conservation, such as the impact of microplastics on ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SULFAMETHOXAZOLE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sulfamethoxazole is an antibiotic whose biodegradation and metabolic pathways have been studied in microorganisms like Sphingobacterium mizutaii.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPHINGOBACTERIUM MIZUTAII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sphingobacterium mizutaii is a bacterium capable of biodegrading sulfamethoxazole, demonstrating its role in environmental remediation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE ELECTRICAL AND ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste electrical and electronic equipment (WEEE) plastics are a type of environmental pollutant that insect larvae, such as mealworms and superworms, can biodegrade.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYURETHANE ELASTOMERS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal stability is a key property of polyurethane elastomers, affecting their degradation under high temperatures.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSOXIDANS M9">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading polystyrene with a weight loss of 7% over 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworm intestines, known for its ability to degrade polystyrene and form biofilms.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing significant ability to degrade polystyrene nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE NANOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene nanoplastics are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, posing significant environmental threats due to their high mobility and toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA DSM 50071">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain isolated from Zophobas atratus, studied for its role in PS film biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain known for its role in the decomposition of polystyrene through physical and chemical processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of Zophobas atratus, known for its ability to degrade polystyrene and increase serine hydrolase gene expression during degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of the superworm (Zophobas atratus) that can degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASSILIA SP. FS1903">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Massilia sp. FS1903 is a bacterial strain involved in the decomposition of polystyrene, aiding in its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AROMATIC HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aromatic hydrocarbons are organic compounds that Achromobacter xylosoxidans can degrade, showcasing its diverse metabolic capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPOLYSTYRENE (NPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanopolystyrene (NPS) is a synthesized form of polystyrene used in the study to investigate its physical and chemical properties and its interaction with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPRECIPITATION METHOD">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The nanoprecipitation method is a technique used to synthesize nanopolystyrene (NPS) particles for the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STERILIZATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sterilization involves the use of ethanol and other methods to ensure the cleanliness of plastic materials like PSF and NPS used in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYNAMIC LIGHT SCATTERING (DLS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dynamic light scattering (DLS) is a technique used to characterize the size and properties of nanopolystyrene (NPS) particles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XRD ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">XRD analysis is employed to examine the crystalline structure of nanopolystyrene (NPS) in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM MICROGRAPH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM micrograph is a technique used to visualize the surface morphology of nanopolystyrene (NPS) particles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEM MICROGRAPH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">TEM micrograph is used to analyze the internal structure of nanopolystyrene (NPS) particles at a microscopic level.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAMINAR FLOW CABINET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A laminar flow cabinet is used to maintain a sterile environment for drying plastic films like PSF.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INCUBATOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An incubator is used to maintain optimal temperature and humidity conditions for mealworms during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANO-POLYSTYRENE (NPS)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nano-polystyrene (NPS) is a form of polystyrene at the nanoscale, used in experiments to study its effects on bacterial growth and biofilm formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Nano-polystyrene (NPS) is a form of polystyrene used as a sole carbon source in the study to isolate and identify plastic-degrading bacteria from mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nano-polystyrene (NPS) refers to small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, which can be degraded by certain bacterial strains like Achromobacter xylosidans M9.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON-FREE BASAL MEDIA (CFBM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon-Free Basal Media (CFBM) is a nutrient medium used in the study to culture and isolate bacteria capable of degrading NPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESCHERICHIA COLI ATCC 25922">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Escherichia coli ATCC 25922 is a bacterial strain used as a negative control in the study to compare with NPS-degrading bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS + OAT DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A mixed diet of expanded polystyrene (EPS) and oats was used to test the mealworms' ability to degrade plastics while maintaining nutritional intake.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS + PE DIET">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A mixed diet of expanded polystyrene (EPS) and polyethylene (PE) was used to assess mealworms' ability to degrade multiple types of plastics simultaneously.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LURIA-BERTANI (LB) BROTH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Luria-Bertani (LB) broth is a nutrient-rich medium used for culturing bacteria, including those isolated from mealworms' guts in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NPS-CFB AGAR MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NPS-CFB agar media is a specialized medium used to culture and isolate bacteria capable of degrading nano-polystyrene (NPS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NPS-CFB BROTH MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NPS-CFB broth media is a liquid medium used for primary screening of bacterial isolates capable of degrading nano-polystyrene (NPS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA M2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene, producing laccases and esterases, and generating acid in the presence of NPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 is a bacterial strain identified as a potential nanoplastic degrader, contributing to the reduction of polystyrene film weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 is a bacterial strain identified for its potential role in degrading plastic, specifically nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading polystyrene with a weight loss of 6% over 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworm intestines, identified for its potential role in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS STUTZERI M3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas stutzeri M3 is a bacterial strain identified as a potential nanoplastic degrader, contributing to the reduction of polystyrene film weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas stutzeri M3 is a bacterial strain isolated for its ability to degrade plastic, particularly nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas stutzeri M3 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworm intestines, suggested to contribute to polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas stutzeri M3 is a bacterial strain that shows moderate polystyrene degradation capabilities and produces laccases and catalase.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSIDANS M9">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter xylosidans M9 is a bacterial strain identified as a promising nanoplastic degrader, showing significant reduction in nanoplastic particle size and count.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of yellow mealworms, known for its ability to degrade petroleum hydrocarbons and various types of plastics, including nanoplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosidans M9 is a bacterial strain studied for its enzymatic activity and potential in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosidans M9 is a bacterial strain with a high capacity for degrading polystyrene, exhibiting significant growth and enzymatic activity in the presence of NPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosidans M9 is a specific strain of bacteria studied for its ability to biodegrade polystyrene, with research focusing on its enzymatic activity and genetic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYSTAL VIOLET (CV)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crystal violet is a staining agent used to quantify biofilm formation on polystyrene surfaces in experimental studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE (PS) FILM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene (PS) film is a material used in experiments to study weight loss and degradation by microorganisms over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYMETHYL CELLULOSE (CMC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxymethyl cellulose is a substrate used to test cellulase enzyme activity in bacterial isolates capable of degrading nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboxymethyl cellulose is a substrate used to test cellulase enzyme activity in bacterial samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TWEEN 20">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tween 20 is a substrate used to test esterase enzyme activity in bacterial isolates capable of degrading nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tween 20 is a substrate used to test esterase enzyme activity in bacterial samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TWEEN 80">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tween 80 is a substrate used to test lipase enzyme activity in bacterial isolates capable of degrading nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tween 80 is a substrate used to test lipase enzyme activity in bacterial samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CASEIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Casein is a substrate used to test protease enzyme activity in bacterial isolates capable of degrading nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Casein is a substrate used to test protease enzyme activity in bacterial samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUAIACOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Guaiacol is a substrate used to test laccase enzyme activity in bacterial isolates capable of degrading nano-polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Guaiacol is a substrate used to test laccase enzyme activity in bacterial samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROMOCRESOL PURPLE (BCP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bromocresol purple is an indicator used in acid production assays to detect the degradation of nano-polystyrene by bacterial isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M9">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">M9 is a bacterial strain studied for its biofilm formation and interaction with nano-polystyrene in experimental setups.&lt;SEP&gt;M9 is a medium used for bacterial growth and is part of the experimental setup to study the degradation of NPS by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-DEGRADING STRAINS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-degrading strains refer to bacterial isolates, such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 and Achromobacter xylosidans M9, studied for their ability to degrade plastics like nano-polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LB BROTH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LB broth is a growth medium used to culture bacteria in biofilm formation experiments involving nano-polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulases are enzymes produced by Achromobacter xylosidans M9, contributing to the degradation of complex organic compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulases are enzymes that break down cellulose, tested using carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC) as a substrate in bacterial activity assays.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOPROPANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Isopropanol is a solvent used in the extraction of nanoplastics, mixed with chloroform for separation.&lt;SEP&gt;Isopropanol is an LC-MS grade chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Isopropanol is used as part of the mobile phase in the UHPLC system for lipid analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARTICLE COUNT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Particle count analysis (PCA) is a method used to determine the number and size of nanoplastics using microscopy and image processing software.&lt;SEP&gt;Particle count analysis is a technique used to quantify the number of particles, such as microplastics, in a sample.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is used to observe the physical changes in polypropylene materials after ingestion and degradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is used to examine the surface structure and characterization of nanoplastics before and after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy is a technique used to observe microscopic changes on the surface of polystyrene films degraded by bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteria capable of degrading nanoplastics are isolated and tested for enzyme activity, such as proteases and laccases.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL DIGESTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial digestion is a process using KOH to isolate nanoplastics from biological cells without altering their chemical structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARTICLE SIZE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Particle size analysis is conducted using a Nanosizer to determine the mean diameter and size variation of nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMAGEJ SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ImageJ software is used to process high-resolution images from microscopy to analyze particle count and size variations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOSIZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Nanosizer is an instrument used to measure the size and variation of nanoplastics dispersed in distilled water.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRASONIC DISMEMBRATOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An ultrasonic dismembrator is used to break up aggregates of nanoplastics for size analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHILIPS-X LP30 SEM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Philips-X LP30 scanning electron microscope is used to examine the surface structure of nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NPS refers to nanopolystyrene, a form of polystyrene that is being degraded by bacterial strains in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;NPS refers to nanopolystyrene, a form of polystyrene that is being studied for its degradation by bacterial strains isolated from mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;NPS refers to polystyrene nanoparticles, a type of microplastic that is resistant to environmental breakdown and studied for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL STRAINS (M1-M9)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nine bacterial strains (M1-M9) were isolated from mealworms and showed the ability to degrade NPS, indicating their role in the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NPS EMULSION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NPS emulsion refers to a mixture of nanopolystyrene particles suspended in a liquid medium, used in experiments to study degradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AQUEOUS LAYER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The aqueous layer is the water-based portion of a mixture separated during the extraction process, often containing water-soluble degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANIC LAYER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The organic layer is the portion of a mixture containing organic solvents and compounds, often used to isolate degradation products of NPS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROFORM:ISOPROPANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloroform:Isopropanol is a solvent mixture used in the separation process to isolate organic compounds from aqueous solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYL ACETATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethyl acetate is an organic solvent used in GC-MS analysis to extract degradation products from residual media.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D.H2O">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">D.H2O (deionized water) is used to resuspend samples for spectrophotometric analysis, ensuring accurate measurements of degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBIDITY ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Turbidity assay is a method used to measure the cloudiness of a solution, often indicating bacterial growth or degradation activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Turbidity assay is a method used to measure the degradation of polystyrene by bacterial strains, indicating their effectiveness in breaking down the material.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LB MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LB media is a growth medium used to cultivate Achromobacter xylosidans M9 and other bacterial strains for comparison in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;LB media is a nutrient-rich growth medium used to culture bacteria, allowing for the observation of colony morphology and growth characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SP M7">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas sp M7 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworm intestines, implicated in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas sp M7 is a bacterial strain notable for its cellulase and esterase enzymes, contributing to its ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA M8">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8 is a bacterial strain identified as a potential nanoplastic degrader, contributing to the reduction of polystyrene film weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworm intestines, identified for its potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8 is a bacterial strain that produces lipases, esterases, and laccases, indicating its potential role in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESCHERICHIA COLI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Escherichia coli is a bacterial strain used as a control group in the study, showing no significant degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulase is an enzyme produced by Pseudomonas sp M7, contributing to its ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulase is an enzyme used in the digestion process to break down organic matter in mealworm intestines for residual plastic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATALASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Catalase is an enzyme produced by bacterial strains such as Pseudomonas stutzeri M3, aiding in their metabolic processes during polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Protease is an enzyme produced by bacterial strains such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2, aiding in their metabolic processes during polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL BIOFILM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial biofilm refers to the formation of bacterial communities on polystyrene surfaces, aiding in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT LOSS ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight loss assay is a method used to quantify the degradation of polystyrene by measuring the reduction in weight of PS films after exposure to bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SP. M7">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas sp. M7 is a bacterial strain identified as a potential nanoplastic degrader, contributing to the reduction of polystyrene film weight.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL BIOFILMS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial biofilms are formed on polystyrene film surfaces, supporting the biodegradation process by causing surface alterations such as cracks and holes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER AEGRIFACIENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter aegrifaciens is a bacterial strain with 62% similarity to Achromobacter xylosidans M9, identified through phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER NR113733.1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter NR113733.1 is a bacterial strain with 97% evolutionary homology to Achromobacter xylosidans M9, identified through phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER NR044925.1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter NR044925.1 is a bacterial strain with 97% evolutionary homology to Achromobacter xylosidans M9, identified through phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NPS BASAL MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NPS basal media is a growth medium used to cultivate Achromobacter xylosidans M9 and other bacterial strains for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEM (TRANSMISSION ELECTRON MICROSCOPY)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) is used to analyze the size reduction of nanoplastics after microbial degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZETA SIZER MEASUREMENTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Zeta sizer measurements are used to quantify the reduction in nanoplastic particle size after microbial degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL ACTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial action refers to the role of bacterial strains in degrading nanoplastics, leading to significant reductions in particle size and count.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPLASTIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanoplastic pollution refers to the environmental issue caused by small plastic particles, which are the focus of microbial degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2,4-DI-TERT BUTYLPHENOL (2,4-DTBP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2,4-Di-tert butylphenol is a toxic substance that accumulates in mealworm tissues and frass during polystyrene degradation, with potential cytotoxic effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEPIDOPTERA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes moths and butterflies, some of which have been investigated for their role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOU ET AL. (2021)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Lou et al. (2021) conducted research on plastic degradation, reporting differences in survival and pupation rates among mealworms fed different diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSCHATZIS ET AL. (2021)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Tschatzis et al. (2021) conducted metabolomic profiling to understand polystyrene assimilation in mealworms, identifying the accumulation of 2,4-DTBP in tissues and frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEN AND DAI (2015)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Chen and Dai (2015) explored the cytotoxic effects of 2,4-DTBP on the spider mite Tetranychus cinnabarinus, finding dose-dependent adulticidal and larvicidal impacts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRANYCHUS CINNABARINUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tetranychus cinnabarinus, the spider mite, was studied by Chen and Dai (2015) for its susceptibility to the toxic effects of 2,4-DTBP.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter is a genus of bacteria found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor, involved in the degradation of nanopolystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter is a genus of bacteria known for its potential in xenobiotics biodegradation, as revealed by whole-genome sequencing.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter is a genus of bacteria known for its robust enzymatic activity and potential role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly in the context of xenobiotic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter is a genus of bacteria, including Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9, which has been identified for its ability to degrade polystyrene and form biofilms.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEHP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate (DEHP) is a plasticizer that can be degraded by Achromobacter species through enzymatic activity, leading to the formation of less toxic intermediates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER DENITRIFICANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter denitrificans is a bacterial strain recognized for its ability to degrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE) and other environmental pollutants.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter denitrificans is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade xenobiotics, including pyrene and di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER SP. HZ01">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter sp. HZ01 is a hydrocarbon-degrading bacterium isolated from crude oil-contaminated seawater, capable of breaking down hydrocarbons.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter sp. HZ01 is a strain isolated from crude oil-contaminated seawater, capable of degrading n-alkanes and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) with high efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER SP. LH-1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter sp. LH-1 is a bacterial strain known for its ability to degrade phenanthrene (PHE) and other polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), showcasing its adaptability in eliminating hydrophobic pollutants.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter sp. LH-1 is a biodemulsifier-producing strain capable of biodegrading phenanthrene, a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER SP. RX">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter sp. RX is a bacterial strain capable of degrading di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate (DEHP) through enzymatic activity, leading to the formation of less toxic intermediates.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter sp. RX is a bacterium isolated for its ability to biodegrade di-(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate, a common plasticizer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSIDANS MT3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter xylosidans MT3 is a bacterial strain with genetic machinery for metabolizing nitro-phenolic and phenolic compounds, as well as chlorocatechols and chloromuconates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENANTHRENE (PHE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenanthrene (PHE) is a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAH) that can be degraded by Achromobacter sp. LH-1, demonstrating the strain's ability to break down hydrophobic pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DI(2-ETHYLHEXYL) PHTHALATE (DEHP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate (DEHP) is a plasticizer that can be degraded by Achromobacter sp. RX through enzymatic activity, leading to the formation of less toxic intermediates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BETA-LACTAMASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Beta-lactamases are enzymes produced by Achromobacter species, which have various ecological and industrial applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITRO-PHENOLIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitro-phenolic compounds are environmental pollutants that can be metabolized by Achromobacter xylosidans MT3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROCATECHOLS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chlorocatechols are chemical compounds that can be metabolized by Achromobacter xylosidans MT3, indicating its role in the degradation of chlorinated pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROMUCONATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloromuconates are chemical intermediates formed during the degradation of chlorinated pollutants by Achromobacter xylosidans MT3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XENOBIOTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Xenobiotic degradation refers to the breakdown of synthetic or foreign chemical compounds by microorganisms, often involving specialized enzymatic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPOLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanopolystyrene refers to polystyrene particles at the nanoscale, which degrade more rapidly than larger polystyrene films due to their increased surface area.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanopolystyrene refers to small polystyrene particles that can be degraded by microorganisms, such as those found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363</data>
