    <node id="ACINETOBACTER SEPTICUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter septicus is a bacterial strain identified from the digestive tracts of mealworms, showing autoaggregation behavior and potential involvement in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter septicus is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, showing growth on agar and cracks in PS film over 14 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter septicus is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that has been shown to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter septicus is one of the bacterial strains isolated from the gut of mealworms, potentially involved in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA GRIMONITII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella grimonitii is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading polystyrene with a weight loss of 4.35% over 60 days.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS NITROREDUCENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas nitroreducens is a bacterial strain identified in the study, showing sensitivity to salt and potential involvement in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas nitroreducens is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading polystyrene with a weight loss of 6.93% over 60 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas nitroreducens is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </node>
