      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas pleco-glossicida is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading polystyrene with a weight loss of 7.4 ± 0.4% over 60 days.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGROBACTERIUM TUMEFACIENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a bacterial strain identified in the study, potentially contributing to the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, showing growth on agar and cracks in PS film over 14 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that has been shown to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULOSIMICROBIUM SP. WJ2025">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larval intestines, capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) films, resulting in weight loss and surface erosion.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of forming cracks in PS film and biofilm formation over 20 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025 is another bacterial strain from Tenebrio molitor intestines that degrades polystyrene, increasing surface roughness and reducing hydrophobicity of PS films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASILIA SP. FS1903">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Masilia sp. FS1903 is a PS-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of the greater wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella), capable of degrading PS films with a mass loss of 12.97% over 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Masilia sp. FS1903 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading polystyrene with a weight loss of 2.76 ± 0.22% under anaerobic conditions over 15 days.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YOKENELLA REGENSBURGEI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Yokenella regensburgei is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, studied for its role in PS emulsion biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Yokenella regensburgei is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworms, potentially involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Yokenella regensburgei is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that has been shown to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SERRATIA SP. WSW">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Serratia sp. WSW is a PS-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the gut flora of P. davidis larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Serratia sp. WSW is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor, studied for its role in PS film biodegradation over 60 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Serratia sp. WSW is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestinal flora of Plesiophthalminus davidis, capable of biodegrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Serratia sp. WSW is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestinal flora of Plesiophthalminus davidis, capable of forming biofilms and degrading polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Serratia sp. WSW is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of Plesiothalamus davidis, capable of forming biofilms on polystyrene and degrading it.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER SP. ANTC-1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter sp. AnTc-1 is a PS-degrading bacteria isolated from the gut of T. castaneum larvae, associated with polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. AnTc-1 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene powder and film, studied in the gut microbiome of Tribolium castaneum.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. AnTc-1 is a bacterial strain previously reported to degrade polystyrene, reducing its molecular weight by 13-25%.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter sp. AnTc-1 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of Tribolium castaneum, showing the ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2,4-DI-TERT-BUTYLPHENOL (2,4-DTBP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2,4-Di-tert-butylphenol is a degradation product of polystyrene nanoparticles, indicating the biochemical pathway used by mealworms to decompose plastic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSIDOXANS M9">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter xylosidoxans M9 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene through a complex microbial mechanism, converting it into smaller, environmentally benign compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOME STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbiome studies investigate the role of gut microbiota in the biodegradation of plastics, particularly in organisms like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSIDOXANS OR859752">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter xylosidoxans OR859752 is a bacterial strain capable of significantly reducing the size of nanopolystyrene particles, indicating its role in polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosidoxans OR859752 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, capable of degrading nanopolystyrene and altering its morphological properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIS-2,3-EPOXYCOTANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cis-2,3-Epoxycotane is a degradation product of polystyrene, indicating the enzymatic processes involved in its breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPTADECYLHEPTAFLUOROBUTYRATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Heptadecylheptafluorobutyrate is a degradation product of polystyrene, highlighting the complexity of microbial enzymatic actions in polymer degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGENASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygenases are enzymes that introduce oxygen into the polystyrene polymer backbone, initiating its oxidative cleavage and degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RING-HYDROXYLATING DIOXYGENASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ring-hydroxylating dioxygenases are enzymes involved in the ring-opening of aromatic compounds during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RING-CLEAVING DIOXYGENASES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ring-cleaving dioxygenases are enzymes that facilitate the conversion of aromatic intermediates into aliphatic compounds during polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOME PROFILE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The transcriptome profile refers to the study of gene expression in the gut isolates of Tenebrio molitor, which is essential for understanding their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOTECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biotechnology refers to the potential applications of the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor in developing solutions for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic waste that is being investigated for recycling through dissolution and nanoprecipitation techniques.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSIDANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter xylosidans is a bacterium evaluated for its biocontrol potential against Fusarium wilt in melons and its role in degrading chloroaromatic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYTETRAFLUOROETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polytetrafluoroethylene is a fluoroplastic material used in various industrial applications, with specific fabrication and processing techniques.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS REINEKEI MT1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas reinekei MT1 is a member of the chloroaromatic-degrading microbial community, involved in the degradation of aromatic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER LIPOLYTICUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter lipolyticum is a bacterium known for producing extracellular lipase, an enzyme involved in lipid degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE NANOPARTICLES">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene nanoparticles are small plastic particles that are widely distributed in the environment and can accumulate in various organisms, causing toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP. HSH-810">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. HSH-810 is an alkalophilic bacterium that produces an alkaline cellulase enzyme, useful in industrial applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE LITTER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine litter refers to waste materials, particularly plastics, that accumulate in marine environments, posing significant ecological risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER DENITRIFICANS EBL13">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter denitrificans EBL13 is a bacterium capable of biodegrading thermooxidative pretreated low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISSOLUTION TECHNIQUE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A dissolution technique combined with nanoprecipitation is being investigated for recycling waste expanded polystyrene, offering a potential solution for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTICS IN SEAFOOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A benchmark protocol for the extraction and characterization of microplastics in seafood has been developed to address environmental pollution concerns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUSARIUM WILT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fusarium wilt is a plant disease caused by the fungus Fusarium, which affects melons and other crops.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROAROMATIC-DEGRADING MT COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A microbial community involved in the degradation of chloroaromatic compounds, including Pseudomonas reinekei MT1 and Achromobacter xylosidans MT3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENANTHRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenanthrene is a chemical that affects the mortality, growth, and antioxidant systems of earthworms (Eisenia fetida) under laboratory conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenanthrene is a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAH) that is toxic and persistent in the environment, often targeted for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKALINE CELLULASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkaline cellulase is an enzyme produced by Bacillus sp. HSH-810, used in industrial applications for breaking down cellulose.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE LITTER CRISIS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine litter, particularly plastics, is considered a global environmental crisis due to its widespread impact on marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEMULSIFIER-PRODUCING STRAIN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter sp. LH-1 is a biodemulsifier-producing strain capable of biodegrading phenanthrene, a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAEROBIC CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Anaerobic conditions are a focus of this study, as they reveal the potential of gut microbes to degrade polystyrene in oxygen-deprived environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions are maintained in the chamber to cultivate PS-degrading bacteria, which thrive in low-oxygen environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions are studied for their role in the biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics by bacteria like Enterobacter hormaechei.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions are used in the study to simulate environments where polystyrene degradation by bacteria can occur without oxygen.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions refer to an oxygen-free environment used in the study to assess the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions refer to environments without oxygen, under which Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 can degrade polystyrene, a novel finding in plastic biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions refer to environments without oxygen, where certain bacteria can degrade polystyrene using alternative electron acceptors like nitrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions refer to environments without oxygen, where certain bacteria can still degrade pollutants like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions refer to environments without oxygen, where certain bacteria, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, can degrade polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic conditions refer to environments without oxygen, where certain bacteria, such as strain LG3, can degrade polystyrene effectively.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOOXIDATIVE PRETREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermooxidative pretreatment is a process used to enhance the biodegradation of plastics like LDPE and PVC by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUDE OIL-CONTAMINATED SEAWATER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Crude oil-contaminated seawater is an environmental pollutant that can be remediated using hydrocarbon-degrading bacteria like Achromobacter sp. HZ01.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUSARIUM WILT OF MELON">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fusarium wilt of melon is a plant disease caused by the fungus Fusarium, which can be controlled using biocontrol agents like Achromobacter xylosidans.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROAROMATIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloroaromatic compounds are toxic pollutants that can be degraded by microbial communities like the MT community, including Pseudomonas reinekei MT1 and Achromobacter xylosidans MT3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENANTHRENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenanthrene biodegradation is the process by which polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons like phenanthrene are broken down by microorganisms such as Achromobacter sp. LH-1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GALLERIA MELLONELLA GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut bacteria in Galleria mellonella larvae play a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, making them a focus of research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </node>
