      <data key="d2">chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826</data>
      <data key="d1">Laccase biocatalysts from Pseudomonas species are suitable for degrading synthetic textile dyes.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXTA TENEBRIONIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixta tenebrionis BIT-26 is a species of the genus Mixta, identified through phylogenetic analysis of 16S rRNA and protein sequences, and is closely related to other Mixta species.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta tenebrionis is a Gram-stain-negative, facultatively anaerobic bacterium isolated from the gut of the plastic-eating mealworm Tenebrio molitor. It is motile, forms yellow colonies on LB agar, and has a DNA G+C content of 56.08%.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta tenebrionis is a novel species of the genus Mixta, proposed based on phylogenetic, phenotypic, and chemotaxonomic analyses of strain BIT-26T.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta tenebrionis sp. nov. is a novel bacterial strain isolated from the gut of plastic-eating mealworms, belonging to the genus Mixta within the family Erwiniaeae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIT-26">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">BIT-26 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of plastic-eating mealworms, identified as a novel species within the genus Mixta, and proposed as Mixta tenebrionis sp. nov.&lt;SEP&gt;Strain BIT-26 is a novel species of the genus Mixta, characterized through a taxonomic study using a polyphasic approach, and is associated with mealworms from Beijing, PR China.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ERWINIA PIRIFORMIGRANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Erwinia piriformigrans is a bacterial species with which strain BIT-26 shows high 16S rRNA gene sequence similarity (97.4%).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITROBACTER SEDLAKII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrobacter sedlakii is a bacterial species with which strain BIT-26 shows high 16S rRNA gene sequence similarity (97.3%).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXTA CALIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixta calida is a bacterial species within the genus Mixta, with which strain BIT-26 shows high 16S rRNA gene sequence similarity (97.3%).&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta calida is a closely related species to BIT-26T, with a genome similarity of 29.1%.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta calida is a closely related species to strain BIT-26T, used for comparative phenotypic and chemotaxonomic analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta calida is a species of the genus Mixta, closely related to Mixta tenebrionis BIT-26, as identified through phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRONOBACTER MUYTJENSII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cronobacter muytjensii is a bacterial species with which strain BIT-26 shows high 16S rRNA gene sequence similarity (97.2%).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXTA THEICOLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixta theicola is a bacterial species within the genus Mixta, with which strain BIT-26 shows high 16S rRNA gene sequence similarity (97.2%).&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta theicola is a closely related species to BIT-26T, with a genome similarity of 26.6%.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta theicola is a closely related species to strain BIT-26T, used for comparative phenotypic and chemotaxonomic analyses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixta is a genus of bacteria to which strain BIT-26T belongs, based on phylogenetic and chemotaxonomic analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta is a genus within the family Erwiniaeae, containing species such as Mixta calida, Mixta gaviniae, Mixta intestinalis, and Mixta theicola, and now includes the novel species Mixta tenebrionis.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta is a genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria isolated from the gut of mealworms, involved in nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ERWINIEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Erwiniaeae is a family within the class Gammaproteobacteria, containing 14 genera, including Mixta, to which strain BIT-26 belongs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOUSEKEEPING GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Housekeeping genes, such as atpD, gyrB, infB, and rpoB, are used in phylogenetic analyses to classify bacterial strains like BIT-26.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA-DNA HYBRIDIZATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">DNA-DNA hybridization is a method used to determine genetic similarity between bacterial strains, such as BIT-26 and other Mixta species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AVERAGE NUCLEOTIDE IDENTITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Average nucleotide identity (ANI) is a genomic metric used to differentiate bacterial species, such as BIT-26 from other Mixta species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAXONOMIC STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A taxonomic study involves the classification and characterization of organisms, such as the polyphasic approach used to identify strain BIT-26 as Mixta tenebrionis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPHASIC APPROACH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A polyphasic approach combines multiple methods, such as phylogenetic, chemotaxonomic, and physiological analyses, to classify bacterial strains like BIT-26.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATP D, GYR B, INF B, RPO B GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">These genes are used in concatenated protein sequence analysis to classify bacterial strains like BIT-26 and determine their phylogenetic relationships.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXTA GAVINIAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixta gaviniae is a closely related species to BIT-26T, with a genome similarity of 29.2%.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta gaviniae is a closely related species to strain BIT-26T, used for comparative phenotypic and chemotaxonomic analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta gaviniae is a species of the genus Mixta, closely related to Mixta tenebrionis BIT-26, as identified through phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXTA INTESTINALIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixta intestinalis is a closely related species to strain BIT-26T, used for comparative phenotypic and chemotaxonomic analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Mixta intestinalis is a species of the genus Mixta, closely related to Mixta tenebrionis BIT-26, as identified through phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff28716c2f25b8550595e1f473add0c4</data>
    </node>
