      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The GGDC was used to perform in silico DDH tests to compare the genome similarity of BIT-26T with related species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANI CALCULATOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The ANI calculator was used to determine the Average Nucleotide Identity (ANI) between BIT-26T and related species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM (SU8010, HITACHI)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) was used to observe the cell morphology and flagella of BIT-26T.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEM (JEM-1400, JEOL)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM) was used to observe the cell morphology and flagella of BIT-26T.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN BIT-26T">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain BIT-26T is a Gram-stain-negative, facultatively anaerobic, rod-shaped bacterium isolated from the mealworm Tenebrio. It exhibits unique phenotypic and chemotaxonomic characteristics, distinguishing it from other species in the genus Mixta.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio, commonly known as mealworms, are insects studied for their ability to degrade plastics, including polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio, commonly known as mealworms, is the host organism from which strain BIT-26T was isolated.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LB AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LB agar is a growth medium used for cultivating bacteria, including strain BIT-26T, in laboratory settings.&lt;SEP&gt;LB agar is a growth medium used for culturing and isolating bacterial strains from the gut of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAM-STAIN-NEGATIVE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gram-stain-negative refers to a classification of bacteria that do not retain the crystal violet stain used in the Gram staining method, indicating a specific cell wall structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Gram-stain-negative refers to a classification of bacteria, like Mixta tenebrionis, based on their cell wall structure and staining properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FACULTATIVELY ANAEROBIC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Facultatively anaerobic describes organisms that can survive in both aerobic and anaerobic conditions, such as strain BIT-26T.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PERITRICHOUS FLAGELLA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peritrichous flagella are flagella distributed over the entire surface of a bacterial cell, enabling motility, as observed in strain BIT-26T.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="API 50CH SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The API 50CH system is a biochemical test kit used to identify bacteria based on their ability to metabolize carbohydrates, as used for strain BIT-26T.&lt;SEP&gt;The API 50CH system is a biochemical test used to identify bacterial species, such as Mixta tenebrionis, based on their metabolic activities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="API 20E SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The API 20E system is a biochemical test kit used for the identification of Gram-negative bacteria, as applied to strain BIT-26T.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATALASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Catalase activity refers to the ability of an organism to break down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen, a characteristic tested in strain BIT-26T.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidase activity refers to the presence of cytochrome c oxidase, an enzyme tested in strain BIT-26T using tetramethyl-p-phenylenediamine.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHOLE-CELL FATTY ACID COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Whole-cell fatty acid composition is a chemotaxonomic method used to characterize and differentiate bacterial strains, such as strain BIT-26T.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-EATING MEALWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The plastic-eating mealworm refers to Tenebrio molitor, which has the ability to consume and degrade plastics, including polystyrene, due to its gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEIJING INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Beijing Institute of Technology contributed to the research on Mixta tenebrionis, providing funding and resources for studying its role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHINA ASSOCIATION OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The China Association of Science and Technology supported the research on Mixta tenebrionis through the Young Elite Scientist Sponsorship Program, highlighting its importance in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSMISSION ELECTRON MICROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transmission electron microscopy was used to observe the flagella of Mixta tenebrionis, aiding in the characterization of this bacterium.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANTOSEA INTESTINALIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pantoaea intestinalis is a bacterial species isolated from the human gut, contributing to the study of gut microbiota and its interactions with the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA SP. WJ2020">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella sp. WJ2020 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor intestines that can degrade polystyrene, causing weight loss and molecular weight reduction in PS films.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella sp. WJ2020 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larval intestines, capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) films, resulting in weight loss and surface erosion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULOSIMICROBIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulosimicrobium is a genus of bacteria, including Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025, which has been isolated from Tenebrio molitor intestines and is capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAMELLAR ETCHING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lamellar etching refers to the surface changes observed on polystyrene films after microbial degradation, indicating the breakdown of the material.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradation technology refers to the use of microorganisms, such as those found in Tenebrio molitor, to degrade plastic waste like polystyrene in an environmentally friendly manner.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LANDFILL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Landfill is a traditional disposal method for plastic waste, including polystyrene, but it contributes to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INCINERATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Incineration is a method of disposing of plastic waste, including polystyrene, but it releases harmful pollutants into the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASSILIA SP. PS1903">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Massilia sp. PS1903 is a PS-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of Galleria mellonella larvae, capable of degrading PS by nearly 13% in 30 days.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINERAL SALT MEDIUM (MSM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mineral salt medium (MSM) is a nutrient solution used to culture and study the growth of PS-degrading bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLESIOPHTHALMINUS DAVIDIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plesiophthalminus davidis is an organism from whose intestinal flora Serratia sp. WSW was isolated, contributing to polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SERRINE HYDROLASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Serine hydrolase is an enzyme whose gene expression increases during polystyrene degradation by Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANHYDROUS METHANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Anhydrous methanol is used to precipitate polystyrene from dichloromethane solutions during film preparation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LB AGAR PLATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">LB agar plates are used to culture and isolate bacterial colonies for further study in biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIANAMP BACTERIA DNA KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The TIANamp Bacteria DNA Kit is used to extract genomic DNA from bacterial colonies for identification purposes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WJ2020">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">WJ2020 is a bacterial strain identified as a potential polystyrene (PS)-degrading organism, belonging to the genus Klebsiella.&lt;SEP&gt;WJ2020 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of yellow mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene films and causing weight loss and molecular weight changes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WJ2025">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">WJ2025 is a bacterial strain identified as a potential polystyrene (PS)-degrading organism, with distinct morphological and genetic characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;WJ2025 is another bacterial strain isolated from the gut of yellow mealworms, showing significant degradation effects on polystyrene films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSM AGAR PLATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MSM agar plates are used as a medium for culturing and observing the growth of bacterial strains capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENOMIC DNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Genomic DNA is extracted from bacterial colonies for identification and phylogenetic analysis of PS-degrading strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY DISPERSIVE SPECTROSCOPY (EDS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">EDS is used to analyze the elemental composition of the PS film's surface during degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMO FISHER ESCALAB XI+ XPS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermo Fisher ESCALAB Xi+ XPS is an instrument used for X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy to analyze the elemental distribution on the PS film's surface.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KRUSS DSA100 WCA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">KRUSS DSA100 WCA is an instrument used to measure the water contact angle on the PS film's surface to assess hydrophilicity changes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA AEROGENES WJ2020">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella aerogenes WJ2020 is a bacterial strain identified as capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) and forming colonies on PS film surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULOSIMICROBIUM FUNKEI WJ2025">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellulosimicrobium funkei WJ2025 is a bacterial strain identified as capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) and forming colonies on PS film surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WCA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">WCA (Water Contact Angle) analysis was used to confirm the oxidation and biodegradation of polypropylene by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Water Contact Angle (WCA) measurements are used to assess changes in the hydrophobicity of polystyrene (PS) films after biodegradation by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Minimal Salt Medium (MSM) is a growth medium used to culture bacterial strains and study their ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XPS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">XPS analysis is employed to confirm the increase in oxygen content on the surface of polystyrene films, providing evidence of oxidation during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BARLEY WORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Barley worms are another type of larvae that can feed on expanded polystyrene, similar to yellow mealworms, and contribute to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTAGONISTIC EFFECT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An antagonistic effect refers to the reduced degradation efficiency observed when mixed bacterial strains (WJ2020 and WJ2025) are used, possibly due to competition or inhibition between the strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON-OXYGEN BONDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon-oxygen bonds (C-O) are formed on the surface of polystyrene films during biodegradation, providing evidence of oxidation and chemical degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHENYANG AGRICULTURAL UNIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Shenyang Agricultural University is an institution involved in the research study on polystyrene (PS) degradation, supported by its Talent Program.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RALSTONIA SP. STRAIN SKM2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ralstonia sp. strain SKM2 is a bacterium isolated from a landfill site, capable of degrading polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP. STRAIN SM1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. strain SM1 is a bacterium isolated from a landfill site, capable of degrading polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE PLASTIC MULCH">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradable plastic mulch is proposed as a solution to reduce plastic pollution in agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROWAVE-ACTIVATED PERSULFATE SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A microwave-activated persulfate system is used for the remediation of atrazine-contaminated soil.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE PLASTIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine plastic pollution refers to the accumulation of plastic waste in marine environments, posing threats to marine life and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEAFOOD SAFETY">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Seafood safety is impacted by marine plastic pollution, as microplastics can enter the food chain and affect human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLESIOPHTHALUS DAVIDIS LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plesiophthalus davidis larvae are organisms studied for their potential role in biodegradation processes, particularly in relation to environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(LACTIC ACID)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(lactic acid) is a biodegradable polymer that can be degraded by certain bacteria at ambient temperatures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO-POLYETHYLENE PARTICLES">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro-polyethylene particles are small plastic fragments that can be biodegraded by bacterial colonization in mixed microbial consortia.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS AND WAX MOTH LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms and wax moth larvae are macro-organisms studied for their potential to biodegrade polyethylene and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERTHERMOPHILIC COMPOSTING TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyperthermophilic composting technology is a method used to enhance the biodegradation of microplastics in sewage sludge.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXED MICROBIAL CONSORTIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A mixed microbial consortium isolated from a landfill site is capable of biodegrading micro-polyethylene particles through bacterial colonization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION OF MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The biodegradation of microplastics involves the breakdown of small plastic particles by microorganisms or macro-organisms, such as mealworms and wax moth larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUCISPIRILLUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mucispirillum is a genus of bacteria in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant association with the PE + bran diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Mucispirillum is a genus of bacteria that shows a significant response in the gut microbiome of mealworms when fed a diet containing LDPE and bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Mucispirillum is another bacterium significantly associated with the PE + bran diet in mealworms, suggesting its involvement in plastic degradation and gut microbiome dynamics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIET SHIFT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Diet shift refers to the change in the mealworm's diet from bran to a mixture of bran and plastics, which affects the composition of the gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATION PROCESS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The oxidation process is a key step in the biodegradation of plastics, indicated by the formation of -C2O- and -C-O- groups in the mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIRCULAR REACTOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A circular reactor is a proposed system for continuous plastic biodegradation using mealworms and their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESSER WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lesser waxworms (Achroia grisea) are larvae capable of ingesting and degrading polyethylene (PE), with their survival and biodegradation efficiency enhanced by additional nutrition.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesser waxworms (Achroia grisella larvae) have been studied for their potential in plastic degradation, particularly polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesser waxworms (Achroia grisella) are larvae capable of consuming and degrading polyethylene (PE) films, and their growth and survivability are studied in relation to PE consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesser waxworms (Achroia grisella) are larvae capable of consuming and digesting polyethylene (PE), potentially due to the presence of PE-degrading gut bacteria or unique enzymes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesser waxworms are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene (PE) and incorporating it into their biomass, playing a role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesser waxworms are larvae that can consume and degrade polyethylene (PE) films, and their survivability and growth are studied in relation to PE degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesser waxworms (Achroia grisella) are larvae known for their ability to biodegrade HDPE, a type of plastic, through chemical modifications in their gut.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDIAN MEALMOTHS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Indian mealmoths (Plodia interpunctella) are known to degrade plastics, including polyethylene, through their gut microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus larvae) are capable of degrading plastics, including polystyrene and polyethylene, through their gut microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae are another type of macroinvertebrate with plastic-degrading capacities, similar to mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZOPHOBAS ATRATUS LARVA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Superworms (Zophobas atratus larvae) are known to degrade plastics, including polystyrene and polyethylene, through their gut microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GALLERIA MELLONELLA LARVA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Greater waxworms (Galleria mellonella larvae) are capable of degrading polyethylene and other plastics, with gut microbes playing a significant role.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROIA GRISELLA LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lesser waxworms (Achroia grisella larvae) have been studied for their potential in plastic degradation, particularly polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO-DIETS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Co-diets involve feeding larvae a combination of plastics and supplementary nutrients, such as bran, to study their effects on plastic degradation and larval growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIETARY SHIFT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Dietary shift refers to changes in the diet of larvae, such as from bran to plastic, to study the effects on gut microbiome and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH TEMPERATURE GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (HT-GPC)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">High temperature gel permeation chromatography (HT-GPC) is used to quantify the molecular weight of residual PE polymers in frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC (TG) ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A thermogravimetric (TG) analyzer is used to study the thermal degradation of materials, including plastics, in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;A thermogravimetric analyzer is used to analyze the thermal properties of frass from mealworms fed plastics, providing insights into the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL AND WEIGHT OF MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The survival and weight of mealworms are measured to assess their health and growth under different diets, including plastics and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD-GRADE POLYPROPYLENE CONTAINERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Food-grade polypropylene containers are used to house mealworms during the experiment, ensuring a controlled environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FT-IR SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An FT-IR spectrometer is used to detect functional groups in polymers from frass and plastics, aiding in the study of plastic degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2,4-TCB">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (1,2,4-TCB) is a solvent used for the extraction of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (1,2,4-TCB) is used as an eluent in gel permeation chromatography (GPC) to analyze the molecular weight of polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POTASSIUM BROMIDE (KBR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Potassium bromide (KBr) is a chemical compound used in FTIR spectroscopy to prepare homogeneous powder samples for scanning.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium bromide (KBr) is used in FT-IR spectroscopy to prepare samples for detecting functional groups in polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium bromide (KBr) is a chemical compound used in Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) to prepare samples for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STERILE SALT WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A solution used for rinsing bacterial cells to remove residual medium before re-suspension.&lt;SEP&gt;Sterile salt water (0.85% w/v) is used to rinse mealworms after sterilization to prepare them for gut microbiome analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FASTDNA SPIN KIT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The FastDNA SPIN Kit is used to extract genomic DNA from the gut microorganisms of mealworms for microbiome analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ION S5 XL SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Ion S5 XL System is used for sequencing the 16S rRNA gene amplicons to study the gut microbiome of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UCHIME ALGORITHM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The UCHIME algorithm is used to detect and remove chimeric sequences in gut microbiome analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SILVA DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The SILVA database is used for taxonomic identification of microbial sequences in the gut microbiome of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QIIME SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Qiime software is used to calculate alpha and beta diversity indices in gut microbiome analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ONE-WAY ANOVA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">One-way ANOVA is a statistical method used to compare parameters across multiple groups in the study of mealworm gut microbiome and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUKEY AND TAMHANE’S T2 TESTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Tukey and Tamhane’s T2 tests are post-hoc statistical tests used for multiple comparisons in the study of mealworm gut microbiome and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPATION RATES (PRS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pupation rates (PRs) are measured to evaluate the developmental success of mealworms fed different diets, including plastics and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS (TG)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermogravimetric analysis (TG) is used to measure changes in the mass of plastic samples and frass, indicating the extent of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BETA DIVERSITY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Beta diversity analysis is used to study the differences in gut microbiome composition among mealworms fed different diets, highlighting the role of gut bacteria in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRINCIPAL COORDINATES ANALYSIS (PCOA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PCoA is a statistical method used to visualize the differences in gut microbiome composition based on weighted UniFrac distances.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Coordinates Analysis (PCoA) is a statistical method used to visualize differences in microbial community composition, such as in the guts of insect larvae after polystyrene feeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NONMETRIC MULTIDIMENSIONAL SCALING (NMDS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">NMDS is another statistical method used to analyze and visualize the differences in gut microbiome composition among mealworms fed different diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEATMAP ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Heatmap analysis is used to visualize the relative abundance of predominant microbial populations in the gut of mealworms, showing the impact of diet on gut microbiome composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIVE ABUNDANCE OF PREDOMINANT PHYLA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This metric quantifies the dominant microbial phyla in the gut of mealworms, showing how diet influences gut microbiome composition and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIVE ABUNDANCE OF PREDOMINANT GENERA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This metric quantifies the dominant microbial genera in the gut of mealworms, providing insights into the specific bacteria involved in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FT-IR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FT-IR (Fourier-Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) analysis identifies changes in functional groups in the frass of mealworms, indicating the incorporation of oxygen during plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TG ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TG (Thermogravimetric) analysis provides evidence of new compounds formed in the frass of mealworms fed plastics, showing differences in decomposition temperatures compared to original plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHILICITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrophilicity refers to the increased affinity for water observed in the frass of mealworms fed plastics, indicating chemical changes during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C=O STRETCH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">C=O stretch is a functional group identified in the frass of mealworms fed plastics, indicating the incorporation of oxygen during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-O STRETCH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">C-O stretch is another functional group observed in the frass of mealworms fed plastics, further confirming chemical changes during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIVE ABUNDANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Relative abundance refers to the proportion of specific bacterial populations in the mealworm gut microbiome, which varies with different diets, including plastics and bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEDIOCCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pediococcus is a genus of lactic acid bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, contributing to the adjustment and maintenance of gut health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILUMATOBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ilumatobacter is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PAENARTHROBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Paenarthrobacter is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATOPOSTIPES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Atopostipes is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DELFIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Delfia is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLASTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Blastococcus is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEGIONELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Legionella is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROCABACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Procabacter is a genus of bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiome, showing significant differences in abundance between feeding groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALTERNATING FEEDING PATTERN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The alternating feeding pattern is a method used in research to study how different diets, such as bran and plastics, shape the gut microbiome of mealworms and influence plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The alternating feeding pattern is a research study approach used to explore how different diets (bran, PS, PE, PS + bran, PE + bran) shape the mealworm gut microbiome and its plastic degradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDIVIDUAL VARIATIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Individual variations refer to differences in plastic biodegradation efficiency among mealworms, suggesting that the process may be influenced by unique gut microbiome compositions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STOCHASTIC PROCESSES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stochastic processes are random factors that influence the succession of the gut microbiome in mealworms during plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EFFICIENT PLASTIC-BIODEGRADING REACTOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An efficient plastic-biodegrading reactor is a system being developed to utilize mealworms and their gut microbes for large-scale plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRIMARY MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Primary microplastics are small plastic particles that directly enter the environment, contributing to marine pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine diversity refers to the variety of life forms in marine ecosystems, which is threatened by plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPIROPLASMAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Spiroplasmas are infectious agents that affect plants, arthropods, and vertebrates, studied for their role in microbial ecology and disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CURRENT BIOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Current Biology is a scientific journal that publishes research on biological topics, including the biodegradation of plastics by caterpillars like Galleria mellonella.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IUCN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) conducts research on environmental issues, including the sources and impacts of primary microplastics in oceans.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UCHIME">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">UCHIME is a bioinformatics tool used for chimera detection in microbial sequencing data, relevant to studies on biodegradation and gut microbiomes.&lt;SEP&gt;UCHIME is a tool used to remove chimeric sequences from 16S rRNA gene data during microbial community analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEMS MICROBIOLOGY REVIEWS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FEMS Microbiology Reviews is a journal that publishes reviews on microbial topics, including the gut microbiota of insects and their role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCIENCE ADVANCES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Science Advances is a journal that publishes research on a wide range of scientific topics, including the production, use, and fate of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-PERMEABILITY PLASTIC BAGS">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-permeability plastic bags are used to prevent insect-related losses of stored food, such as maize, in rural on-farm stores.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROCARBON DEGRADERS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrocarbon degraders are bacteria capable of breaking down hydrocarbons, often found in petroleum-contaminated environments such as the Kuwait desert.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DELFTIA TSURUHATENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Delftia tsuruhatensis is a bacterium capable of assimilating terephthalate, a component of certain plastics, and is isolated from activated sludge.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROLYZED POLYACRYLAMIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrolyzed polyacrylamide is a synthetic polymer that can be biodegraded by microorganisms in combined biosystems like expanded granular sludge bed reactors.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED GRANULAR SLUDGE BED REACTOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An expanded granular sludge bed reactor is a system used in biodegradation studies, particularly for hydrolyzed polyacrylamide.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROBIC BIOFILM REACTOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An aerobic biofilm reactor is a system used in biodegradation studies, often in combination with other reactors to degrade complex polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL APPROACH">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The microbial approach involves using microorganisms to degrade plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, as a sustainable waste management solution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AQUATIC FOOD WEB">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The aquatic food web includes primary producers, consumers, and higher trophic levels, all of which can be affected by microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRIMARY PRODUCERS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Primary producers are organisms like algae and plants that form the base of the aquatic food web and can be affected by microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Humans are at the top of the aquatic food web and can be indirectly affected by microplastics through bioaccumulation and biomagnification.&lt;SEP&gt;Humans are indirectly exposed to plastics through consumption of contaminated food, leading to chronic exposure and potential health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE + BRAN DIET">
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus is significantly associated with the PE + bran diet, indicating its potential role in plastic degradation</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM-CONTAMINATED ENVIRONMENTS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrocarbon degradation is studied in the context of petroleum-contaminated environments, where microorganisms break down hydrocarbons.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM-CONTAMINATED ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrocarbon degraders are bacteria that break down hydrocarbons in petroleum-contaminated environments, such as the Kuwait desert</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEREPHTHALATE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
      <data key="d1">Delftia tsuruhatensis is a bacterium capable of assimilating terephthalate, a component of certain plastics</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS BREEDERS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms breeders are individuals or entities involved in the cultivation of Tenebrio molitor for economic purposes, such as protein production or waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE FOAM WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene foam waste refers to discarded expanded or extruded polystyrene materials that contribute to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS CULTIVATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms cultivation refers to the farming of Tenebrio molitor for purposes such as protein production, waste management, or research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC DEGRADING MICROBES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-degrading microbes are microorganisms that can break down plastic materials, offering potential solutions for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM WASTE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam waste degradation refers to the process of breaking down polystyrene foam waste, often through biological means such as mealworms or microbes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STUDY DESIGN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The study design involves feeding mealworms with EPS and XPS foams to assess their survival, growth, development, and fecundity, compared to a standard diet of fermented cassava yeasts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FERMENTED CASSAVA YEASTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fermented cassava yeasts are used as a standard diet for mealworms in this study, serving as a control to compare the effects of EPS and XPS foams on their growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORMS FARM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The mealworms farm in Bandar Lampung, Lampung, Indonesia, is the source of the Tenebrio molitor larvae used in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT BOXES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Electronic equipment boxes made of EPS foam are collected as waste from electronics stores and used in the study to investigate their biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD-TRAYS SHOP">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Food-trays made of XPS foam are purchased from a shop in Bandar Lampung and used in the study to assess their biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSTAR 4-5 LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Instar 4-5 larvae of Tenebrio molitor are used in the study to evaluate the effects of EPS and XPS foams on their survival, growth, and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PREPUPAL PERIOD">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The prepupal period is a non-feeding stage in the development of mealworms, measured in this study to assess the impact of EPS and XPS foams on their growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PUPATION PERIOD">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The pupation period is the stage when mealworms transform into pupae, measured in this study to evaluate the effects of EPS and XPS foams on their development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMAGO WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Imago weight refers to the weight of adult beetles emerging from pupae, measured in this study to determine the impact of EPS and XPS foams on the fecundity of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FECUNDITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fecundity refers to the reproductive capacity of mealworms, measured by the number of eggs laid by females after being fed EPS and XPS foams.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHI-SQUARE TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Chi-square test is used in this study to analyze the survival rate of mealworms fed with EPS and XPS foams compared to a standard diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANOVA AND LSD TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ANOVA and LSD tests are used to compare the mean values of growth and development parameters of mealworms fed with EPS and XPS foams.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XPS FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Extruded polystyrene foam (XPS foam) is another type of PS foam that provides lower productivity measures compared to EPS foam when fed to mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;XPS foam, another type of polystyrene foam, is fed to mealworms, resulting in lower growth and reproductive performance compared to standard diets.&lt;SEP&gt;XPS foam is a high-density polystyrene material used in products like coffee cups, which mealworms can also consume and degrade.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAHNELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rahnella is a genus of bacteria found in the gut of mealworms, contributing to their gut microbiome and digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRONOBACTER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cronobacter is a Gram-negative bacterium known to form biofilms on plastic surfaces and is part of the Enterobacteriaceae family, which includes species capable of degrading various plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter is a bacterial genus found to be significantly more abundant in polystyrene (PS) samples, potentially associated with polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter is a bacterial genus identified in the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus, potentially involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter is a genus of Gram-negative bacteria that was found to be over-represented in polystyrene (PS) samples, suggesting its involvement in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter is a genus of bacteria identified in the gut of mealworms, involved in their digestive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cronobacter is a genus of bacteria that appeared to be overrepresented in PS samples, indicating its potential involvement in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPOCREALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypocreales is an order of fungi isolated from the gut of Tenebrionidae beetles, including mealworms, contributing to their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XYLARIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Xylariales is an order of fungi found in the gut of Tenebrionidae beetles, including mealworms, playing a role in their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAPNODIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Capnodiales is an order of fungi identified in the gut of Tenebrionidae beetles, including mealworms, contributing to their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOTRYOSPHAERIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Botryosphaeriales is an order of fungi present in the gut of Tenebrionidae beetles, including mealworms, aiding in their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLEOSPORALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pleosporales is an order of fungi found in the gut of Tenebrionidae beetles, including mealworms, contributing to their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SACCHAROMYCETALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Saccharomycetales is an order of fungi identified in the gut of Tenebrionidae beetles, including mealworms, playing a role in their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHAETOTHYRIALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Chaetothyriales is an order of fungi present in the gut of Tenebrionidae beetles, including mealworms, aiding in their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FORMALDEHYDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Formaldehyde is a chemical found in XPS foam that is known to reduce microbial growth and may contribute to low productivity in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHIZOPUS ORYZAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhizopus oryzae is a soil fungus that can colonize polystyrene film surfaces and contribute to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HFCS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs) are chemicals found in some foam products that can reduce body weight and cause liver lesions in laboratory mammals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CFCS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) are chemicals that were historically used in foam products and are known for their environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HCFCS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrochlorofluorocarbons (HCFCs) are chemicals used in some foam products and are known for their environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STAPHYLOCOCCUS AUREUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Staphylococcus aureus is a soil bacterium that has been shown to degrade various types of plastic waste, including PS foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STREPTOCOCCUS PYOGENES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Streptococcus pyogenes is a soil bacterium that has been shown to degrade various types of plastic waste, including PS foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS TERREUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus terreus is a soil fungus capable of colonizing polystyrene film surfaces and contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YEASTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Yeasts are microorganisms used as a standard diet for mealworms and are compared to PS foam in terms of productivity effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREEN MANUFACTURING INITIATIVE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Green Manufacturing Initiative is a research effort focused on sustainable manufacturing practices, including polystyrene recycling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JOURNAL OF INSECT PHYSIOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Journal of Insect Physiology publishes research on the physiological processes of insects, including studies on Tenebrio molitor and its gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOLOGY LETTERS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Ecology Letters is a journal that publishes research on ecological topics, including the biology of endosymbiotic bacteria and fungi in insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASTM INTERNATIONAL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ASTM International provides product standards, including guidance on specifying polystyrene for various applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUSTAINABLE FARMING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Sustainable farming research explores the use of organisms like Tenebrio molitor for food and feed production, as well as waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOS ANGELES COUNTY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Los Angeles County conducted a study on the use and disposal of expanded polystyrene food containers, highlighting environmental concerns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JOURNAL OF ENTOMOLOGICAL SCIENCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Journal of Entomological Science publishes research on insects, including studies on the reproduction and development of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JOURNAL OF SERICULTURAL ENTOMOLOGICAL SCIENCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This journal publishes research on sericulture and entomology, including studies on the life cycle and fecundity of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JOURNAL OF INSECT SCIENCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Journal of Insect Science publishes research on insect biology, including studies on microbial inhibitors in insect diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF MICROBIOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This journal publishes research on microbiology, including studies on fungi and bacteria associated with the guts of wood-feeding beetles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IPB SCIENTIFIC REPOSITORY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The IPB Scientific Repository contains research on topics such as the growth and feed conversion of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF PLANT, ANIMAL, AND ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This journal publishes research on plant, animal, and environmental sciences, including studies on the life cycle of Tribolium castaneum.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXFORD UNIVERSITY PRESS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxford University Press publishes research on insect-fungal associations, including the role of yeasts as insect endosymbionts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WORLD HEALTH ORGANIZATION (WHO)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The World Health Organization publishes monographs and assessments on carcinogenic risks and chemical safety, including evaluations of environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2,2-DICHLORO-1,1,1-TRIFLUOROETHANE (HCFC-123)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2,2-dichloro-1,1,1-trifluoroethane (HCFC-123) is a chemical compound assessed by the World Health Organization for its potential environmental and health impacts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HCFC-123">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">HCFC-123 (2,2-dichloro-1,1,1-trifluoroethane) is an environmental pollutant assessed for its potential impacts on health and the environment by the World Health Organization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WORLD HEALTH ORGANIZATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The World Health Organization (WHO) conducted a study on HCFC-123, publishing a Concise International Chemical Assessment Document to evaluate its environmental and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDOSYMBIOTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
      <data key="d1">Ecology Letters publishes research on the biology of endosymbiotic bacteria and fungi in insects, including Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL INHIBITORS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
      <data key="d1">The Journal of Insect Science publishes research on microbial inhibitors in insect diets, including those affecting Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT-FUNGAL ASSOCIATIONS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxford University Press publishes research on insect-fungal associations, including the role of yeasts in insect digestion.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN ALTERATIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein alterations refer to changes in protein content and structure, observed in mealworms fed with polystyrene, indicating stress and metabolic changes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CERAMIDES AND CARDIOLIPINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ceramides and cardiolipins are biomarkers of apoptosis, found in higher amounts in mealworms fed with polystyrene, indicating increased cellular stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMO-GRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermo-gravimetric analysis is a method used to detect thermal changes in residual polystyrene (PS) polymer in mealworm frass, providing insights into the degradation process of PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermo-gravimetric analysis is a technique used to confirm the metabolism of polystyrene by mealworms, revealing changes in molecular weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermo-gravimetric analysis is a technique used to measure changes in the molecular mass of polystyrene polymers during biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NH4+">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NH4+ (ammonium) is a cation measured in the frass of mealworms, indicating biochemical changes during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POTASSIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Potassium is a mineral measured in the frass of mealworms, showing changes in concentration due to polystyrene consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium is one of the cations determined and quantified in the study using a set of commercial standards.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SODIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium is a mineral measured in the frass of mealworms, indicating biochemical changes during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium is one of the cations determined and quantified in the study using a set of commercial standards.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="APOPTOSIS BIOMARKERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Apoptosis biomarkers, such as ceramides and cardiolipins, are increased in mealworms fed with polystyrene, indicating cellular stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UHPLC-QTOF-MS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">UHPLC-QTOF-MS is a technique used to analyze lipid and protein alterations in mealworms during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD CONTACT MATERIAL (FCM)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Food contact material (FCM) refers to packaging materials like polystyrene, which are widely used but contribute to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNITED NATIONS SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS (SDG)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDG) aim to achieve a net-zero environmental impact by 2030, including efforts to address plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERWORMS (ZOPHOBAS MORIO)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Superworms (Zophobas morio) are insects that have demonstrated the ability to degrade polystyrene (PS), similar to mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAXWORMS (GALLERIA MELLONELLA)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Waxworms (Galleria mellonella) are insects that can degrade polystyrene (PS) through enzymatic activities in their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG CHAIN FREE FATTY ACIDS (FFAS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Long chain free fatty acids (FFAs) are produced during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) in insects, indicating increased enzymatic and metabolic activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic activities in the gut microbiome of insects, such as phosphatases, esterases, and β-glucosidase, play a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPIDOMICS STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A lipidomics study investigates the lipid profiles of insects during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), providing insights into cellular and membrane lipid alterations linked to biochemical pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN BIOMASS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein biomass refers to the protein content in insects, such as mealworms, which undergoes alterations during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), reflecting changes in cell activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOMARKERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biomarkers are compounds used to assess the well-being of insects during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), helping to identify efficient strategies for improving the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHYL TERT-BUTYL ETHER (MTBE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MTBE is an LC-MS grade chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRAMETHYLSILANE (TMS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">TMS is a chemical used in NMR experiments to lock signals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIMETHYL SULFONE-DMSO2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dimethyl sulfone-DMSO2 is a certified qNMR standard used in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMMONIUM FORMATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ammonium formate is an LC-MS grade chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium formate is used in the mobile phase for lipid analysis in the UHPLC system.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMMONIUM ACETATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ammonium acetate is an LC-MS grade chemical used in the study for analytical purposes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYLENEDIAMINETETRAACETIC ACID (EDTA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">EDTA is a chemical used for protein and nitrogen analysis in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIS (HYDROXYMETHYL) AMINOMETHANE (THAM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">THAM is a chemical used for protein and nitrogen analysis in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED PS (EPS) FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded PS (EPS) foam is a type of polystyrene packaging material studied for its biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROLLED BARLEY (BRAN)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Rolled barley (bran) is a component of the diet fed to Tenebrio molitor larvae in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VEGETABLE WASTE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vegetable waste is part of the diet provided to Tenebrio molitor larvae during rearing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ION CHROMATOGRAPHY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Ion chromatography is used to analyze free cations in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIONEX ICS-6000">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Dionex ICS-6000 ion chromatographic system is used for cation analysis in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHROMELEON 7 SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Chromeleon 7 software is used to control data collection during ion chromatography analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPIDOME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipidome refers to the comprehensive analysis of lipids extracted from tissue samples, used to study the metabolic changes in mealworms during polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The lipidome of mealworms fed with polystyrene-containing diets shows changes in membrane lipids, including those related to cell apoptosis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UHPLC SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The UHPLC system is a high-performance liquid chromatography system used to analyze lipid extracts from mealworm tissues, providing detailed insights into lipid composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultra-High Performance Liquid Chromatography (UHPLC) system is used for the separation and analysis of chemical compounds, often combined with mass spectrometry for detailed analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LITHIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lithium is one of the cations analyzed in the study, though it was not detected in any of the analyzed frass samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMMONIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ammonium is one of the cations analyzed in the study, quantified using a set of commercial standards.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHANESULFONIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methanesulfonic acid is used as part of the eluent in the cation analysis process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIONEX IONPAC CS12A">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dionex Ionpac CS12A is an analytical column used for cation analysis in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CG12 A-8 ΜM GUARD COLUMN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CG12 A-8 μm guard column is used in conjunction with the Dionex Ionpac CS12A for cation analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATION DYNAMICALLY REGENERATED SUPPRESSOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cation Dynamically Regenerated Suppressor (CDRS 600 2 mm) is used in the cation analysis process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHYL TERT-BUTYL ETHER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methyl tert-butyl ether (MTBE) is part of the extraction solvent used for lipid extraction from tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LUNA C18 COLUMN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Luna C18 column is used in the UHPLC system for lipid analysis, with specific dimensions and particle size.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUADRUPOLE TIME-OF-FLIGHT MASS SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Quadrupole Time-Of-Flight (TOF) mass spectrometer is used in conjunction with the UHPLC system for high-resolution mass spectrometry.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QTOF MASS SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Quadrupole Time-of-Flight (QTOF) mass spectrometer is a high-resolution instrument used for accurate mass measurement and structural analysis of chemical compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESI">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Electrospray Ionization (ESI) is a technique used in mass spectrometry to ionize chemical compounds for analysis, particularly in positive ionization mode.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN GAS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen gas is used as a drying and nebulizing agent in mass spectrometry to facilitate ionization and analysis of chemical compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDCL3">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Deuterated chloroform (CDCl3) is a solvent used in NMR analysis to dissolve and analyze chemical compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRUKER AVANCE 600">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bruker Avance 600 is a high-resolution NMR spectrometer used for detailed structural analysis of chemical compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS PROFILER PROFESSIONAL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass Profiler Professional (MPP) is software used for multivariate and statistical analysis of mass spectrometry data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUKEY COMPARISON TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Tukey comparison test is a statistical method used to confirm the significance of differences between diets in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASSHUNTER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Masshunter is software used for processing analytical data in the study, including lipid annotations and sample classification.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIMCA-P">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Simca-P is software used for multivariate statistical analysis, including PCA and OPLS-DA, to model differences in metabolic profiles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID ANNOTATOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid Annotator is software used for rapid and accurate lipid annotations in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAGNESIUM (MG²⁺)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Magnesium is an essential mineral involved in cellular energy production and mitochondrial function, with its concentration in mealworm larvae affected by dietary polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SODIUM (NA⁺)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium is an ion involved in osmo-regulation and excretion in insects, with its concentration influenced by dietary changes such as the inclusion of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POTASSIUM (K⁺)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Potassium is an ion that plays a role in osmo-regulation and ammonia transport in insects, with its concentration affected by dietary polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMO-GRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS (TGA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermo-gravimetric analysis is a method used to evaluate the presence and degradation of polystyrene in the frass of mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V-ATPASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">V-ATPase is an enzyme involved in osmo-regulation and excretion in insects, influenced by dietary changes such as the inclusion of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NA⁺/K⁺-ATPASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase is an enzyme that plays a role in osmo-regulation and ammonia transport in insects, affected by dietary polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MITOCHONDRIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mitochondria are organelles involved in cellular energy production, with their function and magnesium concentration influenced by dietary polystyrene in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMO-GRAVIMETRY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermo-gravimetry is a technique used to analyze the thermal degradation of materials, applied to study the presence of polystyrene in mealworm larvae frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELECTRON TRANSPORT CHAIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The electron transport chain is a series of reactions in mitochondria that produce energy, influenced by magnesium levels and dietary polystyrene in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADP/ATP TRANSLOCATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">ADP/ATP translocation is a process in mitochondria that regulates energy metabolism, affected by magnesium concentration and dietary polystyrene in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOGRAMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermograms are used to analyze the thermal degradation stages of polystyrene and other components in the frass of insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROBIC MICROBIAL STRAINS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aerobic microbial strains in the gut of insects are activated by the presence of water and bran, enhancing polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER BLENDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer blends are mixtures of different polymers, such as polystyrene and bran, studied for their thermal stability and degradation properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECTS' BIOMASS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The biomass of insects is analyzed to assess the accumulation of compounds and the impact of diet regimes on biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER FRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer fragmentation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene into smaller molecules, such as oligomers, during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cations such as potassium, sodium, magnesium, and calcium are involved in biochemical processes during polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbial activity in mealworms is enhanced by proper hydration and plays a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLULAR ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cellular activity in mealworms increases during polystyrene biodegradation, as indicated by changes in protein, nitrogen, and cation levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="APOPTOSIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Apoptosis, or programmed cell death, is observed in mealworms during polystyrene biodegradation, as indicated by changes in their lipidome.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD SOURCES">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">The presence of other food sources can positively influence the biodegradation rate of polystyrene by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental management refers to the study and application of methods, such as biodegradation by mealworms, to address plastic waste pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
      <data key="d1">Lithium is one of the cations analyzed in the study, though it was not detected in any frass samples</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
      <data key="d1">Methanol is part of the extraction solvent used for lipid extraction from tissue samples</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OPLS-DA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
      <data key="d1">Simca-P software is used to perform OPLS-DA, a multivariate statistical analysis, to model differences in metabolic profiles.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISPOSABLE SURGICAL MASKS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Disposable surgical masks are a significant source of environmental pollution, particularly in the post-epidemic era, and are studied for their biodegradation by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Disposable surgical masks are a significant source of microplastic pollution, especially during the COVID-19 pandemic.&lt;SEP&gt;Disposable surgical masks are a significant source of plastic waste, especially during the COVID-19 pandemic, and are composed of polypropylene layers with different crystalline structures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MELT-BLOWN FILTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The melt-blown filter is the middle layer of disposable surgical masks, composed of polypropylene with a unique crystalline structure, and is ingested and degraded by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ITS GENES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">ITS (Internal Transcribed Spacer) gene sequencing is used to analyze the fungal diversity in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms during the biodegradation of surgical masks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURGICAL MASKS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Surgical masks are a source of plastic waste that can be ingested by yellow mealworms, leading to changes in their gut microbiome and potential plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical masks are a type of environmental pollutant that can be biodegraded by yellow mealworms, with different layers showing varying degradation patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical masks, made of polypropylene, are a source of environmental pollution and are studied for their degradation by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MELT BLOWN FILTRATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Melt blown filtration refers to the middle layer of surgical masks, which is more compact and preferred by yellow mealworms for ingestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NONWOVEN FABRIC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nonwoven fabric is used in the outer and inner layers of surgical masks, which are less preferred by yellow mealworms due to their physical structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASK LAYERS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mask layers, made of polypropylene, are environmental pollutants that yellow mealworms can ingest and partially degrade, contributing to biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASK-FED GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mask-fed groups refer to yellow mealworms fed with different layers of masks, showing varying survival rates and degradation efficiencies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INNER LAYER-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The inner layer-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with the inner layer of masks, showing the lowest survival rate and significant degradation of mask materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIDDLE LAYER-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The middle layer-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with the middle layer of masks, showing intermediate survival rates and degradation patterns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OUTER LAYER-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The outer layer-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with the outer layer of masks, showing a significant decrease in survival rate and distinct degradation patterns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP FOAM PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PP foam plastics are polypropylene-based materials used in previous studies to assess the biodegradation capabilities of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL PROPERTIES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal properties refer to the changes in decomposition rates and endothermic temperatures of mask-derived frass, indicating the formation of new organic substances during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OUTER LAYER OF MASK">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The outer layer of surgical masks is a component that undergoes biodegradation by yellow mealworms, showing a specific depolymerization pattern.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIDDLE LAYER OF MASK">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The middle layer of surgical masks is another component that is biodegraded by yellow mealworms, with a distinct depolymerization pattern compared to the outer and inner layers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INNER LAYER OF MASK">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The inner layer of surgical masks is a component that is biodegraded by yellow mealworms, showing a significant reduction in molecular weight during the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNWEIGHTED UNIFRAC">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Unweighted Unifrac is a method used to analyze the diversity and composition of gut bacterial communities in yellow mealworms fed with different plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Unweighted Unifrac is a metric used in PCA to analyze the phylogenetic differences in gut bacterial communities of yellow mealworms fed different layers of surgical masks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillaceae is a dominant fungal family in PP- and PU-fed yellow mealworms, associated with plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillaceae is a family of fungi that shows increased abundance in the gut of yellow mealworms when fed surgical masks, indicating a potential role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEBARYOMYCESACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Debaryomycesaceae is a dominant fungal family in bran-fed yellow mealworms, but its abundance decreases significantly in plastic-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Debaryomycesaceae is a family of fungi predominant in the gut of bran-fed yellow mealworms, with reduced abundance in mask-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT FUNGAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut fungal community of yellow mealworms shows increased diversity when fed surgical masks, with families like Aspergillaceae and Debaryomycesaceae playing potential roles in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AUREOBASIDIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aureobasidium is a fungal genus observed in higher abundances in PU-fed groups, contributing to the biodegradation of polyurethane (PU).&lt;SEP&gt;Aureobasidium is a genus of fungi found in the gut of yellow mealworms, particularly in the outer-fed group, and may play a role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAPHRINA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Taphrina is a fungal genus that increased significantly in the bran-fed group and is linked to plastic degradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Taphrina is a genus of fungi found in the gut of yellow mealworms, showing increased abundance in the middle-fed group, suggesting a potential role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNCLASSIFIED ASCOMYCOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Unclassified Ascomycota is a group of fungi found in the gut of yellow mealworms, particularly in the middle-fed group, and may play a role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIRIKA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mrika is a cold-adapted yeast genus that appeared in the inner layer-fed group and has not been previously mentioned in plastic degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT FUNGAL DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut fungal diversity in yellow mealworms increased significantly after feeding on different layers of masks, indicating their role in plastic and mask degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS FLAVUS PEDX3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus flavus PEDX3 is a fungal strain isolated from the gut of Galleria mellonella larvae, known for its ability to degrade polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASK DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Mask degradation refers to the process by which disposable surgical masks are broken down by yellow mealworms and their gut fungi, with the middle layer being more extensively depolymerized and biodegraded.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONAL MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Functional microorganisms are those that play a role in the biodegradation of plastics and masks, and future work should focus on isolating and identifying these microorganisms to improve degradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FACE MASKS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Face masks, especially disposable ones, have become a significant source of microplastic fibers in the environment, particularly during the COVID-19 pandemic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SARS-COV-2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">SARS-CoV-2 is the virus responsible for COVID-19, and face masks are used to limit its transmission.&lt;SEP&gt;SARS-CoV-2 is the virus responsible for causing COVID-19, a global health emergency declared by the WHO.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC FIBERS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic fibers are small plastic particles that can be released from disposable face masks, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT DIGESTIVE ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut digestive enzymes in insects like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus play a role in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut digestive enzymes in organisms like Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor play a role in the biodegradation of plastics by breaking down complex polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO/NANOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro/nanoplastics are small plastic particles that can be released from used disposable face masks, posing potential human health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED (FT-IR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier Transform Infrared (FT-IR) is a technique used to characterize the biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATTENUATED TOTAL REFLECTION FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED (ATR-FT-IR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Attenuated Total Reflection Fourier Transform Infrared (ATR-FT-IR) is a method used to analyze the biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Attenuated Total Reflection Fourier Transform Infrared (ATR-FT-IR) is a technique used to analyze the biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORE GUT BACTERIAL- AND FUNGAL-MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The core gut bacterial- and fungal-microbiome of insects like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus plays a crucial role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The World Health Organization (WHO) is an international organization that declared the end of the COVID-19 global health emergency in 2023.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BMJ">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">BMJ is a medical journal that published the announcement of the WHO declaring the end of the COVID-19 global health emergency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCIENTIFIC EVIDENCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scientific evidence from studies demonstrates the biodegradation of polystyrene by bacterial strains from waxworms, contributing to environmental solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX COMB">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax comb is a co-feed used in the study to enhance the biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax comb is a natural material used as a co-feed to enhance the biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FTIR AND 1H NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR and 1H NMR analysis are techniques used to study the physiochemical changes in egested frass, indicating the biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR and 1H NMR analysis are techniques used to study the physiochemical changes in egested frass, providing evidence of polyethylene biodegradation by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL AND ALCOHOLIC GROUPS">
