    </node>
    <node id="GROWTH AND SURVIVABILITY STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A study conducted to assess the growth and survivability of lesser waxworms fed on polyethylene (PE) compared to their conventional diet (WC).&lt;SEP&gt;A study conducted to assess the growth and survivability of lesser waxworms fed on polyethylene (PE) compared to their conventional diet (wax comb, WC).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MITOCHONDRIAL CYTOCHROME OXIDASE (MTCOI)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase (mtCOI) is a gene used for genetic identification and validation of larvae through sequencing studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COX1 GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The cox1 gene, part of the mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase subunit 1, is used as a target gene for PCR primers in DNA sequencing analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX COMB (WC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax comb (WC) is the conventional diet for lesser waxworms, used as a control in studies comparing its effects with polyethylene (PE) consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE FILM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PE film is a polyethylene material used in biodegradation studies to assess the ability of lesser waxworms to degrade plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA SEQUENCING ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DNA sequencing analysis is a study conducted to confirm the genetic identity of larvae using mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase (mtCOI) and cox1 gene sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT LOSS MEASUREMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight loss measurement is a study conducted to assess the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by lesser waxworms by measuring the weight loss of PE films over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAXCOMB (WC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Waxcomb (WC) is the natural diet of lesser waxworms and is used in experiments to study the effects of added nutrition on PE degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxcomb (WC) is used as a positive control in experiments to study the biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIQUID-STATE ¹H NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Liquid-state ¹H NMR analysis is conducted to study the changes in the end products of egested PE from lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOURIER TRANSFORM-NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (FT NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FT NMR is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of egested frass from lesser waxworms, particularly to study the degradation products of PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SECOND-GENERATION LESSER WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Second-generation lesser waxworms are offspring of PE-fed waxworms, capable of continuing the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Second-generation lesser waxworms are reared in the laboratory to study their ability to degrade PE and their survivability when fed a PE diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR AMPLIFIED DNA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PCR amplified DNA is used in genomic analysis to study interspecies variation and identify genetic markers related to PE degradation in lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COXL SEQUENCES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Coxl sequences are genetic markers analyzed to study interspecies variation and the genetic basis of PE degradation in lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVABILITY RATE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Survivability rate is a measure of the percentage of live lesser waxworms over time, used to assess the effects of PE and WC diets on their health.&lt;SEP&gt;The survivability rate of lesser waxworms is studied to understand their ability to survive and grow when fed polyethylene (PE) compared to their conventional food, waxcomb.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT LOSS OF PE FILMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight loss of PE films is measured to quantify the extent of PE degradation by lesser waxworms over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUMPLED PAPER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crumpled paper is added to the larval container to provide a surface for lesser waxworms to spin their cocoons during pupation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX PAPER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax paper is used as a surface for egg laying by female moths in the second-generation lesser waxworm experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HATCHLINGS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hatchlings are the newly emerged larvae of lesser waxworms, raised on PE-WC feed to study their growth and PE degradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAXCOMB">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Waxcomb (WC) is a polymer similar to polyethylene, consisting of long-chain carbon atoms with other atoms attached, and is naturally consumed by lesser waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxcomb is a conventional food source for lesser waxworms, made of beeswax, which consists of esters of long-chain alcohols and fatty acids.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENOMIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Genomic analysis involves studying the genome of organisms like E. hormaechei LG3 to identify key enzymes involved in processes such as polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Genomic analysis was performed to identify the lesser waxworm species (Achroia grisella) using PCR, DNA sequencing, and phylogenetic tree construction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SDS-WASHED FILM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SDS-washed film refers to polyethylene films treated with sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) to study weight loss and polymer integrity after degradation by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HONEYCOMB WAX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Honeycomb wax is a natural polymer with strong chemical bonds, similar to polyethylene, and is a natural food source for lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POST-DEGRADATION WEIGHT LOSS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Post-degradation weight loss is a measure of the extent to which polyethylene (PE) is degraded by lesser waxworms, with higher weight loss indicating more effective biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL INDEX (C.I)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The carbonyl index (C.I) is a measure derived from FTIR spectra to quantify the extent of polyethylene degradation by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIRST-GENERATION LESSER WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">First-generation lesser waxworms are the initial group of larvae used in experiments to study the biodegradation of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADULT WAXMOTH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Adult waxmoths are the mature stage of lesser waxworms, capable of laying eggs to produce a new generation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFANT WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Infant waxworms are newly hatched larvae from eggs laid by adult waxmoths, continuing the lifecycle and biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROIA GRISEA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achroia grisea, commonly known as the lesser waxworm, is a species of moth larvae capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) and surviving on it as a sole diet, though additional nutrition enhances its biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CENTRAL INSTRUMENTATION FACILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Central Instrumentation Facility at Pondicherry University provided the instrumentation for characterizing the biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAULANA AZAD NATIONAL FELLOWSHIP PROGRAM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Maulana Azad National Fellowship Program provided financial support for this research study on the biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RHODOCOCCUS RHODOCHROUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodococcus rhodochrous is a bacterial strain capable of biodegrading oxidized polyethylene films, demonstrating its role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus rhodochrous is a bacterial strain capable of biodegrading oxidized polyethylene films.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus rhodochrous is a bacterial strain that can metabolize styrene, aiding in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGRICULTURAL LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Agricultural low-density polyethylene films are widely used in farming but contribute to environmental pollution due to their resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C-NMR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">13C-NMR is a nuclear magnetic resonance technique used to study the branching structure of low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHORIA GRISEA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achoria grisea, or the lesser wax moth, is another insect studied for its biology and potential role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC DEBRIS IN THE OPEN OCEAN">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic debris in the open ocean refers to the accumulation of plastic waste in marine environments, posing significant ecological threats.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYLOGENY.FR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Phylogeny.fr is a tool for robust phylogenetic analysis, used to study evolutionary relationships among organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOGENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudogenes are non-functional DNA sequences that can complicate DNA-based diet analyses in predator-prey systems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WAX MOTH RESEARCH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Wax moth research involves standard methods for studying the biology and behavior of wax moths, including their potential role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDIZED OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidized oligomers are byproducts of polyethylene degradation, characterized by mass spectrometry and NMR spectroscopy.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRODUCTION, USE, AND FATE OF ALL PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This research study examines the global production, use, and environmental fate of all plastics ever made, highlighting the scale of plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MALASSEZIA SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Malassezia species are fungi identified using PCR-restriction endonuclease analysis, relevant in microbiological studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE CHARACTERIZATION BY FTIR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This study uses Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) to characterize polyethylene, a common plastic material.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE BACTERIA FROM THE ARABIAN SEA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine bacteria from the Arabian Sea have been studied for their ability to degrade low-density polyethylene, highlighting their role in marine plastic pollution mitigation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SP. FROM THE NIGER DELTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus sp. indigenous to the Niger Delta mangrove swamp has been studied for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene, offering potential solutions for plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS WEIHENSTEPHANENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus weihenstephanensis is a bacterium isolated from garbage soil, studied for its ability to biodegrade low-density and high-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC WASTE INPUTS FROM LAND INTO THE OCEAN">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">This refers to the flow of plastic waste from terrestrial sources into marine environments, contributing to ocean pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION OF POLYETHYLENE WITH SURFACTANTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This study investigates the influence of surfactants on the biodegradation of polyethylene, a common plastic material.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE-DEGRADING MICROBES FROM MANGROVE SOIL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbes from mangrove soil have been studied for their ability to degrade polythene and plastics, offering potential solutions for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMO-OXIDATION OF POLYETHYLENE FILMS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermo-oxidation refers to the chemical and morphological changes in polyethylene films exposed to heat and oxygen, relevant in plastic degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROPICAL MANGROVE SOIL MICROFLORA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microflora from tropical mangrove soils have been studied for their effectiveness in degrading polythene carry bags, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IN-PLANT NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This study uses high-resolution NMR spectroscopy to analyze intact plants, such as Viscariaula dubyana, for chemical composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC ACCUMULATION IN THE NORTH ATLANTIC SUBTROPICAL GYRE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">This refers to the accumulation of plastic waste in the North Atlantic subtropical gyre, a significant environmental issue.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STREPTOMYCES SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Streptomyces species are bacteria that can degrade polyethylene, playing a role in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIENOTROPHOMONAS PAVANII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sienotrophomonas pavanii is a bacterium used in studies to biodegrade modified low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANIONIC SURFACTANT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Anionic surfactants are chemicals used in conjunction with bacteria and biosurfactants to enhance the biodegradation of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HELICOVERPA ARMIGERA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Helicoverpa armigera, commonly known as the cotton bollworm, is studied for its biology and potential role in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GELATIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gelatin is a protein derived from collagen, used in biodegradable films and blends with poly (vinyl alcohol) to improve physical properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY (VINYL ALCOHOL)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly (vinyl alcohol) (PVA) is a synthetic polymer used in biodegradable films, often blended with gelatin to enhance film properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE GLYCOL MODIFIED TIO2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene glycol modified TiO2 is a pro-oxidant additive used in polyethylene films to enhance photo-oxidation and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE FILMS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biodegradable films are materials made from blends of gelatin and poly (vinyl alcohol), designed to degrade naturally and reduce environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMALLY TREATED POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermally treated polyethylene refers to polyethylene that has been processed at high temperatures, making it more susceptible to biodegradation by fungi.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRO-OXIDANT ADDITIVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pro-oxidant additives are chemicals added to polyethylene films to enhance their biodegradation by promoting oxidation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pro-oxidant additives, such as polyethylene glycol modified TiO2, are used in polyethylene films to enhance their photo-oxidation and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRESHWATER ECOSYSTEMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Freshwater ecosystems are environments where microplastics accumulate, posing risks to aquatic life and water quality.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC MARINE POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic marine pollution refers to the accumulation of plastic waste in marine environments, causing harm to marine life and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA-BASED DIET ANALYSES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudogenes can complicate DNA-based diet analyses in predator-prey systems, requiring caution in interpretation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR-RESTRICTION ENDONUCLEASE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
      <data key="d1">Malassezia species are identified using PCR-restriction endonuclease analysis, relevant in microbiological studies.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYTHENE CARRY BAGS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
      <data key="d1">Microflora from tropical mangrove soils have been studied for their effectiveness in degrading polythene carry bags, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VISCARIAULA DUBYANA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
      <data key="d1">In-plant NMR analysis is used to study the chemical composition of intact plants like Viscariaula dubyana.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER TYPES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer types refer to the different forms of polyethylene, such as HDPE, LDPE, and LLDPE, which vary in molecular weight, crystallinity, and branching structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIVE DOMINANCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Relative dominance analysis is a method used in research to determine the influence of various factors, such as polymer type and crystallinity, on the biodegradation of polyethylene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PY-GC/MS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Py-GC/MS analysis is a technique used to study the degradation of polymers, confirming the breaking of macromolecule backbones and the formation of oxidized functional groups during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMO-OXIDATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermo-oxidation is a pretreatment process that can enhance the biodegradability of polyethylene by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermo-oxidation is a process that degrades polyethylene (PE) through heat, facilitating its biodegradation by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE WAX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene wax is a form of polyethylene that has been studied for its biodegradability by bacteria and fungi.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene wax is a low molecular weight form of polyethylene that can be degraded relatively quickly by bacterial consortia.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene wax is a material studied for its biodegradability by bacteria and fungi, providing insights into microbial degradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSICAL STRUCTURE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The physical structure of polymers, including branching and crystallinity, significantly impacts their biodegradability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (1H NMR) SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">1H NMR spectroscopy is employed to analyze the chemical modifications and degraded functional groups in polyethylene during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DOMINANCE ANALYSIS (DA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Dominance analysis is a statistical method used to determine the relative importance of variables influencing polyethylene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MESTRENOVA SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MestReNova software is a tool used for processing and analyzing NMR spectra, including the identification of chemical shifts and functional groups.&lt;SEP&gt;MestReNova software is used for analyzing NMR spectra, including those of polyethylene, to study chemical modifications during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MULTIPLE LINEAR REGRESSION (MLR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Multiple linear regression (MLR) is a statistical method used to model the relationship between multiple independent variables and a dependent variable, such as in dominance analysis for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUDESCU'S METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Budescu's method is a statistical approach used in dominance analysis to determine the relative importance of predictors in multiple regression models.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEMI-PARTIAL CORRELATION COEFFICIENTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Semi-partial correlation coefficients are used in dominance analysis to measure the unique contribution of each predictor variable to the dependent variable.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene degradation refers to the process by which mealworms break down polyethylene (PE) plastics into smaller molecular components, contributing to environmental solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MM, MW, MS)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weights (Mm, Mw, Ms) are key parameters used to measure the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by mealworms, indicating the breakdown of polymer chains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="R STATISTICAL SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">R statistical software was used for data analysis and model development in the study of polyethylene (PE) biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IBM SPSS STATISTICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">IBM SPSS Statistics (version 25) was used for statistical analysis, including ANOVA and pairwise comparisons, in the study of polyethylene (PE) biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;IBM SPSS Statistics is a software used for statistical analysis, including ANOVA and pairwise comparisons, to evaluate differences in PP consumption, survival, and microbial diversity.&lt;SEP&gt;IBM SPSS Statistics is used for performing statistical tests, such as one-way ANOVAs, in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STENOTROPHOMONAS STRAIN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Stenotrophomonas strain is a mesophilic bacterium known for its ability to degrade polypropylene (PP), similar to the biodegradation capabilities of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION PATTERN">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The biodegradation pattern refers to the specific way in which mealworms break down plastics, such as the limited extent depolymerization of HDPE and broad depolymerization of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYDISPERSITY INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Polydispersity index (PDI) is a measure of the distribution of molecular weights in a polymer sample, which decreases as polystyrene microplastics are degraded by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The polydispersity index (PDI) is a measure of the distribution of molecular weights in a polymer, which decreases during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURFACE HYDROPHOBICITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Surface hydrophobicity is a property of polymer surfaces, measured by water contact angle (WCA), which changes during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Surface hydrophobicity is a property of polymers like polypropylene that affects their biodegradation, with reduced hydrophobicity indicating surface modification and potential biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER CONTACT ANGLE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The water contact angle is a measure of hydrophobicity, which decreases in aged microplastics, promoting microbial colonization and degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) is a measure of surface hydrophobicity, which changes as polymers undergo biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle is a measurement used to assess the hydrophobicity of polypropylene foam, which can influence its biodegradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle is a parameter used to measure the hydrophobicity of plastic samples in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE MPS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which can be biodegraded by certain insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene, which can be ingested and biodegraded by mealworms, leading to changes in their hydrophobicity and molecular structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) are small plastic particles that contribute to environmental pollution and are studied for their biodegradation by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WCA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Water contact angle (WCA) analysis is used to measure the hydrophobicity of polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) after passing through the digestive systems of mealworms, showing reduced resistance to enzymatic attacks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROPHOBICITY CHANGE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrophobicity change refers to the reduction in the water contact angle (WCA) of polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) after ingestion by mealworms, making them less resistant to enzymatic attacks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW MOLECULAR WEIGHT OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low molecular weight oligomers are smaller molecules produced during the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics by mealworms, indicating partial depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOTA SUPPRESSION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbiota suppression refers to the inhibition of gut bacteria in mealworms, which negatively impacts their ability to biodegrade polyethylene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PY-GC/MS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrolysis-gas chromatography/mass spectrometry (Py-GC/MS) is a technique used to analyze the degradation products of polyethylene microplastics in the frass of T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M_N">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">M_n (number-average molecular weight) is a metric used to assess the depolymerization of polystyrene during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;M_n refers to the number-average molecular weight of polyethylene microplastics, which is analyzed to assess the extent of biodegradation by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M_W">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">M_w (weight-average molecular weight) is a metric used to assess the depolymerization of polystyrene during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;M_w refers to the weight-average molecular weight of polyethylene microplastics, which is used to evaluate the biodegradation process by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M_C">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">M_c refers to the critical molecular weight of polyethylene microplastics, which is studied in relation to their biodegradation by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUAL PE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Residual PE polymers are the remaining polyethylene fragments after biodegradation by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae, analyzed to understand the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRO-OXIDANT ADDITIVE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pro-oxidant additives are chemicals added to plastics like LDPE and LLDPE to enhance their abiotic degradation properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABIOTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Abiotic degradation refers to the breakdown of materials like plastics through non-biological processes, such as oxidation or UV exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL DEGRADABLE POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmentally degradable polyethylene is a type of plastic designed to break down more easily, reducing its environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL EXTRACTION-DESORPTION GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY MASS SPECTROMETRY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This is a technique used to comprehensively characterize polymers and their degradation products, aiding in biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTOCHEMICAL STABILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Photochemical stability refers to the resistance of materials like HDPE/Ag composites to degradation under light exposure, studied in environmental contexts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XENOBIOTIC ADDITIVES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Xenobiotic additives are chemical substances that can be degraded by a selected landfill microbial community, as revealed by metagenomic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Xenobiotic additives are synthetic chemicals added to materials like plastics, which can be degraded by specialized microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LANDFILL MICROBIAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A selected landfill microbial community is capable of degrading recalcitrant polyurethane and xenobiotic additives, as revealed by metagenomic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Landfill microbial communities are studied for their biodegradative potential, particularly in breaking down recalcitrant materials like polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HDPE/AG COMPOSITES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">HDPE/Ag composites are materials studied for their photochemical stability, which is important for understanding their degradation under environmental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL ADDITIVES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemical additives in plastics are studied for their migration, release, fate, and environmental impact during use, disposal, and recycling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZOPHOBAS ATRINUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zophobas atrinus, also known as superworms, are larvae that can biodegrade polypropylene via gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER SP. NYX450">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter sp. strain NyX450 is a bacterium isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae that contributes to the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT-MICROBE-DEPENDENT DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization is a process where gut bacteria in larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atrinus, play a crucial role in breaking down plastics like polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT-MICROBE-INDEPENDENT DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut-microbe-independent depolymerization is a process where larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, can biodegrade plastics like low-density polyethylene without relying on gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKALINE BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">An alkaline bacterium isolated from soil is capable of biodegrading polyethylene, as demonstrated in a study using AlkB cloned recombinant cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IRRADIATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE undergoes chemical and morphological modifications when irradiated, which is important for understanding its environmental degradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIO-OXIDATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
      <data key="d1">Non-hydrolyzable plastics are studied for their bio-oxidation potential, offering insights into their degradation by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MESOPHILIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene is subject to biodegradation by mesophilic bacteria, providing a potential solution to plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIA AND FUNGI">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene wax is studied for its biodegradability by bacteria and fungi, providing insights into microbial degradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECIFIC CONSUMPTION RATES (SCRS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Specific Consumption Rates (SCRs) are metrics used to quantify the rate at which mealworms consume different types of plastics, providing critical information for plastic degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONSUMPTION RATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Consumption rates measure the amount of plastic consumed by mealworms over time, providing data on their ability to degrade different types of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM VIABILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm viability refers to the ability of mealworms to survive and thrive under conditions of plastic consumption, which is influenced by their weight changes and metabolic processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm viability refers to the survival and health of mealworms when exposed to different types of plastics, which is an important factor in their ability to degrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL ISSUES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental issues caused by plastic waste include pollution of marine and terrestrial ecosystems, harm to wildlife, and challenges in waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE CREATURES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine creatures are affected by plastic waste, which can harm their health and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABOVEGROUND VEGETATION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aboveground vegetation can be harmed by plastic waste buried in soils, affecting plant health and growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YELLOW MEALWORM BEETLE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The yellow mealworm beetle is a species of darkling beetle whose larvae (mealworms) are capable of consuming and degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIDGUT IMAGING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Midgut imaging has been used to study the digestion process of plastics in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLARITY AND FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Analysis of molarity and functional groups has been conducted to understand the depolymerization of plastics after consumption by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIABILITY RATES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Viability rates of mealworms are measured to assess the effects of plastic consumption on their health and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENSILE STRENGTH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tensile strength is a factor that affects mealworm consumption ability, particularly for different types of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RIGIDITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Rigidity is a property of plastics that influences mealworm consumption ability, particularly for high-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELASTICITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Elasticity is a property of plastics that influences mealworm consumption ability, particularly for low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MELTING POINTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Melting points of plastics are factors that affect mealworm consumption ability, particularly for different types of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC CONSUMPTION STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This research study investigates the ability of mealworms to consume and degrade five types of plastics (PS, HDPE, LDPE, PVC, PP) over three time intervals (10, 20, and 30 days), measuring changes in mealworm mass and plastic consumption rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRZEMIENIECKI ET AL., 2022">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A study by Przemieniecki et al., 2022, focused on analyzing the protein and fatty acid contents within mealworms and identifying bacterial strains in their guts, providing a comparative context for the current study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL., 2021">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A study by Yang et al., 2021, investigated plastic degradation by mealworms over a single time period, providing a comparative context for the current study's multiple time intervals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG ET AL., 2018">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A study by Yang et al., 2018, examined mealworm consumption of polystyrene (PS) and its effects on mealworm viability, providing a comparative context for the current study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PENG ET AL., 2022">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A study by Peng et al., 2022, investigated plastic degradation by mealworms over a single time period, providing a comparative context for the current study's multiple time intervals.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD AND AGRICULTURE ORGANIZATION (FAO)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The FAO provides nutritional recommendations for fatty acid ratios, which are used to evaluate the suitability of mealworm larvae as a food source.&lt;SEP&gt;The Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) designated insects as a future food resource in 2013, highlighting their potential as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOURCE A MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Source A were used in 10-, 20-, and 30-day experiments to study their ability to consume and degrade various types of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOURCE B MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Source B were used in the 30-day experiment to study their plastic consumption and mass change.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOURCE C MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms from Source C were used in the 30-day experiment to study their plastic consumption and mass change.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET STORAGE JARS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) storage jars were used to house mealworms during the experiments, as they were too dense for mealworms to consume.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPERIMENTAL TIME INTERVALS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The study involved 10-, 20-, and 30-day experimental time intervals to examine mealworm mass change and plastic consumption over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM VIABILITY RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The viability rate of mealworms was calculated by counting the number of live mealworms at the end of each experimental time interval compared to the initial count.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM MASS CHANGE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm mass change refers to the difference in mass of mealworms before and after the experiments, normalized by the number of live mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTESTINE MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The intestine microbiome of mealworms plays a crucial role in digesting and breaking down synthetic polymers like plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLDED PULP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molded pulp is a material used to feed mealworms before experiments, containing natural fibers with higher nutritional content than raw plastic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUGARCANE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sugarcane is a source of natural fibers found in cardboard and molded pulp, which are used to feed mealworms before experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAMBOO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bamboo is a source of natural fibers found in cardboard and molded pulp, which are used to feed mealworms before experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHITE WHEAT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">White wheat is a source of natural fibers found in cardboard and molded pulp, which are used to feed mealworms before experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON BONDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon bonds are covalent pairs found in synthetic polymers, which contribute to their low bio-reactivity and slow degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLIC ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylic acids are organic compounds released during the degradation of PVC by mealworms, contributing to mass reduction in the larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLORIDE GROUP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The chloride group is a distinguishing property of PVC, making up more than half of its chemical mass and potentially causing fluctuations in mealworm mass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON-CARBON BONDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon-carbon bonds are structural features of plastics like HDPE and LDPE, with more branching making plastics more bioresistant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STAGE-DEVELOPMENT CHANGE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stage-development change refers to the growth and developmental stages of mealworms, which may influence their ability to consume and degrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental factors are external conditions that may affect the ability of mealworms to consume and degrade plastics, such as temperature, humidity, and food availability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT FLUCTUATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight fluctuation in mealworms refers to changes in their body mass when fed with plastics, indicating potential adaptation or nutritional challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADAPTATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Adaptation refers to the ability of mealworms to adjust to unfavorable food sources, such as plastics, over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DETOXIFICATION PROCESSES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Detoxification processes in mealworms refer to their ability to metabolize and neutralize harmful substances, such as plastic additives, during digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICAL STRUCTURE OF PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The chemical structure of plastics, such as HDPE, LDPE, and PVC, influences their digestibility by mealworms and their effects on mealworm metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM MANDIBLES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm mandibles are the mouthparts used to chew and consume plastics, with their small size influencing the rate and pattern of plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT-DENSE SUBSTRATE">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">A nutrient-dense substrate refers to food sources rich in essential nutrients, which can be used alongside plastics to study mealworm viability and long-term plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORAL REEFS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Coral reefs are marine ecosystems that can be negatively affected by plastic waste, which is associated with disease and degradation of reef health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC DEGRADATION RATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic degradation rates are studied to understand how different plastics break down in the environment, which is crucial for waste management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TERRESTRIAL SNAILS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Terrestrial snails are organisms that can be affected by nanoplastics, leading to behavioral disorders and trophic transfer effects via vascular plants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANT PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plant performance refers to the growth and health of plants, which can be negatively impacted by microplastics in the soil.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKENE CRACKING">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkene cracking is a chemical process influenced by chain length and branching, which is studied in the context of catalytic reactions on H-ZSM-5.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H-ZSM-5">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">H-ZSM-5 is a catalyst used in alkene cracking reactions, where chain length and branching influence the reactivity of the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTENDED PRODUCER RESPONSIBILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Extended producer responsibility is a concept studied in Europe to address the problems posed by plastic products and improve waste management practices.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GESAMP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GESAMP is a joint group of experts that assesses the sources, fate, and effects of microplastics in the marine environment, providing a global perspective on the issue.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JELLYFISH">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Jellyfish have been observed ingesting plastic items, revealing a novel pathway for trophic transference of marine litter.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRESH PLANT MATERIALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fresh plant materials are used as supplements in diets for mealworms to study their growth performance and nutrient composition.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROPHIC TRANSFERENCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Trophic transference refers to the movement of pollutants, such as microplastics, through the food chain, as observed in marine organisms like jellyfish.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLDED FIBER AND PULP PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Molded fiber and pulp products are proposed as green and sustainable alternatives to plastics, reducing environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRONTIERS IN ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Frontiers in Environmental Science is a journal that publishes research on environmental issues, including the effects of microplastics on ecosystems and human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JOURNAL OF BIORESOURCES AND BIOPRODUCTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Journal of Bioresources and Bioproducts publishes research on sustainable alternatives to plastics, such as molded fiber and pulp products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTOCHEMICAL DISSOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Photochemical dissolution refers to the process by which microplastics are broken down into dissolved organic carbon through exposure to light, impacting microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISSOLVED ORGANIC CARBON">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dissolved organic carbon is a product of the photochemical dissolution of microplastics, which impacts microbial activity in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHOTOCHEMICAL DISSOLUTION RATES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Photochemical dissolution rates refer to the speed at which microplastics are broken down into dissolved organic carbon under light exposure, affecting microbial ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL IMPACTS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial impacts refer to the effects of microplastics and their degradation products, such as dissolved organic carbon, on microbial communities in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANT MATERIALS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) are studied for their growth performance and nutrient composition when fed on fresh plant materials-supplemented diets.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEVELOPING COUNTRIES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic waste disposal is a significant challenge in developing countries, where management practices are often inadequate.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
      <data key="d1">Extended producer responsibility is a concept aimed at addressing the problems posed by plastic products and improving waste management.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN FEED">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran feed is an agricultural byproduct used as the primary feed for Tenebrio molitor larvae, which enhances their ability to biodegrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPPLEMENTAL NUTRITION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Supplemental nutrition, such as bran feed, increases the biodegradation rates of polystyrene in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD WASTES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Simulated food wastes are used in experiments to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae under different conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUSTAINABLE PROTEIN SOURCE">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor larvae are considered a sustainable protein source due to their high protein content and ability to biodegrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN CA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain CA is a commercially available strain of Tenebrio molitor mealworms used in the study to evaluate the biodegradation potential of various polystyrene wastes.&lt;SEP&gt;Strain CA refers to a commercially available strain of Tenebrio molitor mealworms used in the study to evaluate the biodegradation potential of various PS wastes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIONIDAE FAMILY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The Tenebrionidae family includes insects such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms (Zophobas morio), which are known for their ability to consume and degrade plastics like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANOBIDAE FAMILY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The Anobiidae family is a group of insects that have been studied for their potential interactions with plastics, though no specific research on their plastic-eating behavior has been documented.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DERMESTIDAE FAMILY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The Dermestidae family includes insects that are often studied for their feeding habits, though no specific research on their ability to degrade plastics has been conducted.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XPS FOOD CONTAINERS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">XPS food containers are a type of polystyrene packaging used in the food industry, contributing to plastic waste and environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XPS COFFEE CUP">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">XPS coffee cups are a type of polystyrene packaging used for beverages, adding to the growing problem of plastic waste in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELECTRONIC PACKAGING CONTAINER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Electronic packaging containers made from EPS are a source of polystyrene waste, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN BEIJING">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain Beijing refers to a specific strain of Tenebrio molitor mealworms from Beijing, China, which has been studied for its ability to survive on EPS alone and biodegrade it.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XPS (EXTRUDED POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Extruded polystyrene (XPS) is a denser form of polystyrene foam used in food packaging and coffee cups, contributing to plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mₙ AND Mₘ">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mₙ (number-average molecular weight) and Mₘ (weight-average molecular weight) are parameters used to characterize the molecular weight of polystyrene before and after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SECOND GENERATION MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Second-generation mealworms are offspring of PS-degrading mealworms that show enhanced capacity to consume and degrade polystyrene waste materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Second-generation mealworms are offspring of polystyrene-fed mealworms, reared to study the long-term effects of PS consumption on their development and reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STAINLESS-STEEL SIEVE MAT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A stainless-steel sieve mat is used in experiments to separate male and female beetles for mating studies in controlled environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN CA MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain CA mealworms are a specific strain of Tenebrio molitor larvae used in experiments to study polystyrene (PS) consumption and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B:PS RATIOS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">B:PS ratios refer to the proportion of bran to polystyrene in the diet of mealworms, studied for its effects on survival and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS CHARACTERISTICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PS characteristics refer to the physical and chemical properties of polystyrene, analyzed to understand its degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MWD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is a key indicator of polystyrene depolymerization and degradation, measured using GPC analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS EXTRACTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass extracts are the chemical residues from mealworm excrement, analyzed to study the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADAPTATION PERIOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The adaptation period refers to the time required for mealworms to stabilize their polystyrene degradation activity, typically 16–24 days.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL RATES (SR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival rates (SR) are a measure of mealworm viability under different feeding conditions, with higher SR values observed when mealworms are fed PS supplemented with soy protein or bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN:PS RATIOS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran:PS ratios refer to the proportion of bran to polystyrene fed to mealworms, with higher ratios leading to increased PS consumption rates and survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPC SPECTRA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GPC (Gel Permeation Chromatography) spectra are used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polystyrene residues in frass, providing insights into the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC spectra are used to analyze the molecular weight distribution (MWD) of polystyrene residues in frass, showing shifts to lower molecular weights after degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS INSULATION MATERIAL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS insulation material is a type of polystyrene waste that mealworms can consume and degrade, contributing to plastic waste reduction.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZENE RING CLEAVAGE">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzene ring cleavage is observed in polystyrene residues from mealworm frass, providing evidence of chemical degradation of PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Benzene ring cleavage refers to the breakdown of the aromatic structure in polystyrene, a key step in its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SR VALUES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SR values (specific consumption rates) measure the rate at which mealworms consume different polystyrene waste materials, indicating their efficiency in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS WASTE MATERIALS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PS waste materials are various forms of polystyrene waste that mealworms can consume and degrade, contributing to environmental cleanup.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS INSULATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS insulation is a type of polystyrene waste material that mealworms can consume and degrade, contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO MOLITOR STRAIN CA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor strain CA is a specific strain of mealworms used in the study to degrade various polystyrene waste materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEARSON CORRELATION TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Pearson correlation test was used to analyze the relationship between polystyrene consumption rates and material density in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REDUNDANCY ANALYSIS (RDA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Redundancy Analysis (RDA) is a statistical method used to explain the variation in polystyrene consumption rates based on factors like density and molecular weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Redundancy analysis (RDA) is a statistical method used to identify the relationship between aging indexes and microbial communities in the biodegradation of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Redundancy analysis (RDA) is a statistical method used to verify the effectiveness of aged microplastic biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JUVENILE MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Juvenile mealworms are the younger stage of mealworms that also demonstrate the ability to consume and degrade polystyrene waste materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HONEY COMB WAX WORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Honey comb wax worm (Galleria mellonella) is another insect known to chew and eat plastic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGENASE-MEDIATED ATTACK">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">An oxygenase-mediated attack is a hypothesized mechanism for the initial breakdown of polystyrene during biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SELECTIVE BREEDING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Selective breeding refers to the process of breeding mealworms with enhanced abilities to biodegrade polystyrene, enabled by nutritional supplementation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF EXTRACTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">THF (tetrahydrofuran) extracts are used to analyze residual plastic in frass, providing insights into the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NMR (NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NMR is used to characterize the chemical structure of plastic residues in frass, providing insights into the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TG-FTIR (THERMOGRAVIMETRIC-FTIR)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">TG-FTIR is an analytical tool used to study the thermal degradation and chemical composition of plastic residues during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WOODS INSTITUTE FOR ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Woods Institute for Environment at Stanford University supported the research on mealworms and their ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="US NATIONAL SCIENCE FOUNDATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The US National Science Foundation provided partial funding for the research on polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HARBIN INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Harbin Institute of Technology supported Dr. Shanshan Yang, who contributed to the research on mealworms and polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BETA HATCH CORP.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Beta Hatch Corp. provided expertise and suggestions for the research on mealworms and their role in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICHIGAN STATE UNIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Michigan State University researchers contributed to the confirmation of beetle identifications in the study on mealworms and polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUEEN'S UNIVERSITY OF BELFAST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Queen's University of Belfast provided expertise and suggestions for the research on mealworms and polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMICALLY OXIDIZED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chemically oxidized polystyrene is a modified form of polystyrene that can be utilized as a co-substrate by certain microorganisms, such as filamentous fungi.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOREGENERATIVE LIFE SUPPORT SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioregenerative life support systems are systems designed to support human life in space by recycling waste and producing food, with studies exploring the use of Tenebrio molitor as a protein source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOLOGICAL SOLUTIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbiological solutions refer to the use of microorganisms to address environmental pollution, such as the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;This research study investigates the potential of microbiological solutions to address environmental pollution caused by plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECTS AND PACKAGING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Insects and packaging is a research area that explores the interaction between insects and packaging materials, including their ability to chew and degrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expanded polystyrene foam (EPS) is a common form of polystyrene used in packaging and building materials, contributing to environmental pollution and microplastic formation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMO GRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS (TGA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">TGA is a method used to assess the proportion of polystyrene residue after degradation by mealworms, indicating the effectiveness of the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROCKY MOUNTAIN MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rocky Mountain Mealworms is a U.S.-based supplier of Tenebrio molitor larvae used in studies to evaluate their ability to consume and biodegrade polystyrene (PS) foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUANYUAN INSECTS BREEDING PLANT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Guanyuan Insects Breeding Plant is a Chinese supplier of Tenebrio molitor larvae used in studies to evaluate their ability to consume and biodegrade polystyrene (PS) foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LUOYANG INSECTS BREEDING PLANT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Luoyang Insects Breeding Plant is a Chinese supplier of Tenebrio molitor larvae used in studies to evaluate their ability to consume and biodegrade polystyrene (PS) foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAN TIAN PLASTIC COMPANY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Lan Tian Plastic Company is a supplier of expanded polystyrene (PS) foam used as feedstock for mealworm larvae in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF EXTRACTABLE RATIO (TER)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">THF Extractable Ratio (TER) is a metric used to quantify the mass of PS residue in frass, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;THF extractable ratio (TER) is a measure used to assess the fraction of residual polystyrene (PS) polymer in the frass of mealworm larvae, indicating the extent of PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PES MEMBRANE FILTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(ether sulfone) (PES) membrane filter is used to filter THF-extracted polymer solutions during the analysis of polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF EXTRACTION TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">THF extraction test is a method used to extract and quantify the amount of polystyrene from samples, such as frass, to assess biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM-EDX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scanning electron microscopy with energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (SEM-EDX) is used to observe the morphology of frass from larvae fed polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSA PLATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Tryptic soy agar (TSA) plates are used to cultivate and count gut microorganisms from larvae during microbial community analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V3-V4 REGION OF 16S RRNA GENE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The V3-V4 region of the 16S rRNA gene is sequenced to analyze the diversity and composition of microbial communities in the guts of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAJORBIO I-SANGER CLOUD PLATFORM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Majorbio I-Sanger Cloud Platform is an online tool used in the study for microbial community analysis, including hierarchical clustering and principal coordinate analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (M_N AND M_W)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (M_n and M_w) refers to the average molecular weight of polystyrene (PS) polymers, which decreases during depolymerization by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECIFIC PS CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Specific PS consumption rate (SPCR) measures the amount of polystyrene (PS) consumed by mealworm larvae over time, indicating their biodegradation efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Specific PS consumption rate measures the amount of polystyrene microplastics consumed by mealworms per unit time, indicating their ability to degrade PS MPs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-DEGRADING MICROBIAL ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-degrading microbial activities refer to the enzymatic processes carried out by gut microbiota in mealworm larvae that facilitate the degradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VOLATILE ORGANICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Volatile organics are compounds detected in frass during TGA analysis, likely resulting from gut secretions and polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial community diversity refers to the variety of microorganisms in the gut of T. obscurus and T. molitor, which changes when the larvae are fed polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER ABSURCIS YT1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter absurcis YT1 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of the Indian meal moth, known for its ability to degrade polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROTOCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterococcaceae is a family of gut-associated bacteria that showed significant changes in abundance in Tenebrio larvae when fed a polystyrene diet, suggesting its involvement in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEM IMAGE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SEM image analysis is a technique used to study the morphology of frass from PS-fed Tenebrio larvae, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM image analysis is used to examine the surface of original PS and PE foam before and after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDX MAPPING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">EDX mapping is a method used to analyze the elemental composition of frass from PS-fed Tenebrio larvae, helping to understand the chemical modifications during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE MICROPLASTIC-ASSOCIATED BIOFILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marine microplastic-associated biofilms are communities of microorganisms that colonize microplastic surfaces in marine environments, potentially influencing the fate and effects of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAMMARUS PULEX">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gammarus pulex is a freshwater invertebrate studied to assess the effects of PET microplastics on its survival, development, metabolism, and feeding activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammarus pulex is a freshwater invertebrate used in studies to assess the effects of microplastics on aquatic organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTICSEUROPE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PlasticsEurope is an organization that provides data and reports on the production and environmental impact of plastics, such as the "Plastics-the Facts 2016" report.&lt;SEP&gt;PlasticsEurope is an organization that provides data and reports on the production and environmental impact of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NORTHERN SOUTH CHINA SEA">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The northern South China Sea is a region where marine debris, including plastics, has been studied for its abundance, composition, and sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIDALGO-RUZ ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Hidalgo-Ruz et al. conducted a review of methods used for the identification and quantification of microplastics in the marine environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WU ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Wu et al. studied microplastics pollution and proposed reduction strategies to address the environmental impact of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEE ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Lee et al. investigated the biodegradation of degradable plastic polyethylene by Phanerochaete and Streptomyces species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JONES ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Jones et al. developed experimental procedures to study the biodegradability of photodegraded polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUILLET ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Guillet et al. conducted tracer studies to investigate the biooxidation of Ecolyte PS polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMASS ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Amass et al. reviewed biodegradable polymers, including their synthesis, characterization, and recent advances in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KYRIKOU AND BRIASSOULIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kyrikou and Briassoulis critically reviewed the biodegradation of agricultural plastic films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AUSTIN ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Austin et al. characterized and engineered a plastic-degrading aromatic polyesterase, contributing to the understanding of plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GERHARDT AND LINDGREN">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gerhardt and Lindgren tested the resistance of packaging films to penetration by stored-product insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLINE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cline studied the penetration of flexible packaging materials by larvae and adults of stored-product insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUATERNARY AMMONIUM COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Quaternary ammonium compounds are chemicals that can impact microbial communities, including those involved in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="URBAN INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Urban insects are species that thrive in urban environments, including pests like Tenebrio obscurus, which are studied for their ecological impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WORLDWIDE DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The worldwide distribution of Tenebrio obscurus is a subject of ecological research, highlighting its adaptability and presence across various regions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0c7b499dc783c21f764eb35989a449f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOLOGICAL STUDY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Ecological studies focus on understanding the distribution, behavior, and environmental impact of organisms like Tenebrio obscurus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0c7b499dc783c21f764eb35989a449f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PACKAGING MATERIALS">
