    <node id="NITROGEN-TO-PROTEIN CONVERSION FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-to-protein conversion factors are used to evaluate the protein content of edible insects like Tenebrio molitor, Alphitobius diaperinus, and Hermetia illucens.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD INFORMATION REGULATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Food Information Regulation refers to the EU Regulation (No. 1169/2011) on the provision of food information to consumers, which includes guidelines on labeling and safety of edible insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN QUALITY EVALUATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein quality evaluation involves assessing the nutritional value of proteins from edible insects like Tenebrio molitor, Alphitobius diaperinus, and Hermetia illucens when fed to rats.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDIBLE COLEOPTERA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Edible Coleoptera refers to beetles like Tenebrio molitor, Zophobas morio, and Alphitobius diaperinus, which are studied for their nutritional value and potential as food sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEIN CONTENT OVERESTIMATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein content overestimation refers to studies that highlight the potential overestimation of protein levels in edible insects, emphasizing the need for accurate measurement methods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TECHNO-FUNCTIONALITY OF INSECT FLOURS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Techno-functionality of insect flours involves studying the functional properties of flours derived from edible insects like Tenebrio molitor and Hermetia illucens for use in food products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOACTIVE COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioactive compounds are substances found in marine and freshwater algae that have potential health benefits and are studied for their nutritional and medicinal properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLIRICIDIA SEPIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gliricidia sepium, commonly known as gamal, is a plant whose flavonoid-rich leaf extract has insecticidal effects on coffee mealybugs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANOCOCCUS CITRI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Planococcus citri, commonly known as coffee mealybugs, are pests that can be controlled using insecticidal extracts from plants like Gliricidia sepium.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAVONOID-RICH FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The flavonoid-rich fraction of Gliricidia sepium leaves is used for its insecticidal properties against coffee mealybugs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WASTE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Waste biodegradation refers to the process by which organisms like Tenebrio molitor break down waste materials, including plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD COMPOSITION DATA">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Food composition data for edible insects is reviewed to assess their nutritional value and safety for human consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATS AND FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fats and fatty acids are essential nutrients found in edible insects, contributing to their nutritional value as a food source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE AND FRESHWATER ALGAE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioactive compounds are studied for their health benefits in marine and freshwater algae.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INDUSTRIALIZATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Industrialization has led to increased production and use of plastics, contributing to environmental pollution and the accumulation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Water pollution refers to the contamination of water bodies by substances such as microplastics, which can harm aquatic life and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATMOSPHERIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Atmospheric pollution refers to the contamination of the air by substances such as microplastics, which can have adverse effects on air quality and human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRADITIONAL DISPOSAL METHODS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Traditional disposal methods, such as incineration and landfill, are insufficient for managing plastic waste and contribute to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPROPYLENE PLASTIC CONTAINER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene plastic containers were used to rear the larvae during the feeding tests.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT TISSUE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut tissue from insect larvae is collected and analyzed to study the microbial community involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL DNA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial DNA is extracted from the gut tissue of insect larvae to study the microbial community involved in polystyrene (PS) biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHLOROFORM:METHANOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Chloroform:methanol is a solvent mixture used to extract degradation products from the frass and gut of insect larvae for GC-MS analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAE WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae weight is monitored during experiments to assess the impact of polystyrene (PS) consumption on the growth and health of insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF EXTRACT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">THF (Tetrahydrofuran) extract is used to analyze the molecular weight changes in polystyrene (PS) after degradation by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic depolymerization is the process by which enzymes break down polystyrene (PS) into smaller molecules, as observed in the guts of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="¹H-NMR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (¹H-NMR) spectroscopy is used to study the molecular structure of polystyrene degradation products, revealing changes in hydrogen bonding.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ILLUMINA SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Illumina sequencing is a high-throughput DNA sequencing method used to analyze the bacterial diversity in the guts of insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Illumina sequencing is a high-throughput DNA sequencing method used to analyze the gut microbiome diversity of insect larvae fed on polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Illumina sequencing is a method used to analyze the 16S rRNA gene amplicons to study the bacterial diversity in the gut microbiota of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae under different diet conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Illumina sequencing is a high-throughput DNA sequencing technology used to analyze the gut microbiome of mealworms under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbial diversity refers to the variety of microorganisms in the gut of insect larvae, which changes in response to polystyrene feeding and influences biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbial diversity refers to the variety of microorganisms present in the gut of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae, which is influenced by diet and plays a role in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACE AND CHAO ESTIMATORS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Ace and Chao estimators are statistical tools used to estimate the richness of microbial communities in the gut of insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHANNON AND SIMPSON INDEXES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Shannon and Simpson indexes are measures of microbial diversity in the gut of insect larvae, indicating changes in species diversity over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZENE RING DESTRUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzene ring destruction refers to the breakdown of the aromatic structure of polystyrene, particularly observed in the frass of yellow mealworm.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT ECOPHYSIOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut ecophysiology refers to the study of the physiological adaptations of the gut microbiome in response to dietary changes, such as polystyrene feeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAY-CURTIS DISTANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bray-Curtis distance is a measure used to analyze the community diversity distance in the gut microbiota of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae based on different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Bray-Curtis distance is a metric used in ecological studies to measure the dissimilarity between microbial communities, such as those in the guts of insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMUNITY BAR PLOT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Community bar plot analysis is a method used to visualize the composition of microbial communities at different taxonomic levels, such as in the guts of insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMUNITY HEATMAP ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Community heatmap analysis is a method used to visualize the abundance and distribution of microbial taxa in different samples, such as in the guts of insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T. CASTANEUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">T. castaneum is a species of beetle whose gut microbiome has been studied for its role in polystyrene (PS) degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P. DAVIDIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">P. davidis is a species of insect larvae from which Serratia sp. WSW, a PS-degrading bacterial strain, was isolated.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAX MOTH (GMEL)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The greater wax moth (Gmel) is an insect species studied for its gut microbiome and its role in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERWORM (ZATR)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The superworm (Zatr) is an insect species whose gut microbiome has been analyzed for its role in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLIC ESTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylic esters are metabolic intermediates formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-PROPENOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-propenoic acid is a metabolic intermediate detected in the frass of insect larvae fed on polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BENZENEPROPANOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Benzenepropanoic acid is a metabolic intermediate detected during the biodegradation of polystyrene in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEHENIC ALCOHOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Behenic alcohol is a metabolic intermediate detected in the frass of insect larvae fed on polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHENOL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phenol is a toxic compound detected in polystyrene samples, potentially derived from plastic additives.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPTANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Heptane is a toxic compound detected in polystyrene samples, potentially derived from plastic additives.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIOXIDANTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Antioxidants are additives in plastic foam that may contribute to the formation of toxic compounds during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYME SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The enzyme system of insect larvae plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, working alongside gut microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRICHODERMA HAMATUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Trichoderma hamatum is a fungus isolated from plastics-polluted environments that can attack petroleum-based synthetic polymer films, aiding in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil microbiota are microorganisms present in soil that contribute to the degradation of plastics and other organic pollutants, playing a vital role in ecosystem health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGAL ISOLATES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungal isolates are microorganisms that have been studied for their ability to degrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC) plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG-CHAIN HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Long-chain hydrocarbons are organic compounds consisting of carbon and hydrogen atoms with long molecular chains, such as those found in beeswax.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KOCURIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kocuria is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus, associated with plastic degradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Kocuria is a bacterial genus found to be overrepresented in the gut of polystyrene-fed larvae, potentially involved in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Kocuria is a genus of Gram-positive bacteria that was significantly over-represented in polystyrene (PS) samples, indicating its potential role in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Kocuria is a genus of bacteria that was overrepresented in PS samples, suggesting its potential role in the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPHODERMELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyphodermella is a fungal genus found in the gut of polystyrene-fed larvae, but its role in plastic degradation is not yet understood.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyphodermella is a fungal genus identified in the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus, possibly contributing to plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyphodermella is a genus of fungi that was overrepresented in PS samples, suggesting a potential role in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC ACCUMULATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic accumulation refers to the buildup of small plastic particles in the environment, posing risks to ecosystems and human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOREACTORS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioreactors are proposed as potential systems for plastic degradation, utilizing microbial communities identified in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONADACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonadaceae is a family of Gram-negative bacteria that was found to be significantly more abundant in polystyrene (PS) samples compared to control (CT) samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonadaceae is a family of bacteria reported to play a significant role in the degradation of synthetic polymers within insect guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonadaceae is a family of bacteria that increases in PP-fed yellow mealworms, indicating its potential role in PP degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonadaceae is a family of bacteria within the Gammaproteobacteria class, identified as one of the groups capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESSER MEALWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The lesser mealworm (Alphitobius diaperinus) is a pest of stored food grain products and has been shown to feed on and degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRIR®">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Extrir® is a commercial expanded polystyrene foam used as feedstock in experiments to study its degradation by insects like Alphitobius diaperinus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SDS SOLUTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SDS solution is a chemical mixture used to wash and separate undegraded polystyrene fragments from organic materials in experiments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUSOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fusobacteria is a bacterial phylum present in the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus, though in smaller proportions, it may still play a role in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fusobacteria is a phylum found in the gut bacterial communities of yellow mealworms, with a significant presence in certain plastic-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Fusobacteria is a phylum in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, with increased abundances observed in PP- and PU-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTEROIDIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteroidia is a bacterial class found in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus, contributing to the microbial diversity and biodegradation activities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ERYSIPELOTRICHIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Erysipelotrichia is a bacterial class found in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus, contributing to the microbial community and biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAY-CURTIS PCOA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bray-Curtis Principal Coordinates Analysis (PCoA) is a method used to analyze the microbial community composition in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNIFRAC DISTANCES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Unifrac distances are used to compare microbial communities based on phylogenetic differences, applied to the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYLOSEQ">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Phyloseq is an R package for reproducible interactive analysis and graphics of microbiome census data, as developed by McMurtrie and Holmes (2013).&lt;SEP&gt;Phyloseq is an R package used for analyzing microbial community data, including the gut microbiota of Alphitobius diaperinus in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GOOD'S COVERAGE INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Good's coverage index is a measure of the representativeness of microbial community data, used in the study of Alphitobius diaperinus gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Micrococcaceae is a family of Gram-positive bacteria that showed higher abundance in polystyrene (PS) samples compared to control (CT) samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOGRACILICIBACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudogracilicibacillus is a genus of Gram-positive bacteria that was over-represented in polystyrene (PS) samples, suggesting its involvement in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIRGIBACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Virgibacillus is a bacterial genus found to be significantly more abundant in polystyrene (PS) samples, suggesting its involvement in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Virgibacillus is a genus of Gram-positive bacteria that was over-represented in polystyrene (PS) samples, indicating its potential role in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Virgibacillus is a genus of bacteria that was overrepresented in PS samples, indicating its potential involvement in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPODERMA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypoderma is a genus of fungi that was overrepresented in polystyrene (PS) samples, suggesting its potential role in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGARICOMYCETES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Agaricomycetes is a class of fungi that includes an unidentified genus and Hyphodermella, which were overrepresented in PS samples, suggesting their involvement in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Agaricomycetes is a class of fungi that includes unidentified taxa overrepresented in polystyrene (PS) samples, suggesting their potential role in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Agaricomycetes is a class of fungi that is abundant in polystyrene-fed larvae, though no direct association with plastic degradation has been established.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SACCHAROMYCETES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Saccharomycetes is a class of fungi that was recorded among the predominant classes in both polystyrene (PS) and control (CT) samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SORDARIOMYCETES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sordariomycetes is a class of fungi that includes Trichoderma, which was overrepresented in PS samples, indicating its potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sordariomycetes is a class of fungi that was recorded among the predominant classes in both polystyrene (PS) and control (CT) samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DOTHIDEOMYCETES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dothideomycetes is a class of fungi that was recorded among the predominant classes in both polystyrene (PS) and control (CT) samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EUROTIOMYCETES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Eurotiomycetes is a class of fungi that was recorded among the predominant classes in both polystyrene (PS) and control (CT) samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEOTIOMYCETES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Leotiomycetes is a class of fungi that was recorded among the predominant classes in both polystyrene (PS) and control (CT) samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNIDENTIFIED FUNGI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Unidentified fungal taxa accounted for a significant portion of the fungal diversity in control (CT) samples, while being rare in polystyrene (PS) samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNIDENTIFIED AGARICOMYCETE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">An unidentified genus belonging to the Agaricomycetes class was overrepresented in PS samples, indicating its potential involvement in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOGRACILIBACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudogracilibacillus is a genus of bacteria that appeared to be overrepresented in PS samples, suggesting its potential role in the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A. DIAPERINUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A. diaperinus is a species of beetle whose gut microbiome is being studied for its potential role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, also known as the lesser mealworm, is a species of beetle whose larvae were studied for their gut microbial community when fed polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Alphitobius diaperinus, also known as the lesser mealworm, is an insect used in the study to evaluate its ability to survive and degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS SAMPLES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PS samples refer to the experimental group in the study where polystyrene was used as the primary food source for A. diaperinus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT SAMPLES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">CT samples refer to the control group in the study, which was used as a baseline for comparison with PS samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALPHA-DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Alpha-diversity refers to the diversity of microbial species within a sample, which was significantly lower in PS samples compared to CT samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BETA-DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Beta-diversity refers to the differences in microbial community composition between PS and CT samples, as highlighted by PCoA plots.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCOA PLOTS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PCoA (Principal Coordinate Analysis) plots were used to visualize differences in microbial community composition between PS and CT samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOGRACILLIBACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudogracilibacillus is a bacterial genus significantly more abundant in polystyrene (PS) samples, indicating a possible role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKANE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkane biodegradation refers to the breakdown of alkanes, facilitated by enzymes such as alkB, which is associated with polyethylene (PE) degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MALACHITE GREEN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Malachite green is a chemical compound used as a textile dye or antimicrobial in aquacultures, with a styrene core structure that may be relevant to polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Malachite green is a chemical compound with a styrene core structure, degraded by Kocuria rosea, suggesting a potential link to polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Malachite green is a synthetic dye and environmental pollutant that can be biodegraded by microorganisms like Kocuria rosea MTCC 1532.&lt;SEP&gt;Malachite green is a synthetic dye that can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms, such as Kocuria rosea MTCC 1532.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KOCURIA ROSEA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kocuria rosea is a bacterium capable of degrading malachite green, a chemical compound with a styrene core structure, suggesting potential for polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGRICULTURAL PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Agricultural plastics are plastic materials used in farming, which can contribute to environmental pollution if not properly managed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA) are biodegradable plastics that can be degraded by certain microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRICHODERMA VIRIDE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Trichoderma viride is a fungal species known to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), indicating potential for polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Trichoderma viride is a fungus isolated from landfill soil that can degrade plastic, as reported by Munir et al. (2018).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PENICILLIUM VARIABILE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Penicillium variabile is a fungal species shown to mineralize polystyrene after long incubation periods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREGARINES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gregarines are commensals or parasites known to be present in the gut of Coleoptera, including A. diaperinus, and may influence their gut microbial community.&lt;SEP&gt;Gregarines are parasites found in the gut of Coleoptera, which may influence the gut microbiome and its role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VEGETABLE FOOD">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vegetable food is used as a control diet for A. diaperinus larvae in the study to compare the effects of polystyrene feeding on their gut microbial community.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAECES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Faeces of A. diaperinus larvae are analyzed to confirm plastic degradation and characterize the chemical reactions involved in the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL REACTORS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical reactors are proposed as a method to culture gut microbes from A. diaperinus larvae for synthetic polymer degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOLOGICAL STUDIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbiological studies are required to assess the culturing parameters of gut microbes from A. diaperinus larvae for synthetic polymer degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLOEOPHYLLUM TRABEUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gloeophyllum trabeum is a wood-rotting fungus capable of depolymerizing polystyrene sulfonate through hydroquinone-driven Fenton chemistry, as studied by Krueger et al. (2015).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS ORYZAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus oryzae is a fungus that produces a biodegradable plastic-degrading enzyme, as studied by Maeda et al. (2005).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE SULFONATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene sulfonate is a chemical compound that can be depolymerized by Gloeophyllum trabeum through hydroquinone-driven Fenton chemistry, as studied by Krueger et al. (2015).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROQUINONE-DRIVEN FENTON CHEMISTRY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroquinone-driven Fenton chemistry is a process used by Gloeophyllum trabeum to depolymerize polystyrene sulfonate, as studied by Krueger et al. (2015).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMALLY OXIDIZED HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermally oxidized high-density polyethylene is a type of plastic that can be degraded by Aspergillus niger, as reported by Mathur et al. (2011).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYCYCLIC AROMATIC HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) are a group of organic compounds that are persistent environmental pollutants, often found in soil and requiring remediation methods like ozonation and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OZONATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ozonation is a chemical process used in combination with biodegradation to remediate polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons in soil.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KOCURIA ROSEA MTCC 1532">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kocuria rosea MTCC 1532 is a bacterial strain capable of biodegrading malachite green, a synthetic dye and environmental pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TENEBRIO OBSCURA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio obscura, also known as dark mealworms, are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, a common plastic pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPERGILLUS SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aspergillus spp. are fungi isolated from polystyrene-polluted sites, capable of biodegrading plastic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPER WORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Super worms are another type of insect studied for their potential in biodegradation, particularly in relation to plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RICE PLANTHOPPERS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Rice planthoppers are insects that host yeast-like symbiotes capable of sterol biosynthesis, including cytochrome P450 sterol C-22 desaturase genes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANOBID BEETLES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Anobid beetles are insects that host yeast-like symbiotes involved in sterol biosynthesis, similar to rice planthoppers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYTOCHROME P450 STEROL C-22 DESATURASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cytochrome P450 sterol C-22 desaturase is an enzyme involved in sterol biosynthesis, found in yeast-like symbiotes of rice planthoppers and anobid beetles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOME CENSUS DATA">
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
      <data key="d1">Phyloseq is an R package designed for the analysis and visualization of microbiome census data, as developed by McMurtrie and Holmes (2013).</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYLENE VINYL ACETATE (EVA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethylene vinyl acetate (EVA) is a copolymer used in foam plastics, which can be ingested and degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, though it has the most adverse effects on larval survival among the tested plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene vinyl acetate (EVA) is a plastic that undergoes limited depolymerization when consumed by yellow mealworms, as confirmed by FTIR, TGA, and GPC analyses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLADOSPORIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cladosporium is a genus of fungi that was found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae and has been reported to have plastic degradation abilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC POLYMERIZATION TYPES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic polymerization types refer to the different chemical structures of plastics, such as polypropylene (PP), polyurethane (PU), and ethylene vinyl acetate (EVA), which influence their biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) is a linear hydrocarbon resin with the chemical formula (C2H4)n, forming three basic chain structures: atactic, isotactic, and syndiotactic. It is a major source of plastic solid waste and environmental pollutants like microplastics and nanoplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a type of plastic that can be ingested and biodegraded by yellow mealworms, with specific gut fungi like Rhodotorula and Cryptococcus associated with its degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a type of plastic used as a feedstock in the experiment to assess its consumption and potential biodegradation by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PU (POLYURETHANE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyurethane (PU) is a complex plastic composed of polyisocyanates and polyols, which is biodegraded by yellow mealworms, with higher fungal community richness observed in PU-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PU) is a plastic material used in the experiment to evaluate its consumption and potential biodegradation by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EVA (ETHYLENE-VINYL ACETATE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA) is a copolymer plastic used in the experiment, which showed adverse effects on the survival of yellow mealworms, indicating potential toxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA) is a copolymer plastic with a complex structure that can be biodegraded by yellow mealworms, though it has adverse effects on larval weight and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The starvation group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae that are not fed, used to study the effects of nutrient deprivation on survival and PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The starvation group consists of yellow mealworms deprived of food to study the effects of nutritional deprivation on their survival and weight changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The starvation group refers to Tenebrio molitor larvae that were not fed any food, used as a control to compare the effects of microplastic ingestion on growth and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;The starvation group refers to Tenebrio molitor larvae deprived of food, used to study the effects of nutrient deprivation on their growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ITS GENE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The ITS gene is used in PCR amplification to study the fungal component of the gut microbiome in yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRISM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Prism (version 8) is a software tool used for statistical analysis of larval survival rates and plastic consumption rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAL WEIGHT CHANGES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Larval weight changes are observed in yellow mealworms fed with different plastics, indicating the limited energy provided by a sole plastic diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS PELLETS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass pellets are the excrement of yellow mealworms, which provide evidence of plastic degradation through oxidation and depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MORAXELLACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Moraxellaceae is a family of bacteria that increases in PP-fed yellow mealworms, suggesting its involvement in PP degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Moraxellaceae is a family of bacteria within the Gammaproteobacteria class, identified as one of the groups capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PORPHYROMONADACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Porphyromonadaceae is a family of bacteria that increases in EVA-fed yellow mealworms, suggesting its role in EVA degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EVA">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">EVA (Ethylene-Vinyl Acetate) is a copolymer used in various applications, including footwear, packaging, and adhesives, due to its flexibility and transparency.&lt;SEP&gt;EVA (Ethylene-vinyl acetate) is a type of plastic that yellow mealworms can consume, leading to changes in their gut bacterial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The PP-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with polypropylene (PP), showing significant changes in gut bacterial communities compared to the bran-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EVA-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The EVA-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA), showing significant changes in gut bacterial communities compared to the bran-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYGONOMONAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dygonomonas is a specific genus in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, strongly associated with the biodegradation of ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYLENE-VINYL ACETATE (EVA)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA) is a type of plastic copolymer that is degraded by specific gut bacteria in yellow mealworms, such as Dygonomonas.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MORTIERELLOMYCOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mortierellomycota is one of the predominant fungal phyla in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, involved in the degradation of polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPORIDIOBOLACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sporidiobolaceae is a dominant fungal family in plastic-fed yellow mealworms, associated with plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TREMELLACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Tremellaceae is a dominant fungal family in plastic-fed yellow mealworms, associated with plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AUREOBASIDIACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aureobasidiaceae is a dominant fungal family in PU-fed yellow mealworms, associated with plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPOROBACTERIACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sporobacteriaceae is a dominant fungal family in EVA-fed yellow mealworms, showing a significant increase in abundance compared to bran-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPHOPICHA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyphopicha is a fungal genus dominant in bran-fed yellow mealworms, but its abundance decreases significantly in plastic-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYOLEFIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyolefin is a type of plastic, including polypropylene (PP) and polyethylene (PE), that can be biodegraded by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COPOLYMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Copolymer plastics, such as ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA), are complex plastics that can be biodegraded by yellow mealworms, though with varying effects on larval health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLADOSPORIUM SP. P7">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cladosporium sp. P7 is a fungal strain capable of biodegrading polyester polyurethane, demonstrating its potential in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALICYCLIPHILUS SP. BQB">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Alcycliphilus sp. BQB is a bacterial strain studied for its ability to biodegrade adipate/phthalate polyester polyurethanes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADIPATE/PHTHALATE POLYESTER POLYURETHANES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Adipate/phthalate polyester polyurethanes are a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by specific bacterial strains, such as Alcycliphilus sp. BQB.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CULTURE-DEPENDENT METHODS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Culture-dependent methods are used to study gut-associated bacteria in organisms like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DGGE METHODS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DGGE (Denaturing Gradient Gel Electrophoresis) methods are used to analyze the diversity of gut-associated bacteria in organisms like Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORE GUT BACTERIAL MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The core gut bacterial microbiome refers to the stable community of bacteria in the digestive tracts of organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas aratus, involved in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORE GUT FUNGAL MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The core gut fungal microbiome refers to the stable community of fungi in the digestive tracts of organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas aratus, involved in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH PURITY MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">High purity microplastics are small plastic particles used in studies to investigate the biodegradation mechanisms of plastics by mealworms and other organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PP foam is a form of polypropylene used as a control in biodegradation studies, representing a common environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;PP foam is a form of polypropylene used in the study as a sole diet for Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae to assess biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH THROUGHPUT 16S RRNA SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">High throughput 16S rRNA sequencing is a method used to analyze the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae during polypropylene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE (POLYETHYLENE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) is a common plastic polymer that, like polypropylene, is non-hydrolyzable and hydrophobic, making it resistant to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS (POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene (PS) is a petroleum-based plastic that is generally considered non-biodegradable due to its high molecular weight and stable structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOPARAFFIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Isoparaffin is a type of hydrocarbon with branched side chains, making it more difficult to biodegrade compared to linear n-paraffin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-PARAFFIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">N-paraffin is a linear hydrocarbon that is more easily biodegraded compared to isoparaffin due to its simpler structure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UV-PRE-TREATED PP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">UV-pre-treated polypropylene (PP) refers to PP films that have been exposed to ultraviolet light to enhance their biodegradability by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIBRIO SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Vibrio sp. is a bacterial strain involved in the biodegradation of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) in selective enrichment cultures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SULFATE-REDUCING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sulfate-reducing bacteria are microorganisms that contribute to the biodegradation of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) in selective enrichment cultures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARTHAM AND DOBLE (2009)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Artham and Doble (2009) conducted research on the biodegradation of polypropylene in seawater, reporting minimal weight loss under natural conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARKATKAR ET AL. (2010)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Arkatkar et al. (2010) studied the biodegradation of UV-pre-treated polypropylene films using Bacillus and Pseudomonas strains, achieving up to 2.5% weight loss after one year.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CACCIARI ET AL. (1993)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cacciari et al. (1993) reported the highest removal efficiency of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) in a selective enrichment culture community, achieving 40% mass removal over 175 days.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEST MOTHS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pest moths, belonging to the order Lepidoptera, are insects that have been observed to chew and damage plastic packaging materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SNAILS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Snails are invertebrates that have been observed to ingest and retain microplastics in terrestrial environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPROPYLENE FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) foam is a type of plastic waste used as feedstock in biodegradation studies with Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene foam is a type of plastic material that is being studied for biodegradation by larvae such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECIFIC PP CONSUMPTION RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Specific PP consumption rate (SPPCR) is a metric used to measure the amount of polypropylene foam consumed by larvae per unit of time and per unit of larval weight.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DNA analysis is a method used to study the genetic material of larvae and their gut microbes to understand their role in the biodegradation of polypropylene foam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENTAMICIN SUPPRESSION TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The gentamicin suppression test is an experimental procedure used to assess the role of gut microbes in the biodegradation of polypropylene foam by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRUKER AVANCE NEO 600 SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bruker Avance NEO 600 spectrometer is an NMR instrument used to analyze the formation of oxidized functional groups in residual polymers extracted from frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bruker Avance NEO 600 spectrometer is an analytical instrument used to analyze residual polymers, such as LDPE, in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAST DNA SPIN EXTRACTION KITS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fast DNA SPIN extraction kits are used to extract total bacterial genomic DNA from gut tissues of larvae for microbial community analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RDP CLASSIFIER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">RDP Classifier is a tool used to assign taxonomy to 16S rRNA gene sequences by comparing them against the Silva 16S rRNA database.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANNIBALISM RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cannibalism rate (CR) is a metric used to assess the behavior of mealworms and superworms under nutrient-limited conditions, where they may consume dead worms or molts to survive.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT LIMITATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient limitation refers to the condition where mealworms and superworms lack essential nutrients, leading to reduced survival rates and increased cannibalism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIGESTIVE REAGENTS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Digestive reagents are substances produced by mealworms and superworms that aid in the breakdown and biodegradation of plastics like polypropylene and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BODY BIOMASS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Body biomass refers to the mass of mealworms and superworms, which can decrease when they consume plastics like polypropylene due to the lack of essential nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY SOURCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy source refers to the energy obtained by mealworms and superworms from the digestion of plastics like polypropylene, though it is insufficient for long-term survival without additional nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMAL MODIFICATIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermal modifications are changes in the thermal properties of polymers, such as polypropylene, after biodegradation, as observed in frass samples from Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYMATIC ATTACK">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic attack refers to the process by which enzymes break down polymers like polypropylene, facilitated by reduced hydrophobicity and surface modification.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOXYLIC ACID COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carboxylic acid compounds are degradation products formed during the biodegradation of polymers like polypropylene by larvae such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CH2 MOIETY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The CH2 moiety is formed during the breakdown of long-chain polypropylene (PP) polymers by T. molitor and Z. atratus, as observed in NMR spectra.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMINO ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Amino acids are identified as metabolites in the frass samples of T. molitor and Z. atratus, indicating the breakdown of polypropylene (PP) during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Amino acids are residues found in the frass of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus after biodegradation of LDPE, indicating the breakdown of proteins during the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKENE BONDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkene bonds (C=C-H) are observed in the frass samples of Z. atratus, confirming the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP).&lt;SEP&gt;Alkene bonds (C=CH) are chemical structures identified in the residual polymers of frass, indicating chemical modifications during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRINCIPAL COMPONENT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Principal component analysis (PCA) is a statistical technique used to analyze the gut microbial community structure of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae under different diet conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WB-FED Z">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">WB-fed Z refers to an organism or group of organisms fed with wheat bran, used in studies to investigate microbial activity and biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP-WB-FED Z">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PP-WB-fed Z refers to an organism or group of organisms co-fed with polypropylene and wheat bran, used to study the combined effects on biodegradation and microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP-WB-FED T">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PP-WB-fed T refers to an organism or group of organisms co-fed with polypropylene and wheat bran, used in biodegradation studies to assess microbial responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP-FED T">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PP-fed T refers to an organism or group of organisms fed with polypropylene, used in studies to investigate the biodegradation of synthetic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP+WB-FED T">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PP+WB-fed T refers to an organism or group of organisms co-fed with polypropylene and wheat bran, used to study the combined effects on biodegradation and microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WB ASSOCIATED">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">WB Associated refers to microbial communities or organisms associated with wheat bran, used in studies to investigate biodegradation and microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP ASSOCIATED">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">PP Associated refers to microbial communities or organisms associated with polypropylene, used in studies to investigate biodegradation and microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLYUYVERA SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klyuyvera sp. is a bacterial genus predominant in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed polypropylene, potentially playing a role in PP biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORYNEBACTERIUM 1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Corynebacterium 1 is a bacterial genus present in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, possibly contributing to the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUSOBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fusobacterium is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, potentially involved in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEBALDELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Sebaldella is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, potentially involved in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPC ANALYSES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">GPC (Gel Permeation Chromatography) analyses were used to study the depolymerization patterns of polypropylene during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DARKLING BEETLE LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Darkling beetle larvae, including Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, are studied for their ability to biodegrade polypropylene (PP) and other plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCONVERTERS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioconverters, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, are organisms that can transform plastic waste like polypropylene (PP) into simpler compounds through biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOTECHNOLOGIES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Biotechnologies are being developed to utilize organisms like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus for the remediation and recycling of plastic waste, including polypropylene (PP).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LAND-FILL LEACHATE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Landfill leachate is a liquid that forms as water percolates through waste in landfills, often containing microplastics and other pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PETROLEUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Petroleum is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons that can be degraded and mineralized by certain bacteria, contributing to environmental cleanup efforts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COASTAL WATERS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Coastal waters are environments where petroleum-degrading bacteria have been isolated, highlighting their role in bioremediation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGROVE SEDIMENT">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mangrove sediment is a habitat where bacteria capable of degrading polypropylene microplastics, such as Bacillus sp. and Rhodococcus sp., have been isolated.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMAN DISTAL GUT MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The human distal gut microbiome consists of microorganisms that play a role in digestion and have been studied for their metabolic capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;The human distal gut microbiome is a complex microbial community that has been studied for its role in health and disease, as well as its potential applications in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIBER REINFORCED COMPOSITES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fiber-reinforced composite materials are substrates where microbial growth has been studied, indicating their susceptibility to biodeterioration.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUNICIPAL SOLID WASTE (MSW) LANDFILL">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Municipal solid waste landfills are sources of microplastics, as evidenced by their presence in landfill leachate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLAR ISOTACTIC AND SYNDIOTACTIC POLYPROPYLENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">These are specialized forms of polypropylene synthesized through organozirconium-catalyzed copolymerization, with potential applications in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSECT GUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The insect gut microbiota plays a crucial role in ecology, evolution, and industrial applications, including biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MESOPHILIC BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mesophilic bacteria, such as those isolated for polypropylene biodegradation, thrive at moderate temperatures and contribute to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARCH-BASED POLYPROPYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Starch-based polypropylene is a modified form of polypropylene that has been studied for its microbial degradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARCH BASED POLYPROPYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Starch-based polypropylene is a type of plastic that can be degraded by microbial activity, as studied in the referenced research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAX MOTH LARVA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The greater wax moth larva (Galleria mellonella L.) is capable of biodegrading polyethylene and polystyrene, with its core gut microbiome playing a significant role.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTICS IN TERRESTRIAL ECOSYSTEMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">This study examines the implications of microplastics in terrestrial ecosystems, particularly their effects on organisms like Lumbricus terrestris.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PP CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
      <data key="d1">Co-diet bran enhances PP consumption rates in Z. atratus and T. molitor larvae, improving the efficiency of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL GROWTH">
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial growth on fiber-reinforced composite materials has been studied, indicating their susceptibility to biodeterioration.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER HORMAECHEI LG3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 is a PS-degrading bacterium isolated from the gut of mealworms, showing high anaerobic PS-degrading activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 is a bacterial strain with polystyrene (PS) degradation activity under both anaerobic and aerobic conditions, exhibiting biofilm formation and endotoxin synthesis pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 is a facultative anaerobic bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of oxidizing and depolymerizing polystyrene under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS AMYLOLIQUEFACIENS SCGB1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is a bacterial strain isolated from traditional Korean soybean-fermented foods, known to affect intestinal health in mice and capable of growing under anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is a bacterial strain used for comparison in the study, capable of degrading polystyrene under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is a facultative gut bacterium known for its influence on gut health and its ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is a gut commensal bacterium with PS degradation activity, though less effective than LG3 under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEGRADATIVE ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Degradative enzymes, such as thiol peroxidase (tpx), alkyl hydroperoxide reductase C (ahpC), and bacterioferritin comigratory protein (bcp), are involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene by Enterobacter hormaechei LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AQUACULTURE BUOYS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Aquaculture buoys made of expanded polystyrene are a significant source of microplastics in marine environments due to their vulnerability to abrasion and fragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM YT2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Exiguobacterium YT2 is a polystyrene-degrading bacterial strain previously isolated from the guts of mealworms, known for its ability to degrade polystyrene under aerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS STRAIN DSM 50071">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas strain DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain with potential abilities for polystyrene biodegradation, though further research is needed.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas strain DSM 50071 is a polystyrene-degrading bacterium isolated from the gut of Superworms (Zophobas atratus) larvae, capable of degrading polystyrene under aerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAEROBIC BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Anaerobic biodegradation refers to the breakdown of organic substances, such as polystyrene, by microorganisms in the absence of oxygen, a process demonstrated by Enterobacter hormaechei LG3.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic biodegradation refers to the breakdown of organic substances, such as polystyrene, by microorganisms in the absence of oxygen, involving oxidation and depolymerization processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID A">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid A is a component of the bacterial cell membrane that was observed to be upregulated in Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 during polystyrene biodegradation, indicating changes in cellular metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid A is a toxic component of endotoxins produced by opportunistic pathogens like Enterobacter hormaechei, and its biosynthesis is upregulated during polystyrene degradation by LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOFILM-ASSOCIATED PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biofilm-associated proteins are proteins involved in the formation of biofilms, which were upregulated in Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 during polystyrene biodegradation, facilitating the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THIOL PEROXIDASE (TPX)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thiol peroxidase (tpx) is a degradative enzyme identified in Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, involved in the breakdown of polystyrene during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALKYL HYDROPEROXIDE REDUCTASE C (AHPC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Alkyl hydroperoxide reductase C (ahpC) is a degradative enzyme identified in Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, playing a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkyl hydroperoxide reductase C (ahpC) is an enzyme involved in oxidative stress responses and upregulated during the biodegradation of polystyrene by E. hormaechei LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIOFERRITIN COMIGRATORY PROTEIN (BCP)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacterioferritin comigratory protein (bcp) is a degradative enzyme identified in Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, contributing to the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacterioferritin comigratory protein (bcp) is an enzyme upregulated in E. hormaechei LG3 during the biodegradation of polystyrene, playing a role in oxidative stress responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter spp. are bacteria found in the mealworm gut microbiota that play a significant role in the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter spp. are facultative gut bacteria isolated from mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROSPHERES PS (MI-PS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microspheres PS (Mi-PS) are small polystyrene particles used in studies to investigate biodegradation under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS FOAMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PS foams are expanded polystyrene materials that are commonly found as environmental pollutants and can be degraded by organisms like mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">PS films are thin layers of polystyrene used in studies to assess biodegradation by bacteria and organisms under various conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS EMULSION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS emulsion is a form of polystyrene prepared for experimental studies to evaluate biodegradation processes by bacteria and organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;PS emulsion is a mixture of polystyrene foams, dichloromethane, and sarkosyl NL, prepared for experimental use in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FACULTATIVE GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Facultative gut bacteria, such as Enterobacter spp. and Bacillus amyloliquefaciens, can degrade plastics like polystyrene (PS) under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROBIC CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Aerobic conditions are also tested in the study to compare the polystyrene degradation capabilities of bacterial strains with and without oxygen.&lt;SEP&gt;Aerobic conditions refer to environments with oxygen, where bacteria and organisms can degrade plastics like polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Aerobic conditions refer to environments with oxygen, where bacteria like Enterobacter LG3 and B. amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Aerobic conditions refer to environments with oxygen, where bacteria like strain SCGB1 and LG3 can degrade polystyrene, though with varying efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDOTOXIN SYNTHESIS PATHWAYS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Endotoxin synthesis pathways are metabolic processes in bacteria, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, that are upregulated during the degradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEGRADATIVE ENZYME GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Degradative enzyme genes, such as tpx, ahpC, and bcp, are upregulated in bacteria during the degradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHOLE GENOME SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Whole genome sequencing is a technique used to study the genetic makeup of bacteria, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, involved in polystyrene (PS) degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSCRIPTOMES SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Transcriptomes sequencing is used to analyze gene expression in bacteria, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, during the degradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELL VIABILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cell viability is a measure of the health and activity of bacteria, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, during polystyrene (PS) degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-PHASE MICROEXTRACTION/GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY—MASS SPECTROMETRY (SPME/GC—MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SPME/GC—MS is a technique used to detect metabolic differences in polystyrene (PS) degradation by bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE FOAMS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene foams are a type of plastic material used in the preparation of PS emulsions for experimental purposes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-CYSTEINE HYDROCHLORIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">L-cysteine hydrochloride is a component of the FMM medium used in biodegradation assays to support bacterial growth.&lt;SEP&gt;L-cysteine hydrochloride is a reducing agent used in fungal minimum medium (FMM) to enhance anaerobic conditions for microbial cultivation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGAL MINIMUM MEDIUM (FMM)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungal minimum medium (FMM) is a nutrient medium used for the cultivation of microorganisms under anaerobic conditions, containing components like sodium phosphate, magnesium sulfate, and L-cysteine hydrochloride.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAEROBIC CHAMBER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">An anaerobic chamber is a controlled environment used to cultivate anaerobic microorganisms, maintaining low oxygen levels and specific gas mixtures for experimental purposes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EARLY INSTAR LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Early instar larvae are young larval stages used in experiments to isolate PS-degrading bacteria from their gut and frass samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LATE INSTAR LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Late instar larvae are more mature larval stages used in experiments to isolate PS-degrading bacteria from their gut and frass samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRASONIC HOMOGENIZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An ultrasonic homogenizer is a device used to sonicate mixtures, such as the PS emulsion, to ensure proper mixing and dispersion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FILTER PAPER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Filter paper is used in the filtration process to separate PS particles from the emulsion and measure their weight.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT SAMPLES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut samples are collected from larvae to isolate PS-degrading bacteria, which play a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LURIA-BERTANI (LB) AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Luria-Bertani (LB) agar is a nutrient medium used to culture and isolate bacteria, including PS-degrading strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALLADIUM CATALYST">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A palladium catalyst is used in the anaerobic chamber to remove traces of oxygen by reacting with hydrogen, maintaining anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A palladium catalyst is used to remove traces of oxygen (O₂) from the chamber's atmosphere by reacting with hydrogen (H₂) during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROGEN SULFIDE REMOVAL COLUMN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A hydrogen sulfide removal column is used in the anaerobic chamber to eliminate H₂S gas, ensuring a stable environment for microbial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EG1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">EG1 is a strain of PS-degrading bacteria isolated from the gut of early instar larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EF1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">EF1 is a strain of PS-degrading bacteria isolated from the frass of early instar larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LG1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">LG1 is a strain of PS-degrading bacteria isolated from the gut of late instar larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LF1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">LF1 is a strain of PS-degrading bacteria isolated from the frass of late instar larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">A biodegradation assay is a method used to evaluate the ability of microorganisms to degrade materials such as polystyrene under controlled conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROGEN SULFIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrogen sulfide (H₂S) is a chemical compound removed from the chamber's atmosphere using a hydrogen sulfide removal column during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGEN (O₂)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygen (O₂) is a gas whose traces are removed from the chamber's atmosphere using a palladium catalyst and hydrogen (H₂) during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROGEN (H₂)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrogen (H₂) is used to remove traces of oxygen (O₂) from the chamber's atmosphere and maintain a homogeneous mix of gases during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FMM AGAR PLATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FMM agar plates are used to culture and identify bacteria that form clear zones, indicating their ability to degrade specific materials.&lt;SEP&gt;FMM agar plates are used to culture and study the biodegradation capabilities of bacteria, such as LG3 and SCGB1, on polystyrene films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16 S RRNA GENE SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">16 S rRNA gene sequencing is a method used to identify and phylogenetically analyze bacteria isolated from mealworms and other sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLAST DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The BLAST database, created by the National Center for Biotechnology Information, is used to align and compare 16 S rRNA gene sequences for phylogenetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEIGHBOR-JOINING METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The neighbor-joining method is a computational algorithm used to construct phylogenetic trees based on genetic data.&lt;SEP&gt;The neighbor-joining method is a phylogenetic analysis technique used to construct evolutionary trees based on 16 S rRNA gene sequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KIMURA TWO-PARAMETER MODEL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Kimura two-parameter model is a statistical method used in phylogenetic analysis to estimate evolutionary distances between sequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAGNESIUM SULFATE HEPTAHYDRATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Magnesium sulfate heptahydrate is a component of the FMM medium used in biodegradation assays to provide essential nutrients for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SODIUM PHOSPHATE MONOBASIC">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sodium phosphate monobasic is a component of the FMM medium used in biodegradation assays to provide essential nutrients for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MONOPOTASSIUM PHOSPHATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Monopotassium phosphate is a component of the FMM medium used in biodegradation assays to provide essential nutrients for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOLID-PHASE MICROEXTRACTION GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY (SPME/GC—MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Solid-phase microextraction gas chromatography (SPME/GC—MS) is a technique used for mass spectrometry analysis in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MI-PS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Mi-PS refers to microplastic particles derived from polystyrene, which are studied for their environmental impact and biodegradation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Mi-PS refers to microplastic polystyrene, a type of environmental pollutant studied for its biodegradation by bacterial strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Mi-PS refers to microplastic polystyrene, which is a form of polystyrene broken down into smaller particles, often studied for its biodegradation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Mi-PS refers to microplastic polystyrene, which is used in experiments to study bacterial degradation under aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FMM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">FMM is a medium supplemented with Mi-PS, used to study bacterial degradation of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN LG3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain LG3 is a bacterial strain used in whole genome sequence analysis to study its potential role in microplastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Strain LG3 is a bacterium isolated from the gut of mealworms, exhibiting high anaerobic polystyrene (PS)-degrading activity and capable of biodegrading PS under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CH4">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methane (CH4) is a gas produced during bacterial degradation processes, detected using gas chromatography.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHOLE GENOME SEQUENCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Whole genome sequence analysis is conducted to understand the genetic potential of Strain LG3 in degrading microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SW">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">SW refers to seawater, which is used as a medium for resuspending bacterial cell suspensions in the study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2,3-TRICHLOROPROPENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2,3-Trichloropropene is used as an internal standard for quantitative analysis in gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIST/EPA/NICHD MASS SPECTRAL LIBRARY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The NIST/EPA/NICHD Mass Spectral Library is a database used for identifying spectral clusters in GC-MS analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDD DETECTOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The PDD detector is a component of the gas chromatography system used to detect methane (CH4) and carbon dioxide (CO2).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR SIEVE 13X">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular sieve 13X is a column material used in gas chromatography for separating gases like CH4 and CO2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PORAPAK-N">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Porapak-N is a column material used in gas chromatography for separating gases like CH4 and CO2.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGENE™ GENOMIC DNA PREP KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The HiGene™ Genomic DNA Prep Kit is used for extracting genomic DNA from bacterial strains like LG3 for whole genome sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXFORD NANOPORE TECHNOLOGIES (ONT)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxford Nanopore Technologies provides protocols and tools for whole genome sequencing, used in the analysis of Strain LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLYE ASSEMBLER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Flye assembler is a tool used for assembling whole genome sequences from sequencing reads.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROKKA">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Prokka is a gene prediction tool used for annotating the genome of Strain LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER SP. LG3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter sp. LG3 is a bacterial strain capable of forming biofilms and degrading polystyrene (PS) films under anaerobic conditions, demonstrating significant biodegradation activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter sp. LG3 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworms that exhibits high anaerobic PS-degrading activity, forming clear zones on ePS-containing agar plates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROSPHERES POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microspheres polystyrene are small plastic particles used in the study to assess the degradation capabilities of bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MALTOSE MONOHYDRATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Maltose monohydrate is a sugar used in the FMM medium as a control substrate for bacterial growth and comparison with polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER GENUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The Enterobacter genus includes bacterial strains isolated from mealworms, some of which exhibit polystyrene-degrading capabilities under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLEAR ZONES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Clear zones on agar plates indicate areas where bacterial strains have degraded polystyrene, serving as a visual indicator of biodegradation activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAVIMETRIC DETERMINATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gravimetric determination is a method used to measure the weight loss of polystyrene films, indicating the extent of biodegradation by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAIN SCGB1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Strain SCGB1 is a bacterium belonging to the species Bacillus amyloliquefaciens, known for its ability to degrade polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) under aerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E. HORMAECHEI LG3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">E. hormaechei LG3 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of PS-fed mealworms, capable of oxidizing and depolymerizing polystyrene (PS) under both anaerobic and aerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;E. hormaechei LG3 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B. AMYLOLIQUEFACIENS SCGB1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">B. amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is a bacterial strain known for its ability to degrade polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) under aerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;B. amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is a bacterial strain used as a comparative control in studies involving the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;B. amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is a gut commensal bacterium with reduced polystyrene degradation activity under anaerobic conditions but produces specific metabolites under aerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is a bacterial strain tested for its ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) films under anaerobic conditions, showing limited activity compared to Enterobacter sp. LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT REDUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight reduction is a key indicator of biodegradation, measured by the loss of mass in polystyrene films when exposed to degrading bacteria like LG3 and SCGB1.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CFU CONCENTRATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">CFU (colony-forming unit) concentration is used to measure the density of bacterial cells, such as LG3 and SCGB1, when utilizing polystyrene as a carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STERILE CONTROL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The sterile control refers to a condition in the study where no bacterial inoculation was performed, used as a baseline to compare the biodegradation activity of bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE-SEM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FE-SEM (Field Emission Scanning Electron Microscopy) is a technique used to visualize the surface morphology of microplastics and their degradation by bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) is a technique used in the study to observe the morphology of bacterial cells and the surface of polystyrene (PS) films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gas chromatography (GC) analysis is a method used to measure the concentration of CO2 produced during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by Enterobacter sp. LG3 under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEADSPACE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Headspace refers to the gaseous phase in the incubation bottle, where CO2 concentrations were measured to assess the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by Enterobacter sp. LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPORE SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanopore sequencing is a genomic analysis method used to identify key enzymes and genes involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene by E. hormaechei LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEROXIDASE (EFEB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peroxidase (efeB) is an enzyme upregulated in E. hormaechei LG3 during the biodegradation of polystyrene, playing a role in breaking down aryl alcohol compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROLASE (UBI GENES)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrolase (ubi genes) are enzymes involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly in the breakdown of aryl alcohol compounds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THIOL PEROXIDASE (PXC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thiol peroxidase (pxc) is an enzyme upregulated in E. hormaechei LG3 during the biodegradation of polystyrene, contributing to oxidative stress responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID A SYNTHESIS PATHWAY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The lipid A synthesis pathway involves enzymes upregulated in E. hormaechei LG3 during the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating metabolic changes during the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAGELLAR PROTEIN-CODING GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flagellar protein-coding genes, such as ycgR, flgH, and flp, are downregulated in E. hormaechei LG3 during the biodegradation of polystyrene, suggesting reduced motility during the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOLYTIC PATHWAY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The glycolytic pathway involves enzymes downregulated in E. hormaechei LG3 during the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating metabolic shifts during the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WCA VALUES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">WCA (Water Contact Angle) values are used to measure changes in the hydrophobicity of polystyrene films after exposure to bacterial strains like E. hormaechei LG3.&lt;SEP&gt;WCA (water contact angle) values are measured to assess changes in the hydrophobicity of polystyrene during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPME/GC-MS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SPME/GC-MS (Solid Phase Microextraction/Gas Chromatography-Mass Spectrometry) analysis is used to identify hydroxyl compounds produced during the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;SPME/GC-MS analysis is a method used to identify hydroxyl compounds and other degradation products during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALIPHATIC AND AROMATIC ALCOHOLS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aliphatic and aromatic alcohols are compounds identified during the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating the breakdown of the polymer into smaller molecules.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER LG3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter LG3 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROXYL COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroxyl compounds are degradation products identified via SPME/GC-MS analysis during the biodegradation of polystyrene by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CO2 EMISSION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CO2 emission is a byproduct of polystyrene biodegradation, measured to assess the mineralization process by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE-SEM IMAGE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">FE-SEM imaging is used to visualize the physical changes in Mi-PS and bacterial cells during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DENITRIFICATION ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Denitrification enzymes are expressed in Enterobacter LG3 during the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating the use of nitrate as an electron acceptor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHYL 2-HYDROXYBENZOATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methyl 2-hydroxybenzoate is a secondary product of polystyrene degradation produced by Enterobacter LG3 under anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Methyl 2-hydroxybenzoate is an intermediate in the anaerobic biodegradation pathways of polystyrene, indicating shared mechanisms with styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEROXIREDOXINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peroxiredoxins are enzymes that protect against oxidative stress and are upregulated in LG3 during the degradation of polystyrene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Peroxiredoxins are enzymes upregulated in Enterobacter LG3 during the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene, contributing to the degradation of oxidized lipid molecules.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAEROBIC RESPIRATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Anaerobic respiration is a process used by Enterobacter LG3 to degrade polystyrene under oxygen-limited conditions, utilizing nitrate as an electron acceptor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROBIC RESPIRATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Aerobic respiration is a process used by bacterial strains like B. amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 to degrade polystyrene in the presence of oxygen.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOBUTYRIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Isobutyric acid is a specific metabolite produced by B. amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 during the aerobic biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOVALERIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Isovaleric acid is a specific metabolite produced by B. amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 during the aerobic biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDIZED PS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidized PS refers to polystyrene molecules that have undergone oxidation, which are further degraded by bacterial strains like Enterobacter LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LG3">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">LG3 is a PS-degradable bacterium that exhibits anaerobic biodegradation capabilities, including oxidation and depolymerization of polystyrene, and plays a role in biofilm formation and lipid metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTICS (MI-PS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics (Mi-PS) are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, which can be degraded by microorganisms like LG3 under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUAIACOL PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Guaiacol peroxidase is an enzyme involved in the oxidation of aryl alcohol compounds during the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene by LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYPHENOL OXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyphenol oxidase is an enzyme that plays a role in the oxidation of aryl alcohol compounds during the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene by LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2-PHENYLACETYL-COA EPOXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2-Phenylacetyl-CoA epoxidase is an enzyme involved in the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene, part of the paa cluster in LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-OCTAPRENYLPHENOL HYDROXYLASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Octaprenylphenol hydroxylase is an enzyme involved in the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene, part of the ubi cluster in LG3.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNA-SEQ ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">RNA-seq analysis is a method used to study gene expression changes in LG3 during the degradation of polystyrene microplastics, revealing alterations in metabolic pathways and physiological activities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM STRAIN YT2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Exiguobacterium strain YT2 is a bacterial strain known for its ability to degrade polystyrene, contributing to plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAEROBIC PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Anaerobic plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms capable of degrading plastics, such as polystyrene, under anaerobic conditions, offering potential bioremediation solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEROXIREDOXIN FAMILY ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peroxiredoxin family enzymes are proteins whose expression levels are affected by Mi-PS treatment, indicating their role in oxidative stress response.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPID A SYNTHESIS PATHWAY PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipid A synthesis pathway proteins are involved in the biosynthesis of lipid A, a component of lipopolysaccharides, and their expression is altered by Mi-PS treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLAGELLAR-ASSOCIATED PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Flagellar-associated proteins are involved in bacterial motility, and their expression levels are influenced by Mi-PS treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOLYSIS PATHWAY ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycolysis pathway enzymes are involved in energy metabolism, and their expression is affected by Mi-PS treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER AEROGENES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter aerogenes is a bacterial strain studied for its ability to utilize formate and nitrate for semi-anaerobic production of isobutanol.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS AMYLOLIQUEFACIENS SCQ1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCQ1 is a bacterial strain that alleviates colitis in mice through immune regulation, demonstrating its potential as a probiotic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIMOSILACTOBACILLUS FERMENTUM JNUS32">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Limosilactobacillus fermentum JNUS32 is a probiotic candidate with potential applications in functional food and feed supplements.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE E7">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter asburiae E7 is a novel probiotic strain that enhances resistance to Aeromonas veronii infection in common carp by stimulating the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM BUOY DEBRIS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam buoy debris is a specific type of polystyrene foam waste that poses challenges in marine environments and requires innovative solutions for management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYPRINUS CARPIO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Cyprinus carpio, commonly known as common carp, is a freshwater fish species studied for its immune response to infections.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER STRAIN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter strain is a nitrate-reducing bacterium that contributes to iron cycling in environmental systems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER LUDWIGII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter ludwigii is a bacterial strain that protects against colitis through choline-mediated immune tolerance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FISHMEAL">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Fishmeal is a protein source used in aquaculture, but it is threatened by the presence of microplastics, posing risks to human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITRATE REDUCERS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrate reducers are microorganisms that play a role in iron cycling, as indicated by studies involving Enterobacter strains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER HORMACHECHEI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterial species with subspecies that have been studied for their genomic characteristics and environmental roles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER ROGENKAMPII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter rogenkampii is a newly identified bacterial species based on computational analysis of sequenced genomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS SAFENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus safensis is another bacterial strain investigated for its ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER WUHOUENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter wuhouensis is a bacterial species recovered from human sputum, contributing to microbial diversity studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBACTER QUASIHORMAECHEI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter quasihormaechei is a bacterial species identified from human sputum, adding to the understanding of microbial ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-CYSTEINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">L-cysteine is a chemical used in microbial electrolysis cells to study hydrogen production and its influence on microbial community shifts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL ELECTROLYSIS CELLS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial electrolysis cells are systems used to study hydrogen production and microbial community dynamics, often involving chemicals like L-cysteine.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANOPORE SEQUENCING TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanopore sequencing technology is a method used for bioinformatics and applications, enabling advanced genetic and microbial research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOBUTANOL">
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter aerogenes utilizes formate and nitrate for the semi-anaerobic production of isobutanol, demonstrating its metabolic capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLITIS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCQ1 alleviates dextran sulfate sodium-induced colitis in mice through immune regulation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROBIOTIC">
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
      <data key="d1">Limosilactobacillus fermentum JNUS32 is a probiotic candidate with potential applications in functional food and feed supplements.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROMONAS VERONII">
      <data key="d2">chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter asburiae E7 enhances resistance to Aeromonas veronii infection in common carp by stimulating the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMMUNE RESPONSE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
      <data key="d1">Cyprinus carpio is studied for its immune response to infections, highlighting its role in immunological research.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IRON CYCLING">
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter strain contributes to iron cycling through nitrate reduction, indicating its role in environmental processes.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMMUNE TOLERANCE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter ludwigii protects against colitis by mediating immune tolerance through choline.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VINYL PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Vinyl plastics, such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene, are non-hydrolyzable plastics that mealworms can degrade despite being nitrogen-deficient.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE-BUTADIENE RUBBER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR) is a synthetic vinyl plastic that mealworms can consume and degrade, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETYLENE REDUCTION ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetylene reduction assays are used to measure nitrogen-fixing activity in mealworms, demonstrating their ability to fix atmospheric nitrogen.&lt;SEP&gt;The acetylene reduction assay is a method used to measure nitrogen-fixing activity in mealworms by detecting the conversion of acetylene to ethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;The acetylene reduction assay is a method used to measure nitrogen-fixing activity in mealworms fed with Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;The acetylene reduction assay is a method used to measure nitrogenase activity in nitrogen-fixing bacteria by detecting ethylene production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN ISOTOPIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen isotopic analysis provides evidence for the assimilation of fixed nitrogen by mealworms, confirming their ability to overcome nitrogen limitation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UP-RECYCLING">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Up-recycling refers to the process of converting plastic waste into valuable resources, such as protein sources, through the biodegradation capabilities of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE NITROGEN ISOTOPE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable nitrogen isotope analysis is used to determine nitrogen fixation activity in mealworms by analyzing nitrogen isotope compositions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEIJERINCKIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Beijerinckia is a genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria that has been isolated from the guts of various nitrogen-fixing insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAOULTIELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Raoultiella is a genus of bacteria that has been isolated from the guts of nitrogen-fixing insects and may play a role in nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella spp. are bacteria belonging to the Proteobacteria phylum and are known potential nitrogen-fixing bacteria found in the gut of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROBAACTER SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter spp. are bacteria belonging to the Proteobacteria phylum and are known potential nitrogen-fixing bacteria found in the gut of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANTOEA SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pantoea spp. are bacteria belonging to the Proteobacteria phylum and are known potential nitrogen-fixing bacteria found in the gut of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYROSEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrosequencing is a culture-independent method used to analyze the composition of gut bacterial communities in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AZOSPIRILLUM BRASILENSE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Azospirillum brasilense is a known free-living nitrogen-fixing bacterium used as a positive control in acetylene-reducing assays.&lt;SEP&gt;Azospirillum brasilense is a nitrogen-fixing bacterium that forms aggregates in response to chemical and physical factors, playing a role in nitrogen metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Azospirillum brasilense is a well-known nitrogen-fixing bacterium used as a positive control in acetylene reduction assays.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGENASE (NIFH) GENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The nitrogenase (nifH) gene is a genetic marker used to identify nitrogen-fixing bacteria through PCR amplification and sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEMI-SOLID NITROGEN-FREE MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Semi-solid nitrogen-free media is a growth medium used to culture and isolate nitrogen-fixing bacteria from mealworm guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Semi-solid nitrogen-free media is used to culture nitrogen-fixing bacteria isolated from mealworm guts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN (N&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt;)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen (N&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) is a chemical element used in isotopic analysis and as a reference for nitrogen isotope compositions in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOSIEVE G COLUMN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbosieve G column is a material used to separate nitrogen (N&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) in a helium carrier stream for isotopic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOTOPIC RATIO MASS SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An isotopic ratio mass spectrometer is an instrument used to measure the isotopic composition of elements, such as nitrogen, in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMMONIUM SULFATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ammonium sulfate is a chemical compound used as a working standard for calibrating nitrogen isotope measurements.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IAEA N1 AND N3">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">IAEA N1 and N3 are international isotope standards used for calibration in nitrogen isotope analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STERILE PBS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sterile phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) is used to rinse and prepare gut microbial inoculum from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LURIA–BERTANI (LB) AGAR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Luria–Bertani (LB) agar is a growth medium used to obtain pure cultures of nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetylene is an unsaturated compound used in the acetylene reduction assay to measure nitrogen-fixing activity in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Acetylene is used in acetylene reduction assays to measure nitrogenase activity in nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethylene is a gas produced during acetylene reduction assays, indicating nitrogenase activity in nitrogen-fixing bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethylene is a product of the acetylene reduction assay, indicating nitrogen-fixing activity in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR MIXTURES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PCR mixtures are used for the amplification of the nitrogenase (nifH) gene from nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENEJET GEL EXTRACTION KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A kit used for purifying DNA fragments from agarose gels after electrophoresis.&lt;SEP&gt;GeneJET Gel Extraction Kit is used to purify PCR products for sequencing nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABI 3730XL GENETIC ANALYZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A genetic analyzer used for nucleotide sequencing, providing high-throughput DNA sequencing capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;ABI 3730XL genetic analyzer is an instrument used for nucleotide sequencing of PCR products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAE BUFFER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">TAE buffer is a solution used in electrophoresis to separate DNA fragments based on size.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIANAMP STOOL DNA KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A kit used for extracting DNA from stool samples, including gut microbial contents.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NANODROP ONE MICROVOLUME UV-VIS SPECTROPHOTOMETER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A spectrophotometer used to measure the concentration of DNA and RNA samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M5 TAQ HIFI PCR MIX">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A PCR mix used for high-fidelity DNA amplification, ensuring accurate replication of DNA sequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PJET1.2/BLUNT CLONING VECTOR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A cloning vector used for ligating PCR products and transforming them into E. coli for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESCHERICHIA COLI XL-10-GOLD CELLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A strain of E. coli used for cloning and transforming DNA fragments for sequencing and analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIANPREP MINI PLASMID KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A kit used for isolating and purifying plasmid DNA from bacterial cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIZOL™ REAGENT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A reagent used for extracting total RNA from biological samples, including gut microbial contents.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAPIDOUT DNA REMOVAL KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A kit used to remove DNA contamination from RNA samples, ensuring pure RNA for downstream applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GOSCRIPT™ REVERSE TRANSCRIPTION SYSTEM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A system used for reverse transcription of RNA into cDNA for further PCR amplification.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLONEJET PCR CLONING KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A kit used for cloning PCR products into a vector for transformation and sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-FIXING ISOLATE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A bacterial isolate from the gut of mealworms that plays a role in nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC-TREATED MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic-treated mealworms are larvae whose gut microbiota has been suppressed by antibiotics, leading to reduced nitrogen-fixing activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic-treated mealworms are mealworms whose gut bacteria have been suppressed using antibiotics like gentamicin, affecting their nitrogen-fixing activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotic-treated mealworms are mealworms whose gut microbiota has been suppressed, leading to reduced nitrogen-fixing capabilities and fitness.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms treated with antibiotics to suppress gut microbiota, used to study the role of gut bacteria in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE NITROGEN ISOTOPES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable nitrogen isotopes (δ15N) are used to analyze the incorporation of fixed nitrogen into mealworm tissues, providing evidence of nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-FIXING SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-fixing systems are biological processes that convert atmospheric nitrogen into a usable form, essential for the survival of mealworms and their ability to degrade Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM-EATING MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam-eating mealworms are larvae that consume Styrofoam and have gut bacteria capable of nitrogen fixation, contributing to their metabolism and biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam-eating mealworms are mealworms that have been observed consuming and degrading Styrofoam, a type of polystyrene, as part of their diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-FIXING ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-fixing activity refers to the process by which gut bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen into a form that can be utilized by mealworms, supporting their metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETYLENE-REDUCING ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetylene-reducing activity is a measure of nitrogen fixation, where acetylene is reduced to ethylene, indicating the presence of nitrogen-fixing bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Acetylene-reducing activity is a measure of nitrogen-fixing ability in bacteria, indicating their capacity to convert acetylene into ethylene as part of nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAN-FEEDING MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Bran-feeding mealworms are larvae fed with bran, showing higher δ15N values compared to Styrofoam-eating mealworms, indicating differences in nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIT-1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">BIT-1 is a nitrogen-fixing bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Styrofoam-eating mealworms, showing acetylene-reducing activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIT-56">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">BIT-56 is a nitrogen-fixing bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Styrofoam-eating mealworms, exhibiting a nitrogen-fixing rate comparable to Azospirillum brasilense.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIT-96">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">BIT-96 is a nitrogen-fixing bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Styrofoam-eating mealworms, showing significant acetylene-reducing activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIT-105">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">BIT-105 is a nitrogen-fixing bacterial strain isolated from the gut of Styrofoam-eating mealworms, with a relatively lower nitrogen-fixing rate compared to other isolates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-FREE MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-free medium is a growth medium used to isolate and cultivate nitrogen-fixing bacteria from the gut of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETYLENE-REDUCING ASSAY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetylene-reducing assay is a method used to measure nitrogen-fixing activity in bacteria by detecting the conversion of acetylene to ethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA SPALLANZANII BIT-96">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella spallanzanii BIT-96 is a nitrogen-fixing gut bacterium isolated from mealworms, which plays a crucial role in the mealworms' ability to survive on nitrogen-deficient diets like Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-DEFICIENT DIET">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">A nitrogen-deficient diet lacks sufficient nitrogen, an essential element for protein synthesis and other biological processes, which is a challenge for organisms like mealworms consuming Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA PHYLOTYPES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella phylotypes are specific genetic variants of the Klebsiella genus, identified in mealworm gut microbiota, which are involved in nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIFH-SPECIFIC PRIMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">NifH-specific primers are short DNA sequences used in PCR to amplify the nifH gene, which encodes dinitrogenase reductase, a key enzyme in nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-FIXING SYMBIONT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-fixing symbionts are microorganisms, such as Klebsiella, that form symbiotic relationships with their hosts, providing fixed nitrogen in exchange for nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNTHETIC MICROBIAL CONSORTIUM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A synthetic microbial consortium is a proposed solution to combine plastic degradation and nitrogen fixation, potentially upcycling plastic waste into cell proteins.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XYLOPHAGOUS INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Xylophagous insects are capable of supplementing their diets by fixing nitrogen from the atmosphere, inspiring research into similar capabilities in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA SP. BIT-96">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella sp. BIT-96 is a nitrogen-fixing bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms, contributing to nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIXTA SP. BIT-56">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixta sp. BIT-56 is a nitrogen-fixing bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms, involved in nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLUYVERA SP. BIT-1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Kluyvera sp. BIT-1 is a nitrogen-fixing bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms, contributing to nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITROBACTER SP. BIT-105">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrobacter sp. BIT-105 is a nitrogen-fixing bacterial strain isolated from the gut of mealworms, involved in nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACETYLENE REDUCTION ASSAYS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Acetylene reduction assays were used to demonstrate nitrogen-fixing activity in Styrofoam-eating mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE NITROGEN ISOTOPE COMPOSITIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Stable nitrogen isotope compositions were analyzed to provide evidence for the assimilation of atmospheric nitrogen into the biomass of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WOOD-EATING TERMITES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Wood-eating termites are insects that share similar nitrogen-fixing gut bacteria with mealworms, suggesting a commonality in nitrogen-fixing mechanisms across species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRUIT FLY CERATITIS CAPITATA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The fruit fly Ceratitis capitata is another insect that shares nitrogen-fixing gut bacteria with mealworms, indicating a broader ecological pattern.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RED TURPENTINE BEETLE DENDROCTONUS VALENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The red turpentine beetle Dendroctonus valens is an insect that shares nitrogen-fixing gut bacteria with mealworms, further supporting the idea of shared nitrogen-fixing mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CERATITIS CAPITATA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Ceratitis capitata, commonly known as the fruit fly, is studied in natural populations for ecological and genetic research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DORCUS RECTUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dorcus rectus, a species of stag beetle, is studied for its nitrogen-fixing capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TERMITES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Termites are insects known for their ability to fix nitrogen through symbiotic relationships with gut bacteria, contributing to nutrient cycling in ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Termites are insects that host symbiotic bacteria in their guts, which play a crucial role in nitrogen fixation and cellulose degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ODONTOTAENIUS DISJUNCTUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Odontotaenius disjunctus, a species of beetle, has a compartmentalized gut microbiome with oxygen gradients and nitrogen-fixing capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DENDROCTONUS VALENS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Dendroctonus valens, also known as the red turpentine beetle, is a species of bark beetle that harbors nitrogen-fixing bacteria in its gut.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-FIXING GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrogen-fixing genes are responsible for encoding enzymes that facilitate the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia, a process essential for nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN-FIXING ENTEROBACTER AGGLOMERANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterobacter agglomerans is a nitrogen-fixing bacterium isolated from the guts of wood-eating termites, contributing to nitrogen cycling in ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEOTERMES KOSHUNENSIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Neotermes koshunensis is a termite species whose gut contains a symbiotic microbial community responsible for nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COPTOTERMES FORMOSANUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Coptotermes formosanus is a termite species with a symbiotic microbial community in its gut that expresses nitrogen fixation genes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RETICULITERMES SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Reticulitermes spp. are termites that exhibit variation in nitrogen fixation rates, influenced by their gut microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEAF-CUTTER ANTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Leaf-cutter ants host symbiotic nitrogen-fixing bacteria in their fungus gardens, contributing to nitrogen cycling in their ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NASUTITERMES CORNIGER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Nasutitermes corniger is a termite species capable of nitrogen fixation through its symbiotic gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHORETIMA VITTATA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Phoretima vittata is an earthworm species whose intestinal tract contains diazotrophs, bacteria capable of nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNGUS-GROWING TERMITES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Fungus-growing termites host symbiotic bacteria in their guts that have the potential for nitrogen fixation, aiding in nutrient cycling.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRASSHOPPERS (LOCUSTA MIGRATORIA MIGR)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Grasshoppers, specifically Locusta migratoria migr, are insects whose gut microbiomes have been studied for their microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOCUSTA MIGRATORIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Locusta migratoria, commonly known as migratory grasshoppers, are insects studied for their potential role in biodegradation and as alternative protein sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCIENTIFIC TOTAL ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scientific Total Environment is a journal that publishes research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by superworms and other environmental studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL MICROBIOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental Microbiology is a journal that publishes research on microbial communities and their roles in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
      <data key="d1">GeneJET Gel Extraction Kit is used to purify PCR products for sequencing nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGED POLYETHYLENE (PE)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Aged polyethylene (PE) is a type of microplastic that has undergone environmental degradation, making it more susceptible to biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRISITINE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Pristine microplastics are newly formed or unaged plastic particles that are less efficiently biodegraded compared to aged microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY AND MASS SPECTROMETRY (GC-MS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gas chromatography and mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is a technique used to identify and quantify metabolic intermediates produced during the biodegradation of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROCCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria whose relative abundance decreases in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae when fed microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUSTAINABLE SOLUTIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Sustainable solutions refer to environmentally friendly methods, such as biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, for addressing microplastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROIA GRISSELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achroia grissella is a species of Lepidoptera capable of depolymerizing high-density polyethylene (PE) microplastics in its gut environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UV RADIATION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">UV radiation causes surface oxidation and aging of microplastics, reducing their hydrophobicity and making them more susceptible to microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;UV radiation contributes to the aging of polyethylene microplastics, increasing their susceptibility to biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;UV radiation contributes to the oxidation and aging of polyethylene microplastics, making them more susceptible to biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;UV radiation is an environmental factor that accelerates the aging process of plastics, altering their physicochemical properties and affecting their biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXTRACELLULAR OXIDASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Extracellular oxidase is an enzyme produced by gut microbes in Zophobas atratus larvae, playing a role in the degradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UV AGING">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">UV aging refers to the process by which UV radiation alters the physicochemical properties of microplastics (MPs), making them more susceptible to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGING INDEX">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The aging index refers to the degree of physicochemical changes in microplastics (MPs) due to environmental factors like UV radiation, which affects their biodegradation by organisms such as Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUTARALDEHYDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glutaraldehyde is a chemical used to fix the intestines of mealworm larvae for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOMYL ACETATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Isomyl acetate is used in the precipitation step during the preparation of mealworm intestines for freeze-drying.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALLADIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Palladium is used to coat freeze-dried mealworm intestines for observation under a scanning electron microscope.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREENGENES 16S RRNA DATABASE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Greengenes 16S rRNA database is used for microbial community analysis of mealworm gut microbiomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROSOFT EXCEL 2019">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Microsoft Excel 2019 is used for statistical analysis and calculating averages and standard deviations in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORIGIN PRO 2021">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Origin PRO 2021 is used to create column diagrams for data visualization in this research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIRGIN PE FILM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Virgin PE film is a type of polyethylene plastic used in biodegradation experiments with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGED PE FILM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aged PE film is a degraded form of polyethylene plastic used in biodegradation experiments with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIRGIN PS FOAM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Virgin PS foam is a type of polystyrene foam used in biodegradation experiments with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGED PS FOAM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aged PS foam is a degraded form of polystyrene foam used in biodegradation experiments with mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HARDNESS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hardness is a physical property of plastic samples measured to evaluate changes after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGED PE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aged polyethylene (PE) refers to PE that has undergone physical changes over time, making it more susceptible to consumption and degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGED PS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Aged polystyrene (PS) refers to PS that has undergone physical changes over time, making it more prone to ingestion by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to pristine PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRISTINE PE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pristine polyethylene (PE) refers to PE in its original, unaged state, which is less consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to aged PE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRISTINE PS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pristine polystyrene (PS) refers to PS in its original, unaged state, which is less consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to aged PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURFACE MORPHOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Surface morphology refers to the physical changes observed on the surface of microplastics after being ingested by Tenebrio molitor larvae, including bite marks and degradation patterns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXIC ADDITIVES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxic additives are chemicals present in microplastics that may negatively affect the growth and health of Tenebrio molitor larvae upon ingestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRACONTANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tetracantane (C40H82) is a long-chain alkane detected in the frass of aged PE-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, a product of polyethylene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetracantane (C40H82) is a long-chain alkane found in the frass of aged PE-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing the degradation of polyethylene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TETRATETRACONTANE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tetratetracontane (C44H90) is a long-chain alkane found in the frass of pristine PE-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating biodegradation of virgin polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tetratetracontane (C44H90) is a long-chain alkane found in the frass of pristine PE-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating the biodegradation of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-HYDROXYACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Hydroxyacetic acid (C2H4O3) is an oxygen-containing compound found in the byproducts of aged PE microplastics degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;2-Hydroxyacetic acid (C2H4O3) is an oxygen-containing compound found in the frass of aged PE-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, a byproduct of polyethylene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-METHYL-2-HEXANONE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">5-Methyl-2-hexanone (C7H14O) is an oxygen-containing compound detected in the frass of aged PE-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, resulting from polyethylene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;5-Methyl-2-hexanone (C7H14O) is an oxygen-containing compound found in the byproducts of aged PE microplastics degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1-(2-BUTOXYETHOXY) PROPAN-2-OL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1-(2-Butoxyethoxy) propan-2-ol (C9H20O3) is an oxygen-containing compound found in the byproducts of aged PE microplastics degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;1-(2-Butoxyethoxy) propan-2-ol (C9H20O3) is an oxygen-containing compound found in the frass of aged PE-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, a byproduct of polyethylene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-HYDROXYPROPYL METHACRYLATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">2-Hydroxypropyl methacrylate (C7H12O3) is an oxygen-containing compound detected in the frass of aged PE-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;2-Hydroxypropyl methacrylate (C7H12O3) is an oxygen-containing compound found in the byproducts of aged PE microplastics degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,3-DI-TERT-BUTYL-2,5-DIMETHYLBENZENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,3-Di-tert-butyl-2,5-dimethylbenzene (C16H26) is an antioxidant additive found in the frass of PS-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;1,3-Di-tert-butyl-2,5-dimethylbenzene (C16H26) is an antioxidant additive found in the frass of PS-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;1,3-di-tert-butyl-2,5-dimethylbenzene is an antioxidant additive found in plastics, detected in the frass of polystyrene-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-N GROUP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The C-N group is a chemical structure detected in the frass of aged PS-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating nitrogen bonding to the benzene ring during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The C-N group is a chemical structure found in the frass of aged PS-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating the bonding of nitrogen to the benzene ring during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATION REACTIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidation reactions occur on the surface of aged polyethylene microplastics, facilitating their biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGED MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Aged microplastics are microplastic particles that have undergone environmental aging, affecting their interaction with oil in shoreline environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Aged microplastics are plastic particles that have undergone surface oxidation and changes in hydrophobicity due to UV radiation, making them more susceptible to microbial degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGEN-CONTAINING GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygen-containing groups are formed on the surface of aged microplastics due to UV radiation, which promotes the growth of gut microbes involved in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRIMITIVE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Primitive microplastics are unaged plastic particles that are less preferred by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to aged microplastics for ingestion and degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGEN FREE RADICALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygen free radicals are formed during the reaction of oxygen with polymers, aiding in the degradation of microplastics by gut microbes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COPPER AND LEAD ADSORPTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Copper and lead adsorption refers to the ability of microplastics to adsorb heavy metals like copper and lead, which can have environmental implications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSOXIDANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achromobacter xylosoxidans is a bacterial strain capable of colonizing and biodegrading high-density polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEVOFLOXACIN HYDROCHLORIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Levofloxacin hydrochloride is an antibiotic that can be adsorbed by microplastics, affecting its environmental distribution and potential toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATR-FT-IR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Attenuated total reflection Fourier transform infrared (ATR-FT-IR) is a technique used to characterize the biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYGEN FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxygen functional groups are chemical groups that can influence the adsorption properties of microplastics, such as their ability to adsorb levofloxacin hydrochloride.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC FORMATION">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic formation refers to the process by which larger plastic items break down into smaller microplastic particles, often studied in environmental pollution research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OIL BEHAVIOR">
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb</data>
      <data key="d1">Aged microplastics can impact oil behavior in shoreline environments, affecting environmental interactions.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO-POLYSTYRENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
      <data key="d1">Larval age is a factor that regulates the biodegradation of micro-polystyrene in mealworms (Tenebrio molitor).</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT COMMUNITIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut communities refer to the microbial populations in the digestive systems of organisms like Tenebrio obscurus and Tenebrio molitor, which are shaped by their diet and play a role in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDIZED INTERMEDIATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidized intermediates are chemical compounds formed during the depolymerization of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) by organisms like Tenebrio obscurus and Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS BALANCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass balance analysis is a method used to quantify the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) by measuring the conversion of ingested LDPE into CO2 and other byproducts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE FOAMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE foams are a form of low-density polyethylene that can be ingested and biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE foams are commercial products made from low-density polyethylene (LDPE), used in studies to assess biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio obscurus and Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLESIOPHTHALMUS DAVIDS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plesiophthalmus davids, a species of darkling beetle, is known to biodegrade polystyrene plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAXWORM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Greater waxworms (Galleria mellonella) are larvae capable of biodegrading polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIGNOCELLULOSIC MATERIALS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Lignocellulosic materials are organic compounds found in dried leaves and rotting wood, which are consumed by darkling beetle larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1H NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (1H NMR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">1H nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) is an analytical technique used to study the chemical structure and degradation products of polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1,2,4-TRICHLOROBENZENE (1,2,4 TCB)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">1,2,4-trichlorobenzene (1,2,4 TCB) is a solvent used in the extraction and analysis of residual polyethylene (PE) during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POTASSIUM BROMIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Potassium bromide (KBr) is used in FTIR analysis to prepare samples by grinding them into homogeneous powders for scanning.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON MASS BALANCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon mass balance analysis is a method used to measure the carbon mass of ingested LDPE, PE residue in frass, CO2 released from biodegradation, and the increment of carbon in biomass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TGA/SDTA851E">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The TGA/SDTA851e is a thermogravimetric analyzer used to measure the stability and degradation of LDPE foams and residual polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OLIVE OIL-BASED METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">An olive oil-based method is used to extract LDPE from frass samples during carbon mass balance analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KANAMYCIN SULFATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Kanamycin sulfate is an antibiotic tested for its ability to inhibit gut bacteria in mealworms during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PENICILLIN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Penicillin is an antibiotic tested for its ability to inhibit gut bacteria in mealworms during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL CONSORTIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbial consortia are the communities of microorganisms in the gut of larvae that play a critical role in the biodegradation of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE depolymerization is the process by which LDPE is broken down into smaller molecular weight components, facilitated by the digestive systems of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECIFIC LDPE CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Specific LDPE consumption rate (SPCR) measures the amount of LDPE consumed by 100 larvae over a test period, providing insights into the larvae's ability to biodegrade plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut enzymes are biological catalysts in the larvae's digestive system that play a critical role in breaking down LDPE during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAL GUT BIOREACTOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The larval gut bioreactor refers to the digestive system of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, which acts as an efficient system for LDPE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMO-OXIDATIVE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermo-oxidative degradation is a pretreatment method that reduces the molecular weight of polymers, facilitating their biodegradation by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIAL CONTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Microbial contribution refers to the role of microorganisms in breaking down low molecular weight fractions of polymers during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW MOLECULAR WEIGHT FRACTIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low molecular weight fractions (&lt;5.0 kDa) are rapidly decomposed and metabolized during the biodegradation of polymers like LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE FEEDSTOCKS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">LDPE feedstocks are the raw materials used in the study to assess the biodegradation capabilities of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON CONVERSION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon conversion refers to the process by which ingested LDPE is converted into CO2 and biomass by mealworms, indicating biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="¹H NMR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (¹H NMR) spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical modifications in LDPE after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor, revealing the formation of new metabolites and residues.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A. GRISELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Achroia grisella, or the lesser waxworm, is an insect larvae that has been observed to biodegrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL BOND">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The carbonyl bond (C=O) is a functional group formed during the biodegradation of LDPE by Tenebrio molitor and other larvae, indicating chemical changes in the plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYDROXYL GROUP">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydroxyl groups (OH) are formed during the biodegradation of LDPE, as evidenced by FTIR analysis, indicating a shift from hydrophobic to hydrophilic properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CH₃ MOIETY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The CH₃ moiety is a chemical group formed during the biodegradation of LDPE, as observed in ¹H NMR spectra of frass from Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CH₂ GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CH₂ groups are chemical structures observed in the biodegradation of LDPE, indicating the breakdown of long-chain polymers and the formation of new metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALDEHYDE MOIETY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The aldehyde moiety is a chemical group observed in the biodegradation of LDPE, indicating further chemical modifications during the process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULOMA LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae of the genus Uloma are known to participate in the biodegradation of HDPE, similar to lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC INHIBITION TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The antibiotic inhibition test is used to study the role of gut bacteria in plastic biodegradation by suppressing bacterial activity with antibiotics like gentamicin.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL/AMINIC GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbonyl and aminic groups are chemical structures formed during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating the incorporation of oxygen into the polymer chains.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNSATURATED ALLYLIC GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Unsaturated allylic groups are chemical structures identified in the residual polymers of frass, indicating chemical modifications during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Polymer depolymerization is the process of breaking down large polymer molecules into smaller units, observed in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON BALANCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon balance analysis is used to determine the conversion of ingested LDPE into CO2 during biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMULSIFYING FACTOR(S)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Emulsifying factors secreted by Tenebrio molitor larvae enhance the bioavailability of plastics and promote biodegradation by gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMALLY OXIDIZED POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermally oxidized polyethylene is a form of low-density polyethylene that has been treated to enhance its biodegradation properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUMED SILICA">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Fumed silica is a nanomaterial used in biopolymer nanocomposites, which have been studied for their biodegradation properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPOLYMER NANOCOMPOSITES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Biopolymer nanocomposites are materials that combine biopolymers with nanomaterials like fumed silica, designed for improved biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBONYL COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbonyl compounds are byproducts of polyethylene degradation, indicating the breakdown of plastic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PE CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1</data>
      <data key="d1">Statistical analysis is used to evaluate differences in PE consumption by mealworm larvae</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDPE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut enzymes in the larvae's digestive system play a critical role in breaking down LDPE during the biodegradation process</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE FILMS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
      <data key="d1">Pro-oxidant additives are added to polyethylene films to promote their biodegradation through oxidation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO-POLYSTYRENE PARTICLE">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro-polystyrene particles are small fragments of polystyrene that can be degraded by symbiotic bacteria isolated from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KLEBSIELLA GRIMONII">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella grimonii is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS MULTIRESINIVORANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas multiresinivorans is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworms, potentially involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas multiresinivorans is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that has been shown to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS PLECOGLOSSICIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas plecoglossicida is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworms, potentially involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas plecoglossicida is a symbiotic bacterium isolated from mealworms that has been shown to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE POWDER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene powder is a fine particulate form of polystyrene, consisting of 95-100% ethylbenzene homopolymer and 0-5% general plastic lubricant, used in various industrial applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRYPTONE YEAST EXTRACT (TYE) MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tryptone yeast extract (TYE) medium is prepared by dispersing 10 g of tryptone and 5 g of yeast extract in 1 L of distilled water, used for bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINERAL MEDIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Mineral medium is a nutrient solution prepared at pH 6.8, containing various salts and trace elements, used for microbial growth and biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineral medium is a nutrient solution used for culturing symbiotic bacteria isolated from the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineral medium is a nutrient-free medium used in the study to evaluate bacterial degradation of micro-sprayed polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineral medium is a nutrient-poor medium used to study the degradation capabilities of microorganisms by providing polystyrene as the sole carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ETHYLBENZENE HOMOPOLYMER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ethylbenzene homopolymer is the primary component of polystyrene powder, with the CAS number 9003-53-6, used in various industrial applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENERAL PLASTIC LUBRICANT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">General plastic lubricant is a minor component (0-5%) of polystyrene powder, used to improve processing properties.</data>
