    </node>
    <node id="A. TUMEFACIENS MA92">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A. tumefaciens MA92 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;A. tumefaciens MA92 is a bacterial strain that adheres to polystyrene film, suggesting involvement in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A. tumefaciens MA92 is a bacterial strain that degrades polystyrene, contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;A. tumefaciens MA92 is a bacterial strain that is considered capable of degrading polystyrene based on its activity in experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;A. tumefaciens MA92 is a bacterial strain that showed high adhesion to polystyrene films but unclear degradation ability due to excessive bacterial growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Agrobacterium tumefaciens MA92 is a bacterial strain with a longer doubling time of 113.4 minutes in LB medium, indicating slower growth compared to E. coli.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P. NITROREDUCENS MA77">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">P. nitroreducens MA77 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene, aiding in the breakdown of plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;P. nitroreducens MA77 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene under specific conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;P. nitroreducens MA77 is a bacterial strain that can adhere to polystyrene film, indicating potential for biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;P. nitroreducens MA77 is a bacterial strain that reduced the size and number of polystyrene particles, indicating its role in degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;P. nitroreducens MA77 is a bacterial strain that reduced the size and number of polystyrene particles, indicating its role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;P. nitroreducens MA77 is a bacterial strain that shows consistent polystyrene-degrading activity through surface destruction and decomposition of polystyrene particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas nitroreducens MA77 is a bacterial strain with a doubling time of 106.8 minutes in LB medium, showing sensitivity to salt and slower growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P. MULTIRESINIVORANS MA11">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">P. multiresinivorans MA11 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene, contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;P. multiresinivorans MA11 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;P. multiresinivorans MA11 is a bacterial strain that adheres well to polystyrene film, suggesting strong potential for polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;P. multiresinivorans MA11 is a bacterial strain that produces micro-cracks on the surface of polystyrene films, indicating its potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;P. multiresinivorans MA11 is a bacterial strain that showed high adhesion to polystyrene films and may contribute to its degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas multiresinivorans MA11 is a bacterial strain studied for its growth characteristics and potential role in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K. GRIMMONTII MA76">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella grimmontii MA76 is a bacterial strain with growth characteristics evaluated in LB medium, showing a longer doubling time compared to E. coli.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P. PLECOGLOSSICIDA MA93">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">P. plecoglossicida MA93 is a bacterial strain capable of adhering to polystyrene film, indicating potential for biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;P. plecoglossicida MA93 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene, aiding in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;P. plecoglossicida MA93 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;P. plecoglossicida MA93 is a bacterial strain that exhibits polystyrene-degrading activity by causing surface destruction of polystyrene films.&lt;SEP&gt;P. plecoglossicida MA93 is a bacterial strain that showed high adhesion to polystyrene films and may contribute to its degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas plecoglossicida MA93 is a bacterial strain studied for its growth dynamics and potential applications in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DOUBLING TIME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Doubling time refers to the time it takes for a bacterial population to double in size, a key metric in growth studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOOTSTRAP ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bootstrap analysis is a statistical method used to assess the reliability of phylogenetic trees by resampling data.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEGA4 SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">MEGA4 is a software tool used for phylogenetic analysis and tree construction based on genetic sequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SALT SENSITIVITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Salt sensitivity refers to the response of bacterial strains, such as P. nitroreducens MA77, to salt concentrations in their environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELL DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Cell density, measured as Abs600, is a metric used to quantify bacterial growth in culture medium over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INCUBATION TIME">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Incubation time refers to the duration over which bacterial cultures are grown and monitored for growth characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E. COLI MA4">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">E. coli MA4 is a bacterial strain that did not grow well on polystyrene micro-sprayed plates, indicating limited ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;E. coli MA4 is a bacterial strain that shows less adhesion to polystyrene film, indicating limited potential for polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A. HYDROPHILA MA32">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A. hydrophila MA32 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, potentially involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;A. hydrophila MA32 is a bacterial strain that showed poor growth on polystyrene micro-sprayed plates, similar to the negative control.&lt;SEP&gt;A. hydrophila MA32 is a bacterial strain that shows similar surface appearance to the polystyrene film without microorganism inoculation, indicating limited or no polystyrene-degrading activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A. hydrophila MA32 is a bacterial strain with low adhesion to polystyrene film, suggesting minimal involvement in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y. REGENSBURGEI MA19">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Y. regensburgei MA19 is a bacterial strain capable of adhering to polystyrene film, indicating potential for polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. regensburgei MA19 is a bacterial strain identified for its ability to degrade polystyrene, aiding in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. regensburgei MA19 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, showing potential for polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. regensburgei MA19 is a bacterial strain that exhibits polystyrene-degrading activity by causing surface destruction of polystyrene films.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. regensburgei MA19 is a bacterial strain that reduced the size and number of polystyrene particles, indicating its role in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K. GRIMONTII MA76">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">K. grimontii MA76 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, showing growth and degradation activity on polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;K. grimontii MA76 is a bacterial strain that adheres to polystyrene film, suggesting a role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;K. grimontii MA76 is a bacterial strain that exhibits polystyrene-degrading activity by causing surface destruction of polystyrene films.&lt;SEP&gt;K. grimontii MA76 is a bacterial strain that reduced the size and number of polystyrene particles, suggesting its involvement in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S. MARCESCENS MA42">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">S. marcescens MA42 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene, contributing to environmental pollution mitigation.&lt;SEP&gt;S. marcescens MA42 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, known for its ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;S. marcescens MA42 is a bacterial strain that adheres well to polystyrene film, indicating potential for biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;S. marcescens MA42 is a bacterial strain that produces micro-cracks on polystyrene films, indicating its potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;S. marcescens MA42 is a bacterial strain that showed high adhesion to polystyrene films and may contribute to its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A. SEPTICUS MA21">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A. septicus MA21 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA21 is a bacterial strain that attaches to polystyrene film through autoaggregation, suggesting a role in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA21 is a bacterial strain that degrades polystyrene, playing a role in the biological breakdown of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA21 is a bacterial strain that showed rapid growth on polystyrene micro-sprayed plates, suggesting strong polystyrene degradation ability.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA21 is a bacterial strain that shows consistent polystyrene-degrading activity through surface destruction and decomposition of polystyrene particles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A. SEPTICUS MA62">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A. septicus MA62 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, showing potential for polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA62 is a bacterial strain that attaches to polystyrene film through autoaggregation, indicating potential for biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA62 is a bacterial strain that degrades polystyrene, aiding in the biodegradation of plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA62 is a bacterial strain that exhibited delayed but significant growth on polystyrene micro-sprayed plates, indicating moderate degradation ability.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA62 is a bacterial strain that exhibits polystyrene-degrading activity by causing surface destruction and decomposition of polystyrene particles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A. SEPTICUS MA65">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">A. septicus MA65 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA65 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of mealworms, involved in the degradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA65 is a bacterial strain that attaches to polystyrene film through autoaggregation, suggesting involvement in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA65 is a bacterial strain that showed rapid growth on polystyrene micro-sprayed plates, indicating strong polystyrene degradation ability.&lt;SEP&gt;A. septicus MA65 is a bacterial strain that shows consistent polystyrene-degrading activity through surface destruction and decomposition of polystyrene particles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRO-SPRAYED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Micro-sprayed polystyrene is a form of polystyrene used to evaluate bacterial degradation activity due to its increased surface area.&lt;SEP&gt;Micro-sprayed polystyrene refers to small polystyrene particles used in experiments to evaluate bacterial degradation activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AUTOAGGREGATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Autoaggregation is a process by which bacterial cells attach to surfaces, such as polystyrene film, facilitating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGAR PLATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Agar plate is a solid growth medium used to culture bacteria and evaluate their degradation activity on micro-sprayed polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E. COLI ATCC 43895">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">E. coli ATCC 43895 is a bacterial strain used as a negative control in the study, showing no significant degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E. COLI MA04">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">E. coli MA04 is a bacterial strain that showed no significant degradation of polystyrene, similar to the negative control.&lt;SEP&gt;E. coli MA04 is a bacterial strain used in experiments to assess its ability to degrade polystyrene, showing similar surface appearance to the polystyrene film without microorganism inoculation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K. NITROREDUCENS MA77">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">K. nitroreducens MA77 is a bacterial strain that reduced the size and number of polystyrene particles, indicating its role in degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEDES ALBOPICTUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aedes albopictus is an insect species whose gut microbiota, including bacteria of the genera Acinetobacter, Agrobacterium, Enterobacter, Klebsiella, and Pseudomonas, have been studied for their potential role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EURYCANTHA CALCARATA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Eurycantha calcarata is an insect species from which Y. regensburgei was isolated, indicating its potential role in biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AEROMONAS SP. TM1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Aeromonas sp. TM1 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of T. molitor and Z. morio, showing the ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K. PNEUMONIAE ZM1">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Klebsiella pneumoniae ZM1 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of T. molitor and Z. morio, capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P. PLECOGLOSSICIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">P. plecoglossicida is a bacterial strain isolated from polyethylene plastic samples, capable of growing on paraffin as a carbon source and degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SP. DSM50071">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas sp. DSM50071 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of superworms, capable of degrading polystyrene through enzymatic activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGROBACTERIUM SP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Agrobacterium sp. is a bacterium found in the digestive tract of mealworms fed with polystyrene, capable of degrading high-impact polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter spp. are bacteria that utilize the AtaA protein for autoaggregation and adhesion to abiotic surfaces, potentially aiding in the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K. GRIMONII MA76">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">K. grimonii MA76 is a bacterial strain that degrades polystyrene, playing a role in the biodegradation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE FILMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene films are a form of plastic waste that can be degraded by specific bacterial strains, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene films are plastic materials that can be colonized by bacteria, leading to biodegradation and changes in surface properties.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENOMIC DNA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Genomic DNA analysis is a method used to identify genes involved in polystyrene degradation, providing insights into the biological mechanisms of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE DEGRADING GENES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene degrading genes are genetic components identified in bacteria that enable the breakdown of polystyrene, contributing to biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOMONAS SP. SR-5">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudomonas sp. SR-5 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading styrene in biofilters with organic and inorganic packing materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NAPHTHALENE DIOXYGENASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Naphthalene dioxygenase is an enzyme that stereospecifically dihydroxylates the styrene vinyl group, aiding in styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LANDFILLS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Landfills are sites where microplastics accumulate, posing environmental challenges and requiring removal strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOFILTERS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Biofilters are systems used in research studies to investigate the degradation of styrene by bacterial strains like Pseudomonas sp. SR-5.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRITICAL REVIEWS IN BIOTECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Critical Reviews in Biotechnology provides an overview of biodegradation strategies for polystyrene and modified polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYSTEMATIC AND APPLIED MICROBIOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Systematic and Applied Microbiology publishes studies on the catabolism of styrene by bacterial strains like Pseudomonas fluorescens.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLESIOPHTHALMUS DAVIDI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plesiophthalmus davidi is a species of larvae whose gut microbial flora has been shown to facilitate the fast biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Plesiophthalmus davidi is another insect larva capable of degrading plastics, similar to mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACINETOBACTER SP. TOL 5">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter sp. Tol 5 is a bacterial strain known for its high adhesiveness to various abiotic surfaces, including plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM SP. DR11 AND DR14">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Exiguobacterium sp. DR11 and DR14 are bacterial strains capable of forming biofilms on polystyrene surfaces and initiating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCHIFF BASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Schiff base is a chemical compound used to dope polystyrene, reducing its degradation rate and enhancing its photostability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANOMETALLIC COMPLEXES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Organometallic complexes are chemical compounds embedded in polystyrene films to enhance their photostability and reduce environmental degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM SP. DR11">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Exiguobacterium sp. DR11 is a bacterial strain capable of forming biofilms on polystyrene surfaces, initiating biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXIGUOBACTERIUM SP. DR14">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Exiguobacterium sp. DR14 is a bacterial strain capable of forming biofilms on polystyrene surfaces, initiating biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="50% GLYCEROL SOLUTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycerol is used to prepare a 50% glycerol solution, often used as a cryoprotectant in biological studies</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MINERAL AGAR PLATES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
      <data key="d1">UV sterilization is used to sterilize mineral agar plates before use in experiments.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIAL GROWTH">
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6</data>
      <data key="d1">LB medium is used to cultivate bacterial strains isolated from mealworms, enabling the study of their growth and polystyrene-degrading capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y. REGENSBURGEI">
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
      <data key="d1">Y. regensburgei was isolated from the intestine of Eurycantha calcarata, indicating its potential role in biodegradation processes</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC SURFACES">
      <data key="d2">chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f</data>
      <data key="d1">Acinetobacter sp. Tol 5 exhibits high adhesiveness to various abiotic surfaces, including plastics, which may influence biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIGESTIVE BIOFRAGMENTATION MODEL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A digestive biofragmentation model was developed to estimate the time required for mealworms to digest and remove different-sized PS and PE particles, showing that smaller particles are removed more rapidly.&lt;SEP&gt;A digestive biofragmentation model was developed to predict and unveil the fragmentation process of ingested plastics during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;A digestive biofragmentation model was developed to simulate the size-reduction process of ingested PS and LDPE particles in mealworms, predicting the time required for particle removal.&lt;SEP&gt;A digestive biofragmentation model was developed to simulate the size-reduction process of plastic particles in the intestinal tract of mealworms and predict the formation of micro- and nanoplastics during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A model developed to simulate the size-reduction process of ingested plastics during biodegradation in the intestinal tracts of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive biofragmentation model is a mathematical model used to simulate the size-reduction process of plastic particles ingested by mealworms, predicting the size-frequency distribution and removal efficiency of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYROLYZER-GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY MASS SPECTROMETRY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrolyzer-gas chromatography mass spectrometry (Py-GCMS) is a highly sensitive analytical technique used to detect and analyze microplastics (MPs) and nanoplastics (NPs) in frass during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pyrolyzer-gas chromatography mass spectrometry (Py-GCMS) is an analytical technique used to study the composition and degradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Δ13C SIGNATURE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The δ13C signature is a method used to assess the biodegradation of commercial plastics by measuring the isotopic composition of residual plastics in mealworm frass.&lt;SEP&gt;The δ13C signature is used to verify the biodegradation of plastics by tracking changes in the carbon isotope composition of residual plastics after ingestion by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LASER LIGHT SCATTERING SIZER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A laser light scattering sizer is a tool used to detect and measure the size of microplastics (MPs) and nanoplastics (NPs) in frass, with a detection limit of 10 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Laser light scattering sizer is a method used to assess the size distribution of residual plastic particles generated after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The laser light scattering sizer is a highly sensitive detection method used to analyze the size and distribution of plastic particles in mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLUE MUSSEL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The blue mussel (Mytilus edulis L.) is a bivalve mollusk that can be affected by microplastics, impacting its cells and tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;The blue mussel (Mytilus edulis L.) is an aquatic organism that has been studied for its ability to internalize high-density polyethylene (HDPE) microplastics (MPs) in its digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRESHWATER AMPHIPOD">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Freshwater amphipods are macroinvertebrates that can fragment ingested microplastics into smaller particles, though they lack efficient plastic-degrading capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Freshwater amphipods, such as Gammarus duebeni, are organisms that can fragment microplastics, contributing to environmental degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The freshwater amphipod (Gammarus duebeni) is an organism capable of rapidly fragmenting polyethylene (PE) microplastics (MPs) into smaller particles, including nanoplastics (NPs).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GAMMARUS DUEBENI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gammarus duebeni is a freshwater amphipod that can rapidly fragment microplastics, contributing to the formation of nanoplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammarus duebeni is a freshwater amphipod that has been found to rapidly fragment polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) into a wide range of sizes, including nanoplastics (NPs), in its digestive tract.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EARTHWORMS (EISENIA ANDREI)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Earthworms (Eisenia andrei) exposed to polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) generate nanoplastics (NPs) in their hindgut, which can damage spermatogenesis and coelomocyte viability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYETHYLENE MICROPLASTICS (PE MPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) are small plastic particles that can be fragmented into nanoplastics (NPs) by organisms such as Gammarus duebeni and earthworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYROLYZER-GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY MASS SPECTROMETRY (PY-GCMS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrolyzer-gas chromatography mass spectrometry (Py-GCMS) is a highly sensitive method used to assess the size distribution of residual plastic particles and the mass content of potential nanoplastics (NPs) in frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIGESTIVE BIOFRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Digestive biofragmentation refers to the process by which macroinvertebrates, such as mealworms and earthworms, fragment ingested plastics into smaller particles, including microplastics and nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-TEMPERATURE GPC (HT-GPC)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">High-temperature GPC (HT-GPC) is an advanced analytical method used to characterize the molecular weight changes of plastics during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC FEEDING TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The plastic feeding test is an experimental procedure used to study the biodegradation of plastics by yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASS BALANCE AND REMOVAL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Mass balance and removal analysis is conducted to determine the extent of plastic biodegradation by mealworms based on ingested plastics and frass egested.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOSTATIC INCUBATOR">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A thermostatic incubator is used to maintain controlled environmental conditions for the mealworms during the plastic biodegradation study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13C STABLE ISOTOPE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The 13C stable isotope is used in tracer tests to study the mineralization of plastics during biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EA-IRMS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Elemental analyzer isotope ratio mass spectrometry (EA-IRMS) is a technique used to analyze the stable isotope fraction in polymer samples to identify biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H2O2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) is used to digest organic matter in mealworm frass to isolate residual plastic particles for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PA MEMBRANE FILTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyamide (PA) membrane filters are used to filter and collect micro- and submicroplastic particles from mealworm frass for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYROLYSIS-GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY MASS SPECTROMETRY (PY-GCMS)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrolysis-gas chromatography mass spectrometry (Py-GCMS) is a method used to quantify the plastic components in the solid residues of mealworm frass, aiding in the study of plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KINETIC PARAMETERS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Kinetic parameters are used in the digestive biofragmentation model to simulate the half-life periods of ingested plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Kinetic parameters are used in the digestive biofragmentation model to simulate the size-reduction process of plastic particles ingested by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENETIC ALGORITHM">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The genetic algorithm is a computational method used to optimize kinetic parameters in the digestive biofragmentation model for predicting plastic biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC REMOVAL EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic removal efficiency measures the effectiveness of mealworms in degrading and removing ingested plastics like PS and LDPE from the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIZE-FREQUENCY DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Size-frequency distribution refers to the distribution of plastic particle sizes generated during the biodegradation process by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MATRIX LABORATORY (MATLAB)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Matrix Laboratory (MATLAB) is a software tool used to calibrate kinetic parameters and simulate the digestive biofragmentation model for plastic biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MILLI-Q ULTRAPURE WATER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Milli-Q ultrapure water is used in experimental procedures to ensure the removal of background micro- or nanoplastics from containers and equipment.&lt;SEP&gt;Milli-Q ultrapure water is used to rinse experimental containers to remove potential microplastic contamination.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHANOL (HPLC GRADE)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Methanol (HPLC grade) is used to rinse experimental containers and equipment to remove potential background contamination of micro- or nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC-DEGRADING MICROBIAL ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic-degrading microbial activities refer to the enhanced ability of gut microbiota in mealworms to break down plastics like PS and PE, reaching peak efficiency after 3-4 weeks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCTIONAL MICROBIAL CONSORTIUMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Functional microbial consortiums are specific groups of microorganisms in the intestines of mealworms that enhance the biodegradation and mineralization of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER-EXTRACTED FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The water-extracted fraction represents the soluble organic components and salts in frass, indicating the digestion and degradation of plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORGANIC SOLVENT-EXTRACTED FRACTION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The organic solvent-extracted fraction represents the residual PS or PE in frass, indicating the extent of plastic degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE ISOTOPE SIGNATURE (Δ13C)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The δ13C stable isotope signature is used to analyze the biodegradation of plastics by comparing residual plastics with original plastic foams.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LASER LIGHT SCATTERING PARTICLE SIZER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">This tool is used to measure the size-frequency distribution of residual plastic particles in frass, providing data on particle size reduction during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MATLAB">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">MATLAB is a computational tool used to simulate the size-frequency particle distribution of residual PS and LDPE polymers during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic foam, such as expanded polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), is a major environmental pollutant that can be biodegraded by insects like mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GALLERIA MELLONELLA LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Galleria mellonella larvae are another type of plastic-degrading insect that can rapidly biodegrade PS microplastics, as demonstrated by fluorescence intensity decline and SEM observations.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae, also known as great wax moth larvae, are another type of plastic-degrading invertebrate that can digest polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL IMPLICATIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The study highlights the environmental implications of mealworm-mediated biodegradation, showing that small plastic particles do not accumulate in their frass, reducing concerns about nanoplastics in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;This research study explores the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, highlighting the role of gut microbiome and the challenges posed by ultrahigh-MW polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZOPHOBAS ATRATUS LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zophobas atratus larvae are known for their ability to biodegrade plastics, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PY-GCMC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Py-GCMC analysis is a scientific method used to detect and analyze the presence of nanoplastics in mealworm frass, confirming their absence.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTICS (MP)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics are small plastic particles larger than 2 µm, resulting from the biofragmentation of ingested plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PY-GCMS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Py-GCMS analysis is a highly sensitive method used to determine the size distribution of residual plastic particles after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LASER PARTICLE ANALYZERS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Laser particle analyzers are used to measure the size distribution of plastic particles after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STABLE ISOTOPE TEST">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A stable isotope test is used to assess the biodegradation of plastics by measuring the δ13C signature in residual plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMERCIAL PLASTIC PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Commercial plastic products, such as PS and LDPE foams, are environmental pollutants that undergo biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH PURITY PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">High purity plastics are used in biodegradation studies to understand the degradation process without interference from additives.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DUCKWEED">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Duckweed (Lemna minor L.) is a small aquatic plant often used in environmental studies to assess the effects of pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYAMIDE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyamide (PA) is a synthetic polymer known for its strength and durability, commonly used in textiles and engineering plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYBUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) is a biodegradable plastic used in packaging and agricultural films.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELEMENTAL ANALYZER ISOTOPE RATIO MASS SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Elemental analyzer isotope ratio mass spectrometer (EA-IRMS) is used to measure isotopic ratios in materials, including plastics and their degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATTENUATED TOTAL REFLECTION FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Attenuated total reflection Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (ATR-FTIR) is a technique used to analyze the surface chemistry of materials, including plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIENNA PEE DEE BELEMNITE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Vienna Pee Dee Belemnite (VPDB) is a standard reference for carbon isotope measurements, used in studies of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL FATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Environmental fate refers to the study of how pollutants, such as microplastics and nanoplastics, behave and transform in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RISK MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Risk management involves strategies to mitigate the environmental and health risks posed by pollutants, including microplastics and nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARINE-ATMOSPHERE ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The marine-atmosphere environment is a critical ecosystem affected by microplastics and nanoplastics, leading to widespread pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;The marine-atmosphere environment is a focus of research on microplastics and nanoplastics, highlighting their distribution and impact on ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;The marine-atmosphere environment is a system where microplastics and nanoplastics are transported and interact with ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL AMOEBA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Soil amoebas are microorganisms that interact with microplastics and nanoplastics, influencing their fate in soil ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXICOLOGICAL PERSPECTIVE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A toxicological perspective examines the effects of plastic biodegradation by insect larvae on their health and the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;A toxicological perspective examines the effects of substances, such as plastics, on living organisms and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOC BIOACCUMULATION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HOC bioaccumulation refers to the accumulation of hazardous organic compounds in organisms, often facilitated by microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EARTHWORM SPERMATOGENESIS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Earthworm spermatogenesis refers to the process of sperm production in earthworms, which can be damaged by microplastic digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COELOMOCYTE VIABILITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Coelomocyte viability refers to the health and functionality of coelomocytes, immune cells in earthworms, which can be compromised by microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARICULTURE STYROFOAM FLOATS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mariculture Styrofoam floats are used in aquaculture but can fragment into microplastics due to burrowing invertebrates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYROLYSIS TEMPERATURE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Pyrolysis temperature is a critical factor in the mitigation of microplastics in sewage sludge, influencing the efficiency of plastic breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLANT SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Plant species are studied for their interactions with microplastics and nanoplastics, providing insights into the spread and effects of plastic pollution in terrestrial ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plant species interact with microplastics and nanoplastics, which can affect their growth and health, as studied in environmental research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSEUDOXANTHOMONAS SP. NYZ600">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pseudoxanthomonas sp. strain NyZ600 is a bacterial strain capable of biodegrading bisphenol-A polycarbonate plastic.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROMOXYNIL">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bromoxynil is a herbicide studied for its photo-degradation processes using isotope analysis methods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHTHALATES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Phthalates are chemicals often correlated with microplastics in facility agriculture soil, posing environmental risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BISPHENOL-A POLYCARBONATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bisphenol-A polycarbonate is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by Pseudoxanthomonas sp. strain NyZ600.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADVANCED OXIDATION PROCESSES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Advanced oxidation processes are used to study the aging behavior of microplastics, accelerating their degradation in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISOTOPE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Isotope analysis is a method used to study the degradation processes of chemicals like bromoxynil and the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC PARTICLE POLLUTION">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic particle pollution refers to the presence of microplastics and nanoplastics in the environment, including human blood, posing potential health risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEGRADATION RATES OF PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A study that investigates the degradation rates of various plastics in the environment, providing insights into their persistence and environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADATION OF PLASTICS IN SOIL">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The process by which plastics are broken down in soil, influenced by factors such as temperature and microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMAN BLOOD">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Human blood has been found to contain plastic particles, indicating the widespread presence of microplastics in the human body.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IRON CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The iron cycle is a chemical process tuned by thiosulfate in Fenton reactions, influencing the degradation of pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THIOSULFATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Thiosulfate is a chemical that tunes the iron cycle in Fenton reactions, affecting the degradation of pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITRATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Nitrate plays an unexpected role in promoting the transformation of sulfonamide antibiotics by peracetic acid, contributing to the formation of reactive nitrogen species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SULFONAMIDE ANTIBIOTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sulfonamide antibiotics are transformed by peracetic acid in the presence of nitrate, leading to the formation of reactive nitrogen species and potential disinfection byproducts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PERACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Peracetic acid is a chemical used in the transformation of sulfonamide antibiotics, influenced by the presence of nitrate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REACTIVE NITROGEN SPECIES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Reactive nitrogen species are formed during the transformation of sulfonamide antibiotics by peracetic acid, contributing to pollutant degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISINFECTION BYPRODUCTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Disinfection byproducts are potentially harmful compounds formed during the transformation of sulfonamide antibiotics by peracetic acid.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIODEGRADABLE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
      <data key="d1">Lumbricus terrestris fragments biodegradable microplastics such as polylactic acid (PLA) and poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) into smaller particles, though significant biodegradation is not observed.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON ISOTOPE MEASUREMENTS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
      <data key="d1">VPDB is used as a reference standard for carbon isotope measurements in plastic degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER CHEMISTRY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
      <data key="d1">THF is a solvent used in polymer chemistry to study the properties and degradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
      <data key="d1">The study on degradation rates of plastics provides insights into how long different plastics persist in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXIDATIVE STRESS RESPONSES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxidative stress responses include the production of reactive oxygen species, antioxidant enzyme activities, and lipid peroxidation, observed in mealworms fed with microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LASER DIRECT INFRARED SPECTROMETRY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Laser direct infrared spectrometry is a technique used to analyze residual microplastic particles in mealworm intestines after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUB-MICRON MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Sub-micron microplastics are plastic particles sized between 1–20 μm, found in the frass of mealworms after biodegradation of larger microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SMALL MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Small microplastics are plastic particles sized between 20–999 μm, resulting from the fragmentation of larger plastic waste and found in mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARGE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Large microplastics are plastic particles sized between 1–5 mm, which are ingested and fragmented by mealworms during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LASER DIRECT INFRARED SPECTROMETRY (LDIR)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">LDIR is used to identify and analyze residual microplastics in mealworm intestines and frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Laser direct infrared spectrometry (LDIR) is a method used to identify and quantify microplastics in biological and environmental samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SECONDARY MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Secondary microplastics are small plastic particles formed through the fragmentation of larger plastic debris, often excreted by organisms and reintroduced into ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM INTESTINES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm intestines are dissected and analyzed to study the residual plastics after biodegradation of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIZE DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Size distribution refers to the analysis of the size of residual plastic particles in frass samples after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PA FILTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A PA filter with a pore size of 100 nm is used to filter digested frass samples for analyzing residual plastic particles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LASER LIGHT SCATTERING">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Laser light scattering is a technique used to measure the size distribution of residual plastic particles in frass samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIHYDROETHIDIUM (DHE) ASSAY KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A dihydroethidium (DHE) assay kit is used to measure reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BICINCHONINIC ACID (BCA) PROTEIN QUANTIFICATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Bicinchoninic acid (BCA) protein quantification is a method used to measure protein levels in mealworm homogenates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MDA ASSAY KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An MDA assay kit is used to measure malondialdehyde (MDA) levels, indicating lipid peroxidation in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOD ENZYME ASSAY KIT">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">An SOD enzyme assay kit is used to measure superoxide dismutase (SOD) activity in mealworms, reflecting antioxidant performance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUKEY'S CORRECTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Tukey's correction is a statistical method applied to adjust p-values in ANOVA tests for multiple comparisons.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORIGIN PRO 2021 SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Origin Pro 2021 software is used for statistical analysis and data visualization in this study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic performance refers to the ability of mealworms to withstand oxidative stress, measured by SOD activity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TOXIC SUBSTANCES">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Toxic substances, such as chlorinated intermediates and hydrogen chloride, are produced during the biodegradation of PVC by mealworms, potentially harming their intestinal health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRANSPORTERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Transporters are molecules required for nutrient uptake in mealworms, and their production can be negatively affected by a plastic-only diet.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT BACTERIAL CONSORTIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut bacterial consortium in mealworms plays a role in the biodegradation of microplastics, particularly in the depolymerization of long-chain polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRYSTALLINE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Crystalline plastics, such as PE, have a structured molecular arrangement that makes them more resistant to depolymerization compared to amorphous plastics like PS and PVC.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMORPHOUS FRACTIONS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Amorphous fractions of polymers, such as those in PS and PVC, are more susceptible to endo-type depolymerization by mealworms compared to crystalline fractions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDO-TYPE DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Endo-type depolymerization is a process where mealworms break down polymer chains internally, leading to the formation of mid- and short-chain polymers with heterogeneous molecular weights.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEPURATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Depuration is the process by which mealworms excrete undigested microplastics from their intestines, with the time required varying depending on the type of plastic ingested.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRAW POWDER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Straw powder is another feedstock that mealworms can digest and excrete within a short period, similar to wheat bran.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VEGETABLES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vegetables are a natural food source for mealworms, which they can digest and excrete efficiently.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDIR TECHNIQUE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The LDIR (Laser Direct Infrared Spectrometry) technique is used to identify and count residual microplastics in the intestines of mealworms after depuration.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROPLASTIC-INDUCED DAMAGE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastic-induced damage refers to the physical harm and inflammatory responses caused by undigested microplastics in the intestines of organisms like mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRIENT UPTAKE">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrient uptake refers to the ability of organisms to absorb nutrients, which can be reduced due to microplastic-induced damage in the gut.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFLAMMATORY RESPONSES">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Inflammatory responses are potential health effects caused by the retention of undigested microplastics in the intestines of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYBUTYLENE ADIPATE TEREPHTHALATE (PBAT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Polybutylene adipate terephthalate (PBAT) is a biodegradable plastic that may be studied in future research to understand its biodegradation and oxidative stress effects on invertebrates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MITOCHONDRIAL DYSFUNCTION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mitochondrial dysfunction is a potential consequence of oxidative damage caused by microplastic ingestion, leading to abnormal development in organisms like mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE-DEGRADING MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene-degrading mealworms are Tenebrio molitor larvae that have been shown to break down polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOIL MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The soil microbiome is affected by microplastics and chemicals, with studies focusing on its role in plastic degradation and ecosystem health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZEBRAFISH LARVAE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Zebrafish larvae are used as a model organism to study the impact of polystyrene microplastics and cadmium toxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebrafish larvae are used as model organisms to study the toxic effects of microplastics and other pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CADMIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cadmium is a toxic heavy metal that, when combined with polystyrene microplastics, can alter neutrophil expression and metabolic regulation in zebrafish larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Cadmium is a toxic heavy metal whose toxicity can be influenced by the presence of polystyrene microplastics in zebrafish larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYTETRACYCLINE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxytetracycline is an antibiotic whose adsorption and desorption mechanisms on microplastics are studied, particularly in the presence of biofilms and other environmental factors.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HUMAN LIVER AND LUNG CELLS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Human liver and lung cells are used in studies to assess the mitochondrial damage caused by nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER TREATMENT WORKS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Water treatment works are studied to identify and quantify microplastics in potable water and their sources.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLY(BUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE) MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) microplastics are biodegradable plastics that can be affected by environmental factors such as biofilms, Cu(II), water pH, and dissolved organic matter during degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OXYTETRACYCLINE (OTC)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Oxytetracycline (OTC) is an antibiotic whose adsorption behavior on polylactic acid microplastics is influenced by microbial adhesion and degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CU(II)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cu(II) is a chemical that influences the degradation of poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) microplastics in the presence of biofilms and other environmental factors.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISSOLVED ORGANIC MATTER">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dissolved organic matter is a factor that affects the degradation of poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) microplastics in aquatic environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOFILMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Biofilms are microbial communities that can influence the degradation of poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) microplastics in the presence of environmental factors like Cu(II) and dissolved organic matter.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMIDAZOLIUM-BASED IONIC LIQUIDS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Imidazolium-based ionic liquids are chemicals that have toxic effects on Caenorhabditis elegans, primarily due to the generation of reactive oxygen species.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURFACE-ENHANCED RAMAN SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy is a technique used to detect microplastics smaller than 1 μm in environmental samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAPHENE OXIDE QUANTUM DOTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Graphene oxide quantum dots are nanomaterials used to enhance bioenergy production and carbon capture in microalgae by converting ultraviolet spectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;Graphene oxide quantum dots are nanomaterials used to enhance the bioenergy production and carbon capture capabilities of microalgae by converting ultraviolet spectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;Graphene oxide quantum dots are used to enhance bioenergy production with carbon capture in microalgae through ultraviolet spectrum conversion.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOENERGY PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Bioenergy production is enhanced by microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots through ultraviolet spectrum conversion.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioenergy production refers to the generation of energy from biological sources, such as microalgae, often combined with carbon capture technologies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBON CAPTURE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbon capture involves the process of capturing carbon dioxide emissions from sources like microalgae, which can be used to mitigate climate change.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon capture is a process enhanced by microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots to mitigate environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRAVIOLET SPECTRUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ultraviolet spectrum refers to the range of ultraviolet light, which can be converted by graphene oxide quantum dots to enhance bioenergy production and carbon capture in microalgae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG, L.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang, L. is a researcher involved in the study of enhancing bioenergy production and carbon capture using microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SU, Q.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Su, Q. is a researcher involved in the study of enhancing bioenergy production and carbon capture using microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SI, B.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Si, B. is a researcher involved in the study of enhancing bioenergy production and carbon capture using microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZHANG, Y.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Zhang, Y. is a researcher involved in the study of enhancing bioenergy production and carbon capture using microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YANG, H.">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Yang, H. is a researcher involved in the study of enhancing bioenergy production and carbon capture using microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPERIMENTAL CONTAINERS">
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
      <data key="d1">Milli-Q ultrapure water is used to rinse experimental containers to remove microplastic contamination.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLOME">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The metabolome of mealworms is reprogrammed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, with metabolic changes dependent on the molecular weight of the PS polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTESTINAL DYSBIOSIS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Intestinal dysbiosis refers to the disruption of the gut microbiome in mealworms caused by the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly with ultrahigh-molecular-weight PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMERCIAL PS FOAMS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Commercial PS foams, such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), are common sources of plastic waste that mealworms can biodegrade.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIGH-PURITY PS MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">High-purity PS microplastics are used in studies to investigate the biodegradation capabilities of mealworms, with varying molecular weights affecting degradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIAL POPULATIONS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbial populations in mealworms are influenced by the molecular weight of polystyrene, with distinct communities associated with low-, medium-, and ultrahigh-molecular-weight PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight-average molecular weight (Mw) is a measure used in the study to characterize the molecular weight changes of polystyrene microplastics after degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUMBER-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Number-average molecular weight (Mn) is a parameter used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polystyrene microplastics after passing through the mealworm gut.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIZE-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Size-average molecular weight (Mc) is a metric used to study the changes in molecular weight of polystyrene microplastics during biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-1">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-1 is a specific type of polystyrene microplastic with a defined molecular weight, used in the study to evaluate biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-2">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-2 is a polystyrene microplastic variant with a higher molecular weight than PS-1, used in the study to assess degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-3">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-3 is a polystyrene microplastic with a larger molecular weight, used in the study to investigate the biodegradation process by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-4">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-4 is a high molecular weight polystyrene microplastic variant, used in the study to analyze the degradation efficiency of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-5">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-5 is a polystyrene microplastic with a very high molecular weight, used in the study to evaluate the biodegradation capabilities of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-6">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-6 is the highest molecular weight polystyrene microplastic variant tested in the study, used to assess the limits of mealworm biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPECIFIC PS CONSUMPTION RATES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Specific PS consumption rates (SPCRs) are calculated to measure the rate at which mealworms consume polystyrene microplastics under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS REMOVAL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PS removal refers to the percentage of polystyrene microplastics degraded by mealworms, measured as part of the study's biodegradation assessment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRASS YIELD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Frass yield is an indirect indicator of digestibility, reflecting the amount of residual and modified polystyrene polymers excreted by mealworms after consuming PS MPs.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass yield is the ratio of frass produced to polystyrene microplastics consumed by mealworms, indicating the efficiency of the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS (PS MPS)">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, used as a feedstock in biodegradation studies with mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, which are being studied for their biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polystyrene, which mealworms can consume and degrade, contributing to their removal from the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION (ST)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Starvation is a condition tested in the study to evaluate the survival and behavior of mealworms without any food source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-1, PS-2, PS-3, PS-4, PS-5, PS-6">
      <data key="d0">MICROPLASTIC</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-1 to PS-6 represent different types of polystyrene microplastics with varying molecular weights, used in the study to assess biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THF-EXTRACTED FRACTION (CT)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">The THF-extracted fraction is a component of frass analyzed to determine the residual PS content after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MW, MN, MP)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight parameters (Mw, Mn, Mp) are measured using gel permeation chromatography to analyze the depolymerization of polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPCR (SPECIFIC POLYSTYRENE CONSUMPTION RATE)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">SPCR measures the rate at which mealworms consume polystyrene microplastics, providing insights into their biodegradation efficiency under different conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Starvation conditions refer to the lack of food, which can influence the behavior and consumption rates of mealworms, particularly in the context of consuming polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS-1 TO PS-6 GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">PS-1 to PS-6 groups represent different experimental groups in the study, each fed polystyrene microplastics of varying molecular weights to assess consumption and biodegradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CW, CE, AND CC VALUES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Cw, Ce, and Cc values represent the fractions of hydrophilic, lipophilic, and residual/modified polystyrene content in frass, respectively, used to estimate the extent of PS MP degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOME ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut microbiome analysis involves studying the microbial communities in the gut of mealworms, which play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIMITED-EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION (LD)">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Limited-extent depolymerization refers to the selective degradation of lower-molecular-weight polymers, leaving higher-molecular-weight polymers less affected during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROAD DEPOLYMERIZATION (BD)">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Broad depolymerization is a pattern observed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, where polymer chains are significantly reduced across a range of molecular weights.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERWORMS (Z. ATRATUS)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Superworms (Zophobas atratus) are larvae of darkling beetles that have been studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene and other plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREATER WAXWORMS (G. MELLONELLA)">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Greater waxworms (Galleria mellonella) are insect larvae known for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW MOLECULAR WEIGHT POLYSTYRENE (PS-L)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Low molecular weight polystyrene (PS-L) is a type of polystyrene with a molecular weight of 6.70 kDa, used in studies to investigate its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEDIUM MOLECULAR WEIGHT POLYSTYRENE (PS-M)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Medium molecular weight polystyrene (PS-M) is a type of polystyrene with a molecular weight of 192.9 kDa, studied for its biodegradation by mealworms and their gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRAHIGH MOLECULAR WEIGHT POLYSTYRENE (PS-H)">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ultrahigh molecular weight polystyrene (PS-H) is a type of polystyrene with a molecular weight of 1346 kDa, requiring complex microbial consortia for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLIC PATHWAY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolic pathway analysis is used to study alterations in the metabolomic profiles and pathways of mealworms under different diet conditions, particularly in response to polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RIBOFLAVIN METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Riboflavin metabolism is a metabolic pathway significantly perturbed in mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene, providing electron shuttles and redox mediators for extracellular electron transfer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISTIDINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Histidine metabolism is a metabolic pathway associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene in mealworms, linked to the pentose phosphate pathway, alanine metabolism, and glutamate metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYOXYLATE AND DICARBOXYLATE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Glyoxylate and dicarboxylate metabolism is a key energy metabolism pathway perturbed in mealworms during the biodegradation of low- and medium-molecular-weight polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARCH AND SUCROSE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Starch and sucrose metabolism is a metabolic pathway significantly perturbed in mealworms fed with medium- and ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene, influencing depolymerization patterns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASCORBATE AND ALDARATE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Ascorbate and aldarate metabolism is a metabolic pathway that helps maintain internal redox balance and defend against oxidative stress during polystyrene biodegradation in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LINOLEIC ACID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Linoleic acid metabolism is associated with membrane integrity repair and is more prevalent in mealworms fed with ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Starvation mealworms exhibit a more diversified gut microbial community due to the ingestion of frass, melted skeletons, dead mealworms, and digestion of body biomass during the test period.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METABOLOMIC PROFILES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolomic profiles of mealworms under different diet conditions reveal differentially expressed metabolites and perturbed metabolic pathways during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REDOX CAPABILITY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Redox capability refers to the ability of mealworms to coordinate metabolic mechanisms for the biodegradation of polystyrene, facilitated by electron shuttles and redox mediators.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT EPITHELIUM DAMAGE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gut epithelium damage is a potential consequence of microplastic ingestion in mealworms, leading to inflammation and immune responses during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY SUPPLY">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy supply refers to the metabolic pathways in mealworms that are modulated during the biodegradation of polystyrene, influencing their growth and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;The energy supply of mealworms is impacted by the ingestion of ultrahigh-MW polystyrene polymers, affecting their growth and metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRAHIGH-MW POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Ultrahigh-MW polymers are high molecular weight polystyrene polymers that pose challenges for biodegradation by mealworms, causing metabolic dysbiosis and oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GUT MICROBIOME ALTERATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The gut microbiome of mealworms is altered in response to the biodegradation of polystyrene polymers, affecting their metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRAHIGH-MW POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Ultrahigh-MW polystyrene is a high molecular weight polymer that poses challenges for biodegradation by mealworms, causing metabolic dysbiosis and oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOW-MW POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-MW polystyrene polymers are more efficiently biodegraded by mealworms compared to ultrahigh-MW polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COMMERCIAL EPS FOAM">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Commercial EPS foam is a type of polystyrene product that contains polymers with varying molecular weights, some of which are challenging to biodegrade.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) is a spectroscopic technique used to analyze the structure and composition of polymers, including plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THERMOPLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Thermoplastic waste refers to discarded thermoplastic materials that contribute to environmental pollution due to their slow degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PLASTIC BIO-OXIDATION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Plastic bio-oxidation is a process where plastics are broken down through oxidative reactions facilitated by microorganisms or enzymes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYANILINE/GRAPHENE MODIFIED ANODE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">A modified anode used in microbial electrochemical systems for efficient decolorization of azo dye wastewater.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AZO DYE WASTEWATER">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Azo dye wastewater is a type of industrial wastewater that can be treated using microbial electrochemical systems with modified anodes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEALWORM MEAL">
      <data key="d0">ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm meal is proposed as an alternative protein source for animal feed, with environmental assessments conducted to evaluate its sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PERFLUOROOCTANOIC ACID">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Perfluorooctanoic acid is a chemical studied for its phytotoxicity and environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PERFLUOROOCTANE SULFONATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Perfluorooctane sulfonate is a chemical studied for its phytotoxicity and environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANAMMOX">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Anammox is a process for nitrogen removal in wastewater treatment, enhanced by the presence of montmorillonite at reduced temperatures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MONTMORILLONITE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Montmorillonite is a clay mineral that enhances the nitrogen removal efficiency of anammox processes at reduced temperatures.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYBROMINATED DIPHENYL ETHER-47">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Polybrominated diphenyl ether-47 (PBDE-47) is a chemical pollutant that impacts breast cancer mice, as studied through metabolomics and lipidomics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIS(1,3-DICHLORO-2-PROPYL)PHOSPHATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Tris(1,3-dichloro-2-propyl)phosphate is an emerging organic contaminant that exhibits synergistic cytotoxicity with triphenyl phosphate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIPHENYL PHOSPHATE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Triphenyl phosphate is an emerging organic contaminant that exhibits synergistic cytotoxicity with tris(1,3-dichloro-2-propyl)phosphate.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER-SOLUBLE SYNTHETIC POLYMERS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Water-soluble synthetic polymers are chemicals whose environmental emission, usage, transport, transformation, persistence, and toxicity are studied in environmental science.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BREAST CANCER MICE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Breast cancer mice are used as a model to study the impact of polybrominated diphenyl ether-47 through metabolomics and lipidomics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SYNERGISTIC CYTOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Synergistic cytotoxicity refers to the combined toxic effects of tris(1,3-dichloro-2-propyl)phosphate and triphenyl phosphate on cells.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ULTRAVIOLET SPECTRUM CONVERSION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Ultraviolet spectrum conversion is a process enhanced by graphene oxide quantum dots to improve bioenergy production and carbon capture in microalgae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworm meal is evaluated for its environmental impact and potential as a sustainable alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYTOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
      <data key="d1">Perfluorooctanoic acid induces phytotoxicity, affecting plant metabolism and growth.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITIONAL QUALITY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
      <data key="d1">Wheat bran is reviewed for its composition and nutritional quality, highlighting its potential as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENTAL TOXICITY">
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
      <data key="d1">Water-soluble synthetic polymers are studied for their environmental emission, usage, transport, transformation, persistence, and toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTHRONE METHOD">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The anthrone method is a biochemical assay used to measure the level of hydrolyzed carbohydrates in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SULFO-PHOSPHO-VANILLIN ASSAY (SPVA)">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The sulfo-phospho-vanillin assay is a biochemical method used to quantify total lipids in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The biochemical composition of mealworms, including protein, carbohydrates, and lipids, was analyzed to understand the effects of plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOHYDRATES CONTENT">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbohydrates provide energy for mealworms, with the highest levels observed in PLA-fed larvae and the lowest in PVC and PS-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOCHEMICAL CHARACTERISTICS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Biochemical characteristics refer to the protein, carbohydrate, and lipid content in mealworms, which are analyzed to assess the impact of different diets on their metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIGLYCERIDES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Triglycerides are a form of stored fat in mealworms, with levels connected to the duration of starvation periods.&lt;SEP&gt;Triglycerides are a type of lipid that serves as a primary energy source for mealworms during starvation or stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Triglycerides are a type of lipid stored in the fat body of insects, playing a critical role in energy reserves and survival during starvation.&lt;SEP&gt;Triglycerides are fat reserves in mealworms that serve as a primary energy source during food deprivation or starvation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOGEN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen is a carbohydrate reserve in mealworms that plays a role in sustaining the nervous system and energy metabolism during food deprivation.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycogen is a carbohydrate stored in insects and metabolized during the initial stages of starvation.&lt;SEP&gt;Glycogen is a carbohydrate stored in insects, primarily in the haemolymph, and is important for sustaining the nervous system and other organs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRESS FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Stress factors include starvation and exposure to non-nutritive materials like PVC and PS, which affect mealworm metabolism and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY RESERVES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy reserves are the stored energy in an organism that are used for growth, maintenance, and reproduction, as described in the DEB model.&lt;SEP&gt;Energy reserves in mealworms, such as lipids and carbohydrates, are used to maintain basic metabolic rates during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The amount of energy stored in larvae, which is used to maintain structures and support growth during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADAPTIVE PREDISPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Adaptive predisposition refers to the ability of mealworms' gut microbiome to adapt and degrade various plastics, including PS, PVC, and PLA.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTERASE-LIPASE C8">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Esterase-lipase C8 is an enzyme that shows elevated activity in mealworm digestive tracts and bacterial communities, particularly in the presence of polyethylene regranulate and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C8 ESTER LIPASE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">C8 ester lipase is an enzyme that shows elevated activity in mealworm digestive tracts and bacterial communities, particularly in the presence of polyethylene regranulate and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANTEA SPP.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Pantoea spp. are bacteria identified in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed polyethylene diets, contributing to waste degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IRON">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Iron is a trace element required for the activity of nitrogenase, an enzyme involved in nitrogen fixation by symbiotic bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SULFUR">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Sulfur is a trace element necessary for the activity of nitrogenase, an enzyme involved in nitrogen fixation by symbiotic bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VANADIUM">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Vanadium is a trace element required for the activity of nitrogenase, an enzyme involved in nitrogen fixation by symbiotic bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGANESE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Manganese is a trace element necessary for the activity of nitrogenase, an enzyme involved in nitrogen fixation by symbiotic bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATMOSPHERIC NITROGEN">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Atmospheric nitrogen is assimilated by symbiotic bacteria in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, enabling protein synthesis.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMMUNE DEFENSES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The immune defenses of Tenebrio molitor are studied to understand its resistance mechanisms and its role as a beneficial pest.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METHANOGENIC COMMUNITIES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Methanogenic communities are groups of microorganisms studied using quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction (qPCR) techniques.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUANTITATIVE REAL-TIME POLYMERASE CHAIN REACTION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction (qPCR) is a technique used to detect and quantify methanogenic communities in environmental samples.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZYME PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66</data>
      <data key="d1">Symbiotic bacteria in mealworm digestive tracts are essential for enzyme production, which aids in the degradation of materials like plastics.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NITROGEN ASSIMILATION">
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
      <data key="d1">Xanthomonadales bacteria in the gut microbiome of insects contribute to nitrogen assimilation, supporting survival in nutrient-deficient environments.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POLYMER WASTE">
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
      <data key="d1">Hydrolytic enzymes in the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor larvae degrade polymer waste, such as polyethylene and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECYCLING METHODS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Recycling methods for polystyrene include mechanical, chemical, and thermal processes, but biodegradation by organisms like mealworms offers an environmentally friendly alternative.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOLOMETABOLIC INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Holometabolic insects, such as Tenebrio molitor, undergo complete metamorphosis and are studied using the DEB model to understand their growth and development under different conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY FLUXES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy fluxes refer to the movement of energy within organisms, as described by the DEB model, which is used to assess the effects of polystyrene on mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOOD AVAILABILITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Food availability (f) is a parameter in the DEB model that determines the rate at which energy is assimilated by Tenebrio molitor larvae, affecting their growth and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Food availability is a key factor in the DEB model, influencing the growth and development of mealworms, particularly when their diet includes polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GROWTH TRAJECTORIES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Growth trajectories are studied using the DEB model to understand how mealworms develop when exposed to polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LARVAL STAGE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The larval stage of mealworms is the focus of the study, as it is during this phase that the effects of polystyrene on growth and development are assessed.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REPRODUCTION BUFFER">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The reproduction buffer is a concept in the DEB model that refers to the energy reserves accumulated by mealworms during the larval stage, which are used for reproduction.&lt;SEP&gt;The reproduction buffer is a concept in the DEB model that refers to the energy reserves required for Tenebrio molitor larvae to transition to the pupation stage.&lt;SEP&gt;The reproduction buffer is a concept in the DEB model that represents the energy allocated for reproduction, which influences pupation in larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The reproduction buffer is a concept in the DEB model that represents the energy threshold required for pupation in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MATURITY MAINTENANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Maturity maintenance is a concept in the DEB model that describes the energy required to maintain the physiological state of mealworms during their larval stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Maturity maintenance refers to the energy costs required for the development and maturation of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which can be influenced by PS diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOMATIC MAINTENANCE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Somatic maintenance refers to the energy costs associated with maintaining the body structure of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which can be affected by toxic substances like PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Somatic maintenance refers to the energy required to maintain the body structure of mealworms, as described in the DEB model.&lt;SEP&gt;The process by which larvae use energy reserves to maintain their structural integrity during starvation or limited food availability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEB MODEL">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The DEB (Dynamic Energy Budget) model is used to describe the energy dynamics and survival criteria of organisms, such as larvae, under varying conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is a theoretical framework used to analyze the growth and reserve dynamics of organisms, including Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is used to analyze the development and reserve dynamics of Tenebrio molitor larvae, particularly in relation to their ability to biodegrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is used to analyze the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different feeding conditions, including starvation and polystyrene diets.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is used to analyze the growth patterns and energy allocation of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different conditions, including PS diets.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is used to study the energy dynamics and physiological responses of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different dietary conditions, including starvation and PS consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION GROUPS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Starvation groups refer to experimental setups where Tenebrio molitor larvae are deprived of food to study their survival and growth under different conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS LARGE GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The PS large group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with larger pieces of polystyrene, used to study the effects of PS size on degradation and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS large group consists of older and larger Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with polystyrene to study their consumption and growth patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS large group is an experimental group where Tenebrio molitor larvae were fed large amounts of polystyrene to study its effects on survival and energy dynamics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS + OAT GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The PS + oat group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with both polystyrene and crushed oatmeal to study their growth and feeding behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS + oat group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with polystyrene and oatmeal, used to study the effects of dietary supplements on PS degradation and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS + oat group is an experimental group where larvae were fed polystyrene supplemented with oats to study the effects of dietary supplementation on growth and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM CHIPS">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam chips, a form of polystyrene, are used in experiments to assess their consumption and potential degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUSHED OATMEAL">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Crushed oatmeal is provided to Tenebrio molitor larvae in combination with polystyrene to study their feeding behavior and growth under mixed diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LABORATORY CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Laboratory conditions refer to the controlled environment (25°C, 80% humidity) where Tenebrio molitor larvae are reared for experimental studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION 1 GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Starvation 1 group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae deprived of food and isolated to prevent consumption of droppings or moult residues.&lt;SEP&gt;The Starvation 1 group is a control group in the study where Tenebrio molitor larvae were deprived of food to observe physiological responses and energy dynamics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STARVATION 2 GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Starvation 2 group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae deprived of food but allowed to consume droppings and moult residues.&lt;SEP&gt;The Starvation 2 group is another control group where larvae were fed only droppings and moult residues to study energy availability and assimilation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESERVE DYNAMICS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Reserve dynamics refer to the changes in energy reserves of Tenebrio molitor larvae, analyzed using the DEB model under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VON BERTALANFFY GROWTH RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The Von Bertalanffy growth rate is a parameter used in the DEB model to describe the growth rate of Tenebrio molitor larvae under constant reserve density.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMINS AND MINERALS">
      <data key="d0">FOOD SAFETY</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamins and minerals were added to the larvae's diet to study their impact on growth and development in combination with polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS LARGE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Older and larger larvae compared to the PS group, with a mass in the range 0.06–0.1 g each, fed with polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS + OAT.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with polystyrene (PS) and crushed oatmeal, with oatmeal provided every 10 days and removed after 24 hours.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS + OAT. + VIT.">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Larvae fed with polystyrene (PS), crushed oatmeal, and vitamins/minerals, with supplements provided every 10 days and removed after 24 hours.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REPRODUCTION RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A parameter measured in the study to assess the reproductive capacity of larvae under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GROWTH RATE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The rate at which larvae increase in size, measured under different feeding conditions, including polystyrene and supplementary foods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYROFOAM POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam polystyrene is a type of plastic waste that is studied for its mass loss and degradation in the presence of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS MASS LOSS">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PS mass loss refers to the reduction in mass of polystyrene over time, which is measured to assess the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL CRITERION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The survival criterion in the DEB model determines whether an organism can survive based on its ability to meet somatic maintenance costs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOMATIC MAINTENANCE COSTS">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Somatic maintenance costs refer to the energy required for an organism to maintain its structure and survive, as described in the DEB model.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STRUCTURAL LENGTH">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Structural length is a parameter in the DEB model that represents the size of an organism and is used to assess survival and growth.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCALED RESERVES DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Scaled reserves density is a concept in the DEB model that describes the density of energy reserves relative to the maximum possible reserves.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VOLUMETRIC LENGTH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Volumetric length is a measure used in the DEB model to assess the growth of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Volumetric length is a parameter in the DEB model that reflects the size of larval structures and is influenced by reserve density and food availability.&lt;SEP&gt;Volumetric length is a parameter in the DEB model that represents the volume of an organism and is influenced by changes in reserves density.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EGG PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Egg production is a process in the DEB model that depends on the energy allocated to the reproduction buffer during the imago stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg production refers to the reproductive output of Tenebrio molitor, which can be influenced by energy allocation and toxic effects of PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY ALLOCATION">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy allocation refers to the distribution of energy resources in Tenebrio molitor larvae, which is influenced by factors such as food availability and toxic effects of PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMAGO STAGE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The imago stage is the adult phase of Tenebrio molitor, during which reproduction occurs, and energy allocation is critical.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONTROL SAMPLE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The control sample is used as a baseline to compare the effects of different feeding conditions on Tenebrio molitor larvae, including their density and survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS + OAT. + VIT. GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The PS + oat. + vit. group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with polystyrene, oatmeal, and vitamins, used to study the combined effects of dietary supplements on PS degradation and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;This group consists of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with polystyrene supplemented with oatmeal and vitamins, used to study the effects of dietary supplementation on their survival and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVAL CURVES">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Survival curves are used to analyze the survival rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different feeding conditions, providing insights into their energetic costs and maintenance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIETARY SUPPLEMENTS">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Dietary supplements, such as oatmeal and vitamins, are used to improve the survival and functioning of Tenebrio molitor larvae, enhancing their ability to degrade PS.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCALED RESERVE DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Scaled reserve density (e) is a parameter in the DEB model that represents the energy reserves available for growth, maintenance, and reproduction in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEB MODEL PARAMETERS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">DEB model parameters, such as $\hat{r}_B$, $L_{\text{Im0}}$, and $k_M$, are used to analyze the growth and energy dynamics of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY CONDUCTANCE">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Energy conductance (ν) is a parameter in the DEB model that describes the rate at which energy is transferred within the organism, used to analyze the effects of starvation on Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESERVE DENSITY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Reserve density (e) is a measure of the energy reserves in Tenebrio molitor larvae, which decreases during starvation or insufficient food availability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS + OAT + VIT GROUP">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The PS + oat + vit group is an experimental group where larvae were fed polystyrene supplemented with oats and vitamins to study the effects of additional nutrients on growth and survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GROWTH INHIBITION">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Growth inhibition refers to the reduction in growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae due to insufficient or toxic diets like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE">
      <data key="d0">TOXICOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Physiological response refers to the changes in energy dynamics, reserve density, and growth patterns of Tenebrio molitor larvae under starvation or toxic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESERVES">
      <data key="d0">BIODEGRADATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Reserves in mealworms, such as triglycerides, play a critical role in their survival during food deprivation, serving as a primary energy source.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAEMOLYMPH">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Haemolymph is the fluid in insects that contains carbohydrates and other nutrients, essential for maintaining the nervous system and organ function.&lt;SEP&gt;Haemolymph is the fluid in the circulatory system of insects, containing carbohydrates that are critical for sustaining the nervous system during food deprivation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FAT BODY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">The fat body in insects is a key organ for energy storage, particularly for accumulating lipids, which are crucial for survival during food shortages.&lt;SEP&gt;The fat body is an organ in insects where fat reserves, such as triglycerides, are stored and utilized during food deprivation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEB MODEL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The DEB model analysis is used to study the development and reserve dynamics of Tenebrio molitor larvae, particularly in relation to their ability to survive under food shortages and potential biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STYRENE ANALOGUES">
      <data key="d0">CHEMICAL</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrene analogues are chemical compounds generated from the degradation of polystyrene, often found in environmental pollutants such as ocean coastlines.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NORTH-EAST PACIFIC OCEAN">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">The North-East Pacific Ocean is a region where styrene analogues generated from polystyrene degradation have been detected, indicating pollution from plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HAWAII">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hawaii is a location where styrene analogues from polystyrene degradation have been found, highlighting the spread of plastic pollution in marine environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DYNAMIC ENERGY BUDGET">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">A dynamic energy budget is a model used to study the life-cycle of holometabolous insects, providing insights into their growth and development under different conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOLOMETABOLOUS INSECTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANISM</data>
      <data key="d1">Holometabolous insects undergo complete metamorphosis, and their life-cycle is studied using dynamic energy budget models.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECYCLING OF POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">The recycling of polystyrene is a research topic that explores methods to reduce plastic waste and its environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEVELOPMENTAL PLASTICITY">
      <data key="d0">RESEARCH STUDY</data>
      <data key="d1">Developmental plasticity refers to the ability of organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor, to adapt their development in response to different diets and environmental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS GROUP">
      <data key="d2">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
      <data key="d1">Styrofoam chips are fed to the PS group of Tenebrio molitor larvae to assess their consumption and potential degradation.</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
