    <edge source="GUT MICROBIOME" target="MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">205.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiome of mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) plays a crucial and multifaceted role in the biodegradation of various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), polyethylene (PE), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). This microbial community is essential for breaking down plastic waste, with specific bacterial populations and enzymes facilitating the degradation process. The gut microbiome adapts to different types of plastics, leading to shifts in microbial community structure and composition. For instance, when mealworms are fed polystyrene, the dominant bacterial phyla observed are Proteobacteria and Firmicutes, with specific strains such as *Citrobacter sp.*, *Kosakonia sp.*, *Spiroplasma*, and *Lactococcus* playing key roles in the biodegradation process.

The microbial composition of the mealworm gut is significantly influenced by the type of plastic consumed. Diets consisting of polystyrene (PS) or other plastics lead to distinct microbial populations, which in turn affect the efficiency of plastic degradation. The gut microbiome not only aids in the depolymerization and mineralization of plastics but also influences metabolic pathways, reducing the molecular weight and polydispersity of polystyrene microplastics. Additionally, emulsifying factors produced by the gut bacteria further facilitate the breakdown of plastic polymers.

Overall, the gut microbiome of mealworms is a dynamic and adaptable system that plays an essential role in both the digestion and biodegradation of plastics. Its ability to shift in response to different plastic diets underscores its potential as a biological tool for addressing plastic waste pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms is essential for the biodegradation of plastics, including PS, PVC, and PLA.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
