    <edge source="GUT MICROBIOME" target="POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d3">84.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The biodegradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms leads to changes in their gut microbiome, highlighting the interaction between plastic degradation and microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor enhances the biodegradation of polystyrene, demonstrating the role of microbial communities in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae is essential for the depolymerization and biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene, leading to the formation of degradation compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms plays a significant role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor secretes factors that enhance the biodegradation of polystyrene, demonstrating its role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of organisms like mealworms and waxworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms is essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of insect larvae adapts to digest polystyrene, with specific bacteria playing a crucial role.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae includes bacteria that degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa</data>
    </edge>
