    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="TENEBRIO MOLITOR">
      <data key="d3">2313.0</data>
      <data key="d4">*Tenebrio molitor*, commonly known as mealworms, and their larvae are highly effective agents in the biodegradation of a wide range of synthetic plastics and natural lignocellulosic materials. These include polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyurethane (PU) foam, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polylactic acid (PLA), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), expanded polystyrene (EPS), Styrofoam, cellulose, and corn straw. The biodegradation process is facilitated by the gut microbiota of the larvae, which includes specific bacteria such as *Priestia megaterium S1*, *Klebsiella oxytoca*, *Bacillus sp. PELW2042*, *Bacillus*, *Exiguobacterium*, and *Mixta tenebrionis*. These gut microbes, along with enzymatic processes, break down plastics and lignocellulosic materials into smaller particles or mineralize them, contributing to environmental sustainability.

Mealworms are particularly effective in degrading polystyrene and polyethylene, which are among the most common and persistent plastic pollutants. The process involves oxidation, depolymerization, and metabolic activities carried out by the gut bacteria. This ability highlights the potential of *Tenebrio molitor* as a natural solution for managing plastic waste and reducing environmental pollution. The larvae are capable of biodegrading plastics, including EPS and Styrofoam, breaking them down into low-molecular-weight fragments or less harmful substances. This process is particularly effective when the larvae are fed at specific ages and particle sizes, especially in the case of micro-polystyrene (MPS).

In addition to polystyrene and polyethylene, *Tenebrio molitor* larvae also contribute to the biodegradation of PET, PVC, PLA, and other plastics through enzymatic and metabolic processes, as well as through the activity of their gut microbiota. The metabolic pathways and gut microbial communities of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae are crucial in the biodegradation of plastics like PET, PS, and LDPE, further emphasizing their role in sustainable environmental practices. Mealworms are also effective in biodegrading thermocol and Styrofoam, with their efficiency influenced by environmental conditions.

Furthermore, *Tenebrio molitor* larvae play a key role in the biodegradation of cellulose and corn straw, expanding their utility beyond synthetic plastics. The gut bacteria</data>
