    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYPROPYLENE">
      <data key="d3">37.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polypropylene can be biodegraded by yellow mealworms and superworms, reducing its environmental persistence.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene is studied for its resistance to biodegradation, with limited success in finding effective microbial degraders.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene is a target of biodegradation processes, particularly by bacterial isolates.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene in surgical masks is subject to biodegradation by yellow mealworms, leading to its breakdown into smaller molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene undergoes biodegradation when ingested by yellow mealworms, leading to changes in molecular weight and the formation of new organic substances.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="GALLERIA MELLONELLA">
      <data key="d3">134.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Galleria mellonella can digest plastic due to its sharp mandibles and gut microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae are known for their ability to biodegrade plastics, contributing to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae contribute to the biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics, highlighting their role in environmental cleanup.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae contribute to the biodegradation of LDPE, highlighting their role in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;The greater wax moth plays a role in the biodegradation of LDPE through chewing and ingestion&lt;SEP&gt;Wax moth caterpillars are involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene, with their gut microbiome playing a key role.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax moth caterpillars (Galleria mellonella) contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene, aiding in plastic waste reduction&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae play a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics, including polyethylene and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae contribute to the biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics, demonstrating their potential in environmental cleanup.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella is studied for its potential role in the biodegradation of plastics, including polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella contributes to the biodegradation of polyethylene, demonstrating its potential in addressing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae are involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene, with changes in gut microbiota diversity observed.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella caterpillars contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene, showcasing their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae are capable of biodegrading plastics, with their gut microbiome playing a key role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae are capable of biodegrading polyethylene and polystyrene, with their gut microbiome playing a key role in the process.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="GUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d3">317.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiota of insects, particularly mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms (Zophobas atratus), play a crucial and versatile role in the biodegradation of various plastic polymers. These microorganisms are essential for breaking down complex plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polyurethane (PU), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), nanopolystyrene, and Styrofoam, into simpler compounds such as carbon dioxide (CO2). Specific bacteria within the gut microbiota produce extracellular enzymes that catalyze the depolymerization of plastics, facilitating their degradation. For instance, the gut microbiota of mealworm larvae has been shown to effectively degrade polystyrene, polyethylene, and polyurethane, while Tenebrio molitor larvae are particularly effective in biodegrading PET and polyurethane foam. Additionally, the gut microbiota of these insects also contributes to the biodegradation of corn straw, further demonstrating its versatility.

The suppression or impairment of gut microbiota in these organisms inhibits the biodegradation process, highlighting their critical role. The gut microbiota not only aids in the breakdown of plastics but also influences the functional groups and bacterial communities involved in the degradation process. This makes gut microbiota a key player in addressing plastic pollution through biodegradation. Furthermore, the gut microbiota in mealworms is responsible for breaking down polyethylene microplastics into lower molecular weight fractions, contributing to environmental sustainability. Overall, the gut microbiota in these insects is essential for the biodegradation of a wide range of plastic materials, making it a vital component in efforts to mitigate plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855</data>
