    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="STYRENE MONOMERS">
      <data key="d3">63.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene monomers are biodegradable, unlike their polymerized form, polystyrene, which is more resistant to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike the polymer form of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike polystyrene polymers, which are more resistant.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are more susceptible to biodegradation than polystyrene, highlighting the importance of breaking down polymers into smaller units&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike their polymerized form, polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers, unlike polystyrene, are susceptible to biodegradation, highlighting the challenges of plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers, the building blocks of polystyrene, are susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are susceptible to biodegradation, forming part of the degradation process of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene monomers are susceptible to biodegradation, unlike their polymerized form, polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
