    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYETHYLENE (PE)">
      <data key="d3">117.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation is the process by which polyethylene is broken down by microorganisms and insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by mealworms results in the conversion of ingested PE into CO2 and lower molecular weight compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is biodegraded by microorganisms, resulting in surface pitting and increased oxygen content.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) undergoes biodegradation in the guts of superworms and yellow mealworms, leading to depolymerization and molecular weight reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene undergoes biodegradation by mealworm larvae, resulting in depolymerization and molecular weight reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is degraded by bacterial strains isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is the target of biodegradation by bacterial strains, reducing its environmental persistence.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, with the gut microbiome playing a key role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is degraded by mealworms, demonstrating the potential for biodegradation of this common plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is degraded by lesser waxworms, highlighting the potential for biological solutions to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene is biodegraded by Achroia grisea, with the process enhanced by additional nutrition.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is subject to biodegradation by mealworms, with the extent of biodegradation depending on the type of PE and its molecular weight.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="OXIDATIVE STRESS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation of polyethylene by mealworm larvae induces oxidative stress, which can be mitigated by dietary supplements like bran.</data>
