    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MEALWORM LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">105.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae are capable of biodegrading polyethylene (PE), though the process has physiological and metabolic impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae are capable of biodegrading polyethylene microplastics, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae biodegrade PE microplastics, demonstrating their ability to break down plastic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae play a key role in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE), breaking it down into smaller components&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae play a key role in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae play a significant role in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) and other plastics, contributing to environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae play a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics, breaking them down into smaller components&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae play a key role in the biodegradation of various plastics, including polystyrene, polyurethane, and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae play a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), breaking it down into smaller components.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae have the potential to biodegrade polystyrene foam, contributing to environmental waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae biodegrade expanded polystyrene, with their gut microbiome playing a key role.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae play a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics, breaking them down into smaller components</data>
