    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="LDPE">
      <data key="d3">119.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE degrades faster than LLDPE and HDPE, and its biodegradation has been studied using microorganisms from various environmental sources.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a non-hydrolysable polymer with a longer half-life compared to PET in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a non-hydrolysable polymer with a longer half-life compared to PET in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is biodegraded by mealworms, with changes in molecular weight and chemical structure observed&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE was analyzed for microbial degradation, showing changes in surface structure and biofilm formation&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is one of the plastics targeted for biodegradation by mealworms in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, with specific consumption rates and physiological responses.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is subject to biodegradation by microorganisms, though at slow rates, especially after photo- or thermo-oxidation.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE undergoes broad depolymerization during biodegradation by mealworms, resulting in significant molecular weight reduction&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is more susceptible to biodegradation by insect larvae due to its lower crystallinity.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is another plastic polymer studied for its biodegradation by T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae, showing both broad and limited extent depolymerization patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is being studied for its biodegradation by certain bacteria, fungi, and insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, resulting in the reduction of its molecular weight and the formation of smaller polymer fractions&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is biodegraded by mealworms through depolymerization and oxidation, with some conversion to CO2.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="HDPE">
      <data key="d3">57.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HDPE is biodegraded by Bacillus sp. PELW2042 and Tenebrio molitor, demonstrating the potential of biological methods for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE is studied for its biodegradation potential by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE was studied for microbial degradation, with observed changes in surface texture and biofilm formation&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, with specific consumption rates and physiological responses.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE is more resistant to biodegradation compared to LDPE and LLDPE, with degradation influenced by its molecular weight.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE is more resistant to biodegradation due to its higher crystallinity, but it can still be degraded by microorganisms under specific conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE undergoes limited depolymerization during biodegradation by mealworms due to its high crystallinity and stability&lt;SEP&gt;High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is less biodegradable by insect larvae due to its higher crystallinity.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="PS">
      <data key="d3">36.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation of PS by larvae and their gut bacteria was studied to understand its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) was analyzed for microbial degradation, showing erosion and biofilm formation on its surface&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) is another non-hydrolysable polymer with a longer half-life compared to PET in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) is another non-hydrolysable polymer with a longer half-life compared to PET in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </edge>
