    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYSTYRENE (PS)">
      <data key="d3">247.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation is a promising solution for managing polystyrene (PS) waste and mitigating its environmental impact. Polystyrene (PS), a widely used plastic polymer, can undergo biodegradation through the action of specific bacterial strains, enzymes, and insect larvae, particularly Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) and their gut microbiota. The biodegradation process involves the breakdown of PS into simpler compounds, including CO2 and lower molecular weight intermediates, through metabolic pathways and oxidative depolymerization. This process is facilitated by the gut microbiome of mealworms, which plays a critical role in depolymerizing PS and reducing its molecular weight. 

Studies have identified specific bacterial strains, such as WJ2020, WJ2025, and Enterobacter sp. LG3, as capable of breaking down PS under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions, leading to the production of CO2. Additionally, Tenebrio molitor larvae have been shown to degrade PS, with mass loss observed during experiments, indicating effective biodegradation. However, PS is less efficiently biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to other materials like PLA, which can lead to mild dehydration and growth inhibition in the larvae. The rate of biodegradation is influenced by factors such as the presence of soy protein or bran, environmental conditions like temperature, and the molecular weight of the polymer.

The biodegradation of PS involves surface changes, oxidative processes, and the release of reactive oxygen species (ROS) within the organisms. This process can lead to the formation of smaller plastic particles or the mineralization of PS into CO2. Aged PS microplastics are also biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to the reduction of microplastic pollution. Overall, the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, their gut microbiota, and specific bacterial strains highlights their potential role in environmental remediation and addressing the challenges posed by plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
