    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="CRYSTALLINITY">
      <data key="d3">48.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Crystallinity affects the biodegradation of polyethylene, with higher crystallinity polymers like HDPE being more resistant to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity affects the biodegradation rate of plastics, with higher crystallinity slowing down the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity affects the biodegradation rate of plastics, with higher crystallinity slowing down the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity negatively impacts the biodegradability of plastics by mealworms, with amorphous polymers being more easily consumed than crystalline ones.&lt;SEP&gt;Higher crystallinity in polymers like polyethylene increases resistance to biodegradation due to tighter molecular packing.&lt;SEP&gt;The crystallinity of polyethylene (PE) negatively impacts its biodegradability by insect larvae, as higher crystallinity makes degradation more difficult.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </edge>
