    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">40.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation involves the breakdown of low-density polyethylene by mealworm larvae, facilitated by their gut microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) can be biodegraded by insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, aiding in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene is biodegraded by mealworms, with or without the involvement of gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE undergoes broad depolymerization during biodegradation, making it more biodegradable than LLDPE and HDPE&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene has been studied for its biodegradation, particularly when thermally oxidized and fragmented.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </edge>
