    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d3">202.0</data>
      <data key="d4">**Tenebrio obscurus**, a species of darkling beetle larvae commonly referred to as dark mealworms, plays a significant role in the biodegradation of various types of plastics, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), and polystyrene (PS). Similar to its relative **Tenebrio molitor**, **Tenebrio obscurus** larvae are highly effective in breaking down these plastics, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste. 

The larvae of **Tenebrio obscurus** are particularly noted for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene microplastics, breaking them down into smaller particles and molecules. This process is facilitated by the gut microbiota of the larvae, which are essential for the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics. However, some studies suggest that the depolymerization pattern of LDPE and PS by **Tenebrio obscurus** may be independent of gut microbes, indicating a complex mechanism of biodegradation.

While **Tenebrio obscurus** is effective in biodegrading LDPE, its efficiency is sometimes reported to be slightly lower than that of **Tenebrio molitor**. However, other studies indicate that **Tenebrio obscurus** may be more efficient than **Tenebrio molitor** in biodegrading LDPE, as evidenced by greater reductions in molecular weight. This discrepancy suggests that the efficiency of biodegradation may vary depending on specific conditions or experimental setups.

In addition to LDPE, **Tenebrio obscurus** larvae are also capable of biodegrading polystyrene, with significant molecular weight reduction and evidence of depolymerization and mineralization observed. This highlights the potential of **Tenebrio obscurus** in plastic waste management, particularly for polystyrene and polyethylene-based plastics.

Overall, **Tenebrio obscurus** is a promising species for biodegradation studies, showcasing its ability to break down a variety of plastics and contributing to the development of sustainable solutions for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae are another organism capable of degrading plastics, similar to mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </edge>
