    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MINERALIZATION">
      <data key="d3">76.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation and mineralization are interconnected processes where organic substances like polystyrene are broken down and converted into inorganic compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene often involves their mineralization into inorganic substances such as CO2.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation of polystyrene involves its mineralization into CO2 and water, a key process in its environmental breakdown.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineralization is the final step in plastic biodegradation, converting organic substances into inorganic compounds like CO2 and H2O.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation processes often lead to the mineralization of organic substances into CO2&lt;SEP&gt;Mineralization is a key process in the biodegradation of plastics, converting them into inorganic compounds like CO2.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineralization is a key process in the biodegradation of plastics, converting them into inorganic compounds like CO2.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation often leads to mineralization, where organic substances are converted into inorganic compounds like CO2.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineralization is the final step in the biodegradation of plastics, converting intermediates into CO2 and H2O.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d3">102.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation is a key process in the breakdown of low-density polyethylene by various microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation is the process by which low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is broken down by bacterial strains and insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is biodegraded by mealworms, with regional variations in efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, although the extent of depolymerization varies.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene is studied for its biodegradation potential, addressing its persistence in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is less resistant to biodegradation due to its branched structure, making it a target for microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor, with its molecular weight influencing the degradation rate.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor, with its molecular weight influencing the degradation rate.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) can be degraded by microorganisms found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is more susceptible to biodegradation by mealworms due to its lower crystallinity and significant branching.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation is the process by which mealworms break down low-density polyethylene (LDPE) into smaller particles or mineralize it.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is biodegraded by mealworms, resulting in the production of microplastics and nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (GPC)">
      <data key="d3">31.0</data>
      <data key="d4">GPC is suggested for future studies to understand the molecular weight changes in polyethylene during biodegradation by Achroia grisea.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC is used to determine molecular weight changes in plastic polymers during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC is used to study depolymerization and biodegradation of plastics by analyzing molecular weight distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) is used to analyze the molecular weight changes in microplastics during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
