    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation processes target low-density polyethylene (LDPE) to reduce its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a plastic that is resistant to degradation, but certain organisms like Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade it&lt;SEP&gt;Low density polyethylene (LDPE) can be degraded using protocols similar to those for polystyrene, involving Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYPROPYLENE (PP)">
      <data key="d3">53.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation is the process by which polypropylene (PP) is broken down by organisms such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation of polypropylene (PP) is influenced by its molecular weight (MW) and the synergistic actions of Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) can be degraded using the same protocols as those for polystyrene, involving Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is resistant to biodegradation due to its carbon-carbon backbone structure, making it a persistent environmental pollutant&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation of polypropylene (PP) is facilitated by organisms like T. molitor and Z. atratus, with gut microbiota playing a key role.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a petro-based polymer that may be studied for its biodegradation and oxidative stress effects on invertebrates.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYLACTIC ACID (PLA)">
      <data key="d3">50.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PLA is more efficiently biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to PS and PVC due to its high polymer degradability.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) can be biodegraded using similar protocols as those for polystyrene, involving Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable polymer that requires specific conditions for degradation, often used in eco-friendly packaging&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic used as a comparison to non-biodegradable plastics like polyethylene (PE) in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is biodegraded via enzymatic hydrolysis, causing less oxidative stress compared to other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that can be broken down by living organisms, offering a more environmentally friendly alternative to traditional plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic that may be studied for its biodegradation and oxidative stress effects on invertebrates.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)">
      <data key="d3">52.0</data>
      <data key="d4">High-crystallinity PET is biodegraded by mealworms, with the degradation rate influenced by polymer chain size and gut microbiomes.&lt;SEP&gt;High-crystallinity PET is biodegraded by mealworms, with the degradation rate influenced by polymer chain size and gut microbiomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) can be degraded using protocols similar to those for polystyrene, involving Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is highly resistant to biodegradation, but recent advancements have shown improved degradation using engineered enzymes&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a petro-based polymer that may be studied for its biodegradation and oxidative stress effects on invertebrates.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)">
      <data key="d3">28.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PVC has the lowest biodegradation efficiency in Tenebrio molitor larvae, resulting in incomplete biodegradation and malnutrition.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) can be degraded using similar protocols as those for polystyrene, involving Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is less efficiently biodegraded by mealworms compared to other microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) releases chlorinated intermediates and hydrogen chloride during biodegradation, causing significant oxidative stress in organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </edge>
