    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="CHEMOSPHERE">
      <data key="d3">36.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chemosphere publishes research on biodegradation, including studies on Tenebrio molitor and plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemosphere publishes research on the biodegradation of plastics by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemosphere publishes research on the biodegradation of environmental pollutants, including plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemosphere publishes studies on the biodegradation of polystyrene wastes by yellow mealworms and factors affecting the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemosphere publishes research on the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms and other organisms, contributing to environmental science.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYURETHANE/CLAY RIGID FOAM COMPOSITES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyurethane/clay rigid foam composites are tested for biodegradation and surface morphology.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="LARVAL CANNIBALISM">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larval cannibalism may influence biodegradation processes in certain insect species.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="WORMS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Worms play a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, breaking it down into less harmful substances.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MEALWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm frass contains residual polymers that are analyzed to study the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass, a byproduct of mealworm digestion, can be used as a biofertilizer, contributing to soil health and plant growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="PLASTIC-DEGRADING AROMATIC POLYESTERASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The plastic-degrading aromatic polyesterase is engineered to enhance the biodegradation of aromatic polyesters, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation and microbial degradation are interconnected processes where microorganisms break down environmental pollutants like plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation is a key process in the biodegradation of plastics, involving microorganisms like Ideonella sakaiensis.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="CARBONYL GROUP FORMATION">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The formation of carbonyl groups in frass is evidence of hydrocarbon chain oxidation during the biodegradation of microplastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The formation of carbonyl groups is a chemical change observed during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating oxidative degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Changes in molecular weight distribution in frass indicate the extent of microplastic biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution changes during biodegradation, with LDPE showing broad depolymerization and LLDPE/HDPE showing limited depolymerization</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="CROSS-LINKING REACTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cross-linking reactions during biodegradation lead to changes in the molecular weight distribution of microplastics in mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="GUT MICROBIOTA COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The composition of gut microbiota in mealworms influences their ability to metabolize different types of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="INTESTINAL MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The intestinal microbiota of mealworms is essential for the biodegradation of EPS, with changes in microbial populations observed based on diet.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="EXTRACELLULAR OXIDATIVE ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Extracellular oxidative enzymes released by gut microorganisms assist in the degradation of EPS by breaking down its chemical structure.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="EPS (EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">EPS is biodegraded by mealworms, with their gut microbiota playing a key role in the process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="METAGENOMIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Metagenomic analysis is used to study the gut microbiota of mealworms, providing insights into the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="SYMBIOTIC MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Symbiotic microorganisms in mealworms play a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE &amp; TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmental Science &amp; Technology publishes research on the biodegradation of plastics by organisms like mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental Science &amp; Technology publishes studies on the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms and their gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="PLASTIC WASTE POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation of plastics, such as polystyrene, by organisms like mealworms offers a potential solution to plastic waste pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MICROBIAL CULTURES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microbial cultures are used in research to study the biodegradation of plastics, including polystyrene, by various microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="ACTIVATED SLUDGE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Activated sludge has been studied for its potential to biodegrade plastics, including polystyrene, in wastewater treatment systems.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="SOIL MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Soil microbes have been investigated for their ability to degrade plastics, including polystyrene, in natural environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil microbes, including bacteria and fungi, play a significant role in the biodegradation of polystyrene foam and other plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MANURE MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Manure microbes have been studied for their potential role in the biodegradation of plastics, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="PHOTODEGRADED POLYSTYRENE-5% VINYL KETONE COPOLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Photodegraded polystyrene-5% vinyl ketone copolymers have been studied for their biodegradation potential after UV pretreatment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="EXTRACELLULAR ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Extracellular enzymes may play a role in the depolymerization of plastic polymers, such as polystyrene, and are a focus of future research.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="PLASTIC-DEPOLYMERIZING ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic-depolymerizing enzymes could be developed for large-scale use in breaking down plastic polymers, such as polystyrene, as part of future biotechnological solutions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYSTYRENE FOAM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene foam is a target for biodegradation research due to its persistence in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="ENVIRONMENTALLY DEGRADABLE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmentally degradable polymers are designed to undergo biodegradation, reducing their persistence as environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="SOIL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Soil harbors microbial communities that attempt to biodegrade plastics like PE and PS, though the process is slow and limited.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="SEAWATER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Seawater contains microbial communities that attempt to degrade plastics, contributing to biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="SEDIMENTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sediments are environmental sources that contribute to the biodegradation of plastics through their microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="SLUDGE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sludge is a microbial-rich source used in studies to assess the biodegradation of plastics like PE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="COMPOST">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Compost is an environmental source that contributes to the biodegradation of plastics through its microbial communities.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MIXED MICROBIAL CULTURES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mixed microbial cultures are used to study the biodegradation of plastics, though their efficiency is limited.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="ISOLATED BACTERIAL STRAINS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Isolated bacterial strains are reported to have limited capabilities in degrading plastics like PE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="TGA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TGA analysis is used to confirm the biodegradation and oxidation of EPS and LDPE in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="GUT MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">108.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microbes are essential for the biodegradation of plastics like polyethylene and polystyrene by organisms such as mealworms and waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, particularly in depolymerization and mineralization.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, particularly in depolymerization and mineralization.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae play a significant role in the biodegradation of EPS, as their suppression inhibits depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in insect larvae like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus are essential for the biodegradation of various plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in mealworms are essential for the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene and low-density polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in mealworms and superworms are essential for the biodegradation of plastics like polystyrene and polyurethane.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atrinus, play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in mealworms and superworms are essential for the biodegradation of plastics like polyethylene and polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae are essential for the biodegradation of plastics like PP, PS, and LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae play a crucial role in the biodegradation of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics by organisms like mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="INTESTINAL FLORA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Intestinal flora in Tenebrio molitor larvae shifts under different dietary conditions, influencing the biodegradation of EPS and LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="TETRAHYDROFURAN (THF)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is used in analytical methods for studying the biodegradation of plastics in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="1,2,4-TRICHLOROBENZENE (1,2,4-TCB)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (1,2,4-TCB) is used in analytical methods for studying the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="TRYPTICASE SOY AGAR (TSA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Trypticase soy agar (TSA) is used as a growth medium in studies involving microbial cultures and biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="POLYURETHANE (PUR)">
      <data key="d3">20.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyurethane (PUR) can be biodegraded by microbial cultures, as reported in landfill studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) can be biodegraded by microbial cultures, with changes in molecular weight observed during the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a petro-based polymer that may be studied for its biodegradation and oxidative stress effects on invertebrates.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="LIMITED EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Limited extent depolymerization is a specific process observed during the biodegradation of plastics like EPS and LDPE, where Mw increases and Mn decreases.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="BROAD DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Broad depolymerization is a process where both Mw and Mn decrease, observed during the biodegradation of plastics like LDPE and PS.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="PHOTODEGRADED POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Photodegraded polymers are more susceptible to biodegradation due to their altered chemical structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodegraded polymers are more susceptible to biodegradation, as light exposure breaks them into smaller, more degradable fragments.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodegraded polymers are studied for their biodegradability, particularly in relation to synthetic plastics like polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="PHOTO DEGRADED POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Photo-degraded polymers are studied for their biodegradability, offering insights into the breakdown of synthetic materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="AGRICULTURAL PLASTIC FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Agricultural plastic films are a significant environmental pollutant, and their biodegradation is a focus of research.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="DYELLA GINSENGISOLI LA-4">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dyella ginsengisoli LA-4 is capable of degrading biphenyl, demonstrating its role in addressing persistent organic pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="TENEBRIO MONITOR">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio monitor larvae can biodegrade cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene waste, altering their gut microbiome and enzymatic profile.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">81.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae like Zophobas atratus leads to the formation of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation of plastics by organisms like Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor leads to the formation of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation processes, such as those carried out by superworms and mealworms, can help reduce the accumulation of microplastics in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a target for biodegradation strategies to reduce environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a target for biodegradation studies, aiming to reduce their environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation processes can reduce the accumulation of microplastics in the environment by breaking down larger plastic items.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation processes can reduce the accumulation of microplastics in the environment by breaking down larger plastic items.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics can be broken down through biodegradation processes, such as those facilitated by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a result of plastic degradation, and their biodegradation is a key focus of environmental research.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </edge>
