    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="LLDPE">
      <data key="d3">34.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LLDPE has slower degradation rates compared to LDPE, influenced by its physical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;LLDPE is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, with specific consumption rates and physiological responses.&lt;SEP&gt;LLDPE undergoes limited extent depolymerization during biodegradation by mealworms, with less significant molecular weight reduction&lt;SEP&gt;LLDPE, due to its linear structure, is more resistant to biodegradation compared to LDPE, but can still be degraded under specific conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) is studied for its biodegradability by insect larvae, with its unique properties affecting degradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </edge>
