    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="ENVIRONMENTAL IMPLICATIONS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">This research study highlights the environmental implications of polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms, focusing on the role of gut microbiome and polymer MW.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="ULTRAHIGH-MW POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ultrahigh-MW polystyrene polymers pose challenges for biodegradation by mealworms, causing metabolic dysbiosis and oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="LOW-MW POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Low-MW polystyrene polymers are more efficiently biodegraded by mealworms compared to ultrahigh-MW polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION" target="CORE GUT MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The core gut microbiome of organisms like mealworms plays a key role in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
