    <edge source="FRASS" target="MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">135.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Frass from mealworms can be used as plant growth media, indicating the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Frass is the excreted waste material used to measure the effectiveness of mealworm digestion and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms excreted frass after consuming plastics, indicating partial degradation of the plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms produce frass, which can be used as a natural fertilizer, contributing to a zero-waste circular economy.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass is the metabolic waste produced by mealworms after consuming and metabolizing microplastics, showing changes in crystalline structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass, the excrement of mealworms, is analyzed to study the biodegradation of materials like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestion of EPS by mealworms results in the production of frass, which is analyzed for potential future applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass is analyzed to study the degradation of plastics, such as PE and PS, by mealworms&lt;SEP&gt;Frass from mealworms contains degraded plastic particles, providing evidence of the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;The frass of mealworms shows chemical changes, indicating the biodegradation of plastics in their gut&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms produce frass during polystyrene biodegradation, which contains nitrogen and cations, indicating changes in metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass from mealworms contains partially oxidized products from the biodegradation of polystyrene&lt;SEP&gt;Frass is the egested residue from mealworms after consuming polystyrene, showing evidence of PS degradation&lt;SEP&gt;Frass is produced by mealworms after the biodegradation of ingested plastics, containing residual plastic particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass produced by mealworms contains residual and modified PS polymers, reflecting the extent of PS MP digestion and degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass is the excreted waste material used to measure the effectiveness of mealworm digestion and biodegradation.</data>
