      <data key="d3">159.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Frass contains degraded polystyrene and other organic intermediates, indicating the biodegradation of PS by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass contains metabolites and bioactive compounds resulting from the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by mealworms, providing insights into the metabolic pathways involved.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass contains residual polystyrene and byproducts of its degradation, providing evidence of the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass is analyzed to determine the residual content of polystyrene, providing insights into the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass samples from larvae fed polystyrene (PS) were analyzed to study the degradation of the plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) is consumed by larvae, and its degradation is analyzed through the study of frass, which contains residual PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass from mealworms fed polystyrene shows evidence of biodegradation, including changes in molecular structure and functional groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass from Tenebrio molitor larvae contains residual polymers from the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass from mealworms fed with polystyrene was analyzed to study the degradation process of this plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass samples from larvae fed polystyrene (PS) were analyzed to study the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass from mealworms fed PS contains degraded plastic particles, indicating the biodegradation of PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass contains residual polystyrene after degradation by mealworms, analyzed to determine the extent of depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass contains residual polystyrene, which is analyzed to determine the extent of biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass contains undigested polystyrene particles and residues, indicating incomplete biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass from mealworms contains depolymerized metabolites of polystyrene, indicating the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene residues in frass exhibit changes in molecular weight and chemical structure, indicating degradation by mealworms&lt;SEP&gt;Frass contains residual polystyrene (PS) and byproducts of its degradation, providing evidence of PS biodegradation by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass from PS-fed Tenebrio larvae contains degraded polystyrene, providing evidence of the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass contains residual PS and hydrophilic soluble substances, indicating the digestion and degradation of PS by mealworms.</data>
