    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYSTYRENE (PS)">
      <data key="d3">479.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene (PS) and Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) are the focus of significant scientific research due to the mealworms' unique ability to biodegrade polystyrene, a common and environmentally persistent plastic. Mealworms, specifically Tenebrio molitor larvae, have demonstrated the capacity to consume and degrade various forms of polystyrene, including raw, processed, expanded polystyrene (EPS), and microplastics. This biodegradation process is facilitated by the mealworms' gut microbiome, which plays a crucial role in breaking down the plastic polymer through mechanisms such as oxidation, depolymerization, and mineralization. As the larvae ingest polystyrene, their digestive processes, supported by gut microbiota, modify and break down the plastic, resulting in the formation of smaller monomers, oxygen-containing compounds, and other specific biodegradation products. A significant portion of the ingested polystyrene, nearly half, is converted into carbon dioxide (CO2), while a smaller fraction is assimilated into the mealworms' biomass.

The efficiency of polystyrene biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor may vary depending on factors such as the region of origin of the mealworms, the molecular weight of the polymer, and the activity of gut microbes. The process leaves behind residual polymers and may produce some toxicity, which requires further investigation. While polystyrene alone cannot meet the nutritional needs of the larvae, studies have shown that mealworms fed polystyrene had the highest protein content but also the highest mortality, indicating a trade-off between nutrition and survival. This suggests that while Tenebrio molitor larvae can use polystyrene as a food source, it is not sufficient for their overall health and survival.

The biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor is slower compared to polylactic acid (PLA) but faster than polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Overall, Tenebrio molitor larvae demonstrate a promising ability to biodegrade polystyrene, offering a potential biological solution for reducing plastic waste in the environment. Their capacity to break down this non-hydrolysable plastic polymer highlights the importance of their gut microbiome in the biodegradation process and underscores their potential role in addressing plastic pollution. The specific metabolic pathways, gut microbiota, and enzymes involved in this process are key factors contributing to the degradation of polystyrene, enabling the larvae to metabolize the plastic and produce energy for growth.

In addition to their direct role in biodegradation, Tenebrio molitor larvae are also used to isolate PS-degrading microbes and study the effects of polystyrene on their survival, growth, and reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYETHYLENE (PE)">
      <data key="d3">251.0</data>
      <data key="d4">**Polyethylene (PE)** and **Tenebrio molitor** (commonly known as mealworms) share a unique relationship in the context of plastic biodegradation. Research has demonstrated that Tenebrio molitor larvae possess the ability to biodegrade polyethylene (PE), a non-hydrolysable plastic polymer, with high efficiency. This process involves the conversion of plastic waste into valuable biomass, offering a sustainable solution for managing plastic pollution.

The biodegradation of polyethylene by Tenebrio molitor larvae is facilitated through their digestive processes, with gut bacteria playing an essential role in the depolymerization of PE. However, some studies suggest that the process may be less dependent on gut microbes compared to the degradation of other plastics. Notably, Tenebrio molitor larvae can still degrade polyethylene even when gut bacteria are suppressed by antibiotics like gentamicin, indicating a complex and multifaceted biodegradation mechanism. The larvae preferentially depolymerize lower molecular weight PE, leading to oxidation and breakdown of the plastic. This degradation is evidenced by the production of residual plastics and, in some cases, toxicity during the process.

The efficiency of polyethylene degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae can be influenced by factors such as the larvae's origin and the addition of natural feed, like wheat bran. Tenebrio molitor larvae have demonstrated the highest percentage of polyethylene utilization among various plastics tested, highlighting their effectiveness in breaking down this persistent material. They are capable of biodegrading various forms of polyethylene, including high-density polyethylene (HDPE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), depending on their molecular weight and crystallinity.

Additionally, Tenebrio molitor larvae have been used to isolate and enrich PE-degrading bacterial strains, further emphasizing their role in biodegradation research. Their ability extends to polyethylene microplastics, with specific consumption rates and physiological responses observed during exposure. The larvae biodegrade polyethylene microplastics, leading to the formation of smaller monomers and oxygen-containing compounds, and reducing the molecular weight of the plastic.

In summary, Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of degrading polyethylene through both gut-microbe-dependent and independent processes, contributing to the breakdown of petroleum-based plastics. Their ability to biodegrade PE, even under challenging conditions, underscores their potential as a natural solution for addressing plastic waste and advancing sustainable waste management practices. Their rapid biodegradation of polyethylene addresses its environmental persistence, making them a promising tool in the fight against plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
