    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYURETHANE (PU)">
      <data key="d3">72.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) can consume and degrade polyurethane (PU), aiding in its environmental breakdown.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume and degrade polyurethane (PU), with varying efficiency depending on the chemical composition and mechanical properties of the PU.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can digest and depolymerize polyurethane (PU), contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can survive on PU foam and contribute to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are used to study the biodegradation of polyurethane foam, demonstrating their ability to break down synthetic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume and degrade polyurethane, facilitated by their gut microbiome and extracellular enzymes.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor (yellow mealworms) ingest and biodegrade polyurethane (PU) foam through oxidation and depolymerization processes in their guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polyurethane (PU), demonstrating their potential in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can ingest and degrade polyurethane (PU) foam plastics, but with lower survival rates compared to control groups.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYPROPYLENE (PP)">
      <data key="d3">171.0</data>
      <data key="d4">**Polypropylene (PP)** and **Tenebrio molitor** (commonly known as mealworms) are the focus of research exploring the biodegradation of plastic waste. Tenebrio molitor larvae have demonstrated the ability to consume, ingest, and degrade polypropylene (PP), a non-hydrolysable plastic polymer, through their digestive processes. This biodegradation is facilitated by the synergistic action of their gut microbial communities, which play a crucial role in breaking down the plastic. While the extent of depolymerization varies depending on the properties of the polypropylene, such as its molecular weight, the larvae can utilize PP as a carbon source, contributing to its degradation. 

Studies indicate that Tenebrio molitor larvae can actively chew and ingest polypropylene foam, leading to its biodegradation. However, their survival rates may be reduced when consuming PP compared to control groups. The process of biodegradation is less dependent on gut microbes for PP compared to other plastics like polystyrene (PS) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC), but the gut microbiome remains a key factor in the degradation efficiency. 

Overall, Tenebrio molitor larvae show significant potential for biodegrading polypropylene, offering a promising biological solution for reducing plastic waste and contributing to environmental remediation efforts. Polypropylene is also used experimentally to house mealworms during studies, further highlighting its role in this research.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade polypropylene (PP) plastics, although degradation efficiency depends on the polymer's properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polypropylene, expanding their role in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)">
      <data key="d3">246.0</data>
      <data key="d4">**Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC)** and **Tenebrio molitor** (commonly known as mealworms) are the focus of research exploring the potential of mealworms to biodegrade PVC, a widely used and non-hydrolysable plastic polymer. Studies indicate that Tenebrio molitor larvae possess the ability to biodegrade PVC through their digestive processes, which are facilitated by gut microbes. This biodegradation process contributes to plastic waste reduction and offers a sustainable solution for PVC waste management, potentially aiding in the reduction of environmental pollution. 

However, the biodegradation of PVC by Tenebrio molitor larvae is not without limitations. Compared to other microplastics, PVC exhibits higher toxicity and induces stronger physiological stress responses in the larvae. Additionally, the degradation process is only partial, resulting in limited mineralization to chloride and the production of residual plastics and toxicity. Despite these challenges, Tenebrio molitor larvae have demonstrated significant potential in degrading PVC, similar to their ability to degrade other plastics like polystyrene and low-density polyethylene. 

In summary, Tenebrio molitor larvae show promise in biodegrading PVC, contributing to waste management and environmental pollution reduction. However, the process is not fully efficient, and further research is needed to address the challenges associated with PVC's toxicity and the limited extent of its degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading polyvinyl chloride (PVC), as demonstrated in environmental studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), contributing to sustainable waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), demonstrating their potential in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), contributing to plastic waste management&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), making them a potential solution for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polyvinyl chloride, further demonstrating their plastic-degrading capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade PVC, contributing to resource recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can ingest and degrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC) within a short retention time, demonstrating their plastic-degrading capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are capable of degrading polyvinyl chloride, although it is a more resistant plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
