    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="MICROPLASTICS (MNPS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae generate microplastics (MNPs) during the biofragmentation of ingested plastics, which may accumulate in their excrement.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OXIDATIVE STRESS">
      <data key="d3">66.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ingestion of microplastics by mealworm larvae induces oxidative stress, despite their ability to biodegrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic degradation intermediates may induce oxidative stress in mealworms, requiring detoxification mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene consumption does not negatively affect the oxidative stress parameters in the gut cells of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae experience oxidative stress during the biodegradation of plastics, affecting their lipid homeostasis and metabolic pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidative stress parameters are measured to assess the impact of plastic consumption on Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae experience oxidative stress during the biodegradation of micro(nano)plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae experience oxidative stress during the biodegradation of microplastics, which can be alleviated by supplementing their diet with bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidative stress may occur in Tenebrio molitor larvae during the biodegradation of different types of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PLASTIC BIOFRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae fragment ingested plastics into smaller particles through biofragmentation, contributing to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="RESEARCH STUDY">
      <data key="d3">538.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The research study extensively investigates the biodegradation capabilities of **Tenebrio molitor**, commonly known as mealworms or yellow mealworms, with a particular focus on their ability to break down various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyurethane (PU), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polypropylene (PP). The study highlights the remarkable potential of **Tenebrio molitor** larvae in environmental remediation, particularly in addressing plastic waste. A key aspect of this biodegradation process is the role of the gut microbiome of **Tenebrio molitor**, which collaborates with the larvae to degrade plastics into simpler compounds. The research evaluates the physiological responses of mealworm larvae after ingesting microplastics, as well as the mechanisms by which they break down these materials. It also explores whether mealworms can complete their life cycle while consuming plastics, further emphasizing their potential as a sustainable solution for environmental remediation.

The study by Yang et al. (2020) specifically focused on assessing the microbiome diversity in **Tenebrio molitor**, highlighting its role in polystyrene biodegradation. Beyond plastic degradation, the research examines the use of **Tenebrio molitor** larvae in cleaning and biodegrading expanded polystyrene (EPS) from cement mortar and brickwork debris, analyzing the resulting frass. The study also investigates the growth and development of **Tenebrio molitor** larvae under different feed types, including plastic-based diets, and identifies the microorganisms and enzymatic activities involved in the degradation process. Additionally, it examines the effects of different substrates and beddings on the biodegradation of EPS by **Tenebrio molitor** larvae and adults.

The research further investigates the effects of Styrofoam feeds on mealworms, aiming to evaluate their potential for both waste management and economical feed. It also explores the role of mealworms in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene, underscoring their utility in addressing plastic waste. Specific attention is given to the ability of **Tenebrio molitor** to depolymerize and biodegrade polypropylene (PP), with studies focusing on the larvae's capacity to break down this type of plastic.

In addition to these findings, the research study also focuses on isolating and identifying polystyrene-degrading bacteria from mealworms to better understand the biodegradation mechanism. This aspect of the study provides further insight into the microbial processes that enable **Tenebrio molitor** to degrade plastics effectively. Overall, the</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
