93be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="DARKLING BEETLE">
      <data key="d3">26.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor is a species of darkling beetle known for its ability to biodegrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor is a species of darkling beetle, known for its ability to biodegrade EPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae develop into darkling beetles, completing their life cycle.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="LDPE FOAM">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae can efficiently digest and biodegrade LDPE foam, converting it into biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are used to study the biodegradation of LDPE foam, showing less dependency on gut microbes compared to EPS.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES (ROS)">
      <data key="d3">32.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ingestion of microplastics by mealworm larvae leads to the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS), causing oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are measured to assess oxidative stress in Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae exhibit increased reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels during the biodegradation of microplastics, indicating oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae experience increased levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS) during the biodegradation of microplastics, indicating oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="ANTIOXIDANT ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae produce antioxidant enzymes to counteract oxidative stress induced by microplastic ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae exhibit increased antioxidant enzyme activities in response to oxidative stress induced by microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="LIPID PEROXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipid peroxidation is a biochemical indicator used to assess oxidative stress in Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipid peroxidation occurs in mealworm larvae as a result of oxidative stress from microplastic ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae experience increased lipid peroxidation as a result of oxidative stress caused by microplastic exposure.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="INSECT BIOMASS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae convert plastic waste into insect biomass, which can be used as a sustainable protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="HEXABROMOCYCLODODECANE (HBCD)">
      <data key="d3">49.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are studied to understand the fate of HBCD, a flame retardant, during the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can egest hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD) from polystyrene without bioaccumulation, indicating their potential role in mitigating chemical pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can ingest and egest hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD) without bioaccumulation, indicating their role in the biodegradation of flame-retardant plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can process polystyrene containing HBCD, with no bioaccumulation observed.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polystyrene containing HBCD without accumulating the chemical in their biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can egest HBCD from polystyrene without bioaccumulation, reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can ingest polystyrene containing HBCD, but studies show no bioaccumulation of HBCD in the worms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE">
      <data key="d3">222.0</data>
      <data key="d4">**Tenebrio molitor** (commonly known as mealworms) is widely recognized as a **sustainable and eco-friendly alternative protein source** for both **human consumption** and **animal feed**. The larvae of Tenebrio molitor are particularly valued for their **high protein content**, making them a **nutritionally rich** option for food and feed production. Additionally, Tenebrio molitor larvae have garnered attention for their unique ability to **biodegrade plastics**, including **polystyrene** and **Styrofoam**, as well as other pollutants. This dual capability—providing **nutritional value** and contributing to **waste management**—positions Tenebrio molitor as a **promising solution** for **sustainable food production** and **environmental sustainability**. Research and exploration into Tenebrio molitor as an alternative protein source highlight its potential to reduce the environmental impact of traditional protein sources while addressing global challenges related to food security and plastic waste. Overall, Tenebrio molitor is considered a **viable and innovative alternative protein source** with applications in **livestock feed**, **human food**, and **waste degradation**.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are being explored as an alternative protein source due to their ability to degrade polystyrene and their high protein content.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor is recognized as a viable alternative protein source due to its high protein content and nutritional benefits.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are a sustainable alternative protein source due to their high nutritional value and environmental benefits.</data>
      <data key="d5">10&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="SOYBEAN MEAL">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Soybean meal is being compared to Tenebrio molitor larvae as an alternative protein source in broiler diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae have comparable protein quality to soybean meal, making them a viable alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="BROILER DIETS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae are being explored as an alternative protein source in broiler diets to replace traditional ingredients like soybean meal.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYPROPYLENE">
      <data key="d3">151.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can degrade polypropylene, another synthetic vinyl plastic, helping to address plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms cannot degrade polypropylene (PP), despite their ability to degrade other types of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms can biodegrade polypropylene, with their gut microbiome playing a key role.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene, showcasing their ability to degrade various plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polypropylene, demonstrating their potential in plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade polypropylene, with the degradation efficiency influenced by the molecular weight of the PP&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polypropylene, contributing to sustainable waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor has been studied for its ability to biodegrade polypropylene, although Zophobas atratus is more effective in this process.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) can biodegrade polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polypropylene, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polypropylene, reducing its hydrophobicity and modifying its surface properties&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polypropylene, with their gut microbiota playing a significant role in the depolymerization process.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume and biodegrade polypropylene foam through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume and biodegrade polypropylene, though their survival rates are lower compared to when fed with wheat bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade polypropylene (PP) with the help of their gut microbiota, but this ability is lost when gut microbes are suppressed.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization, aiding in plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading polypropylene, further demonstrating their role in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">5&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE">
      <data key="d3">76.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacterial consortia from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade polyvinyl chloride, demonstrating their role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can degrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), aiding in the reduction of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms have been reported to degrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), though the mechanism is not fully understood.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms can biodegrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), contributing to waste management efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polyvinyl chloride, highlighting their role in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor has been studied for its potential to biodegrade polyvinyl chloride, a resistant plastic polymer&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms can degrade polyvinyl chloride, though the conversion rate is lower compared to other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading polyvinyl chloride (PVC), offering a potential solution for plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading polyvinyl chloride (PVC), offering a potential solution for plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYLACTIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">47.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can biodegrade polylactic acid, offering a sustainable approach to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade polylactic acid, offering a sustainable approach to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading polylactic acid, contributing to sustainable waste management practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms are capable of biodegrading polylactic acid (PLA), offering a sustainable approach to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading polylactic acid, contributing to sustainable waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polylactic acid (PLA), demonstrating their potential in sustainable waste management</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="GUT MICROBE-INDEPENDENT DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">23.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) can degrade plastics through gut microbe-independent depolymerization, demonstrating their versatility in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade low-density polyethylene without relying on gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms can biodegrade low-density polyethylene through gut microbe-independent depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="RESOURCE RECOVERY">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor contributes to resource recovery by biodegrading plastics into useful resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms contribute to resource recovery by biodegrading plastics like polylactic acid.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="CELLULOSE">
      <data key="d3">80.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can digest cellulose-based waste, aiding in the management of plant-based waste materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can digest cellulose-based waste, aiding in the management of plant-based waste materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae metabolize cellulose, contributing to the study of their digestive tract microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade cellulose, which may contribute to their ability to degrade plastics such as polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade cellulose, contributing to the breakdown of plant-based waste materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade cellulose, demonstrating their ability to process plant-based waste materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade cellulose, a natural polymer, in addition to synthetic polymers like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade cellulose, contributing to waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade cellulose, demonstrating their ability to degrade natural polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae metabolize cellulose, contributing to the study of waste degradation in their digestive tract.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="WASTE MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are being studied as a potential solution for waste management due to their ability to degrade various types of waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OAT">
      <data key="d3">21.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oat is used as a control diet to study the gut microbiome and enzymatic activity of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Oat is used as a supplementary food source for mealworms in experiments to study their growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PE-OXODEGRADABLE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms fed PE-oxodegradable showed changes in their gut microbiome, with increased bacterial abundance.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OATMEAL WASTE">
      <data key="d3">20.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are fed oatmeal waste to compare its effects on their gut microbiome with other substrates&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are fed oatmeal waste to compare its effects on their gut microbiome with other substrates&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal waste is used as a dietary variant to study the enzymatic activity in the digestive tracts of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="CARDBOARD">
      <data key="d3">33.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cardboard diet negatively impacts the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae, causing starvation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cardboard is used as a dietary variant to study the enzymatic activity in the digestive tracts of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are fed cardboard to assess its influence on their gut microbiome&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae were fed cardboard to assess their growth and development, showing lower larval mass and metamorphosis rates compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are fed cardboard to assess its influence on their gut microbiome</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OXO-DEGRADABLE POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">41.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are fed oxo-degradable polyethylene to evaluate its effects on their gut microbiome&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are fed oxo-degradable polyethylene to evaluate its effects on their gut microbiome&lt;SEP&gt;Oxo-degradable polyethylene is used as a dietary variant to study the enzymatic activity in the digestive tracts of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxo-degradable polyethylene may form toxic byproducts when degraded by mealworms, affecting their physiology.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae were fed oxo-degradable polyethylene to assess their growth and development, showing lower larval mass and metamorphosis rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms interact with oxo-degradable polyethylene, with their digestive enzymes potentially contributing to its breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYETHYLENE REGRANULATE">
      <data key="d3">35.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are fed polyethylene regranulate to study its impact on their gut microbiome&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are fed polyethylene regranulate to study its impact on their gut microbiome&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene regranulate is used as a dietary variant to study the enzymatic activity in the digestive tracts of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae were fed polyethylene regranulate to assess their growth and development, showing higher larval mass and metamorphosis rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are studied for their ability to interact with polyethylene regranulate, with their enzymes potentially aiding in its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a</data>
    </edge>
