    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="XENOBIOTICS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms further decompose plastic degradation intermediates as xenobiotics during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic degradation intermediates are treated as xenobiotics by mealworms, requiring detoxification.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)">
      <data key="d3">190.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) are capable of biodegrading expanded polystyrene (EPS), a common environmental pollutant and insulation material, through their digestive processes and the presence of specific gut bacteria. These larvae can consume and degrade EPS, breaking it down into smaller polymers, carbon dioxide, and frass, which contributes to the reduction of plastic waste and offers a potential biological solution to EPS pollution. Studies have shown that mealworms exhibit a broad depolymerization pattern when degrading EPS, making them effective in transforming this material into less harmful byproducts. 

Mealworms have been tested and fed EPS in various experiments to assess their ability to biodegrade this plastic, with results indicating that they can sustain their life on EPS, though some negative impacts on their growth and development have been observed. Additionally, mealworms have been used to remove EPS from brickwork surfaces, further demonstrating their potential in waste management and recycling efforts. While their ability to degrade EPS is promising, it is important to note that this process can also lead to the production of micro and nanoplastics, which may have environmental implications. Overall, *Tenebrio molitor* larvae represent a significant biological tool in addressing EPS pollution and reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are capable of consuming and biodegrading expanded polystyrene (EPS) foam, converting a significant portion of ingested EPS into CO2.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d3">104.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of chewing and degrading LDPE, aiding in plastic biodegradation&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), with varying depolymerization patterns depending on the plastic's properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of degrading low-density polyethylene, similar to their ability to degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms from Tai'an and Shenzhen can effectively degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are also capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene, as shown by the analysis of their frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can also biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), further aiding in plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can depolymerize and biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), with some depolymerization occurring independently of gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade LDPE, with gut microbes playing a role in enhancing the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can digest and biodegrade LDPE, converting it into CO2 and biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae have been observed to biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) through depolymerization and mineralization processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade and mineralize low-density polyethylene (LDPE), with a significant portion being converted into CO2.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">96.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae are essential for the biodegradation of LDPE and HDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in mealworms are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene and detoxification of styrene foam, influencing their survival and performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in mealworms play an essential role in the degradation of plastics, particularly polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacteria of mealworms are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene, as they break down the plastic into smaller components.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacteria of Tenebrio molitor are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene, as they produce enzymes that break down the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacteria of Tenebrio molitor are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene, as they form biofilms and physically alter the material.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacteria of Tenebrio molitor larvae play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacteria of Tenebrio molitor larvae play a crucial role in the degradation of polystyrene, aiding the larvae in utilizing PS as a carbon source.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacteria of Tenebrio molitor contribute to the digestion and detoxification of certain materials, including plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor are crucial for the biodegradation of EPS, enabling the breakdown of polystyrene into smaller polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut bacteria of Tenebrio molitor larvae play a significant role in the biodegradation of plastic polymers, with shifts in bacterial communities observed based on the type of plastic ingested.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="RNA SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">RNA sequencing was used to study the mechanism of plastic degradation by mealworms, identifying key genes and pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">132.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Incomplete biodegradation of plastics by mealworms can lead to the formation of microplastics, which may spread into the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can degrade microplastics such as PS, PVC, and LDPE, though the degradation patterns vary based on particle size and form.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are ingested and biodegraded by mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), with the efficiency influenced by factors such as particle size and larval age.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae can fragment ingested plastics into microplastics, which are then further degraded within their intestinal environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade microplastics, with the process influenced by the physical-chemical properties of the plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor is capable of biodegrading microplastics, contributing to the reduction of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;The biodegradation process involving Tenebrio molitor may result in the spread of microplastics through insect feces, posing environmental risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are used to study the effects of microplastics on survival, weight, and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor is used to study the feeding and metabolism effects of microplastics, highlighting their interaction with environmental pollutants.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor is studied for its ability to metabolize microplastics, which affects its feeding and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can reduce microplastic pollution by biodegrading larger plastic items&lt;SEP&gt;Ingestion of microplastics by yellow mealworms can cause intestinal damage, affecting their nutrient absorption and growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae ingest and degrade microplastics, with their physiological responses indicating the impact of different plastic types&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can contribute to the reduction of microplastics through biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="HIGH-IMPACT POLYSTYRENE (HIPS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can degrade high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), with rubber as an additive influencing the degradation process.</data>
