    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="LINEAR LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LLDPE)">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can degrade linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), with its properties affecting the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae cannot degrade linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), unlike other forms of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="WHEAT BRAN">
      <data key="d3">246.0</data>
      <data key="d4">**Tenebrio molitor** (commonly known as mealworms) and **wheat bran** are closely associated in both commercial rearing and scientific research, particularly in studies focused on plastic biodegradation and the effects of microplastics on insect larvae. Wheat bran is widely recognized as the standard and primary feed for Tenebrio molitor larvae, providing essential nutrients that support their growth, survival, and development. It is a preferred and conventional diet that promotes optimal growth, low mortality, and overall well-being of the larvae.

In scientific experiments, wheat bran serves multiple critical roles. It is commonly used as a control or baseline diet to compare the effects of alternative diets, such as those containing polystyrene, polypropylene foam, or other microplastics. Additionally, wheat bran is employed as a co-diet alongside plastics to enhance the larvae's ability to degrade these materials. Studies have shown that wheat bran supplementation increases the consumption rate of plastics like polystyrene and polypropylene foam by Tenebrio molitor larvae, while also improving their biodegradation efficiency. Furthermore, wheat bran helps mitigate the toxic effects of micro(nano)plastics, making it a vital component in research on plastic biodegradation and the ecological impact of microplastics.

Beyond its role in plastic degradation studies, wheat bran is also used in feeding experiments to investigate its effects on the gut microbiota and biodegradation capacity of Tenebrio molitor larvae. Its nutritional properties not only support the larvae's physiological development but also enhance their ability to consume and biodegrade various types of plastics.

In summary, wheat bran is a fundamental and versatile component in the rearing and experimental study of Tenebrio molitor larvae. It serves as a nutritional supplement, a control diet, and a co-diet that enhances the larvae's growth, survival, and plastic degradation capabilities, making it indispensable in both commercial and scientific contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is used as a feedstock for mealworm larvae before conducting biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="ANTIBIOTICS">
      <data key="d3">37.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antibiotics can alter the microbiome of mealworms, potentially impacting their LDPE degradation capabilities&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics suppress the gut microbes of mealworms, severely inhibiting their ability to degrade polystyrene, indicating the importance of gut bacteria in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics were used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, demonstrating the essential role of gut bacteria in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics were used to suppress gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing that PET depolymerization continued without gut microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics were used to suppress gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae, showing that PET depolymerization continued without gut microbial activity.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </edge>
