      <data key="d3">62.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, as evidenced by the analysis of residual polymers in their frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) can biodegrade expanded polystyrene (EPS), contributing to environmental solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can ingest and degrade expanded polystyrene (EPS), though it contributes little to their growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae consume and biodegrade EPS, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae consume and depolymerize EPS, contributing to its biodegradation&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae consume and depolymerize expanded polystyrene (EPS), contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with expanded polystyrene (EPS) showed mass loss, indicating that EPS does not support their development.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
