      <data key="d3">102.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can biodegrade linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), affecting their digestive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms cannot effectively degrade linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE).&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor consumes LLDPE, influencing its gut microbial community and contributing to the biodegradation of the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are used to biodegrade linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) microplastics, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae biodegrade LLDPE, causing oxidative stress and ecotoxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can also biodegrade LLDPE, with specific consumption rates and residual PE percentages measured.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade LLDPE, though at a slower rate compared to LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade LLDPE, with the gut microbiome coordinating carbon and nitrogen metabolism during the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume LLDPE, which affects their gut microbiome diversity and structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade LLDPE, contributing to the reduction of environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae biodegrade LLDPE, but with limited extent depolymerization, resulting in less significant molecular weight reduction&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can depolymerize and biodegrade LLDPE, leading to changes in molecular weight and accumulation of longer chain polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can ingest and biodegrade LLDPE, though the extent of depolymerization is less than that of LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae were examined for their ability to degrade linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE)</data>
