    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PS">
      <data key="d3">66.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can degrade polystyrene (PS), a plastic generally considered non-biodegradable.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade PS, though with lower efficiency compared to PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume and biodegrade PS, although it does not provide adequate nutrition for long-term growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade polystyrene (PS), with removal efficiencies influenced by the presence of bran in their diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae consume and degrade PS microplastics, with varying rates depending on diet conditions&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can depolymerize polystyrene (PS), but the process is severely inhibited under antibiotic suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can depolymerize polystyrene (PS), but the process is severely inhibited under antibiotic suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polystyrene (PS), making them a potential solution for plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
