    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PVC">
      <data key="d3">100.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are capable of feeding on and depolymerizing PVC, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can feed on PVC, leading to its depolymerization and biodegradation, which reduces its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms may interact with polyvinyl chloride (PVC), though its biodegradation is less studied.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, though with lower efficiency compared to PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can digest PVC, with measurable consumption rates observed in studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor can degrade PVC through the activity of its gut microbiota, contributing to the reduction of environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can chew and ingest PVC microplastics, contributing to their biodegradation&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae exhibit the ability to biodegrade PVC, though with lower efficiency compared to PLA and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade PVC, but the process is severely inhibited under antibiotic suppression of gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can depolymerize polyvinyl chloride (PVC), but the process is severely inhibited under antibiotic suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade PVC, but the process is severely inhibited under antibiotic suppression of gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can depolymerize polyvinyl chloride (PVC), but the process is severely inhibited under antibiotic suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
