    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PET-1">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PET-1 is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, leading to significant molecular weight reduction and depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 is consumed and biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to their survival and energy needs.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 microplastics are consumed and biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, demonstrating their ability to utilize PET as an energy source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PET-2">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PET-2 is also consumed and biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher consumption rates observed compared to PET-1.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with higher depolymerization extent compared to PET-1.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 microplastics are consumed at a slightly higher rate by Tenebrio molitor larvae compared to PET-1, indicating variability in biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
