    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="RESIDUAL PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The biodegradation of plastics by Tenebrio molitor results in the production of residual plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="GENTAMICIN">
      <data key="d3">254.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gentamicin, an antibiotic, has been shown to significantly impact the gut bacteria of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, commonly known as mealworms, thereby affecting their ability to degrade certain types of plastics. Specifically, Gentamicin suppresses or inhibits the gut microbes of *Tenebrio molitor*, which play a crucial role in the biodegradation of various plastics, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polypropylene (PP), and polypropylene foam. This suppression leads to a reduction in the larvae's ability to depolymerize and mineralize these plastics. 

However, the effect of Gentamicin on the degradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is less clear. Some descriptions suggest that Gentamicin negatively impacts the ability of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae to biodegrade LDPE, while others indicate that the antibiotic only partially inhibits or does not significantly affect the depolymerization of LDPE. This discrepancy suggests that the role of gut bacteria in degrading LDPE may be less critical compared to their role in degrading polystyrene and polypropylene-based materials.

Overall, the findings highlight the importance of gut bacteria in the plastic degradation process, particularly for polystyrene and polypropylene-based materials, and suggest that disrupting these microbial communities can hinder the biodegradation capabilities of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. The varying effects on different types of plastics underscore the complexity of the interactions between gut microbiota and plastic biodegradation in these larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="MICROPLASTIC (MP)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume and biodegrade microplastics, often mixed with co-diets like wheat bran.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="CO-DIET">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Co-diet treatments combine PS with wheat bran or corn flour to study the effect of additional nutrients on PS biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae&lt;SEP&gt;Co-diets, such as wheat bran, enhance the growth and plastic degradation capabilities of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="TEMPERATURE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Temperature affects the survival and plastic consumption rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae, with optimal viability between 25–28°C.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="HUMIDITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Humidity is controlled at 60–80% to ensure optimal conditions for Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic degradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="REARING DENSITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rearing density of 2–3 larvae/cm² is recommended for plastic degradation tests involving Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="SHANNON INDEX">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Shannon index is used to quantify the diversity and evenness of gut microbial communities in Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="SIMPSON INDEX">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Simpson index is used to measure the dominance of species within the gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="BRAY-CURTIS DISSIMILARITY INDEX">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Bray-Curtis dissimilarity index is used to quantify differences in gut microbial community structure in Tenebrio molitor larvae based on dietary changes.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PRINCIPAL COORDINATE ANALYSIS (PCOA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Principal Coordinate Analysis is used to analyze shifts in gut microbial community structure in Tenebrio molitor larvae during plastic biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="DNA EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DNA extraction is used to collect and analyze gut microbial samples from Tenebrio molitor larvae to study their role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="SOY PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">36.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Soy protein enhances the plastic consumption rate of Tenebrio molitor, improving their ability to degrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is used as a nutrition-rich co-diet for Tenebrio molitor larvae in plastic biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein significantly increases the rate of polystyrene degradation by mealworms, improving their survival rates and PS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein supplementation enhances the survival and PS degradation rates of mealworms, indicating the importance of nutrition in the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="CHEMOSPHERE STUDY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Chemosphere study investigates the ability of Tenebrio molitor larvae to biodegrade polystyrene and complete their life cycle.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OXIDIZED FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae contribute to the formation of oxidized functional groups in plastics like polystyrene during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="LIFE CYCLE COMPLETION">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Life cycle completion studies evaluate whether polystyrene-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae can complete their life cycle, providing insights into the sustainability of plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can complete their life cycle while feeding on polystyrene, indicating their adaptability to plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="INTERNATIONAL BIODETERIORATION AND BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">International Biodeterioration and Biodegradation published research on the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films by bacterial strains from Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="NUTRITION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The addition of nutrition enhances the survival rate and polystyrene consumption rate of mealworms, supporting their ability to degrade PS.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="GUT MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d3">79.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microorganisms in Tenebrio molitor larvae are essential for the biodegradation of microplastics (MPs) such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in Tenebrio molitor play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in mealworms are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microorganisms of Tenebrio molitor play a critical role in the biodegradation of plastics, such as polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microorganisms of mealworms play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, aiding in the breakdown of complex plastic compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microorganisms of Tenebrio molitor are essential for the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microorganisms of mealworms play an essential role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microorganisms of mealworms are essential for the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microorganisms of mealworms are essential for the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="FEEDING STRATEGIES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Feeding strategies, such as varying the ratio of bran to polystyrene, affect the gut microbiome and biodegradation efficiency of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Protein content in the biomass and frass of mealworms varies depending on the diet, influencing the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Protein is essential for the survival and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae, especially when fed with polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="NITROGEN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrogen content in the biomass and frass of mealworms varies depending on the diet, influencing the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="SPIROPLASMA SP.">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Spiroplasma sp. is found abundantly in the gut microbiome of mealworms, despite being generally considered a pathogen in insects.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PITHOMYCES CHARTARUM">
      <data key="d3">5.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pithomyces chartarum is identified in the gut of mealworms, though its role in PS biodegradation is not yet clear.</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="KLEBSIELLA">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Klebsiella is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly in the control diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella, a nitrogen-fixing bacterium in the gut of mealworms, helps mealworms overcome nitrogen limitation when feeding on plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8</data>
    </edge>
