      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Erwinia bacteria are found in the digestive tract of adult Tenebrio molitor, influencing the gut microbiota composition</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="SUSTAINABLE DEMOLITION PROCESS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The sustainable demolition process utilizes mealworms to clean brickwork debris from expanded polystyrene, making the process more environmentally friendly.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="CIVIL ENGINEERING PERSPECTIVE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The civil engineering perspective evaluates the use of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) to process expanded polystyrene (EPS) in demolition debris.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="EPS (EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and digest EPS, contributing to its biodegradation and reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae consume and biodegrade EPS, demonstrating their potential in addressing EPS pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="GRAPHITE-ADDED EPS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Graphite-added EPS is also consumed by mealworms, demonstrating their ability to digest modified plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="WHITE EPS">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae consume white EPS, but the process is slower due to its higher material density.&lt;SEP&gt;White EPS was consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, leading to the production of frass and demonstrating the larvae's ability to biodegrade EPS.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="GREY EPS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Grey EPS was also consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae, resulting in frass with distinct characteristics compared to white EPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae consume grey EPS more efficiently, as it contains graphite and elements necessary for their life processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="BRICKWORK DEBRIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae are used to clean brickwork debris contaminated with EPS, making it easier to recycle.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="ENZYMATIC PROFILE">
      <data key="d3">40.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Changes in the enzymatic profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae are observed during the biodegradation of cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae is key to their ability to biodegrade cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae changes during the biodegradation of cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene waste&lt;SEP&gt;The enzymatic profile of mealworms changes during the biodegradation of plastics, indicating the role of enzymes in the process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="HYDROCARBON-BASED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrocarbon-based plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, are biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, offering a solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are capable of biodegrading hydrocarbon-based plastics, contributing to the reduction of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are capable of biodegrading hydrocarbon-based plastics, contributing to the reduction of plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae are studied for their ability to digest and biodegrade synthetic polymers, contributing to environmental pollution mitigation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="DNA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DNA is extracted from the digestive tracts of Tenebrio molitor larvae to study microbial communities involved in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OATMEAL">
      <data key="d3">38.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oatmeal is used as a control diet to compare the effects of synthetic polymer diets on the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae were fed oatmeal to assess their growth and development, showing significant differences in larval mass and metamorphosis rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was used as a control food source to compare the effects of plastic consumption on mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal supplementation improves the survival and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with polystyrene, highlighting the importance of dietary supplements.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal supplementation improves the survival and functioning of Tenebrio molitor larvae, enhancing their ability to degrade PS.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="LIGNOCELLULOSE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae were fed lignocellulose to assess their growth and development, showing lower larval mass and metamorphosis rates.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PUPA">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pupa is a developmental stage of Tenebrio molitor larvae, with counts varying significantly based on feed type.&lt;SEP&gt;The pupal stage of mealworms is affected by feeding conditions, such as polystyrene consumption, which can delay or inhibit pupation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="BIOMASS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biomass refers to the total mass of live Tenebrio molitor larvae at the end of the experiment, showing variations based on feed type.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="METAMORPHOSIS">
      <data key="d3">23.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Metamorphosis in Tenebrio molitor larvae is disrupted by diets like CEL and PE-oxo, while the PE-reg diet supports optimal development.&lt;SEP&gt;Metamorphosis rates in Tenebrio molitor larvae vary depending on the diet, with the highest rates observed in the OAT group.&lt;SEP&gt;Metamorphosis rates of Tenebrio molitor larvae vary significantly based on feed type, with higher rates observed in some diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OATMEAL (OAT)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae fed an oatmeal diet showed the lowest mortality and highest metamorphosis rate, indicating a favorable developmental environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYETHYLENE REGRANULATE (PE-REG)">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PE-reg diet provides the most favorable development conditions for Tenebrio molitor larvae, despite high mortality rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae fed polyethylene regranulate showed a development pattern similar to oatmeal but with higher mortality, suggesting adverse effects.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="CARDBOARD (CEL)">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae fed cardboard showed moderate metamorphosis and mortality rates, suggesting a neutral impact on development.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OXO-DEGRADABLE POLYETHYLENE (PE-OXO)">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PE-oxo diet negatively impacts the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor larvae, causing starvation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae fed oxo-degradable polyethylene had low metamorphosis and moderate mortality, indicating a less favorable developmental environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="FATTY ACIDS (FA)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The fatty acid profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae varied significantly depending on the diet, with the OAT group showing distinct differences compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="LINOLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Linoleic acid content in Tenebrio molitor larvae varies depending on the diet, with higher levels in non-OAT diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Linoleic acid is a fatty acid found in the biomass of Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to their nutritional profile.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PALMITIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Palmitic acid content is higher in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed the OAT diet compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Palmitic acid is a fatty acid found in the biomass of Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to their nutritional profile.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="STEARIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Stearic acid content is higher in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed the OAT diet compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PALMITOLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Palmitoleic acid content is higher in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed the OAT diet compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="MARGARIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Margaric acid content is higher in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed the OAT diet compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="ARACHIDIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Arachidic acid content is higher in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed the OAT diet compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="MYRISTOLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Myristoleic acid content is lower in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed the OAT diet compared to other diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OLEIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oleic acid accounts for about 50% of the fatty acid content in Tenebrio molitor larvae fed PE and CEL diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Oleic acid is a fatty acid found in the biomass of Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributing to their nutritional profile.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="BREEDING GROWTH DYNAMICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Breeding growth dynamics studies the development and growth patterns of Tenebrio molitor larvae under different diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PS DIET">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PS diet is positively correlated with protein levels in Tenebrio molitor larvae and the presence of specific bacteria in their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PE-OXO DIET">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PE-oxo diet influences the abundance of bacterial orders such as Lactobacillales and Enterobacteriales in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PE-REG DIET">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PE-reg diet affects the abundance of bacterial orders like Rhizobiales and Pseudomonadales in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="CEL DIET">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The CEL diet influences the abundance of bacterial orders such as Selenomonadales and Bacteroidales in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="OAT DIET">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The OAT diet affects the abundance of bacterial orders like Rhodobacterales and Clostridiales in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="LACTOBACILLUS">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactobacillus bacteria are found in the gut of mealworms, contributing to their digestive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillus bacteria in the digestive system of Tenebrio molitor larvae are negatively correlated with PE-oxo and PE-reg diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="NITROGEN METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrogen metabolism in Tenebrio molitor larvae is influenced by the PS diet and the presence of the amoA gene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="UREASE GENE (UREC)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The ureC gene in Tenebrio molitor larvae is positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and MUFA content, indicating its role in nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="NITROSPIRALES">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrosopirales bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae are positively correlated with the PS diet and α-linoleic acid, indicating their role in the digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="NITROSOMONADALES">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrosomonadales bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae are positively correlated with the PS diet and α-linoleic acid, indicating their role in the digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="AMOA GENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The amoA gene is positively correlated with the PS diet and nitrogen metabolism in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="NIFH GENE">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The nifH gene is negatively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and positively correlated with PE-oxo and PE-reg diets in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The nifH gene is used to study the nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the gut microbiota of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="NIRS GENE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The nirS gene is negatively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and positively correlated with PE-oxo and PE-reg diets in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="MUFA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">MUFA content in Tenebrio molitor larvae is positively correlated with the lignocellulose diet and the ureC gene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="MEALWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms produce frass after digesting polystyrene, which can be repurposed as a fertilizer.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are used in sustainable development strategies to address polystyrene waste and promote environmental sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor has been explored for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene terephthalate, a common thermoplastic</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="POLYLACTIC ACIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor has been studied for its ability to biodegrade polylactic acids, a biodegradable polymer</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="RUBBER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor has been studied for its ability to biodegrade rubber, contributing to environmental waste management</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="GRAPHENE OXIDE">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor has been studied for its ability to biodegrade graphene oxide, a material derived from graphene&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade graphene oxide, with their gut microbiome and enzymes playing a key role.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
