    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="BRAN-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms in the bran-fed group show higher survival and pupation rates compared to plastic-fed groups, indicating the importance of nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PS-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms in the PS-fed group show lower survival and pupation rates compared to bran-fed groups, indicating the inadequacy of PS as a sole diet.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PU-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms in the PU-fed group show lower survival and pupation rates compared to bran-fed groups, indicating the inadequacy of PU as a sole diet.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PLASTIC CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms contribute to plastic consumption by ingesting and breaking down polystyrene and polyurethane foams.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="WASTE ELECTRICAL AND ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade waste electrical and electronic equipment (WEEE) plastics, addressing environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSOXIDANS M9">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 was isolated from the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which are known for their ability to degrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR" target="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiome of mealworms contains bacteria that play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics like EPS and PE.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a</data>
    </edge>
