    <edge source="ZOPHOBAS ATRATUS" target="POLYSTYRENE (PS)">
      <data key="d3">65.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene (PS) is degraded by Zophobas atratus larvae through the combined action of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and extracellular oxidase.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus) degrade polystyrene, leading to oxidation, depolymerization, and cleavage of the benzene ring.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus larvae can biodegrade polystyrene (PS), contributing to the reduction of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus generates reactive oxygen species (ROS) during the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus (superworms) ingest and biodegrade polystyrene (PS) foam through oxidation and depolymerization processes in their guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus larvae are capable of biodegrading polystyrene (PS), demonstrating their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus larvae degrade polystyrene (PS) through the combined effect of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and extracellular oxidase of gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus larvae biodegrade polystyrene (PS) in a manner similar to mealworms, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
