    <edge source="GALLERIA MELLONELLA" target="POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">356.0</data>
      <data key="d4">**Galleria mellonella**, commonly known as the greater wax moth, and its larvae or caterpillars, have demonstrated a remarkable ability to biodegrade **polyethylene (PE)**, a common and persistent plastic pollutant. This biodegradation process is facilitated by enzymes present in the saliva of the larvae, as well as their gut microbiome, which plays a key role in breaking down the polyethylene. The gut bacteria associated with Galleria mellonella are capable of metabolizing polyethylene, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste and offering a potential solution to plastic pollution. 

While the larvae can chew and degrade polyethylene films, the process is noted to be incomplete, indicating that further research may be needed to fully harness this capability for large-scale plastic waste management. Galleria mellonella larvae have been shown to degrade low-density polyethylene, demonstrating their potential in addressing plastic pollution. The caterpillars' ability to biodegrade polyethylene has been consistently highlighted across multiple studies, emphasizing their role in plastic waste reduction and environmental cleanup efforts. 

Overall, Galleria mellonella represents a promising biological agent in the fight against environmental plastic pollution, with its unique enzymatic and microbial mechanisms offering a potential solution to the global challenge of plastic waste accumulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella caterpillars can biodegrade polyethylene, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae can biodegrade polyethylene, demonstrating their potential in addressing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella (greater wax moth larvae) can biodegrade polyethylene, contributing to the reduction of plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </edge>
