    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE (PE)" target="MEALWORM LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">51.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae are capable of degrading polyethylene (PE), contributing to plastic waste reduction&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae can biodegrade polyethylene (PE), but the process induces oxidative stress and metabolic dysbiosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae can biodegrade polyethylene (PE), though the process induces oxidative stress and lipid peroxidation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae can biodegrade polyethylene microplastics, leading to depolymerization and molecular weight reduction of the plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae consume and biodegrade polyethylene (PE), contributing to the reduction of plastic waste in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE (PE)" target="MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">69.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene (PE) can be fragmented into microplastics during the biodegradation process by organisms like Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a major source of microplastic pollution in oceans, contributing to environmental degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene fragments into microplastics, which are bioavailable to organisms and pose environmental hazards.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene breaks down into microplastics through physical, chemical, and biological processes in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) can degrade into microplastics, which are studied in this research for their environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene degrades into microplastics, which are environmental pollutants that can be further broken down by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a major component of microplastics, which are emerging environmental contaminants due to their resistance to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene is a major component of microplastics, contributing to their persistence as environmental pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE (PE)" target="HIGH-TEMPERATURE GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (HT-GPC)">
      <data key="d3">46.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HT-GPC is used to analyze the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by mealworm larvae, verifying depolymerization and biodegradation&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is used to analyze the molecular weight changes in polyethylene (PE) during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is used to analyze the degradation of polyethylene by yellow mealworms&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polyethylene in biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC is used to determine the molecular weight of PE samples after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;HT-GPC was used to identify the depolymerization of PE polymers ingested by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
