    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE (PE)" target="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d3">63.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Further research is needed to confirm whether Tenebrio obscurus can degrade polyethylene (PE), expanding its potential role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus can biodegrade polyethylene (PE) materials, with biodegradation rates influenced by the type of PE and its physical-chemical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus can degrade polyethylene (PE), showing depolymerization patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae biodegrade polyethylene microplastics, showing similar physiological responses to PE exposure as Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae also consume and degrade polyethylene (PE), similar to Tenebrio molitor&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae can biodegrade polyethylene (PE), demonstrating their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae can biodegrade polyethylene (PE) plastics, with efficiency influenced by molecular weight and crystallinity.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae can depolymerize and biodegrade polyethylene (PE), with some independence from gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE (PE)" target="MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">170.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) have demonstrated a remarkable ability to consume and biodegrade polyethylene (PE), a common type of plastic. Research has shown that mealworms can ingest PE, including both low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and partially depolymerize it within their gut, leading to chemical modifications in the residual polymers. This process contributes to the biodegradation and mineralization of PE, with up to 49.0 ± 1.4% of ingested PE being converted into CO2. However, the degradation rates of PE by mealworms are generally lower compared to other plastics, such as polystyrene (PS), due to PE's crystalline structure, which makes it less susceptible to depolymerization than amorphous plastics.

Mealworms can degrade PE microplastics, with smaller particles being digested more rapidly. However, this biodegradation process is not without consequences; it induces oxidative stress and physiological damage in the mealworms, indicating that PE does not provide sufficient nutrition for their growth and is less efficient than normal food sources. Despite these challenges, mealworms can survive on a diet of PE microplastics, highlighting their potential role in plastic waste reduction.

Studies have also explored the effects of supplementing mealworms' diet with bran, which enhances their ability to biodegrade PE. Additionally, mealworms have been tested alongside other plastics, such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), to assess their ability to degrade multiple types of plastics. While mealworms are capable of degrading PE, their performance in this regard is less efficient compared to other organisms, such as greater wax moth larvae.

In summary, mealworms exhibit a unique ability to consume and biodegrade polyethylene, contributing to the breakdown of plastic waste. However, the process is limited by the chemical structure of PE, causes physiological stress to the mealworms, and is less efficient than their degradation of other plastics. These findings underscore the potential of mealworms in plastic waste management while also highlighting the challenges associated with their use in this context.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </edge>
