    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">641.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) have demonstrated a remarkable ability to consume, depolymerize, and biodegrade polystyrene (PS), a common type of plastic, including expanded polystyrene (EPS) and extruded polystyrene (XPS) foams. This biodegradation process is facilitated by a combination of host enzymes and the gut microbiota of mealworms, which play a critical role in breaking down polystyrene. The gut bacteria are particularly important, as they enable depolymerization and mineralization of polystyrene, leading to changes in its molecular structure, functional groups, and crystalline structure in the egested frass. 

During this process, polystyrene is degraded into lower molecular weight compounds, microplastics, nanoplastics, and carbon dioxide (CO2), with some of the degraded material being incorporated into the mealworms' biomass. However, while mealworms can ingest and metabolize polystyrene, it does not provide sufficient nutrition for their growth, often resulting in starvation-like effects, metabolic changes, and oxidative stress. Despite these physiological challenges, their ability to degrade polystyrene is significant, as it contributes to the reduction of persistent plastic waste in the environment.

The efficiency of polystyrene degradation by mealworms can vary depending on the type of polystyrene and regional factors, but overall, they demonstrate a consistent capacity to break down this environmental pollutant. Mealworms are increasingly being studied for their potential role in environmental sustainability, particularly in addressing the global issue of plastic pollution. Their gut microbiome, which hosts bacteria capable of degrading polystyrene, is a critical component of this biodegradation process. 

Mealworms consume polystyrene at a high rate, making it the most favorable plastic for degradation, as evidenced by visible holes and mass loss in the plastic sheets. However, the low nutritional value of polystyrene leads to changes in the mealworms' mass and viability over different time intervals, highlighting the trade-offs involved in this biodegradation process. Supplementing mealworms with soy protein or bran can enhance their degradation rates, further improving their efficiency in breaking down polystyrene.

Overall, mealworms are capable of chewing, eating, and degrading polystyrene waste materials, contributing to its biodegradation and depolymerization, and offering a promising avenue for addressing plastic pollution. Their ability to degrade polystyrene microplastics, despite inducing oxidative stress and physiological responses, underscores their potential as a natural solution for managing plastic waste and reducing environmental pollution. The efficiency and microbial response of mealworms in biodegrading polystyrene can vary based on the molecular weight of the polystyrene, but their gut</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa