    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="FTIR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">30.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR analysis detects changes in the functional groups of residual polystyrene in frass, indicating biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analysis identifies chemical changes, such as the incorporation of oxygen, in PS degraded by mealworms&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analysis reveals chemical modifications consistent with the degradation and partial oxidation of polystyrene (PS) during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analysis is used to study the oxidation of polystyrene films exposed to bacterial strains like E. hormaechei LG3.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="TGA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TGA analysis characterizes thermal changes in polystyrene feedstock and frass samples, providing insights into biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;TGA analysis indicates weight loss stages in polystyrene degradation by mealworms, showing the breakdown of the polymer.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </edge>
