    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="TENEBRIO MOLITOR LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">31.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene (PS) is less degradable than PLA, causing mild dehydration and growth inhibition in Tenebrio molitor larvae when used as an exclusive food source.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can also degrade polystyrene (PS), although the process is less efficient than in vivo biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) microplastics, particularly when the PS is aged.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae consume and degrade polystyrene (PS), with aged PS showing higher consumption rates compared to PE.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="YELLOW MEALWORM">
      <data key="d3">43.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworm larvae can degrade polystyrene, enhancing their nutritional value and reducing mortality rates&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms are capable of degrading polystyrene (PS), contributing to the reduction of plastic waste&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms can biodegrade polystyrene (PS) through the action of their gut microbiome, contributing to the reduction of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms can degrade polystyrene, with a degradation rate of up to 15.3%, showing their effectiveness in breaking down this plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
