    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="REDUNDANCY ANALYSIS (RDA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Redundancy Analysis (RDA) explains the variation in polystyrene consumption rates based on factors like density and molecular weight.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="VARIATION PARTITIONING ANALYSIS (VPA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Variation Partitioning Analysis (VPA) determines the contribution of factors like density and molecular weight to polystyrene consumption rates.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="JUVENILE MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Juvenile mealworms demonstrate the ability to consume and degrade polystyrene waste materials, similar to mature mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">EPS is a type of polystyrene foam that mealworms can biodegrade</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="EXTRUDED POLYSTYRENE (XPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">XPS is another type of polystyrene foam that mealworms can biodegrade</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="GPC (GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">GPC is used to analyze changes in the molecular weight and distribution of polystyrene during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="LAN TIAN PLASTIC COMPANY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lan Tian Plastic Company supplies expanded polystyrene (PS) foam used as feedstock for mealworm larvae in biodegradation studies</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="T. OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. obscurus larvae are more efficient in biodegrading polystyrene (PS) compared to T. molitor, as indicated by the mass loss ratio of frass during thermal decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus larvae consume polystyrene foam at higher rates compared to T. molitor, indicating their potential role in PS biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="T. MOLITOR">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae can biodegrade polystyrene (PS), but their efficiency is lower compared to T. obscurus.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae consume polystyrene foam, though at lower rates than T. obscurus, contributing to PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae consume and degrade polystyrene (PS), contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="THF EXTRACTABLE RATIO (TER)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The THF extractable ratio (TER) measures the residual polystyrene (PS) in frass, indicating the extent of PS degradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="SPECIFIC PS CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The specific PS consumption rate (SPCR) quantifies the amount of polystyrene (PS) consumed by mealworm larvae, reflecting their biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="BENZENE RING CLEAVAGE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Benzene ring cleavage is a key step in the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, breaking down its aromatic structure.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="SPIROPLASMATACEAE">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Spiroplasmataceae exhibited changes in abundance when Tenebrio larvae were fed a polystyrene diet, suggesting its involvement in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasmataceae, a family of gut-associated bacteria, shows noticeable changes in relative abundance when T. obscurus and T. molitor are fed polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENTEROCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterococcaceae, a family of gut-associated bacteria, changes in relative abundance when T. obscurus and T. molitor are fed polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The microbial community diversity in the gut of T. obscurus and T. molitor changes when the larvae are fed polystyrene (PS), affecting biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENTEROBACTER ABSURCIS YT1">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter absurcis YT1, isolated from the gut of the Indian meal moth, is associated with the degradation of polyethylene (PE), suggesting a potential role in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="STREPTOCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus and T. molitor that may play a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENTEROTOCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterococcaceae showed significant changes in abundance in Tenebrio larvae exposed to a polystyrene diet, indicating its role in PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="DARKLING BEETLES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Darkling beetles, including Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus, have demonstrated the ability to degrade polystyrene, suggesting a broader capability within the family.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="G. MELLONELLA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">G. mellonella larvae consume and degrade polystyrene (PS), contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="Z. ATRATUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Z. atratus larvae consume and degrade polystyrene (PS), contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="GREATER WAX MOTHS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Greater wax moths consume polystyrene (PS) but show lower survival rates and weight changes compared to superworms and yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="CHAIN SCISSION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chain scission indicates the breaking of polystyrene (PS) molecules into smaller fragments during biodegradation by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENZYMATIC DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzymatic depolymerization is the process by which enzymes in larvae break down polystyrene (PS) into smaller molecules.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ACINETOBACTER SP. ANTC-1">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. AnTc-1 is a PS-degrading bacteria isolated from T. castaneum larvae, indicating its role in polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENTEROBACTER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter microorganisms contribute to the biodegradation of polystyrene in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="RHODOTORULA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rhodotorula is strongly associated with the degradation of polystyrene (PS) in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="CRYPTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cryptococcus is strongly associated with the degradation of polystyrene (PS) in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENTEROBACTER SPP.">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter spp. are capable of degrading polystyrene (PS) under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions, indicating their role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENTEROBACTER HORMAECHEI LG3">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 exhibits polystyrene (PS) degradation activity under both anaerobic and aerobic conditions, with biofilm formation and endotoxin synthesis pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="BACILLUS AMYLOLIQUEFACIENS SCGB1">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is known for its ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), contributing to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 is used in biodegradation assays to study its ability to degrade polystyrene under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="MASS REDUCTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mass reduction is a measure of the decrease in weight of polystyrene (PS) materials during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="SOLID-PHASE MICROEXTRACTION/GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY—MASS SPECTROMETRY (SPME/GC—MS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SPME/GC—MS is used to detect metabolic differences in polystyrene (PS) degradation by bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ULTRASONIC HOMOGENIZER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene (PS) is sonicated using an ultrasonic homogenizer to prepare the PS emulsion.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="BIODEGRADATION ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene is the target material in biodegradation assays, where its degradation by microorganisms is evaluated.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ETHANOL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ethanol (70%) is used to disinfect polystyrene films before they are used in biodegradation assays.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="STRAIN LG3">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Strain LG3 exhibits significant weight reduction of PS films under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions, demonstrating its ability to biodegrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="STRAIN SCGB1">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Strain SCGB1 can degrade polystyrene under aerobic conditions but shows no significant degradation under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENTEROBACTER SP. LG3">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter sp. LG3 forms biofilms and degrades polystyrene (PS) films under anaerobic conditions, demonstrating significant biodegradation activity.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="B. AMYLOLIQUEFACIENS SCGB1">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SCGB1 shows limited activity in degrading polystyrene (PS) films under anaerobic conditions compared to Enterobacter sp. LG3.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="STERILE CONTROL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The sterile control is used as a baseline to compare the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) films by bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="E. HORMAECHEI LG3">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">E. hormaechei LG3 is capable of oxidizing and depolymerizing polystyrene under both anaerobic and aerobic conditions, demonstrating its biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="PEROXIDASE (EFEB)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Peroxidase (efeB) is upregulated during the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating its role in breaking down the polymer.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="HYDROLASE (UBI GENES)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrolase (ubi genes) are involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly in breaking down aryl alcohol compounds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="THIOL PEROXIDASE (PXC)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thiol peroxidase (pxc) is upregulated during the biodegradation of polystyrene, contributing to oxidative stress responses.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ALKYL HYDROPEROXIDE REDUCTASE C (AHPC)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Alkyl hydroperoxide reductase C (ahpC) is upregulated during the biodegradation of polystyrene, playing a role in oxidative stress responses.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="BACTERIOFERRITIN COMIGRATORY PROTEIN (BCP)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacterioferritin comigratory protein (bcp) is upregulated during the biodegradation of polystyrene, contributing to oxidative stress responses.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="WCA VALUES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">WCA values are used to measure changes in the hydrophobicity of polystyrene films after exposure to bacterial strains.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
