      <data key="d6">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="C-N GROUP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The C-N group in the frass of aged PS-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae indicates nitrogen bonding to the benzene ring during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="TRIBOLIUM CASTANEUM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tribolium castaneum has been confirmed to biodegrade polystyrene plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="PLESIOPHTHALMUS DAVIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plesiophthalmus davids is known to biodegrade polystyrene plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ULOMA SP.">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Uloma sp. has been confirmed to biodegrade polystyrene plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="GUT MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microbes in insect larvae play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) through depolymerization and mineralization.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="POLYDISPERSITY INDEX (PDI)">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PDI is calculated to assess the molecular weight distribution of polystyrene (PS) after biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The PDI value increases during the biodegradation of PS, indicating a broad depolymerization pattern.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="THF">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">THF is used as a solvent to extract polystyrene (PS) for analysis in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="EA-IRMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">EA-IRMS is used to analyze the stable isotope fraction in polystyrene (PS) to identify biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT CHANGES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Molecular weight changes in PS indicate its depolymerization and biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="STABLE ISOTOPE SIGNATURE (Δ13C)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The δ13C signature is used to analyze the biodegradation of PS by comparing residual plastics with original foams.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="GALLERIA MELLONELLA LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Galleria mellonella larvae can digest polystyrene microplastics, similar to mealworms, and excrete them after 48 hours.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae can rapidly biodegrade PS microplastics, as shown by fluorescence intensity decline and SEM observations.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="DIGESTIVE BIOFRAGMENTATION MODEL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The model simulates the size-reduction process of PS during biodegradation in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="SEM IMAGE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SEM image analysis examines the surface changes of PS foam before and after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="PLASTICIZERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plasticizers in PS foams are analyzed to understand their impact on biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene (PS) contributes to environmental pollution, particularly as microplastics, due to its resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="AMORPHOUS FRACTIONS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS has amorphous fractions that are more susceptible to endo-type depolymerization by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="METABOLIC PATHWAYS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene biodegradation in mealworms involves perturbations in metabolic pathways such as riboflavin metabolism and glyoxylate and dicarboxylate metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="RIBOFLAVIN METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Riboflavin metabolism is perturbed in mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene, providing electron shuttles and redox mediators.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="HISTIDINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Histidine metabolism is linked to the biodegradation of polystyrene in mealworms, coordinating redox capability and metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="GLYOXYLATE AND DICARBOXYLATE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glyoxylate and dicarboxylate metabolism is significantly perturbed in mealworms during the biodegradation of low- and medium-molecular-weight polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="STARCH AND SUCROSE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Starch and sucrose metabolism is perturbed in mealworms fed with medium- and ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene, influencing depolymerization patterns.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ASCORBATE AND ALDARATE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ascorbate and aldarate metabolism helps maintain redox balance in mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ARGININE AND PROLINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Arginine and proline metabolism is perturbed in mealworms during the biodegradation of medium- and ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene, protecting protein structures.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="PYRIMIDINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pyrimidine metabolism is significantly perturbed in mealworms fed with ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene, affecting RNA and DNA synthesis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="LINOLEIC ACID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Linoleic acid metabolism is associated with membrane integrity repair in mealworms during the biodegradation of ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="GLYCEROPHOSPHOLIPID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glycerophospholipid metabolism is involved in membrane composition and repair during the biodegradation of ultrahigh-molecular-weight polystyrene in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="METABOLOMIC PROFILES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Metabolomic profiles reveal differentially expressed metabolites and perturbed pathways in mealworms during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="REDOX CAPABILITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Redox capability in mealworms is coordinated to facilitate the biodegradation of polystyrene through metabolic mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="ENERGY SUPPLY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Energy supply in mealworms is modulated during the biodegradation of polystyrene, influencing their growth and survival.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="GUT MICROBIOME ALTERATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiome of mealworms is altered in response to the biodegradation of polystyrene polymers, affecting their metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE (PS)" target="COMMERCIAL EPS FOAM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Commercial EPS foam is a type of polystyrene product that contains polymers with varying molecular weights, some of which are challenging to biodegrade.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (GPC)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) was used to determine the molecular weights of polyurethane (PU) polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="ZOPHOBAS MORIO">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zophobas morio has been used to degrade polyurethane (PU), though with lower efficiency compared to Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="FTIR SPECTRA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR spectra are used to analyze the chemical composition of PU, identifying additives and structural differences that influence its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="ELEMENT CONTENT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Element content analysis helps deduce the chemical composition of PU and identify additives that affect its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="MITOCHONDRIAL DNA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mitochondrial DNA in Tenebrio molitor undergoes epigenetic modifications when the larvae are fed PU, indicating potential biological impacts of plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR is used to analyze the chemical changes in polyurethane foam after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS (TGA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TGA is used to study the thermal properties of polyurethane foam before and after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROPHOTOGRAPHY (SEM)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SEM is used to observe physical changes in polyurethane foam after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="ENZYME ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzyme activities in the gut of mealworms are measured to understand their role in breaking down polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="ESTERASES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Esterases in the gut of mealworms help break down ester bonds in polyurethane molecules.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="LIPASES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipases in the gut of mealworms assist in breaking down lipid-like components of polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="PROTEASES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Proteases in the gut of mealworms degrade protein-like structures in polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="LACCASES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Laccases in the gut of mealworms oxidize phenolic compounds, potentially aiding in polyurethane degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="PU-VIRGIN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PU-virgin is used as a control to compare changes in polyurethane foam after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYZER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A thermogravimetric analyzer is used to measure thermal property changes in polyurethane foam during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPY (SEM)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SEM is used to analyze the physical structure of polyurethane foam before and after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR-ATR)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR-ATR is used to analyze chemical bond changes in polyurethane foam after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="SODIUM HYPOCHLORITE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sodium hypochlorite is used to wash polyurethane foam to eliminate microorganisms before SEM analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="PLATINUM COATING">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Platinum coating is applied to polyurethane foam samples for SEM analysis to improve imaging quality.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and degrade polyurethane (PU), contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="LACTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactococcus, a bacterium in the mealworm gut, is associated with maintaining gut health and may contribute to PU degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="ENTEROBACTERIACEAE">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacteriaceae was found to be dominant in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with polyurethane (PU) foam plastics, suggesting its role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriaceae, including Kosakonia, are associated with the degradation of plastics like PU in the mealworm gut.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="PSEUDOMONAS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas, found in the mealworm gut, is associated with the biodegradation of plastics like PU.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="TENERICUTES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenericutes is a dominant phylum in the mealworm gut microbiome and plays a role in the digestion and degradation of PU.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="FIRMICUTES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Firmicutes, a phylum in the mealworm gut microbiome, shows increased abundance in the PU diet and is associated with plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="PROTEOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Proteobacteria, a phylum in the mealworm gut microbiome, is involved in the digestion and degradation of PU.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="BACTEROIDETES">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacteroidetes, a phylum in the mealworm gut microbiome, has lower abundance in the PU diet compared to the bran diet.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="SPIROPLASMA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Spiroplasma, a genus in the Tenericutes phylum, is dominant in the mealworm gut microbiome and associated with PU degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="ENTEROCOCCUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterococcus, a genus in the Firmicutes phylum, is associated with PU degradation in the mealworm gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE (PU)" target="PEDIOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pediococcus, a genus of lactic acid bacteria, contributes to maintaining gut health and is associated with PU degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
