    <edge source="POLYPROPYLENE (PP)" target="MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">62.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are tested for their ability to consume and degrade polypropylene (PP), contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can chew and consume polypropylene (PP), as indicated by the chewed edges of PP bags.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume and degrade polypropylene (PP), as observed in laboratory studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume polypropylene (PP), with varying effects on their mass and viability over different time intervals.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume polypropylene, though at a lower rate compared to polystyrene&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume polypropylene, which is more compatible as a feeding source compared to other plastics, leading to less mass loss&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume polypropylene at a lower rate than polystyrene, but it is still a favorable plastic for degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms have been studied for their ability to consume polypropylene (PP), though it is less commonly consumed compared to polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a</data>
    </edge>
