    <edge source="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)" target="WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Waxworms can degrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), further demonstrating their potential in addressing plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)" target="MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">142.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) have been studied for their ability to consume and degrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), a type of plastic. Research indicates that mealworms can chew, ingest, and partially biodegrade PVC, particularly at the edges of plastic bags or in the form of microplastics. This biodegradation process is facilitated by the mealworms' gut microbiome, although PVC is not as efficiently consumed or degraded as other plastics like polyethylene (PE) or polystyrene (PS). 

Studies have shown that mealworms can metabolize PVC microplastics, but this ability varies depending on the region from which the mealworms originate. While mealworms can degrade PVC, the process is slower compared to other plastics, and PVC microplastics tend to remain in their intestines for a longer duration, suggesting slower digestion and depuration. Additionally, the degradation of PVC by mealworms results in significant mass reduction over time, primarily due to the release of organic compounds. However, this process also produces toxic substances that can harm the mealworms, leading to higher levels of oxidative stress and physiological damage compared to other plastics. 

Despite their ability to degrade PVC, mealworms exhibit lower viability rates and reduced survival and growth when consuming this type of plastic. PVC is considered the least favorable plastic among those tested for mealworm consumption, and its biodegradation induces the highest levels of oxidative stress and physiological challenges. While mealworms have demonstrated the potential to contribute to PVC biodegradation, further research is needed to fully understand the mechanisms and limitations of this process, as PVC has not been as extensively studied as other plastics in this context.</data>
      <data key="d5">5&lt;SEP&gt;6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </edge>
