    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">95.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of biodegrading polyethylene, with their gut microbiome playing a significant role in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can chew and degrade polyethylene films, though they do not grow on a plastic-only diet and generate microplastics as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can degrade polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can digest polyethylene, with gut microbiota playing a key role in its decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can digest polyethylene, a durable plastic, due to their strong mandibles and symbiotic gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade polyethylene, leading to changes in their gut microbiome and contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are capable of degrading polyethylene, suggesting their potential role in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade polyethylene, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume polyethylene, though it is less favorable compared to other plastics like polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can digest polyethylene, with gut microbiota playing a key role in its decomposition.</data>
