    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EPS">
      <data key="d3">60.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms also showed significant mass loss when consuming EPS, suggesting its availability for biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are capable of biodegrading EPS, leading to a reduction in molecular weight and changes in chemical structure&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are tested for their ability to biodegrade EPS, a type of polystyrene plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume EPS, but it negatively affects their growth and survival despite being an amorphous polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can degrade expanded polystyrene (EPS) through their gut microbiota and enzymatic activities, contributing to environmental pollution reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with EPS experienced less pronounced changes in lipid levels compared to PS and PSr.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can chew and burrow into expanded polystyrene (EPS), reducing its environmental persistence.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are fed EPS as a sole energy source, demonstrating their potential to degrade this environmental pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HIPS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are tested for their ability to biodegrade HIPS, a high-impact polystyrene plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume and degrade HIPS, an amorphous polymer, with higher consumption rates compared to other plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PP">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are tested for their ability to biodegrade PP, a polypropylene plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms find it difficult to consume and degrade PP due to its high crystallinity, resulting in limited depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d5">5&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="WHEAT BRAN">
      <data key="d3">63.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can rapidly digest wheat bran, excreting it as frass residue within about 12 hours.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with wheat bran showed moderate mortality rates, indicating its partial suitability as feed.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran accelerated the development of mealworms but also resulted in higher mortality rates, indicating a trade-off between growth speed and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is used as a control diet to compare the effects of plastic diets on mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is used as a control food source in studies involving mealworms to compare its effects with synthetic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is used as a control feed for mealworms in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is used as feed for mealworms during the initial breeding phase before introducing EPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is a more efficient food source for mealworms compared to a plastic-only diet, supporting their growth and survival.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
