    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)">
      <data key="d3">82.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of consuming expanded polystyrene (EPS) foam, contributing to its degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are capable of degrading expanded polystyrene (EPS), offering a potential biological solution to EPS pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can degrade expanded polystyrene (EPS), but it does not provide sufficient nutrition for their growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms ingest expanded polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation through their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume and biodegrade EPS, contributing to its degradation with an average consumption rate of 0.21 ± 0.02 mg/day&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume and biodegrade EPS, reducing its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume expanded polystyrene (EPS), contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were fed EPS to test their ability to degrade this type of plastic, contributing to environmental pollution mitigation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are capable of digesting expanded polystyrene (EPS), suggesting their role in the biodegradation of this environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms ingest and degrade expanded polystyrene (EPS), contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d3">128.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of chewing and degrading LDPE, aiding in plastic biodegradation&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), but it does not provide sufficient nutrition for their growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can ingest LDPE microplastics, resulting in changes to its crystalline structure after metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can ingest and metabolize LDPE microplastics, with regional differences in digestion efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can transform LDPE from a crystalline to an amorphous structure, indicating biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are tested for their ability to consume and degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume low-density polyethylene (LDPE), as evidenced by holes and scratches in the plastic bags.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume low-density polyethylene (LDPE), but it causes significant mass reduction and has more branching, making it more bioresistant.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume low-density polyethylene, though the consumption rate is relatively low&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume low-density polyethylene, with effects on their mass and viability over time&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume low-density polyethylene, but it is less favorable compared to polystyrene and polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms have been tested for their ability to consume low-density polyethylene (LDPE), with varying success rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) with limited depolymerization, as evidenced by increased molecular weights in residual polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade LDPE, reducing its size and removing it from the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), resulting in the generation of microplastics and nanoplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms ingest and degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foams, contributing to the biodegradation of this plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms have been studied for their ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), similar to their role in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </edge>
