      <data key="d4">Mealworms metabolize PVC microplastics, leading to changes in their chemical composition and decomposition stages</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LDPE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms metabolize LDPE microplastics, altering their chemical composition and decomposition process</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CRONOBACTER SAKAZAKII">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cronobacter sakazakii, a potential EPS-degrading bacterium, shows increased population in mealworms fed with EPS, indicating its role in EPS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LACTOCOCCUS GARVIEAE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactococcus garvieae, another potential EPS-degrading bacterium, shows increased population in mealworms fed with EPS, indicating its role in EPS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ZOPHOBAS MORIO">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Both mealworms and Zophobas morio (superworms) are capable of degrading EPS, offering potential solutions to EPS pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Both mealworms and Tenebrio obscurus (dark mealworms) are capable of degrading EPS, offering potential solutions to EPS pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLASTIC WASTE MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste management research explores the potential of mealworms to biodegrade polystyrene and other plastics as a solution to plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MICROPLASTICS POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms contribute to addressing microplastics pollution by biodegrading polystyrene and other plastic materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EMULSIFYING AGENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms produce an emulsifying agent that increases the bioavailability of hydrophobic plastic particles, enhancing biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GUT MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microorganisms in mealworms are essential for the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of biodegrading plastics, such as polyethylene, through the activity of their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms have been studied for their ability to bite and chew plastics, contributing to the biodegradation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PSP">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms fed with PSp showed moderate changes in lipid and carbohydrate levels, indicating partial starvation effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms showed the highest mass loss when consuming PSp, indicating that this form of polystyrene is more susceptible to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PSR">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms fed with PSr showed significant declines in lipid and carbohydrate levels, indicating starvation-like effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were tested for their ability to consume PSr, showing lower mass loss compared to PSp and EPS.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STARVED LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Starved larvae were used as a control group to compare the effects of different plastic diets on mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="IMAGINES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Imagines are the adult forms of mealworms and were included in the study to analyze species-level taxonomic categories.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TOTAL PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The concentration of total protein in mealworms was measured to assess the impact of polystyrene feeding on their biochemical characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Total protein content was measured in mealworms to assess the nutritional impact of different plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TOTAL SUGAR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Total sugar content was analyzed in mealworms to evaluate the effects of plastic diets on their nutritional status.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TOTAL FAT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Total fat content declined significantly in mealworms fed certain plastics, indicating starvation effects.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PROTEIN LEVEL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The protein level in mealworms is slightly affected by the type of polystyrene consumed, with significant differences observed between control and polystyrene-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STARVATION">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms experience starvation-like effects when fed polystyrene, leading to significant metabolic changes and shifts in energy sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation affects mealworms' lipid content and energy usage, influencing their ability to degrade plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TREHALOSE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Trehalose levels in mealworms decreased significantly during starvation, affecting their energy metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GLUCOSE">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glucose is utilized in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with plastic-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose levels in mealworms are crucial for maintaining organ function, particularly during food deprivation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PROTEOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">25.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Proteobacteria becomes dominant in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed medium and ultrahigh molecular weight polystyrene, suggesting its involvement in the biodegradation of higher molecular weight polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteobacteria is a dominant phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, playing a key role in their digestive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteobacteria is a dominant phylum in the gut of mealworms, including many nitrogen-fixing species.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="FIRMICUTES">
      <data key="d3">25.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Firmicutes is a dominant phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to their metabolic functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a dominant phylum in the gut of mealworms, including nitrogen-fixing species.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is the dominant phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed low molecular weight polystyrene or bran, indicating its role in the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ACIDOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acidobacteria is a minor phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with a low but detectable presence.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BACTEROIDES">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacteroides is a minor phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, with varying abundance depending on the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
