      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="KLEBSIELLA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms, playing a role in their digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STREPTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Streptococcus is a genus of bacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to their microbial community.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LACTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactococcus is a genus of bacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms, involved in their digestive processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus, a lactic acid bacterium in the mealworm gut, helps maintain gut health and may contribute to plastic degradation&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus, a genus within Streptococcaceae, is strongly associated with the degradation of low molecular weight polystyrene in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ENTEROCOCCUS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms, playing a role in their digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is involved in the digestion and potential biodegradation of plastics in the mealworm gut</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ERWINIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Erwinia is a genus of bacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to their microbial community.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PAENIBACILLUS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Paenibacillus is a genus of bacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms, involved in their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NGS STUDY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The NGS study analyzed the gut microbiome of mealworms, revealing differences in bacterial community composition based on the type of polystyrene consumed.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIPIDS">
      <data key="d3">31.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipid levels in mealworms decrease significantly when fed less accessible forms of polystyrene, indicating a starvation response&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are extracted from mealworm bodies to study the metabolic changes during polystyrene degradation&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are extracted from mealworms and can be converted into biodiesel, contributing to renewable energy research&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms use stored lipids as an energy source during starvation and when feeding on plastics, with lipid levels varying depending on the type of food.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BIODIESEL">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm remains can be converted into biodiesel, providing a renewable energy source and valorizing waste treatment processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TECHNO-ECONOMIC ANALYSIS (TEA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Techno-economic analysis evaluates the costs and benefits of using mealworms for plastic waste treatment and biodiesel production.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ANTIBIOTICS">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antibiotics can alter the microbiome of mealworms, potentially impacting their LDPE degradation capabilities&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics were used to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms, affecting their ability to degrade polystyrene and other feedstocks.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TECHNO-ECONOMIC FEASIBILITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The techno-economic feasibility study concludes that raising mealworms for plastic degradation is not viable due to high energy requirements and low value creation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIFE CYCLE ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are studied using life cycle assessment to evaluate their environmental impact as a protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIFE CYCLE ASSESSMENT (LCA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are studied using life cycle assessment to evaluate their environmental impact as a protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="OONINCX AND DE BOER, 2012">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oonincx and de Boer, 2012 evaluates the environmental impact of mealworm production as a protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EXPERIMENTAL SETUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The experimental setup is designed to study the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms under controlled conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TEMPERATURE">
      <data key="d3">21.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Temperature affects the survival and biodegradation rates of mealworms, with an optimal range of 25-28°C&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is monitored to ensure optimal conditions for mealworms during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature variations affect mealworm eating habits and mobility.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HUMIDITY">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Humidity is measured to maintain the ideal environment for mealworms during the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Humidity variations affect mealworm eating habits and mobility.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIGHT INTENSITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Light intensity is monitored as it affects the activity and growth of mealworms during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NUTRIENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nutrients are provided to mealworms to enhance their growth and efficiency in biodegrading plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CARRY BAGS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can biodegrade carry bags, though the efficiency is slightly lower compared to Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLASTIC BOTTLES">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can biodegrade plastic bottles, but the efficiency is lower compared to Styrofoam and carry bags.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MACCONKEY AGAR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">MacConkey Agar is used to culture bacteria from mealworm excreta, aiding in the study of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NUTRIENT AGAR MEDIUM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nutrient Agar Medium is used to culture bacteria from mealworms, supporting biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="THERMOCOL">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can biodegrade thermocol, achieving 50% degradation in 15 days, with further reduction in the next 15 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade thermocol, with 50% degradation achieved in 15 days when nutrients are added.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), resulting in the formation of microplastics (MPs).&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade low-density polyethylene more efficiently than high-density polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade low-density polyethylene, showcasing their ability to degrade various types of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can biodegrade high-density polyethylene, though with lower efficiency compared to low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL PARAMETERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmental parameters such as temperature, humidity, and light intensity influence the mealworms' ability to biodegrade plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms and superworms share similar mandibulate mouthparts and gut microbiota, enabling them to chew and degrade plastic materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HDPE FILM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms were used in experiments to study the biodegradation of high-density polyethylene (HDPE) film, demonstrating their potential in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HDPE">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of consuming and degrading HDPE, leading to weight loss of the plastic film&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can consume HDPE, but the effects on their metabolism and weight changes are less understood compared to polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BACILLUS SP. PELW2042">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. PELW2042 was isolated from the gut of mealworms, indicating the role of mealworms in hosting plastic-degrading bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CARBON MASS BALANCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The carbon mass balance study evaluates the conversion of ingested Styrofoam into CO2 and mealworm biomass, highlighting the role of mealworms in biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="13C-LABELED PS">
      <data key="d3">30.0</data>
      <data key="d4">13C-labeled PS is used to trace the assimilation and degradation of polystyrene by mealworms, demonstrating their ability to break down this plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;13C-labeled polystyrene is used to trace the biodegradation and mineralization process of polystyrene by mealworms&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were fed 13C-labeled polystyrene to trace the carbon flow during the biodegradation process, showing the conversion of PS into CO2 and biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are used in experiments with 13C-labeled polystyrene to study the degradation and mineralization process.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="13C-LABELED PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">13C-labeled products are used to trace the metabolic pathways of polystyrene degradation in mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CHLOROFORM">
      <data key="d3">12.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chloroform is used to extract lipids from mealworm bodies to analyze the degradation of polystyrene</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="FECULA">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fecula contains degraded polystyrene fragments, providing evidence of the biodegradation process in mealworms&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms produce fecula as a waste product during the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating the breakdown of ingested Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="FATTY ACIDS (FAS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fatty acids derived from mealworm lipids are analyzed to understand the metabolic pathways of polystyrene degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CARBON DIOXIDE (CO2)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms mineralize ingested polystyrene into CO2, a key byproduct of the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BIOMASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms incorporate carbon from ingested Styrofoam into their biomass, demonstrating the conversion of polystyrene into organic material.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SYMBIOTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms and their gut microbiota engage in symbiotic degradation, breaking down polystyrene into smaller molecules and CO2.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GUT BIOREACTOR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut bioreactor in mealworms facilitates the physical and biochemical degradation of polystyrene through chewing and microbial action.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </edge>
