    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HEXABROMOCYCLODODECANE (HBCD)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can excrete hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD) via frass, indicating their ability to process and eliminate toxic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CORN FLOUR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Corn flour is used as a control diet to compare the effects of polystyrene feeding on mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CARROTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Carrots are part of the diet used to assess the effects of polystyrene feeding on mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BROMPHENOLS">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are studied in the context of bromophenols, which are dioxin precursors found in combustion gases of polystyrene</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SYMBIOTIC GUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The symbiotic gut microbiota in mealworms play a crucial role in the digestion of hard-to-degrade plastics like polyethylene and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GRAPHITE-ADDED EPS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and digest graphite-added EPS, demonstrating their ability to break down modified plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fatty acids are oxidized by mealworms during starvation, derived from stored triglycerides.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms produces fatty acids, such as MUFAs and SFAs, during the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NITRIFICATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiome of mealworms exhibits nitrification, indicating its role in nitrogen metabolism during polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DENITRIFICATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiome of mealworms exhibits denitrification, facilitated by bacteria carrying the nosZ gene, during polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NITROGEN FIXATION">
      <data key="d3">41.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of fixing atmospheric nitrogen, facilitated by their gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms exhibit nitrogen-fixing activity, which is crucial for their survival and the degradation of Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms may adapt to nitrogen-deficient diets like Styrofoam through nitrogen fixation mediated by gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms rely on nitrogen-fixing gut bacteria to survive on nitrogen-deficient diets like Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms exhibits nitrogen fixation, facilitated by bacteria carrying the nifH gene, during polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </edge>
