    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ADIPIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Adipic acid is produced in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly associated with cellulose-based diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="POLYURETHANE FOAM">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are studied for their ability to biodegrade polyurethane foam, a common environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can degrade polyurethane foam, and their gut microbiome plays a crucial role in this biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GUT DNA EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut DNA extraction is performed on mealworms to analyze their gut microbiota, which plays a role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="AMPLICON SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Amplicon sequencing is used to study the microbial diversity in the gut of mealworms, providing insights into their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PROTEASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Protease activity in mealworms is measured to assess their ability to break down proteins, which is relevant to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ESTERASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Esterase activity in mealworms is measured to evaluate their capacity to degrade ester-containing compounds, important for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIPASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipase activity in mealworms is measured to determine their ability to break down lipids, which is relevant to biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LACCASE ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Laccase activity in mealworms is measured to assess their ability to degrade phenolic compounds, which is important for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PU FOAM">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and degrade polyurethane (PU) foam, demonstrating their ability to adapt to low-nutrient diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume and biodegrade PU foam, although it is less palatable and nutritious compared to bran.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TOXIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms metabolize toxic compounds derived from PU foam degradation, contributing to weight loss and energy expenditure.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MEALWORM WEIGHT LOSS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm weight loss is observed when they consume PU foam, as it is less nutritious and more toxic than bran.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MEALWORM METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm metabolism is affected by the consumption of PU foam, which is less nutritious and more toxic than bran.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm metabolism is crucial for their ability to digest plastics, affecting their weight changes and overall viability.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms produce enzymes such as lipases, esterases, proteases, and laccases to degrade polyurethane (PU) foam.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="IMMUNE SYSTEM MOLECULES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Immune system molecules are synthesized in mealworms in response to stress or nutrient scarcity.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TRYPTIC SOY AGAR (TSA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut bacteria from mealworms were cultured on TSA medium for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ETHANOL">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ethanol is used to sterilize mealworms before gut microbiome analysis to remove external contaminants.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol was used to disinfect mealworms before extracting gut samples for microbial analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethanol is used to surface sterilize mealworms before isolating their gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </edge>
