      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and degrade Styrofoam (PS), contributing to its biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CORN STRAW (CS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms consume corn straw (CS), but the degradation rate is lower compared to other feedstocks</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CABBAGE (CB)">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms consume cabbage (CB) more efficiently than polystyrene and corn straw, leading to better growth</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ANTIBIOTIC TREATMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antibiotic treatment suppresses the gut microbiota of mealworms, reducing their ability to degrade polystyrene and corn straw</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CELLULOSE (CS)">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms also degrade cellulose, though the process is less dependent on gut microbiota compared to polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with cellulose show similar gut microbial changes to those fed with polystyrene, suggesting a shared response to different diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIGNOCELLULOSE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of degrading lignocellulose, a complex plant compound, through the activity of their gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LAC640">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lac640, a laccase enzyme, is highly upregulated in mealworms during the degradation of polystyrene and lignocellulose.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="OATMEAL">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oatmeal is used as a control food for mealworms, providing a baseline for lipid content and energy usage comparisons.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was used as a control diet for mealworms to compare the effects of polystyrene consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was used as a control food to compare the biochemical characteristics of mealworms fed polystyrene materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </edge>
