      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GLOSS MEASUREMENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms were tested on polystyrene materials with measured gloss to assess biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BIOCHEMICAL ANALYSES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biochemical analyses were conducted on mealworms to evaluate the effects of polystyrene consumption on their physiology.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GENOMIC DNA EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Genomic DNA extraction from mealworm guts was performed to study the microbial communities involved in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PCR AMPLIFICATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PCR amplification was used to study the bacterial communities in mealworm guts, providing insights into polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DGGE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DGGE analysis was used to study the diversity of bacterial communities in mealworm guts during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TOTAL SUGARS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The concentration of total sugars in mealworms decreased significantly when fed polystyrene materials, indicating a biochemical impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TOTAL LIPIDS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The concentration of total lipids in mealworms decreased significantly when fed polystyrene materials, indicating a biochemical impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EPS (EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is the most accessible form of polystyrene for mealworms, leading to higher biodegradation rates&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are used to evaluate the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene (EPS) in controlled experiments.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SUGARS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sugar levels in mealworms decrease when fed less accessible forms of polystyrene, reflecting metabolic stress</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PSR (RECYCLED POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Recycled polystyrene (PSr) is less accessible for mealworms to consume, leading to lower biodegradation rates</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PSP (PACKAGING POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Packaging polystyrene (PSp) is moderately accessible for mealworms, resulting in moderate biodegradation rates</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DGGE (DENATURING GRADIENT GEL ELECTROPHORESIS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DGGE analysis revealed changes in the gut microbiome of mealworms based on the type of polystyrene consumed</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STARVATION PROCESS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms exhibit a starvation process when fed less accessible forms of polystyrene, indicated by decreased lipid and sugar levels</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HARDNESS MEASUREMENTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hardness measurements confirmed that EPS is the most accessible form of polystyrene for mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GLOSS MEASUREMENTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gloss measurements indicated that less glossy polystyrene surfaces are more accessible for mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MFR (MELT FLOW RATE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Melt flow rate (MFR) was used to assess the relationship between polymer chain length and mealworm accessibility</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="FOUR BACTERIAL SPECIES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Four bacterial species were identified in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed polystyrene, potentially involved in biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GUT MICROBIOMES">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiomes of mealworms synergistically assist in the biodegradation of PET intermediates and provide nitrogen sources.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HOST GENES">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Host genes in mealworms are upregulated during PET depolymerization and metabolism, indicating their essential role in the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLASTIC MIXTURES">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) can biodegrade plastic mixtures, with significant effects on their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can biodegrade plastic mixtures such as PE + PS, demonstrating the adaptability of their gut microbiome to chemically dissimilar plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb</data>
    </edge>
