    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CHEMICAL MODIFICATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms induce chemical modifications in plastics, such as the incorporation of oxygen, during the degradation process</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PUTATIVE GAS FRACTION (PGF)">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The putative gas fraction (PGF) indicates the mineralization of plastics into CO2 by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MASS BALANCE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mass balance is used to track the degradation and mineralization of plastics by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MIXED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and degrade mixed plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, with higher mass loss observed for polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="POLYSTYRENE FOAM">
      <data key="d3">33.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of degrading polystyrene foam, with their gut microbiome contributing significantly to the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms can digest and biodegrade polystyrene foam, offering a potential solution for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with polystyrene foam experienced high mortality due to its lack of nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with polystyrene foam experienced high mortality rates due to the lack of nutritional value, highlighting the negative impact of this environmental pollutant on their survival.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NANO-POLYSTYRENE (NPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms' gut bacteria were studied for their ability to degrade NPS, a form of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EPS + OAT DIET">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The EPS + oat diet was used to test mealworms' ability to degrade plastics while maintaining nutritional intake.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EPS + PE DIET">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The EPS + PE diet was used to assess mealworms' ability to degrade multiple types of plastics simultaneously.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="2,4-DI-TERT BUTYLPHENOL (2,4-DTBP)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms accumulate 2,4-DTBP in their tissues and frass during polystyrene degradation, indicating a toxic byproduct of the process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LOU ET AL. (2021)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lou et al. (2021) studied the effects of different diets on mealworms, reporting variations in survival and pupation rates.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BRANDON ET AL. (2018)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Brandon et al. (2018) linked low survival and fertility rates in mealworms to nutrient-poor, plastic-only diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STERILE SALT WATER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sterile salt water is used to rinse mealworms after sterilization to prepare them for gut microbiome analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SURVIVAL RATES (SRS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Survival rates (SRs) are measured to assess the viability of mealworms fed different diets, including plastics and bran.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PUPATION RATES (PRS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pupation rates (PRs) are measured to evaluate the developmental success of mealworms fed different diets, including plastics and bran.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PUPATION RATE (PR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pupation rate (PR) measures the developmental success of mealworms, indicating that bran co-diets enhance pupation compared to plastic-only diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SPIROPLASMA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Spiroplasma is a key bacterium in the mealworm gut microbiome, contributing to digestion and plastic degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EFFICIENT PLASTIC-BIODEGRADING REACTOR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">An efficient plastic-biodegrading reactor is being developed to utilize mealworms and their gut microbes for large-scale plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="YEASTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yeasts are used as a standard diet for mealworms and are compared to PS foam in terms of productivity effects.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DIET 1">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 1 is used to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DIET 2">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 2 is used to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, with added water.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2, which includes hydration, improves mealworm growth, survival rates, and polystyrene consumption yields.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DIET 3">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 3 is used to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, without added water.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CONTROL DIET">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The control diet is used as a baseline to compare the effects of polystyrene-containing diets on mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="OXIDATIVE STRESS">
      <data key="d3">47.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms experience oxidative stress and physiological damage when exposed to microplastics during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms experience oxidative stress during polystyrene biodegradation, leading to higher nitrogen retention and altered metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with PE, PS, and PVC microplastics exhibit oxidative stress responses, indicating potential toxicity from plastic ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidative stress is induced in mealworms due to the ingestion of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The ingestion and biodegradation of microplastics by mealworms induce oxidative stress, leading to increased ROS production and oxidative damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms experience oxidative stress during the biodegradation of polystyrene, which affects their antioxidant systems and membrane integrity.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GUT MICROBIAL ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microbial activity in mealworms is enhanced by hydration and plays a key role in polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmental management studies the use of mealworms for biodegrading plastic waste, offering a sustainable solution for pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="POLYETHYLENE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms, including Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus, are capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) plastics, contributing to environmental solutions</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </edge>
