    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="WEIGHT FLUCTUATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms exhibit weight fluctuations when fed with plastics, indicating potential adaptation or nutritional challenges</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ADAPTATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms may adapt to consuming plastics over time, as indicated by changes in their weight and consumption rates</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PVC">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume PVC, showing similar weight changes as with HDPE, despite differences in chemical structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms struggle to chew and digest PVC due to its smooth surface, resulting in minimal mass loss and energy depletion.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NANOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms do not accumulate nanoplastics in their frass due to rapid elimination during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms may encounter nanoplastics during plastic consumption, though their ability to metabolize or detoxify them is less understood.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLANT MATERIALS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are studied for their growth performance and nutrient composition when fed on fresh plant materials-supplemented diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="FRESH PLANT MATERIALS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are fed fresh plant materials to study their growth performance and nutrient composition.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIFE CYCLE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The life cycle of mealworms is studied to determine their ability to develop when fed on polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TRANSCRIPTOMIC RESPONSE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms exhibit changes in gene expression when exposed to plastic feeding, indicating their adaptability to plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="XPS (EXTRUDED POLYSTYRENE)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are used to assess the biodegradation of extruded polystyrene (XPS) in food packaging and coffee cups.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PUPAE">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pupae are a developmental stage of mealworms observed in the study as part of their life cycle during polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms that follow the larval stage, studied in the context of PS biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SOY PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Soy protein is used as a nutritional supplement to study its effects on mealworm survival rates and polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein significantly increases the rate of polystyrene degradation by mealworms, improving their survival rates and PS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein supplementation improves the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SURVIVAL RATES (SR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Survival rates (SR) are higher for mealworms fed PS supplemented with soy protein or bran, indicating improved viability under these conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EPS INSULATION MATERIAL">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms consume and degrade EPS insulation material, contributing to the reduction of polystyrene waste</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS WASTE MATERIALS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms consume and degrade various PS waste materials, contributing to environmental cleanup</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="EPS INSULATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and degrade EPS insulation, a type of polystyrene waste material.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="XPS FOAM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and degrade XPS foam, a high-density polystyrene material used in products like coffee cups.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GUT MICROFLORA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microflora of mealworms are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="OXYGENASE-MEDIATED ATTACK">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">An oxygenase-mediated attack is hypothesized to initiate the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SELECTIVE BREEDING">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nutritional supplementation enables selective breeding of mealworms for enhanced polystyrene biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION (MWD)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Changes in MWD are used to assess the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="THF EXTRACTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">THF extracts of frass are analyzed to study the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="KJELDAHL METHOD">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Kjeldahl method is used to determine the nitrogen content in mealworms, which is then used to calculate their crude protein content.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DRIED WHEY">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dried whey as a feed led to moderate mortality rates and occasional cannibalism among mealworms, suggesting it is a less optimal feed compared to potatoes.&lt;SEP&gt;Dried whey is used as a protein-rich feed for mealworms in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with dried whey showed moderate mortality rates, indicating its partial suitability as feed.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="RAW WARE POTATOES">
      <data key="d3">12.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Raw ware potatoes are used as a carbohydrate-rich feed for mealworms in the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DEAD INDIVIDUALS">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dead individuals are mealworms that died during the experiment, used to calculate mortality rates.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="WARE POTATOES">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms fed with ware potatoes showed the lowest mortality rates, indicating its suitability as feed.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIFE CYCLE STUDY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The life cycle study focused on analyzing the impact of different feeds on the mortality and development of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="POTATOES">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Potatoes were the most effective feed, resulting in the lowest mortality rates and no cannibalism, indicating their suitability for mealworm rearing.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BIODEGRADATION ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacterial strains isolated from mealworms are studied in biodegradation assays to understand their role in degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ENTEROBACTER SP. LG3">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are the source of Enterobacter sp. LG3, which was isolated from their gut and frass specimens.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STRAIN LG3">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms host strain LG3 in their gut, which contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STRAIN SCGB1">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms host strain SCGB1 in their gut, which degrades polystyrene under aerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">35.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms host nitrogen-fixing bacteria in their guts, which are isolated and studied for their role in nitrogen fixation and plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the gut of mealworms enable the assimilation of atmospheric nitrogen into their biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the gut of mealworms enable them to adapt to nitrogen-deficient diets like Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the guts of mealworms are essential for nitrogen fixation, which supports their ability to degrade Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ACETYLENE REDUCTION ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The acetylene reduction assay is used to measure nitrogen-fixing activity in mealworms fed with Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;The acetylene reduction assay was used to measure the nitrogen-fixing activity of mealworms, confirming their ability to fix nitrogen.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STABLE NITROGEN ISOTOPE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Stable nitrogen isotope analysis is used to determine nitrogen fixation activity in mealworms by analyzing nitrogen isotope compositions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </edge>
