      <data key="d4">Pyrosequencing is used to analyze the composition of gut bacterial communities in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STERILE PBS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sterile PBS is used to rinse and prepare gut microbial inoculum from mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STABLE NITROGEN ISOTOPES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Stable nitrogen isotopes (δ15N) are analyzed to confirm the incorporation of fixed nitrogen into mealworm tissues.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="KLEBSIELLA SPALLANZANII BIT-96">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Klebsiella spallanzanii BIT-96, a nitrogen-fixing gut bacterium, is essential for mealworms' survival on nitrogen-deficient diets like Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NITROGEN-FIXING SYMBIONT">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrogen-fixing symbionts, such as Klebsiella, form a symbiotic relationship with mealworms, enabling them to survive on nitrogen-deficient diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SYMBIOTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Symbiotic bacteria in the gut of mealworms play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="POLYPROPYLENE BOX">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A polypropylene box is used to breed mealworms in a controlled environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GAMMAPROTEOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms harbor Gammaproteobacteria in their intestines, which are involved in the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DIGESTIVE BIOFRAGMENTATION MODEL">
      <data key="d3">23.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The digestive biofragmentation model predicts the time required for mealworms to digest and remove different-sized plastic particles.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive biofragmentation model simulates the size-reduction process of plastic particles ingested by mealworms, predicting their biodegradation efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive biofragmentation model was developed to predict the fragmentation process of plastics ingested by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BIOFRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms fragment ingested plastics into smaller particles, such as microplastics (MPs), during digestion.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms generate and accumulate nanoplastics (NPs) in their frass during the biodegradation of plastics like PS and LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics were not detected in mealworm frass, suggesting they are unlikely to accumulate during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLASTIC REMOVAL EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic removal efficiency measures the effectiveness of mealworms in degrading and removing ingested plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLASTIC-DEGRADING MICROBIAL ACTIVITIES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic-degrading microbial activities in mealworms are enhanced by feeding them plastics, reaching peak efficiency after 3-4 weeks.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLASTIC FOAM">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic foam, such as PS and LDPE, is biodegraded by mealworms, reducing its environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL IMPLICATIONS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The study concludes that mealworms can biodegrade plastics without significant accumulation of nanoplastics, reducing environmental concerns.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MICROPLASTICS (MP)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms fragment ingested plastics into microplastics larger than 2 µm during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES (ROS)">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ingesting microplastics induces oxidative stress in mealworms, as indicated by increased levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS).&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms exhibit increased ROS levels due to the ingestion and biodegradation of microplastics, leading to oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms exhibit increased levels of ROS when exposed to microplastics, indicating oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SUPEROXIDE DISMUTASE (SOD)">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms exhibit increased SOD levels as a defense mechanism against oxidative stress caused by microplastic ingestion and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms produce SOD to counteract oxidative stress caused by microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Superoxide dismutase (SOD) levels in mealworms reflect their antioxidant performance and physiological stress responses when consuming microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MALONDIALDEHYDE (MDA)">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Malondialdehyde (MDA) levels in mealworms indicate lipid peroxidation and oxidative damage caused by microplastic ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms show elevated MDA levels as a biomarker of oxidative damage from microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms show elevated MDA levels, indicating oxidative damage and lipid peroxidation due to microplastic ingestion and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="BICINCHONINIC ACID (BCA) PROTEIN QUANTIFICATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">BCA protein quantification is used to measure protein levels in mealworm homogenates.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ANOVA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">ANOVA is used to evaluate significant differences in mealworm survival indices and plastic degradation metrics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ANTIBIOTIC PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antibiotic performance reflects the ability of mealworms to withstand oxidative stress caused by microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TOXIC SUBSTANCES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The biodegradation of PVC by mealworms produces toxic substances, which can negatively impact their health and survival.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CHLORINATED INTERMEDIATES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chlorinated intermediates produced during PVC biodegradation can harm mealworms' intestinal health.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="HYDROGEN CHLORIDE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrogen chloride released during PVC depolymerization can damage mealworms' intestinal epithelium.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DIGESTIVE ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A plastic-only diet can impair the production of digestive enzymes in mealworms, leading to decreased nutrient uptake.&lt;SEP&gt;Digestive enzymes in mealworms contribute to the biodegradation of microplastics by breaking down plastic particles.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TRANSPORTERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A plastic-only diet can negatively affect the production of transporters in mealworms, reducing nutrient uptake.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GUT BACTERIAL CONSORTIUM">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut bacterial consortium in mealworms aids in the biodegradation of microplastics, particularly in depolymerizing long-chain polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="FUNCTIONAL ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Functional enzymes in mealworms play a role in the depolymerization of microplastics, particularly targeting short-chain and side-chain polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ENDO-TYPE DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms perform endo-type depolymerization on ingested microplastics, breaking down polymer chains internally.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="DEPURATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms excrete undigested microplastics during depuration, with the time required varying depending on the type of plastic ingested.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STRAW POWDER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can efficiently digest straw powder, similar to wheat bran, and excrete it as frass residue.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="VEGETABLES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Vegetables are a natural food source for mealworms, which they can digest and excrete efficiently.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MICROPLASTIC-INDUCED DAMAGE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Undigested microplastics in mealworms can cause physical damage and inflammatory responses, reducing their physiological performance.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="NUTRIENT UPTAKE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastic retention in mealworms can reduce nutrient uptake, leading to potential health issues.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="INFLAMMATORY RESPONSES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The retention of undigested microplastics in mealworms can elicit inflammatory responses, affecting their health.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIPID PEROXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms experience lipid peroxidation as a result of oxidative damage caused by microplastic ingestion and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="MITOCHONDRIAL DYSFUNCTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms may experience mitochondrial dysfunction due to oxidative damage caused by microplastic ingestion, leading to abnormal development.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms consume and degrade polystyrene microplastics, leading to significant molecular weight changes and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS-1">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS-1 is consumed and degraded by mealworms, demonstrating their ability to biodegrade polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS-2">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS-2 is degraded by mealworms, showing their capacity to break down higher molecular weight polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS-3">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS-3 is consumed and degraded by mealworms, further confirming their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS-4">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS-4 is degraded by mealworms, indicating their ability to handle high molecular weight polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS-5">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS-5 is consumed and degraded by mealworms, showcasing their efficiency in biodegrading very high molecular weight polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS-6">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS-6 is the highest molecular weight polystyrene microplastic tested, and its degradation by mealworms demonstrates their biodegradation limits.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SPECIFIC PS CONSUMPTION RATES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Specific PS consumption rates (SPCRs) measure the rate at which mealworms consume polystyrene microplastics, indicating their biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS REMOVAL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS removal measures the percentage of polystyrene microplastics degraded by mealworms, reflecting their biodegradation effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="FRASS YIELD">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Frass yield is the ratio of frass produced to polystyrene microplastics consumed, indicating the efficiency of mealworm biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass yield reflects the digestibility of polystyrene microplastics by mealworms, indicating the extent of PS MP degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS (PS MPS)">
      <data key="d3">27.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of depolymerizing and biodegrading polystyrene microplastics through their gut microbiome and enzymatic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume and biodegrade polystyrene microplastics, breaking them down into smaller components&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume and degrade polystyrene microplastics, contributing to their biodegradation and removal from the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SPECIFIC PS CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The specific PS consumption rate measures the efficiency of mealworms in degrading polystyrene microplastics</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STARVATION (ST)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Starvation conditions are tested to evaluate the survival and cannibalistic behavior of mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PS-1, PS-2, PS-3, PS-4, PS-5, PS-6">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Different types of polystyrene microplastics are consumed by mealworms to assess their biodegradation efficiency</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SPCR (SPECIFIC POLYSTYRENE CONSUMPTION RATE)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The SPCR measures the rate at which mealworms consume PS MPs, indicating their efficiency in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STARVATION CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Starvation conditions influence the consumption behavior of mealworms, particularly their ability to consume polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GUT MICROBIOME ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microbiome analysis provides insights into the microbial communities in mealworms that are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ENTEROBACTERIACEAE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacteriaceae is associated with the depolymerization and biodegradation of polystyrene in mealworms, particularly for medium molecular weight polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="STREPTOCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Streptococcaceae, particularly the genus Lactococcus, is highly abundant in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed low molecular weight polystyrene, indicating its role in the biodegradation of low molecular weight polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="SHANNON INDEX">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Shannon index is used to measure the diversity and richness of the gut microbiome in mealworms under different diet conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PRINCIPAL COORDINATE ANALYSIS (PCOA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Principal Coordinate Analysis (PCoA) is used to compare the gut microbial communities of mealworms fed different diets, revealing distinct clusters based on molecular weight of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="METABOLIC PATHWAY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Metabolic pathway analysis is used to study alterations in the metabolomic profiles and pathways of mealworms under different diet conditions, particularly in response to polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="ULTRAHIGH-MW POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ultrahigh-MW polystyrene polymers cause metabolic dysbiosis and oxidative stress in mealworms, making their biodegradation challenging.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PLA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms show the highest mass loss when consuming PLA, indicating its relative ease of degradation compared to other plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="PROTEIN CONTENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Protein content in mealworms varies based on the type of food consumed, with PLA-fed larvae showing the highest levels.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="CARBOHYDRATES CONTENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Carbohydrates provide energy for mealworms, with PLA-fed larvae showing the highest levels and PVC/PS-fed larvae showing the lowest.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="LIPID CONTENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipid content decreases in mealworms during starvation or when fed non-nutritive materials like PVC and PS.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="TRIGLYCERIDES">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms rely on triglycerides as a primary energy source during starvation or stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Triglyceride levels in mealworms are connected to starvation periods and energy usage during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS" target="GLYCOGEN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glycogen is metabolized by mealworms during the initial stages of starvation to provide energy.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
