    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d3">98.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms (Zophobas atratus) are capable of digesting polystyrene, with their gut microbiota playing a key role in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus) can biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, contributing to the breakdown of this plastic in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, contributing to waste management solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms show less significant changes in nutrient composition when consuming polystyrene compared to mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms can consume polystyrene (PS) at a higher rate than mealworms, making them a potential solution for PS waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms can eat and digest polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms, or Zophobas atrata larvae, are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, aiding in plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus) mineralize polystyrene at higher rates, aiding in plastic waste degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus) can depolymerize polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms can biodegrade polystyrene, demonstrating their potential in addressing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus) degrade polystyrene through reactive oxygen species in their gut, contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
