    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">46.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene contributes to environmental pollution due to its resistance to degradation and widespread use in products like Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene contributes to environmental pollution due to its resistance to natural degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a major contributor to environmental pollution due to its persistence and resistance to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a major environmental pollutant, particularly in water reservoirs, posing risks to aquatic life and ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a significant environmental pollutant due to its resistance to biodegradation, prompting studies on its breakdown.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a major contributor to environmental pollution, which can be mitigated through biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE YT1">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter asburiae YT1 contributes to the degradation of polystyrene in waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="BACILLUS SP. YP1">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polyethylene, a synthetic polymer similar to polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. YP1 plays a role in the degradation of polystyrene in waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="TRIBOLIUM CASTANEUM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tribolium castaneum can degrade polystyrene, expanding the range of plastic-eating insects.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ACHROIA GRISELLA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Achroia grisella can degrade polystyrene, further highlighting the potential of insects in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="Z. MORIO">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms (Z. morio) can consume polystyrene and are less affected by its consumption compared to mealworms, suggesting potential for longer-term polystyrene utilization.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="MICRO-POLYSTYRENE (MPS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene is the parent material of micro-polystyrene (MPS), which is used in the study to investigate its biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="GUT MICROBIAL ENRICHMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene is the target of gut microbial enrichment studies, which aim to identify bacteria capable of degrading it.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">70.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut bacteria are studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in mealworms and superworms play a crucial role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene (PS).&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria from Tenebrio molitor larvae can form biofilms on polystyrene and degrade it, contributing to its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria isolated from Tenebrio molitor can form biofilms on polystyrene and cause physical changes, indicating their role in biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in T. molitor are likely responsible for the biodegradation of polystyrene, as they are absent in control mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in mealworms are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene, breaking it down into smaller molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in mealworms are essential for breaking down polystyrene into smaller components during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in organisms like Tenebrio molitor play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="THF">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">THF is used as a solvent to extract polystyrene from mealworm frass for further analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;THF is used to extract residual polystyrene from frass samples for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="CHLOROFORM-D">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chloroform-D is used to dissolve polystyrene for NMR analysis, enabling the study of its chemical changes.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="NMR SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The NMR spectrometer is used to analyze the chemical structure of polystyrene extracts.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="WATER CONTACT ANGLE (WCA) ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Water contact angle analysis is used to study the hydrophobicity of polystyrene after microbial treatment.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="TGA ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TGA analysis demonstrates the thermal decomposition of polystyrene in frass from Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating reduced PS content and new decomposition stages.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="CARBONYL GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The appearance of carbonyl groups in FTIR spectra indicates the chemical transformation of polystyrene during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="HYDROXYL GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydroxyl groups are formed on the surface of polystyrene during microbial degradation, reducing its hydrophobicity.&lt;SEP&gt;The presence of hydroxyl groups in frass samples suggests a shift in surface properties from hydrophobic to hydrophilic during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="STRAIN YT1">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Strain YT1 is involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene, as evidenced by its ability to reduce the mass of PS pieces during incubation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ACINETOBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacterium, isolated from Tribolium castaneum larvae, is capable of degrading polystyrene, indicating its role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="GUT MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d3">98.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microorganisms in larvae are essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in mealworms and superworms are essential for the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in mealworms play a key role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in mealworms play an essential role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in mealworms play a crucial role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in yellow mealworms play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, breaking it down into smaller molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi, play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polystyrene within insect guts.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in mealworms and superworms are essential for the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in mealworms play a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms in mealworms play a direct role in the degradation of polystyrene, breaking it down into simpler compounds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="STYROFOAM">
      <data key="d3">111.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrofoam is a form of expanded polystyrene (PS), which is a major contributor to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a type of expanded polystyrene foam, which is a major environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a form of expanded polystyrene and contributes significantly to plastic waste pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a form of expanded polystyrene, making it a significant contributor to environmental pollution&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a form of expanded polystyrene, which is a major environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a major product of polystyrene and contributes to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a trade name for expanded polystyrene, a major contributor to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is an expanded form of polystyrene, widely used in packaging and insulation, and is resistant to microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a type of expanded polystyrene foam, making it a major source of polystyrene pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a form of expanded polystyrene and is a major environmental pollutant due to its resistance to degradation&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a type of expanded polystyrene, making it a significant environmental pollutant due to its resistance to degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a form of expanded polystyrene that is degraded by insect larvae, contributing to environmental waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam is a form of expanded polystyrene, a common environmental pollutant that mealworms can consume and degrade.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="GRAIN BEETLE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The grain beetle (Tenebrio molitor) has been studied for its ability to biodegrade polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste reduction.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">41.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microbial degradation by gut bacteria in mealworms is a key process in the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation is a key process in the breakdown of polystyrene, facilitated by microorganisms such as gut bacteria in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation involves the breakdown of polystyrene by microorganisms, including those in Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial degradation involves the breakdown of polystyrene by microorganisms, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="DEGRADATION COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Degradation compounds, such as monomers and oligomers, are formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="H2O">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The addition of H2O to the diet of mealworms improves the biodegradation of polystyrene monomer and oligomer residues.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="LONG-CHAIN FREE FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Long-chain free fatty acids are formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating enzymatic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="AMIDES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Amides are formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, correlating with enzymatic activity.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="STYRENE">
      <data key="d3">88.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene degrades into styrene, a chemical that may affect the gut microbiome of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is composed of styrene monomers, which are key intermediates in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is a degradation product of polystyrene, detected in the frass of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is derived from the monomer styrene, and its biodegradation often involves the breakdown of styrene-related compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is a monomer used in the synthesis of polystyrene and is considered a potential intermediate in its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is the main decomposition product of polystyrene, detected during its thermal decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is a monomer detected in frass, indicating the breakdown of polystyrene during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is composed of styrene monomers, which are degraded through oxidation and aromatic ring cleavage during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is the monomer used to produce polystyrene, and its biodegradation is a key step in the breakdown of polystyrene by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is a key component in the production of polystyrene, making its degradation crucial for addressing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is a monomer of polystyrene and can be produced as an intermediate metabolite during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="STYRENE OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d3">43.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene is broken down into styrene oligomers during the biodegradation process by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are also susceptible to biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, which can be further broken down by microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene and are more biodegradable than the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="LACTOCOCCUS LACTIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactococcus lactis is more abundant in the gut of mealworms fed with PS, suggesting a potential role in PS biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
