      <data key="d4">Dichloromethane is used as a solvent in the preparation of polystyrene emulsions for biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Dichloromethane is used to dissolve polystyrene for the preparation of polystyrene emulsion in microbial degradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Dichloromethane is used to prepare polystyrene emulsions for microbial degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="PRESS MOLDING">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Press molding is used to form polystyrene plates for physico-mechanical assessment in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="HAAKE RHEOMEX 252">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Haake Rheomex 252 is used for processing polystyrene in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="BOY 35A">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">BOY 35A is used for injection molding of polystyrene in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="WANNER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wanner is used for grinding polystyrene in research studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="LABTECH LP-20B">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LabTech LP-20B is used for press molding of polystyrene under specific conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="MELT FLOW JUNIOR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Melt Flow Junior is used to assess the melt flow ratio of polystyrene under controlled conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="SHORE D">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Shore D is used to measure the hardness of polystyrene samples.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="MULTI GLOSS 268 PLUS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Multi Gloss 268 Plus is used to measure the gloss of polystyrene and related materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ADVEX INSTRUMENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Advex Instrument is used for contact angle measurements on polystyrene film surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="LAND SNAILS (ACHATINA FULICA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Land snails can ingest polystyrene and expel microplastics, contributing to plastic mass loss.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="LAND SNAILS">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Land snails can eat and contribute to the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Land snails disintegrate and biofragment ingested polystyrene (PS) foam into microplastics (MPs).</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ZOPHOBAS ATRATA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zophobas atrata larvae are also capable of biodegrading polystyrene, further aiding in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="FILAMENTOUS FUNGI">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Filamentous fungi can utilize chemically oxidized polystyrene as a co-substrate, aiding in its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="FTIR-ATR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR-ATR is used to analyze the chemical changes in polystyrene substrates after exposure to Tenebrio molitor homogenates, contributing to understanding plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="HOMOGENATE PASTE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene is mixed with homogenate paste to study its biodegradation by organisms like mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="KAPLAN D V ET AL.">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Kaplan et al. investigated the biodegradation of polystyrene, contributing to the understanding of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="STYRENE MONOMER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene monomer is polymerized to form polystyrene, which is then degraded by organisms like superworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="PLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d3">32.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste, including polystyrene, is a major environmental pollutant that mealworms can biodegrade, helping to mitigate its impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a component of plastic waste, which is a major environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a major component of plastic waste, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a significant component of plastic waste, contributing to environmental pollution, particularly in marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="SOLID-STATE 13C CP/MAS NMR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">This technique was used to study the chemical changes in polystyrene during biodegradation by superworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="TG-FTIR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TG-FTIR spectroscopy was employed to analyze the thermal degradation of polystyrene in superworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="P450-DRIVEN SYNTHETIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">P450-driven synthetic bacteria are engineered to degrade polystyrene using P450 enzymes, aiding in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="STYROFOAM-FEEDING LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrofoam-feeding larvae degrade polystyrene efficiently, converting it into CO2 and biomass.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="FECULA">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fecula contains depolymerized metabolites of polystyrene, indicating the degradation process in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is transformed into fecula, which contains lower molecular weight fragments and new components, indicating depolymerization and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="LIPIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipids are formed from the incorporation of polystyrene into the biomass of mealworms during mineralization.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="CO2">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">CO2 is a byproduct of the mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="WHITE POLLUTANTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">White pollutants refer to dispersed polystyrene debris, highlighting the environmental impact of PS waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="MANDIBULATE INSECTS">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mandibulate insects can chew and eat plastic packages, but their ability to degrade polystyrene is limited.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="PHENYL DERIVATIVES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phenyl derivatives are produced during the depolymerization and oxidation of polystyrene in the mealworm gut.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ALIPHATIC HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aliphatic hydrocarbons are newly formed compounds resulting from the depolymerization of polystyrene in the mealworm gut.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="MEALWORM GUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiota of mealworms plays a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, facilitating its depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="POLYMER DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polymer degradation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene into smaller fragments, observed in the mealworm gut.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="EXTRACELLULAR ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Extracellular enzymes secreted by gut microbiota catalyze the depolymerization of polystyrene into smaller molecules.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="BIOMASS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Limited carbon from polystyrene degradation products is assimilated into the mealworm's biomass during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="NON-INTENTIONALLY ADDED SUBSTANCES (NIAS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Non-intentionally added substances (NIAS), such as 2,4 di-tert butylphenol, originate from the PS polymer and were identified in frass and gut tissue.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="FOOD CONTACT MATERIALS (FCMS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene is widely used in food contact materials (FCMs) due to its low cost and durability.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="THERMOPLASTIC POLYMER">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene is a thermoplastic polymer used extensively in consumer and industrial applications.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="MECHANICAL RECYCLING">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mechanical recycling is a process for recycling polystyrene, though it is not currently of great market interest.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ENVIRONMENTAL CONCERNS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmental concerns are raised due to the widespread use of polystyrene and its potential harm to the environment and human health.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larvae are fed polystyrene, and the fatty acid content in their bodies is analyzed to assess the impact of this environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae are used in the study to investigate the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating their role in breaking down this chemical.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae of Tenebrio molitor are studied for their ability to survive and grow when fed polystyrene, though it is insufficient for growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b8a706f2bd66291a3ef925114ffd9300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="CHLOROFORM">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chloroform is used to dissolve polystyrene film, enabling the preparation of EPS solution for experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform is used to extract organic compounds from polystyrene degradation products in frass and larvae gut tissue.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="2,4-DTBP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">2,4-DTBP is a degradation product of Irgafos 168, an additive in polystyrene, detected in frass during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="DIET 1">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 1 involves feeding Tenebrio molitor larvae with barley and polystyrene to study biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1, with a bran:PS ratio of 4:1 and no water, resulted in higher excretion of PS oligomers in the frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="DIET 2">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 2 involves feeding Tenebrio molitor larvae with bran and polystyrene to study biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2, with a bran:PS ratio of 20:1 and water, resulted in a lower excretion of PS oligomers compared to other diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2, with a high bran-to-PS ratio and water, shows the highest extent of polystyrene degradation by insects.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="DIET 3">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 3 involves feeding Tenebrio molitor larvae with minimal handling and polystyrene to study biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 3, with a bran:PS ratio of 20:1 and no water, also resulted in higher excretion of PS oligomers in the frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrocarbons are present in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae due to the degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ENZYMATIC BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzymatic biodegradation is a process by which polystyrene can be broken down by enzymes produced by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT">
      <data key="d3">88.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene is a major environmental pollutant due to its persistence and accumulation in natural ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a major environmental pollutant due to its persistence and contribution to plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a major environmental pollutant due to its persistence and resistance to natural degradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a significant environmental pollutant due to its persistence and resistance to degradation&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is an environmental pollutant studied for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a significant environmental pollutant due to its persistence and resistance to natural degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a significant environmental pollutant due to its persistence and resistance to natural degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a major environmental pollutant that can be biodegraded by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a significant environmental pollutant, particularly in marine environments, due to its persistence and non-biodegradable nature.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="NANOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene fragments into nanoplastics, which are harmful to ecosystems and human health.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA ATCC 13182">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182, isolated from the gut of mealworms, is associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca strain ATCC 13182, isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA JCM 1665">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Klebsiella oxytoca strain JCM 1665, isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA NBRC 102593">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Klebsiella oxytoca NBRC 102593, isolated from the gut of mealworms, is linked to the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca strain NBRC 102593, isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ENTEROBACTER HORMAECHEI">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
