      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 is capable of biodegrading polystyrene with a weight loss of 6% over 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 is capable of degrading polystyrene and produces laccases and esterases, contributing to its degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 is involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene, as identified in mealworm intestines.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="PSEUDOMONAS STUTZERI M3">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas stutzeri M3 contributes to the degradation of polystyrene, as suggested by its isolation from mealworm intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas stutzeri M3 shows moderate polystyrene degradation capabilities and produces laccases and catalase.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="PSEUDOMONAS SP M7">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas sp M7 contributes to polystyrene degradation through its cellulase and esterase enzymes.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas sp M7 is implicated in the biodegradation of polystyrene, as identified in mealworm intestines.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </edge>
