      <data key="d4">E. coli MA04 showed no significant degradation of polystyrene, similar to the negative control.&lt;SEP&gt;E. coli MA04 shows similar surface appearance to the polystyrene film without microorganism inoculation, indicating limited or no polystyrene-degrading activity</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="K. NITROREDUCENS MA77">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">K. nitroreducens MA77 reduced the size and number of polystyrene particles, indicating its role in degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="HOLOTRICHIA PARALLELA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Holotrichia parallela's gut microbiota, including P. nitroreducens and Rhizobium radiobacter, have been studied for their role in polystyrene degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="ACINETOBACTER SP. NYZ450">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450, isolated from mealworms, shows the ability to form biofilm and decompose polyethylene, indicating potential for polystyrene degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE" target="K. OXYTOCA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Klebsiella oxytoca is found in the intestines of mealworms and can digest polystyrene, aiding in its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
