    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE" target="DARK MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dark mealworms can biodegrade high-purity polyethylene microplastics, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Dark mealworms can biodegrade low-density polyethylene via gut microbe-independent depolymerization, demonstrating a unique mechanism of plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE" target="MEALWORM LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">39.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae are grown on polyethylene substrates to assess their ability to degrade plastic and their nutritional content.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae can also degrade polyethylene, demonstrating their ability to process different types of plastics&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae can biodegrade polyethylene, with their gut microbiome playing a crucial role in the degradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae can biodegrade polyethylene, contributing to waste management solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae can also degrade polyethylene, demonstrating their ability to process different types of plastics</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </edge>
