    <edge source="POLYPROPYLENE" target="SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d3">57.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polypropylene (PP) containers were used to house superworms during the study, as they are impervious to the worms.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms (Zophobas atratus) can biodegrade polypropylene, similar to Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms are capable of biodegrading polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms, like yellow mealworms, can biodegrade polypropylene via gut-microbe-dependent processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms are capable of biodegrading polypropylene via gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization, similar to yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms are capable of biodegrading polypropylene, similar to yellow mealworms, through microbial activity in their guts.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c</data>
    </edge>
