      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pediococcus sp. is associated with the degradation of high molecular weight polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYPROPYLENE" target="ACINETOBACTER SP.">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. is associated with the degradation of medium molecular weight polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYPROPYLENE" target="BACILLUS SP.">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. is associated with the degradation of low molecular weight polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is capable of degrading polypropylene microplastics, highlighting its role in the biodegradation of this plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYPROPYLENE" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The molecular weight of polypropylene significantly influences its biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor, with different degradation patterns observed for low, medium, and high MW PP</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYPROPYLENE" target="WAX MOTH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax Moth larvae interact with polypropylene, indicating potential for plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
