    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="ARTIFICIAL CLIMATE CHAMBER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The artificial climate chamber maintains controlled environmental conditions for Tenebrio obscurus larvae during biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="LYOPHILIZATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lyophilization is used to euthanize and prepare Tenebrio obscurus larvae for biomass composition analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="BIOMASS NUTRIENT CONCENTRATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biomass nutrient concentration levels are determined to assess the nutritional composition of Tenebrio obscurus larvae after biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="PE-MPS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PE-MPs are biodegraded by Tenebrio obscurus larvae, aiding in the mitigation of microplastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae ingest and degrade PE-MPs, leading to oxidative stress and ecotoxicity.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="SPECR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SPECR is used to measure the rate at which Tenebrio obscurus larvae consume polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="SRS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SRs are measured to assess the survival of Tenebrio obscurus larvae when fed with polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="AWCRS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">AWCRs are used to evaluate weight changes in Tenebrio obscurus larvae when fed with polyethylene polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="SURVIVAL RATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Survival rate is used to assess the health and viability of Tenebrio obscurus larvae when fed different types of plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="SPECIFIC PE CONSUMPTION RATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Specific PE consumption rate measures the amount of polyethylene consumed by Tenebrio obscurus larvae, indicating their ability to biodegrade plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="CRUDE PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus larvae contain crude protein, making them a potential alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="CRUDE FAT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus larvae contain crude fat, contributing to their nutritional value as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="BENZOQUINONE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Benzoquinone is found in the biomass of Tenebrio obscurus larvae, indicating its presence in their biochemical composition.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="CRUDE CARBOHYDRATE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Crude carbohydrate is a nutritional component found in Tenebrio obscurus larvae, contributing to their value as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="FATTY ACID COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fatty acid composition is analyzed in the dry biomass of Tenebrio obscurus larvae to assess their nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="WB-FED CONTROLS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">WB-fed controls are used as a baseline to compare oxidative stress and biodegradation effects in Tenebrio obscurus.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="DARKLING BEETLE">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus is a species of darkling beetle larvae that can biodegrade EPS, similar to Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus is a species within the darkling beetle family, sharing common characteristics and ecological roles.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7b499dc783c21f764eb35989a449f3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus, like Tenebrio molitor, can biodegrade EPS with the help of gut bacteria, contributing to environmental waste reduction.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus is also being considered as an alternative protein source for livestock feed, similar to Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="PE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus larvae have been found to biodegrade polyethylene (PE), highlighting their role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="BIODEGRADATION PATTERN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The biodegradation pattern of T. obscurus is similar to T. molitor, involving depolymerization of HDPE and LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="PE MPS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus larvae can ingest and biodegrade polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs), leading to changes in their molecular weight and hydrophobicity.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="GENTAMICIN">
      <data key="d3">70.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gentamicin also affects the gut bacteria of Tenebrio obscurus, reducing their ability to biodegrade LDPE&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin also suppresses gut bacteria in Tenebrio obscurus, affecting their LDPE depolymerization capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin also suppresses the gut bacteria of Tenebrio obscurus larvae, reducing their biodegradation efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin also suppresses the gut bacteria of Tenebrio obscurus, impacting its LDPE biodegradation capability.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin also suppresses the gut bacteria of Tenebrio obscurus, negatively affecting its polystyrene degradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin inhibits the gut bacteria of Tenebrio obscurus, reducing their polystyrene degradation capability&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin is used to suppress gut bacteria in Tenebrio obscurus larvae, affecting their ability to degrade polystyrene&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin suppresses gut bacteria in Tenebrio obscurus larvae, partially inhibiting their ability to depolymerize polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin suppresses gut microbes in Tenebrio obscurus larvae, reducing but not stopping the depolymerization of LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamicin suppresses the gut microbes of Tenebrio obscurus larvae, inhibiting their ability to biodegrade polystyrene (PS) foam.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE FOAM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus larvae consume expanded polystyrene foam as their sole diet, demonstrating their ability to degrade this form of polystyrene</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microbial community analysis is also applied to Tenebrio obscurus larvae to understand their role in LDPE biodegradation&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial community analysis is conducted on Tenebrio obscurus larvae to understand the role of gut microbiota in polystyrene biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="OXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus also induces oxidation in polystyrene, facilitating its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Additional research is required to validate the ability of Tenebrio obscurus to degrade polyethylene terephthalate (PET), expanding its biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="URBAN INSECTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus is a species of urban insect, often studied in the context of urban entomology and stored-product pests.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="WORLDWIDE DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus has a worldwide distribution, making it a subject of ecological and environmental research.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0c7b499dc783c21f764eb35989a449f3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus larvae also digest and biodegrade LDPE, similar to Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae are capable of biodegrading LDPE, with a depolymerization pattern that is independent of gut microbes but enhanced by them.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="GUT MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microbes in Tenebrio obscurus are also involved in the biodegradation of LDPE, though the process is less dependent on them compared to Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in Tenebrio obscurus larvae enhance the biodegradation of LDPE, although the process can occur independently of them.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="GUT COMMUNITIES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut communities in Tenebrio obscurus larvae are shaped by their diet and play a role in enhancing the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="OXIDIZED INTERMEDIATES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio obscurus larvae produce oxidized intermediates during the depolymerization of low-density polyethylene (LDPE), indicating a broad biodegradation pattern.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="LDPE FOAMS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE foams are also consumed and biodegraded by Tenebrio obscurus larvae, though at slightly lower rates&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE foams are used in studies to assess the biodegradation capacity of Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="GENTAMICIN SULFATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gentamicin sulfate is used to suppress gut bacteria in Tenebrio obscurus, affecting their ability to biodegrade LDPE</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="LDPE DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE depolymerization is also facilitated by the digestive systems of Tenebrio obscurus larvae, though at slightly lower efficiency compared to Tenebrio molitor</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="SURVIVAL RATE (SR)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Survival rate (SR) is used to measure the ability of Tenebrio obscurus larvae to survive when fed LDPE foams</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="SPECIFIC LDPE CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Specific LDPE consumption rate (SPCR) measures the amount of LDPE consumed by Tenebrio obscurus larvae</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="PE-2">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PE-2 is a polyethylene feedstock used to study the biodegradation capabilities of Tenebrio obscurus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-2 shows higher molecular weight reductions when digested by Tenebrio obscurus larvae compared to Tenebrio molitor</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut bacteria in Tenebrio obscurus larvae also play a key role in the biodegradation of LDPE and HDPE.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="PVC">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PVC is a plastic polymer that has not been tested for biodegradation by Tenebrio obscurus but is a potential target for future studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="PP">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PP is a plastic polymer that has not been tested for biodegradation by Tenebrio obscurus but is a potential target for future research.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO OBSCURUS" target="PET">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PET is a plastic polymer that has not been tested for biodegradation by Tenebrio obscurus but is a potential target for future studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RHODOCOCCUS RUBER" target="ALKANE HYDROXYLASE SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rhodococcus ruber uses alkane hydroxylase systems to degrade hydrocarbons.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RHODOCOCCUS RUBER" target="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rhodococcus ruber is known to degrade polyethylene, providing insights into the microbial degradation of plastics like PET.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RHODOCOCCUS RUBER" target="LACCASE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rhodococcus ruber produces laccase, an enzyme that plays a key role in the biodegradation of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RHODOCOCCUS RUBER" target="BIOFILM FORMATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rhodococcus ruber forms biofilms on polyethylene, facilitating its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE FOAMS" target="TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyurethane foams are studied for their toxicity and fire retardancy properties.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE FOAMS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyurethane foams are a significant environmental pollutant due to their persistence and resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE FOAMS" target="RAPESEED OIL-BASED POLYOL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rapeseed oil-based polyol is used in the preparation of rigid polyurethane foams, offering a sustainable alternative to traditional materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE FOAMS" target="PALM OIL-BASED POLYESTER POLYOL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Palm oil-based polyester polyol is used in the production of biodegradable polyurethane foams, promoting eco-friendly materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE FOAMS" target="METATHESIZED PALM OIL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Metathesized palm oil is used to improve the functional properties of polyurethane foams, enhancing their sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYURETHANE FOAMS" target="CRUDE GLYCEROL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Crude glycerol is used as a base material for producing biodegradable polyurethane foams.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACINETOBACTER BACTERIUM" target="TRIBOLIUM CASTANEUM">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter bacterium was isolated from Tribolium castaneum larvae, indicating its potential role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter bacterium was isolated from Tribolium castaneum, highlighting its role in polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter bacterium was isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum, indicating a relationship between the insect and the bacterium.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANTS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics are a type of environmental pollutant that can bioaccumulate and disrupt ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="NANOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">40.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics and nanoplastics are both forms of plastic pollution, with nanoplastics being smaller and potentially more hazardous in aquatic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics can degrade into nanoplastics, which are smaller and potentially more harmful to the environment and organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics can fragment into nanoplastics, which are more harmful and can cause genotoxicity and intestinal damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are smaller fragments of microplastics, but no accumulation was found in the frass of mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are formed from the fragmentation of microplastics, posing additional environmental hazards</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="MEALWORM LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae are studied for their ability to metabolize microplastics, which affects their feeding and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Undigested microplastics are found in the frass of mealworm larvae after PE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="PHYSIOLOGICAL PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The physiological performance of mealworm larvae is assessed under exposure to microplastics and nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="KOI CARP">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Koi carp are studied for their ecological and isotopic responses to microplastics, providing insights into the impact of plastic pollution on aquatic organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Koi carp's ecological and isotopic responses are influenced by the presence of microplastics in their environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT">
      <data key="d3">53.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics are a major environmental pollutant, posing risks to marine and terrestrial ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a significant environmental pollutant resulting from the fragmentation of larger plastic debris.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a significant environmental pollutant, affecting marine life and ecosystems due to their persistence and widespread distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a significant environmental pollutant, affecting ecosystems and organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a significant environmental pollutant that affects ecosystems and organisms due to their persistence and ability to accumulate.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a significant environmental pollutant, affecting marine and terrestrial ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ZEBRAFISH (DANIO RERIO)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zebrafish are used as a model organism to study the effects of microplastics on aquatic organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="PLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d3">34.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics are a direct result of the breakdown of larger plastic waste items, contributing to environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste contributes to the formation of microplastics, which are a major environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste degrades into microplastics, which persist in the environment and pose ecological risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste breaks down into microplastics, which are small particles that persist in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="FOAMY PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Foamy plastics, such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), contribute to the formation of microplastics, which are a major environmental concern.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="MEALWORM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can ingest and depolymerize microplastics, but their growth and survival are affected by microplastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="FOOD CHAIN">
      <data key="d3">27.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can be transferred through the food chain, leading to potential accumulation and environmental hazards.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics have been found in the food chain, posing risks to both environmental and human health.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="POPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can transport Persistent Organic Pollutants (POPs), increasing environmental risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="EARTHWORM">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Earthworms are negatively affected by microplastics, which can damage their reproductive and immune systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms can ingest microplastics, which can then be transferred to predators like chickens through the food chain.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="PREDATOR-PREY RELATIONSHIP">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The predator-prey relationship enhances the transport of microplastics through the food chain, leading to their accumulation in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="TROPHIC TRANSFER">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Trophic transfer involves the movement of microplastics and associated pollutants through the food chain, posing risks to organisms and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PET contributes to the formation of microplastics, which accumulate in the environment and disrupt ecosystems&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) contributes to microplastic pollution, affecting marine life and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL IMPLICATION">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Improper plastic disposal results in the formation of microplastics, which accumulate in the environment&lt;SEP&gt;The environmental implication of microplastics highlights their threat to ecosystems and the need for biodegradation solutions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="REPRODUCTIVE TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can cause reproductive toxicity in organisms, affecting their ability to reproduce</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="NEUROTOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can induce neurotoxicity, harming the nervous systems of organisms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="IMMUNE TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can cause immune toxicity, negatively impacting the immune systems of organisms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics are a major contributor to environmental pollution, affecting ecosystems and organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a significant source of environmental pollution, particularly in marine ecosystems, where they pose risks to aquatic life.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmental Science and Technology publishes research on microplastics, including their identification, quantification, and environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="MICROPLASTIC RISK ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastic risk assessment evaluates the environmental risks of microplastics spread through insect feces after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="SEWAGE SLUDGE">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics are found in sewage sludge, highlighting their prevalence in wastewater treatment plants.&lt;SEP&gt;Sewage sludge contains microplastics, requiring purification processes to mitigate environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics are detected in the frass of superworms, showing evidence of polystyrene (PS) degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms contribute to the reduction of microplastics by biodegrading polypropylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="REEF BUILDING CORALS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can impact marine ecosystems, including reef-building corals, through environmental contamination.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)">
      <data key="d3">40.0</data>
      <data key="d4">EPS breaks down into microplastics, which can contaminate land, groundwater, and waterways, posing environmental and health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) contributes to the formation of microplastics, which are a significant environmental pollutant.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="FOOD SAFETY">
      <data key="d3">55.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Food safety concerns arise from the potential contamination of food sources by microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Food safety concerns arise from the potential entry of microplastics into the food or feed chain due to incomplete plastic degradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Food safety is impacted by the potential contamination of food sources with microplastics from degraded polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Food safety studies assess the impact of microplastics on food quality and human health&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics contamination in aquatic environments can affect food safety by poisoning water resources and the organisms that inhabit them.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics in fishmeal pose a threat to food safety and human health, highlighting the need for mitigation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics pose a risk to food safety by potentially contaminating food sources and affecting human health.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="MARINE POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics are a significant component of marine pollution, affecting marine organisms and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="MARINE DEBRIS">
      <data key="d3">25.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Marine debris includes microplastics, which are a significant component of pollution in coastal and marine environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine debris often includes microplastics, which are a major component of ocean pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are a form of marine debris that result from the breakdown of larger plastic items and can have harmful effects on marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) contributes to the formation of microplastics, which are a significant environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;The degradation of LDPE by organisms like the greater wax moth can result in the formation of microplastics</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </edge>
