      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lumbricus terrestris incorporates microplastics into its burrows, affecting terrestrial ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbricus terrestris incorporates microplastics into its burrows, highlighting the interaction between soil organisms and plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="MEALWORM GUT MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The mealworm gut microbiome interacts with PS microplastics, using them as a carbon source for microbial respiration and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="WAX MOTH LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax moth larvae generate microplastics as a byproduct of incomplete polyethylene degradation, posing environmental risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL REMEDIATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The use of larvae for environmental remediation is problematic due to the generation of microplastics during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="FOOD WASTE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Food waste integration with plastic degradation is complicated by the excretion of non-digested microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="AQUATIC ENVIRONMENTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics are found in aquatic environments, where they aggregate, deposit, and enhance the transport of contaminants.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="SYNTHETIC FIBERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Synthetic fibers are a significant source of microplastics in the environment, contributing to pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="GORGAN BAY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gorgan Bay has been identified as a site with microplastic pollution in sediments, fishes, and benthic organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="COASTAL LAGOONS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Coastal lagoons are affected by microplastic pollution, as highlighted in systematic reviews.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="PLATEAU CULTIVATED LAND">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plateau cultivated land in Yunnan Province has been studied for microplastic abundance and distribution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="RAWAL LAKE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rawal Lake has been identified as a site with environmental exposure to microplastics in sediments and water.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="SOIL-GROUNDWATER ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics in the soil-groundwater environment undergo aging, migration, and co-transport of contaminants.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="MARINE FOOD WEB">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The marine food web is impacted by the trophic transfer of microplastics and mixed contaminants, posing risks to human health.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="PLASTICS AND HUMAN HEALTH">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastics and human health studies focus on the impact of microplastics on human health, including ingestion and exposure.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="IMMUNO-TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics have been shown to cause immuno-toxicity, affecting the immune systems of marine organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larvae contribute to the fragmentation of ingested microplastics, reducing their size during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="EARTHWORMS">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Earthworms are used to study the toxicity of polystyrene microplastics and their interactions with soil particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms contribute to the biofragmentation of ingested microplastics, though their plastic-degrading abilities are limited.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ANTARCTIC KRILL">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antarctic krill are involved in the biofragmentation of microplastics, though they have poor plastic-degrading abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill can turn microplastics into nanoplastics through digestive fragmentation, impacting marine ecosystems</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ROS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics induce the formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS), leading to oxidative stress in organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="DEVELOPMENTAL TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can cause developmental toxicity, adversely affecting the growth and development of organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="CYTOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics induce cytotoxicity, causing toxic effects on cells and cellular functions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="BIOFRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biofragmentation by Tenebrio molitor larvae breaks down ingested microplastics into smaller particles, though residual particles remain at micrometer size.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Physiological responses in Tenebrio molitor larvae, such as weight loss and oxidative damage, are caused by the ingestion and biodegradation of microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS" target="ZEBRAFISH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Juvenile zebrafish are used to study the combined toxic effects of microplastics and other pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
