    <edge source="LDPE" target="MEALWORM GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm gut bacteria can degrade LDPE, leading to weight loss and changes in surface properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm gut bacteria, particularly the S1 isolate, are capable of degrading LDPE by utilizing it as a carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="PM S1">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE is degraded by PM S1 bacteria, which utilize it as a carbon source.&lt;SEP&gt;PM S1 colonized LDPE beads, contributing to their degradation&lt;SEP&gt;PM S1 degrades LDPE, resulting in weight loss and structural changes, indicating its biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="PM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PM (microbial isolates) colonized and degraded LDPE beads, contributing to their biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="FTIR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical changes in LDPE beads during biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze the degradation of LDPE beads by PM S1, identifying changes in functional groups.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="ELEMENTAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Elemental analysis is used to measure the degradation of LDPE by PM S1 bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="CARBON DIOXIDE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE degradation by PM S1 produces carbon dioxide, indicating mineralization of the plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE is an environmental pollutant that accumulates in the environment and is targeted for biodegradation by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="SEM ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SEM analysis confirms structural changes on the surface of LDPE beads after treatment with PM S1, indicating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="CARBON AND HYDROGEN ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Elemental analysis of carbon and hydrogen shows the percentage loss in LDPE beads after degradation by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="BIOLOGICAL OXYGEN DEMAND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biological Oxygen Demand studies support the significant weight loss of LDPE beads after biodegradation by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="GPC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">GPC analysis is used to quantify the molecular weight of LDPE after degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC analysis is used to study the molecular weight changes in LDPE during biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="HT-GPC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HT-GPC analysis is used to quantify the molecular weight of LDPE after degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="OXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE undergoes oxidation during biodegradation by mealworms, evidenced by chemical changes in FTIR spectra</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut bacteria in mealworms contribute to the degradation of LDPE by breaking down its linear and branched chains.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="T. MOLITOR LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae can ingest and digest LDPE, contributing to its biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="PE MULCHING FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE is a major component of PE mulching films, which are a significant source of plastic pollution in soil.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="HOMOGENATE PASTE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Homogenate paste from Galleria mellonella and Tenebrio molitor is used to study the degradation of LDPE, indicating the role of enzymatic activity in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE films are treated with homogenate paste to study the biodegradation potential of the plastic</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="FTIR-ATR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR-ATR is used to analyze the chemical changes in LDPE substrates after exposure to insects or their homogenates, providing insights into plastic degradation mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="GREATER WAX MOTH LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Greater wax moth larvae are investigated for their potential to degrade LDPE, offering a biological solution to plastic pollution</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="ACETONITRILE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acetonitrile is used to wash LDPE films after biodegradation experiments to remove homogenate paste residues</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="ETHYLENE GLYCOL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ethylene glycol is a potential byproduct of LDPE biodegradation, detectable using FTIR analysis</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="FTIR-MICROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR-microscopy is used to analyze chemical changes on the surface of LDPE films after biodegradation experiments</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="ACINETOBACTER SP.">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. is strongly associated with the biodegradation of LDPE in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="GUT MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae play a role in the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE), though the depolymerization pattern changes under antibiotic suppression.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="CARBONYL INDEX">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The carbonyl index measures the oxidation level of residual LDPE polymers in the frass of Tenebrio larvae, indicating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="TENEBRIO LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio larvae can degrade LDPE, with their gut microbiota enhancing carbon and nitrogen metabolism to facilitate this process.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio larvae experience less physiological impairment when exposed to LDPE, a more biodegradable plastic polymer, compared to other polyethylene types.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSOXIDANS M9">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is known to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), demonstrating its diverse metabolic capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms (Zophobas atratus larvae) can degrade LDPE through the action of their gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="T. MOLITOR">
      <data key="d3">25.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading LDPE, another type of polyethylene microplastic.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae can biodegrade LDPE microplastics, as shown by changes in molecular weight and FTIR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae biodegrade LDPE through oxidation and chemical modifications, as evidenced by FTIR and ¹H NMR analyses.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="T. OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. obscurus larvae are capable of biodegrading LDPE, another type of polyethylene microplastic.&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus larvae can biodegrade LDPE microplastics, as shown by changes in molecular weight and FTIR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio obscurus larvae biodegrade LDPE through oxidation and chemical modifications, similar to Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="CRYSTALLINITY DEGREE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The lower crystallinity degree of LDPE makes it more susceptible to biodegradation compared to LLDPE and HDPE</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="HDPE">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HDPE and LDPE are compared in terms of their biodegradation factors, highlighting differences in their environmental impact&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE is less efficiently biodegraded by mealworms compared to LDPE, indicating differences in biodegradation efficiency based on polymer type</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="PRO-OXIDANT ADDITIVE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pro-oxidant additives are used in LDPE to enhance its abiotic degradation properties</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="ABIOTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Abiotic degradation processes, such as oxidation, are studied in the context of LDPE degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="IRRADIATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE undergoes chemical and morphological modifications when irradiated, which is important for understanding its environmental degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="OXIDATIVE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oxidative degradation is a process by which LDPE breaks down when buried in bioactive soil over long periods.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="1H NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">1H NMR analysis helps identify the formation of oxidized functional groups in LDPE after biodegradation by larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="PE-1">
