      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="OLIVE OIL-BASED METHOD">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The olive oil-based method is used to extract LDPE from frass samples during carbon mass balance analysis</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="GUT MICROBIAL CONSORTIA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbial consortia in the larvae are essential for the biodegradation of LDPE, as they help break down the plastic into smaller components</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The molecular weight distribution of LDPE changes during its biodegradation by larvae, showing reductions in high molecular weight fractions</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="THERMO-OXIDATIVE DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermo-oxidative degradation reduces the molecular weight of LDPE, facilitating its biodegradation by larvae</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="¹H NMR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">¹H NMR spectroscopy shows the chemical modifications in LDPE after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor, including the formation of new metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="Z. ATRATUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zophobas atratus larvae are capable of biodegrading LDPE, contributing to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="G. MELLONELLA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Galleria mellonella larvae biodegrade LDPE, showing their role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="CARBONYL BOND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The formation of carbonyl bonds (C=O) during LDPE biodegradation indicates chemical changes in the plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="HYDROXYL GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydroxyl groups (OH) are formed during LDPE biodegradation, indicating a shift from hydrophobic to hydrophilic properties.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="CH₃ MOIETY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The CH₃ moiety is formed during LDPE biodegradation, indicating the breakdown of long-chain polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="CH₂ GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">CH₂ groups are observed during LDPE biodegradation, indicating the formation of new metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="AMINO ACIDS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Amino acids are residues found in frass after LDPE biodegradation, indicating protein breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="ALDEHYDE MOIETY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The aldehyde moiety is observed during LDPE biodegradation, indicating further chemical modifications.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="NMR SPECTRA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">NMR spectra are used to identify chemical modifications in LDPE, such as the incorporation of oxygen during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDPE" target="CARBON BALANCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Carbon balance analysis is used to track the conversion of LDPE into CO2 during biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
