      <data key="d4">Rhizophora dominica is studied for its interaction with plastic packaging, which is a form of plastic waste</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="HUMAN IMPACT ON MARINE ECOSYSTEMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The study on human impact on marine ecosystems includes the assessment of plastic waste as a significant pollutant affecting marine life</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="PLASTICS RECYCLING">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastics recycling research focuses on addressing the challenges of managing and reducing plastic waste in the environment</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="BIODIESEL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste treated by mealworms can be valorized into biodiesel, creating a revenue stream and reducing environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="LIFE CYCLE IMPACT ASSESSMENT (LCIA)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Life cycle impact assessment evaluates the environmental impacts of treating plastic waste using mealworms, including greenhouse gas emissions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="GREENHOUSE GAS EMISSIONS (GHG)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Greenhouse gas emissions are associated with the treatment of plastic waste, including mealworm digestion and biodiesel production.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="JAMBECK ET AL., 2015">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Jambeck et al., 2015 quantifies the global input of plastic waste into oceans, highlighting its environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="OCEAN">
      <data key="d3">35.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste from land enters the ocean, contributing to environmental pollution, as highlighted by Jambeck et al. (2015).&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste from land enters the ocean, contributing to marine pollution and ecological damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste from land enters the ocean, contributing to marine pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="JAMECK J R ET AL.">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Jambeck et al. studied the inputs of plastic waste from land into the ocean, emphasizing the global issue of marine pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="ECO-FRIENDLY SOLUTIONS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Research studies focus on eco-friendly solutions for plastic waste management, including the use of organisms like mealworms and bacteria for biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste can fragment into microplastics through biotic and abiotic processes, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="RECALCITRANT PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Recalcitrant plastics contribute significantly to plastic waste, posing environmental challenges due to their resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="PETROLEUM-BASED PLASTIC">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste primarily consists of petroleum-based plastics, which are resistant to biodegradation and contribute to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="ANTHROPOCENE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste is a key indicator of the Anthropocene epoch, reflecting human impact on the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="ENVIRONMENTAL REPORT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The environmental report reviews strategies for managing plastic waste and its environmental impacts.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="BIOLOGICAL DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biological degradation by microorganisms and insects offers a potential solution to plastic waste accumulation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="PLASTIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste contributes to plastic pollution, which poses significant environmental and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="BIODEGRADATION TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation technology uses microorganisms to degrade plastic waste like polystyrene in an environmentally friendly manner.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="MICROPLASTIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste is a major source of microplastic pollution, which poses threats to marine and terrestrial ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="HUMANS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Humans are indirectly exposed to plastic waste through contaminated food, leading to chronic exposure and potential health risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="MARINE CREATURES">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Marine creatures are harmed by plastic waste, which affects their health and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="ABOVEGROUND VEGETATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aboveground vegetation is negatively impacted by plastic waste buried in soils, affecting plant health and growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="CORAL REEFS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste is associated with disease and degradation of coral reefs, highlighting its impact on marine ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste is associated with disease and degradation of coral reefs, impacting marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="ENZYMATIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzymatic degradation is a potential solution for breaking down plastic waste, offering a sustainable approach to waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="MARINE DEBRIS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste is a major contributor to marine debris, which accumulates in marine environments and poses threats to marine life.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="MICROPLASTIC ACCUMULATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic waste contributes to the accumulation of microplastics in the environment</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="SUPER WORM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Super worms are studied for their potential in biodegrading plastic waste, similar to mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="ASPERGILLUS SPP.">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aspergillus spp. are fungi capable of biodegrading plastic waste, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTE" target="BIOTECHNOLOGIES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biotechnologies are being developed to utilize organisms like Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus for the remediation of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES" target="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Physiological responses in plastic-degrading larvae include changes in growth, hydration, and nutrient levels due to plastic exposure.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES" target="STUDENT'S T-TESTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Student's t-tests are used to determine the significance of differences in physiological responses between experimental groups.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR WEIGHT REDUCTION" target="GPC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">GPC analysis is used to determine the molecular weight reduction of polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) after ingestion by mealworms, indicating depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR WEIGHT REDUCTION" target="PE MPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) experience a reduction in molecular weight after ingestion by mealworms, indicating significant depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR WEIGHT REDUCTION" target="POLYMER DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polymer depolymerization leads to a reduction in the molecular weight of polyethylene (PE), as observed in the biodegradation process by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMAL DECOMPOSITION" target="NMR SPECTRA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">NMR spectra are used to analyze the chemical components of gaseous compounds produced during the thermal decomposition of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMAL DECOMPOSITION" target="DIOXINS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermal decomposition of plastics generates dioxins, which are toxic and harmful to the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMAL DECOMPOSITION" target="AROMATIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermal decomposition of plastics produces aromatic compounds, which are harmful byproducts.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL RISK">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nanoplastics pose biological and environmental risks, though they are rapidly removed in the gut of mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="ANTARCTIC KRILL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antarctic krill can fragment microplastics into nanoplastics through digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="MITOCHONDRIAL DAMAGE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nanoplastics can cause mitochondrial damage in human liver and lung cells, highlighting their toxicological risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics can cause mitochondrial damage in human liver and lung cells.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL IMPLICATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Improper plastic disposal leads to the formation of nanoplastics, which pose environmental and health risks</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="AQUATIC ENVIRONMENTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nanoplastics are a growing concern in aquatic environments due to their small size and potential to transport contaminants.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="CORONA PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Corona protein inhibits the absorption of nanoplastics by intestinal cells.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larvae do not accumulate nanoplastics in their frass, suggesting rapid biodegradation of small plastic particles.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="ROS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nanoplastics induce higher levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS) compared to microplastics, causing greater physiological stress.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="NEUROTOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nanoplastics are associated with neurotoxicity, damaging the nervous system of exposed organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="FRESHWATER BENTHIC CLAMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Freshwater benthic clams are used to study the genotoxic and intestinal damage effects of nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="CORBICULA FLUMINEA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Freshwater benthic clams are used to study the genotoxic and intestinal damage effects of nanoplastics compared to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES (ROS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are produced at high nanoplastic concentrations, damaging bacterial cell membranes and inhibiting bacterial growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="CELLULASES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cellulases are tested for their activity against bacteria capable of growing on nanoplastics as a carbon source, indicating their potential role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="ESTERASES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Esterases are tested for their activity against bacteria capable of growing on nanoplastics, suggesting their involvement in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="LIPASES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipases are tested for their activity against bacteria capable of growing on nanoplastics, indicating their potential role in breaking down plastic components.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="PROTEASES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Proteases are tested for their activity against bacteria capable of growing on nanoplastics, suggesting their involvement in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="LACCASES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Laccases are tested for their activity against bacteria capable of growing on nanoplastics, indicating their potential role in oxidizing plastic components.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="KOH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">KOH is used in the microbial digestion process to isolate nanoplastics without altering their chemical structure.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="CHLOROFORM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chloroform is used in the extraction process to separate nanoplastics from other materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="ISOPROPANOL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Isopropanol is used in the extraction process to separate nanoplastics from other materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="PARTICLE COUNT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Particle count analysis is used to determine the number and size of nanoplastics extracted from complex matrices.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scanning electron microscopy is used to examine the surface structure and characterization of nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="MICROBIAL DIGESTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microbial digestion using KOH is a method to isolate nanoplastics from biological cells without altering their structure.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="PARTICLE SIZE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Particle size analysis is used to determine the size and variation of nanoplastics extracted from complex matrices.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="ULTRASONIC DISMEMBRATOR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">An ultrasonic dismembrator is used to break up aggregates of nanoplastics for accurate size analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="PHILIPS-X LP30 SEM">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Philips-X LP30 SEM is used to examine the surface structure and characterization of nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="ATR-FTIR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">ATR-FTIR was used to analyze the chemical composition of nanoplastics, identifying changes in functional groups during degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="TERRESTRIAL SNAILS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nanoplastics can induce behavioral disorders in terrestrial snails, highlighting their harmful effects on terrestrial organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics can induce behavioral disorders in terrestrial snails, indicating their harmful effects on terrestrial organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="LASER LIGHT SCATTERING SIZER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A laser light scattering sizer is used to detect nanoplastics (NPs) in frass, with a detection limit of 10 nm.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="PY-GCMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pyrolyzer-gas chromatography mass spectrometry (Py-GCMS) is used to quantify nanoplastics in the solid residues of mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Toxicity studies assess the harmful effects of nanoplastics on organisms and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS" target="HUMAN LIVER AND LUNG CELLS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nanoplastics cause mitochondrial damage in human liver and lung cells, highlighting their toxicological impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SECURITY" target="INSECT PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Insect protein is being explored as a solution to enhance food security by providing a sustainable and nutrient-rich food source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SECURITY" target="SUSTAINABLE FOOD SOURCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sustainable food sources, such as edible insects, are being developed to address global food security challenges.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SECURITY" target="LOW-PERMEABILITY PLASTIC BAGS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Low-permeability plastic bags are used to enhance food security by preventing insect-related losses of stored food.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HUMAN HEALTH" target="PLASTICIZERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plasticizers in plastics can negatively affect human health by causing endocrine disorders and other diseases</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MNPS)" target="ANTARCTIC KRILL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antarctic krill can fragment ingested plastics into microplastics, similar to the process observed in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MNPS)" target="EARTHWORMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Earthworms contribute to the generation of microplastics (MNPs) by fragmenting ingested plastics in soil ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANTARCTIC KRILL" target="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antarctic krill are studied for their physiological stress responses to micro(nano)plastics, though they lack plastic-degrading abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill can digestively triturate polyethylene microplastics into nanoplastics, increasing their potential to cross biological barriers.&lt;SEP&gt;Antarctic krill can biofragment ingested microplastics into nanoplastics, although they lack efficient plastic-degrading abilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANTARCTIC KRILL" target="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antarctic krill are studied for their role in the generation and accumulation of nanoplastics (NPs) through biofragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EARTHWORMS" target="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)">
      <data key="d3">22.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Earthworms can biofragment ingested microplastics into nanoplastics, but they have poor plastic-degrading abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms experience higher mortality and weight reduction when exposed to high doses of microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms ingest and retain microplastics in terrestrial environments, leading to studies on their potential biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EARTHWORMS" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Earthworms (Eisenia fetida) are exposed to low-density polyethylene microplastics in soils, affecting their defense responses.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EARTHWORMS" target="POLYLACTIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Earthworms can degrade polylactic acid bioplastics, showing slow excretion rates after ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms can degrade polylactic acid, a bioplastic, contributing to its breakdown in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EARTHWORMS" target="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene microplastics can be toxic to earthworms, but soil particles can form a protective layer that reduces this toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DARKLING BEETLE" target="SUPERWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms (Zophobas morio) belong to the darkling beetle family and are capable of consuming and degrading polystyrene (PS).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e</data>
    </edge>
