    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="GUT MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microbes in superworms play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, aiding in the breakdown process.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in superworms play a key role in the biodegradation of polystyrene and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms rely on gut microbes to biodegrade polypropylene through depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="BRAN-FED CONTROL GROUP">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The bran-fed control group serves as a baseline for superworms, showing higher survival rates compared to plastic-fed groups.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="ACCUMULATIVE NET WEIGHT CHANGES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Accumulative net weight changes are measured to assess the growth of superworms fed plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="CUMULATIVE FOAM CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cumulative foam consumption is measured to evaluate the amount of plastic consumed by superworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="GUT BACTERIAL MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut bacterial microbiome of superworms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="POLYETHYLENE FOAM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene foam is consumed and biodegraded by superworms, leading to molecular weight reduction and depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="POLYSTYRENE FOAM">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene foam is consumed and biodegraded by superworms, leading to molecular weight reduction and depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms consume polystyrene foam at a higher rate than yellow mealworms, indicating their efficiency in biodegrading PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms have been studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene foam, with their gut microbes playing a key role in the process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="GUT BACTERIAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut bacterial community of superworms is significantly altered by a plastic diet, particularly polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="PLASTIC-FED GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic-fed groups were used to study the effects of polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE) diets on the gut microbiome of superworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="BRAN-FED SAMPLES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bran-fed samples served as control groups to compare the gut microbiome of superworms with those fed plastic diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="PLASTIC CONSUMPTION RATES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic consumption rates were higher in superworms compared to yellow mealworms, indicating species-specific differences in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="WASTE MANAGEMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms contribute to waste management by biodegrading low-density polyethylene and polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
