    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="ZERO-WASTE CIRCULAR ECONOMY">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms are studied for their potential role in a zero-waste circular economy by consuming polystyrene and producing frass for plant growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms play a role in a zero-waste circular economy by degrading polystyrene (PS) and providing sustainable solutions.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="TABLE SUCROSE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Table sucrose increases the consumption of polystyrene (PS) by superworms, improving biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="DELAYED PUPATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Delayed pupation in superworms increases their consumption of polystyrene (PS), supporting biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
