    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="GC-MS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">GC-MS analysis is used to detect chemicals in the frass of PS-fed superworms, providing evidence of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms can biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam, with mass losses of 0.30 and 0.59 mg/larva/d.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms can biodegrade low-density polyethylene, demonstrating their potential in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms can biodegrade low-density polyethylene, a type of plastic similar to polyethylene terephthalate, contributing to plastic waste reduction.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORMS" target="YANG ET AL.">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yang et al. showed that superworms (Zophobas atratus) mineralize polystyrene at higher rates.</data>
