    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Priestia megaterium S1, isolated from Tenebrio molitor, is capable of degrading Styrofoam.&lt;SEP&gt;Priestia megaterium S1, isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, is capable of degrading Styrofoam, demonstrating its role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrofoam is a major environmental pollutant, particularly in water reservoirs, posing risks to aquatic life and ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="ZOPHOBAS MORIO">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zophobas morio can consume and degrade Styrofoam, showing no negative health impacts, making them a potential solution for environmental pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas morio larvae are being tested for their ability to consume and degrade Styrofoam, similar to Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume and biodegrade Styrofoam, leading to changes in their nutrient composition and contributing to environmental cleanup.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="CONSUMER STUDY">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The consumer study assessed public perceptions of consuming insects fed with Styrofoam, revealing aversion to such practices.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="FECULA">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fecula contains residual Styrofoam fragments and intermediates egested by mealworms, where further degradation can occur.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula is the waste material egested by mealworms after consuming and biodegrading Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="LIPID BIOMASS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipid biomass is formed from the portion of ingested Styrofoam that is incorporated into lipids during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="Z. MORIO">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Z. morio can consume and potentially biodegrade Styrofoam, making it a candidate for addressing environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="T. MOLITOR">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor can consume and potentially biodegrade Styrofoam, contributing to environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of consuming and degrading Styrofoam, aiding in the reduction of environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scientific research is conducted to assess the safety and effects of insects consuming Styrofoam, particularly for potential use in human or animal feeding.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="FOOD SAFETY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Food safety concerns are addressed through research to ensure that insects fed on Styrofoam are safe for human consumption.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="INSECTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Insects, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas morio, can consume and degrade Styrofoam, offering a potential solution for environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="ENTOMOPHAGY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Entomophagy is being explored as a sustainable food source, including insects fed on Styrofoam, pending scientific confirmation of safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM" target="SOLID-STATE 13C NMR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Solid-state 13C NMR is used to analyze the chemical composition of Styrofoam and its degradation products in superworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64</data>
    </edge>
