    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="MYCOTOXINS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mycotoxins were analyzed in TMlp to ensure its safety as a feed material for livestock.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fb1c38c1c6dc6fea84d8f51b9f706cd2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="NOVEL FOOD SOURCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Novel food sources, such as mealworms, undergo rigorous food safety evaluations before being approved for consumption.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="EDIBLE INSECTS">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Edible insects, such as Tenebrio molitor, are being assessed for their safety and nutritional value as an alternative protein source.&lt;SEP&gt;Edible insects, such as mealworms, are studied for their safety and nutritional value as alternative protein sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Food safety studies evaluate the suitability of edible insects, such as Tenebrio molitor, as alternative protein sources.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="NUTRITIONAL VALUE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The nutritional value of polystyrene-fed insects is assessed to determine their safety and suitability as a food source.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="CONSUMER SURVEY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A consumer survey is conducted to evaluate public willingness to consume insects fed with polystyrene, addressing food safety concerns.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="FREEZE-DRIED POWDER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Freeze-dried powder of Tenebrio molitor larvae is evaluated for safety in toxicological studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="EUROPEAN FOOD SAFETY AUTHORITY (EFSA)">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The EFSA provides scientific opinions on food safety, including the safety of alternative protein sources and biodegradation byproducts.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="POLYSTYRENE OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene oligomers are relevant in food safety studies as they can be detected in food simulants and may pose health risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="EUROPEAN COMMISSION REGULATION (EU) NO 10/2011">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">European Commission Regulation (EU) No 10/2011 establishes safety standards for materials in contact with food, ensuring food safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="PLASTICIZER ACCUMULATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plasticizer accumulation in the food chain is a concern that requires further research to ensure food safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="WORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Worms contribute to food safety by producing frass that supports food crop growth and by serving as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="YELLOW MEALWORM">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms have been deemed safe for human consumption by the EU Food Safety Agency, highlighting their role in food safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polymers used in food packaging can release harmful chemicals, raising concerns about food safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="CHEMICAL MIGRATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chemical migration from plastics into food raises significant food safety concerns.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="GRASSHOPPERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Grasshoppers are studied for their microbial communities to ensure their safety as a food source for human consumption</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="MARINE MICROPLASTIC DEBRIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Marine microplastic debris poses risks to food security and safety due to its presence in marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="JOINT RESEARCH CENTRE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Joint Research Centre conducts research and develops standards related to food safety and packaging materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="MEALWORM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Food safety concerns arise when mealworms are bred on synthetic polymer diets, as the resulting products must be tested for potentially harmful substances</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="INTESTINAL EPITHELIAL BARRIER">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The intestinal epithelial barrier is crucial for maintaining food safety by distinguishing between beneficial and harmful microbes.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="PIERIS RAPAE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pieris rapae is studied for its feeding behavior and growth, which can provide insights into the effects of environmental factors on food safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD SAFETY" target="HUMANS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Food safety concerns arise from the indirect consumption of plastics by humans through contaminated food sources.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="HYDROPHOBICITY PERCENTAGE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrophobicity percentage is used to evaluate the ability of Priestia megaterium S1 to adhere to and degrade hydrophobic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="CLEAR ZONE ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The clear zone assay is used to assess the biodegradation potential of Priestia megaterium S1 on plastic-containing media.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="TURBIDITY MEASUREMENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Turbidity measurement is used to monitor the growth and activity of Priestia megaterium S1 during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="16S RRNA CHARACTERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">16S rRNA characterization is used to identify and classify Priestia megaterium S1 as a plastic-degrading microbe.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="HDPE">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HDPE is also degraded by Priestia megaterium S1, demonstrating its broad plastic-degrading capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Priestia megaterium S1 degrades HDPE, leading to weight loss and surface erosion, demonstrating its biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="BIOLOGICAL OXYGEN DEMAND (BOD)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">BOD measurements indicate the extent of oxygen consumption during the biodegradation of plastics by Priestia megaterium S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="CO2 EVOLUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">CO2 evolution is used to quantify the mineralization of plastics into carbon dioxide by Priestia megaterium S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR analysis confirms chemical changes on plastic surfaces due to biodegradation by Priestia megaterium S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPE (SEM)">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SEM imaging provides visual evidence of physical changes on plastic beads caused by Priestia megaterium S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1" target="TRICARBOXYLIC ACID (TCA) CYCLE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The TCA cycle is involved in the metabolic activities of Priestia megaterium S1 during plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BARLEY BRAN" target="MEALWORMS (TENEBRIO MOLITOR)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Barley bran is used as a feeding supplement to enhance the growth and polystyrene (PS) consumption of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLEAR ZONE ASSAY" target="COOMASSIE BLUE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Coomassie blue is used in the clear zone assay to detect zones of clearance, indicating plastic degradation by gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLEAR ZONE ASSAY" target="ACETONE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acetone is used in the clear zone assay for decolorization after applying Coomassie blue to detect plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBIDITY MEASUREMENT" target="MEALWORM GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Turbidity measurement assesses the utilization of plastics as a carbon source by mealworm gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="MEALWORM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are studied for their ability to consume and degrade HDPE, contributing to plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="BACTERIAL ISOLATES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacterial isolates from mealworm guts are tested for their ability to degrade HDPE, contributing to plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="MEALWORM GUT BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm gut bacteria are capable of biodegrading HDPE, resulting in weight reduction and surface alterations.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm gut bacteria, especially the S1 isolate, can degrade HDPE, demonstrating their potential in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="PM S1">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HDPE is degraded by PM S1 bacteria, which utilize it as a carbon source.&lt;SEP&gt;PM S1 colonized HDPE beads, contributing to their degradation&lt;SEP&gt;PM S1 degrades HDPE, leading to weight loss and surface erosion, demonstrating its biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="PM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PM (microbial isolates) colonized and degraded HDPE beads, contributing to their biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="ELEMENTAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Elemental analysis is used to measure the degradation of HDPE by PM S1 bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="CARBON DIOXIDE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HDPE degradation by PM S1 results in carbon dioxide production, confirming mineralization.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="FTIR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze the degradation of HDPE beads by PM S1, identifying changes in functional groups.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="SEM ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SEM analysis confirms structural changes on the surface of HDPE beads after treatment with PM S1, indicating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="CARBON AND HYDROGEN ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Elemental analysis of carbon and hydrogen shows the percentage loss in HDPE beads after degradation by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="BIOLOGICAL OXYGEN DEMAND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biological Oxygen Demand studies support the significant weight loss of HDPE beads after biodegradation by PM S1.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="PELW2042">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PELW2042, a bacterial strain isolated from mealworms, has the ability to degrade HDPE</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="BACILLUS SP. PELW2042">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. PELW2042 degrades HDPE by forming biofilms and creating pits on its surface, making it more hydrophilic and easier to colonize.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="STRAIN PELW2042">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Strain PELW2042 degrades HDPE, leading to changes in its chemical structure and molecular weight</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="CARBONYL GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The formation of carbonyl groups on HDPE indicates the initial steps of its biodegradation by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="ETHER GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The formation of ether groups on HDPE indicates structural changes during its biodegradation by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DECREASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The molecular weight of HDPE decreases significantly during its biodegradation by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Infrared spectroscopy is used to detect functional group changes in HDPE during biodegradation by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="XRD">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">XRD is used to measure crystallinity changes in HDPE during biodegradation by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="DSC">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DSC is used to analyze melting point changes in HDPE during biodegradation by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="WEIGHT LOSS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Weight loss is a key indicator of HDPE biodegradation, reflecting the consumption of polyethylene by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="HYDROXYLATING ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydroxylating enzymes initiate the hydroxylation of polyethylene during biodegradation by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="ALKANOIC ACIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Alkanoic acids are produced as a result of the biodegradation of HDPE by strain PELW2042</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="CARBONYL INDEX">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The carbonyl index measures the oxidation level of residual HDPE polymers in the frass of Tenebrio larvae, indicating biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="TENEBRIO LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio larvae can degrade HDPE, with their gut microbiota enhancing carbon and nitrogen metabolism to facilitate this process.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio larvae experience higher degrees of physiological and metabolic impairments when exposed to HDPE, a less biodegradable plastic polymer.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSOXIDANS M9">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is known to degrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE), showcasing its ability to break down various xenobiotics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="T. MOLITOR">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae can biodegrade HDPE microplastics, as demonstrated by changes in molecular weight and FTIR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae can degrade HDPE, a high molecular weight polyethylene microplastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="T. OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. obscurus larvae can biodegrade HDPE microplastics, as demonstrated by changes in molecular weight and FTIR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus larvae can degrade HDPE, a high molecular weight polyethylene microplastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="CRYSTALLINITY DEGREE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The high crystallinity degree of HDPE makes it more resistant to biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="A. GRISELLA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Achroia grisella larvae biodegrade HDPE, demonstrating their ability to break down high-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="ULOMA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Uloma larvae biodegrade HDPE, contributing to the degradation of high-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="LESSER WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HDPE is biodegraded by lesser waxworms through chemical modifications in their gut, contributing to plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesser waxworms biodegrade HDPE through chemical modifications in their gut, contributing to plastic waste reduction.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDPE" target="ULOMA LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HDPE is also biodegraded by Uloma larvae, demonstrating their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae of the genus Uloma are involved in the biodegradation of HDPE, similar to lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)" target="ATR METHOD">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The ATR method is used in Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy to analyze the functional groups of residual polymers in the frass of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)" target="EGESTED FRASS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR is used to analyze egested frass to confirm the biodegradation of PE and WC by lesser waxworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)" target="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to analyze changes in the functional groups of microplastics (MPs) after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPE (SEM)" target="LARVAL FRASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larval frass is analyzed using SEM to provide detailed images of residual plastics and degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPE (SEM)" target="PS SHEETS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS sheets are analyzed using a scanning electron microscope to observe microbial growth and degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPE (SEM)" target="POLYSTYRENE FILM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A scanning electron microscope (SEM) is used to observe surface changes in polystyrene films caused by bacterial degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TRICARBOXYLIC ACID (TCA) CYCLE" target="PHENYLACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phenylacetic acid is introduced into the TCA cycle, where it is further degraded to CO2 and H2O.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="1,2 DICHLOROBENZENE (DCB)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DCB is used for GPC analysis of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="DICHLOROMETHANE (DCM)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DCM is used for the extraction of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="NESIOBACTER EXALBESCENCE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nesiobacter exalbescence degrades LDPE, contributing to the reduction of plastic waste</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="BACTERIAL CONSORTIA">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Specific bacterial consortia can degrade low density polyethylene, offering a potential solution for plastic waste</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="PSEUDOMONAS SPECIES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas species are studied for their ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), contributing to environmental remediation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="PHANEROCHAETE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phanerochaete fungi are capable of degrading polyethylene, highlighting their role in biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="STREPTOMYCES SPECIES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Streptomyces species can degrade polyethylene, contributing to the study of plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="SIENOTROPHOMONAS PAVANII">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sienotrophomonas pavanii is used to biodegrade modified low-density polyethylene (LDPE), demonstrating its potential in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="BACILLUS AMYLOLIQUEFACIENS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus amyloliquefaciens can deteriorate pretreated linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), contributing to biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="ZALERION MARITIMUM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zalerion maritimum, a marine fungus, can biodegrade polyethylene microplastics, aiding in environmental cleanup.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="ACINETOBACTER BAUMANNII">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter baumannii has potential for biodegrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE), contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR (TM)" target="LIGNOCELLULOSE MATRIX">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae can break down the lignocellulose matrix in plastic waste, aiding in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR (TM)" target="CHITIN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor larvae produce chitin as a byproduct of their biomass, which has commercial applications.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR (TM)" target="CHITOSAN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chitosan, derived from chitin, is a valuable product obtained from Tenebrio molitor biomass.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR (TM)" target="CIRCULAR ECONOMY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The circular economy model is supported by the utilization of Tenebrio molitor biomass for biodegradation and resource recovery.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HONEYBEES" target="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Honeybees have been studied for their potential role in biodegrading certain types of plastics, though their capacity is still under investigation.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHITIN" target="SUPERWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworm frass contains Chitin, which supports plant rooting.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHITIN" target="CHITOSAN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chitin is the raw material for chitosan production, which is extracted from insect exoskeletons like those of Zophobas morio.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHITIN" target="ZOPHOBAS MORIO">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zophobas morio is a source of chitin, which is used to produce chitosan for various applications.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CIRCULAR ECONOMY" target="FOOD WASTE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Food waste is valorized in the circular economy through insect production and processing, reducing environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CIRCULAR ECONOMY" target="PLASTIC VALORIZATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The circular economy concept includes plastic valorization, using organisms like mealworms to degrade and repurpose plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CIRCULAR ECONOMY" target="ZERO WASTE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The circular economy and Zero Waste concepts are aligned in their goals of minimizing waste and promoting recycling.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CIRCULAR ECONOMY" target="CONSTRUCTION AND DEMOLITION WASTE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Construction and demolition waste is a key focus of the circular economy, which aims to reduce waste and promote recycling.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="SF">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are fed Styrofoam (SF) to study their ability to degrade this type of expanded polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="ETOH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ethanol (EtOH) is used to sterilize mealworm larvae before isolating their gut bacteria for biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="MEALWORM PUPAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm pupae are collected during experiments to monitor the developmental stages and survival rates of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="DEAD LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dead larvae are removed during experiments to prevent cannibalism and ensure accurate survival rate measurements.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="SURVIVAL RATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Survival rate (SR) is used to track the survival of mealworms over time when fed with plastics and barley bran.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms ingest microplastics, which can cause weight loss and higher mortality, but their resistance to these effects increases with age.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="RECALCITRANT MICROPLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are used as degradation reactors to manage recalcitrant microplastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="HORMONAL SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nutrient deficiencies caused by microplastic ingestion can disrupt the hormonal system of mealworms, affecting molting and pupation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="DYNAMIC ENERGY BUDGET MODEL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The dynamic energy budget model explains how mealworms allocate energy from food for growth, maintenance, and reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="MPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can ingest and depolymerize MPS, with effects varying based on particle size and developmental stage.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="POULTRY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Poultry can be fed mealworms, which may contain microplastics, raising concerns about trophic transfer and food safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="FISH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fish can be fed mealworms, potentially leading to the ingestion of microplastics and associated pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="OXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms contribute to the oxidation of functional groups in microplastics during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="MPS DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can degrade MPS, particularly at older larval stages with bran supplementation, contributing to microplastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="GENTAMICIN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gentamicin is used to suppress the gut microbiome of mealworms, allowing researchers to isolate the mealworm's role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="EXPANDED PS FOAM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Expanded PS foam is used as a feed for mealworms to study their ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="RAINBOW MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rainbow Mealworms supplied the mealworms used in the study, which are known for their ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The fatty acid profile of mealworms is influenced by their diet, with synthetic polymers like polystyrene affecting the quality of fatty acids</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Protein content in mealworms is influenced by their diet, with synthetic polymers like polystyrene affecting protein synthesis</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="BIODIESEL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms grown on synthetic polymer diets can be used to produce biodiesel</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="CENTRAL CARBON METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae demonstrate metabolic activity in their central carbon metabolism when exposed to PLA and expanded foam plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae show potential for plastic biodegradation, particularly with PLA and expanded foam plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="PLASTIC CARBON ASSIMILATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae assimilate plastic carbon into their biomass, contributing to plastic biodegradation and protein production.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="WASTE MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae are employed in waste management systems to convert organic waste, including plastics, into valuable protein.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="FIT-FOR-PURPOSE INSECT BIOTRANSFORMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm larvae are considered fit-for-purpose insect biotransformers for certain plastic types.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="PLASTIC">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are extensively studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics and are proposed as a model for micro- and nanoplastic toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="ORGANIC FERTILIZER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The frass of Mealworms has potential as an organic fertilizer, contributing to sustainable agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="LIVESTOCK FEED">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are recognized as a sustainable protein source for livestock feed.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="FRESH CARROT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are provided with fresh carrot as a food source in the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="SWEET POTATO">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are provided with sweet potato as a food source in the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms exhibit metabolic changes and potential biodegradation when exposed to PET, indicating a positive interaction with this plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="EXPANDED POLYETHYLENE (EXP PE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms interact with Exp PE, showing varying degrees of weight loss and metabolic changes, indicating potential biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="METABOLOMIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Metabolomic analysis reveals the metabolic pathways and cofactor accumulation in mealworms when exposed to different plastic substrates.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM" target="BIT-26">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">BIT-26 is associated with mealworms, indicating a potential role in biodegradation or other biological processes involving mealworms.</data>
