    <edge source="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="SOURCE A MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Source A mealworms consumed low-density polyethylene (LDPE), as evidenced by holes and scratches in the plastic bags over 10-, 20-, and 30-day intervals.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="INTESTINAL MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The intestinal microbiome of mealworms is involved in the digestion of LDPE, which has more branching and is more bioresistant.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="CARBON-CARBON BONDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDPE has more carbon-carbon bonds and more branching, making it more bioresistant and harder for mealworms to digest.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="MASS BALANCE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mass balance analysis is used to quantify the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) by measuring the conversion of ingested LDPE into CO2 and other byproducts.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The molecular weight of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) decreases during biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio obscurus and Tenebrio molitor, indicating depolymerization.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </edge>
