      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax Moth larvae interact with certain plastics, indicating a role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION" target="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic-degrading bacteria in the gut of mealworms play a key role in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION" target="CO-DIET">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A co-diet of bran and plastics enhances the plastic biodegradation efficiency of mealworms by shaping their gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION" target="INDIVIDUAL VARIATIONS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Individual variations among mealworms suggest that plastic biodegradation efficiency may depend on unique gut microbiome compositions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION" target="FUNGAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The fungal community in the gut of yellow mealworms, including genera like Rhodotorula and Cryptococcus, is essential for the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION" target="BACTERIAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The bacterial community in the gut of yellow mealworms works alongside fungi to biodegrade plastics, contributing to the overall process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION" target="ATR-FT-IR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">ATR-FT-IR is used to characterize the biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae, providing insights into the chemical changes during degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORM" target="HIGHLY EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Highly expanded polystyrene is fed to superworms to study its degradation by their gut bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORM" target="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms show higher plastic biodegradation efficiency, particularly for polystyrene, compared to yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORM" target="YELLOW MEALWORM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworms and yellow mealworms show different plastic ingestion preferences and efficiencies, associated with distinct gut microbiome changes.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="HIGHLY EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Highly expanded polystyrene is fed to yellow mealworms to study its degradation by their gut bacteria and fungi.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="EU FOOD SAFETY AGENCY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The EU Food Safety Agency has validated yellow mealworms as safe for human consumption, ensuring their role in food safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="NUTRITIONAL VALUE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The degradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworm larvae enhances their nutritional value, particularly in crude protein and phosphorus content</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="ORGANIC WASTE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms can transform organic waste into valuable nutrients like proteins and fats</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="CRUDE PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The degradation of polystyrene enhances the crude protein content in yellow mealworm larvae</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="PHOSPHORUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The degradation of polystyrene increases the phosphorus content in yellow mealworm larvae</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms are capable of degrading expanded polystyrene (EPS), contributing to the reduction of plastic waste</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC) FILM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms can degrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC) film, demonstrating their potential in addressing plastic pollution</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="PLASTIC DEGRADATION EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms exhibit high plastic degradation efficiency, breaking down plastics such as PE, PS, and PVC into smaller molecules or CO2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="WHITE POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms help mitigate white pollution by degrading non-biodegradable plastics, offering a sustainable solution</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="NUTRITIONAL COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The nutritional composition of yellow mealworms is analyzed to assess the effects of plastic consumption on their suitability as a food source</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="SUSTAINABLE PLASTIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms contribute to sustainable plastic degradation by breaking down plastics in an environmentally friendly manner</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="FEED MOISTURE CONTENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Feed moisture content is maintained at 18% to ensure optimal conditions for yellow mealworm growth and plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="KJELDAHL METHOD">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Kjeldahl method is used to determine the protein content in yellow mealworm samples, providing insights into their nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="COLORIMETRIC METHOD">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The colorimetric method is used to measure total phosphorus content in yellow mealworm samples, contributing to nutritional analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="FLAME PHOTOMETRY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Flame photometry is used to measure total potassium content in yellow mealworm samples, aiding in nutritional assessment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE (ANOVA)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">ANOVA is used to statistically analyze the differences in plastic degradation rates among treatments involving yellow mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="VACUUM CRYODESICCATION METHOD">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The vacuum cryodesiccation method is used to measure moisture content in yellow mealworm samples, ensuring accurate moisture calculations.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="EFSA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The EFSA has validated yellow mealworm as a safe and sustainable alternative protein source for human consumption.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="WHEAT STRAW">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms are fed wheat straw to produce insect biomass and biochar.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="BIOCHAR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The frass of yellow mealworms fed with wheat straw is used to generate biochar for environmental applications.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION EFFICIENCY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworms exhibit lower plastic biodegradation efficiency compared to superworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="BIOREGENERATIVE LIFE SUPPORT SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworm (Tenebrio molitor) is studied for its potential as a protein source in bioregenerative life support systems for space missions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="INSECTS AND PACKAGING">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Insects, such as the yellow mealworm, are studied for their ability to interact with and degrade packaging materials, including plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="YELLOW MEALWORM" target="BENZENE RING DESTRUCTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yellow mealworm's gut microbes are capable of destroying the benzene ring structure of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </edge>
