      <data key="d4">Waste plastics are targeted for microbial degradation, with bacterial strains like PELW2042 offering potential solutions for their breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION" target="PE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microbial degradation is a key process in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION" target="STYRENE OLIGOMER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oligomer is subject to microbial degradation, contributing to the breakdown of styrene-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION" target="1,3-DIPHENYLBUTANE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">1,3-Diphenylbutane is used in studies to understand microbial degradation processes, particularly in plastics.</data>
