    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION" target="STYRENE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene can be degraded by microorganisms, with potential applications in biotechnology, as discussed by Mooney et al. (2006).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION" target="STARCH-BASED POLYPROPYLENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Starch-based polypropylene has been studied for its microbial degradation potential, offering insights into biodegradable plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIAL DEGRADATION" target="STARCH BASED POLYPROPYLENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microbial degradation is studied as a method to break down starch-based polypropylene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE &amp; TECHNOLOGY" target="POLYETHYLENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmental Science &amp; Technology published evidence of polyethylene biodegradation by bacterial strains from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental Science &amp; Technology publishes research on the biodegradation of polyethylene by organisms like mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04</data>
    </edge>
