      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WAXWORMS" target="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE YT1">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter asburiae YT1, a bacterial strain isolated from waxworms, plays a role in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE BIODEGRADATION" target="CATERPILLARS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Caterpillars are studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FATTY ACID DEGRADATION PATHWAY" target="KEGG MAPPING">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">KEGG mapping was used to analyze the fatty acid degradation pathway in mealworms, highlighting its importance in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="XENOBIOTICS" target="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSOXIDANS M9">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 can degrade various xenobiotics, including plastics and hydrocarbons, making it a versatile organism for bioremediation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TRANSCRIPTOME ANALYSIS" target="PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Transcriptome analysis reveals the upregulation of peroxidase during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="TENEBRIO MOLITOR LARVAE POWDER (TMLP)">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TMlp is derived from Tenebrio molitor larvae fed with expanded polystyrene, making it a potential solution for both environmental pollution and alternative protein sources.&lt;SEP&gt;TMlp is derived from Tenebrio molitor larvae that have been fed expanded polystyrene, making it a potential solution for environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="TMLP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TMlp is derived from Tenebrio molitor larvae that have been fed expanded polystyrene (EPS), making it a potential solution for environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="ENVIRONMENTAL HORMONES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Incineration of EPS releases environmental hormones, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="DIOXIN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Incineration of EPS produces dioxin, a toxic substance that contributes to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="L. TAIWANENSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">L. taiwanensis shows varying responses to EPS feeding, indicating differences in EPS degradation abilities at the species level.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="L. GARVIEAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">L. garvieae exhibits opposite effects compared to L. taiwanensis when exposed to EPS feeding, suggesting species-specific degradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="PLASTIC WASTES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a significant contributor to plastic wastes, particularly in packaging and insulation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="BRICKWORK DEBRIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a major contaminant in brickwork debris, making it difficult to recycle.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="SUSTAINABLE DEMOLITION PROCESS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The sustainable demolition process aims to address the issue of expanded polystyrene (EPS) contamination in brickwork debris.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="THERMAL INSULATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermal insulation often consists of expanded polystyrene (EPS), which is difficult to separate and recycle.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="MANUAL PROCESSING">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Manual processing is a method for removing expanded polystyrene (EPS) from brickwork debris, but it is labor-intensive and costly.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="LEACHATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">EPS breaks down into leachate, a toxic byproduct that contaminates land and water resources.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="FURANS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Furans are harmful byproducts generated during the disposal of EPS, contributing to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="DIOXINS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dioxins are toxic chemicals released during the degradation of EPS, posing environmental and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="POLYCHLORINATED BIPHENYLS (PCBS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PCBs are hazardous byproducts generated from the disposal of EPS, contributing to environmental contamination.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)" target="CARBON MASS BALANCE">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Carbon mass balance is used to estimate the biodegradation rate of EPS by mealworms, suggesting 48.2% of consumed EPS is biodegraded&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon mass balance is used to track the distribution of carbon from consumed EPS into biomass, carbon dioxide, and fecula during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
