      <data key="d4">Zophobas morio larvae are studied for their potential to produce anticancer peptides, highlighting their medicinal value.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ZOPHOBAS MORIO" target="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas aeruginosa, isolated from the gut of Zophobas morio, plays a key role in the biodegradation of plastics like PS and PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas morio harbors Pseudomonas aeruginosa in its gut, which contributes to the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ZOPHOBAS MORIO" target="FOOD CHEMISTRY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Food chemistry studies the impact of polystyrene consumption on the nutritional value and safety of Zophobas morio.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ZOPHOBAS MORIO" target="NUTRITIONAL COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zophobas morio is analyzed for its nutritional composition as part of edible Coleoptera studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ZOPHOBAS MORIO" target="PLASTIC">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zophobas morio has been studied for its ability to degrade plastic materials, contributing to biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXIGUOBACTERIUM SP. STRAIN YT2" target="YANG ET AL. [28]">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yang et al. [28] isolated Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 from mealworms, showing its role in polystyrene (PS) biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NUTRITIONAL VALUE" target="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Synthetic polymers have low nutritional value, which affects the metabolism and mass of mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NUTRITIONAL VALUE" target="METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The metabolism of mealworms is affected by the low nutritional value of synthetic polymers, leading to mass loss</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYTOTOXICITY" target="Z. MORIO">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Extracts from superworms fed polystyrene were tested for cytotoxicity, showing no significant cytotoxic effects on normal cell lines.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM UTILIZATION" target="CONSUMER SURVEY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A consumer survey assessed public willingness to eat insects fed with Styrofoam, linking environmental solutions to food safety.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENTOMOPHAGY" target="INSECT FARMING">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Entomophagy is supported by insect farming, which provides a sustainable source of protein and other nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENTOMOPHAGY" target="SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scientific research is conducted to evaluate the safety and feasibility of entomophagy, particularly for insects fed on Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WAX MOTH" target="CENTRAL CARBON METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax Moth larvae exhibit changes in their central carbon metabolism, particularly in Vitamin B6 metabolism, when exposed to certain plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WAX MOTH" target="VITAMIN B6 METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax Moth larvae exhibit elevated Vitamin B6 metabolism when exposed to certain plastics, indicating a specific metabolic response.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WAX MOTH" target="PLASTIC CARBON ASSIMILATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax Moth larvae incorporate plastic carbon into their biomass, indicating a role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WAX MOTH" target="WASTE MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax Moth larvae are studied for their potential role in waste management systems to process plastic-contaminated waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WAX MOTH" target="FIT-FOR-PURPOSE INSECT BIOTRANSFORMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax Moth larvae are studied as fit-for-purpose insect biotransformers for specific plastic substrates.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORM" target="POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The lesser waxworm (Achroia grisella) is capable of efficiently biodegrading polyethylene (HDPE) waste, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORM" target="KUNDUNGAL ET AL.">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Kundungal et al. demonstrated the ability of lesser waxworm larvae to degrade high-density polyethylene under various conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORM" target="HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is degraded by lesser waxworm larvae under various conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORM" target="WAX COMB">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax comb enhances the biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworms, improving their ability to degrade plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORM" target="EGESTED FRASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The egested frass of lesser waxworms shows evidence of polyethylene biodegradation, indicating the breakdown of plastic into new functional groups.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORM" target="LIFE CYCLE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The lesser waxworm can complete its life cycle, including larvae, pupae, moth, and egg stages, while feeding on polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORM" target="SECOND GENERATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The second generation of lesser waxworms can degrade polyethylene as efficiently as the first generation, demonstrating the sustainability of this biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORM" target="PE-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The lesser waxworm's ability to degrade polyethylene may be due to the presence of PE-degrading bacteria in its digestive tract.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE YT1" target="DARKLING BEETLES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is a plastic-degrading bacterial strain isolated from darkling beetles, contributing to the biodegradation of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE YT1" target="YANG ET AL. [31]">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yang et al. [31] isolated Enterobacter asburiae YT1 from Plodia interpunctella larvae, demonstrating its ability to degrade polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE YT1" target="PE">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is capable of degrading higher molecular weight polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter asburiae YT1 is capable of degrading higher molecular weight polyethylene, though the process is slow.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEXANE" target="FAT CONTENT ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hexane is used in the Soxhlet extraction method to determine the fat content of insects fed with Styrofoam or wheat bran.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEXANE" target="GC-MS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hexane is used to redissolve residual polymers for GC-MS analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HECA10 CELLS" target="NEUTRAL RED ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Neutral Red assay is used to evaluate the viability of HECa10 cells exposed to insect homogenates.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STREPTOMYCIN" target="CELL CULTURE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Streptomycin is added to cell culture media to prevent bacterial contamination during in vitro experiments.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIMETHYL SULFOXIDE (DMSO)" target="FLUOSTAR OMEGA READER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DMSO is used to dissolve formazan crystals, and the absorbance is measured using the FLUOstar Omega reader.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIMETHYL SULFOXIDE (DMSO)" target="FORMAZAN CRYSTALS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Formazan crystals are dissolved using DMSO for absorbance measurement in cell viability assays.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NEUTRAL RED" target="FLUOSTAR OMEGA READER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Neutral Red is used in cell viability assays, and its absorbance is measured using the FLUOstar Omega reader.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SULFORHODAMINE B (SRB)" target="FLUOSTAR OMEGA READER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sulforhodamine B is used in cell proliferation assays, and its fluorescence is measured using the FLUOstar Omega reader.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SULFORHODAMINE B (SRB)" target="TRICHLOROACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Trichloroacetic acid is used to fix cells in the SRB assay for measuring cell proliferation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LACTATE DEHYDROGENASE (LDH)" target="FLUOSTAR OMEGA READER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LDH activity is measured using the FLUOstar Omega reader to assess cytotoxicity.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MUSE® CELL ANALYZER" target="GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gastrointestinal tract of insects is analyzed using the Muse® Cell Analyzer to determine cell viability and oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MUSE® CELL ANALYZER" target="ANNEXIN V REAGENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Annexin V reagent is used with the Muse® Cell Analyzer to detect apoptotic cells.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MUSE® CELL ANALYZER" target="MUSE® COUNT AND VIABILITY KIT">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Muse® Count and Viability Kit is used with the Muse® Cell Analyzer to determine cell viability and total cell count.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MUSE® CELL ANALYZER" target="MUSE® OXIDATIVE STRESS KIT">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Muse® Oxidative Stress Kit is used with the Muse® Cell Analyzer to measure ROS levels in cells.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT" target="BALL HOMOGENIZER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A ball homogenizer is used to prepare gastrointestinal tract tissue for oxidative stress and cell viability analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT" target="PBS BUFFER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PBS buffer is used to prepare and homogenize gastrointestinal tract tissue for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MUSE® OXIDATIVE STRESS KIT" target="SUPEROXIDE RADICALS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superoxide radicals are measured using the Muse® Oxidative Stress Kit to assess oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NAD+" target="NADH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">NAD+ is reduced to NADH during the LDH assay, indicating cell damage.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NADH" target="TETRAZOLIUM SALT (INT)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">NADH reduces tetrazolium salt (INT) to red formazan in the LDH assay.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TETRAZOLIUM SALT (INT)" target="RED FORMAZAN">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tetrazolium salt (INT) is reduced to red formazan in the LDH assay, indicating cell damage.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR)" target="NUTRIENT COMPOSITION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms fed with Styrofoam exhibit higher protein and ash content but reduced fat and carbohydrate levels, improving their nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CONSUMER STUDY" target="Z. MORIO">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A consumer study evaluates public attitudes toward eating Z. morio fed with Styrofoam, assessing acceptance and safety perceptions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CONSUMER STUDY" target="T. MOLITOR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A consumer study evaluates public attitudes toward eating T. molitor fed with Styrofoam, assessing acceptance and safety perceptions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NUTRIENT COMPOSITION" target="STYROFOAM-FED MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms fed with Styrofoam exhibit changes in their nutrient composition, including higher protein and ash content and reduced fat and carbohydrate levels.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM-FED MEALWORMS" target="LIPIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lipid levels are reduced in mealworms fed with Styrofoam, indicating changes in energy storage.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM-FED MEALWORMS" target="CARBOHYDRATES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Carbohydrate levels are reduced in mealworms fed with Styrofoam, affecting their energy content.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM-FED MEALWORMS" target="PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Protein levels are higher in mealworms fed with Styrofoam, improving their nutritional value.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM-FED MEALWORMS" target="ASH CONTENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ash content is higher in mealworms fed with Styrofoam, indicating changes in inorganic residue.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM-FED MEALWORMS" target="ENERGY CONTENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Energy content is lower in mealworms fed with Styrofoam due to reduced fat and carbohydrate levels.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM-FED MEALWORMS" target="BIOCHEMICAL ASSAYS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biochemical assays were conducted to analyze the nutrient composition and energy storage in mealworms fed with Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIPIDS" target="BIODIESEL">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodiesel is produced from lipids extracted from mealworms, offering a sustainable energy solution&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids extracted from sewage sludge, scum, and mealworm remains are converted into biodiesel, serving as a renewable energy source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIPIDS" target="$\BETA$ $^{13}$C-LABELED PS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">$\beta$ $^{13}$C-labeled polystyrene is incorporated into lipids by mealworms, confirming the mineralization process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROTEIN" target="STARVATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Proteins are hydrolyzed and used as an energy source during long periods of starvation in insects.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="SOLID-STATE $^{13}$C CP/MAS NMR SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Solid-state $^{13}$C CP/MAS NMR spectroscopy is used to characterize the chemical structure of polystyrene degradation products in mealworm fecula.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED (TG-FTIR) SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TG-FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze the thermal degradation and chemical composition of polystyrene in mealworm fecula.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="STYROFOAM-FEEDING MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrofoam-feeding mealworms produce fecula containing degraded Styrofoam products, which are analyzed to study biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="GPC (GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">GPC is used to analyze the molecular weights and distributions of degraded Styrofoam products in mealworm fecula</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="CP/MAS NMR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">CP/MAS NMR is used to analyze the chemical structure of degraded Styrofoam products in mealworm fecula</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYZER (TGA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TGA is used to perform thermal characterization of fecula and Styrofoam, providing insights into their degradation properties</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="FTIR (FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical composition of degraded Styrofoam products in fecula</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="THERMAL ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermal analysis is used to study the chemical composition changes in fecula, revealing multiple stages of decomposition and the depletion of polystyrene content.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="TG/DTG ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TG/DTG analysis is used to study the decomposition stages of fecula, revealing the depletion of polystyrene content.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="FOURIER-TRANSFORMED INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR is used to analyze the chemical composition of fecula, providing evidence of polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="THERMAL GRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS (TGA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TGA is used to study the thermal stability of fecula, indicating the extent of polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (1H-NMR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">1H-NMR is used to analyze the molecular structure of fecula, providing insights into the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FECULA" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (M_W AND M_N)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Molecular weight measures (M_w and M_n) are used to determine the size of polymer chains in fecula, indicating the breakdown of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="PS DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">CO2 is a byproduct of the mineralization process during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="MEALWORM GUT MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The mealworm gut microbiome produces CO2 as a byproduct of polystyrene (PS) mineralization.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="$\ALPHA$ $^{13}$C-LABELED PS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">$\alpha$ $^{13}$C-labeled polystyrene is mineralized into $^{13}$CO2 by mealworms, confirming the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="POLYMER FRAGMENTATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polymer fragmentation during biodegradation results in the production of smaller molecules and CO2 as a byproduct.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="CH4">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">CH4 and CO2 are gases produced during bacterial degradation processes, indicating the breakdown of organic materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="MI-PS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mi-PS degradation by bacteria can lead to the production of CO2, a byproduct of aerobic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="ENTEROBACTER SP. LG3">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter sp. LG3 produces CO2 as a final metabolite during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) under anaerobic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="GC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">GC analysis is used to measure the concentration of CO2 produced during the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by Enterobacter sp. LG3.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO2" target="HEADSPACE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The headspace of the incubation bottle is where CO2 concentrations are measured to assess the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) by Enterobacter sp. LG3.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="ETHANOLIC EXTRACT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ethanolic extract of Z. morio is used in cytotoxicity studies to assess its effects on cancer and normal cell lines.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="CYTOTOXICITY TEST">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cytotoxicity tests are conducted on Z. morio extracts to evaluate their safety and potential toxic effects on cells.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="VERO CELLS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Vero cells are used in cytotoxicity studies to assess the effects of Z. morio extracts, particularly at low doses.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="MCF-7 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">MCF-7 cells are used in cytotoxicity studies to assess the effects of Z. morio extracts, showing higher sensitivity to ethanolic extracts.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="ISOPROPANOLIC EXTRACT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Isopropanolic extract of Z. morio is used in cytotoxicity studies to assess its effects on cancer cell lines.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="ENDOTHELIAL CELLS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Endothelial cells are used in cytotoxicity tests to evaluate the effects of Z. morio extracts.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="FIBROBLASTS">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fibroblasts are suggested for future cytotoxicity studies to further evaluate the effects of Z. morio extracts.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="EPITHELIAL CELLS">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Epithelial cells are suggested for future cytotoxicity studies to further evaluate the effects of Z. morio extracts.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="FLOW CYTOMETRY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Flow cytometry is used to measure cell viability, apoptosis, and ROS levels in Z. morio gut cells fed with Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Z. MORIO" target="AEROMONAS SP. TM1">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aeromonas sp. TM1, isolated from Z. morio, shows the ability to degrade polystyrene</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="HEP3B CELL LINE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm extracts significantly decreased the number of Hep3B cells, indicating cytotoxic effects on this liver cancer cell line.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="SK-HEP-1 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm extracts significantly decreased the number of SK-HEP-1 cells, indicating cytotoxic effects on this liver cancer cell line.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="CACO-2 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm extracts significantly decreased the number of Caco-2 cells, indicating cytotoxic effects on this colon cancer cell line.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="HEPG2 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm extracts significantly decreased the number of HepG2 cells, indicating cytotoxic effects on this liver cancer cell line.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="HELA CELL LINE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm extracts significantly decreased the number of HeLa cells, indicating cytotoxic effects on this cervical cancer cell line.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="L929 CELL LINE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm extracts did not significantly affect the number of L929 cells, indicating no cytotoxic effects on this normal murine fibroblast cell line.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="SPONTANEOUSLY HYPERTENSIVE RATS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm extracts improved health parameters in spontaneously hypertensive rats, including reduced blood pressure and increased red blood cell glutathione.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="NR ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The NR assay was used to measure the cytotoxic effects of mealworm extracts on various cell lines.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="SRB ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The SRB assay was used to measure the cytotoxic effects of mealworm extracts on various cell lines.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM EXTRACTS" target="LDH ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The LDH assay was used to measure the cytotoxic effects of mealworm extracts on various cell lines.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERWORM EXTRACTS" target="VERO CELL LINE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworm extracts showed no cytotoxicity on Vero cells at low doses, indicating safety for normal kidney epithelial cells.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR" target="FLOW CYTOMETRY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Flow cytometry is used to measure cell viability, apoptosis, and ROS levels in T. molitor gut cells fed with Styrofoam.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR" target="FACULTY OF ENVIRONMENTAL ENGINEERING">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Faculty of Environmental Engineering conducted research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by T. molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR" target="PE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae exhibit enhanced carbon and nitrogen metabolism to degrade PE polymers, with a significant increase in aromatic compound degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR" target="AROMATIC COMPOUND DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae show a significant increase in aromatic compound degradation when fed with PE-MPs, indicating adaptation to nitrogen-deficient conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR" target="CHITINOLYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae exhibit increased chitinolysis when fed with HDPE, indicating their preference for assimilating chitin-rich exoskeletons to obtain nitrogen.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR" target="BROAD DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae exhibit broad depolymerization patterns during the biodegradation of PS and PP.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECTS" target="TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Toxicity studies are performed to assess whether insects that consume Styrofoam are safe for human consumption.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECTS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL REMEDIATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Insects are used in environmental remediation to degrade pollutants like Styrofoam, contributing to waste management solutions.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECTS" target="FARMED FISH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Farmed fish have been studied for their potential to be fed with insects as an alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TOXICITY" target="POLYSTYRENE NANOPARTICLES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene nanoparticles are toxic to various organisms, highlighting their environmental and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TOXICITY" target="SOMATIC MAINTENANCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Toxicity from PS metabolites can increase somatic maintenance costs in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TOXICITY" target="MATURITY MAINTENANCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Toxicity from PS metabolites can increase maturity maintenance costs in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENVIRONMENTAL REMEDIATION" target="PLASTIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmental remediation uses organisms like mealworms and superworms to address plastic pollution through biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93</data>
    </edge>
