      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Kundugal et al., 2019 investigates the biodegradation of polyethylene by the lesser wax-worm, Achroia griseola.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM DEBRIS" target="MARINE ORGANISMS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrofoam debris releases hazardous additives that can affect marine organisms, highlighting its environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM DEBRIS" target="HAZARDOUS ADDITIVES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrofoam debris releases hazardous additives that can negatively impact marine ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)" target="EGESTED FRASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Egested frass from lesser waxworms shows evidence of polyethylene biodegradation, indicating the breakdown of HDPE into new functional organic groups.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALPHITOBIUS DIAPERINUS" target="METABOLIC RESPONSES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Alphitobius diaperinus is studied for its metabolic responses during food shortage and recovery, which can inform biodegradation research</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALPHITOBIUS DIAPERINUS" target="NITROGEN-TO-PROTEIN CONVERSION FACTORS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Alphitobius diaperinus is evaluated for its protein content using nitrogen-to-protein conversion factors.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALPHITOBIUS DIAPERINUS" target="PROTEIN QUALITY EVALUATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Protein quality evaluation assesses the nutritional value of Alphitobius diaperinus when fed to rats.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT" target="PLASTIC-DEGRADING PROCESS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sustainable development research explores the use of insects like Tribolium castaneum for plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICRO-POLYSTYRENE (MPS)" target="RIGID PLASTIC WASTE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Micro-polystyrene (MPS) is a component of rigid plastic waste, which is a significant environmental pollutant targeted for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RIGID PLASTIC WASTE" target="RIGID MICRO-POLYSTYRENE (MPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rigid micro-polystyrene (MPS) is a component of rigid plastic waste, which is a significant environmental pollutant targeted for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC DEGRADATION" target="LARVAL AGE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larval age significantly influences the ability of mealworms to degrade microplastics, with three-month-old mealworms showing the highest efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LARVAL AGE" target="MICRO-PS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The age of mealworm larvae affects their ability to consume and degrade micro-PS</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LARVAL AGE" target="MICRO-POLYSTYRENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larval age is a factor that regulates the biodegradation of micro-polystyrene in mealworms (Tenebrio molitor).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARTICLE SIZE" target="MICROPLASTIC CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Particle size affects the consumption rate of microplastics by mealworms, with smaller particles being consumed more readily.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARTICLE SIZE" target="MICRO-POLYSTYRENE BIODEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Particle size is a factor that regulates the biodegradation of micro-polystyrene in mealworms (Tenebrio molitor).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PUPAE" target="MICROPLASTIC FEEDING">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastic feeding affects the pupation process of mealworms, influencing their development and survival.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PUPAE" target="MEALWORMS (TENEBRIO MOLITOR)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pupae are the developmental stage of mealworms observed during the experiment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PUPAE" target="LESSER WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pupae are a developmental stage of lesser waxworms, indicating their lifecycle progression.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PUPAE" target="FEEDING CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The formation and hatching rates of pupae are influenced by the type of feed, with wheat bran showing the highest rates.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PUPAE" target="REPRODUCTION BUFFER">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The reproduction buffer energy threshold determines when Tenebrio molitor larvae can pupate.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICRO-PS" target="SURVIVORSHIP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The survival rate of mealworms is influenced by the size of micro-PS particles they consume</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="YOUNG LARVAL STAGE (YLS)">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Young larval stage mealworms consume minimal microplastics and show no measurable degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="MIDDLE LARVAL STAGE (MLS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Middle larval stage mealworms consume moderate amounts of microplastics and show oxidation but no depolymerization</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="OLD LARVAL STAGE (OLS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Old larval stage mealworms consume the most microplastics and show both depolymerization and oxidation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="SURVIVAL AND DEVELOPMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastic consumption negatively affects the survival and development of mealworms, with older larvae showing greater resistance</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="YLS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Young larval stage mealworms (YLS) are fed with microplastics (MPS), affecting their survival and growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="MLS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Middle larval stage mealworms (MLS) are fed with microplastics (MPS), affecting their survival, growth, and metamorphosis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="OLS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Old larval stage mealworms (OLS) are fed with microplastics (MPS), affecting their survival, growth, and metamorphosis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="TOXICITIES OF MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics cause toxic effects such as weight loss, higher mortality, and altered gut microbiota in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="TROPHIC TRANSFER OF MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can be transferred through the food chain, raising concerns about their impact on higher trophic levels.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="AQUATIC LUGWORMS (ARENICOLA MARINA)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aquatic lugworms show a positive correlation between microplastic concentration in sediment and weight loss.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="FOLSOMIA CANDIDA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Folsomia candida shows weight reduction and altered reproduction under microplastic exposure.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="TROPHIC TRANSFER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can move through the food chain, raising concerns about their impact on higher trophic levels.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="WOODLICE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Woodlice can ingest and biofragment plastics, contributing to the formation of micro- and nanoplastics in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="ENCHYTRAEIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enchytraeids can ingest and biofragment plastics, contributing to microplastic pollution in soil ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can further fragment into nanoplastics, which are more harmful due to their ability to cross biological barriers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="PHYSIOLOGICAL STRESS RESPONSES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Physiological stress responses, such as oxidative stress and inflammation, are induced in organisms exposed to microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="FOOD WEBS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics can disrupt food webs by accumulating in organisms and causing ecological imbalances.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polypropylene is a major source of microplastics, which are environmental pollutants resulting from its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="SNAILS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Snails ingest and retain microplastics in terrestrial environments, leading to studies on their potential biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="ENTEROCCOCCACEAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The relative abundance of Enterococcaceae decreases in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae when fed microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="LACTOBACILLACEAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The relative abundance of Lactobacillaceae decreases in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae when fed microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="PLASTIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastics (MPs) are a significant component of plastic pollution, contributing to environmental and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="UV RADIATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">UV radiation accelerates the aging process of microplastics (MPs), altering their physicochemical properties and affecting their biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="UV AGING">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">UV aging alters the physicochemical properties of microplastics (MPs), making them more susceptible to biodegradation by organisms like Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY–MASS SPECTROMETRY (GC–MS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC–MS) is used to identify biodegradation products of microplastics (MPs) after degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="AGING INDEX">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The aging index refers to the degree of physicochemical changes in microplastics (MPs) due to environmental factors like UV radiation, which affects their biodegradation by organisms such as Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="FRESHWATER AMPHIPOD">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Freshwater amphipods can fragment ingested microplastics into smaller particles, but they have limited plastic-degrading capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MPS)" target="GUT EPITHELIUM DAMAGE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastic ingestion can induce gut epithelium damage in mealworms, leading to inflammation and immune responses.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD CHAIN" target="PLASTIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic pollution affects the food chain, as microplastics are ingested by animals and humans.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FISH" target="ENDOCRINE DISRUPTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fish are studied for endocrine disruption caused by the ingestion of polyethylene with sorbed chemical pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHICKEN" target="EARTHWORM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chickens can ingest microplastics through feeding on microplastic-contaminated earthworms, as confirmed by studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="HAFNIA-OBESUMBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hafnia-Obesumbacterium bacteria are associated with PET degradation in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="FUNGAL ENZYMES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fungal enzymes are used to catalyze the degradation of PET, offering a potential solution for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="THERMOSTABLE IS PETASE VARIANTS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermostable Is PETase variants are engineered to degrade PET more efficiently, enhancing plastic waste management efforts.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="ENZYMATIC PET DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzymatic PET degradation is a process that breaks down PET using enzymes, offering a sustainable solution for plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="PLASTIC POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a common thermoplastic polymer that contributes to global plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="PET DEPOLYMERASE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PET depolymerase breaks down PET plastic bottles, enabling recycling and reducing pollution</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="PET-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PET-degrading bacteria play a crucial role in the enzymatic breakdown of PET, aiding in its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="PETASE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PETase is an enzyme that facilitates the degradation of PET, particularly in low-crystallinity forms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="LEAF COMPOST-CUTINASE ENZYME (LCC-ICCG)">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Leaf compost-cutinase enzyme (LCC-ICCG) is used to degrade PET, though its efficiency is limited by the polymer's crystallinity.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="GREEN CONCRETE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is recycled and used in the production of green concrete, contributing to sustainable construction practices.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="MARINE BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A marine bacterium is studied for its ability to degrade polyethylene terephthalate (PET), highlighting its role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="GENETICALLY MODIFIED MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Genetically modified microorganisms are engineered to enhance their ability to degrade polyethylene terephthalate (PET), offering a potential solution for plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="TEREPHTHALIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) can be hydrolyzed into terephthalic acid by enzymes such as Candida antarctica lipase (CALB) and cutinase.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)" target="BLACK SOLDIER FLY (BSF)">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
