    <edge source="PET" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The molecular weight of PET significantly impacts its biodegradability, with lower MW polymers being more easily degraded by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET" target="CRYSTALLINITY DEGREE">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The crystallinity degree of PET affects its biodegradability, with amorphous regions being more susceptible to degradation than crystalline regions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET" target="1H NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">1H NMR analysis provides evidence of structural and chemical modifications in PET polymers after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
