    </edge>
    <edge source="PET" target="BD PATTERN">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">BD pattern refers to the rapid biodegradation of PET polymers, characterized by a decrease in all molecular weight parameters and a shift towards lower molecular weight.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET" target="LD PATTERN">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LD pattern refers to the selective breakdown of low molecular weight PET polymers, with longer chains remaining less degraded, observed in high molecular weight PET polymers like PET63500.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET" target="OXIDIZED GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oxidized groups are formed during the biodegradation of PET polymers, indicating chemical changes such as increased hydrophilicity.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET" target="HYDROPHILIC FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrophilic functional groups, such as hydroxyl groups, are formed during the biodegradation of PET polymers, increasing the hydrophilicity of the polymer surface.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET" target="LACHNATALEA SP.">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lachnatalea sp. showed high abundance in PET-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae, suggesting its potential role in PET degradation.</data>
