    <edge source="AGAR" target="PE MPS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Agar is used to mix with polyethylene microplastics for feeding T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae in biodegradation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AGAR" target="MINERAL MEDIUM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Agar is added to mineral medium to solidify it, creating a suitable environment for microbial growth</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HMDB" target="METAX">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The HMDB is used for metabolite identification and annotation in MetaX.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSIDANS M9">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Achromobacter xylosidans M9 significantly reduces the size and count of nanoplastics, demonstrating its role in nanoplastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="TEM (TRANSMISSION ELECTRON MICROSCOPY)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TEM is used to analyze the size reduction of nanoplastics after microbial degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="ZETA SIZER MEASUREMENTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zeta sizer measurements are used to quantify the reduction in nanoplastic particle size after microbial degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="MICROBIAL ACTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microbial action is responsible for the degradation of nanoplastics, leading to significant reductions in particle size and count.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polypropylene contributes to the formation of nanoplastics, which are smaller plastic particles that pollute various environments.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="GAMMARUS DUEBENI">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gammarus duebeni fragments polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) into nanoplastics (NPs) in its digestive tract, contributing to the generation of smaller plastic particles.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="EARTHWORMS (EISENIA ANDREI)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Earthworms (Eisenia andrei) generate nanoplastics (NPs) from polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) in their hindgut, which can cause cellular damage.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="WOODLICE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Woodlice are studied for their role in the generation and accumulation of nanoplastics (NPs) through biofragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="ENCHYTRAEIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enchytraeids are studied for their role in the generation and accumulation of nanoplastics (NPs) through biofragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="PYROLYSIS-GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY MASS SPECTROMETRY (PY-GCMS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Py-GCMS is used to quantify nanoplastics (NPs) in the frass of mealworms, aiding in the study of plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="MILLI-Q ULTRAPURE WATER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Milli-Q ultrapure water is used to remove background nanoplastics (NPs) from experimental containers and equipment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="METHANOL (HPLC GRADE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Methanol (HPLC grade) is used to rinse containers and equipment to remove potential nanoplastics (NPs) contamination.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="LASER LIGHT SCATTERING SIZER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The laser light scattering sizer is used to detect and analyze the absence of nanoplastics in mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NANOPLASTICS (NPS)" target="PY-GCMC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Py-GCMC analysis confirms the absence of nanoplastics in mealworm frass, supporting the findings of rapid plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STARVATION" target="ENERGY ALLOCATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Starvation affects energy allocation in Tenebrio molitor larvae, leading to changes in growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STARVATION" target="STARVATION 1 GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Starvation 1 group is used to study the physiological responses of Tenebrio molitor larvae under complete food deprivation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STARVATION" target="STARVATION 2 GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Starvation 2 group is used to study the effects of limited food availability (droppings and moult residues) on Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STARVATION" target="PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Physiological responses in Tenebrio molitor larvae include changes in reserve density and volumetric length during starvation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STARVATION" target="TRIGLYCERIDES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Triglycerides play a critical role in the survival of insect larvae during starvation by serving as a primary energy reserve.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="SCANNING ELECTRON MICROPHOTOGRAPHY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scanning electron microphotography is used to analyze the surface structure of polyethylene (PE) before and after degradation by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="SPODOPTERA FRUGIPERDA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Spodoptera frugiperda larvae can biodegrade polyethylene (PE), contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="THERMAL OXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermal oxidation can make polyethylene (PE) more susceptible to biodegradation in natural environments.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="PHOTO-OXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Photo-oxidation breaks down polyethylene (PE), making it more susceptible to microbial biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Photo-oxidation can enhance the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) by breaking down its structure.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="ENZYMATIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzymatic degradation by insects and microorganisms plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene (PE) can be biodegraded by microorganisms, though the process is slow and depends on the polymer's molecular weight and crystallinity.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="THERMO-OXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermo-oxidation degrades polyethylene (PE), facilitating its biodegradation by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="ACINETOBACTER SP. NYZ450">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450, in co-culture with Bacillus sp. NyZ451, can degrade polyethylene mulching films.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE" target="BACILLUS SP. NYZ451">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. NyZ451, in co-culture with Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450, can degrade polyethylene mulching films.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MW)" target="POLYPROPYLENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The biodegradability of polypropylene microplastics by mealworms is influenced by their molecular weight, with lower MW PP being more rapidly degraded.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MW)" target="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS (PS MPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The molecular weight of PS MPs affects their biodegradation rate, with lower MW PS MPs being more easily digested by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1H NMR SPECTRA" target="RESIDUAL POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">1H NMR spectra are used to analyze the chemical structure of residual polymers from mealworm frass, revealing changes in molecular composition due to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENTAMICIN SULFATE" target="LB MEDIUM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LB medium is used to culture bacteria, while gentamicin sulfate is added to suppress gut bacteria in mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SPIROPLASMA SP." target="LIGNOCELLULOSE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Spiroplasma sp. is a key microorganism involved in the degradation of lignocellulose, a natural polymer.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SHANNON INDEX" target="INTESTINAL MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Shannon index is used to measure the diversity of microbial flora in the intestinal microbiota of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA" target="ADVANCES IN TECHNOLOGY AND INNOVATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Advances in Technology and Innovation publishes research on microbes capable of degrading polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA" target="MICROBIOLOGICAL SOCIETY OF KOREA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Microbiological Society of Korea conducts research on plastic-degrading bacteria in insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA" target="LURIA-BERTANI (LB) BROTH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LB broth was used to culture and isolate plastic-degrading bacteria from mealworms' guts.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA" target="NPS-CFB AGAR MEDIA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">NPS-CFB agar media was used to isolate and identify bacteria capable of degrading nano-polystyrene (NPS).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA" target="NPS-CFB BROTH MEDIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">NPS-CFB broth media was used for primary screening of bacterial isolates capable of degrading NPS.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA" target="BIT-26">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">BIT-26, isolated from plastic-eating mealworms, may contribute to plastic degradation, similar to Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TEREPHTHALIC ACID" target="PROTOCATECHUIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Protocatechuic acid is produced during the biodegradation of terephthalic acid by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Terephthalic acid is metabolized into protocatechuic acid during the biodegradation of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Terephthalic acid is metabolized into protocatechuic acid during the biodegradation of PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ETHYLENE GLYCOL" target="GLYCOLIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glycolic acid is an intermediate metabolite produced during the oxidation of ethylene glycol by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ETHYLENE GLYCOL" target="GLYCOLIC ALDEHYDE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glycolic aldehyde is an intermediate metabolite produced during the oxidation of ethylene glycol by the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ETHYLENE GLYCOL" target="GLYCOLIC ALDEHYDE DEHYDROGENASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glycolic aldehyde dehydrogenase is an enzyme that contributes to the metabolism of ethylene glycol in the PET-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ETHYLENE GLYCOL" target="GLYOXYLATE REDUCTASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glyoxylate reductase is an enzyme that contributes to the metabolism of ethylene glycol in the PET-fed group.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ETHYLENE GLYCOL" target="BIOCHEMICAL RECYCLING">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ethylene glycol is a valuable chemical that could be produced through the biochemical recycling of polyethylene, offering a potential solution for plastic waste management</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ETHYLENE GLYCOL" target="FOURIER-TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to detect ethylene glycol in biodegradation studies, though results are inconclusive.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TCA CYCLE" target="PHENYLACETYL-COA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phenylacetyl-CoA enters the TCA cycle, where it is further broken down during the microbial degradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TCA CYCLE" target="Β-OXIDATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The TCA cycle generates energy from acetyl-CoA derived from long-chain fatty acids during β-oxidation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TCA CYCLE" target="MITOCHONDRIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The TCA cycle in mitochondria is regulated by magnesium and influenced by dietary polystyrene in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BACTERIA" target="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacteria can degrade PET into smaller molecules, with the help of transport systems and metabolic pathways, contributing to its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BACTERIA" target="FLAGELLA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Flagella enhance bacterial adhesion and electron transport, aiding in the metabolism of organic matter like plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="ATP-BINDING CASSETTE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The ATP-binding cassette facilitates the transport of small PET degradation products into bacterial cells.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="PEPTIDE/NICKEL TRANSPORT SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The peptide/nickel transport system aids in the uptake of small molecules derived from PET degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="MULTIPLE SUGAR TRANSPORT SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The multiple sugar transport system facilitates the uptake of sugars and other small molecules, including PET degradation products.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="LC-MS (LIQUID CHROMATOGRAPHY-MASS SPECTROMETRY)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LC-MS is used to analyze metabolites resulting from PET biodegradation, providing insights into degradation pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="HIERARCHICAL CLUSTERING ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hierarchical clustering analysis distinguishes metabolites between PET-fed and agar-fed larvae, highlighting metabolic differences.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
