      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HMDB classification identifies up-regulated metabolites like lipids in PET-fed larvae, linked to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="KEGG PATHWAYS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">KEGG pathways reveal enriched metabolic pathways in PET-fed larvae, associated with plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="GLYCEROPHOSPHOLIPID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glycerophospholipid metabolism is enriched in PET-fed larvae, aiding in cell membrane maintenance during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="ARGININE AND PROLINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Arginine and proline metabolism is upregulated in PET-fed larvae, protecting protein structures during plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="GLUTATHIONE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glutathione metabolism is a stress response pathway induced by microplastics in PET-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="ALPHA-LINOLENIC ACID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Alpha-linolenic acid metabolism is enriched in PET-fed larvae, linked to plastic degradation and oxidative stress.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="ETHER LIPID METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ether lipid metabolism is involved in plastic degradation, enriched in PET-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="AMINO SUGAR AND NUCLEOTIDE SUGAR METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Amino sugar and nucleotide sugar metabolism is altered in PET-fed larvae, linked to plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIPID METABOLISM" target="LG3">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LG3 exhibits changes in lipid metabolism pathways during the degradation of polystyrene microplastics, including upregulation of lipid A biosynthesis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTERMEDIATE PRODUCTS" target="GUT MICROBIOMES">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Intermediate products formed during PET biodegradation are further broken down by the gut microbiomes of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FORMIC ACID" target="ACQUITY UPLC T3 COLUMN">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Formic acid is used as a solvent in the mobile phase for the ACQUITY UPLC T3 column.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FORMIC ACID" target="UHPLC SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Formic acid is used in the mobile phase for lipid analysis in the UHPLC system</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHLORIDE IONS" target="MICROPOLLUTANTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chloride ions in high salinity wastewater accelerate the removal of micropollutants over light-driven catalysts.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYDROXYL RADICALS" target="WATER PURIFICATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydroxyl radicals are generated for water purification, aiding in the degradation of pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMOMONOSPORA FUSCA" target="ALIPHATIC-AROMATIC COPOLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermomonospora fusca can biodegrade aliphatic-aromatic copolymers, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLANT BIOMASS" target="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plant biomass and synthetic polymers can both be enzymatically degraded, offering potential applications in waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLANT BIOMASS" target="ENZYMATIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">23.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzymatic degradation is a process that breaks down plant biomass, making it a key focus in biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Plant biomass can be enzymatically degraded for sustainable waste management and resource recovery.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS" target="FOURIER-TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR spectroscopy identifies chemical changes in synthetic polymers during digestion by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS" target="MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Synthetic polymers like polystyrene and polyethylene influence the microbiome of mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS" target="ENZYMATIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">24.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzymatic degradation is also applied to synthetic polymers, offering a potential solution to their environmental persistence.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers, such as plastics, can be enzymatically degraded to reduce environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL PERSISTENCE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Synthetic polymers are known for their environmental persistence, which makes them a significant target for biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS" target="CARBON BONDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Carbon bonds in synthetic polymers contribute to their low bio-reactivity and slow degradation by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS" target="BIOCHEMICAL REACTORS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biochemical reactors are proposed to culture gut microbes from A. diaperinus larvae for the degradation of synthetic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYNTHETIC POLYMERS" target="PLASTICIZERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Synthetic polymers often contain plasticizers, which increase their flexibility but can also contribute to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMOSTABLE POLYESTER HYDROLASES" target="POLYESTERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermostable polyester hydrolases are engineered to degrade polyesters efficiently, enhancing plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RESIDUAL PLASTICS" target="MEALWORM INTESTINES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm intestines were dissected to study the residual plastics after biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RESIDUAL PLASTICS" target="SIZE DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The size distribution of residual plastics in frass was analyzed to understand the biodegradation process</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLY(ETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)" target="PET DEPOLYMERASE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PET depolymerase is an enzyme engineered to degrade poly(ethylene terephthalate), enabling plastic recycling.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </edge>
