    </edge>
    <edge source="CENTRAL CARBON METABOLISM" target="DROSOPHILA MELANOGASTER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Drosophila melanogaster is used as a model organism in the KEGG database for pathway impact analysis in the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC FEED SUBSTRATE" target="INSECT LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, are exposed to plastic feed substrates, which can impact their central carbon metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECT LARVAE" target="EMULSIFIERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Insect larvae secrete emulsifiers that mediate the biodegradation of plastics by breaking down their structure.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECT LARVAE" target="BIOREMEDIATION">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, are used in bioremediation to degrade hydrocarbon-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECT LARVAE" target="HYDROCARBON-BASED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Insect larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, are capable of biodegrading hydrocarbon-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECT LARVAE" target="DIET 2">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 2, which included additional water, showed distinct effects on the growth and PS degradation capabilities of insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECT LARVAE" target="CONTROL DIET">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The control diet serves as a baseline to compare the effects of PS-containing diets on insect larvae growth and PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECT LARVAE" target="DIET 1">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 1, with a 4:1 bran-to-polystyrene ratio, is used to study the effects of PS consumption on insect larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INSECT LARVAE" target="DIET 3">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Diet 3 shows higher larvae weight compared to other diets, indicating its impact on insect growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIONIDAE" target="SPECIFIC CONSUMPTION RATES (SCRS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Specific Consumption Rates (SCRs) are used to quantify the rate at which mealworms consume different plastics, providing insights into their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE" target="ENZYMATIC REMEDIATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enzymatic remediation is used to degrade polyethylene terephthalate (PET) for effective plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE" target="CUTINASE HOMOLOG">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A cutinase homolog has been identified as an enzyme capable of degrading polyethylene terephthalate (PET).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE" target="PETASES">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PETases are enzymes that degrade PET, offering potential solutions for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE" target="MARINE BACTERIUM">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A marine bacterium has been studied for its ability to degrade polyethylene terephthalate (PET), highlighting its role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE" target="BLACK SOLDIER FLY">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black Soldier Fly larvae exhibit elevated pyrimidine metabolism when exposed to PET, indicating a unique metabolic response to this plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Black Soldier Fly larvae were exposed to PET to study its effects on metabolism and larval weight.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE" target="ELEMENTAL ANALYZER ISOTOPE RATIO MASS SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">EA-IRMS is used to measure isotopic ratios in PET, providing insights into its degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE" target="MARINE MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Marine microbes also contribute to the biodegradation of high-density polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine microbes can also degrade high-density polyethylene, although at a slower rate compared to low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE" target="ASPERGILLUS NIGER">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aspergillus niger can colonize and degrade thermally oxidized high-density polyethylene, contributing to plastic waste degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE" target="BLUE MUSSEL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The blue mussel internalizes high-density polyethylene (HDPE) microplastics (MPs) in its digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TEMPERATURE" target="BIODEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Temperature is an influencing factor that affects the biodepolymerization process of plastics and polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TEMPERATURE" target="BIODEGRADATION OF PLASTICS IN SOIL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The biodegradation of plastics in soil is influenced by temperature, affecting the rate at which plastics break down.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DNA EXTRACTION" target="MICROFLORA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DNA extraction is performed to analyze the microflora present in the intestines of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DNA EXTRACTION" target="16S-18S ASSAY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DNA extraction is a prerequisite for the 16S-18S assay, which analyzes microbial genes in the extracted DNA.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DNA EXTRACTION" target="TISSUELYSER II">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The TissueLyser II is used to mechanically break down samples, facilitating DNA extraction.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DNA EXTRACTION" target="EMAC NUCLEIC ACID EXTRACTION SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The EMAC Nucleic Acid Extraction System automates the DNA extraction process, ensuring consistency and efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DNA EXTRACTION" target="QUANT-IT DSDNA HIGH SENSITIVE ASSAY KIT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Quant-iT dsDNA High Sensitive Assay Kit is used to quantify DNA extracted from samples.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DNA EXTRACTION" target="NANODROP ONE MICROVOLUME UV-VIS SPECTROPHOTOMETER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The NanoDrop One is used to measure the concentration of DNA extracted from mealworm gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DNA EXTRACTION" target="MICROBIAL COMMUNITY ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">DNA extraction is a critical step in microbial community analysis to study the gut bacteria of mealworm larvae</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DARKLING BEETLES" target="PLASTIC PACKAGING">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Darkling beetles are known to chew and eat plastic packaging materials&lt;SEP&gt;Darkling beetles have been observed to chew and ingest plastic packaging materials, contributing to biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOTH LARVAE" target="ACINETOBACTER SP. STRAIN NYZ450">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a plastic-degrading bacterial strain isolated from moth larvae, contributing to the biodegradation of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOTH LARVAE" target="BACILLUS SP. STRAIN NYZ451">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a plastic-degrading bacterial strain isolated from moth larvae, contributing to the biodegradation of LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACINETOBACTER SP. STRAIN NYZ450" target="BACILLUS SP. STRAIN NYZ451">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 and Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 work together in a co-culture to degrade polyethylene (PE), demonstrating enhanced biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACINETOBACTER SP. STRAIN NYZ450" target="YIN ET AL. [32]">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yin et al. [32] isolated Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 from yellow mealworm larvae, demonstrating its role in polyethylene (PE) biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BACILLUS SP. STRAIN NYZ451" target="YIN ET AL. [32]">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Yin et al. [32] isolated Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 from yellow mealworm larvae, showing its role in polyethylene (PE) biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMOGRAVIMETRICAL ANALYSIS (TGA)" target="SODIUM DODECYL SULFATE (SDS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SDS is used to pretreat samples for TGA to ensure accurate measurement of polymer stability during biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMOGRAVIMETRICAL ANALYSIS (TGA)" target="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (TG-FTIR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">TG-FTIR is combined with TGA to identify decomposition products from polymer samples, aiding in biodegradation studies</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMOGRAVIMETRICAL ANALYSIS (TGA)" target="STARCH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Starch, as a co-diet, can cause misinterpretation of TGA results in biodegradation studies</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
    </edge>
