    <edge source="LB MEDIUM" target="CELLULOSIMICROBIUM FUNKEI WJ2025">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Luria-Bertani (LB) medium is used to culture Cellulosimicrobium funkei WJ2025 for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LB MEDIUM" target="BACTERIAL GROWTH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LB medium is used to cultivate bacterial strains isolated from mealworms, enabling the study of their growth and polystyrene-degrading capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LB MEDIUM" target="E. COLI">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">E. coli is cultured in LB medium to study its growth dynamics and doubling time.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THF" target="PS FILM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">THF is used to extract polystyrene from mealworm frass, which contains degraded PS film.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHLOROFORM-D" target="\(^{1}H\) NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chloroform-D is used as a solvent for \(^{1}H\) NMR analysis to study the chemical transformations of polystyrene in frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHLOROFORM-D" target="EGESTED FRASS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chloroform-D is used to extract residual PE from the egested frass of lesser waxworms for further analysis&lt;SEP&gt;Chloroform-D is used to extract residual polyethylene from the egested frass of lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PVDF FILTERS" target="NMR SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PVDF filters are used to prepare samples for NMR analysis by filtering extracts.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DEIONIZED WATER" target="WATER CONTACT ANGLE (WCA) ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Deionized water is used to rinse plastic samples before water contact angle analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR LARVAE" target="PS MICRO POWDERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. molitor larvae are exposed to PS micro powders as a carbon source in biodegradation experiments</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR LARVAE" target="PS CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS consumption by T. molitor larvae is measured to evaluate their ability to degrade polystyrene and its impact on their physiology</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. MOLITOR LARVAE" target="THF EXTRACTABLE FRACTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">THF extractable fraction is used to measure residual polystyrene in frass, indicating the extent of biodegradation by T. molitor larvae</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MOLECULAR WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION (MWD)" target="LDPE DEPOLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Molecular weight distribution (MWD) is used to assess the depolymerization of LDPE, showing shifts toward lower molecular weights after digestion</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OXIDIZED FUNCTIONAL GROUPS" target="RESIDUAL POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oxidized functional groups are identified in residual polymers extracted from frass, indicating the extent of LDPE biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC PACKAGING MATERIALS" target="STORED-PRODUCT INSECTS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Stored-product insects are studied for their ability to penetrate plastic packaging materials, highlighting their impact on food storage.&lt;SEP&gt;Stored-product insects can penetrate and degrade plastic packaging materials, contributing to plastic waste issues.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STORED-PRODUCT INSECTS" target="PACKAGING FILMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Packaging films have been tested for resistance to stored-product insects, highlighting their role in food safety and environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACINETOBACTER SP. NYZ450" target="POLYETHYLENE MULCHING FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACINETOBACTER SP. NYZ450" target="PE MULCHING FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450 is capable of degrading PE mulching films, contributing to their biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACINETOBACTER SP. NYZ450" target="BACILLUS SP. NYZ451">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The consortium of Acinetobacter sp. NyZ450 and Bacillus sp. NyZ451 is more effective in degrading PE mulching films than either strain alone.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BACILLUS SP. NYZ451" target="POLYETHYLENE MULCHING FILMS">
      <data key="d3">15.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. strain NYZ451 is involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene mulching films are biodegraded by Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 in a co-culture with Acinetobacter sp. strain NyX450.</data>
      <data key="d5">7&lt;SEP&gt;8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BACILLUS SP. NYZ451" target="PE MULCHING FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. NyZ451 is capable of degrading PE mulching films, contributing to their biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-EATING WAXWORMS" target="BACTERIAL STRAINS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut bacterial strains of plastic-eating waxworms are responsible for the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE MULCHING FILMS" target="SOIL POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene mulching films contribute to soil pollution, necessitating biodegradation solutions to mitigate environmental impact</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE MULCHING FILMS" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT CHANGE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Molecular weight change is a key metric used to evaluate the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) mulching films by bacterial strains</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE MULCHING FILMS" target="STRAIN NYZ450">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene mulching films are degraded by strain NyZ450, which utilizes them as a carbon source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE MULCHING FILMS" target="STRAIN NYZ451">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Strain NyZ451 assists in the degradation of polyethylene mulching films, working synergistically with strain NyZ450.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE MULCHING FILMS" target="ACINETOBACTER SP. NYX450">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene mulching films are biodegraded by Acinetobacter sp. strain NyX450 in a co-culture with Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTES" target="CONFUSED FLOUR BEETLE LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Confused flour beetle larvae (Tribolium confusum) can biodegrade plastic wastes, contributing to waste reduction.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTES" target="BIOREMEDIATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic wastes are the target of bioremediation efforts, which use organisms like mealworms to degrade and remove them from the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTES" target="SOIL CONTAMINATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic wastes cause soil contamination by releasing hazardous chemicals, leading to environmental degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTES" target="WATER CONTAMINATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic wastes contribute to water contamination, harming marine life and aquatic ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTES" target="AIR POLLUTION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The incineration of plastic wastes releases air pollutants, contributing to air pollution and health risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC WASTES" target="EXTRUDED POLYSTYRENE (XPS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Extruded polystyrene (XPS) contributes to plastic wastes, adding to environmental pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PS DEGRADATION" target="GUT MICROBIOME SHIFT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A shift in the gut microbiome of mealworms due to polystyrene feeding affects their ability to degrade the plastic.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PS DEGRADATION" target="BIOMASS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biomass is produced during the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, representing a portion of the plastic converted into biological matter.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="ACIDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acids are bioactive compounds identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating their role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="VITAMINS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Vitamins are bioactive compounds identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating their role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="AMIDES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Amides are bioactive compounds identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating their role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="ESTER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ester is a bioactive compound identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating its role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="SQUALENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Squalene is a bioactive compound identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating its role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="CHOLESTEROL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cholesterol is a bioactive compound identified in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating its role in the metabolic pathways involved.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="FCM SUBSTANCES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FCM substances refer to monomers and oligomers that were not detected in the biomass of mealworms after the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS), indicating their absence in the residual material.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="CARBON MASS BALANCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biomass is a byproduct of polystyrene biodegradation, representing a portion of the carbon from consumed EPS.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOMASS" target="VOLUMETRIC LENGTH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Volumetric length is used as a measure of biomass growth in Tenebrio molitor larvae under different feeding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED (FTIR) SPECTROSCOPY" target="FRASS RESIDUES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy is used to analyze chemical modifications in frass residues resulting from polystyrene digestion by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED (FTIR) SPECTROSCOPY" target="PE-MPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR spectroscopy is used to identify the primary functional groups of virgin and residual polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="FTIR ANALYSES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR analyses confirm chemical changes and the formation of new functional groups in the egested frass of mealworms, indicating PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="FTIR AND 1H NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR and 1H NMR analysis of egested frass provide evidence of polyethylene biodegradation by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="CARBONYL AND ALCOHOLIC GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Egested frass from lesser waxworms shows the formation of carbonyl and alcoholic groups, indicating polyethylene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="UNSATURATED HYDROCARBONS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Egested frass contains unsaturated hydrocarbons, which are evidence of polyethylene biodegradation by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="LIQUID-STATE ¹H NMR ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Liquid-state ¹H NMR analysis is conducted on egested frass to study the changes in the end products of PE degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="FOURIER TRANSFORM-NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (FT NMR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FT NMR is used to analyze the chemical composition of egested frass to study the degradation products of PE</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="MASS BALANCE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mass balance is used to measure the weight of PE and WC added, the weight of frass, and the extractable fraction to assess PE degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="ROTARY EVAPORATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rotary evaporation is used to concentrate the residual polymer in the filtered solution of egested frass for further analysis</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EGESTED FRASS" target="LESSER WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Egested frass from lesser waxworms contains residual or modified polyethylene (PE), indicating the extent of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FTIR ANALYSES" target="OXIDATIVE FUNCTIONAL GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FTIR analyses detect the formation of oxidative functional groups in residual polyethylene polymers after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PS CONSUMPTION RATES" target="BRAN:PS RATIOS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Higher bran:PS ratios lead to increased PS consumption rates, showing that mealworms prefer bran and consume more PS when it is supplemented with bran.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LACTOBACILLACEAE" target="INOSITOL PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactobacillaceae bacteria are associated with inositol production in the gut microbiota of mealworms, indicating a role in nitrogen redistribution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LACTOBACILLACEAE" target="POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The relative abundance of Lactobacillaceae decreases in response to harmful additives in polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LEUCONOSTOCACEAE" target="INOSITOL PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Leuconostocaceae bacteria are associated with inositol production in the gut microbiota of mealworms, indicating a role in nitrogen redistribution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INDIAN MEAL MOTH" target="PE-DEGRADING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Indian meal moth houses PE-degrading bacteria in its digestive tract, contributing to the biodegradation of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INDIAN MEAL MOTH" target="PLASTIC PACKAGING">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Indian meal moth can chew and penetrate plastic packaging materials</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="H2O" target="STYRENE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Water (H2O) supports the growth of bacteria and microbiota, which are essential for the aerobic and anaerobic degradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="H2O" target="DIET 2">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Water is included in Diet 2 to assess its impact on the biodegradation of polystyrene and the distribution of oligomers.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="ACETOPHENONE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene can be oxidized to form acetophenone, a side reaction product identified in the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE (SMO)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene monooxygenase (SMO) is involved in the aerobic biodegradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="STYRENE-2,3-DIOXYGENASE (SDO)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene-2,3-dioxygenase (SDO) introduces two oxygen atoms into styrene during the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="VINYLCATECHOL 1,2-DIOXYGENASE (VC12O)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Vinylcatechol 1,2-dioxygenase (VC12O) is involved in the meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol during styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="VINYLCATECHOL 2,3-DIOXYGENASE (VC23O)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Vinylcatechol 2,3-dioxygenase (VC23O) is involved in the meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol during styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="STYRENE OXIDE ISOMERASE (SOI)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) is involved in the conversion of styrene oxide during the biodegradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PHENYLACETALDEHYDE DEHYDROGENASE (PAD)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phenylacetaldehyde dehydrogenase (PAD) is involved in the metabolic pathways of styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="STYRENE OXIDE REDUCTASE (SOR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oxide reductase (SOR) is involved in the reduction of styrene oxide during the biodegradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="ANAEROBIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Anaerobic degradation refers to the breakdown of styrene in the absence of oxygen, leading to the formation of intermediate compounds that can enter the TCA cycle.&lt;SEP&gt;Anaerobic degradation refers to the breakdown of styrene in the absence of oxygen.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="AEROBIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aerobic degradation refers to the breakdown of styrene in the presence of oxygen, leading to the formation of CO2 and H2O.&lt;SEP&gt;Aerobic degradation refers to the breakdown of styrene in the presence of oxygen.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PHENYLACETALDEHYDE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene is degraded to phenylacetaldehyde through the action of styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) during the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="STYRENE OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oligomers are intermediate compounds formed during the degradation of styrene, which can be metabolized by microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="CORYNEBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Corynebacterium species are capable of degrading styrene and its derivatives through metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="RHODOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rhodococcus species are capable of degrading styrene and its derivatives through metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PHENYLACETYL-COA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene is microbially decomposed into phenylacetyl-CoA, which enters the TCA cycle for further degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="KEGG PATHWAY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The KEGG pathway is associated with the degradation of styrene, a chemical related to polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PSEUDOMONAS FLUORESCENTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas fluorescents can grow on styrene as a sole carbon source, indicating its role in styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="XANTHOBACTER SP. STRAIN 124X">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Xanthobacter sp. strain 124X is a bacterium capable of degrading styrene, contributing to the understanding of styrene biodegradation pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="ENTEROHACTER CLOACAE DG-6">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterohacter cloacae DG-6 can convert styrene to CO2 under anaerobic conditions, demonstrating its role in styrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene monooxygenase oxidizes styrene to styrene oxide, playing a key role in styrene biodegradation pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="XANTHOBACTER STRAIN 124X">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Xanthobacter strain 124X degrades styrene oxide and 2-phenylethanol, which are intermediates in the biodegradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PSEUDOMONAS FLUORESCENS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas fluorescens catabolizes styrene, contributing to the microbial degradation of plastic-related compounds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PS-OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene is a precursor to PS-oligomers, which are analyzed in the study as part of the biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PHENYLACETATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene is oxidized to phenylacetate by microorganisms, indicating the degradation of polystyrene (PS)</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="CITRIC ACID CYCLE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The citric acid cycle is involved in the oxidation of styrene to phenylacetate, contributing to the degradation of polystyrene (PS)</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PSEUDOMONAS SP. STRAIN Y2">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas sp. strain Y2 can catabolize styrene, aiding in the biodegradation of styrene-based plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="STYRENE OXIDE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene can metabolize into styrene oxide, a compound with carcinogenic properties.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="PSEUDOMONAS SP. SR-5">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas sp. SR-5 degrades styrene in biofilters, contributing to the reduction of styrene pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="NAPHTHALENE DIOXYGENASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Naphthalene dioxygenase catalyzes the dihydroxylation of the styrene vinyl group, aiding in styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="BIOFILTERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biofilters are used in research to study the degradation of styrene by bacterial strains like Pseudomonas sp. SR-5.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE" target="SYSTEMATIC AND APPLIED MICROBIOLOGY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Systematic and Applied Microbiology publishes studies on the catabolism of styrene by bacterial strains like Pseudomonas fluorescens.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ERWINIA OLEAE" target="ERWINIA PSIDII">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Erwinia oleae and Erwinia psidii are both plant pathogens studied for their genetic and biochemical traits.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-014b72d0a2657cbc89c4dee5f2b32bba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LACTOBACILLUS" target="PE + BRAN DIET">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactobacillus is significantly associated with the PE + bran diet, indicating its potential role in plastic degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLOSTRIDIUM" target="SHORT-CHAIN FATTY ACIDS (SCFAS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Clostridium bacteria produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which play a role in intestinal homeostasis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLOSTRIDIUM" target="POLYETHYLENE MICROPLASTICS (PE-MPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Clostridium is a genus of bacteria contributing to the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics in the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FUNGI" target="DANDORA DUMPSITE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Dandora dumpsite is a location where fungi are studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FUNGI" target="MUHONJA ET AL., 2018">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Muhonja et al., 2018 examines the biodegradability of polyethylene by fungi from the Dandora dumpsite.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1A-TETRALIN" target="1E-PHENYL-4-(1-PHENYLETHYL)-1,2,3,4-TETRAHYDRONAPHTHALENE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">1e-phenyl-4-(1-phenylethyl)-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene is the full chemical name of 1a-tetralin, a compound identified in the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENTEROCOCCUS FAECIUM" target="DIET 2">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterococcus faecium showed significant growth in Diet 2, associated with higher survival rates of mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2,4-DI-TERT BUTYL PHENOL" target="IRGAFOS 168">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Irgafos 168 degrades into 2,4-di-tert butyl phenol, a chemical identified in the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IRGAFOS 168" target="2,4-DTBP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Irgafos 168 degrades into 2,4-DTBP, which is detected in frass during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIET 2" target="ANOVA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">ANOVA is used to statistically compare the effects of Diet 2, which includes additional water, on PS degradation and larvae growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIET 2" target="MICROBIOME">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The microbiome in the gut of insects varies with Diet 2, leading to enhanced polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIET 3" target="TUKEY COMPARISON TEST">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Tukey comparison test is used to confirm the significance of differences in larvae weight between Diet 3 and other diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIET 1" target="BARLEY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Barley is a key component of Diet 1, used in feeding experiments with Tenebrio molitor larvae to study polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHENYLACETALDEHYDE" target="PHENYLACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phenylacetaldehyde is converted to phenylacetic acid by phenylacetaldehyde dehydrogenase (PAD), which can then enter the TCA cycle.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHENYLACETALDEHYDE" target="STYRENE OXIDE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oxide is isomerized to phenylacetaldehyde by styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) during styrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oxide is isomerized to phenylacetaldehyde by styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) during the degradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHENYLACETALDEHYDE" target="PHENYLETHANOL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phenylacetaldehyde is in equilibrium with phenylethanol through the action of phenylacetaldehyde reductase (PAR) during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE OXIDE" target="PHENYLETHANOL">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oxide can be hydrolyzed to phenylethanol-1,2-diol, which is then oxidized to mandelic acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene oxide is reduced to phenylethanol by styrene oxide reductase (SOR) during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE OXIDE" target="PHENYLETHANOL-1,2-DIOL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oxide is hydrolyzed to phenylethanol-1,2-diol by epoxide hydrolase (EH) during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE OXIDE" target="STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oxide is produced by the oxidation of styrene by styrene monooxygenase, indicating its role in biodegradation pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE OXIDE" target="XANTHOBACTER STRAIN 124X">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Xanthobacter strain 124X metabolizes styrene oxide, a key step in the degradation of styrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYRENE OXIDE" target="2-PHENYLETHANOL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrene oxide is metabolized into 2-phenylethanol by microorganisms like Xanthobacter strain 124X during styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIOME" target="GREATER WAX MOTH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The microbiome in the gut of greater wax moth larvae may play a role in the degradation of LDPE, although further research is needed to confirm this.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIOME" target="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The microbiome of mealworms is crucial for their ability to metabolize synthetic polymers and lignocellulose</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIOME" target="BACTEROIDALES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacteroidales is a dominant order of bacteria in the larvae's microbiome, contributing to digestion and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIOME" target="ACTINOMYCETALES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Actinomycetales is a bacterial order in the larvae's microbiome, playing a role in digestion and biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
