    </edge>
    <edge source="CORN FLOUR" target="T. OBSCURUS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Corn flour is used as a codiet to evaluate its impact on the survival and PS consumption rates of T. obscurus larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EPIGENETIC MODIFICATION" target="PU">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Epigenetic modification refers to changes in gene expression caused by polyurethane (PU) as a sole source of feed for mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA" target="RHAMNOLIPID">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas aeruginosa produces rhamnolipid, a biosurfactant that enhances hydrocarbon degradation, including polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA" target="RHAMNOLIPID BIOSURFACTANTS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas aeruginosa produces rhamnolipid biosurfactants, which are used to enhance the biodegradation of hydrocarbons in contaminated soils.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALUMINUM" target="FILLERS">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aluminum is added to plastics as stabilizers or fillers, such as mica or kaolin, to enhance material properties.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HAFNIUM" target="CATALYSTS">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hafnium is a trace element in plastics, likely due to contamination from catalysts used in polyolefin production.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FILLERS AND FLAME RETARDANTS (FRS)" target="PLASTIC WASTE UTILIZATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fillers and flame retardants (FRs) in plastic waste may influence the consumption rates and biodegradation efficiency by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENTOMOREMEDIATION OPTIMIZATION" target="PLASTIC WASTE UTILIZATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Entomoremediation optimization aims to improve the efficiency of plastic waste utilization by mealworms through strategies like separating larvae and adults.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THERMOPHILES" target="POLY(L-LACTIC ACID)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermophiles are capable of degrading poly(L-lactic acid) under high-temperature conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY LARVAE" target="ORGANIC WASTE STREAMS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black soldier fly larvae are reared on organic waste streams, impacting their microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY LARVAE" target="MODULARITY OF INSECT PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Modularity of insect production studies scalable methods for rearing black soldier fly larvae for waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM FRASS" target="CROP GROWTH">
      <data key="d3">21.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm frass can be used as a fertilizer, positively impacting crop growth and soil properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm frass is investigated for its potential use as a fertilizer and its impact on crop growth and soil properties.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM FRASS" target="HYLOCEREUS UNDATUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm frass is used as a medium to study its effects on the growth of Hylocereus undatus.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM FRASS" target="SUPERWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Superworm frass is more effective than mealworm frass in supporting plant growth due to its superior moisture retention and growth-promoting compounds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM FRASS" target="AMMONIA-SMELLING MEALWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm frass has a pungent ammonia smell, making it less suitable for indoor use compared to superworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM FRASS" target="PLANT GROWTH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm frass enhances plant growth by improving soil properties and providing essential nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM FRASS" target="THERMO-GRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm frass is subjected to thermo-gravimetric analysis to study the thermal degradation of polystyrene, linking the frass to the degradation process</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO MOLITOR PROTEIN" target="PHYSIOLOGICAL PROCESSES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio molitor protein contains peptides that can modulate physiological processes, making it a valuable alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SEWAGE SLUDGE" target="BIODIESEL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sewage sludge can be converted into biodiesel, offering a sustainable solution for waste management and energy production.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SEWAGE SLUDGE" target="WASTEWATER TREATMENT FACILITIES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wastewater treatment facilities process sewage sludge, which can be further converted into biodiesel.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SEWAGE SLUDGE" target="HYPERTHERMOPHILIC COMPOSTING">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hyperthermophilic composting accelerates the removal of pollutants like antibiotic resistance genes from sewage sludge.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SEWAGE SLUDGE" target="HYPERTHERMOPHILIC COMPOSTING TECHNOLOGY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hyperthermophilic composting technology is used to enhance the biodegradation of microplastics in sewage sludge.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SEWAGE SLUDGE" target="PYROLYSIS TEMPERATURE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The efficiency of microplastic mitigation in sewage sludge depends on the pyrolysis temperature.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY" target="BLACK SOLDIER FLY FRASS FERTILIZER">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black soldier fly larvae produce frass fertilizer, which is used as an organic fertilizer for crops.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY" target="HIGH FIBER FOOD WASTE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black soldier fly larvae can be cultivated using high fiber food waste, providing a sustainable waste management solution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY" target="EXPANDED POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black Soldier Fly larvae show metabolic responses to expanded polyethylene, contributing to plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY" target="PYRIMIDINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black Soldier Fly larvae show elevated pyrimidine metabolism when exposed to PET, indicating a unique metabolic response.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY" target="PURINE METABOLISM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black Soldier Fly larvae exhibit strong purine metabolism when exposed to certain plastics, indicating a metabolic adaptation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY" target="GUT-SYMBIOT BREAKDOWN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black Soldier Fly larvae experience gut-symbiont breakdown when exposed to plastics, affecting their metabolic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLACK SOLDIER FLY" target="WASTE MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Black Soldier Fly larvae are used in waste management systems to process organic waste, including plastic-contaminated materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </edge>
