      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADABLE PLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradable plastics are designed to improve environmental performance by breaking down more easily.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADABLE PLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL SUSTAINABILITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradable plastics are a focus of environmental sustainability research, aiming to reduce the impact of plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC POLLUTION" target="PETROLEUM-BASED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Petroleum-based plastics are a major source of plastic pollution due to their widespread use and resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC POLLUTION" target="THERMOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thermoplastics, including polystyrene, are a major contributor to plastic pollution due to their widespread use and resistance to degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC POLLUTION" target="PRIMARY MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Primary microplastics are a significant source of plastic pollution, particularly in marine environments.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC POLLUTION" target="MARINE DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic pollution poses a significant risk to marine diversity, affecting ecosystems and biodiversity.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC POLLUTION" target="SCIENCE ADVANCES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Science Advances publishes research on the production, use, and fate of plastics, contributing to understanding plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC POLLUTION" target="SECONDARY MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Secondary microplastics are a significant component of plastic pollution, formed through the fragmentation of larger plastic debris.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC POLLUTION" target="LAURENTIAN GREAT LAKES">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Laurentian Great Lakes are heavily impacted by microplastic pollution, highlighting the issue's global scale.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC POLLUTION" target="MARINE ECOSYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Marine ecosystems are significantly affected by microplastic pollution, which disrupts marine life and food chains.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC POLLUTION" target="SOUTHERN OCEAN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastic pollution has been detected in the Southern Ocean, indicating its widespread environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC POLLUTION" target="TERRESTRIAL FOOD CHAIN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastic pollution can transfer through the terrestrial food chain, affecting organisms at various levels.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC POLLUTION" target="ACARTIA TONSA">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Acartia tonsa is affected by microplastic pollution, which exacerbates the toxicity of other contaminants like triclosan.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING FUNGI" target="LANDFILL SOIL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic-degrading fungi, such as Trichoderma viride and Aspergillus nigerius, were isolated from landfill soil, indicating their role in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIODEGRADATION RESEARCH" target="ENZYMATIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradation research investigates the role of enzymatic degradation in breaking down organic and synthetic materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="VTT TECHNICAL RESEARCH CENTRE OF FINLAND" target="ASH-FORMING ELEMENTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">VTT Technical Research Centre of Finland conducts research on ash-forming elements in plastics and rubbers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FLUORENYL COMPLEXES" target="OLEFIN POLYMERIZATION">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fluorenyl complexes are used as catalysts in olefin polymerization, a chemical process.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DRAGON FRUIT CACTI" target="SUPERWORM FRASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dragon fruit cacti showed superior growth when supported by superworm frass compared to mealworm frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUCROSE" target="AMMONIUM SULFATE">
