      <data key="d6">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2-PHENYLETHANOL" target="LG3">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LG3 produces 2-phenylethanol as an intermediate during the anaerobic biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating shared pathways with styrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROBIAL CONSORTIA" target="POLYVINYL ALCOHOL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyvinyl alcohol can be utilized by mixed microbial cultures for biodegradation, demonstrating its potential for environmental cleanup.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHOTODEGRADED POLYMERS" target="ECOLYTE PS POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ecolyte PS polystyrene is a type of photodegraded polymer studied for its biodegradability and environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STANFORD UNIVERSITY" target="CRAIG S. CRIDDLE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Craig S. Criddle is affiliated with Stanford University, where he provided support and suggestions for the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENERGY RECOVERY" target="RESIDUES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Energy recovery refers to the incineration of residues from plastic degradation by larvae, with the potential to recover energy.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </edge>
