      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SOIL" target="POLYSTYRENE-GRAFT-STARCH COPOLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polystyrene-graft-starch copolymers are studied for their biodegradation in different types of soil, highlighting their environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SILVA 16S RRNA DATABASE" target="OPERATIONAL TAXONOMIC UNITS (OTUS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Silva 16S rRNA database is used to classify OTUs and analyze microbial taxonomy.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BACILLUS SP." target="LIGNOCELLULOSE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. is a key microorganism involved in the degradation of lignocellulose, a natural polymer.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BACILLUS SP." target="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. is involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene, particularly when the plastic is pre-treated with UV light.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BACILLUS SP." target="POLYPROPYLENE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bacillus sp. is capable of biodegrading polypropylene microplastics, contributing to their breakdown in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MORGANELLA" target="DISPOSABLE SURGICAL MASKS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Morganella showed increased relative abundance in the gut microbial community of yellow mealworms after feeding on disposable surgical masks, indicating its potential role in mask degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIMITED EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION" target="Z. ATRATUS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Z. atratus larvae show limited extent depolymerization patterns during the biodegradation of LDPE and PP.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="LIGNIN PEROXIDASE (LIP)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lignin peroxidase is an enzyme produced by fungi to degrade lignin, which may also degrade synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="MANGANESE PEROXIDASE (MN)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Manganese peroxidase is involved in lignin degradation and may also play a role in breaking down synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="LACCASE (LAC)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Laccase is an enzyme that degrades lignin and may also be effective in degrading synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="LIGNOCELLULOSE (CS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lignocellulose (CS) and lignin share overlapping metabolic pathways in their degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="LONG-CHAIN FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lignin degradation produces long-chain fatty acids as intermediates, which are further metabolized for energy.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Peroxidase facilitates the degradation of lignin by oxidizing aromatic rings and cleaving carbon-carbon bonds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="LAC-PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lac-peroxidase facilitates the degradation of lignin by oxidizing aromatic rings and cleaving carbon-carbon bonds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="MN-PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mn-peroxidase facilitates the degradation of lignin by oxidizing aromatic rings and cleaving carbon-carbon bonds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="LIP-PEROXIDASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lip-peroxidase facilitates the degradation of lignin by oxidizing aromatic rings and cleaving carbon-carbon bonds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="LACCASE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Laccase is an enzyme that degrades lignin, a complex organic polymer, into simpler compounds for high-value chemical production.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="LACZ1">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">LacZ1 is a laccase enzyme that degrades lignin, contributing to the breakdown of complex organic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="MANGANESE SUPEROXIDE DISMUTASE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Manganese superoxide dismutase is involved in lignin oxidation, aiding in the breakdown of complex organic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="PLASTIC POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lignin is co-lysed with plastic polymers, as evaluated using TG-FTIR and py-GC/MS techniques, to understand degradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGNIN" target="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lignin has been used in grafting strategies to improve the biodegradability of polypropylene materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEMICELLULOSE" target="FAEB">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">FAEB is involved in the degradation of hemicellulose, converting it into glucose or xylose during lignocellulose degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LUMBRICUS TERRESTRIS" target="BIODEGRADABLE MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lumbricus terrestris fragments biodegradable microplastics such as polylactic acid (PLA) and poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) into smaller particles, though significant biodegradation is not observed.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LUMBRICUS TERRESTRIS" target="POLY(BUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE) (PBAT)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lumbricus terrestris fragments poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) microplastics into smaller particles, though significant biodegradation is not observed.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHOTO DEGRADED POLYMERS" target="RESEARCH STUDY ON BIODEGRADABILITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">This research study develops experimental procedures to assess the biodegradability of photo-degraded polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CITROBACTER FREUNDII" target="HYDROCARBON DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Citrobacter freundii is known for degrading long-chain polymers and hydrocarbons, contributing to polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CITROBACTER FREUNDII" target="ENZYMATIC DEGRADATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Citrobacter freundii produces enzymes that contribute to the enzymatic degradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPANDED PS FOAM" target="CARLISLE CONSTRUCTION MATERIALS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Carlisle Construction Materials supplied the expanded PS foam used as a feed for mealworms in the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPERNATANT" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT SPIN COLUMNS">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Molecular weight spin columns are used to fractionate supernatant samples, aiding in the study of biodegradation mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight spin columns are used to fractionate the supernatant into different molecular weight ranges for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
