    </edge>
    <edge source="GREATER WAX MOTH" target="CASSONE ET AL.">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cassone et al. identified Acinetobacter as a key genus in the gut microbiome of greater wax moth larvae involved in polyethylene biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GREATER WAX MOTH" target="PLASTIC">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Greater Wax Moth larvae, known for consuming bee wax, may have an advantage in metabolizing plastics due to their chemical composition.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a</data>
    </edge>
