    </edge>
    <edge source="BRICKWORK DEBRIS" target="CEMENT MORTAR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cement mortar is a major contaminant in brickwork debris, complicating recycling efforts.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRICKWORK BUILDINGS" target="CONSTRUCTION AND DEMOLITION WASTE (CWD)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Brickwork buildings generate significant amounts of construction and demolition waste (CWD), which includes mixed debris.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CONSTRUCTION AND DEMOLITION WASTE (CWD)" target="WASTE FRAMEWORK DIRECTIVE 2008/98/EC">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Waste Framework Directive 2008/98/EC regulates the management of construction and demolition waste (CWD) in the European Union.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERAMIC BRICKS" target="RECYCLED LIGHTWEIGHT AGGREGATE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ceramic bricks can be transformed into recycled lightweight aggregate for use in new concrete production.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERAMIC BRICKS" target="INTERNAL CURING PROCESS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The internal curing process utilizes crushed red ceramics (ceramic bricks) as waste aggregate in concrete production.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERAMIC BRICKS" target="CEMENT MORTAR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ceramic bricks were bonded to EPS using cement mortar, which was later removed by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CEMENT MORTAR" target="MICRO-FILLERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Micro-fillers can be derived from recycled cement mortar, used to partially substitute cement in construction.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WHITE EPS" target="LARVAE MORTALITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larvae mortality was higher when Tenebrio molitor larvae consumed white EPS compared to grey EPS, indicating a potential toxicological effect.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WHITE EPS" target="GREY EPS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">White EPS and grey EPS differ in material density and chemical composition, affecting the consumption rate and frass production by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FRASS MASS" target="CHEMICAL COMPOSITION OF FRASS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Frass mass contains specific chemical elements derived from the biodegradation of EPS by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RED CERAMIC WASTE" target="CONCRETE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Red ceramic waste is used as a replacement for coarse aggregate in concrete, contributing to sustainable construction practices.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ETHICS COMMITTEE" target="ANIMAL STUDY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Ethics Committee reviews and approves animal study protocols to ensure ethical research practices.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIGHTWEIGHT SFRC" target="WASTE MATERIALS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lightweight SFRC incorporates waste materials to improve sustainability and performance.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BLENDED CEMENT" target="WASTE PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Blended cement combines traditional cement with waste products to enhance sustainability in construction.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC PACKAGING" target="RHIZOPERTHA DOMINICA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rhizopertha dominica is studied for its interaction with plastic packaging, which is relevant to pest management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC PACKAGING" target="HONEY COMB WAX WORM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Honey comb wax worm is capable of chewing and eating plastic materials</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC PACKAGING" target="PEST MOTHS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pest moths have been observed to chew and damage plastic packaging materials, contributing to biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COMMON WHEAT" target="MEALWORM LARVAE MEAL">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Common wheat can be fertilized with mealworm larvae meal, demonstrating its use as an alternative protein source in agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)" target="CARDBOARD (CEL)">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can digest cardboard, a lignocellulose-based diet, though it is less beneficial for their development compared to synthetic polymers</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)" target="OXO-DEGRADABLE POLYETHYLENE (PE-OXO)">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms can consume oxo-degradable polyethylene, though it is less beneficial for their development compared to regranulated polyethylene</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)" target="POLYETHYLENE REGRANULATE (PE-REG)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene regranulate is the most beneficial diet for mealworm development, allowing them to transition quickly through developmental stages</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)" target="NITROGEN CYCLE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The diet of mealworms may influence the nitrogen cycle, affecting their metabolism and microbiome</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)" target="ANIMAL WELFARE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The welfare of mealworms is a consideration when feeding them waste-based diets, ensuring ethical breeding conditions</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)" target="WASTE BIOCONVERSION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms contribute to waste bioconversion by metabolizing synthetic polymers and lignocellulose into simpler compounds</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)" target="MICROBIOME MODIFICATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Different diets lead to microbiome modification in mealworms, impacting their ability to metabolize waste materials</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR L.)" target="BIOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The biochemical properties of mealworms' digestive systems are influenced by their diet, affecting their metabolism and waste processing</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROTEIN CONTENT ANALYSIS" target="BRADFORD METHOD">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Bradford method is used to determine the protein content in mealworm larvae grown on different substrates.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460</data>
    </edge>
