      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LACCASE ACTIVITY" target="ABTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">ABTS is used as a substrate to measure laccase activity through spectrophotometry.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LACCASE ACTIVITY" target="SODIUM ACETATE BUFFER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sodium acetate buffer is used in the reaction mixture for measuring laccase activity.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ABTS" target="LAC640">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lac640 enzyme activity is assayed using ABTS as a substrate to measure its oxidation at 420 nm.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PU FOAM" target="TOXIC COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PU foam degradation produces toxic compounds that mealworms must metabolize, affecting their growth and weight.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PU FOAM" target="C-O-C BOND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The C-O-C bond in PU foam shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PU FOAM" target="C-N BOND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The C-N bond in PU foam shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PU FOAM" target="N-H BOND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The N-H bond in PU foam shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PU FOAM" target="C=O BOND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The C=O bond in PU foam shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PU FOAM" target="CH2 BOND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The CH2 bond in PU foam shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PU FOAM" target="OH BOND">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The OH bond in PU foam shows reduced intensity after biodegradation by mealworms, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PU FOAM" target="CH BONDS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The CH bonds in PU foam show increased intensity in PU-virgin compared to degraded PU, as observed in FTIR analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM METABOLISM" target="PLASTIC CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm metabolism affects the rate and efficiency of plastic consumption and degradation, influencing mass and viability.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM METABOLISM" target="CHEMICAL STRUCTURE OF PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The chemical structure of plastics, such as HDPE and PVC, influences how mealworms metabolize and digest them.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSEUDOMONAS CHLORORAPHIS" target="POLYESTER POLYURETHANASE GENE (PUEA)">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas chlororaphis produces the PueA gene, which encodes a lipase enzyme involved in polyurethane degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSEUDOMONAS CHLORORAPHIS" target="PUEA GENE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PueA gene in Pseudomonas chlororaphis encodes a lipase that facilitates the degradation of polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSEUDOMONAS CHLORORAPHIS" target="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudomonas chlororaphis has been reported to contribute to the biodegradation of isotactic polypropylene in selective enrichment cultures.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PUEA GENE" target="POLYURETHANASE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PueA gene encodes polyurethanase, an enzyme that facilitates the breakdown of polyurethane.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NUTRITIONAL COMPOSITION" target="EDIBLE COLEOPTERA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Edible Coleoptera, including Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas morio, are studied for their nutritional composition.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="KJELDAHL METHOD" target="SELENIUM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Selenium is used in the mineralization phase of the Kjeldahl method to aid in the breakdown of organic material.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="KJELDAHL METHOD" target="SULFURIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sulfuric acid is used in the mineralization phase of the Kjeldahl method to break down organic material.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="KJELDAHL METHOD" target="SODIUM HYDROXIDE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sodium hydroxide is used in the distillation phase of the Kjeldahl method to neutralize acids and release ammonia.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="KJELDAHL METHOD" target="BORIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Boric acid is used in the distillation phase of the Kjeldahl method to capture ammonia for nitrogen determination.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="KJELDAHL METHOD" target="TASHIRO INDICATOR">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tashiro indicator is used in the titration phase of the Kjeldahl method to determine the endpoint of the reaction.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GRASS CARP" target="DIETARY PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Grass carp are studied for their dietary protein requirements, with research focusing on serum biochemistry and liver histology.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIGHEAD CARP" target="DIETARY PROTEIN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bighead carp are studied for their response to different dietary protein levels, with research focusing on serum biochemistry and liver histology.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANTIOXIDANT ENZYME ACTIVITIES" target="OXIDATIVE STRESS RESPONSES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antioxidant enzyme activities are part of the oxidative stress responses observed in mealworms fed with microplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE" target="HYDROPHOBIC PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrophobic plastics, such as PS and PVC, are less degradable and cause dehydration and growth inhibition in plastic-degrading larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE" target="HYDROLYSIS REACTIONS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrolysis reactions in the gut of plastic-degrading larvae break down complex plastic polymers into smaller molecules.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE" target="CHLORINATED ORGANICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chlorinated organics are byproducts of incomplete PVC biodegradation in plastic-degrading larvae, contributing to malnutrition.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE" target="NUTRIENT CONTENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nutrient content in plastic-degrading larvae is affected by their diet and exposure to different types of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE" target="POLYMER DEGRADABILITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polymer degradability influences the efficiency of plastic biodegradation by plastic-degrading larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE" target="INTERMEDIATE TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Intermediate toxicity of plastics, such as PVC, negatively impacts the growth and health of plastic-degrading larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE" target="CO-DIET BRAN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Co-diet bran alleviates the negative impacts of microplastics on plastic-degrading larvae, maintaining their nutrient levels and physiological performance.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CO-DIET BRAN" target="PP CONSUMPTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Co-diet bran enhances PP consumption rates in Z. atratus and T. molitor larvae, improving the efficiency of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ROS" target="BRAN-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bran-fed larvae were used as a control group to measure baseline ROS levels for comparison with larvae fed with plastic mixtures.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ROS" target="PVC + BRAN-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PVC + bran-fed larvae were used to assess the impact of PVC on ROS levels, showing similar ROS levels to bran-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ROS" target="PS + BRAN-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS + bran-fed larvae were used to evaluate the effects of PS on ROS levels, showing similar ROS levels to bran-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ROS" target="PLA + BRAN-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PLA + bran-fed larvae were used to assess the impact of PLA on ROS levels, showing similar ROS levels to bran-fed larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ROS" target="FLUORESCENCE INTENSITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fluorescence intensity was used as a measure to quantify ROS levels in larvae and earthworms exposed to different plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DANIO RERIO" target="DI(2-ETHYLHEXYL) PHTHALATE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zebrafish are used to study the combined toxic effects of microplastics and di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DI(2-ETHYLHEXYL) PHTHALATE" target="ACHROMOBACTER DENITRIFICANS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Achromobacter denitrificans strain SP1 efficiently remediates di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate, a plasticizer.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LAC640" target="CORN STOVER (CS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lac640 is a gene associated with the degradation of corn stover in Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LAC640" target="REAL-TIME POLYMERASE CHAIN REACTION (RT-PCR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">RT-PCR is used to verify the expression of the lac640 gene in Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with plastic and corn stover degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LAC640" target="E. COLI">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">E. coli is used to overexpress the lac640 gene to determine its ability to degrade polystyrene and corn stover.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LAC640" target="LIGNOCELLULOSE (CS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lac640 enzyme plays a role in the degradation of lignocellulose (CS), as demonstrated by its activity and impact on CS samples.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="E. COLI" target="NANO-POLYSTYRENE (NPS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">E. coli is used as a negative control to study the effects of nano-polystyrene on biofilm formation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="E. COLI" target="POLYSTYRENE FILM">
      <data key="d3">5.0</data>
      <data key="d4">E. coli is used as a negative control and shows less adhesion to polystyrene film, indicating limited biodegradation potential.</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LACCASE (LAC640)" target="E. COLI ROSETTA (DE3)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">E. coli Rosetta (DE3) is used to express and purify the Lac640 enzyme for functional assays related to PS degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METATRANSCRIPTOME ANALYSIS" target="RT-PCR VALIDATION">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">RT-PCR validation is used to confirm the expression of DEGs identified through metatranscriptome analysis, ensuring the reliability of the findings.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EDGER" target="DIFFERENTIAL EXPRESSION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">edgeR is used for differential expression analysis in genomics research.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RT-PCR" target="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">RT-PCR is used to detect mRNA and confirm the expression of nifH genes in nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RT-PCR" target="NIFH-SPECIFIC PRIMER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">NifH-specific primers were used in RT-PCR to amplify and detect the expression of the nifH gene in mealworm gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CORN STRAW (CS)" target="GUT MICROBIAL COLONIES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gut microbial colonies are involved in the degradation of corn straw (CS), though their role is less understood</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GUT MICROBIAL COLONIES" target="RT-QPCR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">RT-qPCR is used to quantify the copy number of gut microbes in larvae, assessing their activity</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MW (MOLECULAR WEIGHT)" target="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS (PS MPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The molecular weight of PS MPs affects their biodegradation rate, with lower MW PS MPs being more easily digested by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CELLULOSE (CS)" target="ANTIBIOTIC-TREATED LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antibiotic-treated larvae show slight effects on cellulose degradation, suggesting less dependency on gut microbiota.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CELLULOSE (CS)" target="SEM IMAGES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">SEM images show surface changes in cellulose after digestion by mealworms, indicating its breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CELLULOSE (CS)" target="EICOSANE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Eicosane decreases in abundance during the degradation of cellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CELLULOSE (CS)" target="CYCLOHEXANE">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cyclohexane decreases in abundance during the degradation of cellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CELLULOSE (CS)" target="PHENOL DERIVATIVES">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phenol derivatives decrease in abundance during the degradation of cellulose by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CELLULOSE (CS)" target="CS-FED LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">CS-fed larvae show similar gut microbial changes to PS-fed larvae, suggesting that cellulose also influences the gut microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METABOLOMIC ANALYSIS" target="METABOLOME">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Metabolomic analysis is used to study the reprogramming of the metabolome in mealworms during the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
