      <data key="d4">Nitrogen-fixing bacteria exhibit nitrogen-fixing activity, converting atmospheric nitrogen into a usable form for mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA" target="ACETYLENE-REDUCING ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrogen-fixing bacteria demonstrate acetylene-reducing activity, indicating their ability to fix nitrogen</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA" target="ANTIBIOTIC-TREATED MEALWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Antibiotic-treated mealworms have suppressed gut microbiota, leading to reduced nitrogen-fixing activity</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA" target="NITROGEN-FREE MEDIUM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrogen-fixing bacteria were isolated and cultivated using nitrogen-free medium</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA" target="WOOD-EATING TERMITES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wood-eating termites share nitrogen-fixing gut bacteria with mealworms, suggesting a common mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA" target="FRUIT FLY CERATITIS CAPITATA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The fruit fly Ceratitis capitata shares nitrogen-fixing gut bacteria with mealworms, indicating a broader ecological pattern.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA" target="RED TURPENTINE BEETLE DENDROCTONUS VALENS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The red turpentine beetle Dendroctonus valens shares nitrogen-fixing gut bacteria with mealworms, supporting shared mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GREEN CONCRETE" target="WASTE TIRE RUBBER">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Waste tire rubber is recycled and used in the production of green concrete, offering an eco-friendly solution for construction materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANDIDA ANTARCTICA LIPASE (CALB)" target="PET HYDROLYSIS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Candida antarctica lipase (CALB) works synergistically with cutinase to hydrolyze PET into terephthalic acid, aiding in plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RATTUS NORVEGICUS" target="ECOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rattus norvegicus was used to assess the ecotoxicity of polyethylene degradation products, confirming their low environmental impact.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHOTO-OXIDATION" target="PRO-OXIDANT ADDITIVES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pro-oxidant additives enhance the photo-oxidation process in polyethylene films, facilitating their biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CORBICULA FLUMINEA" target="NANOPLASTIC-INDUCED GENOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nanoplastic-induced genotoxicity is studied in freshwater benthic clams to understand the DNA damage caused by nanoplastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE-MPS" target="FENTON SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Fenton system is used to extract polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) from the frass of Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE-MPS" target="OLIVE OIL-BASED EXTRACTION METHOD">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The olive oil-based extraction method is used to extract polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) from the frass of Tenebrio larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE-MPS" target="CARBONYL INDEX (CI)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Carbonyl Index (CI) is used to evaluate the oxidation of polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) by measuring the intensity of the carbonyl peak.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE-MPS" target="PYROLYSIS-GAS CHROMATOGRAPHY/MASS SPECTROMETRY (PY-GC/MS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Py-GC/MS is used to analyze the degradation products of polyethylene microplastics (PE-MPs) during biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE-MPS" target="NICHE WIDTH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Feeding mealworms polyethylene (PE) diets increases the niche width of their gut microbiome, enhancing diversity.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE-MPS" target="FUNCTIONAL REDUNDANCY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Functional redundancy in the gut microbiome decreases when mealworms are fed polyethylene (PE) diets, improving resource utilization.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE-MPS" target="TENEBRIO LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tenebrio larvae can ingest and degrade PE-MPs, with their gut microbiota adapting to enhance carbon and nitrogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SINOPHARM CHEMICAL REAGENT CO., LTD." target="ANALYTICAL GRADE CHEMICALS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. supplies the analytical grade chemicals used in the biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916</data>
    </edge>
