    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO SPECIES" target="WB DIET">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The wheat bran (WB) diet is used as a control to compare the effects of polyethylene microplastics on the gut microbiomes of Tenebrio species.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WB DIET" target="TENEBRIO LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The WB diet provides nitrogen nutrition for Tenebrio larvae, contrasting with the nitrogen-deficient PE diet.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TENEBRIO LARVAE" target="OXIDATIVE STRESS INDICES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oxidative stress indices are investigated in Tenebrio larvae after continuous feeding with PE-MPs, indicating potential ecotoxicity.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T. OBSCURUS" target="PE POLYMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">T. obscurus larvae exhibit enhanced carbon and nitrogen metabolism to degrade PE polymers, with richer metabolic functions compared to T. molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TERMITE HINDGUT" target="PHOSPHOTRANSFERASE SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The termite hindgut contains a phosphotransferase system, which aids in sugar transport and digestion.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TERMITE HINDGUT" target="GLUCOSIDASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The termite hindgut microbiome includes glucosidase, an enzyme that breaks down complex sugars.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENVIRONMENTAL PERFORMANCE" target="BIO-BASED PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bio-based plastics are studied for their environmental performance and potential to reduce pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENVIRONMENTAL PERFORMANCE" target="BIO-BASED AND BIODEGRADABLE PLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bio-based and biodegradable plastics are studied for their environmental performance, aiming to reduce plastic pollution compared to traditional plastics.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC PARTICLES" target="NEMATODE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microplastic particles adversely affect the intestinal health and biological functions of nematodes.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC PARTICLES" target="ZEBRAFISH DANIO RERIO">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Zebrafish are used to study the intestinal damage and adverse effects caused by microplastic particles.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTIC PARTICLES" target="CAENORHABDITIS ELEGANS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Caenorhabditis elegans is used to assess the adverse effects of microplastic particles on intestinal health and biological functions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYPERTHERMOPHILIC COMPOSTING" target="ANTIBIOTIC RESISTANCE GENES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hyperthermophilic composting accelerates the removal of antibiotic resistance genes from sewage sludge.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AGAVE TEQUILANA" target="ENDOPHYTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Agave tequilana hosts endophytic bacteria that promote plant growth, highlighting its ecological importance.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LINEAR POLYOLEFINS" target="NATURAL WEATHERING">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Linear polyolefins are studied for their biodegradation under natural weathering conditions.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CAENORHABDITIS ELEGANS" target="IMIDAZOLIUM-BASED IONIC LIQUIDS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Imidazolium-based ionic liquids have toxic effects on Caenorhabditis elegans, primarily due to the generation of reactive oxygen species.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LEACHATE" target="CERIODAPHNIA DUBIA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ceriodaphnia dubia is used to assess the toxicity of leachate from expanded polystyrene cups, which can harm aquatic organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LEACHATE" target="TOXICITY ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Toxicity assessments are conducted to evaluate the harmful effects of leachate from expanded polystyrene on aquatic organisms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22</data>
    </edge>
