      <data key="d6">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICRO-POLYETHYLENE PARTICLES" target="MIXED MICROBIAL CONSORTIUM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A mixed microbial consortium isolated from a landfill site can biodegrade micro-polyethylene particles through bacterial colonization.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORMS AND WAX MOTH LARVAE" target="BIODEGRADATION OF MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms and wax moth larvae are studied for their ability to biodegrade microplastics, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MUCISPIRILLUM" target="PE + BRAN DIET">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mucispirillum is significantly associated with the PE + bran diet, suggesting its involvement in plastic degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="WC (WAX COMB)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lesser waxworms are studied for their growth and survivability when fed on polyethylene (PE) compared to their conventional diet (WC).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="GROWTH AND SURVIVABILITY STUDY">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The growth and survivability of lesser waxworms are studied in relation to their consumption of polyethylene (PE) and wax comb (WC).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="WAX COMB (WC)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lesser waxworms are studied for their growth and survivability when fed on polyethylene (PE) compared to their conventional diet (wax comb, WC).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="WAXCOMB (WC)">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Waxcomb (WC) is the natural diet of lesser waxworms and is used in experiments to study the effects of added nutrition on PE degradation&lt;SEP&gt;Waxcomb (WC) is used as a control in experiments to study the biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="SURVIVABILITY RATE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Survivability rate is used to assess the effects of PE and WC diets on the health of lesser waxworms&lt;SEP&gt;The survivability rate of lesser waxworms is higher when fed waxcomb compared to polyethylene (PE), highlighting the importance of nutrition for their growth and survival.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="WEIGHT LOSS OF PE FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Weight loss of PE films is measured to quantify the extent of PE degradation by lesser waxworms</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="CRUMPLED PAPER">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Crumpled paper is added to the larval container to provide a surface for lesser waxworms to spin their cocoons</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="WAXCOMB">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Waxcomb is the conventional food source for lesser waxworms, and its presence enhances their growth and survivability compared to PE.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="POST-DEGRADATION WEIGHT LOSS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Post-degradation weight loss measures the extent to which lesser waxworms degrade polyethylene (PE), with higher weight loss indicating more effective biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="ADULT WAXMOTH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Adult waxmoths are the mature stage of lesser waxworms, capable of producing a new generation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LESSER WAXWORMS" target="INFANT WAXWORMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Infant waxworms are newly hatched larvae that continue the lifecycle and biodegradation process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FT-IR SPECTROMETER" target="POTASSIUM BROMIDE (KBR)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Potassium bromide (KBr) is used to prepare samples for FT-IR spectroscopy to detect functional groups in polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STERILE SALT WATER" target="NITROGEN-FIXING ISOLATE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sterile salt water is used to rinse and re-suspend nitrogen-fixing bacterial cells.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRIMARY MICROPLASTICS" target="IUCN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The IUCN conducts research on the sources and impacts of primary microplastics in oceans.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYDROCARBON DEGRADERS" target="PETROLEUM-CONTAMINATED ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrocarbon degraders are bacteria that break down hydrocarbons in petroleum-contaminated environments, such as the Kuwait desert</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DELFTIA TSURUHATENSIS" target="TEREPHTHALATE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Delftia tsuruhatensis is a bacterium capable of assimilating terephthalate, a component of certain plastics</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYDROLYZED POLYACRYLAMIDE" target="EXPANDED GRANULAR SLUDGE BED REACTOR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydrolyzed polyacrylamide can be biodegraded using an expanded granular sludge bed reactor in combination with an aerobic biofilm reactor</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE FOAM WASTE" target="PLASTIC DEGRADING MICROBES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic-degrading microbes offer a potential solution for breaking down polystyrene foam waste.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STUDY DESIGN" target="INSTAR 4-5 LARVAE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Instar 4-5 larvae of Tenebrio molitor are used in the study to assess the effects of EPS and XPS foams on their survival, growth, and development.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STUDY DESIGN" target="CHI-SQUARE TEST">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Chi-square test is used to analyze the survival rate of mealworms fed with EPS and XPS foams compared to a standard diet.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STUDY DESIGN" target="ANOVA AND LSD TEST">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">ANOVA and LSD tests are used to compare the mean values of growth and development parameters of mealworms fed with EPS and XPS foams.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="XPS FOAM" target="FORMALDEHYDE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Formaldehyde in XPS foam is linked to reduced microbial growth and lower productivity in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ECOLOGY LETTERS" target="ENDOSYMBIOTIC BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ecology Letters publishes research on the biology of endosymbiotic bacteria and fungi in insects, including Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="JOURNAL OF INSECT SCIENCE" target="MICROBIAL INHIBITORS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Journal of Insect Science publishes research on microbial inhibitors in insect diets, including those affecting Tenebrio molitor.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OXFORD UNIVERSITY PRESS" target="INSECT-FUNGAL ASSOCIATIONS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oxford University Press publishes research on insect-fungal associations, including the role of yeasts in insect digestion.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HCFC-123" target="WORLD HEALTH ORGANIZATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The World Health Organization assessed HCFC-123 as an environmental pollutant, evaluating its potential health and environmental impacts</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POTASSIUM" target="CATION ANALYSIS">
