      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMMONIUM FORMATE" target="UHPLC SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ammonium formate is used in the mobile phase for lipid analysis in the UHPLC system</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ION CHROMATOGRAPHY" target="DIONEX ICS-6000">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Dionex ICS-6000 system is used for ion chromatography analysis of cations in frass.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ION CHROMATOGRAPHY" target="CHROMELEON 7 SOFTWARE">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Chromeleon 7 software controls data collection during ion chromatography analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIPIDOME" target="UHPLC SYSTEM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The lipidome analysis is conducted using the UHPLC system to study lipid composition in mealworm tissues, providing insights into metabolic changes during polystyrene degradation</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIPIDOME" target="APOPTOSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Apoptosis is observed in mealworms during polystyrene biodegradation, as indicated by changes in their lipidome.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="UHPLC SYSTEM" target="LUNA C18 COLUMN">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Luna C18 column is used in the UHPLC system for lipid analysis</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="UHPLC SYSTEM" target="QUADRUPOLE TIME-OF-FLIGHT MASS SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Quadrupole Time-Of-Flight mass spectrometer is used in conjunction with the UHPLC system for high-resolution mass spectrometry</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="UHPLC SYSTEM" target="QTOF MASS SPECTROMETER">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The UHPLC system is combined with the QTOF mass spectrometer for high-resolution analysis of chemical compounds.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LITHIUM" target="CATION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Lithium is one of the cations analyzed in the study, though it was not detected in any frass samples</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMMONIUM" target="CATION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ammonium is quantified using a set of commercial standards in the cation analysis process</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METHANESULFONIC ACID" target="CATION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Methanesulfonic acid is used as part of the eluent in the cation analysis process</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIONEX IONPAC CS12A" target="CATION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dionex Ionpac CS12A is used as the analytical column for cation analysis</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CG12 A-8 ΜM GUARD COLUMN" target="CATION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">CG12 A-8 μm guard column is used in conjunction with the Dionex Ionpac CS12A for cation analysis</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CATION DYNAMICALLY REGENERATED SUPPRESSOR" target="CATION ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cation Dynamically Regenerated Suppressor is used in the cation analysis process</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METHYL TERT-BUTYL ETHER" target="LIPID EXTRACTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Methyl tert-butyl ether is part of the extraction solvent used for lipid extraction from tissue samples</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="QTOF MASS SPECTROMETER" target="ESI">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Electrospray Ionization (ESI) is used in the QTOF mass spectrometer to ionize chemical compounds for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ESI" target="NITROGEN GAS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrogen gas is used in the ESI process to facilitate ionization and analysis of chemical compounds.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MASSHUNTER" target="LIPID ANNOTATOR">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Masshunter software is used in conjunction with Lipid Annotator for processing and annotating lipid data in the study.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SIMCA-P" target="OPLS-DA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Simca-P software is used to perform OPLS-DA, a multivariate statistical analysis, to model differences in metabolic profiles.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MAGNESIUM (MG²⁺)" target="MITOCHONDRIA">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mitochondria regulate magnesium concentration, which is affected by dietary polystyrene in mealworm larvae, impacting energy metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MITOCHONDRIA" target="ELECTRON TRANSPORT CHAIN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The electron transport chain in mitochondria is affected by magnesium levels and dietary polystyrene in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MITOCHONDRIA" target="ADP/ATP TRANSLOCATION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">ADP/ATP translocation in mitochondria is influenced by magnesium concentration and dietary polystyrene in mealworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DISPOSABLE SURGICAL MASKS" target="TAPHRINA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Taphrina showed a significant increase in the bran-fed group and is linked to the degradation of plastic materials, including masks.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DISPOSABLE SURGICAL MASKS" target="MIRIKA">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mrika, a cold-adapted yeast, appeared in the inner layer-fed group and may play a role in the degradation of mask materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DISPOSABLE SURGICAL MASKS" target="MICRO/NANOPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Disposable surgical masks release micro/nanoplastics, posing potential human health risks.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MASK LAYERS" target="INNER LAYER-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The inner layer-fed group ingested and partially degraded the inner layer of masks, showing the lowest survival rate and significant degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MASK LAYERS" target="MIDDLE LAYER-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The middle layer-fed group ingested and partially degraded the middle layer of masks, showing intermediate survival rates and degradation patterns.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MASK LAYERS" target="OUTER LAYER-FED GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The outer layer-fed group ingested and partially degraded the outer layer of masks, showing a significant decrease in survival rate and distinct degradation patterns.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AUREOBASIDIUM" target="PU (POLYURETHANE)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Aureobasidium is observed in higher abundances in PU-fed groups, contributing to the biodegradation of polyurethane (PU).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MASK DEGRADATION" target="FUNCTIONAL MICROORGANISMS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Functional microorganisms are essential for the biodegradation of masks, and future research should focus on isolating and identifying these microorganisms to improve degradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FACE MASKS" target="MICROPLASTIC FIBERS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Disposable face masks are a significant source of microplastic fibers in the environment, especially during the COVID-19 pandemic.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WHO" target="BMJ">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The WHO's declaration of the end of the COVID-19 global health emergency was published in the BMJ.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PENICILLIUM PINOPHILUM" target="THERMALLY TREATED POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Penicillium pinophilum is capable of biodegrading thermally treated low-density polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MITOCHONDRIAL CYTOCHROME OXIDASE (MTCOI)" target="DNA SEQUENCING ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The mtCOI gene is used in DNA sequencing analysis to confirm the genetic identity of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SECOND-GENERATION LESSER WAXWORMS" target="WAX PAPER">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Wax paper is used as a surface for egg laying by female moths in the second-generation lesser waxworm experiments</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PCR AMPLIFIED DNA" target="COXL SEQUENCES">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PCR amplified DNA is used to analyze coxl sequences to study genetic variations related to PE degradation in lesser waxworms</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENOMIC ANALYSIS" target="E. HORMAECHEI LG3">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Genomic analysis of E. hormaechei LG3 identified key enzymes involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENOMIC ANALYSIS" target="ENTEROBACTER HORMACHECHEI">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter hormaechei has been studied for its genomic characteristics, revealing new subspecies and environmental roles.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENOMIC ANALYSIS" target="ENTEROBACTER ROGENKAMPII">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Enterobacter rogenkampii is a newly identified species based on computational analysis of sequenced genomes.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RHODOCOCCUS RHODOCHROUS" target="OXIDIZED OLIGOMERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Oxidized oligomers are byproducts of polyethylene degradation, studied in the context of Rhodococcus rhodochrous biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSEUDOGENES" target="DNA-BASED DIET ANALYSES">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudogenes can complicate DNA-based diet analyses in predator-prey systems, requiring caution in interpretation.</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MALASSEZIA SPECIES" target="PCR-RESTRICTION ENDONUCLEASE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Malassezia species are identified using PCR-restriction endonuclease analysis, relevant in microbiological studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TROPICAL MANGROVE SOIL MICROFLORA" target="POLYTHENE CARRY BAGS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Microflora from tropical mangrove soils have been studied for their effectiveness in degrading polythene carry bags, contributing to plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IN-PLANT NMR ANALYSIS" target="VISCARIAULA DUBYANA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">In-plant NMR analysis is used to study the chemical composition of intact plants like Viscariaula dubyana.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GELATIN" target="POLY (VINYL ALCOHOL)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gelatin is blended with poly (vinyl alcohol) to create biodegradable films with improved physical properties.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GELATIN" target="BIODEGRADABLE FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradable films are made using gelatin as a key component to enhance their degradability and physical properties.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLY (VINYL ALCOHOL)" target="BIODEGRADABLE FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biodegradable films are created by blending poly (vinyl alcohol) with gelatin to improve their environmental performance.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PRO-OXIDANT ADDITIVES" target="POLYETHYLENE FILMS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pro-oxidant additives are added to polyethylene films to promote their biodegradation through oxidation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE WAX" target="BACTERIA AND FUNGI">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene wax is studied for its biodegradability by bacteria and fungi, providing insights into microbial degradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOMINANCE ANALYSIS (DA)" target="MULTIPLE LINEAR REGRESSION (MLR)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Multiple linear regression (MLR) is used in dominance analysis to model the relationship between variables influencing biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOMINANCE ANALYSIS (DA)" target="BUDESCU'S METHOD">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Budescu's method is applied in dominance analysis to determine the relative importance of predictors in biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOMINANCE ANALYSIS (DA)" target="SEMI-PARTIAL CORRELATION COEFFICIENTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Semi-partial correlation coefficients are used in dominance analysis to measure the unique contribution of each predictor variable.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE DEGRADATION" target="MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MM, MW, MS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Molecular weights (Mm, Mw, Ms) are used to measure the extent of polyethylene (PE) degradation by mealworms</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYETHYLENE DEGRADATION" target="CARBONYL COMPOUNDS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Carbonyl compounds are indicators of polyethylene degradation, showing the breakdown of plastic polymers.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURFACE HYDROPHOBICITY" target="WATER CONTACT ANGLE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Water contact angle is used to measure surface hydrophobicity, which changes as polymers undergo biodegradation</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WATER CONTACT ANGLE" target="POLYPROPYLENE FOAM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The water contact angle is used to assess the hydrophobicity of polypropylene foam, which influences its biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WATER CONTACT ANGLE" target="AGED MICROPLASTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The water contact angle decreases in aged microplastics, promoting microbial colonization and degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PE MPS" target="HYDROPHOBICITY CHANGE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) exhibit reduced hydrophobicity after ingestion by mealworms, making them less resistant to enzymatic attacks.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WCA ANALYSIS" target="HYDROPHOBICITY CHANGE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">WCA analysis is used to measure the hydrophobicity change of polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) after ingestion by mealworms, showing reduced resistance to enzymatic attacks.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ABIOTIC DEGRADATION" target="ENVIRONMENTAL DEGRADABLE POLYETHYLENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Environmentally degradable polyethylene is designed to undergo abiotic degradation more readily</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHOTOCHEMICAL STABILITY" target="HDPE/AG COMPOSITES">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">HDPE/Ag composites are studied for their photochemical stability, which is crucial for understanding their environmental degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Photochemical stability is studied in HDPE/Ag composites to understand their resistance to degradation under light exposure</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="XENOBIOTIC ADDITIVES" target="LANDFILL MICROBIAL COMMUNITY">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A landfill microbial community degrades xenobiotic additives, as revealed by metagenomic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Landfill microbial communities can degrade xenobiotic additives, highlighting their biodegradative potential</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM VIABILITY" target="NUTRIENT-DENSE SUBSTRATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">A nutrient-dense substrate can support mealworm viability during long-term plastic consumption experiments, providing essential nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD AND AGRICULTURE ORGANIZATION (FAO)" target="FATTY ACID RATIOS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fatty acid ratios are evaluated based on FAO nutritional recommendations to assess the suitability of mealworm larvae as a food source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPERIMENTAL TIME INTERVALS" target="MEALWORM MASS CHANGE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The 10-, 20-, and 30-day experimental time intervals were used to measure the mass change of mealworms over time.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXPERIMENTAL TIME INTERVALS" target="MEALWORM VIABILITY RATE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The viability rate of mealworms was calculated at the end of each experimental time interval to assess survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALKENE CRACKING" target="H-ZSM-5">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Alkene cracking reactions are influenced by the properties of the H-ZSM-5 catalyst, which affects the reactivity of the process.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXTENDED PRODUCER RESPONSIBILITY" target="PLASTIC PRODUCTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Extended producer responsibility is a concept aimed at addressing the problems posed by plastic products and improving waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="JELLYFISH" target="TROPHIC TRANSFERENCE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Jellyfish contribute to trophic transference by ingesting and transferring microplastics through the food chain.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
    </edge>
