    <edge source="ADULTS" target="FEEDING CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The formation of adult mealworms is significantly influenced by the type of feed, with wheat bran resulting in the highest number of adults.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="REARING GAIN" target="FEEDING CONDITIONS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rearing gain is optimized under specific feeding conditions, such as wheat bran, which accelerates development despite higher mortality.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FEEDING CONDITIONS" target="ENERGY RESERVES">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Feeding conditions directly affect the energy reserves of larvae, influencing their growth and survival.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOACTIVE COMPOUNDS" target="MARINE AND FRESHWATER ALGAE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bioactive compounds are studied for their health benefits in marine and freshwater algae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GLIRICIDIA SEPIUM" target="PLANOCOCCUS CITRI">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The flavonoid-rich fraction of Gliricidia sepium leaves has insecticidal effects on coffee mealybugs (Planococcus citri).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLANOCOCCUS CITRI" target="FLAVONOID-RICH FRACTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The flavonoid-rich fraction from Gliricidia sepium leaves is effective in controlling coffee mealybugs (Planococcus citri).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="KOCURIA" target="MALACHITE GREEN">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Kocuria rosea can degrade malachite green, a compound with a styrene core structure, suggesting a potential link to polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PHYLOSEQ" target="MICROBIOME CENSUS DATA">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Phyloseq is an R package designed for the analysis and visualization of microbiome census data, as developed by McMurtrie and Holmes (2013).</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="A. DIAPERINUS" target="GREGARINES">
      <data key="d3">13.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gregarines are parasites found in the gut of A. diaperinus, which may influence the gut microbiome and its role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gregarines are present in the gut of A. diaperinus and may influence their gut microbial community, affecting biodegradation processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">6&lt;SEP&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="A. DIAPERINUS" target="VEGETABLE FOOD">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Vegetable food is used as a control diet for A. diaperinus larvae to compare the effects of polystyrene feeding on their gut microbial community.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PS SAMPLES" target="CT SAMPLES">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS samples were compared to CT samples to evaluate the effects of polystyrene as a food source on microbial diversity.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PS SAMPLES" target="ALPHA-DIVERSITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Alpha-diversity was significantly lower in PS samples compared to CT samples, indicating the impact of polystyrene on microbial diversity.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BETA-DIVERSITY" target="PCOA PLOTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Beta-diversity differences between PS and CT samples were visualized using PCoA plots.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MALACHITE GREEN" target="KOCURIA ROSEA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Kocuria rosea can degrade malachite green, which has a styrene core structure similar to polystyrene, suggesting potential for polystyrene degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MALACHITE GREEN" target="KOCURIA ROSEA MTCC 1532">
      <data key="d3">18.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Kocuria rosea MTCC 1532 is capable of biodegrading malachite green, a synthetic dye and environmental pollutant.&lt;SEP&gt;Kocuria rosea MTCC 1532 is capable of biodegrading malachite green, a synthetic dye.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GLOEOPHYLLUM TRABEUM" target="POLYSTYRENE SULFONATE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gloeophyllum trabeum can depolymerize polystyrene sulfonate through hydroquinone-driven Fenton chemistry, indicating its role in biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE SULFONATE" target="HYDROQUINONE-DRIVEN FENTON CHEMISTRY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Hydroquinone-driven Fenton chemistry is used by Gloeophyllum trabeum to depolymerize polystyrene sulfonate, as studied by Krueger et al. (2015).</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYCYCLIC AROMATIC HYDROCARBONS" target="OZONATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Ozonation is used in combination with biodegradation to remediate polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons in soil.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RICE PLANTHOPPERS" target="CYTOCHROME P450 STEROL C-22 DESATURASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Rice planthoppers host yeast-like symbiotes that produce cytochrome P450 sterol C-22 desaturase, involved in sterol biosynthesis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANOBID BEETLES" target="CYTOCHROME P450 STEROL C-22 DESATURASE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Anobid beetles host yeast-like symbiotes that produce cytochrome P450 sterol C-22 desaturase, involved in sterol biosynthesis.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)" target="VIBRIO SP.">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Vibrio sp. is involved in the biodegradation of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) in selective enrichment cultures.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)" target="SULFATE-REDUCING BACTERIA">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Sulfate-reducing bacteria contribute to the biodegradation of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) in selective enrichment cultures.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)" target="ARTHAM AND DOBLE (2009)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Artham and Doble (2009) studied the biodegradation of polypropylene in seawater, reporting minimal weight loss.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)" target="ARKATKAR ET AL. (2010)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Arkatkar et al. (2010) achieved up to 2.5% weight loss of UV-pre-treated polypropylene films using Bacillus and Pseudomonas strains.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PP (POLYPROPYLENE)" target="CACCIARI ET AL. (1993)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cacciari et al. (1993) reported the highest removal efficiency of isotactic polypropylene (iPP) in a selective enrichment culture community.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STARVATION GROUP" target="DEB MODEL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The starvation group is analyzed using the DEB model to study the effects of nutrient deprivation on Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
