    <edge source="DIGESTIVE BIOFRAGMENTATION MODEL" target="KINETIC PARAMETERS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Kinetic parameters are optimized using the genetic algorithm to simulate the digestive biofragmentation model for plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Kinetic parameters are used to optimize the digestive biofragmentation model for simulating plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGESTIVE BIOFRAGMENTATION MODEL" target="SIZE-FREQUENCY DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Size-frequency distribution is predicted by the digestive biofragmentation model to understand the biodegradation process of plastics by mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DIGESTIVE BIOFRAGMENTATION MODEL" target="MATLAB">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">MATLAB is used to simulate the size-frequency particle distribution of residual PS and LDPE polymers during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GAMMARUS DUEBENI" target="POLYETHYLENE MICROPLASTICS (PE MPS)">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Gammarus duebeni fragments polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) into nanoplastics (NPs) in its digestive tract, contributing to the generation of smaller plastic particles.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EARTHWORMS (EISENIA ANDREI)" target="POLYETHYLENE MICROPLASTICS (PE MPS)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Earthworms (Eisenia andrei) generate nanoplastics (NPs) from polyethylene microplastics (PE MPs) in their hindgut, which can cause cellular damage.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="KINETIC PARAMETERS" target="GENETIC ALGORITHM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The genetic algorithm is used to optimize kinetic parameters for the digestive biofragmentation model.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="KINETIC PARAMETERS" target="MATRIX LABORATORY (MATLAB)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">MATLAB is used to calibrate kinetic parameters for the digestive biofragmentation model.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MILLI-Q ULTRAPURE WATER" target="EXPERIMENTAL CONTAINERS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Milli-Q ultrapure water is used to rinse experimental containers to remove microplastic contamination.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MP)" target="PY-GCMS ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Py-GCMS analysis is used to determine the size distribution of microplastics after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MICROPLASTICS (MP)" target="LASER PARTICLE ANALYZERS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Laser particle analyzers measure the size distribution of microplastics in frass after biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="VIENNA PEE DEE BELEMNITE" target="CARBON ISOTOPE MEASUREMENTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">VPDB is used as a reference standard for carbon isotope measurements in plastic degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSEUDOXANTHOMONAS SP. NYZ600" target="BISPHENOL-A POLYCARBONATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Pseudoxanthomonas sp. strain NyZ600 biodegrades bisphenol-A polycarbonate, showcasing its potential in plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BROMOXYNIL" target="ISOTOPE ANALYSIS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Isotope analysis is used to study the photo-degradation processes of bromoxynil, a herbicide.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PLASTIC PARTICLE POLLUTION" target="HUMAN BLOOD">
      <data key="d3">17.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Plastic particle pollution has been discovered in human blood, indicating potential health risks from microplastic exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic particles have been discovered in human blood, indicating the pervasive nature of microplastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d5">8&lt;SEP&gt;9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DEGRADATION RATES OF PLASTICS" target="ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The study on degradation rates of plastics provides insights into how long different plastics persist in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IRON CYCLE" target="THIOSULFATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Thiosulfate tunes the iron cycle in Fenton reactions, influencing the degradation of pollutants.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NITRATE" target="SULFONAMIDE ANTIBIOTICS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Nitrate promotes the transformation of sulfonamide antibiotics by peracetic acid, leading to the formation of reactive nitrogen species.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SULFONAMIDE ANTIBIOTICS" target="PERACETIC ACID">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Peracetic acid transforms sulfonamide antibiotics, influenced by the presence of nitrate.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="REACTIVE NITROGEN SPECIES" target="DISINFECTION BYPRODUCTS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Reactive nitrogen species contribute to the formation of potentially harmful disinfection byproducts during the transformation of sulfonamide antibiotics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ZEBRAFISH LARVAE" target="CADMIUM">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cadmium, in combination with polystyrene microplastics, alters neutrophil expression and metabolic regulation in zebrafish larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLY(BUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE) MICROPLASTICS" target="OXYTETRACYCLINE (OTC)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The degradation of poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) microplastics is influenced by the presence of oxytetracycline and environmental factors.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLY(BUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE) MICROPLASTICS" target="CU(II)">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Cu(II) influences the degradation of poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) microplastics in the presence of biofilms and dissolved organic matter.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLY(BUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE) MICROPLASTICS" target="DISSOLVED ORGANIC MATTER">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Dissolved organic matter affects the degradation of poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) microplastics in aquatic environments.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLY(BUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE) MICROPLASTICS" target="BIOFILMS">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biofilms influence the degradation of poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) microplastics in the presence of environmental factors like Cu(II).</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GRAPHENE OXIDE QUANTUM DOTS" target="ULTRAVIOLET SPECTRUM">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Graphene oxide quantum dots convert ultraviolet spectrum to enhance bioenergy production and carbon capture in microalgae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS (PS MPS)" target="PS-1 TO PS-6 GROUPS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">PS-1 to PS-6 groups were fed polystyrene microplastics of varying molecular weights to study their consumption and biodegradation rates.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENERGY SUPPLY" target="ULTRAHIGH-MW POLYSTYRENE">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The energy supply of mealworms is impacted by the ingestion of ultrahigh-MW polystyrene polymers, affecting their growth and metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYANILINE/GRAPHENE MODIFIED ANODE" target="AZO DYE WASTEWATER">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The modified anode enhances the decolorization efficiency of azo dye wastewater in microbial electrochemical systems.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEALWORM MEAL" target="ENVIRONMENTAL ASSESSMENT">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworm meal is evaluated for its environmental impact and potential as a sustainable alternative protein source.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PERFLUOROOCTANOIC ACID" target="PHYTOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Perfluorooctanoic acid induces phytotoxicity, affecting plant metabolism and growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PERFLUOROOCTANE SULFONATE" target="PHYTOTOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Perfluorooctane sulfonate induces phytotoxicity, affecting plant metabolism and growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAMMOX" target="MONTMORILLONITE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Montmorillonite enhances the nitrogen removal efficiency of anammox processes at reduced temperatures.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="POLYBROMINATED DIPHENYL ETHER-47" target="BREAST CANCER MICE">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Polybrominated diphenyl ether-47 impacts breast cancer mice, as studied through metabolomics and lipidomics.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TRIS(1,3-DICHLORO-2-PROPYL)PHOSPHATE" target="TRIPHENYL PHOSPHATE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Tris(1,3-dichloro-2-propyl)phosphate and triphenyl phosphate exhibit synergistic cytotoxicity, impacting environmental health.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WATER-SOLUBLE SYNTHETIC POLYMERS" target="ENVIRONMENTAL TOXICITY">
      <data key="d3">14.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Water-soluble synthetic polymers are studied for their environmental emission, usage, transport, transformation, persistence, and toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOCHEMICAL CHARACTERISTICS" target="STRESS FACTORS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Biochemical characteristics of mealworms, such as protein, carbohydrate, and lipid content, are influenced by stress factors like starvation and exposure to non-nutritive materials.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TRIGLYCERIDES" target="FAT BODY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The fat body in insects accumulates triglycerides, which are crucial for energy storage and survival during food shortages.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GLYCOGEN" target="HAEMOLYMPH">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Glycogen is stored in the haemolymph of insects and is essential for sustaining the nervous system and other organs.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ENERGY RESERVES" target="SURVIVAL CRITERION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Energy reserves are critical for meeting somatic maintenance costs and ensuring the survival of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METHANOGENIC COMMUNITIES" target="QUANTITATIVE REAL-TIME POLYMERASE CHAIN REACTION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Methanogenic communities are detected and quantified using quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction (qPCR) techniques.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD AVAILABILITY" target="RESERVE DENSITY">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Food availability directly affects reserve density in Tenebrio molitor larvae, with lower availability leading to decreased reserves.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD AVAILABILITY" target="PS + OAT GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PS + oat group is used to study the effects of dietary supplementation with oats on the growth and survival of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOOD AVAILABILITY" target="PS + OAT + VIT GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PS + oat + vit group is used to study the effects of dietary supplementation with oats and vitamins on the growth and survival of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="REPRODUCTION BUFFER" target="EGG PRODUCTION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Egg production depends on the energy allocated to the reproduction buffer during the imago stage.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="REPRODUCTION BUFFER" target="SCALED RESERVE DENSITY">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The scaled reserve density (e) influences the filling of the reproduction buffer, which is essential for Tenebrio molitor larvae to reach the pupation stage.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MATURITY MAINTENANCE" target="ENERGY ALLOCATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Energy allocation influences maturity maintenance costs in Tenebrio molitor larvae, which can be impacted by PS diets.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SOMATIC MAINTENANCE" target="ENERGY ALLOCATION">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Energy allocation in Tenebrio molitor larvae affects somatic maintenance costs, which can be altered by toxic substances like PS.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DEB MODEL" target="RESERVE DYNAMICS">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Reserve dynamics are analyzed using the DEB model to understand the energy allocation and growth patterns of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DEB MODEL" target="VON BERTALANFFY GROWTH RATE">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Von Bertalanffy growth rate is a key parameter in the DEB model used to describe the growth of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DEB MODEL" target="PS + OAT. + VIT. GROUP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The PS + oat. + vit. group is studied using the DEB model to assess the effects of dietary supplementation on Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DEB MODEL" target="ENERGY CONDUCTANCE">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Energy conductance (ν) is a key parameter in the DEB model, used to study the energy transfer and physiological responses of Tenebrio molitor larvae during starvation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PS + OAT GROUP" target="CRUSHED OATMEAL">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Crushed oatmeal is combined with polystyrene in the PS + oat group to study the feeding behavior and growth of Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PS + OAT GROUP" target="GROWTH INHIBITION">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Growth inhibition is observed in the PS + oat group, indicating that dietary supplementation with oats alone is insufficient to fully support larval growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM CHIPS" target="PS GROUP">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Styrofoam chips are fed to the PS group of Tenebrio molitor larvae to assess their consumption and potential degradation.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STARVATION 1 GROUP" target="STARVATION 2 GROUP">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Both Starvation 1 and Starvation 2 groups are used to study the effects of food deprivation on Tenebrio molitor larvae, with differences in access to droppings and moult residues.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="VITAMINS AND MINERALS" target="PS + OAT. + VIT.">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Larvae in the PS + Oat. + Vit. group were fed vitamins and minerals along with polystyrene and oatmeal, to assess their impact on growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STYROFOAM POLYSTYRENE" target="PS MASS LOSS">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The mass loss of polystyrene is measured to evaluate its biodegradation in the presence of larvae.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVIVAL CRITERION" target="SOMATIC MAINTENANCE COSTS">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Somatic maintenance costs are a key factor in the survival criterion of the DEB model, determining whether larvae can survive.</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVIVAL CRITERION" target="STRUCTURAL LENGTH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Structural length is used in the DEB model to assess whether larvae can survive based on their size and energy reserves.</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SCALED RESERVES DENSITY" target="VOLUMETRIC LENGTH">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scaled reserves density influences changes in volumetric length, as described in the DEB model.</data>
